globalThis.BLACK_CIRCLE_CONTENT={"episodes":[{"id":"start","label":"Start","number":"00","file":"start-cover.webp","fallback":"Black Circle: The Quietest Power","eyebrow":"World Primer","recap":"The world ranks magicians by visible circle color, but Haru hides an impossible Black Circle behind a harmless Green disguise.","accent":["#29d17d","#171717","#ffd166"],"displayLabel":"Start","displayNumber":"00","displayEyebrow":"World Primer","arcLabel":"World Primer","title":"BLACK CIRCLE: THE QUIETEST POWER","body":"**World Primer · 00**\n\n---\n\nEvery magician is born with a rank they did not choose.\n\nThe **Magic Circle** awakens near the heart during childhood. When magic is channeled, its visible crest can surface at the wrist, throat, collarbone, or another natural casting point. Wherever it appears, its color determines the shape of a life before that life has had the chance to decide anything.\n\nEveryone is taught that a Circle's color cannot change.\n\n**Green Circle** magicians make up roughly sixty percent of the population. They maintain wards, route transit systems, and perform the necessary work that everyone depends on and almost no one names. Green is the foundation of civilization. It is also the easiest rank to dismiss.\n\n**Blue Circle** magicians make up roughly thirty percent. Skilled and respected, they move through institutions with fewer doors closed against them. The system does not place them at the top, but it was built to recognize their value.\n\n**Red Circle** magicians make up roughly nine percent. They become heirs, council officers, commanders, and institutional leaders. When they enter a room, the room adjusts.\n\n**Yellow Circle** magicians are the remaining one percent: the highest rank human magic has officially produced. They are admired, feared, recruited, and never left alone.\n\nBeyond Yellow, the accepted knowledge stops.\n\nWhat lies beyond is called **Black Circle** in old texts: a celestial rank so far past Yellow that comparison loses meaning. No verified public record proves a bearer has ever lived. Most scholars dismiss it as mythology. Most institutions prefer to.\n\n---\n\n**Haru Aerlen** transfers into **Aurelis Arcane Academy** mid-year with an unremarkable Green crest and a careful plan to stay invisible.\n\nThe plan is simple.\n\nThe crest is false.\n\nHis true Circle is Black.\n\nAurelis is beautiful, competitive, and cruel in the precise way elite institutions can be cruel: through traditions no one is supposed to question and rules that sound neutral until they close around the powerless. Rank decides who receives patience and who becomes an example. Rumors do damage before anyone confirms them.\n\nThen **Elira Vale**, Aurelis's Yellow Circle prodigy, treats Haru like a person before she treats him like a rank. Friendship makes invisibility harder. The approaching inter-academy tournament makes it dangerous. And the students of **Noctis Dominion** begin noticing that nothing breaks the way it should around Aurelis's quiet Green transfer.\n\nHaru's problem is no longer simply that he might be discovered.\n\nIt is that the people around him are real.\n\nPower was never the question. The real test is whether he can keep choosing restraint when the institution closes its fist around the ones he cares about, when the tournament turns protection into spectacle, and when every act of mercy leaves another piece of evidence against him.\n\n---\n\nTrue strength does not stand above others.\n\nIt protects them.","cover":"assets/episodes/start-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep1","label":"Episode 1","number":"01","file":"episode-01-cover.webp","fallback":"The Green Circle Transfer Student","eyebrow":"Arrival","recap":"","accent":["#35d07f","#23a6d5","#fff1a8"],"displayLabel":"Episode 1","displayNumber":"01","displayEyebrow":"The Hidden Circle","arcLabel":"The Hidden Circle","title":"The Green Circle Transfer Student","body":"The morning sky over **Aurelis City** caught the sun in layered gold, each cloud edge refracting mana into angles ordinary light should never have held.\n\nBetween the white towers and glass bridges of the capital, floating transport sigils shimmered in neat lanes. Suspended carriages glided soundlessly above the avenues while fountains in the lower plazas threw pale mist across marble steps already crowded with students in navy and cream.\n\nThe new uniform collar had been scratching the same spot on my neck since the night train.\n\nEighteen years old, mid-year transfer from Lumeris District: **Haru Aerlen**, wearing the weakest crest society could tolerate. The uniform fabric was expensive and enchanted, cut with more polish than any district coat I had owned, and beneath the left cuff sat a modest **Green Circle** crest pressed lightly into my inner wrist.\n\nThe gate's ward pressure settled cold behind my sternum as **Aurelis Arcane Academy** rose ahead.\n\nTall crystal windows reflected fractured sunlight beneath six rune-marked towers. White stone stairways climbed past silver-leaf trees and statues of famous alumni who governed cities and imperial ministries.\n\nA quiet pulse stirred behind my ribs.\n\nI pressed two fingers lightly over my sternum.\n\nBeneath my shirt, hidden under layers of quiet mana concealment, my true Magic Circle slept in silence near my heart.\n\nIt did not blaze like Yellow or radiate with noble warmth like Red. It was darker than shadow, exact as law, and vast enough that even fully suppressed it made the air around me feel subtly wrong if my focus slipped. Inside it turned impossible geometry: patterns too old, too complete, and too absolute for modern magical theory to name.\n\nBlack Circle.\n\nI had no intention of letting anyone see it. I had spent years learning how to be small: how to suppress, how to deflect, how to live inside a margin too narrow to invite a second glance.\n\nSix weeks earlier, that plan had stopped being enough.\n\nDuring my final Lumeris district assessment, the Green crest on my wrist had lagged half a beat behind my pulse, leaving a dark bruise in the inspector's reading glass before snapping back to pale emerald. The clerk had blamed a cracked lens and moved on.\n\nThat same evening, an Aurelis transfer packet appeared inside my locked room.\n\nMy written examination scores were copied on the first page. The second held one unsigned sentence:\n\n**THE FOUNDATION REMEMBERS WHAT LUMERIS WAS TAUGHT TO FORGET. COME BEFORE THEY MEASURE YOU AGAIN.**\n\nStaying meant submitting to a second inspection my concealment might not survive. Vanishing would confirm that Lumeris had uncovered something worth hunting. Aurelis offered a public reason to leave and, somewhere beneath its old stone, a possible answer to why the Black Circle had begun resisting sleep.\n\nI did not trust the invitation. I trusted ignorance less.\n\nMana flowed along my wrist in a whisper. The illusion settled neatly under my cuff. Anyone glancing at my sleeve now would see an ordinary **Green Circle crest**: slightly faint, slightly unimpressive, the exact sort of mark people looked past before they finished blinking.\n\nI took a breath and stepped through the gates.\n\nThe stares started almost immediately.\n\nSome students were too busy greeting friends or checking class rosters on the floating directory panels to care. Some were already pretending not to look at anyone ranked beneath them.\n\nNo one walked beside me.\n\nWithin ten paces, enough eyes had shifted my way that the verdict was obvious. They knew the three facts before I reached the main steps: transfer student, mid-year entry, Green Circle.\n\nAt a school like Aurelis, those three facts could turn into a rumor before first bell. I kept walking.\n\nNear the fountain to my right, a pair of Blue Circle boys lowered their voices just enough to make sure I heard them. Farther along the walkway, Red and Blue crests flashed as students noticed the pale green glow at my cuff with amused disbelief. I passed without reacting. Casual cruelty worked best when you refused to hand it a face.\n\nThe entrance hall opened around me like a cathedral built for magical aristocracy.\n\nA domed ceiling glimmered with artificial constellations. Black stone floors mirrored floating chandeliers. Twin staircases curved toward the upper academic wings, and golden banners hung between the pillars bearing the academy seal.\n\nA luminous panel unfolded in the air before me.\n\n**WELCOME, TRANSFER STUDENT: HARU AERLEN**\n**YEAR TWO - SECTION THREE**\n**HOMEROOM: EAST WING, THIRD FLOOR**\n\nInstructor Selene: \"Haru Aerlen?\"\n\nI turned.\n\nA woman in a formal navy academy coat approached with a rune-glass tablet tucked under one arm. Her dark hair was tied back neatly, a slender **Red Circle** crest glowing near the base of her throat. Her expression carried the brisk fatigue of someone who had already solved three administrative headaches before breakfast.\n\nInstructor Selene: \"I'm Instructor Selene Varis. Student Affairs. You're the transfer from Lumeris District?\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nInstructor Selene: \"Come with me. Homeroom has already started.\"\n\nI nodded and followed her into the east corridor.\n\nThe deeper we walked, the clearer the academy's invisible map became. Rooms reserved for top sections sat closer to cleaner mana conduits. Certain hallways displayed only Red and Yellow alumni portraits. The walls themselves knew who the school had been built to remember.\n\nIn a study alcove near the eastern stair, a Green Circle student raised one hand to point out a scheduling error and was cut off mid-sentence by a Blue who did not even slow down to see who had spoken.\n\nSelene noticed where I was looking.\n\nInstructor Selene: \"Aurelis rewards excellence.\"\n\nI wondered how many people repeated that sentence without ever asking who got to define excellence in the first place.\n\nHaru: \"I see.\"\n\nShe glanced at me.\n\nInstructor Selene: \"This school is not easy, Haru. Students here are ambitious, competitive, and mostly from influential houses. You may face resistance.\"\n\nHaru: \"That's expected.\"\n\nA flicker of approval crossed her face.\n\nInstructor Selene: \"Your admission was unusual. Your Circle ranking raised objections, but your written examination scores were extraordinary. Theory, tactical ethics, mana structures, ancient script analysis. Someone argued strongly in your favor.\"\n\nHaru: \"Someone?\"\n\nInstructor Selene: \"I was not told who.\"\n\nThe unsigned sentence surfaced again with unpleasant clarity. Someone had not merely noticed me. They had built a door and waited to see if I would walk through it.\n\nHaru: \"Was the recommendation attached to the Foundation archives?\"\n\nSelene's stride faltered by less than a breath.\n\nInstructor Selene: \"No archive access was listed in your student file. Why?\"\n\nHaru: \"The transfer reading list referenced pre-standardization records. Specifically, the Foundation's suppressed classifications.\"\n\nIt was a gamble sharp enough to test how closely the administration watched old records. Selene paused, studying me with fresh wariness before resuming her stride, though the question stayed with her. Good. If the anonymous sender had reached through Student Affairs, I wanted the noise to travel back to whoever was listening.\n\nShe stopped before a pair of double doors. Inside, a lecture was already underway.\n\nSelene opened the doors and stepped aside.\n\nThe classroom beyond was bright and tiered, with curved rows of desks facing tall windows along one wall. At the front, Instructor Dren stood beside a floating spell diagram. Conversation thinned the moment thirty pairs of eyes caught the faint green crest peeking beneath my cuff.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"The transfer? Haru Aerlen. Introduce yourself.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm Haru Aerlen. I transferred in today. I'll do my best.\"\n\nScattered snickers broke out among the Red and Blue rows until Dren rapped his knuckles sharply against the slate.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"That's enough.\"\n\nThe room settled, though not with much grace.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Back row. Open seat beside Aira.\"\n\nAs I climbed the aisle, I caught a folded paper pellet an inch from my shoulder and let it drop to the floor anyway. No reason to make reflex look deliberate.\n\nAt the back sat a girl with warm brown eyes and chestnut hair tied loosely to one side. Her **Blue Circle** crest glowed on the inside of her wrist, and she looked up without the practiced sizing-up most Blues wore.\n\nThat alone set her apart.\n\nAira: \"Hi. I'm Aira Solen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Haru.\"\n\nAira: \"Nice to meet you.\"\n\nThere was no mockery in it, no hidden angle. Just plain warmth that felt rare enough to make me cautious for half a second.\n\nAt the front, Instructor Dren resumed the lecture.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"As I was saying, magical society classifies human mana potential into four recognized Circle ranks.\"\n\nThe academy liked the word *recognized*. It made the world sound tidy.\n\nChildren learned early that the color awakening near the heart changed how adults listened. Once mana matured, society sorted them cleanly and called it nature.\n\nFour practice disks descended from the ceiling beneath the projected crests. Dren did not start with definitions. He pointed to a Green Circle girl in the second row.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"One thread. Hold the disk level.\"\n\nShe startled, then obeyed. Green light ran from her wrist to the nearest disk. It rose, wobbled once, and steadied. Ten seconds. Twenty.\n\nA registry tag flashed into the air above her without anyone asking for it.\n\n**UTILITY TRACK / MAJORITY COHORT**\n\nTwo students smirked.\n\nThe girl's jaw tightened. Her disk did not tremble.\n\nDren erased the label with a sharp sweep of his hand.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Registry language. Not mine.\"\n\nThat won him more attention than any textbook definition would have.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Green Circle accounts for roughly sixty percent of the population. Lowest average raw reserve, narrowest output margin. Also the backbone of long-duration civic wards, maintenance routes, food seals, transport lines, and every ordinary system people only notice after it fails.\"\n\nThe girl hit thirty seconds.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Release.\"\n\nHer disk settled cleanly without cracking.\n\nLeon raised his hand and stood before Dren called on him.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Mr. Varek. Same instruction.\"\n\nBlue mana struck the second disk. It jumped twice as high as the Green one, bright enough to throw water-colored shadows across the front desks. Several students murmured approval.\n\nAt twenty-two seconds, one edge dipped.\n\nLeon overcorrected. The disk spun out of alignment.\n\nDren caught it before it struck the floor.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Blue Circle. Roughly thirty percent. Stronger average reserves, broader practical application, and most of the skilled professional class.\"\n\nHe set the disk back in midair.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"More is not the same as controlled.\"\n\nLeon sat back down with his spine very straight.\n\nA Red Circle student lifted three disks at once, bending her mana cleanly around the correction runes instead of fighting them.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Red. Roughly nine percent. High reserves, refined affinities, high adaptability.\"\n\nThe registry projection supplied its own verdict:\n\n**LEADERSHIP COMPATIBILITY: HIGH**\n\nThe Red student smiled before she could stop herself.\n\nDren looked straight at the floating letters.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"A prediction repeated often enough becomes a hiring practice. Do not confuse either with character.\"\n\nNo one smiled after that.\n\nThe Yellow disk remained empty. It did not need a volunteer to alter the room. Its projected crest washed gold over the front rows, and students sat straighter beneath a rank none of them had demonstrated.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Yellow Circle. Approximately one percent. Highest recognized modern classification. Most institutions go years without training one.\"\n\nHe dismissed the disks.\n\nThe lesson had never claimed Green meant useless. It had simply arranged the room so everyone knew whose failure deserved patience and whose success was expected to stay polite.\n\nAbove Dren, the four circles rotated slowly, Green, Blue, Red, and Yellow washing their approved colors across thirty faces.\n\nThe unsigned note had promised the Foundation remembered what Lumeris was taught to forget. Asking directly about Black Circle would have been foolish, yet testing whether the academy's syllabus admitted any blind spots might tell me what kind of trap I had walked into.\n\nI raised my hand. That alone surprised half the room.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Yes, transfer student?\"\n\nI kept my face neutral.\n\nHaru: \"Are those four categories exhaustive? The transfer reading mentioned pre-standardization circles.\"\n\nAira turned toward me so quickly her pen scratched across her margin.\n\nTwo students blinked. Someone near the front mouthed *pre-standardization* as if checking whether the phrase sounded less absurd silently. Leon laughed first.\n\nLeon: \"You mean Black Circle?\"\n\nEnough students joined him to fill the classroom in a single wave.\n\nStudent: \"What is this, a storybook?\"\n\nStudent: \"I knew Greens had odd theories, but that's embarrassing.\"\n\nLeon: \"Are we studying children's myths now?\"\n\nRed Student: \"You might as well ask about star dragons and moon gods.\"\n\nAira did not laugh. She watched me instead, curious and wide awake.\n\nInstructor Dren raised one hand, and the noise dropped away. His face showed no mockery, only the patience of a teacher deciding how much weight to give an unexpected question.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"In formal magical theory, the four categories are exhaustive for modern classification. Since Mr. Varek supplied the folklore term, there is no recognized Black Circle.\"\n\nHe turned slightly and summoned a second projection beside the four crests: fragments of ancient script, incomplete diagrams, broken rings.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"In very old texts, however, occasional references appear to anomalous or transcendent mana structures beyond the standard hierarchy. Most scholars treat them as symbolic, religious, or metaphorical. Some consider them preservation errors. A small minority of fringe theorists have speculated that a higher classification was once hypothesized.\"\n\nThe fragments dissolved and reformed.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"There is no verified historical evidence that any human has ever carried such a circle. In academic practice, Black Circle is not treated as reality.\"\n\nHaru: \"So it's impossible?\"\n\nDren folded his arms.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"In magic, 'impossible' is frequently a temporary word. Until something can be observed, measured, or reproduced, it remains speculation. Your task here is to understand what exists, not chase legends that flatter the imagination.\"\n\nHis gaze swept the room before resting briefly on Leon's row.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Understanding what exists includes understanding limits. Power without discipline is not mastery. It is noise with a crest attached.\"\n\nThe class relaxed again. Someone chuckled under their breath.\n\nLeon: \"There you have it.\"\n\nStudent: \"Imagine transferring in and asking about fairy tales.\"\n\nAira glanced between me and the front of the room.\n\nAira: \"Bold first-day question.\"\n\nHaru: \"The reading list was vague.\"\n\nHer eyes lingered on me a second longer than polite conversation required.\n\nAira: \"Somehow, I don't think a reading list carried all of that.\"\n\nI did not answer.\n\nAt the front, Dren dismissed the projection and moved on.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Remember this. Circle rank reflects potential, not guaranteed victory. A stronger Circle can still lose to a weaker opponent with superior control, timing, or judgment. Raw mana matters. It is not everything.\"\n\nThat much, at least, was true.\n\nBeside me, Aira returned to her notes. I left my notebook mostly blank and listened.\n\nThe rest of the lecture covered layered output, mana stability under emotional strain, and the structural ceilings of reinforcement casting across ranks. It was competent instruction, cleanly delivered, and full of small gaps no one in the room knew existed.\n\nI noticed every one of them, not because I was brighter than the students around me, but because I had spent my whole life living outside the chart they thought explained the world.\n\nWhen the bell rang, chatter returned at once.\n\nAira turned to me first.\n\nAira: \"If you need help finding the practical arena, I can show you.\"\n\nBefore I could answer, a shadow fell across our desks.\n\nLeon Varek stood there wearing a smile polished enough to pass for warmth to anyone who wanted things simple.\n\nLeon: \"Aira, we're still meeting at the west courtyard before practical assessment.\"\n\nAira: \"I remember.\"\n\nHis eyes shifted to me.\n\nLeon: \"And you must be in our practical block.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am.\"\n\nHe nodded as if absorbing unfortunate news.\n\nLeon: \"Try not to do anything reckless. Some exercises aren't built for low-capacity circles. I'd hate for your first day to turn into a cautionary tale.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'll be careful.\"\n\nHe looked faintly disappointed that I had not reached for the bait.\n\nAira: \"Leon.\"\n\nLeon: \"What? New students should know the risks.\"\n\nHe straightened and walked off.\n\nAira: \"He does that whenever he wants an audience.\"\n\nHaru: \"He seems efficient about it.\"\n\nThat drew a small, real laugh out of her. For some reason, it stayed with me longer than Leon's warning.\n\nWhen the transition bell echoed down the hall, the class drifted toward the eastern training complex in familiar clusters. Red Circles moved with the easy posture of students accustomed to being admired without asking, Blues gathered in tight debating knots, and Greens drifted toward the edges of the corridor by habit.\n\nAhead of us, a second-year in a green-trimmed uniform stepped aside to let a Red group pass and nearly collided with an automated cleaning construct rather than slow their pace.\n\nAira fell into step beside me anyway. Not close enough to make a show of it, yet close enough that people noticed. That alone seemed to irritate Leon, who kept glancing back from the cluster ahead.\n\nThe training complex dwarfed the classroom blocks, built from white stone braided with metallic mana-thread and etched floor to ceiling with stabilization runes. The main assessment hall opened into a wide circular arena surrounded by tiered observation stands and an overhead projection lattice.\n\nIt was a room designed for talent to be applauded, and for weakness to be displayed.\n\nStudents filed into the stands while Instructor Dren stepped to the center of the stone floor, his boots clicking once against the polished tiles.\n\n**PRACTICAL ASSESSMENT: MANA RESPONSE AND COMBAT ADAPTABILITY**\n\nDren folded his arms.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Listen carefully. This is neither a ranking match nor an excuse to settle personal grudges. Today's objective is control under pressure, reaction speed, adaptive judgment, and efficient output.\"\n\nHe gestured, and new lines rearranged themselves beneath the floating header.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"You will engage in supervised one-on-one assessments. Victory is not determined by brute force. Points are awarded for tactical decisions, precision, composure, restraint, and proper use of arena rules.\"\n\nRed-Haired Student: \"So overwhelming your opponent immediately is discouraged.\"\n\nInstructor Dren: \"It is, when your idea of overwhelming them requires reconstructive healing.\"\n\nA few students laughed.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Lethal intent results in immediate disqualification and disciplinary review. Excessive force lowers your score. Loss of emotional control lowers your score. If you possess the skill to win cleanly and choose chaos instead, I will assume your education failed somewhere.\"\n\nThat last sentence did more work than the official rules.\n\nBeside me, Aira shifted slightly.\n\nAira: \"He says things like that a lot.\"\n\nHaru: \"Does it help?\"\n\nAira: \"Not as much as he hopes.\"\n\nThe corner of my mouth lifted before I caught it.\n\nDren raised one hand, and a roster panel flared above the arena. Matchups began scrolling into place. Students reacted with quiet satisfaction, annoyance, or poorly hidden dread. Friends avoided each other's eyes. Rivals sat upright. Then my name appeared near the bottom of the board.\n\n**HARU AERLEN vs LEON VAREK**\n\nThe temperature in the room changed instantly.\n\nA ripple of interest swept the stands. Several students laughed without bothering to soften it. Others traded knowing glances, already deciding how many seconds the match would last.\n\nLeon laid one hand over his chest in theatrical concern.\n\nLeon: \"Sir, I'm not sure this is fair.\"\n\nInstructor Dren: \"For whom?\"\n\nThat drew louder laughter.\n\nLeon: \"A Blue Circle against a first-day Green transfer might leave the wrong impression.\"\n\nInstructor Dren: \"It will. Put yourself in the arena, not in your ego.\"\n\nAira turned toward me at once, her expression sharp with genuine worry rather than pity.\n\nAira: \"You don't have to prove anything.\"\n\nHaru: \"I don't plan to.\"\n\nAira: \"Leon likes humiliating people when he has an audience.\"\n\nHaru: \"I noticed.\"\n\nAira: \"He's strong, but he performs. If you stay calm and force him to pick his angles, you might last longer than he expects.\"\n\nLast longer. A fair assumption, given what she believed I was.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nA brief flicker of surprise crossed her face, a quick lift of her brows followed by a careful nod, as if plain gratitude had not been the answer she expected from me either.\n\nLeon dropped from the platform into the ring with practiced ease. Blue mana curled around his wrists like water ribbons catching sunlight. He worked the stands naturally, not through loud theatrical gestures, just with the posture of someone accustomed to owning every eye in the room.\n\nI stepped down onto the stone opposite him.\n\nThe barrier sealed overhead with a low hum. Instantly, I felt the weight of the gallery. The Greens watched quietly. The Blues leaned forward eagerly. The Reds looked on with cool detachment. Aira watched with narrowed focus.\n\nInstructor Dren stepped outside the barrier line and raised one hand.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Assessment begins on my signal. First clean disruption of three balance markers, forced surrender, or incapacitation ends the match. Remember the rules.\"\n\nLeon smiled across the ring.\n\nLeon: \"You can yield before we start. Nobody will blame you.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm fine.\"\n\nHis smile sharpened.\n\nDren dropped his hand.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Begin.\"\n\nLeon moved first.\n\nA compressed stream of water mana shot toward me in a crisp horizontal line, fast enough to force a reaction yet measured enough to avoid serious injury. I shifted half a step left. The jet sliced past my ribs and burst into mist against the far barrier.\n\nLeon did not pause. A second stream followed at once, then a third curving inward from my blind side to narrow my footing. Clean sequencing. Better than average for his age.\n\nI retreated two paces and pivoted. The second strike grazed my sleeve. The third missed my shoulder by inches.\n\nMurmurs stirred along the stands. They had expected panic. Leon's eyes narrowed.\n\nHe snapped both wrists upward, and six thin pillars of water mana erupted from the tiles around me in a tight ring. The pillars leaned inward in unison, sealing off the flanks to force a clumsy jump.\n\nEffective against anyone relying on brute escape.\n\nMy shoulders tightened as the arcs converged. Half a second before the cage closed, I slipped sideways through the narrowest seam.\n\nStartled noises broke from the benches. Leon clicked his tongue.\n\nLeon: \"Not bad. For a Green.\"\n\nA few chuckles answered him. He drew more mana.\n\nFive crystalline water lances formed above his shoulders, turning slowly with dense pressure. Sharper than his opening probes, heavy enough that a clean hit would bruise bone. He wanted a public collapse.\n\nLeon: \"Last chance. Yield gracefully.\"\n\nI studied him across the ring. Past the polish stood a boy checking whether the room still belonged to him. That alone would make him sloppy.\n\nHaru: \"I'd rather continue.\"\n\nThe lances fired.\n\nI raised one hand and threw up a thin, uneven mana shield, the exact kind of frantic barrier a Green Circle student might summon under pressure. The first lance shattered across its face. The second deflected off the angled rim. The third tore past my collar as I shifted weight and let the last two spend themselves against the wall behind me.\n\nGasps rose through the tiers. A Green Circle had no business reading a practiced Blue that cleanly.\n\nLeon heard the shift in the stands and hated it. His smile vanished.\n\nLeon: \"Fine.\"\n\nThe air inside the barrier chilled as he pulled ambient moisture into his field. Water gathered around him in spiraling bands, denser and colder than before.\n\nInstructor Dren called from outside the ward line.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Varek. Control your output.\"\n\nLeon's jaw set. He chose pressure anyway.\n\nThree whip-like streams snapped toward me from three angles: left, right, overhead. I threw myself back across the tiles. One lash carved a shallow trench through the reinforced stone where my boot had been. Another clipped my left forearm before I could clear the arc.\n\nA sharp ripple of pain ran up my arm, real enough to make my fingers clench.\n\nI landed beside one of the circular balance markers inlaid in the floor. Leon saw it immediately and switched angles without breaking stride, driving a concentrated pulse of water mana straight into the stone beneath my boots.\n\nI pushed off a heartbeat late. The tile cracked under impact and the glyph flared bright blue.\n\n**POINT: VAREK**\n\nThe overhead panel flashed.\n\nCheers broke from a pocket of Blue Circle students. One Red near the front clapped twice, bored and approving. Down on the lower benches, the Greens stared at the glowing score instead of Leon, as though the point had confirmed a lesson they had been taught years ago.\n\nLeon: \"There it is.\"\n\nHe thought the match had snapped back to normal. I let him keep the thought.\n\nWe circled. He pressed harder now, trading clean form for speed. Sheets of water mana folded into flat blades while low mist crept across the floor to hide my footing. He wanted to force a blunder before the stands finished remembering the first exchange.\n\nI parried one strike with a tight reinforcement pulse, sidestepped the second, and let the third shove me backward.\n\nI could have ended the match in three seconds. Instead, I kept it difficult. Looking weak took far more discipline than being strong.\n\nPain was simple. Pain arrived, named itself, and faded. Arranging my face around it was the real work: letting my shoulders pull inward just enough to look cornered, letting the small laughs travel across the gallery without answering them, keeping my breath thin so no one mistook restraint for threat.\n\nIt had never become natural. Only practiced.\n\nUnderneath the habit, something older lifted its head. Not the Black Circle. Me. The part that wanted, just once, to stop being careful for people who treated caution as proof I belonged beneath their boots. The part that wanted the stands to go dead silent for the right reason.\n\nI swallowed that impulse until my throat ached.\n\nLeon lunged inside close range, condensing water along his forearm into a hardened blade. He swung hard.\n\nI blocked late and let the impact slide me across the stone.\n\nStudent: \"There!\"\n\nStudent: \"Now he's done.\"\n\nLeon had overcommitted. His weight leaned too far over his front boot, opening his centerline for a fraction of a second while his follow-up spell was already forming before he recovered balance. Small flaws. Common ones.\n\nI moved, not fast enough to look unnatural, just quicker than his pride allowed him to expect.\n\nI stepped inside his reach, caught his wrist, and tapped two fingers against the mana node beside his elbow. A tiny pulse. Just enough to sever the channel running through his dominant arm.\n\nHis water construct burst into spray. Leon's eyes went wide.\n\nI pivoted past his shoulder and pressed my palm flat against the nearest balance marker while he was still stumbling for footing. The glyph flashed.\n\n**POINT: AERLEN**\n\nThe entire hall drew one sharp breath and forgot what to do with it.\n\nA Green Circle had scored on a Blue through clean timing, with neither luck nor a wild burst of mana to blame.\n\nLeon spun around, stunned. Voices broke out across the stands in ragged pieces.\n\nStudent: \"What?\"\n\nStudent: \"How did he-\"\n\nStudent: \"Did Leon just drop a point?\"\n\nStudent: \"No, he broke the casting channel-\"\n\nStudent: \"Can Greens even do that?\"\n\nOn the lower bench, a Green Circle boy smiled before remembering to hide it. A Blue beside him glared on Leon's behalf.\n\nUp in the tiers, Aira was already on her feet, her worry gone, replaced by bright, serious focus.\n\nLeon's face flooded red with humiliation.\n\nLeon: \"Fluke.\"\n\nI said nothing. That made it worse.\n\nWater surged around him in a violent spiral and crashed forward in a wide wave across the tiles. Mist exploded upward, choking visibility as students leaned back from the humming barrier.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Varek! Reduce output!\"\n\nLeon pushed harder. He was no longer fighting for points. He was fighting to erase the sound of the room.\n\nThrough the fog, I tracked him easily by his mana flow. His breath had gone ragged, his spell cadence jagged with temper. Six condensed water blades formed around him and launched in staggered waves: two high, two center, two low.\n\nI slowed my breathing and stopped pretending to stumble.\n\nStep left. Pivot. Drop. Turn.\n\nMy palm grazed the first blade and tilted it off-line. The second hissed past my ear. The third shattered on the tiles. I broke the fourth with a tight pulse hidden inside a clumsy-looking block, and slipped clean past the centerline as the last two missed entirely.\n\nNoise in the gallery thinned until only water hissing against the ward wall remained.\n\nLeon saw me step out of the mist untouched, and for the first time fear flickered behind his anger. Fear of not understanding what stood in front of him.\n\nHe drew mana too fast after that. Too much, too quickly, through too tight a rotation.\n\nThe pressure knot above his right wrist thickened, then stopped behaving like an attack and locked into an inward loop.\n\nI felt the collapse begin a breath before the gallery did. So did Dren.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Stop casting!\"\n\nLeon tried to muscle the spell into shape anyway. A compressed, violently vibrating sphere of turbulent water mana bucked above his wrist. If it folded back at that distance, his own Blue Circle would shred the channels in his casting arm.\n\nThe barrier hummed tight. Students recoiled from the railing.\n\nI felt the Black Circle stir behind my ribs. Three choices, none of them clean: expose too much and save him flawlessly, hold back and let his pride cripple him, or take the smaller wound myself.\n\nMercy was never soft in moments like that. It was arithmetic with bad margins.\n\nI stepped straight into the collapsing edge.\n\nLeon's eyes snapped to mine, anger cracking wide open into plain terror.\n\nThe outer shear tore my left sleeve open above the wrist and drove a bright, stinging shock straight down my forearm. While every eye in the stands flinched at the hit, I raised my hand and slipped an impossibly thin cancellation pulse inside ordinary reinforcement mana. Nowhere near true Black Circle output, just enough precision to deny the loop permission to detonate.\n\nThe unstable sphere folded inward with a wet, airless thump.\n\nThere was no blast, no backlash, no shattered bone. The compressed water simply fell around us as harmless rain.\n\nMy fingers went numb to the knuckles. On my exposed inner wrist, my false Green crest flickered half a heartbeat late. I lowered my arm before anyone noticed the hairline of dark light fade beneath my skin.\n\nHigh on the upper observation platform, a tall student in an elite mantle went completely still. Pale-gold hair caught the arena lamps, a brilliant **Yellow Circle** crest glowing at her collarbone as she rose half a step from her seat before the rest of the hall even realized Leon had been spared.\n\nOutside the barrier, Dren's eyes narrowed. He had seen enough to know something about that ending did not belong to a Green transfer.\n\nLeon stared at his intact hand as though his own magic had become a stranger.\n\nVoices crashed back into the hall all at once.\n\nStudent: \"What just happened?\"\n\nStudent: \"Did Varek cancel his own spell?\"\n\nStudent: \"No, the transfer touched it-\"\n\nStudent: \"A first-day Green?\"\n\nStudent: \"That control makes no sense-\"\n\nLeon staggered back a step, wet hair plastered to his forehead.\n\nDren strode straight through the dropping barrier.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Match over.\"\n\nThe panel overhead flashed bright white.\n\n**ASSESSMENT ENDED**\n\nProtests rolled down from the stands.\n\nStudent: \"What? Why?\"\n\nStudent: \"Sir, that's it?\"\n\nStudent: \"He was about to lose!\"\n\nStudent: \"No, Leon would've won-\"\n\nStudent: \"Would he?\"\n\nDren ignored all of them. He stopped in front of Leon first.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"You lost emotional control, exceeded safe output limits, and ignored a direct order.\"\n\nLeon opened his mouth, then shut it.\n\nDren turned to me.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"And you demonstrated unusual tactical timing.\"\n\nHaru: \"Thank you, sir.\"\n\nHis gaze stayed on me a second longer than praise required, flicking briefly toward my torn sleeve before he faced the tiers.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Take the lesson clearly. Rank matters. It is not absolute. Any student who treats natural advantage as a substitute for discipline will eventually be embarrassed.\"\n\nSeveral Greens looked a fraction less small. Several Blues looked sour. Above us, the Yellow Circle student on the upper platform had already turned away toward the exit stairs.\n\nFor the class, the match ended there. For me, a real problem had just begun. Attention had weight, and I could already feel it settling across my shoulders.\n\nAs the class spilled onto the floor, Leon drove his shoulder against mine hard enough to make it deliberate.\n\nLeon: \"You planned that.\"\n\nHaru: \"No. You rushed.\"\n\nHe stopped and half-turned, anger and disbelief colliding across his face for one heartbeat before he walked out.\n\nAira reached me seconds later.\n\nAira: \"That wasn't normal.\"\n\nHaru: \"For a Green Circle?\"\n\nAira: \"For anyone on their first day.\"\n\nThere was no accusation in her voice, only astonishment mixed with the beginnings of real trust. I did not know what to do with either.\n\nHaru: \"It was timing.\"\n\nShe held my gaze, testing the edge of the answer I refused to give.\n\nAira: \"Maybe. I still think there's more to you than timing, Haru.\"\n\nBefore I could answer, a new presence stepped onto the upper observation platform.\n\nThe hall changed without anyone announcing why. Conversations cut off mid-word. Students straightened. Even Leon halted near the doorway.\n\nAt the top of the stairs stood a girl in the formal elite mantle reserved for special-ranking officers. Her posture was razor-clean, her expression composed, and the mana around her carried a bright, heavy refinement that pressed lightly against the skin even from across the floor.\n\nA high-grade **Red Circle** crest glowed at her collarbone.\n\nShe looked down at Dren, then Leon, then settled her gaze squarely on me. Not contempt. Calculation.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Student Council President.\"\n\nSo this was her. Even transfer briefs and hallway whispers carried her reputation. Aurelis's student council did not hang festival banners; it governed disciplinary referrals, emergency witnesses, duel permits, and enough institutional leverage to make noble heirs mind their manners in public.\n\nShe descended the steps at an unhurried pace.\n\nRhea: \"Forgive the interruption. I heard there was unusual activity in the assessment hall.\"\n\nHer voice was calm, polished, and completely opaque.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"The matter is handled.\"\n\nRhea: \"Handled, then. Still, it appears this year's transfer student has already become interesting.\"\n\nNearby students traded quick looks. I kept my face blank.\n\nShe studied me one heartbeat longer, then offered a thin smile, the look of someone who had spotted a loose thread and intended to keep pulling until the seam came apart.\n\nRhea: \"I look forward to seeing more.\"\n\nShe turned and left at the same measured pace, leaving half the room pretending not to watch her climb the stairs.\n\nAira: \"That is bad.\"\n\nHaru: \"Who was that?\"\n\nAira stared at me as if I had asked the name of a storm everyone else knew to board windows for.\n\nAira: \"Rhea Valmont. Student Council President. Red Circle. Once she takes an interest in you, paperwork starts growing teeth.\"\n\nI looked back toward the empty steps where Rhea had disappeared. My first day at Aurelis was not even finished, and hiding had already turned into a spectator sport.\n\n## Interlude: The Wrong Kindness\n\nThe west corridor outside the training complex emptied in stages.\n\nFirst the loudest students poured past, carrying the assessment away in fractured pieces.\n\n*A Green scored on Leon. Leon lost control. Dren looked furious. Elira Vale stood up. No, she didn't. Yes, she did. The transfer did something weird at the end.*\n\nBy the time I reached the side exit, the afternoon had already splintered into four competing versions. Contradiction was useful. It made hiding easier.\n\nI stopped beneath a narrow stone arch where late sunlight fell across the floor in pale gold bars. My left sleeve hung torn above the wrist from Leon's collapsing spell, exposing the false Green Circle more than I liked. I tried to fold the ripped cuff back down. Badly.\n\nMy fingers were still numb from steering the backlash clear of Leon's arm. That annoyed me more than the sting.\n\nHaru: \"You can come out.\"\n\nThe air behind the pillar shifted, and the Yellow Circle girl from the upper platform stepped into the sunlight. The one the passing students had called Elira Vale.\n\nUp close, her poise felt quieter than her crest, and she did not look embarrassed at being caught waiting. Embarrassment would have made this simpler.\n\nElira: \"I was deciding whether approaching you here would feel like an ambush.\"\n\nHaru: \"And?\"\n\nElira: \"I decided it probably would.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\nElira: \"Then I did it anyway.\"\n\nThat surprised a short breath out of me, closer to a laugh than I liked.\n\nIn one hand she held a folded strip of white healer cloth, clean and fine enough that most students would have saved it for a real wound rather than a torn cuff. She followed my gaze.\n\nElira: \"Your arm.\"\n\nHaru: \"It's fine.\"\n\nElira: \"Your sleeve disagrees.\"\n\nHaru: \"Fabric is dramatic.\"\n\nHer mouth softened by a fraction.\n\nElira: \"May I?\"\n\nThe question was the trap. If she had stepped straight in, I could have backed away. If she had ordered me to hold still, I could have disliked her cleanly. Instead she asked, making refusal feel heavier than it deserved to be.\n\nHaru: \"It doesn't need a bandage.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then why offer?\"\n\nThe warmth faded slightly from her expression.\n\nElira: \"Because something can be unnecessary and still kind.\"\n\nI should have said thank you and moved on. Instead I read the shape underneath her words wrong. A Yellow Circle prodigy offering aid to a Green Circle transfer after a spectacle: generous, photogenic, the exact gesture people admired if word traveled. The sort of kindness that could turn me into a footnote in someone else's reputation.\n\nHaru: \"You don't have to prove anything.\"\n\nHer hands stilled.\n\nElira: \"Prove?\"\n\nI knew instantly that I had picked the wrong word. Knowing did not take it back.\n\nHaru: \"People are watching.\"\n\nElira glanced down the empty corridor, then back at me.\n\nElira: \"No one is here.\"\n\nHaru: \"People don't need to be in the room to use what happened.\"\n\nHer expression quieted further.\n\nElira: \"Is that what you think I'm doing?\"\n\nHaru: \"I don't know what you're doing.\"\n\nHonest in the worst possible direction.\n\nElira folded the white cloth once between her fingers.\n\nElira: \"I was trying to hand you something clean before you walked back into a school full of people deciding what your torn sleeve means.\"\n\nI had no answer for that.\n\nShe extended the cloth anyway, neither stepping closer nor pulling back.\n\nElira: \"You can take it without accepting anything else.\"\n\nThat sentence had no business working on me. I reached out and took the cloth.\n\nOur fingers did not brush. She made sure of it. Somehow the care in that distance unsettled me more than the gift itself.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nElira nodded. For half a second I thought that might mend the scrape.\n\nElira: \"You are very careful about not owing anyone.\"\n\nI looked up too fast. Her golden eyes were steady, without a trace of triumph. She had not said it to win an exchange.\n\nHaru: \"Owing people gets complicated.\"\n\nElira: \"So does refusing every kindness before it has a chance to become one.\"\n\nNo accusation sat in her voice, which was why the line stayed lodged under my ribs. She turned toward the hall.\n\nI had three polite, tactical things I could have said. I used none of them. I stood there with the white cloth clutched in my numb fingers and watched her walk back toward the sunlit corridor.\n\nAt the corner, she paused without turning around.\n\nElira: \"I did not stand up because you scored on Leon.\"\n\nMy grip tightened on the fabric.\n\nElira: \"I stood up because you looked hurt and pretended not to be.\"\n\nThen she was gone.\n\nSilence returned to the arch. After she rounded the corner, I wrapped the cloth around my torn sleeve and numb wrist anyway. It was unnecessary. It helped. That made it worse.\n\n---\n\nThe rumors beat the morning bell on my second day.\n\nThat was the trouble with elite academies: nothing stayed small overnight. By the time I crossed the main courtyard of **Aurelis Arcane Academy**, students were already retelling yesterday's assessment as if it had been a championship duel instead of a classroom block.\n\nSome versions were merely inflated. Others had crossed straight into fiction.\n\nStudent: \"He shattered Leon's spell with Green Circle output.\"\n\nStudent: \"No, Leon lost control on his own.\"\n\nStudent: \"I heard Dren stopped it because the transfer was hiding a relic.\"\n\nStudent: \"Someone said his mana moved strangely.\"\n\nStudent: \"Strangely how?\"\n\nStudent: \"No idea. That's what makes it weird.\"\n\nI walked past them with my bag slung over one shoulder, pace unhurried. Starving attention was the surest way to outlast it, though attention had an irritating habit of feeding itself.\n\nNear the central stairs, two Blue Circle boys fell quiet as I approached. One looked straight at the faint green mark beneath my left cuff, then up at my face.\n\nStudent 1: \"That's him.\"\n\nStudent 2: \"He still looks ordinary.\"\n\nStudent 1: \"That's the unsettling part.\"\n\nI kept walking.\n\nSilver-leaf trees along the walkways shimmered in early light while practice glyphs rotated high above the towers. Yesterday I had been invisible. Today I was a question, and questions drew knives faster than insults did.\n\nInside the entrance hall, Aira stood near a floating directory holding two paper cups from the academy cafe. She spotted me before I could think about picking another corridor and lifted one cup.\n\nAira: \"I guessed you'd come through here.\"\n\nHaru: \"You planned for coincidence?\"\n\nAira smiled.\n\nAira: \"I prefer 'efficient friendliness.'\"\n\nShe held the cup out.\n\nAira: \"Tea. I didn't know what you drink, so I picked the lowest-risk option.\"\n\nI looked from the cup to her face.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira: \"You're welcome.\"\n\nShe let a beat pass before falling into step beside me.\n\nAira: \"Also, before the day gets worse, half the academy has decided you're either secretly brilliant or deeply suspicious.\"\n\nHaru: \"Only half?\"\n\nThat made her laugh.\n\nAira: \"You're getting better at this.\"\n\nHaru: \"At collecting trouble?\"\n\nAira: \"At sounding like you're joking.\"\n\nWalking beside her toward the east wing drew fresh glances. Aira ignored them easily. I was less sure I ever would. She took a sip from her cup and dropped her voice.\n\nAira: \"Leon is furious, by the way.\"\n\nHaru: \"I assumed.\"\n\nAira: \"He's pretending not to care, which for Leon is somehow louder than normal anger.\"\n\nHaru: \"Inconvenient.\"\n\nAira: \"For you, yes. For me, it's Tuesday.\"\n\nWe turned into the east corridor just as a fresh wave of whispers rolled through the hall. This time the pitch changed: softer, sharper, edged with reverence.\n\nI felt the mana before I saw her. Warm, radiant, steady as sunlight pressing through tall glass.\n\nStudents ahead shifted sideways almost by reflex, clearing the center of the stone walkway. Blue Circles straightened. Red Circles turned with measured composure. Even two instructors paused.\n\nThen she stepped into view.\n\nIn the quiet west corridor yesterday, she had simply been a tall girl with pale-gold hair and a strip of white healer cloth. Here, with fifty students parting around her, I saw what Aurelis had made of her. Her uniform looked almost ceremonial beneath the elite mantle draped over her shoulders, and at her collarbone blazed an unmistakable **Yellow Circle**. Unhidden. Unsoftened for anyone's comfort.\n\nThe corridor hushed as she walked.\n\nStudent 3: \"It's her.\"\n\nStudent 4: \"Lady Elira.\"\n\nStudent 5: \"Did she really stand up during Section Three's assessment yesterday?\"\n\nAira followed my eyes and leaned closer.\n\nAira: \"That's **Elira Vale**.\"\n\nI kept yesterday's corridor, and the clean folded cloth still tucked inside my coat pocket, to myself.\n\nHaru: \"I gathered she was important.\"\n\nAira gave me a flat look.\n\nAira: \"That might be the most understated sentence anyone has ever spoken about Elira Vale.\"\n\nHaru: \"Should I know more?\"\n\nAira: \"Yellow Circle. One of the youngest high-grade light-affinity magicians in the country. First in magical theory, first in practical control, and somehow still decent enough to make people feel guilty for being awful in front of her.\"\n\nHaru: \"So everyone admires her.\"\n\nAira: \"Admire is a polite word for it.\"\n\nElira moved down the hallway surrounded not by attendants, only by empty air. Nobody crowded her. Unlike the Reds who wore status like a polished edge, she looked faintly weary of the space people carved around her.\n\nThen her eyes shifted across the crowd and landed straight on me.\n\nJust for a second. Neither contempt nor idle curiosity, only calm recognition, the quiet gaze of someone remembering an unfinished conversation. Her eyes flicked once toward my mended left sleeve, narrowed by a hair, and then she walked past.\n\nThe hallway seemed to exhale all at once.\n\nAira turned to me with solemn gravity.\n\nAira: \"If you claim you didn't notice that, I'll be offended on behalf of human eyesight.\"\n\nHaru: \"She looked at me.\"\n\nAira: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe lifted her tea cup toward me like a witness pointing at evidence.\n\nHaru: \"People do that here.\"\n\nAira stared at me.\n\nAira: \"You still don't understand how this academy works.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm learning.\"\n\nAira: \"Lesson one: Yellow Circle students do not casually single out Green Circle transfers in a crowded hall.\"\n\nHaru: \"Maybe she was looking past me.\"\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"Fine. She looked at me.\"\n\nAira folded one arm across her ribs, balancing her cup.\n\nAira: \"Good. Progress.\"\n\nHomeroom felt tighter when we entered a minute later. Yesterday's curiosity had hardened overnight into scrutiny.\n\nLeon was already seated, talking to two Blue Circle classmates in a voice low enough to imply strategy and loud enough to invite listeners. When I walked in beside Aira, the corner of his mouth pulled tight.\n\nLeon: \"Impressive. One lucky assessment and suddenly you have an escort.\"\n\nAira: \"Good morning to you too, Leon.\"\n\nLeon: \"I'm being civil.\"\n\nAira: \"Then I'd hate to hear you rude.\"\n\nI took my seat without answering, which irritated him twice as much.\n\nInstructor Dren arrived moments later and launched straight into multi-layered reinforcement theory without mentioning yesterday once. That did not stop half the class from stealing looks toward the back row.\n\nMidway through the lecture, a faint prickle brushed the edge of my awareness. Someone watching, not from inside the classroom, from higher up. When I glanced toward the tall windows, nothing stood outside except bright morning cloud. Still, the sensation lingered until the bell.\n\n---\n\nLunch at Aurelis was less a meal than a sorting ritual disguised with silverware.\n\nThe dining hall rose in wide tiers beneath panoramic windows overlooking the lower gardens. Students clustered by circle, lineage, and confidence. Even which alcove you chose told the room where you thought you belonged.\n\nAira and I had just carried our trays to a quiet side table when the hall hushed again. People shifted their chairs without needing to be asked.\n\nI did not need to look up to know who had crossed the threshold.\n\nElira Vale walked between the tables with unhurried poise, sunlight catching the amber-gold edges of her hair near the glass. Up close, Yellow Circle mana carried a refinement that made lesser currents waver around it like candle flames near an open window.\n\nShe stopped beside our table. Half the dining hall forgot how to chew.\n\nHer hand paused near the empty chair back for half a breath while her gaze swept the watching tables, measuring every pair of eyes before settling back on us.\n\nElira: \"May I sit here?\"\n\nAira nearly dropped her fork.\n\nAira: \"You're asking us?\"\n\nElira's lips curved slightly.\n\nElira: \"That seemed polite.\"\n\nAira looked at me, back at Elira, and visibly decided to postpone her breakdown until after class.\n\nAira: \"Yes. Of course. Please.\"\n\nElira took the seat across from us. Around us, fifty conversations resumed at half volume while everyone pretended not to listen.\n\nAira recovered first.\n\nAira: \"I'm Aira Solen.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nAira blinked.\n\nElira turned her calm golden gaze onto me.\n\nElira: \"And you're Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe gave no sign in front of Aira that we had already spoken in the west corridor, yet her steady eyes held the faintest echo of yesterday's unspoken challenge.\n\nElira: \"You asked an interesting question in class yesterday.\"\n\nAira stared at me.\n\nAira: \"Your homeroom question reached the Yellow Circle lunch network?\"\n\nHaru: \"Apparently.\"\n\nElira's expression softened slightly.\n\nElira: \"Questions are rarely the problem. Fear of them usually is.\"\n\nHardly standard academy doctrine.\n\nHaru: \"Do you believe Black Circle theory has merit?\"\n\nTwo students at the next table nearly choked trying to eavesdrop discreetly.\n\nElira glanced down at her tea, then back up at me.\n\nElira: \"I believe magical history is incomplete. And I believe most people are comfortable dismissing anything that threatens an order they benefit from.\"\n\nAira's fork stopped halfway to her tray.\n\nAira: \"That is not the answer I expected.\"\n\nElira: \"Most people expect Yellow Circles to defend the system.\"\n\nAira: \"Do you?\"\n\nElira smiled, though a shadow sat beneath it.\n\nElira: \"The system defends itself well enough.\"\n\nFor a brief moment none of us spoke, and the clatter of the dining hall receded until our table felt like its own small island.\n\nElira folded her hands lightly beside her tray.\n\nElira: \"I also wanted to thank you.\"\n\nHaru: \"For what?\"\n\nElira: \"Yesterday. You stopped Leon's spell from collapsing badly enough to injure him.\"\n\nAira whipped toward me so fast her chair scraped the stone.\n\nAira: \"You did what?\"\n\nAcross the hall, pretense died completely. Several tables leaned closer.\n\nHaru: \"I reacted.\"\n\nElira: \"With unusual precision.\"\n\nHaru: \"So I've been told.\"\n\nThis time her smile was genuine, neither grand nor ceremonial, simply warm. It changed her entire face, and for one inconvenient second I forgot how to answer.\n\nAira caught it immediately, glancing between us with the look of someone realizing her peaceful semester had just sprung a leak.\n\nElira set her cup down.\n\nElira: \"There is an advanced practical demonstration this afternoon in the upper training gardens. My group was asked to assist.\"\n\nAira: \"Your group?\"\n\nElira: \"High-tier affinity control.\"\n\nAira made a quiet sound that clearly meant *that is several tiers above our schedule*.\n\nElira looked straight at me.\n\nElira: \"You should come watch, Haru.\"\n\nNo challenge in her tone. Only plain sincerity, which made declining harder.\n\nHaru: \"Why?\"\n\nElira: \"Because I think you notice things other people miss.\"\n\nAira rested her chin on one palm, fully embracing her new job as commentator on my bad decisions.\n\nAira: \"He does that.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira.\"\n\nAira: \"I'm helping.\"\n\nAmusement brightened Elira's eyes.\n\nElira: \"Good. Then perhaps both of you should come.\"\n\nAira: \"Now I definitely have to.\"\n\n---\n\nThe upper training gardens rose across broad stone terraces overlooking the western cliffs. Ornamental trees bordered the walkways, and practice fields lay framed by flowerbeds, crystal pylons, and rune-carved circles built to anchor high-grade output.\n\nUpper-year Reds and Blues already lined the observation rail when Aira and I arrived, with several instructors stationed along the perimeter in case an exercise slipped its leash.\n\nAt the terrace's center, four elite students took their marks: two high-grade Reds, one specialized Blue, and Elira.\n\nEven standing motionless, she anchored the field. Afternoon sun turned the gold of her crest nearly white at its edges. When she raised one hand, the air across the terrace answered: a quiet rise in pressure followed by a clean harmonic hum from the stabilizing stones.\n\nInstructor Maren addressed the crowd from the margin.\n\nInstructor Maren: \"Today's exercise examines synchronized affinity layering under unequal mana loads. Observe balance, hand transitions, and restraint thresholds.\"\n\nThe Reds opened first, braiding fire and wind into intersecting rings while the Blue anchored water barriers to catch stray overflow. Polished, disciplined casting.\n\nThen Elira stepped into the center node.\n\nLight bloomed, never harsh, simply luminous. Golden-white threads unspooled from her fingertips like silk, weaving between the other three spells without pushing them aside. She did not overpower the formation; she refined it, bracing thin seams, smoothing tremor, raising everyone else's work simply by touching the frame.\n\nThe terrace went dead quiet.\n\nAira whispered without looking away.\n\nAira: \"Every time I watch her cast, it feels unfair.\"\n\nHaru: \"She has remarkable control.\"\n\nAira cut a sideways glance at me.\n\nAira: \"Only you could call something that gorgeous 'controlled.'\"\n\nI kept my eyes on the field.\n\nHaru: \"It's beautiful.\"\n\nAira blinked.\n\nAira: \"Oh. Good. You do have a pulse.\"\n\nThe instructors raised the load for the second phase. One of the Red Circle students lost half a beat of alignment, tilting the ring until the outer runes vibrated with a sharp metallic whine.\n\nBefore Maren even lifted a hand, Elira rebuilt the entire geometry.\n\nHer left hand opened. Her right turned. Golden threads multiplied, crossed, and lifted the faltering structure into clean balance so smoothly that most of the spectators would walk away calling the display flawless.\n\nI remembered the half-breath where it had nearly snapped, and the exact stillness with which she refused to let someone else's slip turn into harm.\n\nQuiet applause rose across the terrace. Elira exhaled, lowered her arms, and scanned the crowd until her eyes found me.\n\nShe smiled openly, and not for the gallery.\n\nAira turned her head slowly toward me.\n\nAira: \"Oh no.\"\n\nHaru: \"What?\"\n\nAira: \"Nothing. Just the start of my headaches.\"\n\nHaru: \"You say that a lot.\"\n\nAira: \"Because you keep making it true.\"\n\nWhen the dismissal bell sounded, students crowded forward to praise the casters. I would have slipped out quietly, except a classmate caught Aira by the elbow and pulled her into a loud cluster near the stairs.\n\nThat left me alone by the cliff railing for roughly seven seconds before Elira walked over.\n\nWith the formal demonstration behind her, she seemed both more human and harder to guard against.\n\nElira: \"You came.\"\n\nHaru: \"You invited me.\"\n\nElira: \"Plenty of people accept invitations for the wrong reasons.\"\n\nHaru: \"And what reasons did you expect from me?\"\n\nShe studied me with the late afternoon sun resting lightly across her shoulders.\n\nElira: \"Curiosity, maybe.\"\n\nHaru: \"Harmless, then.\"\n\nElira: \"It can be. It can also change everything.\"\n\nShe turned and laid one hand along the stone rail overlooking the western cliffs. Below us, mist drifted through the lower ravines, catching faint lantern reflections from Aurelis far below.\n\nBehind us, voices praised the exercise while stray golden motes faded from the practice rings. Around Elira, noise always seemed to pull back a step.\n\nElira: \"You weren't impressed the usual way.\"\n\nHaru: \"There's a usual way?\"\n\nHer lips curved.\n\nElira: \"Most people watching a Yellow Circle either admire it loudly or resent it quietly.\"\n\nHaru: \"And I did neither?\"\n\nElira: \"You observed.\"\n\nToo accurate.\n\nHaru: \"You noticed that from the center of a four-way formation?\"\n\nShe turned back toward me.\n\nElira: \"I notice when someone watches the structure instead of the spectacle.\"\n\nNo accusation sat in her voice, which made me more cautious, not less.\n\nHaru: \"Your control was excellent.\"\n\nElira: \"That sounds like a faculty report.\"\n\nHaru: \"Would you prefer a less academic verdict?\"\n\nHer smile arrived at once, warm and brief enough to vanish before I could brace for it.\n\nElira: \"I'd prefer an honest one.\"\n\nI looked back toward the empty practice circles where the last wisps of gold were thinning into evening air.\n\nHaru: \"It was beautiful.\"\n\nI meant the spell. The quiet beat that followed suggested the sentence left too much room for other readings.\n\nElira's expression softened by a degree too fine to name.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nBehind me, I could feel Aira staring from across the terrace. I did not turn.\n\nElira folded her hands lightly in front of her.\n\nElira: \"You really did stop Leon's backlash yesterday.\"\n\nThere it was, laid out cleanly without pretense.\n\nHaru: \"I kept a mistake from getting worse.\"\n\nElira: \"That isn't a denial.\"\n\nHaru: \"It isn't a confession either.\"\n\nFor a second I thought she would press. Instead she looked back out over the cliffside gardens.\n\nElira: \"People assume power is loud.\"\n\nHaru: \"Usually it wants an audience.\"\n\nElira: \"And yours doesn't?\"\n\nEvery muscle in my shoulders locked inwardly, though my face stayed still.\n\nHaru: \"My what?\"\n\nShe did not look at me when she answered.\n\nElira: \"Your presence feels inconsistent.\"\n\nFar more dangerous than Leon's bruised pride or homeroom gossip. Elira was not guessing. She was perceiving.\n\nHaru: \"I'm a Green Circle transfer. There isn't much room for inconsistency in that.\"\n\nShe turned back to face me.\n\nElira: \"That line would work better if you believed it.\"\n\nFor the first time since stepping onto campus, I had no ready deflection. She saw that too, and her voice gentled.\n\nElira: \"You don't have to tell me anything.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then why say it?\"\n\nElira: \"Because sometimes being seen is not the same as being exposed.\"\n\nThat landed harder than any spell Leon had thrown. Most people who noticed an anomaly pushed toward it, wanting a label, leverage, or a secret to spend. Elira spoke as if recognition itself could be an act of care.\n\nHaru: \"You're unusually kind for someone this school treats like royalty.\"\n\nShe let out a soft, amused breath.\n\nElira: \"That may be the least flattering compliment I've ever received.\"\n\nHaru: \"I meant it well.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe glanced past my shoulder toward the terrace, where Aira was very obviously pretending not to stare at us while staring straight at us.\n\nElira: \"Your friend looks worried.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira expects complications.\"\n\nElira: \"Is she usually right?\"\n\nHaru: \"More often than I'd like.\"\n\nElira laughed quietly, unguarded and clear, and I understood in a single beat why people gravitated toward her. Beneath the gold crest and the legend, she actually listened.\n\nElira: \"May I ask you something?\"\n\nHaru: \"You already have.\"\n\nElira: \"One more thing, then.\"\n\nHaru: \"Go ahead.\"\n\nHer expression grew thoughtful.\n\nElira: \"Why did you ask about Black Circle magicians in class?\"\n\nCuriosity would have been easy. Academic theory would have been safe. For reasons I had not fully examined, I gave her neither.\n\nHaru: \"Because I wanted to see how people react to the idea of something outside their chart.\"\n\nShe held my gaze.\n\nElira: \"And what did you learn?\"\n\nHaru: \"Most people laugh first.\"\n\nElira: \"And after that?\"\n\nHaru: \"If it threatens the order they depend on, they get afraid.\"\n\nElira stayed quiet for several seconds, then nodded once, confirming something to herself rather than politely agreeing.\n\nElira: \"That sounds lonely.\"\n\nI should not have felt the sentence as deeply as I did.\n\nHaru: \"Not always.\"\n\nElira: \"No?\"\n\nHaru: \"Sometimes there are people who make it less so.\"\n\nFor the first time, surprise caught her completely off guard: not the words themselves, just the fact that I had spoken them. Faint color touched her cheeks before she looked away down the garden path.\n\nElira: \"You're quieter than most people here.\"\n\nHaru: \"It usually helps.\"\n\nElira: \"Maybe. Silence also makes people underestimate how much you're actually saying.\"\n\nBehind us, the terrace was emptying as instructors dismissed the remaining spectators and practice circles winked out one by one. Cool evening air drifted up from the cliffs.\n\nElira stepped back from the rail.\n\nElira: \"There's a moonlit archive hall in the north library wing. Almost nobody goes there after classes.\"\n\nHaru: \"Should I worry about where this invitation leads?\"\n\nHer eyes brightened.\n\nElira: \"Only if you dislike old books, quiet rooms, and honest conversation.\"\n\nHaru: \"Suspiciously specific.\"\n\nElira: \"I thought you preferred specific.\"\n\nShe was right, which was the entire problem.\n\nHaru: \"You're inviting me to the library.\"\n\nFor a heartbeat, her easy certainty hesitated.\n\nElira: \"I don't usually invite people there. Yes. I am.\"\n\nHaru: \"People will misunderstand.\"\n\nElira: \"People misunderstand most things.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not reassuring.\"\n\nElira: \"It wasn't meant to be.\"\n\nI almost smiled before brisk footsteps interrupted from the side walkway.\n\nAn irritated Blue mana signature arrived half a second before its owner.\n\nLeon: \"Naturally.\"\n\nAira appeared a step behind him, looking deeply unimpressed.\n\nAira: \"I told him this was a terrible idea.\"\n\nLeon stopped three paces off, glancing from Elira to me with stiff disbelief.\n\nLeon: \"Lady Elira, I didn't realize you were entertaining lower-year rumors now.\"\n\nThe warmth drained from the terrace in an instant. Elira's posture cooled by a single unmistakable degree.\n\nElira: \"And I didn't realize my conversations required your approval, Leon.\"\n\nAira's mouth twitched, clearly enjoying the moment far more than she meant to show.\n\nLeon stiffened.\n\nLeon: \"That isn't what I meant.\"\n\nElira: \"Then choose your words more carefully.\"\n\nHis eyes flicked back to me.\n\nLeon: \"With respect, reputations matter here. Especially when someone starts enjoying attention he hasn't earned.\"\n\nAira stepped straight into his line of sight.\n\nAira: \"He earned enough to embarrass you in front of an entire assessment hall.\"\n\nLeon: \"Aira.\"\n\nAira: \"Go ahead. If you're going to be petty, at least be efficient about it.\"\n\nLeon's jaw clenched as yesterday's humiliation flared back to the surface, sharpened by Elira standing right beside us.\n\nElira did not look angry. She looked disappointed, which rattled him worse.\n\nElira: \"If your concern is status, keep it. If your concern is truth, improve your eyesight.\"\n\nAira let out a quiet sound somewhere between shock and applause. Leon went pale, then flushed.\n\nLeon: \"You actually think there's something special about him?\"\n\nElira glanced at me for a fraction of a second before answering.\n\nElira: \"I think dismissing people because their circle is green reflects poorly on everyone who does it.\"\n\nNot the same as saying no. Leon caught the gap at once. His eyes narrowed.\n\nLeon: \"Then I'll prove yesterday was luck.\"\n\nHaru: \"You don't need to prove anything to me.\"\n\nLeon gave a dry, humorless laugh.\n\nLeon: \"Generous from someone hiding behind borrowed mystery.\"\n\nAira: \"Borrowed mystery is a terrible phrase.\"\n\nLeon ignored her.\n\nLeon: \"There's an interclass combat ladder event in three days. Restricted brackets, official observation, no instructor stepping in unless someone breaks. If you're really that composed, enter.\"\n\nHaru: \"I transferred in yesterday.\"\n\nLeon: \"Then decline. Publicly, if you like. Everyone can decide what that means.\"\n\nAira: \"That's manipulation.\"\n\nLeon: \"That's an invitation.\"\n\nElira's voice stayed level.\n\nElira: \"It's coercive and transparent.\"\n\nLeon pivoted toward her quickly, desperate to salvage dignity.\n\nLeon: \"Then he can refuse without shame, can't he?\"\n\nThe challenge hung in the cooling evening air.\n\nI had no interest in attention, no interest in a combat ladder, and certainly no interest in handing Leon a stage. Yet I could already hear the rumor if I walked away: *the Green transfer who surprised a Blue once and hid behind Elira Vale.*\n\nMore importantly, I felt something else.\n\nEyes watching from farther up the garden path. Neither Leon's friends nor stray students. Deliberate, clinical attention, measuring the four of us from behind the trees. Before I could isolate the signature, it vanished cleanly.\n\nElira caught the micro-shift in my gaze.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nI brought my focus back to the terrace. Leon waited. Aira frowned. Elira watched closely.\n\nHaru: \"Fine.\"\n\nAira whipped around.\n\nAira: \"Fine?\"\n\nVindictive relief lit Leon's face.\n\nLeon: \"You accept?\"\n\nHaru: \"I'll enter.\"\n\nAira: \"Wrong answer.\"\n\nElira did not look surprised, only thoughtful.\n\nLeon straightened, visibly relieved that the world had clicked back into a contest he understood.\n\nLeon: \"Good. Then we can all stop pretending.\"\n\nHe walked off before anyone could dull the exit.\n\nAira stared after him, then turned on me as if deciding whether to lecture me or sigh herself into early retirement.\n\nAira: \"Apparently you enjoy difficult lives.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not especially.\"\n\nAira: \"Then why agree?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because he won't stop pushing until he sees the edge once.\"\n\nAira: \"He'll keep pushing anyway.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then I'll deal with that when it happens.\"\n\nAira shut her eyes for a second.\n\nAira: \"I hate when you stay calm like that.\"\n\nElira stepped a half-pace closer, dropping her voice.\n\nElira: \"You sensed something just now.\"\n\nAira glanced sharply between us.\n\nAira: \"What did he sense?\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm not sure.\"\n\nTrue, and not nearly enough.\n\nElira held my gaze steadily.\n\nElira: \"Then be careful.\"\n\nHaru: \"I usually am.\"\n\nElira: \"No,\" she murmured. \"Usually you're controlled. Those aren't always the same thing.\"\n\nAgain, she left a sentence sitting where I could not easily set it down.\n\nAira looked from Elira to me and threw up her hands in surrender.\n\nAira: \"My peaceful academic year is officially dead.\"\n\nElira smiled at her.\n\nElira: \"I'll try not to make it worse.\"\n\nAira: \"That isn't comforting when spoken by a Yellow Circle standing three feet from my profoundly suspicious friend.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm suspicious?\"\n\nAira: \"Profoundly.\"\n\nElira's soft laugh eased the knot in the air for a brief moment.\n\nSunset washed the terrace in amber, carrying the faint smell of spell-scorched stone and garden blossoms up from the cliffs. Then the evening bells rolled across Aurelis, calling students down toward the dining halls and study blocks.\n\nElira turned toward the upper walkway.\n\nElira: \"The library invitation still stands.\"\n\nHaru: \"You're persistent.\"\n\nElira: \"Only when something matters.\"\n\nHer eyes met mine for one quiet second, then she inclined her head to Aira and disappeared into the lantern-lit trees.\n\nAira waited until Elira was out of earshot before folding her arms.\n\nAira: \"Well.\"\n\nHaru: \"Well what?\"\n\nAira: \"A Yellow Circle just invited you to a private library hall, cut Leon down in public, and looked at you like a puzzle she wants to solve gently.\"\n\nHaru: \"Specific interpretation.\"\n\nAira: \"Accurate one. I genuinely don't know whether to worry for you or for the whole academy.\"\n\nHaru: \"That sounds dramatic.\"\n\nAira: \"You asked about Black Circle magic on day one, survived Leon's meltdown, entered the combat ladder, and caught Elira Vale's attention. Dramatic already arrived.\"\n\nShe started down the terrace steps.\n\nAira: \"Come on. If you're going to ruin your quiet life, eat dinner first.\"\n\nI followed her down into the lantern-lit main halls.\n\n## Coda: The Gate Remembered\n\nAfter dinner, Aira peeled off toward the east dormitory stair, leaving me to take the quieter flagstone path past Aurelis's old inner foundation gate.\n\nTwilight had settled deep violet over the towers. My thoughts kept circling back to the upper garden path, to that brief clinical scrutiny disappearing into the trees before I could trace it, and to Elira's voice saying being seen was not the same as being exposed.\n\nThen, as I passed beneath the ancient stone archway, the gate behind me gave one quiet chime.\n\nNo student walking across the lower yard reacted. The note was too soft for ceremony and too exact for wind.\n\nI stopped and turned.\n\nA hairline of light ran through the weathered crest carved high above the arch. Green first. Then Blue. Then Red. Then Yellow. Four obedient colors, the approved ladder of the world, bright enough to look harmless in the dusk.\n\nBeneath them, where solid mortar should have stayed dark, a fifth ring pressed once from inside the stone, ink-black, razor-precise, older than the towers around it, and vanished before the false Green crest on my wrist could even flicker.\n\nThe gate had not welcomed me when I crossed it yesterday morning.\n\nIt had counted me.\n\nA pair of first-years hurried past under the arch, arguing loudly about bracket odds for the coming combat ladder. One glanced down at the pale green glow peeking under my left cuff, dismissed me without slowing, and kept walking. Ordinary cruelty. Useful camouflage.\n\nInside my coat pocket, my fingers brushed the clean folded edge of Elira's healer cloth, still kept from yesterday's corridor.\n\nUp on the garden terrace, someone had watched me with real intent. Inside Aurelis's old stone, ancient magic had recognized the lie I wore. And somewhere across the courtyard, a girl with gold eyes had looked straight past it.\n\nI stood beneath the darkening arch and realized I was no longer sure which discovery frightened me more.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-01-cover.webp","scenes":[{"id":"scene-ep1-sunrise","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-01-aurelis-city-sunrise-imagegen.png","alt":"Sunrise over Aurelis City with crystal spires, glass bridges, and floating transport sigils","title":"Sunrise Over Aurelis City","caption":"Pale gold light stretched across the clouds, between the white towers and glass bridges of the capital.","after":"The morning sky over **Aurelis City** caught the sun in layered gold","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-arrival","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-aurelis-arrival.webp","alt":"Haru arriving before the grand crystal towers and wards of Aurelis Arcane Academy","title":"Aurelis Academy Gates","caption":"The academy rose before me in white stone and fractured sunlight beneath six rune-marked towers.","after":"Tall crystal windows reflected fractured sunlight beneath six rune-marked towers.","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-whispers","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-gate-whispers.webp","alt":"Haru walking through the academy courtyard past whispering students judging his Green Circle crest","title":"The Courtyard Whispers","caption":"Nearby students murmured as I passed, Blue and Red crests flashing as they noticed my Green emblem with amused disbelief.","after":"They knew the three facts before I reached the main steps: transfer student, mid-year entry, Green Circle.","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-selene-entrance","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-selene-entrance.webp","alt":"Instructor Selene Varis with her glowing rune-glass tablet meeting Haru Aerlen under the starry domed hall","title":"Instructor Selene Varis","caption":"Instructor Selene checks transfer credentials beneath the starry dome of the entrance hall, warning that Aurelis rewards only excellence.","after":"Instructor Selene: \"I'm Instructor Selene Varis. Student Affairs. You're the transfer from Lumeris District?\"","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-lecture","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-02-circle-ranks-classroom.webp","alt":"Classroom lecture on the circle ranking hierarchy and four recognized circles","title":"The Circle Hierarchy Lecture","caption":"Instructor Dren explains human mana potential across the four recognized ranks: Green, Blue, Red, and Yellow.","after":"Instructor Dren: \"As I was saying, magical society classifies human mana potential into four recognized Circle ranks.\"","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-classroom-disks","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-classroom-disks.webp","alt":"Classroom practice with elemental disks floating in air as students channel mana under glowing circle crests","title":"Elemental Practice Disks","caption":"Leon Varek channels water-colored blue mana to strike the floating practice disk as Instructor Dren demonstrates circle capacity.","after":"Blue mana struck the second disk. It jumped twice as high as the Green one","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-black-circle-question","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-black-circle-question.webp","alt":"Haru raising his hand to question the mythical fifth Black Circle while students smirk","title":"Questioning the Fifth Circle","caption":"In academic practice, Black Circle is not treated as reality-yet Haru asks about the suppressed records.","after":"Instructor Dren: \"There is no verified historical evidence that any human has ever carried such a circle. In academic practice, Black Circle is not treated as reality.\"","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-arena-platform","src":"assets/scenes/episode-03-ladder-arrival.webp","alt":"The grand circular arena with crystal spires, ward barriers, and amphitheater stands for assessment combat","title":"The Assessment Arena","caption":"The training complex opened into a vast circular arena enclosed by crystalline ward pylons and tiers of watchful students.","after":"The training complex dwarfed the classroom blocks, built from white stone braided with metallic mana-thread","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-duel","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-03-leon-varek-assessment.webp","alt":"Leon Varek conjuring water arcs against Haru Aerlen in the academy assessment arena","title":"The Arena Assessment Duel","caption":"Leon dropped into the arena with easy confidence, blue mana curling around his hands like ribbons of water.","after":"Leon dropped from the platform into the ring with practiced ease.","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-water-lances","src":"assets/scenes/episode-03-water-pressure.webp","alt":"Leon unleashing whipping streams of water mana while Haru raises a subtle geometric mana shield","title":"Water Lances & Faint Shield","caption":"Three whip-like streams crack across the arena floor as Haru deflects the pressure with a faint, precise mana shield.","after":"I raised one hand and threw up a thin, uneven mana shield","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-duel-aftermath","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-duel-aftermath.webp","alt":"Leon staring in shock at his collapsing water spell as Haru stands restrained and Dren observes","title":"Subtle Cancellation","caption":"The unstable water sphere folded inward with a wet, airless thump, falling as harmless rain while Leon stared at his hand.","after":"The unstable sphere folded inward with a wet, airless thump.","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-rhea-notices","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-rhea-notices.webp","alt":"Student Council President Rhea Valmont observing Haru from the high arena balcony","title":"Rhea Valmont's Gaze","caption":"At the top of the stairs stood the Student Council President, her calculation sharp behind an unreadable smile.","after":"At the top of the stairs stood a girl in the formal elite mantle reserved for special-ranking officers.","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-healer-cloth","src":"assets/scenes/episode-03-west-corridor.webp","alt":"Elira and Haru conversing in the moonlit west corridor as Elira offers clean healer fabric for his arm","title":"The Healer's Cloth","caption":"In the quiet west corridor beneath moonlit glass, Elira offers medicinal healer fabric for Haru's wounded arm.","after":"In one hand she held a folded strip of white healer cloth","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-rumor-courtyard","src":"assets/scenes/episode-02-rumor-courtyard.webp","alt":"Haru and Aira walking through the sunlit academy courtyard as students whisper and Leon glares from the colonnade","title":"The Courtyard Rumors","caption":"Whispers ripple across the courtyard as students dissect how a Green Circle transfer broke Leon Varek's water spell.","after":"By the time I crossed the main courtyard of **Aurelis Arcane Academy**, students were already retelling","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-aira-tea","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-04-aira-tea-corridor.webp","alt":"Aira offering Haru tea in the sunlit gallery corridor after classes","title":"Efficient Friendliness","caption":"Aira offers Haru tea in the corridor as rumors of the assessment duel ripple through the academy.","after":"Aira: \"Tea. I didn't know what you drink, so I picked the lowest-risk option.\"","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-elira-corridor","src":"assets/scenes/episode-02-09-elira-corridor-radiance-imagegen.webp","alt":"Elira Vale walking down the academy corridor in ceremonial gold mantle as students part in natural deference","title":"The Parting Corridor","caption":"Students instinctively clear a path down the center of the sunlit gallery as Yellow Circle prodigy Elira Vale passes.","after":"Students ahead shifted sideways almost by reflex, clearing the center of the stone walkway.","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-elira-lunch","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-05-elira-lunch-table.webp","alt":"Elira Vale sitting gracefully at the lunch table with Haru and Aira in the grand hall","title":"The Yellow Circle at Lunch","caption":"Elira Vale joins Haru and Aira at their side table, quiet elegance commanding the attention of the hall.","after":"Elira took the seat across from us.","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-training-terrace","src":"assets/scenes/episode-02-training-gardens.webp","alt":"Elira demonstrating intricate mana circles on the high training terrace overlooking Aurelis spires","title":"Training Terrace Gathering","caption":"Elite students take positions on the high open terrace as Elira demonstrates spell formation above the valley cliffs.","after":"At the terrace's center, four elite students took their marks: two high-grade Reds, one specialized Blue, and Elira.","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-elira-garden","src":"assets/scenes/episode-01-06-elira-yellow-circle-garden.webp","alt":"Elira demonstrating radiant golden-white threads in the upper training terrace gardens","title":"Radiant Silk in the Gardens","caption":"Golden-white threads unfolded from Elira's fingertips like woven silk, refining the spell structure with effortless grace.","after":"Golden-white threads unspooled from her fingertips like silk","orientation":"wide"},{"id":"scene-ep1-terrace-challenge","src":"assets/scenes/episode-02-terrace-challenge.webp","alt":"Leon Varek confronting Haru and Elira on the sunset terrace with narrowed eyes and clenched fists","title":"The Terrace Confrontation","caption":"Leon stops a few paces away in disbelief as sunset gilds the terrace, demanding Haru answer for the assessment duel.","after":"Leon stopped three paces off, glancing from Elira to me with stiff disbelief.","orientation":"portrait"},{"id":"scene-ep1-hidden-watcher","src":"assets/scenes/episode-02-hidden-watcher.webp","alt":"A hooded figure watching Haru and Elira from the shadows of the evening courtyard garden pillars","title":"The Hidden Watcher","caption":"A quiet, calculating presence lingers at the edge of the twilight gardens, observing from the marble colonnade.","after":"Before I could isolate the signature, it vanished cleanly.","orientation":"portrait"}]},{"id":"ep2","label":"Episode 2","number":"02","file":"episode-02-cover.webp","fallback":"The Girl Above the World","eyebrow":"First Light","recap":"","accent":["#ffcf47","#f77f00","#2ec4b6"],"displayLabel":"Episode 2","displayNumber":"02","displayEyebrow":"The Hidden Circle","arcLabel":"The Hidden Circle","title":"A Quiet Humiliation","body":"## Interlude: Elira - A Choice Not To Trust\n\nElira Vale had been taught to mistrust coincidence before she learned advanced shielding.\n\nCoincidence, her first etiquette tutor had warned, was usually ambition wearing soft shoes. A student happened to share her study table, then asked about Yellow Circle channeling. A visiting heir happened to admire the same balcony view, then mentioned alliance proposals over tea.\n\nBy thirteen, Elira understood that being admired was not the same as being seen. By sixteen, she understood that being seen could be worse.\n\nHaru Aerlen was hiding something. That was no longer a suspicion. It was a fact with unfinished edges.\n\nHe had moved too cleanly against Leon. He had studied the assessment floor like someone calculating ways not to win. He had accepted a strip of healer cloth in the west corridor as if kindness were a snare with hidden hinges.\n\nA sensible girl would leave it there. A Yellow Circle prodigy did not need another mystery attached to her name, least of all a Green Circle transfer whose silences felt engineered.\n\nElira stood alone in the upper archive gallery and read the same paragraph three times without absorbing a word. Outside the tall windows, Aurelis glittered under evening lamps: beautiful, ordered, always watching itself.\n\nShe could already hear the arguments for distance.\n\nHer mother's voice: *affection is never private for people in visible positions.*\n\nHer father's: *every attachment creates leverage.*\n\nProfessor Halden's, if he knew enough to weigh in: *anomalous behavior should be observed before approached.*\n\nHer own, most inconvenient of all: Haru had lied. Not by word, perhaps, but by construction. The Green Circle on his wrist did not match the person standing behind it. He had reasons, probably serious ones, and serious reasons did not make secrecy harmless.\n\nElira closed the book.\n\nShe was not naive enough to think kindness required trust. She could stay courteous from across a room. She could step in, if needed, without stepping closer. She could let Aira befriend him, let Dren evaluate him, and keep her own name clear of whatever he carried.\n\nThe thought should have settled her. It did not.\n\nBecause of Leon. Not his bruised pride or Haru's footwork, but the half-second after.\n\nLeon had nearly folded his own collapsing spell straight through his casting wrist. Elira had seen it a breath before Dren moved and had gathered mana on reflex. Then Haru stepped straight into the shear.\n\nHe had hidden the correction inside his own injury. No announcement. No glance toward the upper tiers. No effort to make Leon realize what had been spared. He had let the entire room keep the easier story because the true one would have cost too much.\n\nMost people hid what shamed them. Some hid what profited them. Haru hid something immense and still used it to shield a boy who had tried to humiliate him in front of his classmates.\n\nThat did not make him safe. It made him specific. Specific mattered.\n\nElira crossed to the window and looked down into the courtyard. Students passed in clusters, Circle colors flickering at wrists, collars, and throats. Greens yielded walkway space without realizing they were doing it. Blues occupied space without noticing either. Reds and Yellows were watched. Always watched.\n\nAlong the lower path, Haru walked alone. Not lonely, exactly. Practiced. A person could grow skilled at absence, and Elira knew that craft well enough, even if hers had always been hidden beneath applause.\n\nNear the fountain, a first-year dropped an armload of books, his Green crest flashing bright with panic. Haru bent, helped gather the scattered pages, and handed them back before the boy could finish apologizing. Then he walked on without waiting for thanks.\n\nThe first-year stared after him, bewildered by how cleanly it had happened.\n\nElira's fingers tightened on the stone sill. There. That was the reason. Not the strange precision in his casting. It was the way he treated help like something that might turn into a claim if he stood near it too long. He kept walking away before gratitude could become evidence.\n\nElira understood that instinct. She disliked how easily she understood it.\n\nTrusting him might be foolish. Approaching him might be politically reckless. Wanting to know him might grow dangerous in ways she could not yet classify.\n\nShe accepted all three facts.\n\nTurning from the glass, she drew out a single sheet of pale archive paper and wrote one line:\n\n**Moonlit Archive Hall. Tomorrow evening, if you still want an honest conversation.**\n\nShe paused before signing her name, then added underneath:\n\n**No audience. No assumptions.**\n\nIt was the narrowest door she knew how to offer. Not trust yet. Only a room where trust might decide whether to enter.\n\n## Haru - The Ladder Match\n\nThree days passed with too many whispers and too little quiet.\n\nElira's note from my first evening stayed folded inside my desk like a promise postponed, not forgotten. Leon's ladder challenge had shoved everything private aside, and Aurelis loved nothing more than turning private friction into a crowd event.\n\nThe combat ladder arena was louder than usual. That was Leon's doing.\n\nBy the time I reached the circular battle hall after afternoon classes, half of **Aurelis Arcane Academy** had packed the observation tiers. Students leaned over the brass railings, balancing notebooks and wagers while trading loud predictions about how fast this would end.\n\nThe Green Circle transfer who had disrupted Leon Varek once by \"luck\" was stepping into an official bracket match. To the gallery, it was theater. To Leon, it was damage control. To me, it was an inconvenience.\n\nAira walked onto the upper platform beside me, looked down at the packed hall, looked at me, and looked back down as if estimating the blast radius.\n\nAira: \"This is terrible.\"\n\nHaru: \"You sound certain.\"\n\nAira: \"Because I am. There are too many witnesses here for anything to stay normal.\"\n\nHaru: \"Nothing here has been normal since I arrived.\"\n\nAira: \"True. This is a much louder kind of not normal.\"\n\nShe studied my face more closely.\n\nAira: \"You're calm.\"\n\nHaru: \"Should I not be?\"\n\nAira: \"That depends. Are you calm because you've made peace with public humiliation, or calm because you're about to do something that gives me a stress condition?\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm not that difficult.\"\n\nAira gave me a flat stare.\n\nAira: \"Haru, I say this with affection. You are the human form of hidden complications.\"\n\nThe corner of my mouth twitched before I stopped it.\n\nDown on the arena floor, Leon was already waiting near the center ring in a spotless training coat, sleeves rolled neatly, blond hair catching the lamps in the exact way he probably hoped it would look in someone else's memory.\n\nEven from the platform, I could feel the hairline cracks underneath the polish. He had built too much of himself around being seen as superior. The assessment's embarrassment three days earlier had not simply bruised his pride; it had rattled the scaffolding holding him upright. That made him more reckless, not stronger.\n\nA quiet hush rippled down the stands. I turned.\n\nElira descended the eastern stair with unhurried composure, her elite mantle settled cleanly over her shoulders while the golden light of her **Yellow Circle** crest caught the arena lamps at her collarbone. She never asked for attention; attention simply rearranged itself around her.\n\nAira followed my gaze and let out a slow breath.\n\nAira: \"There it is.\"\n\nHaru: \"What?\"\n\nAira: \"The exact face people make right before their lives get emotionally complicated.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was just looking.\"\n\nAira: \"Mm.\"\n\nElira reached our railing and offered Aira a warm nod before turning to me.\n\nElira: \"You came.\"\n\nHaru: \"I said I would.\"\n\nElira: \"A rare quality at this academy.\"\n\nAira: \"Keeping promises?\"\n\nElira: \"Walking voluntarily into obvious trouble.\"\n\nAira: \"Then yes, that sounds right.\"\n\nI ignored both of them with as much dignity as the situation allowed.\n\nElira glanced down at the bright floor, then back to me.\n\nElira: \"You don't look nervous.\"\n\nHaru: \"People keep expecting that.\"\n\nA quiet smile touched her mouth.\n\nElira: \"Most people fear public judgment more than combat.\"\n\nHaru: \"Do you?\"\n\nFor half a second her eyes shifted, just enough to show the real person living underneath all that grace.\n\nElira: \"I used to.\"\n\nBefore I could answer, an amplified voice rolled across the battle hall.\n\nAnnouncer: \"Combat Ladder Preliminary Match: Leon Varek of Year Two, Section Three versus Haru Aerlen, transfer student of Year Two, Section Three. Standard academy restrictions apply. Non-lethal engagement only. Barrier interference forbidden. Excessive force subject to disciplinary review.\"\n\nAira crossed her arms tight.\n\nAira: \"If you get hurt, I'll be furious.\"\n\nHaru: \"That seems backwards.\"\n\nAira: \"It's efficient.\"\n\nElira held my gaze steadily.\n\nElira: \"Be careful.\"\n\nHer voice was quiet and plain, harder to brush off than any warning I had received on campus.\n\nHaru: \"I will.\"\n\nI turned and took the steps down to the arena floor.\n\n---\n\nThe barrier rose around us in a tall ring of pale blue light.\n\nThe ladder arena was wider than the assessment hall, ringed by perimeter instructors and scored with concentric containment grooves built to absorb high-grade elemental backlash without cracking.\n\nLeon waited opposite me with a practiced smile, thinner and tighter than before.\n\nLeon: \"Glad you showed up.\"\n\nHaru: \"You sounded like you'd be difficult if I didn't.\"\n\nA scatter of laughs broke from the stands. Leon's jaw tightened.\n\nLeon: \"Let's see if you keep that tone once we start.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'll do my best.\"\n\nThe match official lifted one hand.\n\nOfficial: \"Begin.\"\n\nLeon struck without probing. Three compressed streams of water mana surged across the ring in a tight triangle: one high, one center, one skimming flat across the tiles to sweep my footing out from under me.\n\nI stepped once to the right. The high jet missed. I turned my shoulder so the center stream hissed past my coat, then lifted one hand and severed the low sweep with a razor-thin mana edge fine enough to pass for reinforcement dispersion.\n\nThe water burst into harmless spray. Murmurs rose in the gallery.\n\nLeon's eyes narrowed. He snapped straight into a heavier sequence, raising two curving walls of dense water on either flank to box me inward while a line of compressed needles formed directly above my head.\n\nI had planned to keep this modest, just clean enough to settle the bracket and step off the stage.\n\nThen I caught Leon's eyes across the water.\n\nNot discipline. Not focus. Hunger. He did not want a clean win; he wanted the Green transfer broken badly enough that nobody would speak of three days ago ever again.\n\nAfter three days of hallway whispers and smug wagers, a colder piece of me decided I was done cushioning his ego. Not completely. Just enough.\n\nI let a fraction more power wake beneath my ribs. Nowhere near enough to show the truth, yet more than I had allowed on day one. Enough that the false Green crest under my left cuff stretched tight around the flow. Enough that the air around my palms turned crisp and sharp. Enough that the sleeping darkness behind my sternum pulsed once, almost pleased to be acknowledged.\n\nLeon slammed the water walls inward.\n\nI walked straight forward into the closing seam.\n\nMy palm met the left wall of water mana. To the stands, it looked like a sharp reinforcement break. To anyone with high-grade perception, it would have felt far stranger, as though the spell had simply lost permission to remain solid where my fingers touched it.\n\nThe wall collapsed into cold mist. Leon's eyes widened.\n\nI crossed the gap before his overhead needles finished locking. His reflexes were quick: he pivoted hard and forged a hardened water blade along his forearm for close quarters.\n\nI caught his wrist.\n\nThe entire arena went dead quiet. Not because blocking a strike was impressive, but because I stopped it too easily.\n\nLeon poured mana into the construct, trying to force the blade through my guard. I tightened my fingers by a fraction. The hardened water shattered into glass-bright drops.\n\nLeon: \"What-\"\n\nI stepped inside his stance, pressed two fingers lightly against the center of his chest, and released a tight pulse of force. Not enough to crack bone. More than enough to launch him.\n\nLeon lifted off his heels, skidded hard across the containment tiles, and slid to a halt near the outer ring.\n\nGasps detonated across the tiers. Leon coughed, propped himself onto one elbow, and stared back at me in raw disbelief before the stands exploded.\n\nStudent 1: \"No way.\"\n\nStudent 2: \"He threw Leon across the ring.\"\n\nStudent 3: \"That wasn't Green Circle output.\"\n\nStudent 4: \"Then what was it?\"\n\nStudent 5: \"Did he reinforce his whole frame first?\"\n\nStudent 6: \"I didn't even see the cast.\"\n\nUp on the platform, Aira was already standing, caught halfway between shock and vindication. Beside her, Elira had gone completely still, golden eyes locked on my hands with razor focus.\n\nLeon scrambled upright, dignity burned down to cinders. Water mana lashed around him in jagged coils.\n\nLeon: \"You think one hit settles this?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nI kept my voice level across the quiet floor.\n\nHaru: \"I think the next one will.\"\n\nA sharp wave of startled laughs and indrawn breaths swept the bleachers. Up above, Aira clapped one hand over her face.\n\nAira: \"Oh no.\"\n\nBeside her, the faintest curve touched Elira's mouth.\n\nLeon roared and emptied his reserve at once. A crown of water spears materialized overhead, a pressure wave rolled across the floor, and thick mist burst upward to blind the arena while hidden tendrils of compressed water raced through the fog toward my ankles.\n\nAll temper, no control.\n\nI exhaled once and stepped into the mist.\n\nTo the gallery, I vanished. To Leon, I arrived in front of him far too soon.\n\nReal fear flashed across his face. He threw up a water shield; I brushed it apart. He tried to backpedal; I swept his leading boot. He reached for another cast; I tapped the casting line above his wrist and folded the spell shut before it could form.\n\nThen I stopped, one hand hovering an inch from his throat without touching skin, my other palm resting lightly over the center of his active mana channel. One pulse would put him flat on the stone.\n\nI did not need it. The fog thinned, and the whole hall saw the match as it stood: Leon off-balance, wide-eyed, spell dead; me untouched, one breath from finishing him.\n\nThe official did not hesitate.\n\nOfficial: \"Match over! Winner: Haru Aerlen!\"\n\nThe barrier chimed once as it dropped, and noise crashed down from every tier.\n\nNear the lower rail, a Green Circle boy pressed both hands over his mouth as if afraid to let vindication show too openly. Two rows up, a Blue girl stared at Leon's broken posture like someone watching an axiom fail in daylight. On the western tier, a Red Circle heir said nothing at all; his eyes simply shifted from Leon to me, already running numbers.\n\nStudent 7: \"Brutal.\"\n\nStudent 8: \"He took him apart.\"\n\nStudent 9: \"Leon didn't land a single clean hit.\"\n\nStudent 10: \"How is a Green moving like that?\"\n\nStudent 11: \"His output changed.\"\n\nStudent 12: \"No, his control changed.\"\n\nStudent 13: \"Either way, Leon's finished.\"\n\nLeon's life would go on, of course. His pride, at least for today, had died in front of half the academy.\n\nI stepped back and lowered my hands. Leon stood frozen for half a beat, then looked up at the gallery and absorbed the full scale of the silence around his name. His next protest died in his throat as the cold realization hit him that I had never been straining.\n\nWithout a word, he turned and walked straight out of the arena under the weight of three hundred stares. Nobody called after him.\n\nI looked up toward the observation deck. Aira stared at me like a friend whose worst suspicion had just grown fangs. Beside her, Elira watched with quiet, unmistakable admiration. That was going to be a separate problem.\n\n---\n\nI slipped out through the west corridor hoping to beat the post-match crowd. That lasted about forty seconds.\n\nQuick footsteps echoed behind me near the garden stairwell.\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI stopped. She caught up half a breath short, folded her arms, and fixed me with a look immune to polite deflection.\n\nAira: \"One honest answer.\"\n\nHaru: \"Only one?\"\n\nAira: \"For now. What was that?\"\n\nHaru: \"A ladder match.\"\n\nAira stared at me without blinking.\n\nAira: \"I am working very hard to stay your friend.\"\n\nHaru: \"I appreciate that.\"\n\nAira: \"Then meet me halfway.\"\n\nI weighed how much truth stayed safe.\n\nHaru: \"Leon lost his temper. His channeling got sloppy, and he left openings.\"\n\nAira: \"That explains half of it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Only half?\"\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nBefore she could press further, calm footsteps rounded the arch behind her. Elira stepped into the lantern light as though the roar of the arena had never reached her.\n\nElira: \"You fought efficiently.\"\n\nHaru: \"That's generous.\"\n\nElira: \"Would you prefer 'mercilessly'?\"\n\nAira pointed straight at Elira.\n\nAira: \"Thank you. I feel less crazy.\"\n\nA small, real smile slipped past my guard before I could stop it. Both of them caught it at once.\n\nAira: \"You're smiling.\"\n\nHaru: \"Briefly.\"\n\nElira kept her golden eyes on mine.\n\nElira: \"You chose not to hold back as much today.\"\n\nNot phrased as a question.\n\nHaru: \"A little.\"\n\nAira let out a strangled laugh.\n\nAira: \"That was 'a little'?\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nAira pressed two fingers to her forehead.\n\nAira: \"I need a completely new measuring stick for you.\"\n\nElira's gaze softened.\n\nElira: \"You could have injured him badly. You didn't.\"\n\nHaru: \"I didn't want to.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe said it far too easily, as if she had already made up her mind about what lived under my restraint. Worse, she was right.\n\nAira glanced back and forth between us, sighed deeply, and stepped back toward the stairs.\n\nAira: \"I suddenly feel like I'm standing in the middle of something that hasn't officially started yet.\"\n\nHaru: \"You aren't interrupting anything.\"\n\nAira: \"That is the exact sentence people speak right before they accidentally develop feelings.\"\n\nElira laughed softly. For tactical reasons, I said nothing.\n\nAira pointed at both of us.\n\nAira: \"I'm leaving before either of you says something quiet and emotionally catastrophic.\"\n\nHaru: \"Dramatic.\"\n\nAira: \"Accurate.\"\n\nShe disappeared down the stairwell, throwing me one parting glance that clearly promised a longer interrogation at breakfast.\n\nBack inside the emptying arena, high in the last row of the stands, a Green Circle girl with curly auburn hair had not yet moved.\n\nMira Castellan held a worn notebook flat against her chest. Her uniform cuffs were mended with stitches neat enough that only someone used to repairing her own clothes would notice.\n\nGreen Student: \"Had to be luck.\"\n\nMira kept her eyes on the shadowed corridor where Haru had gone.\n\nMira: \"Twice?\"\n\nHer classmate shifted awkwardly on the bench.\n\nGreen Student: \"You really think a Green can keep beating Blues?\"\n\nMira's bright green eyes stayed calm and measuring. Hope was dangerous when held too loudly.\n\nMira: \"I think we're about to find out.\"\n\nDown on the lower tier, two Red Circle students laughed a little too sharply about Leon having an off afternoon, while a Blue upper-year called Haru's timing a cheap trick and sounded slightly too loud about it.\n\n---\n\nIn the west corridor, it was just Elira and me.\n\nThrough the tall windows, the sky beyond Aurelis's towers had shifted into deep gold and violet, and the long shadow of the cliffs stretched across the lower gardens. Mana lamps hummed softly to life along the walls.\n\nElira broke the quiet first.\n\nElira: \"You embarrassed him thoroughly.\"\n\nHaru: \"That wasn't the main goal.\"\n\nElira: \"You didn't mind it either.\"\n\nI thought about it for half a second.\n\nHaru: \"Less than I probably should have.\"\n\nAmusement lit her eyes.\n\nElira: \"That might be the most honest answer you've given me.\"\n\nHaru: \"That sounds like you think I've been lying.\"\n\nElira tilted her head.\n\nElira: \"Guarded. Not dishonest.\"\n\nShe glanced toward the dusk outside the glass, then back to me.\n\nElira: \"Walk with me?\"\n\nHaru: \"People will talk.\"\n\nElira: \"They already are.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then yes.\"\n\nWe exited through the west archway onto the upper garden path. Evening had settled over **Aurelis Arcane Academy**, silver-leaf trees catching warm lantern light and scattering pale patterns across the stone. A few passing students slowed, stared, and looked away too fast, as if caught witnessing something they did not yet know how to explain. Elira moved past every glance without shifting her pace.\n\nWe stopped at a raised overlook where the academy terrace dropped away above the lit avenues of Aurelis City. Elira rested both hands lightly on the stone rail.\n\nElira: \"When I was younger, I came up here whenever the academy felt too crowded.\"\n\nHaru: \"You've been here long?\"\n\nElira: \"Since preparatory division. Everyone assumes that makes things easier.\"\n\nHaru: \"Did it?\"\n\nShe looked out across the glittering avenues below.\n\nElira: \"No. When you wear a Yellow Circle, people decide who you are before you open your mouth. Some admire you, some resent you, some want your attention to rearrange their future.\"\n\nHaru: \"Does it?\"\n\nShe cut a faint smile sideways at me.\n\nElira: \"Sometimes. That's part of the problem.\"\n\nHaru: \"That sounds lonely too.\"\n\nThe line left me before I thought to trim it back. Elira turned toward me, genuine surprise softening her face.\n\nElira: \"It can be.\"\n\nHaru: \"You hide it well.\"\n\nElira let out a quiet breath that was almost a laugh.\n\nElira: \"So do you.\"\n\nShe leaned slightly against the rail, studying my face in the twilight.\n\nElira: \"You knew I was watching from the platform before your match.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira: \"Did it make you nervous?\"\n\nHaru: \"No. More careful.\"\n\nElira: \"Because of me?\"\n\nHaru: \"Partly. And because I'm still figuring out how much of myself this place can see without breaking.\"\n\nShe held my gaze without blinking.\n\nElira: \"Then I'm glad you let it see that much. Hiding gets heavy when you carry it alone too long.\"\n\nHaru: \"You notice too much.\"\n\nElira: \"And you say too little.\"\n\nHaru: \"Balanced, then.\"\n\nA real laugh escaped her, and I felt the corner of my own mouth lift before I remembered to stop it.\n\nElira: \"There. Again.\"\n\nHaru: \"Again what?\"\n\nElira: \"You smile like it surprises even you.\"\n\nI looked out across the city lights to hide the heat creeping up my collar.\n\nHaru: \"Inconveniently specific.\"\n\nElira: \"I warned you I pay attention.\"\n\nSilence settled easily between us. Wind rustled through the silver leaves overhead. Far below, the capital's transit sigils drifted like slow embers across the dark.\n\nThen Elira's voice dropped softer.\n\nElira: \"May I ask something difficult?\"\n\nHaru: \"You always ask dangerous questions very calmly.\"\n\nElira: \"Is that a no?\"\n\nHaru: \"Ask.\"\n\nHer fingers curled slightly against the carved stone.\n\nElira: \"When you broke Leon's water wall today, for one second your mana felt different.\"\n\nOutwardly I did not twitch. Inwardly every alarm snapped upright.\n\nElira spoke before the quiet could freeze.\n\nElira: \"Not louder. Not merely bigger. It felt...\" She searched for the exact weight of it. \"Deeper.\"\n\nOut of every word in the language, she had landed straight on the truest one.\n\nHaru: \"You're very perceptive.\"\n\nElira: \"That isn't an answer.\"\n\nHaru: \"It's the safest one I have.\"\n\nShe did not push.\n\nElira: \"All right.\"\n\nNo demand, no shift in warmth. Just *all right*. Somehow that cost me more than an interrogation would have.\n\nHaru: \"Does it bother you? That I'm hiding things?\"\n\nShe considered the question seriously before meeting my eyes.\n\nElira: \"It worries me more than it bothers me. People usually hide for one of two reasons: to protect someone else, or because they believe nobody would understand.\"\n\nHaru: \"Which do you think I'm doing?\"\n\nHer answer arrived so quietly the breeze nearly stole it.\n\nElira: \"Both.\"\n\nI had no ready defense for that. A stray lock of pale-gold hair brushed her cheek; she tucked it behind one ear without thinking, small and unstudied, and the evening bell tolled from the clock tower before I could decide what to say.\n\nWe had drifted half a pace closer without either of us marking when.\n\nElira: \"If I ask you to come to the north library tonight, am I being too persistent?\"\n\nHaru: \"Probably.\"\n\nElira: \"Will you come anyway?\"\n\nSimpler to refuse. Safer to walk away. Neither option felt honest anymore.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer smile broke open without a trace of hallway polish.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nFootsteps scraped on the lower walkway as two students rounded the arch, froze at the sight of us, and immediately failed at pretending they had seen nothing.\n\nStudent 1: \"Oh.\"\n\nStudent 2: \"Don't say it.\"\n\nStudent 1: \"I didn't say anything.\"\n\nStudent 2: \"Your face said enough.\"\n\nThey hurried down the stairs. Elira shut her eyes for a brief, amused second.\n\nElira: \"Aira was right. People are going to talk.\"\n\nHaru: \"Do you mind?\"\n\nShe held my gaze for a full beat before answering.\n\nElira: \"Not if they're wrong about the important parts.\"\n\nWe turned back toward the residence walkways at an unhurried pace. Most of the academy had drifted indoors for evening study. Halfway down the stone path toward the main stairwell, Elira slowed to a stop.\n\nElira: \"There's something else.\"\n\nHaru: \"That sounds ominous.\"\n\nElira: \"It might be.\"\n\nMy posture tightened at once. She folded her hands lightly in front of her coat.\n\nElira: \"After your match, one of the senior faculty observers stopped me near the platform to ask about you.\"\n\nHaru: \"Normal enough after an upset.\"\n\nElira: \"Not the way he asked.\"\n\nHaru: \"Who?\"\n\nElira: \"Professor Halden. Advanced Mana Research Division.\"\n\nI knew the name by reputation: old structural theory, restricted archive clearance, quiet leverage over administrative review. Not someone I wanted studying my shadow.\n\nHaru: \"What did he ask?\"\n\nElira: \"Whether I had sensed anything unusual in your mana flow.\"\n\nI stopped dead on the flagstone. Elira stopped opposite me.\n\nHaru: \"What did you tell him?\"\n\nElira answered without a flicker of hesitation.\n\nElira: \"That you were precise.\"\n\nI looked straight at her.\n\nHaru: \"You covered for me.\"\n\nElira: \"I answered truthfully.\"\n\nHaru: \"You left things out.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHaru: \"Why?\"\n\nHer golden eyes stayed clear and steady in the lantern light.\n\nElira: \"Because whatever you're carrying, I don't believe you're hiding it to hurt anyone.\"\n\nSomething old and locked tight inside my chest gave half an inch.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nElira: \"Save it. Once senior researchers get curious, they rarely stop looking after one question.\"\n\nHaru: \"Inconvenient.\"\n\nElira: \"You use 'inconvenient' the way normal people use 'catastrophic.'\"\n\nHaru: \"Easier to manage that way.\"\n\nShe smiled and resumed walking with me down to the residence fork where the dormitory paths split. Neither of us moved first.\n\nElira: \"North wing. Moonlit Archive Hall. In an hour?\"\n\nHaru: \"I'll be there.\"\n\nShe nodded once, then hesitated half a breath before turning.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes?\"\n\nHer expression softened.\n\nElira: \"I'm glad you came to Aurelis.\"\n\nSimple, plain, unguarded, and harder to absorb than any spell I had parried today.\n\nHaru: \"I think,\" I said quietly, \"I'm glad too.\"\n\nWarmth deepened in her eyes before she turned up the lit residence path, her gold crest fading slowly among the silver-leaf trees.\n\nI stood at the fork a few seconds longer while clouds drifted across the moon.\n\nProfessor Halden. Advanced Mana Research. The threat around me was no longer a faceless note slipped under a locked door in Lumeris. It was starting to wear names.\n\nBeneath the quiet illusion of the Green crest on my wrist, the Black Circle gave one low, wakeful pulse, as if it too had noticed that secrecy felt heavier the moment someone stood close enough to see through it.\n\n---\n\n## Moonlit Archive: The Question\n\nThat first night in the Moonlit Archive did not become a confession. At the time it felt like failure; later, I realized it was the only reason trust survived at all.\n\nElira waited beside the central reading table with one hand resting on a closed ledger and the other loose at her side, careful not to stand like a judge. The care was deliberate enough that I distrusted it for three full breaths before recognizing she had done it for my sake.\n\nElira: \"No audience. No assumptions.\"\n\nHaru: \"You wrote that three nights ago.\"\n\nElira: \"I meant it then. I mean it more tonight.\"\n\nMoonlight pooled in pale rectangles across the flagstones between towering shelves. She did not rush into the question, which somehow made it heavier when it came.\n\nElira: \"Three days ago in the assessment hall, you took a hit so Leon wouldn't tear open his own casting wrist. Tonight you dismantled him without losing your breath, and Professor Halden noticed the exact second your mana shifted.\"\n\nI looked toward the arched windows. That was confirmation enough.\n\nElira: \"I'm not asking what you are.\"\n\nMy gaze snapped back to her. Her expression remained steady.\n\nElira: \"Not tonight, unless you choose to tell me. I'm asking whether whatever you're hiding is dangerous to you.\"\n\nThe easy lie sat right on my tongue: *No.*\n\nHaru: \"Sometimes.\"\n\nHer fingers tightened once against the leather cover. Only once.\n\nElira: \"Is it dangerous to others?\"\n\nHaru: \"If handled badly.\"\n\nElira: \"By you?\"\n\nI almost smiled, not out of humor, just because she had walked straight to the real question everyone else avoided until panic made them cruel.\n\nHaru: \"By anyone who thinks power gets safer once they own it.\"\n\nShe absorbed that without pretending to know more than she did. That was the first reason I stayed in the room.\n\nElira: \"I can tell something is there.\"\n\nHaru: \"Seeing isn't the same as knowing safely.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nI believed her, which felt riskier than doubting her. Every old habit told me to step back, deflect, keep her outside the blast radius of whatever my secret turned people into. Yet she had offered no claim, no demand, no price. Only quiet space.\n\nHaru: \"I'll show you.\"\n\nHer breath caught.\n\nHaru: \"Not tonight.\"\n\nThe hope in her face did not vanish; it shifted into something steadier, which somehow cost me more.\n\nHaru: \"If I show you tonight just because you covered for me with Halden, I'm not trusting you. I'm reacting because I want trust to be simpler than it is.\"\n\nElira looked down at the table for one heartbeat. When her eyes lifted again, they were bright and level.\n\nElira: \"Then think.\"\n\nNo wounded pride. No flare of temper.\n\nHaru: \"You're disappointed.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer chin lifted slightly, clear and candid.\n\nElira: \"I would still rather be disappointed by a real answer than comforted by a reckless one.\"\n\nI had no proper response for that. I inclined my head once and walked out before gratitude turned into something else I mishandled.\n\nBehind me, the Moonlit Archive settled back into stillness, leaving Elira alone with the knowledge that something waited in the dark, and that I had not handed it over yet.\n\n---\n\n## Moonlit Archive: The Reveal\n\nThree nights after that first conversation in the Archive, I arrived later than either of us had planned.\n\nThe three days between had not been empty.\n\nOn the first morning, Elira caught me outside the dining hall watching two Blue Circle boys turn a Green first-year's dropped tray into public theater. She wanted to step straight into the middle of the hall and dress them down. I wanted to help the boy out before turning him into the centerpiece of a moral lecture.\n\nElira: \"Silence protects the people who did it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Public correction leaves him carrying everyone's eyes long after they forget the lesson.\"\n\nElira: \"Then both of us are deciding what he can endure without asking him.\"\n\nThat ended the argument because she was right. We asked him. The boy wanted a fresh lunch, the spilled soup cleared before someone tracked through it, and an anonymous report logged so the same pair could not call it harmless next week. Elira filed the entry; I fixed the tray. Neither of us got the clean victory we had imagined, and the boy ate in peace.\n\nOn the second day, she joined Aira and me for a tactical drill and rejected my safest route because it sacrificed two simulated support casters to hold the objective. I called it efficient. She called it cowardice dressed up as arithmetic. Aira forced us to run both versions; Elira's saved more people while mine cleared the board eleven seconds faster. Elira did not gloat, which was almost irritating.\n\nBetween those clashes, small ordinary facts gathered. She disliked over-sweet tea. I walked into a doorframe while reading a textbook. She counted room exits when halls got crowded, though less visibly than I did. I learned that her public composure was never effortless; she simply hid the cost of it well.\n\nThree days could not make trust safe. They were enough to prove we could disagree without turning difference into punishment.\n\nThe north library wing felt older than the rest of Aurelis, its stone walls holding centuries of quiet thought. Mana lamps floated low along the vaulted ceiling, casting warm circles over empty reading alcoves. Most students stayed clear after dusk; the silence here echoed back too sharply for people who preferred easy company.\n\nFor me, it felt almost peaceful.\n\nElira was already inside the **Moonlit Archive Hall**. She stood beside an arched window, one hand resting lightly on the spine of an open book she was no longer reading. Moonlight brushed her hair pale gold at the edges. In the stillness of the archive, with no one watching her posture or waiting on her rank, she looked less like an academy symbol and more like someone who had finally set down a heavy coat.\n\nShe looked up as my boots crossed the threshold.\n\nElira: \"You came.\"\n\nHaru: \"You doubted it?\"\n\nElira smiled faintly.\n\nElira: \"No. I just wanted to say it.\"\n\nI stepped deeper into the hall. Tall rows of volumes bound in leather, rune-thread, and preservation cloth stretched into the dimness, and in the center of the floor sat a broad pale stone sigil inlaid flush with the tiles.\n\nHaru: \"You were right. Nobody comes up here.\"\n\nElira slid her book back onto the shelf and walked toward the central reading table.\n\nElira: \"Most students only care for old records if they raise a ranking score.\"\n\nHaru: \"And you?\"\n\nElira: \"I like rooms where people lower their voices.\"\n\nI joined her beside the table. Moonlight lay across one edge of the polished wood, warm lamp glow across the other.\n\nElira: \"I wanted somewhere we could talk without half the academy listening.\"\n\nHaru: \"That already makes this my favorite room on campus.\"\n\nShe laughed quietly. For a while we talked about ordinary things: classes, instructors, academy politics, how quickly polite distance froze into silence whenever rank entered a room. Then, inevitably, the talk drifted toward Magic Circles.\n\nElira walked to the tall arched window overlooking the moonlit gardens.\n\nElira: \"Do you think people would be kinder if circles didn't exist?\"\n\nI leaned lightly against the end of the nearest bookshelf.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nShe glanced back, surprised rather than offended.\n\nElira: \"Immediate answer.\"\n\nHaru: \"People would invent another ladder. Wealth. Family. Affinity type. Appearance. Lineage. Talent. Fear is inventive.\"\n\nElira turned fully toward me.\n\nElira: \"Then what makes circles different?\"\n\nHaru: \"They glow.\"\n\nFor a beat she simply looked at me, then understanding softened her face.\n\nElira: \"Visible judgment.\"\n\nHaru: \"If cruelty looks organized and natural enough, people stop calling it cruelty. They call it order.\"\n\nSilence lingered. Her Yellow crest glowed faintly against her collarbone, reflected in the dark glass.\n\nElira: \"When I awakened as Yellow, everyone celebrated. My family cried from relief. Instructors changed their tone overnight. Other children either idolized me or backed away. Adults called me special before I even knew what the word cost. Nobody asked if I wanted it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Did you?\"\n\nShe looked down at her hands.\n\nElira: \"At first I liked the praise. I was a child. Then I noticed what happened once people decided I stood too high to touch. They stopped speaking plainly. They admired a polite statue that made them feel safe.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not you.\"\n\nElira nodded.\n\nElira: \"Not me.\"\n\nIsolation came in different shapes, and power was one of its sharpest blades.\n\nElira lifted her eyes back to mine.\n\nElira: \"You notice Green Circle students more than anyone else here.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because almost nobody else does.\"\n\nI folded my arms loosely.\n\nHaru: \"A Green student walks into a room and people assign their ceiling before they speak. Quiet means weak. Confident means arrogant. Skilled means suspicious. Winning means luck. The world doesn't merely rank them lower. It narrates them lower.\"\n\nRecognition crossed her face, sharp and free of pity.\n\nElira: \"You talk like you've lived inside that your whole life.\"\n\nHaru: \"Close enough.\"\n\nShe let the answer stand.\n\nElira: \"Blues stay defensive to prove they belong above Greens. Reds live inside gilded cages where no one forgives a single crack.\"\n\nI glanced at her, surprised by how cleanly she stripped the paint off both ranks.\n\nElira: \"And Yellow?\"\n\nI really looked at her then: the drilled poise, the solitude worn so gracefully that everyone mistook it for pride, and the kindness that had somehow survived intact underneath.\n\nHaru: \"Yellow Circles get treated like ideals before they're treated like human beings.\"\n\nShe went completely still. Something deep shifted behind her eyes.\n\nElira: \"That,\" she whispered, \"is the first time anyone has ever named it right.\"\n\nWe let the quiet breathe. Then Elira looked down at the pale stone circle inlaid across the center of the floor.\n\nElira: \"And Black?\"\n\nMy whole body locked before I could tell it not to. She saw it immediately. She did not step closer, did not press, did not raise her voice.\n\nElira: \"You don't have to tell me,\" she murmured. \"If you ever want to... I'll listen.\"\n\nNo demand. No price tag. Only listening.\n\nI stood in the quiet, hearing wind brush the window glass, the low hum of shelf wards, and the slow beat of my own pulse behind my ribs. Every survival habit built over eighteen years shouted *no*. Hide. Deflect. Protect the secret. Protect her. Protect the world from what it would do once it knew.\n\nYet Elira had already seen the outline of danger and answered with care. She had shielded me from Halden without knowing the whole cost.\n\nSome truths are terrifying because they break trust. Others are terrifying because they ask for it.\n\nI let out a long breath.\n\nHaru: \"If I show you, you can't speak of it to anyone.\"\n\nHer eyes widened slightly with the weight of the moment, without a hint of fear.\n\nElira: \"I understand.\"\n\nHaru: \"No one. Not faculty, not friends, not family. Not under pressure or threat. Not unless I give permission.\"\n\nShe straightened, every trace of casual softness replaced by solemn clarity.\n\nElira: \"You have my word.\"\n\nI looked straight into her eyes.\n\nHaru: \"Words are cheap in a world like this.\"\n\nWithout hesitating, she nodded.\n\nElira: \"Then tell me what promise you need.\"\n\nI walked to the edge of the pale foundation sigil where moonlight pooled across the stone. Before taking the final step, I released a soundless isolation veil through the floor, shelves, glass, and old archive wards. Thin enough that no student outside would notice it, yet dense enough to swallow pressure, light, and sound before they reached the corridor beyond.\n\nHaru: \"A binding promise.\"\n\nElira stepped forward, expression completely serious.\n\nElira: \"A mana vow.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nA mana vow was never casual. It braided truth, will, and magic together; breaking one carried real consequences, sometimes permanently scarring a caster's channels.\n\nElira: \"You trust me enough to show me something worth a vow?\"\n\nI looked down at the pale stone between our boots.\n\nHaru: \"I'm deciding that right now.\"\n\nInstead of cooling at the bluntness, her gaze only grew gentler. She stepped into the moonlit circle opposite me.\n\nElira: \"Then let me decide too.\"\n\nShe raised one hand. A small golden sigil bloomed between us, warm and sharp.\n\nElira: \"I, Elira Vale, vow by my circle, my mana, and my will that whatever truth Haru Aerlen entrusts to me tonight, I will not reveal to any other soul without his permission.\"\n\nThe gold ring pulsed once.\n\nElira: \"I will not use it against him. I will not surrender it willingly. And if I am ever forced to choose between guarding his secret and guarding my reputation, I will guard his secret.\"\n\nThat last line startled me enough that my eyes snapped up to hers. Her expression did not flinch. The sigil brightened cleanly, confirming complete alignment between word and intent.\n\nHaru: \"You're serious.\"\n\nElira: \"Completely.\"\n\nDrawing a slow breath, I laid my palm over her golden sigil. A single hair-thin thread of dark mana curled from my skin and looped around her light without dimming it. Even that tiny whisper made the archive air shiver.\n\nHaru: \"Then I, Haru Aerlen, accept your vow. And I vow in return that the truth I show you tonight will never be used to harm you. I will protect you from the burden of it as long as I can.\"\n\nDark thread and golden sigil braided together, then dissolved into a quiet pulse of warm and cold light that sank into the stone beneath us. The vow sealed.\n\nShe had not asked what my power could buy her, or how to aim it. She had placed her own name beneath my secret before she even knew its shape. The difference made my throat tight.\n\nFor a breath, Elira only stared at the space where that single hair-thin thread of darkness had hovered.\n\nElira: \"That mana...\"\n\nI lowered my hand.\n\nHaru: \"Now you see why this can't leave this room.\"\n\nShe nodded slowly, awe edged with clear-eyed caution.\n\nThe vow was done. The room was veiled. Deciding to show her had been a clean thought inside my head. Actually doing it was something else entirely.\n\nMy hands did not know Elira's name as safety. My pulse did not know it. The Black Circle beneath my false Green wrist crest knew pursuit, locked rooms, and leaving before questions turned into chains. In every version of discovery I had rehearsed for years, the truth was torn out of me by enemy magic or exposed because someone I cared about stood in the line of fire.\n\nNo one was forcing anything tonight. I had to open my own hand.\n\nFor one foolish second I wished she would look away just long enough to spare me the instant of becoming visible on purpose. She did not look away.\n\nI dropped my gaze to the floor between us and loosened the concealment over my wrist crest.\n\nThe Black Circle resisted out of sheer habit. I had to steady my breath and remind it that this room was different. Not completely open. Just enough.\n\nThe archive answered at once. Overhead lamps tilted inward. One shelf rune gave a thin, startled chime.\n\nBlack lines unspooled from beneath my boots, stopping cleanly inside the pale foundation ring. One inner circle unfolded between us, no wider than our joined shadows. Ink-black. Silver markings turned inside it with a precision I had never been taught and still did not fully trust. Behind the visible lines lay deeper geometry I kept locked away, a stillness too vast for this hall to hold.\n\nThis was not the whole Circle. Only the smallest honest fragment I could show without waking every ancient ward in Aurelis.\n\nElira stepped back on pure reflex. Her Yellow Circle flared bright at her collarbone, less a challenge than a great light recognizing something far older than itself.\n\nElira: \"Haru...\"\n\nI kept my voice steady.\n\nHaru: \"This is what my Green crest hides. Black Circle.\"\n\nSpeaking the words aloud felt like cracking open the sky. The inner ring pulsed once; a high window hummed at the edge of hearing until I reined the pressure back in.\n\nElira could not look away, and I could not look away from her face. She looked neither small nor worshipful. She looked like Elira: shaken, thinking hard, refusing to let awe think for her.\n\nElira: \"It's real.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer breathing came controlled, though unsteady at the edges.\n\nElira: \"How can something like this exist?\"\n\nHaru: \"I don't fully know. I was born with it. I learned how to hide it before I learned what it was.\"\n\nShe drew one measured breath and steadied her shoulders.\n\nElira: \"May I come closer?\"\n\nEven now. Even staring at this. She asked.\n\nHaru: \"Yes. Slowly.\"\n\nShe advanced one pace at a time, a soft veil of golden light wrapping her like a quiet brace against the room's pressure, and halted right at the rim of the black ring.\n\nThe contrast between us cut like glass. Her mana was brilliant, warm, noble. Mine sat beneath comparison, older than rank.\n\nElira knelt slowly at the boundary, not in submission, simply trying to read the turning silver script without touching it.\n\nElira: \"It feels endless.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is.\"\n\nShe looked up, eyes shining with the sheer scale of what she was witnessing.\n\nElira: \"Earlier, when I said your mana felt deeper...\"\n\nHaru: \"You were right.\"\n\nHer breath trembled once despite her poise.\n\nElira: \"This is beyond Yellow.\"\n\nHaru: \"Far beyond.\"\n\nFor half a second I considered stopping there. It wasn't enough, not if she needed to understand why hiding had been survival.\n\nI lifted one hand. The Black Circle answered with no chant, no circle trace, no formal casting sequence. A single thread of darkness rose from the stone and hovered above my palm, fine as silk and deeper than any natural shadow. It twisted once.\n\nAir thickened across the archive. Moonlight sharpened into bright silver bars on the floor. Overhead lamps dimmed, not extinguished, only subdued, as if bowing to an older law.\n\nHaru: \"This is the smallest amount I can show safely without damaging the building.\"\n\nHer gaze jumped to my face.\n\nElira: \"The smallest?\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI shifted my eyes across the hall to an old wrought-iron bookstand anchored beside a stone pedestal. It weighed enough that two students would have struggled to shift it by hand.\n\nThe black thread drifted across the moonlight and touched the iron.\n\nNo crack. No flare. The bookstand simply stopped obeying ordinary rules. It lifted ten feet off the stone as though gravity had been quietly informed it no longer held jurisdiction there.\n\nElira inhaled sharply.\n\nWith a half-turn of my wrist, the iron rewrote itself without melting or breaking. Solid metal stretched into impossibly fine geometric ribs, unfolding into a suspended lattice of nested rings. Symbols none of the academy's libraries still recorded turned slowly inside the frame.\n\nA heartbeat later, I closed my fingers.\n\nThe lattice folded back into ordinary iron. The bookstand settled silently onto its exact original marks, leaving neither scratch nor dust disturbed.\n\nElira stared at the iron, then at me, then at the dark thread turning calmly above my palm.\n\nElira: \"You changed reality.\"\n\nHaru: \"A little.\"\n\nElira: \"Nobody should be able to say that sentence so calmly.\"\n\nHaru: \"I've had practice.\"\n\nI let the thread dissolve while the Black Circle continued turning slowly beneath my boots.\n\nHaru: \"Ordinary magic pushes, ignites, freezes, reinforces, or heals inside the world's rules. Black Circle negotiates with the rules directly.\"\n\nElira rose slowly to her feet, pale not from panic, but from a sharp mind racing to measure what she had just seen.\n\nElira: \"Then if you wanted to...\"\n\nShe stopped. I finished it for her.\n\nHaru: \"Yes. If I stopped choosing restraint, very little in this world could stop me from unmaking cities, armies, or institutions. Maybe more.\"\n\nThe silence afterward felt enormous.\n\nElira did not recoil. She did not step away. Instead a quiet, aching grief crossed her face: grief for what it meant to carry that alone.\n\nElira: \"You've lived with this by yourself.\"\n\nHaru: \"I had to.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nSudden heat sharpened her voice as she stepped half a pace closer to the circle's rim.\n\nElira: \"You were forced to. Those aren't the same thing.\"\n\nFor one heartbeat I could not answer. Anyone else would have asked what I could destroy, or how to leash me. Standing in moonlight before a power that could erase her without effort, Elira had seen the solitude first.\n\nHaru: \"You should be more afraid than you are.\"\n\nHer golden eyes held mine steadily.\n\nElira: \"Maybe.\" She took one small step nearer. \"I'm not afraid of you.\"\n\nThe Black Circle beneath my feet gave one slow, quiet pulse as if it had heard her. I dropped my gaze.\n\nHaru: \"You should at least be cautious.\"\n\nElira: \"I am.\"\n\nHaru: \"This doesn't look like caution.\"\n\nThe faintest smile touched the corner of her mouth.\n\nElira: \"It's a different kind.\"\n\nI had no answer for that, so I offered the only truth left that was not about destruction.\n\nI turned my palm upward again. Above my skin formed a dark sphere no wider than a flower bud, yet inside it tiny silver stars drifted and turned as if an entire night sky had been folded small enough to hold.\n\nElira's breath caught.\n\nHaru: \"The old fragments call Black Circle celestial for a reason.\"\n\nI released the sphere into the center of the room. Perception bent softly around us. The shelves melted into silhouette. The vaulted ceiling receded beyond sight. Moonlight multiplied until the room felt submerged in clear winter night, leaving us standing beneath a silent, endless sweep of drifting constellations.\n\nElira turned slowly inside the starlight, her golden Yellow glow mingling with the silver-black dusk until she looked less like an academy prodigy and more like someone stepped straight out of old myth.\n\nElira: \"It's beautiful.\"\n\nHaru: \"It's restrained.\"\n\nA quiet, disbelieving laugh slipped out of her.\n\nElira: \"If this is restraint, I don't want to imagine the alternative.\"\n\nHaru: \"You really don't.\"\n\nI closed my hand and let the stars fade. Shelves solidified. The ceiling settled back into stone. Ordinary moonlight returned, though the word *ordinary* no longer fit the room.\n\nElira stood motionless, her eyes fixed entirely on me.\n\nElira: \"You trusted me with this.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira: \"Why?\"\n\nI had expected to need time for that question. I didn't.\n\nHaru: \"Because you looked at me and saw a person before you saw a secret.\"\n\nSomething in her expression softened open, pure tenderness replacing awe. She stepped right to the edge of the black ring, close enough that I could hear her steady breath and see moonlight reflected in her eyes.\n\nElira: \"Haru...\"\n\nShe spoke my name like it mattered. I dimmed the circle beneath my boots until the pressure eased into a quiet, turning law.\n\nHaru: \"I never meant to tell anyone.\"\n\nElira: \"You told me.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer voice dropped low.\n\nElira: \"Then I'll carry it carefully.\"\n\nI believed her. Power could be controlled; threats could be answered. Trust rearranged a person from the inside out.\n\nElira lifted one hand halfway, then checked herself.\n\nElira: \"May I?\"\n\nAgain she asked.\n\nHaru: \"What?\"\n\nHer gaze dipped to the turning black boundary.\n\nElira: \"Touch the edge.\"\n\nFor a second I almost refused, not out of fear of what she would do, only fear of what letting her touch it meant. Still, she deserved more than distance.\n\nHaru: \"Just the visible ring.\"\n\nShe knelt once more and touched two fingertips to a black line turning across the stone.\n\nInstantly her whole frame went still. A pale ripple of gold raced up her hand in quiet resonance. The Black Circle neither pushed her away nor claimed her; it simply registered her with vast, cool neutrality. Her fingers trembled once before steadying. Her Yellow crest flared like a small sun answering night, then eased back under her breathing.\n\nElira closed her eyes.\n\nElira: \"It feels like standing at the edge of the sky.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe opened her eyes slowly, awe settling into something sharper and more personal.\n\nElira: \"And you walk through hallways hiding this under a Green wrist crest, acting like you're fine.\"\n\nHaru: \"I never said I was fine.\"\n\nElira: \"No. You didn't.\"\n\nShe stood again, close enough that the warmth of her gold mana brushed the cool stillness of mine.\n\nElira: \"Then hear me clearly. I don't think you're a mistake. I don't think you're a monster. I don't wish I had looked away.\"\n\nThe last word caught. Her eyes dropped briefly to the fading black ring between our shoes.\n\nElira: \"I am afraid.\"\n\nEvery defensive muscle in me braced. She saw it and frowned in sudden irritation.\n\nElira: \"Don't decide what I mean before I finish.\"\n\nHaru: \"Sorry.\"\n\nElira: \"You aren't.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nA quick breath of laughter escaped her, stripping away the polish and making the moment real enough to survive.\n\nElira: \"I'm afraid of what this power can do. I'm more afraid of what institutions will do if they learn it exists. And I'm afraid you've practiced disappearing so well that one day you'll call walking away protection.\"\n\nI had braced for revulsion. I had zero defense against accuracy.\n\nHaru: \"I might.\"\n\nPain flickered cleanly across her face.\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nThe black ring dimmed another shade. Neither of us pretended danger had stepped back.\n\nHaru: \"Do you regret knowing?\"\n\nShe took long enough to answer that I trusted the word when it came.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nRelief hit hard enough to nearly unlock my knees. She caught it.\n\nElira: \"No regret isn't the same as no fear.\"\n\nHaru: \"I understand.\"\n\nElira: \"You understand the sentence. Understanding me will take longer.\"\n\nI looked down at the pale floor.\n\nHaru: \"I don't know what you need from me now.\"\n\nElira: \"Neither do I.\"\n\nWe stood with the first honest problem between us that neither could solve for the other. After a quiet stretch, she placed one hand over her heart.\n\nElira: \"I can promise one thing tonight. I will keep this secret, not because a mana vow binds me, but because I choose to. If that choice ever changes, I tell you before I act.\"\n\nThat final condition scared me far more than a permanent chain would have. A permanent oath was a wall. This was a door she still owned.\n\nHaru: \"I want to ask you to promise it never changes.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"I won't.\"\n\nHer shoulders dropped by a fraction. I laid my palm over my chest.\n\nHaru: \"Then I promise I'll protect you from whatever follows me.\"\n\nHer expression hardened on the spot.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe rejection hit so sharply the fading black ring shivered.\n\nHaru: \"No?\"\n\nElira: \"You may promise to warn me. You may promise to listen. You may not promise to decide every cost before I'm allowed to see it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Even if it threatens your life?\"\n\nElira: \"There. You're doing it already.\"\n\nHeat flared in her voice. Not Yellow mana. Real temper.\n\nElira: \"You just trusted me with the truth and immediately started building a future where you suffer nobly alone and I stay safely grateful.\"\n\nHaru: \"That isn't what I meant.\"\n\nElira: \"It's what you reached for.\"\n\nDenying it would have been a lie.\n\nHaru: \"I don't know how to care about you without trying to stand between you and harm.\"\n\nHer anger softened, which somehow made the sting sharper.\n\nElira: \"Then learn the difference between standing between and standing beside.\"\n\nHaru: \"Tonight?\"\n\nShe glanced down at the fading black geometry beneath my boots, then out toward the moon.\n\nElira: \"No. Not tonight.\"\n\nThe argument remained unsettled. So did she.\n\nHaru: \"I'm going to make you something.\"\n\nElira blinked.\n\nElira: \"Something?\"\n\nHaru: \"Protection.\"\n\nHer eyes sharpened immediately.\n\nElira: \"Haru-\"\n\nHaru: \"Not because you're weak.\"\n\nShe stopped. I forced myself to finish plainly.\n\nHaru: \"Because this secret can become a route to you. I brought you near it. I need to make sure that doesn't become a wound someone else can open.\"\n\nHer expression gentled, without making the mistake of calling the worry foolish.\n\nElira: \"Then think carefully before you build it.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will.\"\n\nElira: \"And don't make it as an apology.\"\n\nThat landed close enough that I nearly looked away.\n\nHaru: \"Then what should I make it as?\"\n\nElira studied me for a quiet second.\n\nElira: \"A question.\"\n\nI did not fully understand yet, though the word lodged deep.\n\nI released the isolation veil one thread at a time until ordinary night quiet returned to the shelves. Distant academy bells chimed the late hour. I stepped back, and Elira let me.\n\nAt the tall oak doors, I paused.\n\nHaru: \"Good night, Elira.\"\n\nElira: \"Good night, Haru.\"\n\nI walked out before staying felt too easy. Behind me, Elira remained alone inside the pale foundation circle where the stars had floated, looking down at bare stone. The truth had vanished from sight, yet not from the room, and not from her.\n\n## Coda: A Route To Her\n\nThe pendant design refused to stay simple.\n\nI sketched three drafts back in my room after leaving the archive, and all three failed for the same reason: they protected too cleanly. A sealed ward was easy to admire and easier to abuse. It would let me pretend I had solved danger by placing my power between Elira and the world. That was not protection. That was a prettier way of deciding for her.\n\nThe fourth sketch looked worse. Uneven. Conditional. It left deliberate gaps no combat instructor would ever praise. Yet every gap could become a question she was allowed to answer for herself.\n\nWhen the Black Circle stirred behind my ribs, it did not object. That was new.\n\nOutside my window, late students argued over bracket odds on the training path, someone laughing at a Green Circle boy for dropping his guard angle. Ordinary noise. Ordinary cruelty.\n\nI pressed the stylus harder until fine silver dust coated my thumb.\n\nIf my secret had become a route to Elira, then the first law of the route had to be hers. Not mine.\n\n---\n\nThree wings away, in the dark corridor outside the Moonlit Archive Hall, the last dying tremor of Haru's lifted isolation veil brushed the carved mortar beneath the lintel.\n\nA hairline listening glyph hidden deep inside the stone flared once in silent overload, then burned itself out cleanly from the inside.\n\nThin residue of dry ink and scrubbed academic mana curled across the cold flagstones and faded, leaving behind a single faint word pressed into the dust where no ordinary eye would look:\n\n**SEEN.**\n\nAcross the silent courtyard, high in a lit research tower, a narrow silver ledger flipped open by itself on Professor Halden's desk. A dry nib scratched cleanly down the page: three distinct pulses of golden mana, followed by a blank, colorless line where the academy's oldest instruments had refused to record a rank at all.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-02-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep3","label":"Episode 3","number":"03","file":"episode-03-cover.webp","fallback":"A Quiet Humiliation","eyebrow":"Public Duel","recap":"","accent":["#2f80ed","#ff5a5f","#ffffff"],"displayLabel":"Episode 3","displayNumber":"03","displayEyebrow":"The Hidden Circle","arcLabel":"The Hidden Circle","title":"Rumors Under Sunlight","body":"Ordinary places were harder to trust after vows. On my eighth evening at Aurelis, I asked Elira to meet beneath the western garden lamps, not the archive, somewhere either of us could leave without feeling like the floor had an opinion.\n\nShe arrived in her academy uniform, hair loose over one shoulder, Yellow Circle crest dim at her collarbone. She looked at my hands before she looked at my face.\n\nElira: \"You made it.\"\n\nHaru: \"I said I would.\"\n\nElira: \"You also said protection.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is.\"\n\nI opened my palm.\n\nThe pendant rested there, black crystal threaded with silver depth. Small enough to pass as ornament. Quiet enough not to announce what lived inside it. The Black Circle within it was not awake, exactly. More like a door that knew my hand.\n\nElira did not reach for it immediately. That helped.\n\nElira: \"Did you make it as an apology?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nShe watched me with the same careful attention she had given the reveal.\n\nHaru: \"I made it as a question.\"\n\nHer expression softened.\n\nElira: \"And what is it asking?\"\n\nI looked at the pendant because looking at her made honesty less organized.\n\nHaru: \"Whether protection can stay protection if the person receiving it is allowed to refuse.\"\n\nFor a moment, wind moved through the garden leaves and neither of us spoke.\n\nThen Elira held out her hand, palm up. Answering rather than taking.\n\nElira: \"Then ask.\"\n\nHaru: \"Will you carry it?\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer palm stayed open under the lamplight, steady and unhurried. The word was quiet and complete.\n\nI set the pendant in her palm.\n\nOnly then did she touch it with her fingertips. The crystal warmed under her skin, recognizing the vow without tightening around it.\n\nElira: \"It feels like night under glass.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is... not inaccurate.\"\n\nShe smiled faintly.\n\nElira: \"You sound offended by poetry.\"\n\nHaru: \"Only when it is accurate before I am.\"\n\nThat drew the smallest laugh from her. She lifted the chain.\n\nI did not move to place it around her neck until she looked at me and nodded.\n\nEven then, I kept the motion careful. No brush of fingers against skin she had not invited. No turning a gift into a claim.\n\nThe pendant settled near her Yellow Circle crest, dark and quiet against the pale fabric of her uniform. Elira touched it once.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nHaru: \"If it ever activates fully, do not fight it.\"\n\nHer eyebrow lifted.\n\nHaru: \"That was not the whole point of giving it.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nHaru: \"But it is still important.\"\n\nShe considered that, then nodded.\n\nElira: \"Then I will not fight protection that remembers I am a person.\"\n\nThe pendant gave one restrained pulse: faint, but enough.\n\nWe stood beneath the western lamps with more distance between us than the Moonlit Archive had left and less than the world would have understood.\n\nThere was no kiss and no new vow spoken aloud, only a question accepted.\n\nBy morning, the rumors found us anyway.\n\nBefore they reached breakfast, I returned to the north library wing.\n\nThe academy was still in its pale early-morning quiet, the kind that made even old doors sound guilty. Moonlight had faded from the windows, but the archive corridor remembered it in cold silver lines along the floor.\n\nI stood where Elira and I had parted and let the false Green Circle at my wrist breathe just enough mana to read what ordinary detection spells would miss. Someone had been there.\n\nNot close enough to hear everything. My isolation veil had swallowed the dangerous parts. Still, close enough to notice pressure, light, and the shape of magic being hidden too carefully.\n\nA listening glyph had been burned out from the inside.\n\nThat bothered me more than finding it intact would have.\n\nAn intact spell could be studied. A destroyed spell meant the caster knew someone might come looking.\n\nI crouched and touched two fingers to the floor. A trace answered.\n\nDry ink.\nOld paper.\nA clean academic mana signature scrubbed almost bare.\n\nThen, beneath it, one word written in residue so thin it was probably meant for me alone.\n\n**SEEN.**\n\nThe word did not flare. It did not threaten.\n\nThat was what made it effective.\n\nThreats carried emotion: pride, fear, anger, intent sharpened enough to leave a flavor behind. This residue held none of that. It had the clinical quiet of someone placing a pin through an insect and labeling the wing pattern.\n\nI ran a second scan beneath the first and found the part the caster had tried hardest to erase.\n\nNo voice and no face, only a counting sequence.\n\nThree pulses of golden mana.\nOne suppression veil.\nOne impossible pressure spike so deeply muffled that an ordinary archive ward would have mistaken it for old foundation noise.\n\nThe glyph had not heard the truth.\n\nIt had heard the room struggle to keep the truth hidden.\n\nThe hidden Black Circle near my heart stirred once.\n\nI erased the trace completely and stood.\n\nBy the time the morning bell rang, the corridor looked innocent again.\n\nI did not feel innocent.\n\n---\n\nThe rumors began at breakfast.\n\nWhich, in Aurelis Arcane Academy, meant they had actually begun several hours earlier and simply reached full social visibility by morning.\n\nBy the time I entered the main dining hall, the atmosphere had already changed in a way that would have been invisible to anyone less used to being watched.\n\nConversations lowered half a degree when I passed. A few students looked at me too quickly and then away too slowly. Others were less disciplined and openly stared.\n\nNear the side entrance, two Green Circle students whispered over untouched breakfast. One of them had curly auburn hair and a worn notebook tucked beneath her elbow, the same Green Circle girl I had noticed lingering in the last row after Leon's match, watching the arena as if my victory had left a question behind.\n\nCurly-Haired Green Student: \"Maybe he just got lucky.\"\n\nStudent 2: \"With Leon or with Elira?\"\n\nCurly-Haired Green Student: \"Both, probably.\"\n\nShe said it carefully, not cruelly. Like someone testing the safer explanation before admitting she wanted another one to be true.\n\nBy the western tables, two Blue Circle second-years had completely failed to disguise the fact that I was the subject of their discussion.\n\nStudent 3: \"I'm telling you, I saw them leave the north wing together.\"\n\nStudent 4: \"At night?\"\n\nStudent 3: \"Obviously at night. That's why it matters.\"\n\nStudent 5: \"You don't just accidentally end up alone in the Moonlit Archive Hall with Elira Vale.\"\n\nStudent 4: \"Maybe she's recruiting him.\"\n\nStudent 3: \"Recruiting a Green?\"\n\nStudent 5: \"Or slumming. Either way, it's weird.\"\n\nNear the upper terrace, a Red Circle girl dismissed the entire discussion without looking away from her tea.\n\nStudent 6: \"Tournament politics. Elira Vale doesn't do anything by accident.\"\n\nI kept walking.\n\nThe tray in my hand remained level.\nMy expression remained neutral.\nMy internal peace, while not fully intact, was still functional.\n\nAt least until I spotted Aira.\n\nShe was sitting at a side table with two cups of tea and the exact expression of someone who had prepared herself spiritually for nonsense.\n\nAira: \"Good morning.\"\n\nHaru: \"That tone suggests it isn't.\"\n\nAira: \"It depends on your tolerance for public imagination.\"\n\nI sat across from her.\n\nHaru: \"How bad?\"\n\nAira folded her hands with theatrical calm.\n\nAira: \"Current theories include: you and Elira are secretly dating, unofficially engaged through some ancient mana custom, forming a cross-rank political alliance, or participating in a deeply suspicious research partnership.\"\n\nI blinked once.\n\nHaru: \"People need hobbies.\"\n\nAira stared at me.\n\nAira: \"That is your reaction?\"\n\nHaru: \"Should it be different?\"\n\nAira leaned forward.\n\nAira: \"Haru. Half the school thinks the quiet Green Circle transfer student somehow captured the attention of the most admired Yellow Circle girl in Aurelis. Do you understand how socially catastrophic this is for the academy hierarchy?\"\n\nHaru: \"I think the phrase 'socially catastrophic' may be excessive.\"\n\nAira: \"It's generous, actually.\"\n\nBefore I could answer, the room shifted in the familiar subtle way it always did when Elira entered.\n\nThe pressure wasn't oppressive. It was gravitational.\n\nI looked up.\n\nShe was walking through the dining hall with composed steps, academy mantle resting neatly over her shoulders, sunlight catching along the soft gold of her hair. At her collarbone, her Yellow Circle crest glowed as brightly as ever.\n\nAnd below it, hidden in plain sight beneath the appearance of ordinary enchantment, rested the black necklace I had made for her.\n\nMy gaze lingered half a second too long, and Aira caught it.\n\nAira: \"There it is again.\"\n\nHaru: \"What?\"\n\nAira: \"That exact expression.\"\n\nHaru: \"I have no idea what you mean.\"\n\nAira: \"You really should stop saying that to people who can observe.\"\n\nElira reached our table and looked between us, already aware of the atmosphere around the hall.\n\nElira: \"Good morning.\"\n\nAira: \"That depends on how much you enjoy being the center of a very dramatic school myth.\"\n\nElira sat down beside me as if the choice were perfectly ordinary.\n\nWhich, for her, made it more effective.\n\nElira: \"I've heard some of it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Only some?\"\n\nElira: \"Enough to know people are very committed to fiction when denied facts.\"\n\nAira pointed at both of us.\n\nAira: \"You are both being much too calm about this.\"\n\nElira looked genuinely curious.\n\nElira: \"Should we be upset?\"\n\nAira: \"Not upset. At least embarrassed.\"\n\nElira glanced at me, then back at Aira.\n\nElira: \"I don't feel especially embarrassed.\"\n\nAira turned slowly toward me.\n\nAira: \"And you?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nAira leaned back in her chair as if she had just been personally defeated by composure.\n\nAira: \"Unbelievable.\"\n\nElira's lips curved faintly.\n\nElira: \"If people want to talk, they will talk. I don't see much value in giving them fear as well.\"\n\nHaru: \"Agreed.\"\n\nAira looked between us in mounting disbelief.\n\nAira: \"You agreed too quickly.\"\n\nHaru: \"It was a simple point.\"\n\nAira: \"That is not what I mean.\"\n\nElira lowered her gaze briefly to her tea, hiding a small smile.\n\nI chose not to comment. That was strategically wise. Probably.\n\nAround us, the dining hall's attempt at pretending not to observe had now failed completely. Students from three separate tables were openly listening.\n\nOne of the Blue Circle boys near the far column whispered too loudly.\n\nStudent 4: \"They're not even denying it.\"\n\nStudent 5: \"Why would they deny it if it's true?\"\n\nStudent 6: \"No, no, this could still be some terrifyingly calm misunderstanding.\"\n\nAira closed her eyes for a second.\n\nAira: \"I would like it officially recorded that I predicted all of this.\"\n\nElira: \"Repeatedly.\"\n\nAira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nThen the academy bells rang.\n\nConversation shifted reluctantly as students gathered their things and prepared to move toward morning assembly.\n\nBefore we could stand, a silver announcement glyph lit up above the hall.\n\n**MANDATORY ASSEMBLY - GRAND AUDITORIUM**  \n**ALL YEAR DIVISIONS**\n\nThe atmosphere changed instantly.\n\nA mandatory full-school assembly in the middle of the week meant only one of two things.\n\nDisaster.\nOr something prestigious enough that the academy wanted the entire student body present for the announcement.\n\nGiven Aurelis, the second was more likely.\n\nAira rose with her tray.\n\nAira: \"Well. I'm sure this will be normal.\"\n\nHaru: \"You don't sound convinced.\"\n\nAira: \"Because I know where I study.\"\n\n---\n\nThe Grand Auditorium of Aurelis Arcane Academy was designed less like a school hall and more like a ceremonial chamber.\n\nHigh vaulted ceilings held floating banners of the academy crest. A central stage of white stone and rune-etched black glass stood beneath a suspended light formation shaped like interlocking circles. Tiered seating extended outward in arcs, filling rapidly with students and instructors.\n\nBy the time our class found seats, the entire hall was buzzing.\n\nAt the front stood Headmaster Caelum Veris, a severe Red Circle scholar with silver hair, layered ceremonial robes, and the composed expression of a man who believed institutions should always look as if they had planned the future in advance.\n\nBeside him stood several senior faculty, including Professor Halden.\n\nI noticed him immediately.\n\nTall, austere, older than most of the other researchers, his gaze sharp behind thin-framed lenses. He wore the insignia of advanced mana studies and the expression of someone whose interest in the world was rarely kind.\n\nHis eyes moved briefly across the student body. Then, for the smallest moment, paused on me.\n\nNot enough for anyone else to notice. Enough for me.\n\nI kept my expression neutral.\n\nElira, seated on my other side this time due to the arrangement of our row, sensed the shift in my posture.\n\nShe did not look at me. She did not draw attention. She simply lowered one hand to the edge of her seat, where our sleeves nearly touched, and let her fingers rest there in silent reassurance.\n\nPrivate. Invisible to everyone except me.\n\nIt helped more than I wanted it to.\n\nThe Headmaster raised one hand, and the hall fell silent.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"Students of Aurelis Arcane Academy, today we announce an event of considerable significance. As many of you know, once every three years, the continent's leading magical institutions gather for the **Inter-National Schools Tournament**.\"\n\nThe room erupted instantly.\n\nExcitement broke across the hall in a wave. Students sat forward. Instructors exchanged measured looks. Even the more jaded upper years looked interested now.\n\nThe Headmaster waited for silence again.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"This tournament is not merely ceremonial. It is the highest competitive proving ground for elite magical academies across participating nations. Schools will compete across multiple categories of skill, tactical application, magical theory, team coordination, and formal combat.\"\n\nA large projection bloomed above the stage.\n\nNames of famous institutions appeared one after another in floating light.\n\n**Aurelis Arcane Academy**  \n**Valemere Royal Institute**  \n**Noctis Dominion College**  \n**Sol Aeternum Conservatory**  \n**Drakenshard Military Academy**  \n...and more.\n\nThe entire hall was alert now.\n\nWhen Noctis appeared, the room did not become colder. It only felt that way.\n\nStudents who knew the name shifted in their seats with a tension too practiced to be ordinary rivalry.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"The winning institution will be recognized as the premier school of magical excellence for the next tournament cycle.\"\n\nAira leaned toward me at once.\n\nAira: \"There it is.\"\n\nHaru: \"There is what?\"\n\nAira: \"The next terrible thing that will happen to our peace.\"\n\nI considered that.\n\nHaru: \"You may be right.\"\n\nHeadmaster Veris continued.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"Participation is selective. Not every student will represent Aurelis. Competitors will be chosen based on demonstrated ability, composure, academic standing, and strategic suitability. Internal qualification rounds begin immediately.\"\n\nNow the reaction sharpened. This was no abstract announcement; it was a challenge.\n\nStudents all around us were already calculating: rivals, prestige, advancement, visibility, political capital.\n\nLeon Varek, seated several rows ahead, straightened at once with the posture of someone trying to rebuild a public identity through opportunity.\n\nSeveral Red Circle students exchanged focused glances. Blue Circle aspirants sat visibly taller. Even some Greens looked caught between fear and hope.\n\nThe projection changed again, showing the tournament structure.\n\n**Phase 1: Internal Selection**  \n**Phase 2: Delegation Training**  \n**Phase 3: Inter-School Competition**\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"This year, the tournament will be hosted at **Aurelis Arcane Academy** itself.\"\n\nThe hall nearly exploded. That changed everything.\n\nInternational teams would be coming here: their strongest students, their proudest heirs, their champions.\n\nThe eyes of the magical world would be on Aurelis.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"We expect discipline, excellence, and conduct worthy of this institution.\"\n\nAira whispered beside me.\n\nAira: \"So naturally we're going to get arrogance, sabotage, and at least three near-political incidents.\"\n\nElira's shoulders shook once with quiet laughter.\n\nHaru: \"Only three?\"\n\nAira: \"I'm being hopeful.\"\n\nThe Headmaster lifted his hand again.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"Further details will be distributed by class division. Students with qualifying potential will receive direct review notices by the end of the day.\"\n\nThat sentence sent a visible current through the room. Review notices: invitations to prove themselves.\n\nThe assembly ended amid a storm of excitement.\n\nGroups formed instantly across the auditorium aisles as students began speculating, boasting, worrying, and already comparing likely representatives.\n\nAs our row began to empty, Aira looked between me and Elira.\n\nAira: \"I already dislike this tournament and it hasn't even started.\"\n\nElira: \"That seems premature.\"\n\nAira: \"No, it seems experienced.\"\n\nShe turned to me.\n\nAira: \"And you are absolutely going to get selected, which means I'll have to watch stronger and stronger people fail to understand why underestimating you is a bad life decision.\"\n\nHaru: \"That's assuming I participate.\"\n\nAira stared at me.\n\nAira: \"You won an official combat ladder match by dismantling Leon Varek in front of half the academy. Aurelis will drag you into this with administrative elegance if necessary.\"\n\nShe was probably right.\n\nWhich made the day less peaceful than I had hoped.\n\n---\n\nBy midday the campus had transformed.\n\nThe tournament announcement gave the academy a fresh, electric tension. Training arenas filled faster than usual. Students who normally drifted through routine now walked with sharper purpose. Every hallway conversation seemed to circle back to selection odds, team composition, or rival schools.\n\nAnd everywhere Elira and I went, the rumors followed.\n\nNot maliciously, at least not always. Mostly with fascination.\n\nWhen we crossed the central courtyard together after class, a pair of Red Circle girls near the fountain lowered their voices too late.\n\nStudent 7: \"They look comfortable.\"\n\nStudent 8: \"That's what confirms it for me.\"\n\nStudent 7: \"The dating?\"\n\nStudent 8: \"No, the terrifying mutual calm.\"\n\nElira heard them. I heard them. Neither of us reacted.\n\nAfter a few steps, she glanced at me.\n\nElira: \"Should we be more subtle?\"\n\nHaru: \"Would that help?\"\n\nElira considered it.\n\nElira: \"Probably not.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then no.\"\n\nThat earned me a smile, the kind that had become much harder to ignore since the library.\n\nWe walked a little farther in comfortable silence before her hand brushed mine once.\n\nHabit almost made me pull away before anyone could see. I did not.\n\nInstead, I let my fingers close around hers in the middle of the courtyard, where the academy could notice and invent whatever explanation helped it sleep.\n\nElira: \"Does it really not bother you?\"\n\nHaru: \"The rumors?\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer gaze stayed steady on mine, waiting for an honest answer.\n\nI thought about it honestly.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nShe watched me for a second.\n\nElira: \"Why?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because the part that matters is true enough for the talking not to matter much.\"\n\nThat made her stop walking. Completely.\n\nI stopped too and looked back at her.\n\nFor once, Elira seemed caught off guard. Her expression was soft, surprised, and very slightly flushed beneath the afternoon light.\n\nElira: \"You can't just say things like that in the middle of the courtyard.\"\n\nHaru: \"Why not?\"\n\nElira: \"Because I have a reputation for composure.\"\n\nI looked around at the students pretending not to notice us.\n\nHaru: \"You still do.\"\n\nShe laughed under her breath and resumed walking, though a brightness stayed in her expression for the rest of the path.\n\nElira: \"You are becoming unexpectedly difficult.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm told I was always like this.\"\n\nElira: \"By Aira?\"\n\nHaru: \"Reliable source.\"\n\n---\n\nThe qualification notices arrived before the final afternoon bell.\n\nGolden slips of rune-encoded paper manifested at the desks of students selected for internal review.\n\nMine appeared with all the subtlety of fate. So did Elira's.\n\nAnd Aira's, to her visible alarm.\n\nAira looked from her notice to mine and then up toward the ceiling as if appealing to the structure of reality for administrative mercy.\n\nAira: \"Absolutely not.\"\n\nInstructor Dren, who had clearly expected this, folded his arms.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Your complaints have been noted and ignored.\"\n\nAira: \"I'm not even a combat specialist.\"\n\nInstructor Dren: \"The tournament includes tactical and support disciplines.\"\n\nAira: \"That's worse. That means I'm useful.\"\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Congratulations on your self-awareness.\"\n\nA few students laughed.\n\nLeon received a notice too.\n\nHe glanced briefly at mine, jaw tightening only slightly this time. The public humiliation of our last match had not disappeared, but it had matured into something colder. Less theatrical. More competitive.\n\nThat was probably more dangerous.\n\nNear the back of the classroom, the curly-haired Green Circle student from breakfast waited until the last notice had finished unfolding.\n\nBy then I had heard someone call her Mira Castellan.\n\nNothing appeared above her desk.\n\nThe Green Circle student beside her winced.\n\nGreen Student: \"I'm sorry.\"\n\nMira smoothed one thumb over the edge of her worn notebook.\n\nMira: \"It's fine. I didn't expect it.\"\n\nHer eyes moved to my notice, then to the way several students stared at it as if a Green Circle name on gold paper had made the room less stable.\n\nHer expression did not turn bitter. That made it harder to ignore.\n\nShe lowered her voice until only the desk beside her could hear.\n\nMira: \"But maybe next time.\"\n\nAfter class, the selected students were directed to the central strategy chamber for orientation.\n\nThe room was circular, lined with projection crystals and old banners from past Aurelis tournament victories. More than thirty students had been called from different divisions: Reds, Blues, a few Greens. At the front of the chamber, a projection displayed the event categories.\n\n**Formal Duel**  \n**Team Battle**  \n**Mana Control Exhibition**  \n**Tactical Simulation**  \n**Arcane Theory Relay**  \n**Adaptive Field Trial**\n\nAurelis was not just selecting strong students. It was selecting a symbol of itself.\n\nProfessor Halden addressed the room this time.\n\nHis voice was smooth, intelligent, and far too interested in how people reacted when he spoke.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Skill without discipline is noise. Raw mana without judgment is waste. This tournament rewards complete magicians, not merely powerful ones.\"\n\nHis gaze moved across the room, paused on Elira with formal approval, passed across several Red Circle heirs with expectation, and then found me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"And occasionally, it rewards anomalies.\"\n\nA few students shifted, unsure whether that was a compliment.\n\nI understood it as neither. Just attention, the kind I did not want.\n\nThen the orientation crystal at the center of the chamber flickered. Only once.\n\nMost students were already turning toward the doors, too busy with excitement and dread to notice. But I saw the projection stutter beneath the official category list, revealing a second layer of text for less than a heartbeat.\n\n**ANOMALY REVIEW: HARU AERLEN**  \n**COMPATIBILITY PRESSURE POINT: ELIRA VALE**  \n**OBSERVATION PRIORITY: ELEVATED**\n\nThat was not a glitch. Glitches did not choose names.\n\nThe hidden layer vanished before anyone could react.\n\nProfessor Halden was no longer looking at the projection. He was looking at me.\n\nElira's sleeve brushed mine, the smallest possible question.\n\nI gave the smallest possible answer. Not here.\n\nWe left the chamber with the rest of the selected students, but the orientation did not end when the doors closed behind us. It followed in fragments.\n\nA Red Circle candidate loudly wondering why a Green transfer had drawn faculty language normally reserved for experimental formations. A Blue Circle upper-year pretending not to stare at Elira's pendant. Two support students debating whether \"pressure point\" meant romantic leverage, tactical pairing, or both.\n\nAira caught up with us at the corridor turn and immediately slowed when she saw our faces.\n\nAira: \"That is not your normal quiet.\"\n\nHaru: \"I have several.\"\n\nAira: \"Yes, and this is the one that makes me want to move furniture in front of doors.\"\n\nElira's expression stayed composed, but her voice lowered.\n\nElira: \"Something was hidden in the projection.\"\n\nAira did not ask whether we were sure. That was one of the reasons she was dangerous in the useful way.\n\nAira: \"How hidden?\"\n\nHaru: \"Hidden from students. Not necessarily from faculty.\"\n\nAira's eyes shifted toward the strategy chamber doors behind us.\n\nAira: \"Halden?\"\n\nHaru: \"At least Halden.\"\n\nFor once, Aira did not make a joke immediately. She looked down at the folded qualification slip in her hand as if the paper had become heavier.\n\nAira: \"Then the tournament is not just a tournament for us.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe corridor crystal beside us chimed. Not the bright tone used for class notices. Lower. Formal. Administrative.\n\nA new announcement unfolded across the glass in clean silver script.\n\n**TOURNAMENT SECURITY NOTICE**\n\n**ALL SELECTED CANDIDATES WILL UNDERGO ADDITIONAL MANA SIGNATURE INDEXING BEFORE FINAL TEAM ASSIGNMENT.**\n\n**ANOMALOUS READINGS WILL BE REFERRED TO FACULTY REVIEW.**\n\nSeveral students passing behind us cheered as if the words meant prestige. To them, extra indexing sounded like one more ceremony, one more proof that they had become important enough for the academy to measure carefully.\n\nTo me, it felt like a net being described as ribbon.\n\nAira read the notice twice.\n\nAira: \"Anomalous readings.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nAira: \"That is a very unfriendly phrase.\"\n\nElira's fingers brushed the hidden pendant beneath her collar, not enough for anyone else to notice.\n\nElira: \"It gives faculty permission to ask questions without saying what they are searching for.\"\n\nBehind us, two Red Circle candidates laughed about who would register highest under combat pressure. A Blue support specialist wondered aloud whether high sensitivity would improve selection chances. No one sounded afraid. That made it worse.\n\nSystems were easiest to obey when they arrived dressed as opportunity.\n\nThe crystal chimed again.\n\nThis time the notice added a second line, smaller than the first.\n\n**OBSERVATION WINDOWS MAY OPEN DURING LIVE EVENTS.**\n\nAira's mouth flattened.\n\nAira: \"Observation windows.\"\n\nHaru: \"Faculty monitoring.\"\n\nAira: \"That was the optimistic translation. I was asking for the terrifying one.\"\n\nElira looked toward the strategy chamber doors.\n\nElira: \"Pressure.\"\n\nThe word settled between us.\n\nPressure during events. Pressure during indexing. Pressure while students fought, rescued, failed, panicked, protected, and revealed what their bodies knew before their mouths could lie.\n\nFor most candidates, that meant performance analysis. For me, it meant every instinct would have an audience. And for Elira, standing beside me with a hidden Black Circle protection around her throat, it meant someone had already noticed where to press.\n\nI let the false Green Circle at my wrist settle into the harmless rhythm I had practiced for years: weak enough to be dismissed, stable enough to avoid pity, ordinary enough to survive inspection.\n\nThe problem was that ordinary had become difficult to maintain around people I wanted to protect.\n\nI looked through the corridor windows toward the bright campus outside, where students were already turning selection notices into celebration, rivalry, and ambition.\n\nTo them, the world had become larger. To me, it had become narrower.\n\nEvery road now seemed to lead through an arena, an archive, or a room where someone like Professor Halden would ask the right question in the wrong way.\n\nFor the first time since the rumors began, they stopped feeling harmless.\n\nBecause affection was no longer only gossip. Someone had started measuring it.\n\n---\n\nTwo days after the initial qualification notices, the real selection process began.\n\nAurelis Arcane Academy felt less like a school and more like a kingdom preparing for war while pretending it was education.\n\nTraining grounds filled before sunrise. Strategy rooms stayed occupied between classes. Students who had not qualified watched those who had with mixtures of admiration, resentment, relief, and private calculation. Every corridor carried the same tension: who would represent Aurelis, who would fail, and who would embarrass themselves before the visiting academies arrived.\n\nThe rumors about Elira and me hadn't vanished, either, though they had shifted from scandal into background weather. By the time we walked into the central strategy hall, Aira had stopped cataloging new theories and settled for glaring at anyone who slowed down near our shoulders.\n\nThe room was broader than a normal classroom, circular and tiered, with floating screens displaying tournament categories, prior Aurelis performance rankings, and projected team structures. Faculty lined the outer wall with clipboards, crystal tablets, and expressions ranging from professional interest to ruthless evaluation.\n\nProfessor Halden stood at the center.\n\nSo did Instructor Dren, along with two senior examiners: Lady Seraphine Morn of tactical studies, a severe Red Circle strategist with unnervingly precise diction, and Master Ilyas Thorn, a veteran Blue Circle combat instructor whose face looked carved from endurance and disappointment.\n\nProfessor Halden raised one hand, and the low conversation faded.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Today begins the internal selection phase. Not all of you will remain in contention. Some will be sorted into support categories. Some into alternate reserves. A select few will become Aurelis' principal delegation.\"\n\nA display opened behind him.\n\n**SELECTION PHASE - DAY ONE**  \n**INDIVIDUAL EVALUATION**  \n**TEAM POTENTIAL MAPPING**  \n**ADAPTIVE COMPATIBILITY SCORING**\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Your first evaluations will not be public duels.\"\n\nThat disappointed several students immediately. Professor Halden noticed and did not care.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"This tournament is won by institutions, not vanity. We are not selecting the loudest students. We are selecting the most effective.\"\n\nAt the edge of the room, Leon stood a little straighter at that, perhaps trying to signal that he, too, now valued effectiveness over spectacle. I doubted the transformation was complete.\n\nLady Seraphine stepped forward.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"You will be assessed in three stages today. First, mana control precision. Second, tactical responsiveness under altered conditions. Third, provisional team integration. Every score matters.\"\n\nAira leaned slightly toward me.\n\nAira: \"I dislike every phrase she just used.\"\n\nHaru: \"You dislike competence when it's pointed at you.\"\n\nAira: \"That is a hostile interpretation of my personality.\"\n\nElira, on my other side, lowered her voice just enough for only us to hear.\n\nElira: \"It's also accurate.\"\n\nAira gave her a wounded look.\n\nAira: \"You're supposed to be kind.\"\n\nElira: \"I am. That was very gentle.\"\n\nThe first evaluation took place in the **Lattice Chamber**, a long, white training hall divided by suspended crystal nodes. Each student stood on a marked platform while mana-sensitive targets floated through shifting patterns around them. The objective was not power, but exactness.\n\nA student had to strike, redirect, shield, or stabilize the targets according to rapidly changing instructions projected above the chamber.\n\nToo much force cost points.\nToo slow a reaction cost points.\nThe wrong spell choice cost points.\n\nThis was the kind of test that embarrassed prodigies who relied on rank alone.\n\nOne by one, students were called.\n\nA Red Circle spear specialist overcommitted and shattered a target meant to be preserved.\nA Blue Circle earth user reacted too slowly to an inversion sequence.\nA Green Circle support caster performed unexpectedly well by compensating for lower output with flawless timing.\n\nThe examiners noted everything.\n\nWhen Aira's turn came, she walked to the platform like someone approaching an unnecessary administrative betrayal.\n\nAira: \"If this goes badly, I want both of you to remember me kindly.\"\n\nHaru: \"That feels dramatic.\"\n\nAira: \"That's because I'm under pressure.\"\n\nElira: \"You'll do well.\"\n\nAira glanced at her.\n\nAira: \"If you say encouraging things in that voice, it becomes very difficult to fail with dignity.\"\n\nShe stepped onto the platform.\n\nThen, to the surprise of several nearby students, she became completely different.\n\nThe uncertainty vanished. The complaints vanished. The humor thinned into focus.\n\nBlue mana gathered around her hands in crisp, efficient lines. Her specialty was response architecture: small-scale water and air shaping, support geometry, redirection, barrier layering, and tactical adaptation.\n\nThe projection flashed.\n\n**INTERCEPT**\n\nThree targets shot forward at staggered angles.\n\nAira moved immediately. A thin ribbon of water mana snapped from her hand, altering the first target's trajectory without damage. A compressed air pulse slowed the second. A rotating micro-barrier caught the third and lowered it gently into the stabilizing field.\n\n**CONTAIN**\n\nShe sealed a destabilized crystal in a transparent water shell without cracking it.\n\n**REVERSE**\n\nShe redirected an incoming force wave back through a rotating prism array and neutralized it before it touched the platform edge.\n\nBy the end of the sequence, the chamber was quiet.\n\nAira exhaled and stepped off the platform with visible relief.\n\nMaster Thorn looked down at his score slate.\n\nMaster Thorn: \"Excellent support reflexes. High composure under layered instruction. Efficient mana expenditure.\"\n\nAira blinked.\n\nAira: \"That was suspiciously close to praise.\"\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Do not become dependent on it.\"\n\nAira: \"There it is.\"\n\nShe returned to us with the deeply offended dignity of someone who had done very well against her preference.\n\nHaru: \"You were strong.\"\n\nAira: \"I understand. It's upsetting.\"\n\nElira smiled.\n\nElira: \"You're wasted on false modesty.\"\n\nAira: \"That wasn't modesty. It was despair management.\"\n\nElira's turn came not long after.\n\nNo one pretended not to watch.\n\nThe chamber itself seemed to sharpen around her the moment she stepped onto the platform. Her Yellow Circle crest glowed at her collarbone, and the black necklace below it remained quiet, hidden, and known only to us.\n\nThe projection flashed rapidly.\n\n**INTERCEPT**  \n**SPLIT**  \n**STABILIZE**  \n**REINFORCE**  \n**NULL OVERFLOW**\n\nElira tried to hold all five instructions as one thought.\n\nGold-white light divided cleanly across the first four targets. She redirected two, split a third, and built a reinforcement lattice around the unstable node before its descent completed. Too early. The node changed frequency.\n\nHer beautiful lattice became the wrong shape around it.\n\nThe overflow struck one rigid corner and rebounded through the platform. A target crystal jumped its rail. Elira cut three finished spells at once to catch it before it hit the floor. The crystal survived.\n\nThe stabilization ring did not.\n\nRed failure light crossed one section of the platform.\n\nElira stood inside the remains of her own geometry, breathing hard and looking personally betrayed by anticipation.\n\nMaster Thorn: \"Continue.\"\n\nElira exhaled once and cast again.\n\nThis time she waited for the node to answer before deciding what it meant. Her next lattice was less elegant and more accurate. The remaining targets settled without collision, though one harmonic indicator stayed yellow instead of green.\n\nSilence held for one beat. Then the applause came anyway, smaller and more honest.\n\nProfessor Halden's score notation moved.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Exceptional recovery.\"\n\nMaster Thorn: \"High control. One anticipatory error. One unresolved harmonic condition.\"\n\nElira stepped down with her jaw tight.\n\nAira: \"You saved the crystal.\"\n\nElira: \"I trapped the overflow before it existed.\"\n\nHaru: \"You guessed the standard frequency.\"\n\nHer gold eyes cut toward me. For one second I thought I had made it worse. Then she exhaled.\n\nElira: \"Yes. I was solving the instruction I expected.\"\n\nAira: \"I would offer comfort, but you look like you intend to duel the concept of premature inference.\"\n\nElira: \"It knows what it did.\"\n\nThen it was my turn.\n\nThe chamber grew quieter in a different way: expectation, curiosity, and a little suspicion.\n\nThe Green Circle transfer had already disrupted one hierarchy and dismantled Leon in public. Now everyone wanted to know whether that had been context, luck, or something stranger.\n\nI stepped onto the platform.\n\nMy false Green Circle crest stayed steady at my wrist beneath my cuff. My real power remained buried.\n\nThe projection flashed.\n\n**INTERCEPT**\n\nSimple enough.\n\nI used the minimum output required, redirecting the first target with reinforcement and angle rather than force.\n\n**CONTAIN**\n\nA weak-looking shield formed around the crystal. Deliberately weak-looking. It held perfectly.\n\n**NULL OVERFLOW**\n\nA destabilized node descended too quickly for most Greens to manage cleanly. I compressed the mana field around it just enough to neutralize the cascade without revealing how precise the compression truly was.\n\nThe sequence escalated.\n\n**SPLIT**  \n**REVERSE**  \n**REINFORCE**\n\nI kept the work good enough to be undeniable and ordinary enough to survive inspection.\n\nThen something moved behind the instructions. Not mana. Metal.\n\nOne of the upper guide anchors had cracked where old stress hid beneath fresh polish. A suspended silver weight shifted above the junior Green assistant resetting targets at the platform edge.\n\nThe evaluation system did not mark it. The weight was equipment, not threat.\n\nI saw the fracture before it made sound. That was the problem.\n\nIf I moved now, Halden would know I had perceived what the instruments had not. If I waited until the crack became visible, the assistant might not move fast enough.\n\nThe Black Circle offered a thousand quiet corrections. I chose one.\n\nA thread narrower than the false Green crest at my wrist should have been able to form passed through the guide rail. I did not stop the weight. I changed its fall by the width of two fingers. The anchor snapped.\n\nSilver struck the platform beside the assistant instead of his shoulder.\n\nHe stumbled backward. My active shield collapsed because I had taken attention from it. The contained crystal rolled free and stopped against the safety rim.\n\nEvery evaluation light went red. No one applauded. Good.\n\nMaster Thorn crossed the barrier first.\n\nMaster Thorn: \"Injury?\"\n\nThe assistant looked at the fallen weight, then at his own hands.\n\nAssistant: \"No.\"\n\nLady Seraphine: \"That anchor was outside the active sequence.\"\n\nHaru: \"It was falling.\"\n\nAssistant: \"I did not see it until it moved.\"\n\nNeither had the instruments.\n\nProfessor Halden crouched beside the broken metal. He ran one finger along the old fracture and then looked at the silver weight. Its impact mark sat two fingers away from the path gravity should have given it. He looked at me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Mr. Aerlen, what did you cast?\"\n\nThe easy lie arrived first. Reflex. Luck. A Green reinforcement thread catching the rail after the crack.\n\nElira stood beyond the barrier with the expression of someone who had just failed because she acted before evidence and knew exactly what my silence was trying to do.\n\nHaru: \"A correction thread.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Before or after the anchor broke?\"\n\nI could feel every student listening.\n\nHaru: \"Before I heard it break.\"\n\nHalden's face became very still.\n\nMaster Thorn checked the assistant's shoulder and then the shattered shield.\n\nMaster Thorn: \"Safety intervention accepted. Active containment condition failed. Efficiency score remains high. Completion score reduced.\"\n\nThe junior assistant found his voice.\n\nAssistant: \"Reduced? He stopped that from hitting me.\"\n\nMaster Thorn looked at him.\n\nAssistant: \"Sorry. I know I am not scoring.\"\n\nMaster Thorn: \"You are correct anyway. Equipment failure is entered separately. Mr. Aerlen's choice still broke the assigned condition. Both facts remain.\"\n\nThe answer did not make me hero or failure. I distrusted it less for that.\n\nLady Seraphine studied the faint green crest at my wrist.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Your mana economy remains inconsistent with your displayed reserve profile.\"\n\nHaru: \"I focus on control.\"\n\nProfessor Halden rose with the broken anchor in his hand.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Clearly.\"\n\nHis tone gave away nothing. Which made it worse.\n\nI stepped off the platform.\n\nAira: \"That was less subtle than you think.\"\n\nHaru: \"Someone was under it.\"\n\nAira's expression lost its humor.\n\nAira: \"I know.\"\n\nElira looked at me quietly.\n\nElira: \"You chose before the room gave you permission.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira glanced back at her unresolved harmonic light.\n\nElira: \"So did I.\"\n\nThe difference between our choices stood on the platform rubbing an uninjured shoulder. Neither of us pretended that erased the question Halden had begun asking.\n\n---\n\nThe second stage was worse, not because it was combat-heavy, but because it was psychological.\n\nThe **Adaptive Responsiveness Hall** was a shifting arena of illusion screens, partial barriers, environmental distortion fields, and randomized threat constructs. Students were sent in alone and forced to react to unstable conditions with limited information.\n\nFog. Darkness. False allies. Delayed spell response. Enemy decoys. Mana interference. Changing terrain.\n\nIn other words: panic, professionally organized.\n\nAira's description, not mine. She said it while staring at the entrance gate before her turn.\n\nAira: \"This is panic, professionally organized.\"\n\nHaru: \"That's a strong summary.\"\n\nAira: \"If I don't come back, use my notes respectfully.\"\n\nElira: \"You'll come back.\"\n\nAira: \"Yes, but perhaps changed.\"\n\nShe did, in fact, come back: sweaty, irritated, and carrying one of the better times of the rotation.\n\nAira: \"I dislike being competent in stressful environments. It rewards the wrong parts of my personality.\"\n\nElira went in next and emerged with the fastest completion time recorded so far. No one was surprised.\n\nThe truly notable result came from a Red Circle heir named **Cassian Veyr**, whose fire-affinity output was among the highest in the candidate pool but whose tactical patience was approximately nonexistent. He blasted through the course in spectacular fashion, earning both admiration and criticism.\n\nThat mattered. This tournament would need symbols, yes, but not unstable ones.\n\nMy own run was uneventful externally and irritating internally.\n\nThe illusion constructs were too easy to read. The decoys lacked weight in their mana signatures. The trap placements followed logical compression patterns.\n\nI completed the course with a high score, careful not to break the evaluation by being faster than people would accept from a Green.\n\nThat still placed me near the top, which did not improve my anonymity.\n\nBy the time the afternoon phase ended, students were exhausted, overstimulated, and fully aware that Aurelis intended to build a tournament team capable not just of competing, but dominating.\n\nThe final exercise of the day was announced just before dismissal.\n\n**Provisional Team Integration Mapping**\n\nThat got everyone's attention back immediately.\n\nNames began to appear in groups on the suspended projection: temporary squads, mixed-rank combinations, different affinities, balance testing.\n\nAira squinted at the screen.\n\nAira: \"Please let this be reasonable.\"\n\nIt wasn't. Our names appeared together.\n\n**TEAM CANDIDATE GROUP THREE**  \n**ELIRA VALE**  \n**AIRA SOLEN**  \n**CASSIAN VEYR**  \n**HARU AERLEN**  \n**LEON VAREK**\n\nAira stared at the projection in numb disbelief.\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nElira read it once, then again.\n\nElira: \"That is... aggressive.\"\n\nHaru: \"Strategically or emotionally?\"\n\nAira turned to me.\n\nAira: \"Yes.\"\n\nAcross the chamber, Leon had also seen the assignment. His expression froze for a second before flattening into something controlled and difficult.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Leon - Reclassification\n\nLeon Varek did not admit he was wrong about people. He adjusted. That was different.\n\nWrong implied carelessness. Leon was not careless. He made fast assessments, corrected for new data, and moved forward before anyone could turn the correction into confession.\n\nHaru Aerlen had become inconvenient data. Green Circle. Transfer. Quiet. Too calm in the arena. Too controlled when Leon pushed.\n\nHis visible crest said he lacked the output to challenge properly, yet every instinct Leon had left after losing in public refused to dismiss him. Haru was neither naive enough to need unsolicited protection nor arrogant enough to hate cleanly.\n\nThe categories did not hold. Leon disliked that more than the loss.\n\nLoss had grammar. You trained harder. You found the gap. You corrected the weakness before someone else used it again.\n\nHaru did not feel like a gap. He felt like a door Leon had kicked and then discovered was not locked, only waiting to see whether he would try the handle.\n\nElira Vale stood beside him by choice.\n\nThat made the assignment worse. Not because Leon wanted her attention; he had told himself that twice and believed it enough to stop examining the sentence. Because Elira did not make careless choices, and if she had chosen to stand near Haru after seeing more than Leon had seen, then Leon's old categories were not merely incomplete. They were embarrassing.\n\nThe team list glowed above the chamber. Elira. Aira. Cassian. Haru. Leon.\n\nHis name at the end looked like punishment until he noticed Haru's name was not first. That bothered him. It should not have.\n\nLeon looked at the quiet Green Circle transfer and made the smallest correction he could tolerate. Neither rival nor target. Teammate, provisionally.\n\nThe provision mattered. So did the choice.\n\n---\n\nCassian Veyr, by contrast, just laughed.\n\nTall, red-haired, and carrying the kind of confidence usually produced by old family power and excellent combat results, Cassian stepped toward our side of the hall with open amusement.\n\nCassian: \"Well. This looks volatile.\"\n\nAira: \"You say that like it's positive.\"\n\nCassian looked at her and grinned.\n\nCassian: \"I enjoy difficult combinations.\"\n\nAira: \"That is exactly the kind of sentence a problem says.\"\n\nLeon approached a moment later, slower and far less pleased.\n\nLeon: \"If we are being evaluated as a team, I assume everyone understands that personal theatrics will not be tolerated.\"\n\nAira blinked at him.\n\nAira: \"You truly said that out loud.\"\n\nCassian laughed again. Elira's tone remained polite.\n\nElira: \"Then we should all avoid them.\"\n\nLeon looked as if he was trying to decide whether that had been agreement or criticism. It had been both.\n\nProfessor Halden's voice carried across the room.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Group Three. Remain after dismissal. Your first joint exercise begins immediately.\"\n\nAira closed her eyes.\n\nAira: \"It does.\"\n\nI looked at the team list once more.\n\nA Yellow Circle prodigy.\nA calm Blue support specialist.\nA volatile Red combat heir.\nA humiliated Blue rival.\nAnd me.\n\nThis was not a team; it was an experiment. Either Aurelis believed this combination had exceptional potential, or Professor Halden wanted to see what happened when pressure was applied to all the fault lines at once.\n\nGiven the way his gaze found me again from across the chamber, I suspected both.\n\nThe other candidate groups filtered out of the strategy chamber in clusters of relief, annoyance, or self-important discussion, leaving only the five of us and the senior evaluators behind.\n\nThe room felt larger once it emptied. More exposed.\n\nProfessor Halden stood at the center with his hands clasped behind his back. Instructor Dren remained near the projection array, unreadable as ever. Lady Seraphine and Master Thorn took positions at opposite ends of the chamber, clearly preparing to observe rather than assist.\n\nIn other words, if this went badly, it was supposed to.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Group Three was assembled to test high-variance compatibility.\"\n\nAira folded her arms.\n\nAira: \"A dignified way of saying you expect problems.\"\n\nProfessor Halden looked at her over the edge of his lenses.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Miss Solen, if I expected only problems, I would not waste faculty time observing you.\"\n\nAira paused.\n\nAira: \"That was almost encouraging. I resent that.\"\n\nCassian let out a short laugh.\n\nCassian: \"I like her.\"\n\nAira: \"That makes one of us.\"\n\nLeon clicked his tongue softly.\n\nLeon: \"Can we begin without turning this into a social exercise?\"\n\nHaru: \"You say that as if group function isn't partly social.\"\n\nLeon's eyes flicked to me at once.\n\nLeon: \"Some of us are here to compete seriously.\"\n\nCassian glanced between us with open interest.\n\nCassian: \"Did I miss a duel history?\"\n\nAira: \"Several, spiritually.\"\n\nElira exhaled very quietly beside me. Not quite a laugh. Close.\n\nProfessor Halden raised one hand, and a three-dimensional model of the academy grounds appeared above the floor.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Tactical extraction. Enter, secure the simulated relic core, adapt, return it to the recovery zone.\"\n\nThe model shifted. A forested terrain bloomed into view, divided by ravines, false ruins, and layered barrier pockets.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The field will change while you are inside.\"\n\nAira: \"Because static rules might let us keep our dignity.\"\n\nLady Seraphine stepped forward.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Success is not enough. Command response, communication, resource allocation, conflict management. If you win while functioning like a catastrophe, your score will reflect it.\"\n\nCassian smiled.\n\nCassian: \"That seems targeted.\"\n\nLady Seraphine: \"If you feel targeted, improve.\"\n\nHe looked delighted by that.\n\nMaster Thorn gestured toward the west doors.\n\nMaster Thorn: \"Simulation arena. Move.\"\n\n---\n\nThe simulation field occupied an enormous lower training sector beneath the western grounds. It was one of the academy's most expensive strategic facilities: a configurable combat environment with terrain shaping arrays, illusion layering, atmospheric distortion grids, and autonomous construct threats.\n\nWhen the doors opened, we stepped into what looked like the edge of an artificial wilderness.\n\nDense trees.\nBroken stone ruins.\nA shallow stream running through a rocky cut.\nMist hanging low over uneven ground.\n\nAt the center of the field, several crystal pylons hummed softly, feeding power into the simulation grid. Above them, a timer ignited in red light.\n\n**TEAM EXERCISE BEGINS IN 00:30**\n\nAira looked at the terrain and then at the sky, as if silently asking why existence was like this.\n\nAira: \"I would like everyone to know I prefer maps when they're not actively trying to humiliate me.\"\n\nCassian rolled one shoulder, already loosening into combat readiness.\n\nCassian: \"Forest, ruins, limited visibility. Could be worse.\"\n\nAira stared at him.\n\nAira: \"You are definitely someone who says 'could be worse' moments before it becomes worse.\"\n\nElira stepped forward just enough to draw the group's focus without forcing it.\n\nElira: \"We have thirty seconds. We need initial roles.\"\n\nLeon answered immediately.\n\nLeon: \"Fine. Cassian and I take forward pressure. Elira supports central control. Solen handles barrier analysis and rear adaptation. Haru...\"\n\nHe paused a fraction too long.\n\nLeon: \"Haru can assist where needed.\"\n\nAira looked offended on my behalf before I had time to decide whether I cared.\n\nCassian glanced at me.\n\nCassian: \"You're assigning the unknown variable to 'assist where needed'?\"\n\nLeon: \"I'm assigning based on demonstrated output.\"\n\nHaru: \"That's one interpretation.\"\n\nElira's voice remained even.\n\nElira: \"No. We assign based on actual behavior under pressure.\"\n\nLeon turned to her.\n\nLeon: \"And what does that suggest?\"\n\nElira: \"That Haru reads patterns faster than most people here.\"\n\nThere was no hesitation in the way she said it. None.\n\nI felt Leon notice that as much as the words themselves.\n\nElira continued.\n\nElira: \"Cassian applies force well. Aira stabilizes and redirects. You're strongest when your casting has structure and support. I can anchor field balance.\"\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nElira: \"Haru scouts the movement logic and calls the hidden shifts before they happen.\"\n\nCassian's eyebrows rose.\n\nCassian: \"You trust him that much?\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nImmediate. Uncomplicated.\n\nThat word did not do good things to my ability to remain emotionally detached.\n\nLeon heard it too. His expression tightened very slightly.\n\nAira, however, looked mostly vindicated.\n\nAira: \"Good. Finally a role assignment based on reality.\"\n\nThe timer hit ten seconds.\n\nProfessor Halden's voice echoed through the field.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Begin.\"\n\n---\n\nWe moved.\n\nCassian took the lead naturally, flames gathering in sharp coils around his fists without fully manifesting. Leon stayed just off his right flank, water mana already threading in low currents around the ground to test for hidden traps. Aira remained central, scanning the barrier lines and ambient shifts. Elira walked with the calm of someone whose presence alone stabilized the team's rhythm.\n\nI watched the mana beneath the terrain instead of the trees and ruins. Simulation environments always had logic; even chaos was built on design.\n\nThe stream to our left was too quiet.\nThe mist on the right was denser than the ambient temperature justified.\nThe old stone path ahead had micro-distortions suggesting delayed trigger arrays.\n\nHaru: \"Left stream is false-safe. Don't cross it directly. Right mist likely contains visual decoys. Path ahead is pressure-marked under the third and sixth stones.\"\n\nCassian looked back mid-stride.\n\nCassian: \"You see all that this quickly?\"\n\nHaru: \"Enough of it.\"\n\nLeon did not look at me.\n\nLeon: \"Or he's guessing confidently.\"\n\nAira: \"That would matter more if he kept being wrong.\"\n\nCassian laughed.\n\nCassian: \"All right, Green. Lead with your eyes, then.\"\n\nWe adjusted course. Aira used a low-pressure water film to reveal hidden lines in the ground. The third and sixth stones lit up instantly, trap confirmed. Leon said nothing.\n\nThat silence was almost more satisfying than rebuttal.\n\nWe cut through a side corridor of broken ruins instead, where the first constructs attacked.\n\nThey emerged from the stone like armored beasts made of compacted mana and bronze light, four-legged and fast, jaws opening with sharpened arcane edges.\n\nCassian grinned.\n\nCassian: \"Finally.\"\n\nHe launched forward in a burst of red-orange fire, striking the first construct with a reinforced elbow that shattered its shoulder plate. Leon followed with slicing water crescents aimed at the leg joints. Efficient. Controlled. Better than during our direct matches. Competition suited him better than wounded pride.\n\nThe second wave came from above.\n\nHaru: \"Upper left.\"\n\nElira was already moving.\n\nGolden light rose in a curved shield just as two airborne constructs dropped from the ruins. They hit the barrier and rebounded into open range. Aira trapped one in a compressed water sphere long enough for Cassian to destroy it with a downward arc of flame.\n\nThe fifth construct slipped past the front line and lunged toward Aira.\n\nLeon reacted a beat too late. I didn't.\n\nI stepped in, redirected its trajectory with a compact reinforcement pulse, and drove it into the stone wall hard enough to disable the core.\n\nCassian whistled.\n\nCassian: \"You really aren't a normal Green, are you?\"\n\nHaru: \"I get that a lot.\"\n\nAira: \"Not enough, honestly.\"\n\nWe kept moving.\n\nThe deeper we went into the simulation, the more obvious the evaluators' intention became. This was no simple retrieval exercise; it was a pressure chamber.\n\nEvery five minutes the terrain changed.\nThe stream rose and became electrically charged.\nThe ruins shifted positions.\nFalse objective signatures appeared in the distance to split decision-making.\nAt one point, illusion duplicates of our own team members appeared through the trees, each calling conflicting instructions.\n\nAira was the first to identify the pattern.\n\nAira: \"The false voices are too clean. Real mana resonance always drags on the edges in this much distortion.\"\n\nHaru: \"Agreed. Ignore anything that sounds easier than the situation deserves.\"\n\nCassian smiled.\n\nCassian: \"That may be your best tactical principle yet.\"\n\nThen the first real problem arrived.\n\nThe objective chamber, a ruined circular structure at the center of the field, was protected by a layered barrier lock requiring simultaneous affinity alignment at four anchor points.\n\nWe reached the outer ring and stopped.\n\nAira immediately knelt to inspect the runes.\n\nAira: \"This is not one lock. It's four interdependent locks sharing backlash.\"\n\nLeon crouched opposite her, running water mana across the etched lines.\n\nLeon: \"If one anchor is forced incorrectly, the others reset.\"\n\nCassian folded his arms.\n\nCassian: \"Can we break it?\"\n\nAira and Leon answered together.\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nCassian looked mildly offended.\n\nElira stepped toward the ring.\n\nElira: \"What does it require?\"\n\nAira traced the nearest sigil.\n\nAira: \"Balanced input, not brute force. Four nodes. Different densities. One has to modulate, one has to stabilize, one has to pressure, and one has to absorb recoil.\"\n\nCassian pointed at himself.\n\nCassian: \"Pressure. Obviously.\"\n\nLeon nodded toward the fluid channels.\n\nLeon: \"I can handle modulation.\"\n\nAira: \"I'll stabilize.\"\n\nElira looked at the final node.\n\nElira: \"Then I'll absorb recoil.\"\n\nI looked more closely at the line structure and felt the problem immediately.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nEveryone turned toward me.\n\nElira: \"Why?\"\n\nI stepped nearer the lock.\n\nHaru: \"Because the recoil node isn't defensive. It's adaptive. It will mirror the mana signature of whoever takes it and amplify any instability in the other three.\"\n\nAira's eyes widened as she re-read the rune pattern.\n\nAira: \"He's right.\"\n\nLeon frowned.\n\nLeon: \"Then who takes it?\"\n\nThe answer was obvious. I looked at the node.\n\nHaru: \"I will.\"\n\nLeon's disbelief was immediate.\n\nLeon: \"Absolutely not.\"\n\nCassian looked intrigued.\n\nCassian: \"Why not? If he can handle it, let him.\"\n\nLeon: \"Because if he fails, the whole structure resets.\"\n\nAira straightened.\n\nAira: \"And if Elira takes it, her mana profile may overload the adaptive reflection. Haru is the most stable option if the node values control over raw output.\"\n\nLeon looked between the three of us like betrayal had become procedural.\n\nLeon: \"We are seriously trusting the Green on the key node?\"\n\nElira's gaze held steady.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nThat word again, delivered without a flicker of doubt.\n\nCassian's grin sharpened.\n\nCassian: \"Well, now I want to see it.\"\n\nWe took positions: Aira at the stabilization node, Leon at modulation, Cassian at pressure, and me at the recoil-adaptive anchor. Elira stood central, ready to field-correct if the chamber destabilized.\n\nAira counted us in.\n\nAira: \"On three. One. Two. Three.\"\n\nMana flowed.\n\nCassian fed sharp, measured force into his node. The rune blazed red-orange. Leon's water mana coiled into the second, smoothing the rotational rhythm. Aira layered fine support geometry into the third, holding the lock's structure together.\n\nThen I touched the fourth.\n\nEven suppressed, the Black Circle made control like this easy. Too easy.\n\nThe adaptive node drank in my mana and lost its glow. Neither overloaded nor erratic, simply still.\n\nIt had recognized something beyond the scope of its own design and, finding no way to meaningfully reflect it, settled for obedience.\n\nThe risk sat cold under my ribs. I carefully reduced the flow, forcing the structure to behave as though it were merely impressed by excellent Green Circle control.\n\nThe lock rotated. The four anchors aligned. The barrier split open.\n\nCassian laughed aloud.\n\nCassian: \"That was absurdly clean.\"\n\nAira looked at me with narrowed eyes.\n\nAira: \"You make too many impossible things look tidy.\"\n\nLeon said nothing, watching me now with a more serious kind of attention.\n\nInside the chamber, the relic core floated above a pedestal of broken stone, a crystal orb filled with swirling blue-white energy. Simulated, of course, but valuable to the scoring system all the same.\n\nElira stepped toward it.\n\nElira: \"We secure and return.\"\n\nProfessor Halden's voice rang through the arena.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Complication phase.\"\n\nThe chamber floor dropped.\n\nIllusionally, but with enough force coupling to make the body believe it.\n\nThe circular ruin shattered into floating platforms. The forest beyond darkened into stormlight. The relic core split into three copies, each radiating different signatures.\n\nCassian laughed again.\n\nCassian: \"There it is.\"\n\nAira: \"Why do you always sound pleased when things become unreasonable?\"\n\nCassian: \"Because that's when people become interesting.\"\n\nLeon pointed immediately.\n\nLeon: \"Center core. Strongest output. That's the real one.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira turned to me.\n\nElira: \"Which?\"\n\nI studied the three orbs.\nOne burned too brightly, obvious bait.\nOne was too stable for a field under active disruption.\nThe third, slightly dimmer and off to the left, was imperfect in exactly the way reality usually was.\n\nHaru: \"Left.\"\n\nLeon: \"Based on what?\"\n\nHaru: \"The others are cleaner than the simulation's distortion pattern. Left core carries the same environmental drag as the platform grid.\"\n\nAira looked once and immediately nodded.\n\nAira: \"He's right again.\"\n\nCassian grinned at Leon.\n\nCassian: \"You're losing a difficult argument, Blue.\"\n\nLeon ignored him, but only barely.\n\nThe constructs returned then, stronger than before, winged and humanoid this time, armed with projected weapons and shield arrays. Three descended toward the left platform. Two cut off our retreat.\n\nElira moved first.\n\nGolden light spread outward in a radiant ring that slowed the nearest attackers and stabilized the collapsing edge of the platform. Aira reinforced the underside structure with water-binding geometry. Leon cut down one construct with a focused crescent blade that was honestly impressive.\n\nCassian leapt straight through the middle of the chaos, fire erupting around him in controlled bursts as he smashed one of the shielded constructs apart.\n\nI moved to the relic core.\n\nThe moment my hand closed around it, the entire simulation screamed.\n\nRed light flooded the arena.\n\n**OBJECTIVE SECURED**  \n**EXTRACTION WINDOW: 02:00**\n\nA two-minute retreat under escalating pressure. Naturally.\n\nHaru: \"Move.\"\n\nEveryone moved at once. This time no one questioned who had called it.\n\nThat, more than anything else, told me the team exercise was changing us. Not into friends, certainly not yet, but into something more useful: people beginning to trust competence despite themselves.\n\nWe ran the extraction path with the relic core protected at center. Aira adjusted routes on the fly. Leon finally stopped resisting team logic and focused on what he did well: fluid control, barrier cutting, path shaping. Cassian broke anything too slow to avoid. Elira held the group together like a sun at the center of moving gravity.\n\nAnd I watched the shifting field, calling the invisible changes before they happened.\n\nThen the simulation became personal.\n\nThe forest ahead split open into a narrow ravine crossed by a broken bridge, and a student appeared on the far side. Not a real student: an adaptive construct wearing the uniform of an Aurelis first-year, kneeling with one hand pressed to a cracked barrier while shadow beasts hammered against it from three directions. The construct was built well enough to breathe unevenly, to tremble, and to look up with panic that did not belong to a training field.\n\nThe relic core pulsed in my hands.\n\nThe recovery route glowed beyond the ravine. The timer continued falling.\n\nLeon: \"It's a decoy.\"\n\nAira: \"It is also a scoring condition. Look at the barrier line.\"\n\nShe was right. A thin silver marker glowed under the construct's feet, nearly hidden by dust.\n\n**OPTIONAL RESCUE NODE**\n\nCassian's fire flared around one wrist.\n\nCassian: \"We can clear that.\"\n\nLeon: \"And lose time.\"\n\nCassian: \"Then we lose time.\"\n\nLeon turned sharply.\n\nLeon: \"We are carrying the objective.\"\n\nElira looked at the construct, then at the route, then at us. Her expression did not change, but the air around her warmed by one degree.\n\nElira: \"If this were real, we would not leave them.\"\n\nLeon: \"It is not real.\"\n\nHaru: \"The evaluators are watching whether that matters.\"\n\nThat stopped him.\n\nFor a heartbeat, Leon looked exactly like someone standing between the person he had been and the teammate he was not yet sure he could become.\n\nAira's voice softened without losing its edge.\n\nAira: \"If we rescue wrong, we fail both objectives. If we ignore it, we tell the faculty what kind of victory we are willing to accept.\"\n\nThe timer hit forty-seven seconds.\n\nNo time for a speech. Good. Speeches were often what people used when action frightened them.\n\nHaru: \"Cassian, break the beasts from the outside only. Do not touch the construct's barrier. Leon, bridge the ravine with water tension under Aira's stabilizer. Elira, keep the relic core shielded from the rebound. Aira, call collapse timing.\"\n\nFor once, no one argued.\n\nCassian moved first, crossing the broken bridge with three explosive steps and striking the nearest shadow beast so cleanly it shattered without throwing debris into the construct. Leon threw a water line under the bridge and hardened it into a temporary path. Aira layered fine support geometry over his work, teeth clenched in concentration.\n\nElira stepped beside me, gold light closing around the relic core.\n\nElira: \"You included yourself nowhere.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am holding the problem.\"\n\nElira: \"You are part of the team.\"\n\nThe sentence landed faster than I expected.\n\nHaru: \"Then I am holding the center.\"\n\nHer eyes softened for half a second.\n\nThen the ravine wall cracked.\n\nAira: \"Collapse in five.\"\n\nLeon: \"Cassian!\"\n\nCassian caught the false student by the back of the uniform and threw them toward the bridge with more precision than gentleness. Aira caught the construct in a water-cushioned barrier. Leon pulled. Elira sealed the backlash around the relic core.\n\nI stepped onto the cracked bridge last.\n\nThe ravine tried to eat the path under me.\n\nFor one dangerous instant, the Black Circle offered the simplest solution: erase the collapse, rewrite the bridge, make the simulation remember a safer world.\n\nI did none of those things.\n\nI used one Green-level reinforcement pulse at exactly the correct joint and let the broken bridge survive long enough to be crossed by people who had earned the rescue themselves.\n\nWe reached the far side as the ravine folded shut behind us.\n\nThe construct dissolved into silver light.\n\n**RESCUE NODE CLEARED**\n\nLeon stared at the fading marker, breathing hard.\n\nLeon: \"That cost us twelve seconds.\"\n\nAira: \"It also added rescue credit.\"\n\nCassian rolled his shoulders, expression sharper now.\n\nCassian: \"Worth it.\"\n\nLeon looked as if he wanted to disagree. Then he looked at Elira, at Aira, and at the path we had not abandoned.\n\nLeon: \"Next time, call the bridge sooner.\"\n\nThat was not an apology. It was more useful than one.\n\nThree hidden mines beneath the southern ledge.\nA collapsing bridge illusion masking a safe path to the right.\nA delayed strike array embedded in the false exit gate.\n\nI caught the first two.\n\nI almost missed the third because Elira's shield flickered at the edge of my vision and my attention went to her before the field.\n\nLeon saw my eyes shift.\n\nLeon: \"Exit gate, right seam.\"\n\nI cut the warning short with a nod and adjusted the route.\n\nWe adapted, again and again.\n\nWe crossed into the recovery zone with seventeen seconds remaining, and the entire observation gallery fell silent.\n\nWe had won not because we were the strongest squad in the room, but because we had refused to choose between victory and each other.\n\nBeyond the fading walls of the simulation waited a tournament designed to make that refusal costly.\n\n---\n\n## Coda: Cassian Counts Exits\n\nCassian Veyr hated standing still after a fight, so he climbed up to the empty observation gallery while the cleaning sigils were still sweeping arena dust into neat silver rows.\n\nHe rolled his left shoulder until the joint popped, shook a stray spark off his knuckles, and leaned his forearms over the stone rail. From up here, the simulation field looked smaller and less dramatic, more like a box built to test how much weight a handful of teenagers could take before someone cracked.\n\nOut of old Red-house habit, he counted exits anyway. Six clean doors. Two maintenance grates. One high ventilation arch that technically counted as an escape route if you had enough fire-step momentum and zero respect for your own ankles.\n\nHaru had spotted every dead corner down there before Cassian had even finished lighting his hands. That should have made Cassian relax. Instead, it left an itch behind his ribs.\n\nDown in the corridor below, voices faded toward the washrooms. Leon was finally dropping the bruised-pride act because looking foolish had stopped feeling safer than doing the work. Aira still talked like every evaluation was a personal insult, right up until the second she had to catch three people's mistakes at once. Elira watched Haru the way someone watched a friend standing too close to a cliff edge, steady and ready to grab his collar if he stepped back.\n\nAnd Haru kept reaching for the smallest spell in the room when Cassian would have bet his family's signet ring there was more behind it.\n\nCassian flicked the last ember off his thumb and pushed away from the rail.\n\nIf the tournament wanted to break Group Three, it could get in line. Cassian planned to stay close enough to catch the moment the quiet Green ran out of small answers.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-03-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep4","label":"Episode 4","number":"04","file":"episode-04-cover.webp","fallback":"The Circle Beneath the Night","eyebrow":"Moonlit Archive","recap":"","accent":["#080808","#ffd166","#8ecae6"],"displayLabel":"Episode 4","displayNumber":"04","displayEyebrow":"The Hidden Circle","arcLabel":"The Hidden Circle","title":"The Shape of a Team","body":"The recovery zone flared white beneath our feet.\n\nThe relic core in my hands dissolved into particles of blue light, signaling successful extraction. Around the edge of the simulation field, the crystal pylons dimmed one by one. The storm-dark sky above the arena peeled away into transparent runic glass. Trees, ruins, mist, and broken terrain all shimmered and collapsed back into structured mana.\n\nReality returned in layers, and with it came silence: the weight of performance, observation, and judgment.\n\nThe faculty had seen everything. So had the assistant evaluators recording from the outer ring, and the other candidate teams gathered in the observation gallery above the field.\n\nAurelis had wanted to test whether our group would fracture. Instead, we had succeeded: messily at times, unevenly, reluctantly, and undeniably.\n\nCassian rolled his shoulders and let out a satisfied breath.\n\nCassian: \"Well. That was fun.\"\n\nAira bent over with both hands on her knees.\n\nAira: \"Your definition of fun requires review.\"\n\nLeon wiped a line of moisture from his jaw and exhaled slowly, trying very hard to look as though nothing about the exercise had surprised him.\n\nElira stood straight, composed as ever, though the flush in her cheeks and the slight rise and fall of her breathing made the effort she'd spent visible to anyone paying attention.\n\nI was paying attention. That had become a problem of its own.\n\nMaster Thorn stepped forward first, glancing at the timing crystal.\n\nMaster Thorn: \"Fast recovery. Above expected adaptation.\"\n\nLady Seraphine looked over her notes.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Communication discipline improved after the midpoint. Initial hierarchy conflict was a liability. By extraction phase, efficiency overrode ego.\"\n\nHer gaze shifted, very briefly, toward Leon and Cassian. Appropriate.\n\nCassian grinned openly.\n\nCassian: \"Almost complimentary.\"\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Do not become dependent on that either.\"\n\nCassian looked delighted, which I suspected was not the intended effect.\n\nProfessor Halden approached the edge of the field with his hands clasped behind his back.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Interesting.\"\n\nThat was all he said at first. Somehow, from him, it sounded less like praise and more like a blade being tested for sharpness.\n\nHis eyes moved across the team: pausing on Elira with expected approval, on Aira with increased interest, on Cassian with tactical reservation, on Leon with measured dissatisfaction.\n\nThen on me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Mr. Aerlen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Professor.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Your pattern recognition continues to outperform your visible profile.\"\n\nThere were many possible answers to that, most of them bad.\n\nHaru: \"The team made the timing work.\"\n\nProfessor Halden's expression did not change.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Clearly.\"\n\nHe turned away before the exchange could deepen, which was almost worse than if he had pressed.\n\nAira leaned just slightly toward me.\n\nAira: \"I'm beginning to dislike the way he says simple words.\"\n\nHaru: \"That seems healthy.\"\n\nElira glanced at me briefly, just long enough for the warning inside it to land. Be careful.\n\nI knew. The problem was that caution became harder every time someone smart watched too closely.\n\n---\n\nThe official score projections were posted by evening.\n\nAurelis did not believe in suspense when it could create more useful forms of stress.\n\nStudents crowded around the central announcement wall in the main academic court, reading the updated rankings with the focus of people trying not to visibly panic.\n\nLarge floating panels displayed category scores, observed compatibility ratings, and provisional recommendations for tournament roles.\n\nAira stood beside me with her arms crossed and the expression of someone preparing to disagree with existence.\n\nAira: \"If I've somehow become a top support candidate, I'm going to need time to resent my own competence.\"\n\nHaru: \"That seems manageable.\"\n\nAira: \"For you, perhaps.\"\n\nThe panel shifted. Our group's data appeared.\n\n**GROUP THREE - PROVISIONAL TEAM METRIC**  \n**Extraction Success: High**  \n**Adaptation Rating: Exceptional**  \n**Conflict Stabilization: Moderate to High**  \n**Strategic Versatility: Outstanding**\n\nBelow that, individual performance notes began to scroll.\n\n**Elira Vale - Principal Candidate: Multi-Category Core Representative**  \n**Cassian Veyr - Principal Candidate: Heavy Combat / Breaker Role**  \n**Aira Solen - Principal Candidate: Tactical Support / Control Systems**  \n**Leon Varek - Secondary Principal Candidate: Midline Control / Fluid Modulation**  \n**Haru Aerlen - Principal Candidate: Adaptive Field Reading / Duel Reserve / Tactical Flex**\n\nAira read the last line twice, then again.\n\nAira: \"They gave you three roles.\"\n\nHaru: \"That seems inefficient.\"\n\nAira turned to stare at me.\n\nAira: \"No, Haru, it sounds alarming.\"\n\nStudents around us had started noticing too. Blue Circle candidates a few steps away exchanged glances. One Red Circle fourth-year let out a short laugh of disbelief.\n\nStudent: \"A Green got principal status?\"\n\nAnd just like that, the tension around my existence at Aurelis sharpened again. It was one thing to be strange; it was another to be institutionally validated.\n\nElira arrived through the crowd a moment later, moving with her usual calm gravity. She looked at the panel once, then at me.\n\nElira: \"You expected this?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nAira: \"I did. Unfortunately.\"\n\nElira's mouth curved slightly.\n\nElira: \"Then congratulations seem appropriate.\"\n\nAira pointed between us.\n\nAira: \"Don't say it like that. He'll accept it too calmly.\"\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira closed her eyes.\n\nAira: \"There. Exactly.\"\n\nFrom across the court, Cassian Veyr lifted his hand in casual acknowledgment when he saw us. The movement drew no small amount of attention. He made his way through the other candidates without apparent concern for whatever social structures he was disturbing.\n\nCassian: \"Well, that settles it.\"\n\nAira: \"What does it settle?\"\n\nCassian stopped beside us and glanced at the panel.\n\nCassian: \"That we're the most interesting team candidate by far.\"\n\nAira: \"That is not the reassuring word choice you think it is.\"\n\nCassian grinned.\n\nCassian: \"You're the support specialist, Solen. You exist to reassure.\"\n\nAira: \"I exist to survive educational decisions.\"\n\nCassian's attention shifted to me.\n\nCassian: \"You're causing a social crisis, Green.\"\n\nHaru: \"Apparently.\"\n\nCassian: \"Do you enjoy that?\"\n\nHaru: \"Not particularly.\"\n\nCassian looked almost disappointed.\n\nCassian: \"That's a shame. You're very good at it.\"\n\nElira, beside me, seemed faintly amused.\n\nElira: \"That may be because he never tries.\"\n\nCassian laughed once.\n\nCassian: \"That makes it worse. Excellent.\"\n\nBefore the conversation could become stranger, another presence entered the edge of the crowd. Leon.\n\nThe murmurs around the announcement wall shifted at once, not because people feared him, but because they were waiting to see whether he would react badly.\n\nTo his credit, he did not. At least not publicly.\n\nHe stopped near the panel, read the rankings, and looked at his own line with a controlled expression. Secondary Principal Candidate was still strong placement, especially after his recent losses in image.\n\nThen his gaze moved to mine. Measured. Careful.\n\nLeon: \"So they really are building around utility.\"\n\nCassian glanced at him.\n\nCassian: \"Is that your polite version of surprise?\"\n\nLeon ignored him.\n\nLeon: \"I admit, Aerlen, I expected the faculty to be less willing to reward unconventional optics.\"\n\nAira stared.\n\nAira: \"That may be the most elegant insult I've heard all week.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm learning to appreciate creativity.\"\n\nLeon's eyes narrowed a fraction, though the reaction was smaller than it would have been before. Good. He was adapting. Which meant he might eventually become useful, or more dangerous. Often those were the same thing.\n\nLeon looked at the team metrics again.\n\nLeon: \"If Group Three remains together, we will need better coordination than what we had in the simulation.\"\n\nCassian: \"That sounded almost like teamwork.\"\n\nLeon: \"Take a moment to recover from the shock.\"\n\nCassian laughed. Aira blinked.\n\nAira: \"Are we improving or becoming more sarcastic?\"\n\nHaru: \"Both.\"\n\nElira: \"That can still be improvement.\"\n\nFor a brief second, all five of us stood there under the floating rankings, surrounded by staring students and fresh rumor energy, and I could almost see the shape of the thing Aurelis was trying to build.\n\nNeither harmony nor friendship. Capability.\n\nA team made of unstable parts that might, under enough pressure, become stronger than more elegant combinations. A dangerous theory, but not an impossible one.\n\nThe theory became harder to ignore when we were ordered into a closed debrief instead of released to dinner.\n\nThe room assigned to us was one of the smaller tactical classrooms beneath the east wing, all black glass tables, wall-mounted maps, and chairs designed by someone who believed comfort weakened discipline. A crystal recorder floated above the center table, its indicator light glowing a calm blue.\n\nAira sat down and immediately pointed at it.\n\nAira: \"I want that thing to know I am participating under protest.\"\n\nThe recorder pulsed once, dutifully.\n\nCassian dropped into the chair opposite her.\n\nCassian: \"It likes you.\"\n\nAira: \"That is the tragedy of my life.\"\n\nLeon remained standing for a moment, eyes on the replay slate already forming above the table. The simulation had been rendered into clean blue lines: our route, our mistakes, our corrections, the rescue node, the final extraction.\n\nThere was no cheering or rumor. Just proof.\n\nElira took the seat beside me. She did not reach for my hand here; too many crystals, too many possible logs. But her knee touched mine lightly under the table, a quiet point of contact no recorder would understand.\n\nLady Seraphine's voice entered through the recorder.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Group Three will review three failures and three strengths. Begin without faculty prompting. We will assess the conversation afterward.\"\n\nThe light dimmed.\n\nAira stared at the crystal.\n\nAira: \"They are making us evaluate ourselves.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is standard.\"\n\nAira: \"No. Standard is an instructor telling us we were disappointing in specific ways. This requires emotional labor.\"\n\nCassian leaned forward, suddenly more serious.\n\nCassian: \"First failure. I wanted to break the barrier lock.\"\n\nAira blinked.\n\nAira: \"That was... direct.\"\n\nCassian: \"I am capable of learning when insulted by architecture.\"\n\nLeon: \"You also nearly overcommitted on the rescue node.\"\n\nCassian's smile sharpened.\n\nCassian: \"Nearly.\"\n\nLeon: \"Nearly still matters.\"\n\nFor one heartbeat, the old version of Leon showed through: proud, exacting, ready to turn correction into hierarchy.\n\nThen he looked at the replay of himself insisting the false student was only a decoy. His expression tightened.\n\nLeon: \"Second failure. I treated the objective as if it mattered more than the choice around it.\"\n\nThe room went quieter. No one made a joke.\n\nThat was how I knew the sentence had cost him something.\n\nCassian leaned back, firelight low at his wrist.\n\nCassian: \"We can't save everyone in a real fight.\"\n\nHe said it lightly. Too lightly: the kind of lightness people used when a sentence had already cut them once and they had decided jokes were cheaper than bleeding in public.\n\nElira noticed. So did Aira. For once, even Leon did not correct him immediately.\n\nCassian's thumb moved across the old signet ring on his hand. Red Circle heir jewelry was usually decorative arrogance. His looked worn at the edge, as if someone had turned it too often while waiting for news that arrived too late.\n\nCassian: \"My family calls it triage. Very noble word. Makes leaving someone behind sound like strategy instead of memory.\"\n\nThe fire at his wrist shrank to a thin ember.\n\nCassian: \"I am not fond of that kind of strategy.\"\n\nElira answered with the same calm she used in combat.\n\nElira: \"Then we practice saving everyone now, so we know how when it matters. A tournament will try to make us choose between winning and remaining ourselves. We need to decide before the field decides for us.\"\n\nAira looked at her.\n\nAira: \"That works as a team principle.\"\n\nCassian tapped the table once.\n\nCassian: \"Fine. Principle one: we do not abandon people for cleaner victories.\"\n\nLeon did not look away from the replay.\n\nLeon: \"Agreed.\"\n\nAira's eyes softened, but her voice stayed dry.\n\nAira: \"I hate that we are becoming admirable. It raises expectations.\"\n\nThen the replay shifted to my route calls.\n\nThe room became uncomfortable in a different way. Blue lines traced every warning I had given before visible confirmation: trap, decoy, collapse, anchor, false core, real core, safe path. Too many correct answers. Too little visible reason.\n\nLeon watched the sequence twice.\n\nLeon: \"Third failure.\"\n\nI looked at him. He looked back.\n\nLeon: \"We still do not know how to use Haru without exposing him to every question in the room.\"\n\nThat was not the accusation I expected. It was more dangerous because it was useful.\n\nAira sat back slowly.\n\nAira: \"That is... annoyingly insightful.\"\n\nLeon: \"Try not to sound shocked.\"\n\nCassian grinned.\n\nCassian: \"No, let her have this. I was shocked.\"\n\nElira's gaze remained on me.\n\nElira: \"Then principle two: we build explanations before people demand them.\"\n\nHaru: \"You are all assuming this remains our group.\"\n\nAira pointed at the replay slate.\n\nAira: \"Haru. Look at that disaster and tell me the faculty will not become attached.\"\n\nI looked. Five colored paths crossed, split, collided, corrected, and finally converged into the recovery zone with seventeen seconds remaining. It was not elegant. It was alive.\n\nHaru: \"They will become attached.\"\n\nCassian leaned back, satisfied.\n\nCassian: \"Good. Then principle three: if anyone asks, all our success came from overwhelming charm.\"\n\nLeon: \"Rejected.\"\n\nAira: \"Violently rejected.\"\n\nElira smiled faintly.\n\nHaru: \"Recorded.\"\n\nThe crystal above the table pulsed again. Somewhere, Lady Seraphine was probably writing something severe.\n\nFor the first time, I found I did not mind.\n\n---\n\nThe next morning confirmed it.\n\nGroup Three had been summoned for private advanced training.\n\nThat alone was enough to draw attention. Private training slots were usually reserved for confirmed principal candidates or students the faculty wanted closer observation of. In my case, the second possibility felt unpleasantly likely.\n\nWe met at dawn in the upper combat terrace where the eastern towers cast long shadows over the practice rings. The air was cool, the campus quieter than usual. Most students were not yet awake enough to be ambitious.\n\nProfessor Halden was already there. So were Instructor Dren and Lady Seraphine.\n\nAira took one look at the faculty lineup and sighed.\n\nAira: \"I'm beginning to suspect we're not a training group. We're a research incident.\"\n\nProfessor Halden heard her.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"If that were true, Miss Solen, I would not have informed you.\"\n\nAira: \"That is not actually comforting.\"\n\nCassian stretched his arms over his head.\n\nCassian: \"Can we start? Standing still this early feels unnatural.\"\n\nLady Seraphine gestured toward the marked rings.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Today's focus is role compression.\"\n\nLeon frowned.\n\nLeon: \"Role compression?\"\n\nInstructor Dren answered.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"You all performed well when your strengths were allowed to remain distinct. That's useful. It's also fragile. In tournament settings, people get isolated. Plans fail. If one member falls out of position, the whole structure can collapse.\"\n\nProfessor Halden stepped forward.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"We intend to see whether this team remains functional when deprived of comfort.\"\n\nAira: \"That is a remarkably hostile way to describe education.\"\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Yes.\"\n\nHe dropped one palm onto the terrace seal, and the practice rings lit pale blue beneath our boots. That ended the discussion.\n\nThe drill was simple in concept and terrible in execution. Every ten minutes, one team member would be removed from their preferred function: Cassian without front-line pressure, Aira without stable support position, Leon without fluid terrain control, Elira without central anchor authority, and me without advance reading access.\n\nThe exercise was built to make each of us uncomfortable in different ways.\n\nThe first round removed Cassian from direct engagement and forced him into rear interception and protective spacing. He hated it immediately.\n\nCassian: \"This is insulting.\"\n\nAira: \"No, this is growth.\"\n\nCassian: \"I preferred you when you were complaining.\"\n\nHis first interception proved the point against him. Cassian moved too aggressively toward a flank construct, shattered it beautifully, and left the rear lane open for a second attack to slip through.\n\nAira caught the breach with a barrier that made a sound like offended glass.\n\nAira: \"Growth usually includes not abandoning the thing you are protecting.\"\n\nStill, he adapted faster than expected. For all his love of impact, he had excellent instinct for threat lines and proved surprisingly good at breaking attacks before they reached the center.\n\nThe second round removed Aira from her support grid and forced her into movement leadership.\n\nAira: \"This is malicious.\"\n\nElira: \"You can do it.\"\n\nAira: \"Your faith is oppressive.\"\n\nShe did it anyway, and very well.\n\nThe third round took Leon out of structured control and forced him into reactive offense without setup. That one exposed the most.\n\nLeon was strong, refined, and skilled, but much of his confidence depended on environment and rhythm. Without them, he became less certain and less efficient. He froze for half a breath when the second construct changed angle without warning, frustration flashing across his face because there was no elegant setup to claim.\n\nAira: \"Half-step left, then cut on my count.\"\n\nHe moved on her timing. It worked. He noticed that too, and to his credit, instead of lashing out, he adjusted.\n\nBy the fourth rotation, when Elira was barred from acting as central commander, the drill became truly interesting.\n\nWithout meaning to, the rest of us had already started orienting around her. Removing her presence created a temporary vacuum.\n\nCassian wanted motion.\nLeon wanted structure.\nAira wanted coherence.\n\nThey all looked at me. I did not enjoy that.\n\nAira: \"Well?\"\n\nHaru: \"Why are you asking me?\"\n\nAira: \"Because you already mapped the next three exchanges in your head.\"\n\nCassian grinned.\n\nCassian: \"She's got you there.\"\n\nLeon looked irritated that the assessment was accurate.\n\nLeon: \"If you have a plan, say it.\"\n\nI looked at the incoming construct pattern: three high-pressure lanes, one false center, delayed flank emergence.\n\nHaru: \"I'm not replacing her. I'm holding her position until she's back. Cassian left lane, but don't commit past the second marker. Leon cuts the false center and forces the real flank reveal. Aira, hold mid elevation and feed him angle corrections. Elira, right-side reserve only, don't stabilize unless the outer ring breaks.\"\n\nThey moved.\n\nIt failed on the second exchange.\n\nThe false center broke early. Leon turned to force the flank reveal exactly as I had told him. That left Aira's upper lane open half a breath sooner than my pattern allowed.\n\nCassian saw the construct diving toward her. He crossed the second marker.\n\nHaru: \"Cassian, hold...\"\n\nHe did not.\n\nHis Red Circle struck the diving construct apart. The blast saved Aira's lane and exposed his own. A delayed spear came through the left gap, hit the reserve ring beside Elira, and shattered the stone score post behind her.\n\nInstructor Dren dropped the entire field. Silence landed with the dust.\n\nElira had not been hit. Aira's sleeve was scorched where Cassian's fire passed too close. Leon stood in the dead center with his blade raised against a flank that no longer existed.\n\nThe score crystal displayed one word.\n\n**FAILED.**\n\nI looked at Cassian first.\n\nHaru: \"You left the lane.\"\n\nHis grin had vanished.\n\nCassian: \"The construct had Aira.\"\n\nHaru: \"She had mid elevation.\"\n\nAira held up the burned edge of her sleeve.\n\nAira: \"I did until the center opened early.\"\n\nLeon: \"Your correction came late.\"\n\nHaru: \"You followed the false center after it changed.\"\n\nLeon: \"Because you told me to force the flank reveal.\"\n\nThe answer struck harder than the accusation. They had followed me. That was not the same as trusting me.\n\nI had given each person a path and mistaken obedience for coordination. When the construct changed, Leon protected the instruction, Cassian protected Aira, and I protected the plan until the field broke around it.\n\nElira stepped out of the reserve ring.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI knew what she was going to say and still wanted her not to.\n\nElira: \"You planned for our strengths. You did not plan for us to disagree with you.\"\n\nHaru: \"There was no time.\"\n\nElira: \"There was enough time to give six commands.\"\n\nCassian looked toward the shattered post. His hands were open now, fire gone.\n\nCassian: \"I should have called the break before I crossed.\"\n\nAira: \"I should have called that I lost elevation.\"\n\nLeon lowered his blade.\n\nLeon: \"I should have stopped following a dead instruction.\"\n\nNo one offered me a matching sentence. They waited.\n\nHaru: \"I should have told you what mattered instead of deciding every movement.\"\n\nProfessor Halden's pen moved. I hated the sound.\n\nInstructor Dren reset the field with one palm against the central seal. The shattered score post stayed shattered.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Again. No central commander. You may state observations, intentions, and limits. Anyone issuing a full sequence loses speaking privilege.\"\n\nAira stared at him.\n\nAira: \"You can lose speaking privilege in combat?\"\n\nInstructor Dren: \"You are closest to finding out.\"\n\nThe constructs rose.\n\nThis time I did not map three exchanges aloud.\n\nHaru: \"False center. Left delay.\"\n\nCassian: \"Taking left until second marker. If Aira drops, I cross.\"\n\nAira: \"Mid elevation unstable. I can hold one break, not two.\"\n\nLeon: \"I force center after her call.\"\n\nElira stayed inside the reserve ring.\n\nElira: \"Right ring can absorb one spear. I choose which.\"\n\nThe wave entered. Nothing looked elegant.\n\nCassian let the first flank construct pass farther than he liked. Aira redirected it instead of waiting for him to destroy it. Leon saw the false center open and held position, jaw tight, until Aira shouted.\n\nAira: \"Now.\"\n\nHe cut.\n\nThe real flank emerged high. I called the angle, not the answer.\n\nHaru: \"Right, descending.\"\n\nElira chose not to absorb it.\n\nFor one frightening beat, I thought she had missed.\n\nThen the spear crossed her empty reserve ring and entered Cassian's lane where he had already turned to meet it. His fire broke its point. Leon caught the remaining shaft with an ugly Blue barrier that knocked him to one knee.\n\nThe left lane opened again. Cassian could have taken the final construct and secured the clean finish. He looked at Leon instead.\n\nCassian: \"Can you hold?\"\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nCassian abandoned the point and caught the failing barrier with heat beneath it. Aira took the last construct alone. Her water line missed the center, clipped one edge, and sent the target tumbling across the recovery boundary instead of landing upright.\n\nThe field went dark. The score crystal considered us.\n\n**FUNCTIONAL. INCOMPLETE.**\n\nLeon remained on one knee. Cassian offered a hand. Leon looked at it, irritated by needing it, then took it.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"One target uncontrolled. Formation preserved. No team member abandoned.\"\n\nInstructor Dren pointed at the broken post.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Cost visible. Better.\"\n\nProfessor Halden looked at me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Your advance reading is valuable. Your certainty is not the same resource. Learn the difference.\"\n\nI wanted to argue that I had never been certain. The shattered stone behind Elira made that defense unworthy of the air.\n\nHaru: \"Understood.\"\n\nAs we reset, Elira stepped near enough that no one else needed to hear.\n\nElira: \"You trusted us to follow.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira: \"Next time, trust us to contradict you.\"\n\nAcross the ring, Aira inspected her scorched sleeve.\n\nAira: \"I would like the record to show that growth is expensive and this uniform was new.\"\n\nCassian: \"I saved the person inside it.\"\n\nAira: \"You also burned the person inside it.\"\n\nLeon rolled his aching shoulder.\n\nLeon: \"Both facts remain.\"\n\nNo one called us flawless. We were still summoned back for the next training hour.\n\n---\n\nLater that afternoon, the second major tournament notice arrived. This one was public.\n\nPosted in every central corridor, projected over the courtyard, and delivered to candidate groups through official crystal dispatch:\n\n**INTER-NATIONAL SCHOOLS TOURNAMENT - VISITING DELEGATIONS ARRIVE IN 5 DAYS**\n\nAt the bottom of the notice, a red student council seal burned with clean authority.\n\n**Emergency movement protocols authorized by Student Council President Rhea Valmont.**\n\nThe line was small. The reaction to it was not.\n\nTwo Red Circle students near the fountain stopped arguing mid-sentence and stepped out of the walkway. A cluster of Blue Circle candidates lowered their voices without an instructor telling them to. Even the floating notice crystals adjusted their height to keep the council seal visible above the crowd.\n\nAira followed my gaze.\n\nAira: \"President Valmont does not usually attach her name to logistics unless she expects people to behave badly.\"\n\nCassian gave a short laugh.\n\nCassian: \"Or unless she expects them to survive behaving badly.\"\n\nLeon frowned.\n\nLeon: \"You know her?\"\n\nCassian: \"Every Red Circle heir knows Rhea Valmont. Some because they admire her. Most because they have been corrected by her in language their parents could not legally argue with.\"\n\nI remembered the Red Circle student council president watching me after Leon's first defeat, her smile precise enough to feel like a blade placed flat against glass. So she had not vanished after noticing the thread. She had simply been standing where authority stood best: slightly ahead of disaster.\n\nBelow it appeared the list of confirmed competing institutions and the names of several high-profile student champions already known across the continent.\n\nThe campus reaction was immediate. Excitement rose, anxiety sharpened, and prestige became tangible. Students crowded the notices, identifying famous names, rumored prodigies, and rival academy legends.\n\nAurelis' internal selection was no longer theoretical. Time had become short.\n\nAira scanned the projected roster with growing dread.\n\nAira: \"This is worse than I thought.\"\n\nHaru: \"You say that often.\"\n\nAira: \"Because life keeps validating me.\"\n\nShe pointed at the list.\n\nAira: \"Look. Valemere has registered **Soren Ashvale**, the Red Circle duelist with six consecutive academy titles. Drakenshard is fielding **Mara Kest**, that battlefield monster everyone keeps politely calling disciplined. And Noctis Dominion is sending...\"\n\nShe went silent.\n\nThat silence did more than the names before it.\n\nSoren Ashvale had earned whispers of admiration. Mara Kest had earned nervous laughter. Noctis Dominion earned space.\n\nThe students packed around the notice did not all stop talking at once. Instead, conversations thinned in uneven layers. A group of Blue Circle candidates pretended to study Valemere's roster while their eyes kept sliding back to the black Noctis crest. Two Red Circle heirs exchanged looks and said nothing. A Green support student near the edge of the crowd took one step backward without realizing it.\n\nEven the projected banners above the courtyard seemed less bright around Noctis' name, swallowed rather than dimmed.\n\nI had heard of Noctis Dominion College before coming to Aurelis. Everyone had. It was not the kind of school people praised casually. They spoke of its discipline, its victory record, its severe training culture, its graduates who entered military offices, high courts, and private enforcement houses with smiles sharp enough to count as weapons.\n\nNoctis did not produce many beloved heroes. It produced results.\n\nLeon noticed where my gaze had gone.\n\nLeon: \"Their candidates don't break formation.\"\n\nCassian's smile faded by one degree.\n\nCassian: \"No. They break other things.\"\n\nAira looked at him.\n\nAira: \"That was not comforting.\"\n\nCassian: \"It was not meant to be.\"\n\nElira had not moved.\n\nThat was the part that caught me.\n\nElira Vale could stand before faculty, nobles, duelists, gossip, and pressure without giving away anything she did not choose to reveal. Her composure was trained, chosen, and reinforced by a will stronger than most magic I had seen at Aurelis.\n\nNow her stillness had changed from calm into containment. Whatever name waited after Noctis Dominion, Elira already knew its shape, and she was holding the memory in place so it would not touch anyone else before it had to.\n\nElira stepped closer to read. Her expression changed by half a degree. That was enough to matter.\n\nHaru: \"Who?\"\n\nElira answered quietly.\n\nElira: \"**Lucien Voss.**\"\n\nHer fingers closed around the edge of the roster hard enough to crease the projection light. The motion lasted only a heartbeat before she smoothed her hand flat again: composed, public, Yellow Circle perfect.\n\nBut I had seen it. Lucien's name did not frighten her like a rival; it struck something remembered.\n\nCassian, standing just behind us, gave a low whistle.\n\nCassian: \"That's a problem.\"\n\nLeon had approached unnoticed from the side and now looked at the notice with visible tension.\n\nLeon: \"Noctis actually sent him?\"\n\nAira looked between them.\n\nAira: \"That is too much collective discomfort for one name. Who is he?\"\n\nElira's gaze remained on the projection.\n\nElira: \"A Yellow Circle.\"\n\nThat alone quieted her. Elira continued.\n\nElira: \"Noctis Dominion's top prodigy. Lightless affinity variation. Exceptional close-range dominance. He's been called one of the most dangerous young magicians in the tournament circuit.\"\n\nCassian folded his arms.\n\nCassian: \"Dangerous is the polite word.\"\n\nAira: \"And the impolite one?\"\n\nCassian: \"Cruel.\"\n\nAira looked back at the projection, suddenly less theatrical.\n\nAira: \"Noctis listed him as captain.\"\n\nThe roster unfolded beneath Lucien's name in smaller letters.\n\n**Nera Vail - Blue Circle Binding**  \n**Oric Sen - Blue Circle Binding**  \n**Tovan Reiss - Red Circle Close Combat**  \n**Cael Nox - Conduit Support**\n\nThe last entry sat lower than the others, almost like the projection itself had been told to make it less important.\n\nThe roster expanded into formation notes when Aira touched the corner of the projection. Noctis' support pattern unfolded in thin pale lines.\n\nNera Vail occupied Lucien's right flank. The projection listed water-ice binding, joint containment, route suppression, emergency pressure stabilization. Efficient. Clean. Nothing decorative except the rings shown on both wrists, and even those were probably tools pretending to be jewelry.\n\nAira leaned closer despite herself.\n\nAira: \"Their support specialist is good.\"\n\nLeon: \"You can tell from a roster?\"\n\nAira: \"No. I can tell from what the roster does not bother explaining. See how all the containment paths assume she will adjust after Lucien moves? That is not a weak support caster. That is someone trusted to make his cruelty look like strategy.\"\n\nUnder Nera's name, Oric Sen's lines mirrored hers with less range and more defensive redundancy. Then Cael's route appeared. Not beside Lucien. Through him.\n\nAira's mouth flattened.\n\nAira: \"That is not a teammate diagram.\"\n\nElira's voice stayed quiet.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe projection flickered once, showing a practice still from Noctis' qualifying match. Nera stood with one hand extended, blue-gray eyes fixed forward while ice-bindings sealed a hazard corridor. Behind Lucien, Cael's shoulders had locked too high. Nera's gaze had shifted toward him by the smallest fraction.\n\nThen Lucien's hand lifted in the still image. Nera faced forward again.\n\nHaru: \"She saw it.\"\n\nAira: \"And continued.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nAira's expression turned sharper and less amused.\n\nAira: \"I hate mirrors when they are people.\"\n\nLeon frowned.\n\nLeon: \"Conduit support?\"\n\nCassian's expression lost some of its humor.\n\nCassian: \"Noctis likes power routes. A conduit takes strain so the main caster can push harder, longer, or through uglier angles.\"\n\nAira: \"A terrible job, then.\"\n\nElira's voice was quiet.\n\nElira: \"It can be, depending on who is allowed to say no.\"\n\nThe words did not sound theoretical.\n\nAira tapped the corner of the roster slate again, advancing Noctis' qualifying clip past the final buzzer into three unedited seconds of corridor feed their staff had neglected to trim.\n\nIn the grain of the recording, Nera Vail turned toward the pale younger student as he lowered his casting arm. Even through tinny slate audio, her voice came out too clipped for tenderness.\n\nNera: \"Your channels are strained. Report to medical before departure.\"\n\nCael's answer stayed below the pickup threshold. Nera's did not.\n\nNera: \"I am not asking.\"\n\nOric Sen stood behind her in the doorway, unreadable. He did not help, and he did not stop her either.\n\nThe clip ended and snapped back to Lucien's portrait before the roster dimmed.\n\nSmall mercy, I thought, could still be fear wearing a uniform.\n\nA second cold thread joined the first beneath my ribs. Lucien Voss was no longer only a name attached to Elira's history. He was a system walking toward us, wearing a student's face.\n\nA breeze passed through the courtyard. Mana banners shifted overhead.\n\nI watched Elira. There was something in her face I hadn't seen before. History, rather than simple fear.\n\nHaru: \"You know him.\"\n\nShe looked at me then, only for a second, and the answer was already there.\n\nElira: \"A little.\"\n\nAira noticed immediately.\n\nAira: \"How little is 'a little'?\"\n\nElira's voice remained composed.\n\nElira: \"Enough.\"\n\nThat was not enough for me. Not nearly.\n\nStill, we were in the middle of the courtyard, surrounded by students, faculty, and rumor. This was not the place, not yet.\n\nSomething cold moved quietly under my ribs: protectiveness sharpening into focus. If Lucien Voss mattered to Elira's expression even that much, then he mattered to me already.\n\nAnd I had a feeling the tournament was about to stop being merely competitive.\n\n---\n\nThe name **Lucien Voss** followed us through the rest of the day.\n\nIt did not spread like ordinary gossip. Ordinary gossip moved quickly because people enjoyed it. Lucien's name moved carefully because people respected the danger attached to it.\n\nBy evening, every candidate in Aurelis knew Noctis Dominion College was sending its top Yellow Circle prodigy. Every hallway conversation carried fragments of the same uneasy facts: lightless affinity, close-range dominance, cruel.\n\nBeneath all of that, the one detail that mattered most to me remained the one no one else could explain. Elira knew him.\n\nNot well, she had said. Enough.\n\nThat word stayed with me longer than I wanted it to.\n\nI did not ask her in the courtyard. That would have been careless: too many students, too many faculty eyes, too much rumor already turning every glance between us into a public event.\n\nSo I waited until the academy lights came on and the western garden paths grew quiet.\n\nElira stood beside me beneath a silver-leaf tree overlooking the lower city. The black necklace rested against her uniform, hidden beneath the appearance of a simple dark crystal pendant. To anyone else, it was pretty, minorly enchanted, harmless.\n\nTo me, it felt like a vow given shape.\n\nElira: \"You've been quiet.\"\n\nHaru: \"That usually describes me.\"\n\nElira looked at me with the gentle patience of someone who had learned the difference between silence and hiding.\n\nElira: \"Not like this.\"\n\nI exhaled slowly.\n\nHaru: \"Lucien.\"\n\nHer expression changed by less than a breath. Enough.\n\nElira turned toward the city lights below the cliff. For a few seconds, she watched the mana bridges glow in the distance as if the answer might be easier to give while looking at something far away.\n\nElira: \"He was part of the junior tournament circuit two years ago.\"\n\nHaru: \"You fought him?\"\n\nElira: \"Not officially.\"\n\nThat answer sharpened my attention.\n\nElira: \"Noctis Dominion hosted a youth exhibition. It was supposed to be controlled. Formal. Mostly political theater between schools.\"\n\nHaru: \"And it wasn't.\"\n\nShe shook her head once.\n\nElira: \"Lucien fought a Blue Circle student from Valemere. His opponent was skilled, but clearly outmatched. The match should have ended after the first decisive lock.\"\n\nHer hand moved unconsciously toward the pendant.\n\nElira: \"Instead, Lucien kept pressing. Not loudly. Not with rage. He was calm. Smiling. He used lightless binding to smother the boy's mana channels one by one until the barrier system registered surrender.\"\n\nMy chest went cold.\n\nHaru: \"The officials stopped it?\"\n\nElira: \"Late.\"\n\nNo bitterness entered her voice. That somehow made it worse.\n\nElira: \"The boy lived. He recovered enough to continue school. But his casting never fully returned to what it was.\"\n\nShe did not say the next part immediately.\n\nThe wind moved through the silver leaves. Far below, the city lights flickered in ordered constellations, beautiful from a distance and full of people close up. Elira watched them for a moment as if deciding how much of an old ugliness deserved to be carried into this night.\n\nElira: \"Afterward, the official report called it excessive competitive pressure.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is a careful phrase.\"\n\nElira: \"A dishonest one.\"\n\nHer voice stayed even. That made the anger beneath it cleaner.\n\nElira: \"His name is Talen Mire. His family still writes to ask whether anyone at Aurelis knows a healer who can help him cast at full strength again.\"\n\nHer fingers closed once against her palm.\n\nElira: \"I have never known what to answer. I gave a statement. So did two instructors from Valemere. Noctis argued that the surrender barrier had not triggered soon enough, which made continued pressure technically valid.\"\n\nHaru: \"Technically.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer jaw tightened for half a second before settling back into calm. She looked at me then.\n\nElira: \"It was the first time I understood how much cruelty can hide inside rules when the room wants the stronger person to be right.\"\n\nI thought of Green Circle students corrected into silence, Red heirs polished into weapons, Yellow prodigies turned into symbols before they were allowed to become people.\n\nHaru: \"What happened to Lucien?\"\n\nElira's mouth tightened.\n\nElira: \"Nothing public. Noctis praised his restraint because the boy survived.\"\n\nThe Black Circle gave one slow, cold pulse. Elira noticed.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'm listening.\"\n\nElira: \"I know. Listen to this part too.\"\n\nShe stepped closer, not to soften the story, but to make sure I heard the warning inside it.\n\nElira: \"Lucien does not need everyone to agree with him. He only needs the institution around him to hesitate long enough. He lives in that hesitation.\"\n\nBriefly, neither of us spoke.\n\nThe wind moved through the silver leaves above us. Below the cliff, Aurelis City glittered as if the world had no memory of cruelty.\n\nHaru: \"And you intervened.\"\n\nElira looked at me then.\n\nElira: \"After the match, yes.\"\n\nHaru: \"What happened?\"\n\nElira: \"I told him strength without mercy was just fear wearing a crown.\"\n\nDespite everything, that sounded exactly like her.\n\nHaru: \"And what did he say?\"\n\nHer gaze lowered for a moment.\n\nElira: \"That mercy was what powerful people invented to make weakness feel less embarrassing.\"\n\nThe hidden Black Circle beneath my ribs gave one slow pulse. Not anger yet. Recognition.\n\nHaru: \"So he hates weakness.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nHer voice softened, though the correction stayed firm.\n\nElira: \"He needs weakness to exist below him. There's a difference.\"\n\nShe looked past me, toward the darkening city below the western terrace.\n\nElira: \"When the healers reached Talen, Lucien was not angry that they helped. He was angry that they moved before anyone with higher rank allowed them to. A Valemere support student dropped beside Talen and held his wrist so the channel lock would not tear further, and Lucien looked at her as if she had made an error in grammar.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because she interfered?\"\n\nElira: \"Because she did not ask who owned the moment first.\"\n\nI looked at her carefully.\n\nHaru: \"Did he hurt you?\"\n\nElira met my eyes at once.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe answer was immediate, clean, and true.\n\nThen she stepped a little closer, as if she understood the shape of the question beneath the question.\n\nElira: \"Haru, Lucien was never anything like that to me.\"\n\nMy hands stopped moving.\n\nElira: \"Not a suitor. Not a lost affection. Not some complicated past that should make you wonder where you stand.\"\n\nThe directness of it caught me more effectively than subtlety would have.\n\nHaru: \"You noticed.\"\n\nElira's mouth curved faintly.\n\nElira: \"I notice you.\"\n\nThat was unfair, not the way attacks were unfair, but the way kindness could arrive too accurately and leave no room for defense.\n\nTwelve days had passed since the gate first counted me, five since the reveal in the Moonlit Archive. In that time Elira had argued with my routes, beaten me at two strategy boards, lost patience when I called self-erasure practical, and trusted me enough to be visibly tired in my company. I had learned that agreement was not the price of her staying.\n\nElira reached for my hand openly, without drama, and with more certainty than I felt capable of returning without practice.\n\nHer fingers slipped through mine beneath the silver-leaf tree, warm and steady.\n\nElira: \"I want this to belong to us before the school gives it a title. Do you?\"\n\nI looked down at our hands, then back at her.\n\nHaru: \"Yes. I do not know what to call it yet.\"\n\nElira's expression warmed.\n\nElira: \"Neither do I. That is allowed.\"\n\nNeither of us looked away.\n\nSilver leaves shifted above us, turning the last courtyard light into small moving pieces. The academy was still somewhere beyond the garden, raising banners, measuring candidates, teaching itself new ways to watch, but the space beneath the tree held only one question neither of us had named.\n\nElira: \"May I ask one more thing?\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer composure changed by less than a breath.\n\nElira: \"May I kiss you?\"\n\nI could have stopped an arena strike between heartbeats. I could have folded the distance between towers. None of that helped me answer a girl who had made room for refusal inside the thing she wanted. That mattered.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nMy fingers stayed warm around hers, steady enough not to rush her.\n\nShe moved slowly enough that I could have changed my mind and trusted me enough not to pretend I might. Her free hand touched my cheek. Then her lips met mine.\n\nThe kiss was brief and warm, careful without being uncertain.\n\nNo hidden circle opened. No ancient ward answered. My false Green crest remained a modest glow at my wrist beneath my cuff, and the pendant at her collarbone warmed once like a secret choosing not to become a spectacle.\n\nWhen she drew back, she stayed close enough that the next breath belonged to both of us.\n\nElira: \"Still want time before naming it?\"\n\nHaru: \"Less time than I did a minute ago.\"\n\nHer smile arrived slowly.\n\nHaru: \"Aira will be relieved.\"\n\nThat startled a laugh out of her.\n\nElira: \"Aira will say she knew before we did.\"\n\nHaru: \"She may be right.\"\n\nElira leaned lightly against my shoulder for half a second, so brief it could have been missed by anyone not paying attention. I was always paying attention to her now.\n\nElira: \"Lucien is dangerous because he enjoys turning people's bonds into pressure points. He watches who you protect, then tries to make protection look like weakness.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then he will misunderstand me.\"\n\nElira lifted her head.\n\nElira: \"How?\"\n\nI looked toward the dark outline of the tournament banners being raised across the lower courtyard.\n\nHaru: \"Protection is not my weakness.\"\n\nThe necklace at her collarbone gave a faint, hidden pulse. Elira felt it too.\n\nHer fingers tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know how to stop myself.\"\n\nElira: \"Do you?\"\n\nThere was no accusation in it, only concern. Because she had seen the Black Circle. She knew that my restraint was not a habit; it was a choice I had to keep making.\n\nHaru: \"I won't expose myself over an insult.\"\n\nElira: \"And if he threatens someone?\"\n\nHaru: \"Then I will stop him.\"\n\nElira held my gaze.\n\nElira: \"Carefully?\"\n\nI thought of Leon's spell collapsing in harmless rain. Of the archive sealed beneath moonlight. Of the necklace I had made because I could not always be near her.\n\nHaru: \"As carefully as the situation allows.\"\n\nShe looked only partly satisfied with that answer. Which was fair. It was only partly reassuring.\n\n---\n\nThe next morning, Aira stared at our hands for a full five seconds before speaking.\n\nWe were standing outside the upper strategy hall, waiting for Group Three's next training session to begin. Students passed in small clusters, slowing at the sight of Elira Vale and the Green Circle transfer standing together with a complete lack of embarrassment.\n\nAira: \"So we're doing this openly now.\"\n\nElira: \"Good morning, Aira.\"\n\nAira: \"No. I need the historical record to be accurate.\"\n\nHaru: \"The historical record?\"\n\nAira pointed at our joined hands.\n\nAira: \"Are you two officially dating?\"\n\nSeveral students nearby pretended not to hear and failed with impressive unity.\n\nElira did not look away.\n\nElira: \"We kissed. We are choosing this. We have not named it for the hallway.\"\n\nI felt half the hallway react. Aira turned to me.\n\nAira: \"And you agree with that remarkably composed evasion?\"\n\nHaru: \"It was not an evasion.\"\n\nElira: \"That means yes.\"\n\nAira closed her eyes as if a spiritual burden had become administratively complicated.\n\nAira: \"Fine. My sanity has provisional documentation.\"\n\nFrom behind us, Cassian's voice arrived bright with amusement.\n\nCassian: \"I leave you people alone for one evening and diplomacy advances.\"\n\nLeon followed beside him, expression carefully neutral.\n\nLeon: \"It was not subtle before.\"\n\nAira looked genuinely wounded.\n\nAira: \"Leon, if you become emotionally perceptive, this team loses balance.\"\n\nLeon: \"I said it was visible. That is not the same thing.\"\n\nCassian grinned at me.\n\nCassian: \"Congratulations, Green. You've officially made the academy hierarchy worse.\"\n\nHaru: \"That wasn't the objective.\"\n\nCassian: \"The best disasters rarely are.\"\n\nElira smiled, her fingers still comfortably linked with mine. There was no denial or retreat. The rumors around us changed shape in real time. That mattered, because secrecy had ruled so much of my life that even this small public truth felt strangely powerful.\n\nThen the strategy hall doors opened. Professor Halden stood inside.\n\nHis gaze lowered briefly to our hands, then lifted to my face.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"How fortunate. Group cohesion appears to be improving.\"\n\nAira muttered under her breath.\n\nAira: \"I hate when he says normal things in a suspicious voice.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Today's drill will test exactly that.\"\n\n---\n\nThe new exercise was called **Pressure Disclosure**.\n\nThat name alone made Aira object before the rules were finished.\n\nAira: \"Absolutely not. That title sounds like a confession trap with scoring.\"\n\nLady Seraphine, standing beside the projection board, did not blink.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Correct, except the word trap is emotionally imprecise.\"\n\nAira: \"I withdraw nothing.\"\n\nThe drill placed each team member under simulated isolation, then forced the others to decide whether to continue the objective or break formation to assist. It was about priorities: who protected whom, who trusted whom, and who panicked when a bond was stressed.\n\nHalden watched me while Lady Seraphine explained the rules. I understood then. This was observation dressed as training.\n\nThe first two rounds were difficult but manageable. Cassian was isolated behind a collapsing terrain wall and had to rely on Leon's fluid channels to open a route. Leon was pulled into a false duel scenario and forced to accept Aira's support timing instead of controlling everything himself. Both adapted, poorly at first, then better.\n\nThe third round targeted Elira.\n\nThe simulation dimmed, not into ordinary darkness, but into a colorless suppression field that spread across the arena floor and swallowed light as it moved. The construct at its center resembled no student exactly, yet the resemblance in technique was unmistakable: lightless affinity. Lucien.\n\nElira's posture changed by a fraction. Enough that I felt it.\n\nThe construct lifted one hand, and pale-black bands snapped toward her from three angles, designed not to injure but to bind her output and force a delayed release.\n\nHer necklace warmed. I felt it through the vow-thread hidden in the pendant: awareness rather than activation. The simulation was not a true hostile attack, so the pendant held. So did I. Barely.\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHer voice cut across the arena. She had seen me take one step forward. Leon saw it too.\n\nLeon: \"Hold formation.\"\n\nThat surprised me enough to stop.\n\nLeon did not look at me. His water mana spread across the floor, mapping the lightless bands with clean, disciplined arcs.\n\nLeon: \"If you rush, the field collapses inward. She has the center. We break the anchor points.\"\n\nThat was correct. Annoyingly correct.\n\nCassian glanced at me with a sharp grin.\n\nCassian: \"You heard him, Green. Let the Yellow be terrifying for a moment.\"\n\nElira's golden light gathered around her hands as the suppression bands tightened. For half a second, the old story she had told me lived in the air between us: a boy whose mana channels were smothered one by one while officials reacted too late.\n\nThen Elira moved.\n\nShe did not overpower the lightless construct. She understood it. Golden threads slid between the pale-black bands, finding the tiny spaces where control had become arrogance. Aira reinforced the openings. Leon cut the outer anchors. Cassian shattered the left stabilizer with one clean blow.\n\nI stayed where I was.\n\nWhen the final band tried to close around Elira's throat, I released a pulse so small no one else should have noticed: no Black Circle display, just a tiny correction, a refusal.\n\nThe band missed by an inch.\n\nElira broke the construct herself. Golden light burst outward, clean and bright, and the suppression field scattered across the arena like ash.\n\nThe drill ended. Silence followed. Elira turned toward me.\n\nShe had felt the nudge. Yet she did not look angry, only relieved that I had trusted her enough to wait.\n\nLady Seraphine made several notes. Instructor Dren looked almost satisfied. Halden looked fascinated, which was the least comforting reaction available.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Excellent restraint.\"\n\nI did not answer.\n\nProfessor Halden's eyes remained on me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"From everyone.\"\n\nAira leaned toward Cassian.\n\nAira: \"He added that because he realized it sounded targeted.\"\n\nCassian: \"It was targeted.\"\n\nLeon exhaled.\n\nLeon: \"Everything here is targeted.\"\n\nFor once, no one argued.\n\n---\n\nFour more days vanished into drills that felt less like preparation and more like stress-testing glass until it learned not to shatter.\n\nEvery morning before dawn, Instructor Dren ran Group Three through blind-terrain rotations until we stopped asking who held command and started reading each other's shoulders instead. Leon shaved half a second off his transition lag. Cassian learned to call a lane break before he crossed it, grumbling the entire time. Aira filled two notebooks with foreign roster patterns and insisted on making me read Valemere's junior-circuit reports over cold tea.\n\nEvery afternoon, Professor Halden opened unannounced observation windows mid-exercise and watched how closely I stayed to Elira whenever the field tilted.\n\nBy the fifth evening, the academy banners hung full-length from the western battlements, and the visiting delegations finally arrived.\n\nStudents crowded balconies and courtyard rails while the western gate unfolded its ceremonial barrier. Banners from foreign institutions appeared in the air one by one, carried by floating sigils and escort lights.\n\nValemere Royal Institute arrived first in silver-blue carriages pulled by winged mana constructs: polished lines, clean banners, escort mages who bowed to Aurelis faculty instead of pretending not to measure them. Their magic did not press against the air the way Noctis rumors did. It moved like formal footwork. Graceful, dangerous, and honest about both.\n\nAt the head of their student delegation stood Soren Ashvale.\n\nHe was taller than most of the students around him, dark-haired, warm-eyed, and carried himself like a duelist who had learned that confidence did not require making other people smaller. Faint scars crossed the backs of his hands. He wore them openly.\n\nWhen he saw Elira, he approached with a respectful distance already built into his stride.\n\nSoren: \"Lady Vale.\"\n\nElira's public composure settled around her at once.\n\nElira: \"Mr. Ashvale.\"\n\nSoren smiled, neither possessive nor overly familiar.\n\nSoren: \"I've followed your work in the junior circuit. Your paper on layered reinforcement changed how Valemere teaches counter-pressure in formal dueling.\"\n\nElira's surprise lasted only a heartbeat, but I saw it.\n\nElira: \"That is generous.\"\n\nSoren: \"It is accurate.\"\n\nHis gaze moved to me, assessing cleanly without dismissal.\n\nSoren: \"Your partner?\"\n\nElira did not hesitate.\n\nElira: \"Yes. Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nThe word partner moved through me with more force than it should have.\n\nSoren offered his hand.\n\nSoren: \"Lucky man.\"\n\nHis grip was firm, honest, and without challenge.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira's fingers found mine before I could decide whether the answer had been too revealing. Soren noticed, smiled once, and released my hand.\n\nSoren: \"Then I look forward to facing Aurelis properly.\"\n\nHe returned to Valemere without looking back.\n\nAt Valemere, honor had not been taught to Soren as ornament or lineage, but as responsibility. I knew why from the junior-circuit report Aira had forced on me two nights earlier: years ago, a defeated student had stayed down longer than the official record admitted. Soren had been young enough to keep his place, frightened enough to call that caution, and honest enough to condemn his own silence in his appeal. The scars across his hands came from the rescue spell he cast after the judges had already declared the match finished. Watching him step away from Elira without trying to turn courtesy into leverage, I believed he still meant every word of it.\n\nAira appeared beside us as if summoned by social tension.\n\nAira: \"That was the most polite romantic threat I have ever witnessed.\"\n\nHaru: \"He was not threatening.\"\n\nAira: \"No. That is what made it effective.\"\n\nElira's thumb moved once against my hand in quiet recognition. Soren had admired her without trying to claim her, and recognized us without testing whether we were real. That kind of honor was inconvenient. It made what followed feel colder before the next carriage even crossed the gate.\n\nDrakenshard Military Academy rolled in behind Valemere with armored transports that looked more like moving fortresses than student vehicles.\n\nThen the air grew quiet.\n\nThe Noctis Dominion procession entered without music.\n\nBlack glass carriages rolled through the gate under banners marked with a pale eclipse. Their uniforms were dark, formal, and severe, threaded with faint lines of silver. Even their escort lights seemed dimmer than everyone else's.\n\nAt the center carriage, the door opened.\n\nLucien Voss stepped out.\n\nHe was tall and composed, with ash-blond hair cut neatly away from a face too calm to be called gentle. His eyes were pale gray, almost colorless, and the Yellow Circle at his collarbone glowed with a strange muted radiance, like sunlight seen through smoke.\n\nThe crowd lowered its voice instinctively.\n\nOne of the younger Noctis students stepped down behind him too quickly and stumbled when the courtyard's ambient mana pressed against his travel-weary barrier. It was a small mistake. Harmless.\n\nLucien did not turn fully. He only lifted two fingers.\n\nA pale-black ring closed around the student's wrist and snapped his mana flow into stillness. The boy went rigid, breath catching, while the unstable barrier around him folded into perfect shape.\n\nLucien: \"Posture.\"\n\nThe word was soft. That made it worse.\n\nThe Noctis student bowed his head at once. Cael's expression did not change, but something behind his eyes went carefully blank, the look of someone who had learned that visible pain only made correction last longer.\n\nTwo other Noctis students stepped down behind him without looking at the boy for more than a second.\n\nOne was a girl with a narrow braid and blue-gray eyes, her binding rings already aligned around both wrists like jewelry that had forgotten how to be decorative. Nera Vail, if the roster was accurate.\n\nThe other, Oric Sen, wore the same Blue Circle insignia, but his gaze measured exits, teachers, and the distance between Lucien and every possible witness.\n\nTovan Reiss came last, broad-shouldered, red crest burning low and disciplined at his collar. He looked less cruel than Lucien, which did not make him kind; it made him someone trained not to ask whether the formation deserved obedience.\n\nThe younger student Lucien had corrected kept his eyes down. Cael Nox. Conduit support. A route: that was how Noctis had listed him, a way for someone else's power to travel.\n\nNo anger, embarrassment, or apology crossed Lucien's face. He had corrected a person the way someone else might straighten a crooked sleeve.\n\nLucien looked across the courtyard, past the banners, past the faculty, past the staring students. His gaze found Elira, then me beside her, then our joined hands.\n\nA faint smile touched his mouth, neither warm nor surprised, only interested. His fingers tapped once against his side. Only once. Then they went still again.\n\nLucien walked toward us with the patient confidence of someone used to rooms making space before he asked.\n\nElira did not move behind me. I did not step in front of her. We stood side by side. That mattered too.\n\nLucien stopped a few paces away and gave a polished bow just shallow enough to be insulting if someone cared to notice.\n\nLucien: \"Lady Elira. Aurelis looks brighter than I remember.\"\n\nElira: \"Lucien.\"\n\nThere was no honorific or warmth. His eyes moved to me.\n\nLucien: \"And this is?\"\n\nBefore anyone else could answer, Elira's hand tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"Haru Aerlen. He stands with me.\"\n\nThe courtyard heard it. All of it.\n\nAira, standing behind us, whispered with something like reverent exhaustion.\n\nAira: \"Subtle. Terrifying. Very you.\"\n\nLucien's smile did not change, but something behind his eyes sharpened.\n\nLucien: \"A Green Circle partner for Aurelis' golden prodigy.\"\n\nFor less than a heartbeat, the polish cracked. Surprise, not anger, as if he had expected Elira to deny the rumor, laugh it aside, or dress it in political language sensible people could file away. As if a Yellow Circle prodigy genuinely choosing a Green Circle student was something his world had no shelf for.\n\nThen the mask returned: cold, precise, contemptuous. But I had seen it.\n\nMurmurs moved through the crowd. He had not raised his voice. He did not need to. Green Circle. Partner. Golden prodigy. A neat arrangement of words designed to make every watching student decide whether I was unworthy, whether Elira was careless, or whether Aurelis had begun hiding strategy behind romance.\n\nLucien let the murmurs breathe, then looked at our joined hands again, appraising them the way a collector might look at a priceless blade being used to cut rope.\n\nLucien: \"How generous of Aurelis.\"\n\nHis gaze dropped to Elira's pendant, barely enough for the air between us to change.\n\nA thread of his mana drifted outward, pale-black and thin as hair, disguised beneath the ambient pressure of his arrival. It did not attack. That was the danger. The pendant would not respond violently to curiosity. Lucien knew how to make violation look like weather.\n\nI moved nothing visible.\n\nThe Black Circle moved once.\n\nTier 1. Small. Surgical. I did not block his thread; blocking would have taught him shape. I changed the distance instead. For less than a heartbeat, the space between Lucien's drifting mana and Elira's pendant became longer than the courtyard allowed.\n\nHis thread failed to arrive.\n\nLucien's smile did not change. His eyes did.\n\nTo anyone else, nothing had happened. To Yellow perception, the courtyard had skipped: leaf-shadow, breath, mana pressure, all continuing except one narrow line of air that refused sequence.\n\nElira's fingers tightened around mine. She had felt the cold. Lucien had felt the absence.\n\nLucien: \"Interesting.\"\n\nBehind us, Aira's voice dropped to a whisper.\n\nAira: \"That is never a good word from him.\"\n\nLucien looked at me then, no longer as a Green Circle nuisance attached to Elira's reputation, but as an accounting error inside reality. From that moment forward, Lucien stopped testing whether I loved Elira. He began testing what answered when I protected her.\n\nElira's expression did not change.\n\nLucien: \"Most schools would assign a Green Circle escort to logistics, not reputation.\"\n\nThe words passed through the courtyard like a wire pulled tight. Several students laughed before deciding whether it was safe to laugh, then stopped as Elira turned her head just enough for the nearest laugh to die in the student's throat.\n\nI felt Elira's mana warm at my side, steady rather than defensive. I answered before she needed to.\n\nHaru: \"Haru is fine.\"\n\nLucien looked at me directly for the first time. His gaze dropped to my wrist, where the false Green crest showed beneath my cuff. Green. Small. The easiest lie in the world for someone like him to believe.\n\nLucien: \"Is it?\"\n\nHe sounded genuinely curious, which made the insult sharper.\n\nLucien: \"Forgive me. I am trying to understand the local custom. At Noctis, Yellow Circle prodigies are not encouraged to dilute their standing for sentimental experiments.\"\n\nAira inhaled behind us. Cassian's fire crest gave one amused, dangerous flicker. Leon stopped mid-breath.\n\nElira's hand remained in mine. Her fingers did not tighten this time; they relaxed. That was worse for Lucien.\n\nElira: \"Then Noctis remains educationally limited.\"\n\nSomeone in the crowd made a sound that was almost a laugh and almost fear.\n\nLucien's smile became finer.\n\nLucien: \"I see. So it is not a strategy.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nLucien: \"Not a cover.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nLucien: \"Not a charity.\"\n\nFor the first time, her gold crest brightened, only at the edge, enough.\n\nElira: \"Careful.\"\n\nLucien's eyes flicked to the crest, then back to her face.\n\nLucien: \"There she is.\"\n\nThe line was soft, satisfied, and cruel in the precise way of someone who enjoyed making dignity defend itself.\n\nThe Black Circle beneath my ribs pressed cold behind my sternum. Ozone and metal touched the back of my tongue. I kept it asleep.\n\nNear the faculty line, Professor Halden watched the exchange with far too much attention.\n\nLucien inclined his head toward Elira again.\n\nLucien: \"I look forward to seeing whether Aurelis has grown stronger, or merely more sentimental.\"\n\nElira's voice remained calm.\n\nElira: \"It has.\"\n\nLucien's gaze flicked once more to me.\n\nLucien: \"Then I look forward to seeing whether Aurelis protects its choices after the audience loses patience with the performance.\"\n\nHe turned and walked toward the Noctis delegation without waiting for dismissal.\n\nConversations restarted badly, in uneven pieces, as if everyone had to decide which version of the scene they wanted to repeat later.\n\nAira stepped up beside us.\n\nAira: \"I dislike him immediately.\"\n\nHer voice was light. Her pencil had snapped in her hand. She looked down at the two pieces as if personally offended by them.\n\nAira: \"And now I need a new pencil, which makes it political.\"\n\nLeon, on her other side, looked unusually serious.\n\nLeon: \"That was him being polite.\"\n\nHis gaze stayed on the place where Lucien had stood. For once, there was no old rivalry in the way he looked at our joined hands, only the uncomfortable recognition of a room being invited to laugh at rank.\n\nCassian smiled, with no humor in it.\n\nCassian: \"Good. I was worried the tournament might be boring.\"\n\nElira looked at me. Her hand was still in mine.\n\nHaru: \"I'm all right.\"\n\nElira: \"I didn't ask.\"\n\nHaru: \"You were going to.\"\n\nThe corner of her mouth softened despite the tension.\n\nElira: \"Maybe.\"\n\nAcross the courtyard, Lucien paused beside the Noctis banners and looked back once. Not at Elira this time. At me.\n\nElira watched him go.\n\nElira: \"He was surprised.\"\n\nAira frowned.\n\nAira: \"That was surprise?\"\n\nElira: \"And contempt.\"\n\nHer hand tightened once around mine, then steadied again.\n\nElira: \"Lucien believes in the Circle hierarchy the way some people believe in gravity. Not as policy. As truth.\"\n\nHaru: \"And we violated his truth.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nCassian's grin faded.\n\nCassian: \"That sounds worse than irritating him.\"\n\nElira: \"It is. Irritation passes. Lucien corrects violations.\"\n\nLeon looked toward the black banners.\n\nLeon: \"So next time he will be prepared.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nAcross the courtyard, the Noctis formation closed around Lucien again.\n\nCael Nox stood exactly where a conduit support was supposed to stand: close enough to be useful, far enough back that no one important had to look at him unless something went wrong. He flexed the wrist Lucien had corrected, then stopped when Nera Vail's gaze touched the motion. She saw the tremor. So did Oric. Neither of them moved, because Noctis had trained understanding to arrive after permission.\n\nLucien spoke without turning.\n\nLucien: \"Cael.\"\n\nThe boy's hand dropped to his side.\n\nCael: \"Yes, Voss.\"\n\nLucien: \"Tomorrow's calibration will use the extended relay pattern.\"\n\nNera's binding rings clicked once, too softly for most of the courtyard to hear. Oric heard it. Cael heard it. Lucien heard it too.\n\nHis pale eyes shifted toward Nera with the mild interest of a teacher noticing poor posture.\n\nLucien: \"Is there a technical concern?\"\n\nNera's expression stayed composed.\n\nNera: \"Extended relay patterns increase conduit strain if the anchor overcorrects.\"\n\nLucien smiled.\n\nLucien: \"Then the conduit should not overcorrect.\"\n\nCael's face emptied itself of reaction.\n\nThat was what made the moment stay with me. Fear had many shapes; panic was the easiest to notice, but obedience was quieter and far more useful to cruel people.\n\nNera lowered her eyes first.\n\nNera: \"Understood.\"\n\nOric's mouth opened by a fraction, then closed.\n\nLucien turned away as if the conversation had been about equipment. The Noctis formation moved on.\n\nNera looked back once, not at me, at Cael's wrist, then at her own rings. For the first time since Lucien stepped through the gate, her discipline looked less like loyalty than a lock waiting for someone to admit it existed.\n\nThe hidden darkness near my heart pulsed slowly. Patient.\n\nThe visiting schools had arrived. The tournament had not officially started.\n\nBut the first challenge had already been made.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Soren Ashvale - Before the Blade\n\nNear the Valemere carriages, Soren Ashvale watched the Noctis delegation disappear beneath their black banners.\n\nOne of his teammates, a silver-blue support caster with her arms folded tightly against the evening chill, leaned closer.\n\nValemere Student: \"Voss does not waste much time.\"\n\nSoren's expression stayed polite in the way duelists sometimes looked polite right before drawing a blade.\n\nSoren: \"Voss does not compete.\"\n\nHis teammate glanced at him.\n\nSoren: \"He dominates. There is a difference.\"\n\nAcross the courtyard, Elira Vale still stood beside Haru Aerlen with her hand in his, unhidden despite every whisper Lucien had tried to sharpen. Soren saw that too, and looked away first, without bitterness.\n\nValemere Student: \"Will Valemere get involved?\"\n\nSoren adjusted the worn dueling glove over one scarred hand.\n\nSoren: \"If this becomes about honor, yes.\"\n\nHe turned back toward his own delegation, leaving the sentence behind like a promise that had not yet chosen its battlefield.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Mira Castellan - First Page\n\nMira Castellan did not mean to start a record.\n\nShe had come back from the western walkway intending to finish her herbology notes, avoid the louder tournament students, and stop thinking about how Haru Aerlen's false Green crest had held its exact, quiet shape at his wrist when Soren Ashvale shook his hand.\n\nFalse was the wrong word. Mira crossed it out with a nervous scratch of ink. Careful, she wrote instead.\n\nOutside the tall library windows, the tournament perimeter wards hummed as the last visiting carriages cleared the gate. The beveled glass shade on her table lamp caught the shifting blue-gold mana drift from the courtyard and bent it across the brass ring: four standard academy colors refracted in pale bands, with a fifth, darker shadow-ring hugging the bottom rim where the bevel cut the light. Mira's pen froze above the page, her pulse jumping against her collar until the courtyard wards settled and the glass went ordinary again.\n\nAcross the room, two Blue Circle students complained about Green witnesses being allowed near tournament review files. Mira exhaled, wiped a smudge of ink from her thumb, and dipped her pen again before anger could make her handwriting ugly.\n\n*Observed: Haru Aerlen avoids victory language. Elira Vale says partner without hesitation. Visiting champion Soren Ashvale notices both and does not challenge. Lucien Voss tests the air between them and stops smiling.*\n\nShe hesitated, tapping the nib lightly against the inkwell, then added one more line.\n\n*The academy noticed too.*\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Lucien Voss - Distance\n\nLucien closed the Noctis delegation door with two fingers.\n\nThere was no slam or visible anger. The corridor outside his quarters carried the disciplined hush of a foreign school pretending it had not noticed anything worth fearing. That was useful; fear became easier to manage once it had been trained to stand still.\n\nHe removed his gloves first. Left, then right. Order always mattered after contact with something disobedient.\n\nOn the desk, he placed three objects in a line: the tournament arrival slate, a black relay stylus, and a thin strip of detection paper treated with Noctis reagent. The reagent had been developed for old binding residues, inheritance seals, bloodline claims, and sentimental protection charms of the sort families gave heirs before pretending affection was not a security weakness.\n\nLucien touched one end of the paper. Pale-black mana moved from his fingertip.\n\nIt should have crossed the paper in a clean line. Instead, halfway across, the line stopped. Neither blocked nor burned. Stopped as if the rest of the paper had become conceptually farther away.\n\nLucien watched it for nine seconds, then smiled, not with pleasure, with recognition.\n\nLucien: \"There you are.\"\n\nThe pendant had not defended Elira like ordinary protection. Ordinary protection announced a boundary: shield, seal, counterforce, warning flare. This had declined the premise of approach. His mana had not been repelled from the girl; it had been denied the right to define near.\n\nThat was not Yellow. It was not Green. It was not any rank that still needed comparison to feel tall.\n\nHe wrote one word on the slate.\n\n**DISTANCE**\n\nThen another.\n\n**PERSONAL**\n\nThen he stopped. The third word wanted to be impossible.\n\nNoctis disliked that word. Impossible was what weak students said before instruction corrected them, what families said when asked to surrender a child to a better use, the first refuge of the undisciplined mind.\n\nLucien had learned that before he learned formal lightless binding.\n\nHe had been eleven when Magister Voss placed a cup of water on a table and told him to lift it without touching it. He had failed for two hours. At the end, thirsty enough that his tongue felt too large for his mouth, he had asked for water.\n\nHis father had looked almost gentle.\n\nMagister Voss: \"Need is a debt. Debt is a handle. Never offer yours without deciding who may hold it.\"\n\nThen he had drunk the water himself.\n\nLucien had lifted the empty cup on the next attempt. Everyone called that progress.\n\nAfterward, Nera had slipped him half a citrus candy from the training infirmary and pretended she had dropped it by accident. Oric had stood in the doorway and looked away so neither of them would have to name the exchange.\n\nLucien had eaten the candy in one precise bite. He had hated them both for seeing. He had kept them close anyway.\n\nPeople misread that as attachment. Attachment was an unpriced vulnerability. Nera and Oric had been early evidence that order could be made from witnesses if the witnesses learned where to stand. Their presence proved that the child at the table had not merely been denied water; he had become the one who decided when thirst meant obedience.\n\nLucien looked at the stopped reagent line.\n\nElira Vale had chosen a Green Circle student and the world had not rearranged itself to punish the absurdity. That was irritating.\n\nHaru Aerlen had answered approach without moving. That was useful.\n\nThe pendant had made distance personal. That was unacceptable.\n\nLucien wrote the third word.\n\n**SOVEREIGN**\n\nThen he crossed it out, not because it was wrong, but because names given too early became shrines, and shrines attracted fools.\n\nHe opened a second slate and entered the only report Noctis needed for now.\n\n**Elira Vale remains viable pressure locus. Haru Aerlen response layer unclassified. Test through relational boundary, not rank insult alone.**\n\nHis hand paused over the final line.\n\nFor one second, the quarters were too quiet. No Nera outside the door pretending not to worry, no Oric counting exits, no old training room, no cup.\n\nHe corrected the thought before it became indulgence. Quiet was not absence. Quiet was available space.\n\nLucien put his gloves back on. Right, then left.\n\nHe left the failed reagent paper on the desk where he could see it when he woke. Some impossibilities became more obedient after being watched.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Professor Halden - Document First\n\nProfessor Halden did not return directly to the faculty residences after the delegations dispersed.\n\nHe went to the sealed records annex beneath the advanced mana studies wing and opened a drawer that had not been indexed in the public catalog for forty years.\n\nHe had watched the courtyard exchange from the faculty line. Lucien Voss's reaction had been instructive. Not the insult; insults were easy. The surprise: genuine, quickly buried, and therefore more useful than anything Lucien had chosen to say aloud.\n\nElira Vale had publicly chosen to stand beside a Green Circle student, and Lucien Voss had needed a heartbeat to make the fact fit inside his worldview. Halden disliked heartbeats that changed investigations.\n\nThe file inside was thin. Too thin for a myth that had frightened entire institutions.\n\nBlack Circle references did not survive in clean histories. They survived in warnings, redactions, disaster summaries, and committee reports written by people trying to sound calm after doing something unforgivable.\n\nHalden read until the mana lamp beside him burned low.\n\nOne fragment described a city-state that tried to conscript an unclassified celestial-rank caster. Another described a royal college that attempted containment under faculty authority. A third ended halfway through a sentence, the remaining page burned into glass.\n\nHe was not hostile to Haru Aerlen. That would have been simpler.\n\nHe was terrified of what history taught: every time something like Black Circle emerged, institutions fell. Not because the power was evil, necessarily, but because people could not resist trying to control it, weaponize it, or destroy it, and in trying, destroyed everything else.\n\nHalden closed the file with two fingers.\n\nLucien would not remain surprised. That was the problem with intelligent cruelty: it learned.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Document first.\"\n\nHis voice sounded too loud in the archive.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Possess nothing.\"\n\nCuriosity remained. So did fear. For once, he allowed both to stand without pretending one was virtue and the other was weakness.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-04-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep5","label":"Episode 5","number":"05","file":"episode-05-cover.webp","fallback":"Rumors Under Sunlight","eyebrow":"Tournament Arc","recap":"","accent":["#ff006e","#3a86ff","#ffbe0b"],"displayLabel":"Episode 5","displayNumber":"05","displayEyebrow":"Tournament Faultlines","arcLabel":"Tournament Faultlines","title":"The Opening Fault","body":"The tournament began with bells. Not academy bells.\n\nWar bells dressed as ceremony.\n\nAt sunrise, every tower of Aurelis Arcane Academy rang in layered silver tones that moved through the campus like waves across glass. The sound reached the dormitories, the training fields, the guest wings, the city below the cliffs, and every student pretending they had slept well.\n\nI had not slept much. It was not unusual.\n\nWhat was unusual was the reason.\n\nLucien Voss had looked at me once across the courtyard, and the hidden Black Circle beneath my false crest had answered with patience instead of sleep.\n\nThe burned listening glyph in the north library had not left a name behind. It had left **SEEN**.\n\nNow the clean academic residue had a face. That bothered me.\n\nPower did not usually react to people.\n\nIt reacted to threats, systems, ancient seals, collapsing barriers, unstable spells, and things that carried enough force to matter.\n\nBut Lucien was not powerful enough to threaten what I truly was. Not even close.\n\nSo the reaction was not fear. It was recognition.\n\nAs if something in his lightless mana belonged to an old language my power remembered before I did.\n\nI stood before the mirror in my dormitory room and adjusted my sleeve cuff until the false Green Circle crest rested harmlessly against my wrist. Small. Dim. Acceptable.\n\nHaru Aerlen, Green Circle transfer student.\n\nSmall enough for the morning to pass over.\n\nA knock came at my door. I opened it.\n\nAira stood in the hallway with a folded tournament schedule, two nutrient bars, and the expression of someone who had already fought three disasters before breakfast.\n\nAira: \"Eat one.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good morning.\"\n\nAira: \"That is aspirational. Eat.\"\n\nI accepted the bar. Aira watched until I opened it.\n\nAira: \"Elira is waiting near the east stairs. Leon is pretending not to be nervous. Cassian is pretending nervousness is a breakfast seasoning. The opening ceremony begins in forty minutes, and Professor Halden has already looked satisfied twice.\"\n\nHaru: \"That last one seems serious.\"\n\nAira: \"It is the worst item on the list.\"\n\nWe started walking.\n\nThe dormitory corridors were already full of tournament candidates in polished uniforms. Aurelis students wore silver-blue accents. Visiting students wore their own colors: Valemere's white and blue, Drakenshard's iron red, Sol Aeternum's bright sun-gold, Noctis Dominion's severe black and silver.\n\nThe academy no longer felt like a school. It felt like a stage, and everyone on it understood that losing would be remembered.\n\nElira waited near the east staircase with sunlight falling behind her. The black necklace rested beneath the appearance of a dark crystal pendant at her collarbone. Her Yellow Circle crest glowed above it, steady and bright.\n\nShe smiled when she saw me. That helped.\n\nElira: \"You look awake.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is kind.\"\n\nAira: \"It is inaccurate.\"\n\nElira's smile warmed, but her eyes studied my face carefully.\n\nElira: \"Lucien?\"\n\nThere was no need to ask what she meant.\n\nHaru: \"Partly.\"\n\nHer hand brushed mine for one quiet second, brief and unhidden.\n\nElira: \"We face what is in front of us, not what he wants us to imagine.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is strategy.\"\n\nElira: \"It is also self-care.\"\n\nAira looked between us.\n\nAira: \"I dislike how reasonable romance is making both of you.\"\n\nLeon joined us at the bottom of the stairs, adjusting one glove with unnecessary precision.\n\nLeon: \"Are we ready?\"\n\nAira: \"You asked that like a normal teammate. I am unsettled.\"\n\nLeon: \"I can insult someone if that restores your balance.\"\n\nCassian arrived behind him with his red hair tied back and his formal mantle hanging open as if protocol had personally offended him.\n\nCassian: \"Save it for Noctis. They look like funeral statues with excellent posture.\"\n\nLeon: \"Do not start an international incident before the first event.\"\n\nCassian: \"I said excellent posture. That was diplomacy.\"\n\nElira: \"It was almost diplomacy.\"\n\nThe five of us walked toward the Grand Arena together. Group Three: a Yellow Circle prodigy, a hidden Black Circle, a tactical Blue who worried professionally, a prideful Blue learning teamwork against his will, and a Red Circle heir who smiled at danger like it owed him money.\n\nIt should not have worked. That was probably why Aurelis was watching us.\n\n---\n\nThe Grand Arena floated above the central courtyard on six enormous mana pylons.\n\nDuring normal school weeks, it was dormant.\n\nToday it had unfolded into the tournament's opening qualification block, large enough to hold the academy, visiting delegations, city officials, sponsors, judges, faculty, and thousands of projected spectators watching from remote viewing halls across the continent.\n\nTiered seats curved around a massive central field of white stone and black glass. Floating screens displayed school crests. Barrier rings turned slowly overhead like transparent halos. At the highest platform, the judges sat beneath the continental tournament banner.\n\nThe Inter-National Schools Tournament had officially arrived.\n\nAurelis students cheered when our candidates entered.\n\nValemere answered with polished applause.\n\nDrakenshard struck their fists against armored forearms in a rolling metallic rhythm.\n\nSol Aeternum released harmless golden sparks into the air.\n\nNoctis Dominion made no sound at all.\n\nThat silence drew more attention than cheering would have.\n\nLucien stood at the front of their delegation, hands folded behind his back, expression calm enough to look bored.\n\nHis gaze found Elira first. Then me. Then our team. He smiled faintly.\n\nAira: \"I want it recorded that I do not like being smiled at like a research sample.\"\n\nHaru: \"Recorded.\"\n\nCassian: \"I feel flattered.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is because your survival instincts are decorative.\"\n\nBefore Cassian could answer, the arena lights dimmed.\n\nHeadmaster Veris stepped onto the central platform.\n\nHis voice carried through amplification runes.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"Students, faculty, honored guests, and representatives of the participating nations. Aurelis Arcane Academy welcomes you to the Inter-National Schools Tournament.\"\n\nApplause rolled through the arena.\n\nHe continued with the formal history. Prestige. Excellence. The responsibility of power.\n\nThe usual language institutions used when they wanted competition to sound noble.\n\nI listened with half my attention.\n\nThe other half watched the barrier rings.\n\nSomething in the upper eastern array was misaligned by less than a finger's width.\n\nToo small for most people to notice.\n\nToo deliberate to be maintenance error.\n\nI looked toward the faculty platform.\n\nProfessor Halden was already watching me.\n\nBeside him, Lady Seraphine's eyes moved from the barrier ring to my face, then back to the field.\n\nShe had noticed something too. Not enough, but something.\n\nThe Headmaster raised one hand.\n\nHeadmaster Veris: \"This year's opening event will be the **Crest Relay Trial**.\"\n\nThe central field transformed. White stone sank away. Black glass split into five paths. Forest, ruins, water channels, floating bridges, and mirrored corridors rose from the floor in a structured simulation. A display appeared overhead.\n\n**OPENING EVENT: CREST RELAY TRIAL**\n\n**Objective:** Each school sends one five-person team.\n\n**Goal:** Secure three crest fragments and return them to the center gate.\n\n**Scoring:** Time, teamwork, mana control, rescue response, and penalty avoidance.\n\n**Combat:** Limited interference permitted. Direct injury prohibited.\n\n**Warning:** The field changes every five minutes.\n\nAira stared at the rules.\n\nAira: \"Rescue response is scored?\"\n\nLeon: \"That means the field will create rescue scenarios.\"\n\nAira: \"I understand. I object to being emotionally prepared.\"\n\nCassian rolled his shoulders.\n\nCassian: \"Limited interference permitted. Good.\"\n\nElira: \"Direct injury prohibited.\"\n\nCassian: \"Also good. Less paperwork.\"\n\nProfessor Halden approached our team from the side of the platform.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Group Three will represent Aurelis in the opening event.\"\n\nLeon stopped mid-breath.\n\nAira: \"Immediately? We are doing this immediately?\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The tournament rarely improves when students are given time to overthink.\"\n\nAira: \"I have built my identity around overthinking.\"\n\nProfessor Halden ignored her. His gaze settled on me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Aerlen, your role remains tactical movement analysis.\"\n\nHaru: \"Understood.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Try to be appropriately impressive.\"\n\nHaru: \"That seems subjective.\"\n\nHis mouth moved almost into a smile.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Precisely.\"\n\nElira's shoulder shifted closer to mine, just enough for me to feel the warning. Not here. I knew.\n\nOn the opposite platform, Noctis Dominion's five-person roster stepped forward, Lucien at the center.\n\nNera Vail and Oric Sen flanked him in dark Blue Circle uniforms, binding rings quiet against Nera's wrists.\n\nTovan Reiss stood on the right flank, Red Circle striker's stance already coiled.\n\nBehind Lucien stood Cael Nox, the younger conduit student Lucien had corrected the previous evening, face pale and posture ruler-straight.\n\nLucien looked across the distance.\n\nLucien: \"Good luck, Lady Elira.\"\n\nElira: \"To your team as well.\"\n\nHis gaze slid to me.\n\nLucien: \"And to your Green.\"\n\nThe arena heard the phrase. Polite enough to pass, possessive enough to cut.\n\nElira did not move. Her voice stayed calm.\n\nElira: \"Haru is not mine because he is weak.\"\n\nMurmurs erupted across the stands instantly.\n\nElira: \"He is beside me because I trust him.\"\n\nThe murmurs died into silence.\n\nI looked at her. She did not look away from Lucien.\n\nLucien's smile thinned.\n\nHaru: \"We should begin before the opening ceremony becomes philosophical.\"\n\nAira: \"Too late.\"\n\nThe starting sigils lit beneath our feet. The field waited. The timer appeared overhead.\n\n**CREST RELAY BEGINS IN 00:10**\n\nTen seconds. Elira's voice lowered.\n\nElira: \"Roles.\"\n\nLeon answered first.\n\nLeon: \"Cassian and I take forward pressure. Aira handles route stability. Elira anchors defense and high-output response. Haru calls changes.\"\n\nNo hesitation. No jab at anyone's rank. Progress, then.\n\nCassian grinned.\n\nCassian: \"Look at us, blades mostly pointed outward.\"\n\nAira: \"Do not name it. You'll scare it.\"\n\nThe timer struck zero. We moved.\n\n---\n\nThe first section was forest. Not decorative forest. Hostile forest.\n\nTrees shifted when we passed. Roots tried to catch ankles. Silver birds made of mana dove from branches and shattered into blinding flashes if struck too hard.\n\nAcross the field, the other schools entered their own mirrored sections. The paths were separate at first, but the rules allowed intersections later.\n\nI watched the terrain breathe.\n\nHaru: \"Left roots respond to heat. Cassian, do not burn them. Leon, water pressure under the second ridge. Aira, the birds are triggered by sudden mana spikes.\"\n\nCassian: \"Do not burn the roots. Cruel opening.\"\n\nLeon sent a thin water line beneath the ridge. The roots lifted harmlessly before we reached them.\n\nAira shaped a low mist screen that softened the flash-birds' detection. They tilted in the branches, confused, while we passed under them.\n\nElira walked at the center, golden light held close instead of bright.\n\nRestraint from a Yellow Circle looked different from restraint from me. Hers looked like grace. Mine looked like lying.\n\nThe first crest fragment appeared in a hollow of black stone. Too easy.\n\nHaru: \"Stop.\"\n\nEveryone stopped. Even Cassian. That was progress too.\n\nThe hollow shimmered. Aira narrowed her eyes.\n\nAira: \"False floor.\"\n\nLeon crouched.\n\nLeon: \"Backlash net under it. If we grab the crest directly, it marks a penalty.\"\n\nCassian: \"Can we break it?\"\n\nAira and Leon answered together.\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nCassian sighed.\n\nCassian: \"The tournament hates clean targets.\"\n\nElira lifted one hand. Golden threads extended into the hollow, not touching the crest, only measuring the light around it.\n\nElira: \"It wants simultaneous stabilization from three points.\"\n\nAira: \"I can take left.\"\n\nLeon: \"Right.\"\n\nHaru: \"The third point is not in the hollow.\"\n\nThey looked at me. Leon frowned.\n\nLeon: \"The hollow signature is cleaner.\"\n\nHaru: \"Too clean. Real mana under this much distortion should have micro-fluctuations.\"\n\nThat sounded like something a careful Green Circle support student might notice. It was not the whole truth.\n\nI pointed at a tree behind us.\n\nHaru: \"It is using the shadow as an anchor.\"\n\nFor a second, nobody spoke.\n\nCassian: \"That is irritatingly clever.\"\n\nElira smiled slightly.\n\nElira: \"Good catch.\"\n\nI kept my face neutral. Praise was harder to defend against than suspicion.\n\nWe solved the lock in nine seconds.\n\nThe crest fragment rose into Elira's hand, glowing silver-blue.\n\n**AURELIS: FRAGMENT ONE SECURED**\n\nCheers erupted from our side of the arena.\n\nAcross the field, Drakenshard secured theirs through brute coordination, shattering half their trees and taking only one penalty.\n\nValemere was slower but clean. Sol Aeternum moved beautifully. Noctis was fastest.\n\nLucien did not seem to hurry. That was the worst part.\n\nHis team moved as if the field had already been explained to them by someone who disliked surprises.\n\nNera and Oric did not waste motion binding the forest hazards. They touched only the joints that mattered, letting roots freeze half-grown and flash-birds fall asleep mid-dive. Tovan cleared the larger threats with short red strikes that never exceeded the minimum force required.\n\nCael followed three steps behind Lucien with both hands held near his chest, lightless lines running from his wrists into the ground whenever Lucien lifted a finger. Conduit support.\n\nThe phrase looked worse in motion.\n\nCael was not being harmed visibly. The tournament barriers would have reacted if his channels crossed injury thresholds. Instead, he was being spent constantly, quietly, efficiently.\n\nLucien did not turn to check whether the strain hurt. He simply assumed the route would carry him.\n\nAira saw it too.\n\nAira: \"I hate that roster title more now.\"\n\nLeon followed her gaze and said nothing. His silence had weight in it.\n\n---\n\nAt the five-minute mark, the field changed.\n\nThe forest folded into ruins.\n\nWalls rose. Bridges collapsed. Mirrors appeared between stone arches, showing false versions of teammates moving in the wrong directions.\n\nAira groaned.\n\nAira: \"Illusion corridors. Wonderful. I was worried the building did not hate us personally.\"\n\nLeon: \"Stay on formation.\"\n\nCassian: \"Which formation?\"\n\nHaru: \"The one where you do not punch yourself in a mirror.\"\n\nCassian glanced at me.\n\nCassian: \"It was one time in practice.\"\n\nAira: \"It was this morning.\"\n\nThe second crest fragment flashed at the far end of a ruined hall.\n\nBetween us and it stood Valemere's team, neither hostile nor friendly.\n\nIn the center of their formation was Soren Ashvale, the Red Circle duelist we had met with Valemere's advance party. He had dark hair, a neat uniform, and the posture of someone raised to treat pressure as manners.\n\nSoren: \"Aurelis.\"\n\nElira: \"Valemere.\"\n\nSoren's gaze flicked from Elira to me, then to our fragment.\n\nSoren: \"Temporary passage conflict.\"\n\nLeon: \"There are two routes.\"\n\nSoren: \"One is trapped.\"\n\nAira: \"Both are trapped.\"\n\nSoren looked at her.\n\nAira: \"What? They are.\"\n\nThe ruins shifted again.\n\nBehind Valemere, a Noctis lightless thread slipped along the floor. Thin. Almost invisible.\n\nIt was not aimed at us. It was aimed at the bridge beneath Valemere's rear support student.\n\nIf it cut the support line, the bridge would collapse. Valemere would lose time, possibly take injury, and Noctis would gain advantage while remaining technically uninvolved.\n\nLucien was across the field, not looking at us. I had three choices: warn Valemere and reveal what I should not see, do nothing and let the trap spring, or intervene subtly and risk Halden catching another impossible correction.\n\nElira saw my hand twitch.\n\nShe did not stop me. She shifted half a step to cover the angle from the judges. Trust, given shape.\n\nI released a tiny pulse through the floor, timing rather than force.\n\nThe lightless thread struck the bridge support and found it already vibrating at the wrong frequency. Instead of cutting through, it slid aside like a blade hitting oil. The bridge held.\n\nSoren's rear support student stumbled but did not fall.\n\nSoren turned sharply, eyes narrowing at the floor. He had felt enough to know something had happened, not enough to know what.\n\nLucien's gaze moved toward us at last. There it was. Interest sharpening.\n\nAira whispered without moving her lips.\n\nAira: \"Please tell me that was legal.\"\n\nHaru: \"Technically.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is never comforting from you.\"\n\nSoren looked at Elira.\n\nSoren: \"We owe Aurelis a clean passage.\"\n\nElira: \"Then take the right route. The left collapses in twelve seconds.\"\n\nSoren's gaze moved once toward Noctis, where Lucien had still not looked back at the student his thread had nearly dropped. The warmth left Soren's eyes.\n\nSoren: \"That was not dueling.\"\n\nOne of his Valemere teammates stiffened.\n\nValemere Student: \"Should we report it?\"\n\nSoren's hand closed once, scarred knuckles whitening under the glove.\n\nSoren: \"Aurelis saw it. If they need witnesses, Valemere will stand.\"\n\nHe believed her immediately. Valemere took the right lane. We moved left.\n\nCassian laughed under his breath.\n\nCassian: \"I enjoy diplomacy when it has traps.\"\n\nWe reached the second crest fragment at the same time Noctis reached theirs.\n\nLucien lifted his fragment from a mirror basin without touching the water.\n\nI watched the basin ripple backward. The field did not like him.\n\n**AURELIS: FRAGMENT TWO SECURED**\n\n**NOCTIS: FRAGMENT TWO SECURED**\n\nThe crowd roared.\n\nThe visible scoreboard called it equal progress. The field did not.\n\nBeneath the cheering, the terrain gave one subtle shudder under Noctis' path. Neither a penalty nor an alarm, more like a living thing flinching after being handled too neatly. The tournament system recorded outcomes; the old academy infrastructure remembered pressure.\n\nProfessor Halden's head turned toward the lower field array. Lucien's did not.\n\nThat meant he either had not noticed, or had expected it. Neither option improved the situation.\n\nCael stumbled once as the Noctis fragment locked into place.\n\nIt was so brief most of the arena missed it.\n\nNera did not. She reached halfway toward him, then stopped when Lucien's shoulder shifted by a fraction. Barely a motion, yet command enough.\n\nElira saw that too. Her hand tightened around the fragment we carried.\n\nElira: \"He trains hesitation into them.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nAcross the field, Nera's binding rings settled back against her wrists.\n\nHer face returned to Noctis discipline before the judges could read anything from it, though not before Aira caught the flicker.\n\nAira: \"She wanted to help him.\"\n\nLeon: \"Then why didn't she?\"\n\nNera stepped back into formation. Oric moved half a pace closer to Cael, just enough that the younger student's next stumble would look like a planned adjustment if anyone asked.\n\nAira's eyes stayed on them.\n\nAira: \"Because in Noctis, wanting is not the same as being allowed.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is a dangerous difference.\"\n\nAira: \"It is also a useful one.\"\n\nLeon looked across the field, jaw tense.\n\nLeon: \"Then we do the opposite.\"\n\nAira stared at him.\n\nAira: \"That was a very decent sentence, and I need a moment.\"\n\nLeon: \"Take it while moving.\"\n\nFor the first time, the opening event felt less like a trial and more like a race.\n\n---\n\nThe third field change was water.\n\nThe ruins broke apart beneath our feet and became floating platforms over a black lake. Bridges appeared and vanished. Mana currents twisted under the surface. The final crest fragment hovered above the center gate, and every school now shared the same zone. Limited interference was permitted.\n\nThat meant everyone became a problem. Drakenshard charged first.\n\nTheir front-line student, Mara Kest, crossed three unstable platforms with raw reinforcement and no visible concern for physics. She swung a blunt training blade into a barrier pylon, forcing the gate angle to shift toward her team.\n\nCassian's eyes lit up.\n\nCassian: \"I like her.\"\n\nAira: \"You like everyone who creates property damage.\"\n\nLeon: \"Focus.\"\n\nNoctis moved next.\n\nLucien did not chase the fragment. He chased the field.\n\nHis lightless mana sank into the lake and dimmed the currents around the center gate. Platforms began moving out of rhythm, unbroken, technically legal, and calculated to make every rival slip.\n\nElira's expression cooled.\n\nElira: \"He is forcing rescue penalties.\"\n\nHer jaw tightened. She had seen this before: the same suppression logic from the junior circuit, refined until cruelty fit inside tournament legality.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nAira: \"Because if people fall, teams lose time helping them.\"\n\nLeon: \"Or lose points if they don't.\"\n\nCassian's smile disappeared.\n\nCassian: \"That is ugly.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is efficient.\"\n\nElira looked at me.\n\nHaru: \"I did not say I approved.\"\n\nThe first victim was a Sol Aeternum student.\n\nHer platform slid sideways under her foot. She caught herself with a burst of golden fire, but the flame triggered a water inversion. The lake pulled up like a hand and dragged her toward the surface.\n\nHer team moved too slowly. Lucien watched without smiling, which made it worse.\n\nLeon moved first. He did not ask.\n\nHe snapped both hands outward, sending twin lines of water mana across the lake. They wrapped around the Sol student and pulled her sideways, away from the inversion field. It cost us speed. It saved her from a penalty and possible injury.\n\nThe judges' display flashed.\n\n**AURELIS: RESCUE RESPONSE CREDIT**\n\nAcross the water, the Sol Aeternum student caught the edge of her team's platform and looked back at him.\n\nSol Student: \"Thank you.\"\n\nLeon froze for half a second, as if surprised the system had rewarded him for not being selfish.\n\nAira: \"Good choice.\"\n\nLeon: \"I know.\"\n\nAira: \"You are allowed to just accept praise.\"\n\nLeon: \"Not during an event.\"\n\nCassian launched forward before another platform vanished.\n\nHe struck the air with a controlled fire burst, creating upward pressure that stabilized the path for Elira. Elira crossed it instantly.\n\nGolden light spread from her feet in thin rings, reading the lake. Lucien noticed.\n\nFor the first time, he moved directly toward her.\n\nThe arena seemed to feel it. Two Yellow Circles approaching the same center gate: Elira's light, warm and precise; Lucien's lightless field, pale and cold.\n\nThe final crest fragment hovered between them.\n\nLucien: \"Still trying to save everyone?\"\n\nElira: \"Still mistaking cruelty for clarity?\"\n\nHe tilted his head.\n\nLucien: \"Clarity does not require kindness.\"\n\nElira: \"No. But strength does.\"\n\nTheir mana met. The center gate shook from pure contradiction.\n\nGolden threads pushed through lightless rings. Pale bands tried to close around the fragment. Elira did not flinch. She shifted one foot, drew his binding pattern wide, and left an opening behind it for our team.\n\nHaru: \"Aira, third current under Elira's left. Leon, cut the lake pull. Cassian, prepare to break the right pylon, not yet.\"\n\nAira: \"That is a lot of instructions.\"\n\nHaru: \"Keep up.\"\n\nAira: \"I hate that they make sense.\"\n\nShe moved. Leon moved. Cassian waited, which was the most impressive part.\n\nLucien's field tightened.\n\nThe necklace at Elira's throat warmed: a quiet warning, not an activation. Lucien's pale mana slid toward the pendant, curious and invasive.\n\nElira didn't flinch or glance back. She angled her light a fraction sharper, peeling his probe off the crystal and exposing the right pylon cleanly.\n\nCassian: \"Now?\"\n\nHaru: \"Now.\"\n\nCassian shattered the right pylon. The lake current reversed. Leon cut the pull. Aira sealed the backlash.\n\nElira turned Lucien's own ring into an opening and took the final crest fragment with two fingers.\n\nThe center gate blazed silver-blue.\n\n**AURELIS: FINAL FRAGMENT SECURED**\n\nThe arena erupted.\n\nThe event was not over yet. The fragments still had to be returned to the center gate, and Lucien was already reaching for the field beneath us.\n\nHaru: \"Move.\"\n\nWe ran.\n\nPlatforms collapsed behind us in sequence. Nothing visibly illegal, just timed with precision meant to wound.\n\nI called the path as fast as I could without sounding impossible.\n\nHaru: \"Left. Stop. Jump. Leon, anchor. Aira, shield low. Cassian, do not hit that.\"\n\nCassian: \"I was considering it.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira ran beside me, crest fragment in hand, golden light bright around her shoulders.\n\nFor one second, across the chaos, we moved in perfect rhythm. She trusted the part I could say aloud and understood the part I could not.\n\nWe reached the center gate with Noctis half a platform behind us. Lucien lifted one hand.\n\nCael stumbled directly into Cassian's path, shoved sideways by a lightless current disguised as lake backlash.\n\nIf Cassian collided with him, Aurelis would take an interference penalty. If Cassian stopped, Noctis would pass.\n\nCassian saw it too late. I saw it early. So did Leon.\n\nLeon threw a water ribbon around Cassian's waist and yanked him sideways hard enough to make him swear.\n\nCassian: \"Varek!\"\n\nLeon: \"You're welcome!\"\n\nAira caught Cael with a cushion barrier instead of letting him hit the stone.\n\nElira placed the crest fragments into the gate. The gate accepted them. Light shot upward.\n\n**AURELIS ARCANE ACADEMY: CREST RELAY COMPLETE**\n\nThe timer froze.\n\nNoctis arrived two seconds later. Two seconds. In a tournament like this, two seconds could become history.\n\nThe arena thundered. I looked at Lucien.\n\nHe looked at the scoreboard, then at Leon, then at Aira, then finally at me. His expression showed no anger, which made me trust it less.\n\nLucien: \"Interesting team.\"\n\nHaru: \"Functional.\"\n\nLucien's eyes cooled.\n\nLucien: \"For now.\"\n\nThe judges' final scoring display formed overhead.\n\n**OPENING EVENT RESULTS**\n\n**1. Aurelis Arcane Academy**\n\n**2. Noctis Dominion College**\n\n**3. Valemere Royal Institute**\n\n**4. Sol Aeternum Conservatory**\n\n**5. Drakenshard Military Academy**\n\nThe Aurelis section exploded into celebration.\n\nAira looked like she might collapse from relief and insult the floor for catching her.\n\nLeon stared at the scoreboard, quietly stunned.\n\nCassian clapped him on the shoulder hard enough to move him half a step.\n\nCassian: \"Look at that. You saved me from politics.\"\n\nLeon: \"I saved the team from your momentum.\"\n\nCassian: \"That too.\"\n\nElira came to stand beside me. Her hand found mine. This time, in full view of the arena, neither of us hid it.\n\nAcross the field, Lucien watched our joined hands with unreadable calm.\n\nAbove the judges' platform, one of the barrier rings flickered again. Only once. Professor Halden looked up, then at me.\n\nThe scoreboard changed, not for the crowd, only on a hidden layer beneath the visible display.\n\nI saw it for less than a second.\n\n**AURELIS GROUP THREE: PRIORITY ELEVATED**\n\n**HARU AERLEN: RESPONSE PATTERN UNCLASSIFIED**\n\n**NEXT EVENT: DIRECT TEAM COMBAT**\n\nThe hidden text vanished.\n\nElira's fingers tightened around mine. She had not seen the words, yet she had felt me go still.\n\nElira: \"What is it?\"\n\nI looked across the arena. Lucien was still watching. Halden was still measuring. The crowd was still cheering.\n\nHaru: \"The tournament just became honest.\"\n\nElira followed my gaze.\n\nAt the far side of the field, Lucien smiled.\n\nThe next event would not be a relay. It would be a fight.\n\n---\n\nThe next event was announced at noon.\n\nBy then, the whole academy had changed temperature.\n\nAurelis had won the opening relay.\n\nThat should have made the campus lighter. It did not.\n\nVictory attracted celebration from people who wanted to belong to it, resentment from people who thought it should have belonged to them, and attention from everyone trying to understand how a team with a Green Circle transfer had beaten Noctis Dominion by two seconds.\n\nTwo seconds became the most discussed measurement in the school.\n\nStudents repeated it in dining halls.\n\nInstructors analyzed it in corridor corners.\n\nVisiting teams watched us with new caution.\n\nAnd Lucien Voss had not stopped smiling.\n\nIt was the least comforting part.\n\nWe sat in a preparation room beneath the Grand Arena while the tournament officials recalibrated the field for the afternoon match. The room was meant to be quiet. Instead, it held the kind of silence that came before something broke.\n\nAira had three projected rule sheets open at once.\n\nLeon was drying his gloves, though they were already dry.\n\nCassian leaned against the wall with one foot braced behind him, eyes half-closed, looking relaxed enough to be either confident or unconscious. Elira sat beside me.\n\nHer hand rested over the necklace. Not fear. Habit.\n\nI noticed because I noticed everything about her now.\n\nAira: \"I have read the rules six times.\"\n\nLeon: \"And?\"\n\nAira: \"They are still rude.\"\n\nCassian opened one eye.\n\nCassian: \"Rules can be rude?\"\n\nAira: \"These are professionally rude.\"\n\nThe central projection displayed the afternoon event.\n\n**CROWN FIELD TEAM COMBAT**\n\n**Teams:** Two schools at a time.\n\n**Objective:** Capture and hold the central crown sigil for sixty seconds.\n\n**Win Conditions:** Hold crown, force surrender, or disable three opponent markers.\n\n**Restrictions:** No lethal force. No permanent channel damage. No attacks after surrender.\n\n**Field Feature:** The arena changes shape in response to dominant mana pressure.\n\nThat last line bothered me most.\n\nHaru: \"Responsive terrain.\"\n\nElira nodded.\n\nElira: \"The field will favor whoever controls rhythm.\"\n\nLeon: \"Or whoever forces the other team to react.\"\n\nAira: \"So Noctis.\"\n\nNo one contradicted her. The bracket appeared.\n\n**AFTERNOON FEATURE MATCH**\n\n**AURELIS ARCANE ACADEMY vs NOCTIS DOMINION COLLEGE**\n\nCassian smiled fully.\n\nCassian: \"There it is.\"\n\nLeon: \"Do not sound pleased.\"\n\nCassian: \"I am pleased.\"\n\nAira: \"At least pretend your survival instincts attended class.\"\n\nProfessor Halden entered before Cassian could answer. Lady Seraphine followed.\n\nInstructor Dren stood behind them with his arms folded, expression hard to read.\n\nHalden looked at the projection, then at us.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"You have become inconveniently interesting.\"\n\nAira: \"Is that a compliment or a warning?\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Yes.\"\n\nHe adjusted his spectacles, clearing his throat. Lady Seraphine stepped forward.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Noctis will attempt to divide your priorities. They watched the relay. They know Varek will rescue, Solen will stabilize, Veyr will overcommit if provoked, Vale will protect the field from collateral harm, and Aerlen will read patterns.\"\n\nCassian: \"I object to the word overcommit.\"\n\nLeon: \"It is generous.\"\n\nCassian: \"I withdraw my objection to preserve morale.\"\n\nLady Seraphine ignored them.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"If you enter the match as five separate instincts, you will lose.\"\n\nElira: \"Then we enter as one structure.\"\n\nProfessor Halden's gaze moved to me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Aerlen?\"\n\nThere it was. Another test disguised as conversation.\n\nI looked at the projected rules.\n\nHaru: \"Noctis will not try to overpower Elira immediately. Lucien wants her attention, but his better play is to make the rest of us prove we can function without her constantly rescuing us.\"\n\nLeon stopped adjusting his gloves.\n\nHaru: \"Their binders will target Aira first because she repaired too many field problems during the relay. Their striker will pressure Cassian because he looks easiest to provoke. Lucien will use terrain response to isolate Elira from support, then create a situation where I either stay useful within Green limits or fail publicly.\"\n\nAira stared at me.\n\nAira: \"I dislike how complete that was.\"\n\nCassian's smile thinned into something sharper.\n\nCassian: \"Let them try.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is exactly what they want you to think.\"\n\nCassian looked at him, then nodded once.\n\nCassian: \"Then tell me where to stand.\"\n\nLeon blinked.\n\nAira: \"Did Cassian just ask for tactical placement?\"\n\nCassian: \"Do not make it sentimental.\"\n\nElira's shoulder brushed mine.\n\nElira: \"Haru is right. We do not win by reacting to Lucien's cruelty. We win by making it irrelevant.\"\n\nProfessor Halden studied her for a long moment.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"That is an excellent sentence. Try to make it true.\"\n\nInstructor Dren finally spoke.\n\nDren: \"Remember the rule that matters. A strong team does not prove strength by enduring damage it could have prevented.\"\n\nHis eyes found Leon, then Cassian, then me.\n\nDren: \"Protect each other intelligently.\"\n\nIt was the closest thing to warmth we were likely to receive.\n\nThe arena bell rang. The preparation room door opened. Light poured in, and the roar of the stands rolled down the tunnel.\n\nElira stood. So did I. She looked at me once.\n\nElira: \"Carefully.\"\n\nHaru: \"I remember.\"\n\nAira: \"I would like everyone to remember, actually.\"\n\nLeon: \"Agreed.\"\n\nCassian: \"Carefully, then violently.\"\n\nAira: \"Cassian.\"\n\nCassian: \"Fine. Carefully, then decisively.\"\n\nGood enough. We walked into the arena.\n\n---\n\nThe Crown Field looked simpler than the relay, which was how I knew it was worse.\n\nA circular battlefield stretched beneath the floating judge platforms. At the center stood a tall sigil of silver light shaped like a crown. Around it, five raised lanes spiraled outward like the petals of a blade-flower. Each lane held cover pillars, anchor stones, and mana wells that could be claimed or disrupted.\n\nAbove us, the scoreboard displayed both teams.\n\n**AURELIS ARCANE ACADEMY**\n\nElira Vale. Haru Aerlen. Aira Solen. Leon Varek. Cassian Veyr.\n\n**NOCTIS DOMINION COLLEGE**\n\nLucien Voss. Nera Vail. Oric Sen. Tovan Reiss. Cael Nox.\n\nCael stood at the back of the Noctis formation again, posture ruler-straight, face even paler than it had been during the relay.\n\nNera Vail stood left of Lucien, binding rings turning around her wrists in slow, silent loops. She watched Aira the way a lock watches a key.\n\nOric Sen took the right, his Blue Circle crest dimmer than Nera's but steadier, every line of his stance arranged around containment rather than attack.\n\nTovan Reiss rolled his shoulders once, red mana gathering and then vanishing under his skin. He was the kind of striker who knew exactly how much strength was needed to make contact feel inevitable.\n\nNoctis had not sent five stars. They had sent one star and four methods of making him worse.\n\nLucien stood at the center. He looked at Elira.\n\nLucien: \"A direct match. How nostalgic.\"\n\nElira: \"This one has rules.\"\n\nLucien: \"So did the last.\"\n\nHer expression did not change, yet the air around her brightened by a fraction.\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nShe glanced at me, not because she needed restraint, but because she had promised to let me stand beside her.\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nLucien's gaze moved between us.\n\nLucien: \"How gentle.\"\n\nAira muttered behind me.\n\nAira: \"I am going to develop a medical condition from disliking him.\"\n\nThe judge raised one hand.\n\nJudge: \"Crown Field Team Combat begins on signal. Remember: surrender markers are binding. Channel damage beyond recovery thresholds results in disqualification.\"\n\nThe crown sigil pulsed. The arena quieted.\n\nThe judge dropped his hand.\n\nJudge: \"Begin.\"\n\nNoctis moved like a closing door.\n\nTheir two binders spread left and right, pale-black cords unfurling from their hands. Tovan rushed Cassian immediately with a short-bladed mana construct. Cael stayed behind Lucien and began feeding lightless pressure into the terrain.\n\nLucien did not move toward Elira. He moved toward the crown. Exactly as expected.\n\nLeon: \"Formation three.\"\n\nHe said it before I did. Good.\n\nAira anchored a support barrier behind Elira. Leon flooded the right lane with water ribbons to slow Tovan's footwork. Cassian met the striker head-on, keeping his fire compact around his fists instead of flaring wide. Controlled. Better.\n\nElira moved toward the center with golden light folded close.\n\nI moved behind the team, watching the field response.\n\nThe crown sigil reacted to Lucien immediately. The silver light dimmed, not because he held it, but because his mana made the field hesitate.\n\nThe terrain did not know whether lightless affinity counted as absence or control. That was his advantage.\n\nHaru: \"The crown is delaying recognition. He can stall capture without standing in the zone.\"\n\nAira: \"That is allowed?\"\n\nLeon: \"If the field accepts it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then we make the field choose.\"\n\nElira understood.\n\nShe stepped directly into the crown circle.\n\nGolden light spread under her feet, defining the space around the sigil without overwhelming it. The crown brightened.\n\n**AURELIS: CROWN CONTACT**\n\nThe crowd roared. Lucien smiled.\n\nLucien: \"Still so eager to make unclear things beautiful.\"\n\nElira: \"Still so eager to confuse damage with truth.\"\n\nHis pale mana snapped outward. Not at her. At Aira.\n\nTwo lightless cords hooked around Aira's barrier and began draining its structure. Nera and Oric moved in sync, twisting pressure from opposite sides. Aira gasped once in frustration.\n\nAira: \"They are eating the outer layer.\"\n\nLeon: \"Drop it.\"\n\nAira: \"If I drop it, Elira takes left exposure.\"\n\nHaru: \"Thin it. Do not hold shape. Let them take a shell.\"\n\nAira's eyes sharpened.\n\nAira: \"Oh.\"\n\nHer barrier collapsed inward like cloth being folded.\n\nThe lightless cords pulled at an empty layer and overextended.\n\nLeon cut the right cord with a water blade.\n\nCassian, still fighting Tovan, kicked a burst of fire sideways and severed the left anchor without looking.\n\nAira's expression went almost offended.\n\nAira: \"That worked.\"\n\nHaru: \"Keep your guard up. Nera's resetting.\"\n\nAira: \"I hate learning under threat.\"\n\nNoctis lost one binder marker.\n\n**NOCTIS: MARKER ONE DISABLED**\n\nThe arena shouted.\n\nLucien did not look irritated, which worried me. Nera did.\n\nHer expression did not twist into anger. Noctis students seemed trained out of wasteful faces. Still, her next spell changed texture, abandoning the outer barrier and sliding directly toward Aira's casting hand in a needle-thin bind.\n\nIt was a precise attack, neither illegal nor visibly injuring, just sharp enough to make a support caster hesitate the next time she reached for a shield.\n\nI saw it. So did Leon. He moved before I called it.\n\nWater snapped upward between Aira's wrist and Nera's bind, spreading into a thin mirror sheet that caught the pale-black line and bent it away.\n\nAira blinked.\n\nAira: \"You blocked for me.\"\n\nLeon: \"You were busy being targeted.\"\n\nAira: \"That is an emotionally unavailable way to be helpful.\"\n\nLeon: \"It was effective.\"\n\nCassian laughed from the front line.\n\nCassian: \"You two flirt like people dodging knives.\"\n\nOric took advantage of the exchange, sending a low containment ring toward Leon's feet.\n\nHaru: \"Leon, jump.\"\n\nHe did, without questioning, without checking for confirmation. Immediately.\n\nThe ring closed on empty air. Cassian drove Tovan back two steps, and Aira flicked a support glyph through Leon's landing path before he touched down. Small choices. Fast trust. That was how a team became harder to dismantle.\n\nTovan drove Cassian backward with a heavy strike. Cassian blocked, slid, and nearly answered with a wide flame burst, then caught himself.\n\nCassian: \"Varek!\"\n\nLeon was already there.\n\nWater surged under Tovan's feet. Cassian struck the steam cloud Leon created, turning it into a flash screen without heat damage. Tovan's next strike missed.\n\nCassian hit his shoulder marker with a compact flame-enhanced palm.\n\n**NOCTIS: MARKER TWO DISABLED**\n\nCassian laughed once.\n\nCassian: \"Teamwork hits harder than pride. Annoying discovery.\"\n\nLeon: \"I accept your apology.\"\n\nCassian: \"I did not apologize.\"\n\nLeon: \"Emotionally, you did.\"\n\nAira: \"Please continue bonding after we survive.\"\n\nThe crown timer began.\n\n**AURELIS HOLD: 00:10**\n\nTen seconds. We needed sixty.\n\nLucien finally moved. The arena temperature dropped.\n\nHis lightless mana spread beneath the crown circle in a thin, perfect ring. Elira's golden field pressed against it. For a breath, the two Yellow Circles stood inside a boundary of light and absence, neither giving ground.\n\nThen Lucien changed targets. He looked at me.\n\nLucien: \"You call the field well for a Green Circle.\"\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nLucien: \"It was not praise.\"\n\nHaru: \"I chose to receive it generously.\"\n\nSomething like amusement touched his face. Then he lifted one hand.\n\nCael stiffened behind him. The boy's mana surged, too fast and too unstable.\n\nLucien was using him as a conduit.\n\nNot enough to trip the rules instantly, just enough to force the field's lightless pressure through a student with lower tolerance, making coercion look like team amplification. Cael's face went white.\n\nElira saw it. So did I. So did the judges, a heartbeat too slowly.\n\nLucien's pale ring shot toward the crown's anchor stones.\n\nIf it landed, the field would collapse inward, forcing Elira to choose between holding the crown and shielding the arena from backlash. He was not trying to beat her strength. He was trying to make her mercy cost the match.\n\nElira moved to protect the anchor stones.\n\nThe crown timer stopped at twenty-eight seconds.\n\nLucien: \"Predictable.\"\n\nThe word was soft.\n\nHaru: \"Aira. Leon. Cael is the conduit.\"\n\nAira's head snapped toward the Noctis back line.\n\nAira: \"He's overloading.\"\n\nLeon: \"If we cut him loose wrong, it rebounds.\"\n\nCassian: \"Can we reach him?\"\n\nNo. Not physically, and not in time.\n\nI felt the Black Circle open inside my chest, invisible beneath my coat, just enough to turn the world into lines.\n\nEvery spell had structure. Every structure had mercy points: the places where force could be redirected without breaking the person carrying it.\n\nI found Cael's. Elira's necklace warmed at the same instant.\n\nAcross the field, her eyes flicked to mine. She knew I could stop it. She also knew the danger.\n\nHaru: \"Elira. Hold the anchor.\"\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"Trust me.\"\n\nFor one breath, the whole arena went quiet inside my head.\n\nThen she turned back to the crown. She trusted me. That was the most dangerous power I had ever been given.\n\nI raised two fingers.\n\nTo everyone else, it looked like a Green Circle reinforcement gesture. Small. Limited. Almost desperate.\n\nUnder that disguise, a thread of Black Circle precision crossed the field and touched the overload inside Cael's mana channels.\n\nThe Black Circle wanted to erase Lucien's construct entirely. I gave it the smallest permission I could, just enough to ask the spell a question it had no right to understand.\n\nWhy are you hurting the hand that carries you?\n\nThe overload inverted silently.\n\nCael collapsed to one knee, breathing hard, his channels intact.\n\nThe arena's recording crystals stuttered, smearing seven seconds of the official feed into silver static. Most of the crowd missed it in the roar of combat. Not everyone did.\n\nLucien's ring lost its conduit and fractured into harmless pale sparks. The anchor stones held.\n\nThe crown field stabilized under Elira's light.\n\nFor the first time since I had met him, Lucien's expression broke open: offense, sharp and cold, as if reality itself had contradicted him.\n\nThe judges' emergency sigils flashed.\n\n**NOCTIS: UNSAFE CONDUIT PRESSURE WARNING**\n\n**AURELIS: RESCUE RESPONSE CREDIT**\n\nAira stared at Cael.\n\nAira: \"Did we just get rescue credit in team combat?\"\n\nLeon: \"Apparently.\"\n\nCassian: \"That is the funniest possible insult.\"\n\nLucien looked down at Cael. The boy flinched.\n\nElira's voice cut across the field.\n\nElira: \"Do not.\"\n\nLucien turned back slowly.\n\nElira stood in the crown circle, golden light bright around her, expression calm in a way that made the whole arena listen.\n\nElira: \"You used your own teammate as a pressure tool.\"\n\nLucien: \"He volunteered to serve the formation.\"\n\nElira: \"No. He obeyed.\"\n\nThe words landed harder than a spell. Lucien's eyes cooled.\n\nLucien: \"You still believe kindness makes you stronger.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nHer light expanded, exact and luminous.\n\nElira: \"I know it makes us harder to control.\"\n\nThe crown timer resumed.\n\n**AURELIS HOLD: 00:29**\n\nLucien attacked her directly. The arena shook.\n\nPale-black bands erupted from the ground around Elira, each one designed to suppress output, bind movement, and force her into defense. It was the same cruelty she had described from the junior circuit, refined and sharpened.\n\nBut Elira was not that Blue Circle boy from years ago, and she was not alone.\n\nAira layered flexible barriers between the bands.\n\nLeon cut the ones that tried to anchor.\n\nCassian hammered the outer ring whenever Lucien tried to close the formation.\n\nI called every shift I could safely name.\n\nHaru: \"Second band false. Third is real. Low anchor behind Elira. Leon, left. Aira, drop the top shield. Cassian, break after the pulse, not before.\"\n\nCassian: \"You ask for miracles.\"\n\nHaru: \"You enjoy them.\"\n\nCassian: \"Fair.\"\n\nThe team moved as one. Perfect teams followed plans. We followed each other.\n\nThe crown timer climbed past forty, then fifty.\n\nLucien stepped into the circle.\n\nNow he and Elira stood face to face under the crown sigil. Yellow against Yellow. Mercy against control.\n\nLucien: \"You could have stood above everyone.\"\n\nElira: \"I never wanted to stand there.\"\n\nLucien: \"Then you waste what you are.\"\n\nElira: \"No. I choose what I am for.\"\n\nHe struck.\n\nLightless mana folded into a spear of absence and drove toward her center marker.\n\nElira caught it between both hands.\n\nThe impact sent a shock through the arena barrier.\n\nMy body moved before thought. One step.\n\nThen Elira's voice reached me.\n\nElira: \"Stay.\"\n\nI stopped.\n\nEvery instinct in me protested. The Black Circle did not like seeing her under pressure. Neither did I.\n\nStill, this was her fight too.\n\nElira's necklace gave one hidden pulse against her collarbone, letting me know she was safe.\n\nShe drew Lucien's spear inward.\n\nFor a terrible moment, it looked like she was letting it reach her.\n\nThen golden light bloomed inside the lightless construct. Not around it. Inside.\n\nElira had let his spell reveal its own structure, then filled the hollow spaces with light. The spear cracked.\n\nLucien's eyes widened. Confidence broke first, then surprise, then rage, sharp and naked before discipline closed over it again.\n\nElira: \"Strength without mercy is not strength.\"\n\nThe spear shattered.\n\nOverhead, the crown clock ticked through fifty-nine.\n\nElira stepped inside Lucien's guard and pressed two fingers to his shoulder marker just as the last second fell. Golden light flared across the sigil.\n\n**NOCTIS: MARKER THREE DISABLED**\n\n**AURELIS HOLD: 01:00**\n\n**MATCH COMPLETE**\n\n**WINNER: AURELIS ARCANE ACADEMY**\n\nBoth win conditions locked on the same heartbeat, leaving Noctis no margin for a technicality.\n\nFor one breath, nobody moved.\n\nThen the Grand Arena exploded. Sound hit like weather.\n\nStudents screamed. Banners flared. Aurelis' crest filled the overhead screens in silver-blue light. Even instructors who usually considered emotion a weakness were on their feet.\n\nAira sat down directly on the arena floor.\n\nAira: \"I am alive and would like that noted.\"\n\nLeon offered her a hand. She stared at it, then accepted.\n\nAira: \"Character growth is exhausting.\"\n\nLeon: \"I have noticed.\"\n\nCassian looked at the scoreboard, then at Tovan, who was still catching his breath.\n\nCassian: \"Good match.\"\n\nTovan nodded once. Respectful. Enough.\n\nCael remained on one knee behind Lucien.\n\nI walked toward him before anyone could decide whether it was politically appropriate. Leon noticed and followed. Aira did too.\n\nMaybe that was why the judges allowed it. Maybe they were too busy reviewing Noctis' warning penalty.\n\nI stopped a few steps from Cael.\n\nHaru: \"Can you stand?\"\n\nHe looked at me with confusion and something too close to fear.\n\nCael: \"Why?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because the match is over.\"\n\nBriefly, he seemed not to understand why that mattered.\n\nThen Leon extended a hand.\n\nLeon: \"Take it before this becomes more awkward.\"\n\nCael hesitated, then took it.\n\nWhen he stood, he bowed toward us. The movement was Noctis-perfect in form, yet his eyes, when he lifted them, held something Noctis had never taught him to carry. Hope.\n\nLucien watched from the crown circle. His expression was calm again, though not untouched. There was a crack in it now.\n\nElira came to stand beside me. She did not gloat. That would have been too small for her.\n\nElira: \"Lucien.\"\n\nHe looked at her.\n\nElira: \"Mercy did not make us hesitate.\"\n\nHer voice carried through the arena.\n\nElira: \"It told us who we refused to become.\"\n\nLucien said nothing. For once, that silence felt like loss.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Nera Vail - Behind the Ward\n\nNoctis did not go to the public recovery room.\n\nThey withdrew behind a black privacy ward at the eastern tunnel mouth, close enough for the crowd to see the barrier and far enough that no sound could cross it.\n\nLucien stood calm at the center of the formation despite the loss. The air inside the ward felt two degrees colder for it.\n\nNera Vail waited near the edge, binding rings locked flat against both wrists. Beside her, Oric kept his gaze lowered, shoulders set in the practiced stillness of someone who knew attention inside a Noctis room rarely meant reward.\n\nCael sat on the bench because his knees had refused to hold him any longer.\n\nLucien: \"Cael's performance was adequate.\"\n\nCael flinched. Nera's jaw tightened.\n\nNera: \"He collapsed.\"\n\nLucien turned his pale eyes toward her.\n\nLucien: \"He recovered.\"\n\nNera: \"Because Aurelis intervened.\"\n\nThe silence after that sat sharp as wire between them.\n\nLucien: \"Are you questioning the formation?\"\n\nNera looked at Cael, at the gray pressure ring circling his wrist where the conduit strain had bitten too deep, and at Oric, who did not move a millimeter yet stopped breathing for half a second.\n\nNera: \"...No.\"\n\nLucien smiled.\n\nLucien: \"Good. Doubt is contagious.\"\n\nHe stepped through the ward and left them inside it.\n\nOnly after the black shimmer settled did Nera cross to the bench. She kept her hands at her sides; Noctis had trained every gesture of care to look deniable.\n\nNera: \"You should have signaled distress earlier.\"\n\nCael stared at the stone floor.\n\nCael: \"Would you have stopped him?\"\n\nFor several seconds, the privacy ward hummed louder than anyone's breathing.\n\nNera: \"...No.\"\n\nShe turned toward the exit, stopped half a pace away, and spoke without looking back.\n\nNera: \"I would have tried to end the sequence faster.\"\n\nClipped. Insufficient. Still the only margin of mercy left inside a Noctis coat.\n\nShe walked through the ward before Cael could answer. Oric watched the seam close behind her, then nudged a spare healer cloth closer to Cael's boots with the toe of his shoe and followed her out.\n\nCael did not pick the cloth up until he was alone. Even then, he stared at it for a long time before pressing it to his wrist.\n\n---\n\nThe closing ceremony for Aurelis' qualifying rounds happened at sunset. The continental tournament was supposed to continue through three more brackets, yet the opening block (the selection, visiting delegations, relay, and Crown Field match) had ended with Aurelis leading the standings and Group Three at the center of every conversation.\n\nHeadmaster Veris announced provisional results with the solemn pride of a man already imagining commemorative plaques.\n\nAurelis ranked first in the opening block. Noctis second. Valemere third. Drakenshard and Sol Aeternum close behind.\n\nHe never reached the next bracket.\n\nAn Inter-National Schools Council crystal opened above the platform in emergency red.\n\n**REMAINING MATCHES: SUSPENDED PENDING STUDENT-HARM REVIEW**\n\n**OPENING STANDINGS: FROZEN, NOT VOID**\n\n**INVOLVED DELEGATIONS RETAIN COMPETITOR AND WITNESS STATUS**\n\nThe arena did not know whether to boo, argue, or applaud. It attempted all three.\n\nValemere moved first. Soren Ashvale entered a formal demand that the Crown Field sequence remain attached to the bracket record, so no later ruling could describe Cael's collapse without the match that caused it. Drakenshard requested the raw terrain and conduit-pressure logs. Sol Aeternum placed two healers beside the recovery record and refused to let either school summarize their findings alone.\n\nThe tournament had not ended. It had changed arenas. Every visiting school still had points, reputation, injured pride, and a reason to watch what Aurelis and Noctis did next.\n\nVictory did not mean the world had changed yet. Green Circle students were still overlooked. Yellow Circles were still worshiped. Institutions still measured children like resources. Professor Halden was still watching me from the faculty platform, and Lucien Voss was still dangerous.\n\nSomething had shifted all the same. Leon had chosen the team over pride. Cassian had chosen timing over spectacle. Aira had stopped apologizing for being useful. Elira had defeated Lucien without becoming cruel. And I had saved someone I did not know without showing the world what I was.\n\nThat felt small. It was not.\n\nAfter the ceremony, the five of us stood at the edge of the arena while the crowd began to empty.\n\nAira leaned against the railing.\n\nAira: \"I would like tomorrow to contain no tournaments, no hostile prodigies, no hidden scoring systems, and no emotional revelations before breakfast.\"\n\nCassian: \"So a boring day.\"\n\nAira: \"A sacred day.\"\n\nLeon looked down toward the Noctis delegation as they assembled by the causeway carriages.\n\nLeon: \"Voss will not forget this.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"Neither will Cael.\"\n\nWe all looked down.\n\nCael stood near the exit, surrounded by his team yet separate from them. Before the line moved, he glanced up at us and gave one small, quick bow.\n\nWhen Magister Rusk gestured toward the black carriages, Cael did not follow.\n\nHis whole body shook. His voice did too.\n\nCael: \"I require medical observation.\"\n\nThe words sounded memorized until he looked at Rhea Valmont and added a single word.\n\nCael: \"Here.\"\n\nRhea opened a red witness seal between him and the departing formation. Two neutral tournament healers stepped into it, leaving the road back to Noctis visible and the path into Aurelis unblocked.\n\nRhea: \"Overnight medical hold recognized under the student-harm order. No delegation removes him before healer clearance and recorded review.\"\n\nLucien studied Cael for one long second. Then he stepped aside so Rusk, Nera, Oric, and Tovan could board the black carriage. The door shut.\n\nThe carriage rolled down the causeway toward the northern transit gate, leaving Cael behind, while Lucien lingered unhurriedly beneath the far stone arch, waiting for the delegation's transit seal to clear.\n\nAira softened first.\n\nAira: \"Oh, that's inconvenient.\"\n\nLeon: \"What is?\"\n\nAira: \"Now I care.\"\n\nCassian laughed quietly. Elira's hand found mine.\n\nHaru: \"That tends to happen.\"\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nElira: \"Yes. It does.\"\n\nWarmth sat in her voice, and warning beside it. Caring made choices harder. It also made them worth making.\n\nProfessor Halden approached before the moment could become too peaceful.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"An impressive performance.\"\n\nCassian: \"Thank you.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I was speaking to all of you.\"\n\nCassian: \"I accepted collectively.\"\n\nHalden's gaze moved past him to me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Especially you, Aerlen.\"\n\nAira inhaled the way people do before attempting to stop a building from collapsing.\n\nHaru: \"I followed the team plan.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"That is one interpretation.\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened around mine. Halden smiled faintly.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Rest tonight. Tomorrow, we begin reviewing what the tournament revealed.\"\n\nLeon: \"Tomorrow?\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Did you think victory ended evaluation?\"\n\nAira: \"I was hoping.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Hope is not a curriculum.\"\n\nHe turned and walked away. Aira watched him go.\n\nAira: \"I am adding that to my list of sentences I hate.\"\n\nThe arena lights dimmed one by one. Night settled over Aurelis.\n\nElira and I remained at the railing after the others drifted a few steps ahead.\n\nBelow us, the tournament field was being cleaned of residual mana. The crown sigil had vanished, though I could still feel the indentation where it had stood.\n\nElira: \"You saved Cael.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer gaze held mine, steady in the cooling twilight.\n\nElira: \"Carefully.\"\n\nHaru: \"As promised.\"\n\nShe turned fully toward me.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nHaru: \"For keeping a promise?\"\n\nElira: \"For choosing the harder version of it.\"\n\nI did not know what to say to that, so I told the truth.\n\nHaru: \"I wanted to do more.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"I wanted to end it immediately.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"That part of me is not gentle.\"\n\nElira stepped closer.\n\nElira: \"No. But you are.\"\n\nI looked at her. She touched the pendant at her throat.\n\nElira: \"Gentleness is not the absence of terrifying power, Haru. Sometimes it is what terrifying power chooses when it has every other option.\"\n\nThe hidden Black Circle near my heart held its next motion. Awake, and listening.\n\nI let out a slow breath.\n\nHaru: \"You make restraint sound noble.\"\n\nElira: \"It is.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is also tiring.\"\n\nHer smile softened.\n\nElira: \"Then do not carry it alone.\"\n\nFor a heartbeat, the world became simple: her hand in mine, the arena cooling under starlight, the qualifying rounds behind us and the consequences ahead.\n\nThen, high above the emptying arena, one hidden projection crystal flickered once.\n\nA line of text appeared where no audience could see it. I saw it. So did Professor Halden from the far platform.\n\nAnd, from the northern transit arch where Noctis's ward was finally closing, Lucien Voss.\n\nThe first two lines were bad enough.\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED RESPONSE CONFIRMED**\n\n**BLACK CIRCLE THEORY: REOPENED**\n\nThen the projection stuttered, like something buried inside the old system had tried to speak and been gagged by its own protection law.\n\nThree more lines surfaced for less than a breath.\n\n**SEVEN-SECOND RECORD: SEALED**\n\n**COLOR CLASSIFICATION: FAILED**\n\n**AUTHORITY REQUIRED: BLACK**\n\nThe words vanished.\n\nElira felt my hand tighten.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nI looked toward the darkening sky above Aurelis. For years, Black Circle had been a myth, a rumor, a lie according to almost everyone alive. Tonight, somewhere inside the academy's buried infrastructure, the myth had opened its eyes.\n\nHaru: \"The opening block is over.\"\n\nElira followed my gaze.\n\nHaru: \"The secret is not.\"\n\nFar across the courtyard, Lucien smiled once before stepping through the Noctis transit gate.\n\nAnd behind me, Professor Halden began to take notes.\n\n---\n\n## Coda: The Wrong Ripple\n\nWhile the arena emptied, Mira Castellan stood at the repaired courtyard fountain with three other Green Circle students and watched one ripple run backward across the basin.\n\nBehind the reflected tournament lights, a ring darker than shadow surfaced and vanished.\n\nHer friend swallowed hard.\n\nGreen Student: \"What was that?\"\n\nMira flipped open the ink-stained notebook she had carried since Haru's second duel with Leon and sketched the curve before her memory could smooth it away.\n\nMira: \"I don't know. I'm going to find out.\"\n\nAn hour later, her cuffs smudged with dust from the lower stacks of the east library, she pulled down ledgers nobody checked because the titles sounded too dull to hide anything: *Irrigation Charters*, *Foundation Repairs*, *Pre-Standardization Terminology*.\n\nOne scorched folio stopped her cold. Most of the entry had been burned away, leaving three charred fragments across the vellum.\n\n**...CIRCLE ZERO...**\n\n**...safeguard... restraint...**\n\n**...erased for institutional safety...**\n\nHer pencil scratched fast, copying every surviving letter.\n\nA lantern crystal pooled warm light at the end of the aisle. Professor Iris Valen stood between the shelves, watching her hands.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Curious reading for a Green Circle student.\"\n\nMira nearly dropped the folio.\n\nMira: \"I saw something in the fountain. Something that shouldn't exist.\"\n\nValen stepped closer and looked down at the burned page. The warmth in her face did not vanish; it turned careful.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Or something that was made not to exist. There is a difference.\"\n\nMira hugged the notebook to her ribs.\n\nMira: \"Am I in trouble?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Not with me.\"\n\nMira: \"Should I stop?\"\n\nValen studied her for a long moment.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Some truths have teeth.\"\n\nMira's voice stayed quiet, though her fingers did not loosen on the spine.\n\nMira: \"So does ignorance.\"\n\nThe professor's smile returned, smaller and sadder.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Then research carefully, Miss Castellan.\"\n\nAfter midnight, Mira went back to the fountain anyway, because fear had never been half as persuasive as an unfinished page.\n\nThe courtyard lamps had dimmed to academy blue. Tournament banners hung limp in the damp night air. Across the basin's surface, the four approved colors drifted in obedient rings, replaying the day's victories for anyone still awake.\n\nThen the Green ring bent inward, making room for a shape the spell did not know how to draw.\n\nMira held her breath and set one copper coin on the stone rim. It was an old Green Circle superstition: leave copper where nobles dropped gold, prove small things still made a sound.\n\nThe coin slid toward the water on its own, stopped at the lip, and turned black on one side.\n\nMira did not touch it.\n\nShe turned to the back page of her notebook, right below the copied **CIRCLE ZERO** fragment, and drew the exact angle of the ripple, including the shudder where the academy's ward tried to correct itself afterward.\n\nBy morning, the coin was copper again.\n\nThe page was not.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-05-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep6","label":"Episode 6","number":"06","file":"episode-06-cover.webp","fallback":"Selection Heat","eyebrow":"Team Trial","recap":"","accent":["#fb5607","#2ec4b6","#2d3142"],"displayLabel":"Episode 6","displayNumber":"06","displayEyebrow":"Tournament Faultlines","arcLabel":"Tournament Faultlines","title":"The Record That Knew Him","body":"The tournament schedule failed before breakfast. Every remaining bracket went dark beneath the same word: **SUSPENDED**. The standings stayed frozen above the fountain so no school could pretend the opening matches had never happened.\n\nThe fountain in the east arcade kept remembering the wrong colors. Green, Blue, Red, Yellow rose from the basin in obedient rings, then thinned at the edges as if the water had been asked to draw a shape it did not have permission to name.\n\nStudents gathered near it in careful clusters.\n\nNo one wanted to be the first to say Black Circle.\n\nNo one wanted to be the last either.\n\nI crossed the arcade with Elira beside me and the others arranged around us in a formation no one admitted was a formation. Aira carried three folded record sheets. Leon walked close enough to Cael to catch him if the healer seal failed. Cassian looked bored in the theatrical way that meant he had identified six exits and disliked two of them. Rhea Valmont waited beneath the eastern arch with her council seal already lit.\n\nProfessor Halden stood beside her with a thin black case in one hand. Next to him waited Professor Iris Valen, an old archive key-ring looped around two fingers, amber eyes quiet and watchful. That was the first warning.\n\nThe next warning had arrived before dawn, while most of the academy still believed yesterday's match was only a tournament result. Noctis had filed a provisional tournament protest: an interference complaint, a custody query, and a request to freeze our victory as provisional until Aurelis explained why a wounded Noctis conduit had left the field under host-school protection.\n\nTournament law could ignore bad manners. It could not ignore a cross-school claim that a student had been moved, harmed, or hidden under contested authority.\n\nThat was how a match became a Foundation matter. Rhea did not want a trial, and Noctis did not want truth, yet old school law retained one buried reflex from before the Circle hierarchy learned to smile. When one school claimed ownership over a student and another claimed witness protection, the Foundation woke.\n\nRhea lifted the protest packet.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"I can answer this from the council chamber.\"\n\nHaru: \"I do not want to answer their wording first.\"\n\nThe group turned toward me.\n\nI took the unsigned transfer note from inside my coat. I had carried it since Lumeris because destroying the only proof of a trap did not make the trap kinder.\n\nHaru: \"Someone brought me here with a sentence about the Foundation remembering. Yesterday the arena recognized something it was built to erase. Before Noctis escalates that protest into a custody argument, I want the original record.\"\n\nHalden's attention sharpened at the note, though he kept his hands behind his back.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"You are requesting restricted archive access rather than waiting for classification review.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes. If this institution already failed someone like me, I want to know the shape of the failure before it repeats through Cael.\"\n\nProfessor Valen inclined her head toward Rhea, the brass keys shifting softly against her palm.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"The eastern archive requires a pre-standardization steward to break the inner lock. If President Valmont authorizes a witness entry, I will open it.\"\n\nRhea studied me for a long moment, then extinguished the prepared council-room seal.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Request accepted. We go to the east Foundation archive as a witness group. The protest can wait for evidence.\"\n\nFor once, the next room belonged to a choice I had made, not a deadline Lucien had filed.\n\nThe last piece of the morning stood beside the fountain.\n\nMira Castellan had ink smudged along her wrist and two Green Circle students behind her, copying every public notice that appeared above the basin. Tucked tight under her left arm sat the worn notebook from the night before, its back edge darkened by a matte black stain that no academy ink made.\n\nProfessor Valen's gaze flicked to that blackened edge and back to Mira's face, a quiet look passing between them before either spoke.\n\nAira blinked.\n\nAira: \"Mira, are you building a public record table before anyone asks?\"\n\nMira did not look away from the water.\n\nMira: \"Someone should write what adults call obvious before they change what obvious means.\"\n\nRhea's mouth tightened by one almost invisible degree.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Accepted.\"\n\nMira startled.\n\nRhea: \"Bring your pages. Come.\"\n\nSo she did. The gallery entered the trial before the trial had a name.\n\n---\n\nThe east archive door was not listed on any student map.\n\nIt sat behind three council seals, one faculty lock, and a corridor built in the old Aurelis style: pale stone, copper veins, and runes too shallow to be decorative yet too patient to be ordinary wardwork.\n\nThe cold pressure behind my sternum sharpened as we approached, carrying ozone, metal, and old rain.\n\nThe hidden Black Circle moved once beneath the false Green crest at my wrist.\n\nElira's hand brushed mine, a quiet question rather than comfort. I answered by breathing slower.\n\nProfessor Halden noticed. Of course he did.\n\nHalden: \"Mr. Aerlen.\"\n\nRhea looked at him. Halden did not finish, and that mattered.\n\nHe had the expression of a man standing before the most important unknown in his career and choosing, with visible pain, not to reach for it.\n\nHalden: \"The old system responds poorly to possession. If I treat you as an object of study, I may corrupt the evidence I want to understand.\"\n\nLeon stared at him.\n\nLeon: \"Was that restraint?\"\n\nHalden: \"Do not make it sound sentimental.\"\n\nAira wrote it down anyway.\n\nProfessor Valen stepped forward and pressed her key against the outer ward, then pulled back as the stone answered on its own.\n\nSeven copper glyphs lit around the arch one by one. Rhea's council flame flared in answer to the first; Cael went rigid as the second read his damaged channels; Aira's folded sheets warmed in her grip; Leon's Blue Circle flickered, offended yet present beside him. Halden's locked black case clicked once under his thumb. When the sixth glyph turned silver above Mira's ink-stained pages and blackened notebook, Mira went pale.\n\nMira: \"Oh no.\"\n\nAira: \"That is usually the correct response to architecture noticing you.\"\n\nThe seventh mark opened behind my ribs before it burned into the keystone.\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED RESPONSE**\n\nThe false Green Circle at my wrist stayed visible. Barely.\n\nThe door did not ask what I was. It asked who was allowed to stand with me.\n\nNo one stepped forward alone. The arch opened, admitting us together rather than swinging wide for rank.\n\nThe copper veins along the corridor brightened one at a time, lighting only when the group moved in step. Leon tested it by slowing half a pace behind Cael; the nearest vein dimmed immediately. He looked offended that architecture had caught him being protective.\n\nLeon: \"I was still coming.\"\n\nAira: \"Ancient systems love emotional precision and hate excuses.\"\n\nCael almost smiled. Almost.\n\nThen the corridor showed him the first record-scar.\n\nIt appeared inside the wall as a child's handprint, preserved in light, pressed flat against stone from the other side. Neither blood nor ghost: just a record of contact, old enough that the name attached to it had decayed into static.\n\nCael stopped breathing for one second. Elira saw it.\n\nElira: \"You do not have to look.\"\n\nCael's answer came after a pause.\n\nCael: \"At Noctis, not looking is treated as permission for someone else to explain it.\"\n\nSo he looked. We all did.\n\nThe corridor gave us seven more record-scars before the hall: a broken faculty seal; a witness mark scratched out by administrative override; a Green Circle student's protest preserved without a name; a Yellow Circle crest split by a crown-shaped lock; a chair turned away from a hearing table; a healer stamp reading stable beside a pulse record that clearly was not; and one line written again and again in different hands.\n\n**DO NOT LET THEM STUDY HIM ALONE**\n\nNo one asked who him meant. The old system did not answer dishonest questions.\n\nThe child's handprint stayed with me longer than the words. Five pale fingers. A flattened palm. No heroic posture, no formal witness stance, just contact pressed hard enough for old stone to remember pressure long after the name decayed.\n\nBeside it, the chair turned away from the hearing table faced nothing. A chair could accuse a room without a mouth.\n\nI looked at the Green protest again. The text had been scratched until only the opening remained: **I SAW...** The rest had been removed by something cleaner than panic. Administrative override, probably. A legal hand with a steady wrist.\n\nMy own false Green crest warmed under my sleeve. For one breath, the concealment felt less like strategy and more like standing inside an old sentence someone else had failed to finish.\n\nHalden stopped before that line. His face sharpened inward, as if suspicion had met an ancestor and disliked the resemblance.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"This was not merely bad procedure.\"\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It was procedure used as appetite.\"\n\nThe corridor accepted the phrase. The copper vein beside him lit clean silver.\n\nMira copied it from behind us.\n\nMira: \"Procedure used as appetite.\"\n\nHalden looked back.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not quote me for style.\"\n\nMira: \"I am quoting you because it sounds like evidence.\"\n\nProfessor Valen touched the edge of the scratched stone lightly, her expression shadowed by old grief, then motioned us forward. The corridor ended.\n\n---\n\nThe Foundation Record Hall smelled like old rain and colder stars.\n\nLibraries kept books. This room kept decisions.\n\nThe Foundation was older than Aurelis Academy, older than Noctis Dominion College, older than the polite fiction that schools owned the magic they taught. It had begun as a witness compact after the first classification wars: no institution could keep its public recognition if it refused Foundation review in a student-harm dispute. Noctis could sneer at mercy, bury files, and polish cruelty into doctrine. It could not ignore this room without asking every other school in the world to treat its diplomas, rankings, and tournament claims as decorative paper.\n\nSilver water filled the center pool. Around it, seven witness chairs faced one another instead of a bench, throne, or tribunal dais. Above the pool hung damaged projections: old Aurelis script, pre-standardization diagrams, Circle Zero fragments, and one repeating phrase that made the air feel thin.\n\n**SAFEGUARD IS NOT PROPERTY**\n\nThe phrase hovered above the pool without ornament. No crest, no title, no rank marker to help a modern room decide whether it respected the source.\n\nMost warnings wanted obedience: red text, locked doors, pain if crossed. The kind of language institutions used when they were less afraid of harm than of being blamed for harm. This was different. It did not call a safeguard dangerous or sacred. It named what institutions tried to turn one into when no one stopped them.\n\nThe Record Hall shifted again. This time the pool showed storage.\n\nRows of old student slates appeared in the water, each one sealed in a square of light. Names blurred. Circle colors remained: Green, Blue, Red, Yellow. No Black in the visible record. Yet every slate carried the same small mark in its lower corner, a witness count crossed out after the fact.\n\nRhea leaned forward.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Those were altered after acceptance.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"By whom?\"\n\nThe pool answered with a blank seal. Mira copied the blank.\n\nA blank seal was not absence. It was someone powerful enough to remove themselves from consequence and leave the wound shaped like procedure.\n\nCael stared at the crossed-out counts.\n\nCael: \"At Noctis, witness counts are internal.\"\n\nLeon: \"Meaning?\"\n\nCael's throat moved.\n\nCael: \"Meaning they count who is allowed to agree.\"\n\nNo one spoke for a moment.\n\nThe seven witness chairs around us did not need to glow. Their arrangement had become suddenly unbearable in its simplicity: no head of table, no hidden bench, no elevated seat where authority could pretend perspective came from height. Seven chairs facing one another, trying with stone and water to prevent the easiest lie in the world: that one person could safely explain another alone.\n\nCael read the room first. His voice was almost soundless.\n\nCael: \"Noctis would call that treason.\"\n\nProfessor Valen stepped near the pool, amber eyes bright with grief and scholarly hunger.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Older than Noctis. Older than the modern rankings. This predates the clean hierarchy everyone teaches now.\"\n\nThe water answered her: Green, Blue, Red, Yellow. Then the rings collapsed inward and showed a black circle split by witness marks. Neither crown nor weapon. Safeguard.\n\nThe record unfolded in broken sequence: early Circle systems built to measure capacity, then institutions learning to turn measurement into ownership, then children identified as safeguards and isolated for classification. Names surfaced and vanished too quickly to read. Every disappearance left the same stamped euphemisms behind: *Withdrawn for study. Removed for stability. Classification sealed.*\n\nElira's hand found mine.\n\nThe Black Circle stirred, and I held it still.\n\nTier 1. Nothing more. A field-reading small enough to pass beneath the room's attention, though the old water noticed anyway. One ripple bent toward me, then drew back, respecting distance because witnesses were present. Enough to pass unseen. Enough to cost.\n\nThe old hall was already reading Cael like damaged script. If I forced it open, Tier 2 would answer, and every witness in the chamber would see the truth before any of us understood what the room wanted.\n\nSo I used Tier 1 in fragments: one adjustment under Leon's stabilizing ring, one under Elira's gold light, one beneath Cassian's red anchor. Each time, I let someone else's visible magic carry the shape while I quieted what the old system thought it was seeing.\n\nThe cost came fast. Cold pressure gathered behind my sternum. Ozone and metal touched the back of my throat. My false Green crest stayed lit at my wrist, yet it lagged behind my pulse by half a beat.\n\nI tucked my hand against my side before anyone but Elira could see the delay.\n\nThe lag was small, which made it worse. Tier 1 did not tear the concealment open; it pulled at the stitching. Each quiet adjustment since the archive door (the witness count, the corridor's old grammar, the hall's refusal to center me) had left the false Green light slower to arrive.\n\nHalden's gaze dropped straight to my wrist.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Aerlen.\"\n\nI did not look at him.\n\nHaru: \"Later.\"\n\nAira's eyes flicked to my hand. She had not seen the Black Circle; she had seen the Green disguise fail to keep time.\n\nAira: \"Haru, breathe slower.\"\n\nGood. She gave the room a human explanation. I obeyed.\n\nThe record shifted again, showing roles instead of names: a safeguard candidate standing inside a circle of seven witnesses; a faculty observer with one hand raised in refusal; a healer crossing out the word *stable*; a public scribe copying testimony from the gallery; a council flame burning between a child and a committee table.\n\nThen the sequence repeated with pieces stripped away. The public scribe removed first. The healer second. The hostile peer third.\n\nEach removal made the central figure brighter and less human, until the final image showed only a black ring surrounded by classification marks.\n\nThe chamber turned cold enough that breath showed white in the air.\n\nProfessor Valen's voice dropped low.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"They thought isolation clarified the phenomenon.\"\n\nHalden answered before anyone else.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It amplified it.\"\n\nThe pool accepted his correction.\n\n**ISOLATION DISTORTS SAFEGUARD RESPONSE**\n\nThat line hit harder than the earlier warnings, because it was practical. The Black Circle was not only dangerous when I lost restraint. It was dangerous when the world removed every reason restraint had to stay human.\n\nThat was the conclusion I let myself keep.\n\nAnother waited underneath it. I felt it arrive and looked away before it finished, letting my eyes travel from the pool to the edge of Elira's sleeve, then to Aira's pencil, then to the copper line in the floor.\n\nThe record had shown a safeguard growing brighter and less human as people were stripped away. I knew how safe it felt to be alone: no one close enough to be used against me, no one near enough to watch my hands shake after restraint, no one whose trust could become a blade in someone else's mouth. Alone had rules I understood.\n\nThe old record made that instinct look less like discipline and more like the first gear of a machine. I filed the thought away beside all the other things that stayed bearable only while unnamed.\n\nElira looked straight into the pool without flinching.\n\nElira: \"Then witness presence is not sentimental.\"\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"It is containment.\"\n\nCael flinched at the word. Elira caught it and corrected herself immediately.\n\nElira: \"No. Not containment.\"\n\nShe searched for the better word. Mira found it from the gallery mark.\n\nMira: \"Context?\"\n\nThe silver water brightened across the basin.\n\n**CONTEXT PRESERVES PERSONHOOD**\n\nMira stared at the inscription.\n\nMira: \"I was guessing.\"\n\nAira: \"Do that more often.\"\n\nLeon folded his arms.\n\nLeon: \"So the old system is saying Haru is less dangerous when people argue with him?\"\n\nCassian: \"Excellent. We are all naturally gifted.\"\n\nProfessor Halden looked at Leon.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Crude. Yet directionally useful.\"\n\nLeon looked briefly offended by the accuracy. Cael watched the two of them with a confusion that hurt, then very carefully tried a small smile.\n\nThe Record Hall did not reward it. No script appeared, no copper vein lit to score the moment as progress. That restraint made me trust the room a little more; it let the smile belong to Cael before evidence could touch it.\n\nThen the pool showed the last image: a safeguard alone, a classification seal descending, and a city-shaped shadow bending away from the seal as if the world itself had flinched. No blood. No battle. Just absence.\n\nEvery witness mark in the room dimmed at once.\n\nRhea's voice stayed quiet and iron-hard.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"This is why Noctis's dawn protest was only the opening move. They will not stop at tournament procedure.\"\n\nHalden watched the ripple die across the pool. His pen lifted once, hovered over his notebook, then stopped before writing my name. That restraint felt heavier than suspicion.\n\nRhea stepped beside the nearest witness chair, reading the faint inscription along the stone rim of the pool.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Safeguard doctrine required distributed witness presence to prevent classification from becoming containment.\"\n\nMira copied every word, her pencil trembling at *containment*.\n\nBeside her, the eastern crystal went pitch-black.\n\nNoctis had escalated from a tournament protest to a formal Foundation Jurisdiction Claim.\n\nMagister Rusk appeared in relay projection, formal robes clean enough to look like accusation.\n\nMagister Rusk: \"Noctis Dominion College asserts delegation authority over Cael Nox, tournament record correction privilege over the seven-second gap, and formal Foundation classification standing regarding Haru Aerlen's unauthorized intervention.\"\n\nRhea stepped directly into the nearest witness mark, her council flame snapping open at her wrist.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis recognizes the claim only inside protected hearing structure. Cael Nox is a recovering student witness, not a delegation object. Record correction is suspended pending Foundation review.\"\n\nAira wrote. Mira copied. Silver light ran clean around the seven witness chairs.\n\n**PROTECTED HEARING STRUCTURE ESTABLISHED**\n\nRusk's mouth tightened. Lucien did not appear. That worried me more than if he had.\n\nThe relay vanished.\n\nFor one breath, the hall stood silent. Then every notice crystal in Aurelis activated at once, projecting the Record Hall's summons through classrooms, courtyards, dormitory corridors, dining tables, faculty offices, and the public rail above the fountain.\n\n**CLASSIFICATION INQUIRY DEMANDED**\n\n**SUBJECT: HARU AERLEN**\n\n**STATUS: UNCLASSIFIED RESPONSE**\n\n**SAFEGUARD COMPATIBILITY UNDER REVIEW**\n\nIn Mira's hands, the blackened back page of her notebook from the midnight fountain shivered. The dark stain along its margin pulled back like ink retreating from light, exposing her copied **CIRCLE ZERO** fragment in pale silver for two heartbeats before settling dark again.\n\nProfessor Valen's breath caught. Mira stared down at the page, swallowed hard, and kept writing beside the black edge anyway.\n\nThe academy learned my name before I could decide how to carry it.\n\nFirst came silence, then noise rolling down through the old vents and relay crystals: chairs scraping in classrooms, a tray hitting stone in the dining hall, someone in the courtyard shouting that the fountain's water had gone dark, a faculty voice ordering calm in a tone that created none.\n\nRhea closed her eyes for half a breath. When she opened them, she lifted two fingers and sealed the outer vent relays with a sharp pulse of red light.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"No one leaves through an unsealed route. No one answers a relay without a council mark. No one speaks Haru Aerlen's classification as fact.\"\n\nLeon looked toward me.\n\nLeon: \"Is unclassified response better or worse than Green?\"\n\nAira: \"Politically? Worse. Socially? Worse. Academically? Halden is about to become impossible.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I was already impossible.\"\n\nCael had not moved since the crystals activated. He stared at my name hanging in the air with the look of someone watching another person become useful to systems that did not love them.\n\nCael: \"They will call it protection first.\"\n\nI looked at him. His voice stayed flat.\n\nCael: \"Noctis always calls the first wall protection. The second wall is review. The third wall is honor. By the time someone calls it a cage, the lock has a signature.\"\n\nNo one answered quickly. Fast comfort would have insulted him.\n\nElira stepped forward.\n\nElira: \"Then we do not let the first wall stand alone.\"\n\nShe looked at Rhea.\n\nElira: \"Public witness structure.\"\n\nRhea nodded once.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Mira Castellan.\"\n\nMira almost dropped her pen.\n\nMira: \"Yes?\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Can your gallery table copy a correction fast enough to beat rumor?\"\n\nMira glanced at the two Green Circle students beside her. They looked terrified, yet one of them pushed a fresh stack of blank sheets toward her without hesitating.\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nRhea's eyes sharpened.\n\nMira: \"We can make rumor trip.\"\n\nRhea considered that.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Accepted.\"\n\nMira wrote the first public correction while the academy still shook: *Foundation inquiry does not equal classification.*\n\nHer classmate copied beneath it: *Unclassified response is not property.*\n\nThe third added: *Cael Nox remains protected witness.*\n\nThe pages went out through council relay, gallery hands, and fountain reflection. Imperfect, hurried, yet fast enough that when the first frightened rumor reached the west courtyard, it collided with ink-stained handwriting from students nobody had thought to manage.\n\nInside the Record Hall, the silver pool rippled clean from edge to edge, acknowledging the gallery's pages.\n\nLucien still did not appear. His absence pressed harder than his voice would have.\n\nHigh above us, a notice crystal tried to append Noctis commentary to the inquiry line. The Foundation erased it, then burned two final words across every active surface in the hall.\n\n**TRIAL REQUIRED**\n\nHalden spoke them aloud, giving the room's cold dread a human voice.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Trial required.\"\n\nCael's healer seal pulsed once. Under my sleeve, the false Green crest flickered, tired from holding shape around a name the old world had begun to pull apart.\n\nElira saw. She did not cover my wrist. She stepped half an inch closer so anyone watching would have to see her choice too, and did not let go.\n\nThe Record Hall did not let us leave immediately.\n\n*Trial required* had sounded like an ending because people like Rhea could make even danger sound procedural. Old Aurelis did not move like a court clerk. The hall held its doors sealed while the notice crystals overhead continued repeating my name across campus, each repetition shifting depending on who heard it.\n\nIn the dining hall, rumor heard *subject*. In faculty offices, ambition heard *classification*. In the Green Circle dormitory, Mira told me later, some first-years heard *unclassified* and looked at their own wrists as if the word might be contagious.\n\nInside the hall, we felt all of it as pressure rather than sound. Every public reaction leaned against the witness marks around the room, demanding entry into the record before anyone had bothered understanding what they were reacting to. The silver pool darkened and began to churn against its stone lip.\n\nRhea's council seal sharpened.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Contain the surge.\"\n\nThe Foundation did not answer. Halden did.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"No.\"\n\nEveryone looked at him. Especially me.\n\nHalden stood with his black case still latched, knuckles pale around the handle. I could feel the questions inside him the way old wards felt weather. What had the door recognized in me? Why had the fountain failed at black? How much of the seven-second gap belonged to intervention and how much to old safeguard response? He wanted answers so badly the wanting had turned into visible discipline.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not contain public interpretation inside the same hall that just named him subject.\"\n\nRhea's eyes narrowed.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Reason.\"\n\nHalden looked at the witness marks, then back toward the corridor wall and its line: **DO NOT LET THEM STUDY HIM ALONE**.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Because containment makes the hall the first author. Let the public be wrong outside before we turn accuracy into a cage inside.\"\n\nThe sentence cost him. I knew because it cost me to hear it.\n\nRhea considered him for one long breath.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Alternative?\"\n\nHalden opened his empty hand.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Distributed correction. No classification. No theory. Sequence only.\"\n\nMira's pencil rose before she realized she had moved.\n\nMira: \"Sequence?\"\n\nHalden turned toward her, something shifting in his expression as he remembered that public record did not mean adult record.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"What happened in order. Nothing else.\"\n\nMira swallowed.\n\nMira: \"Noctis filed claim. Aurelis requested witness protection. Foundation opened inquiry. Haru Aerlen named unclassified response. Trial required.\"\n\nCael's voice came from beside Leon.\n\nCael: \"Cael Nox protected witness. Not delegation object.\"\n\nMira wrote it down.\n\nLeon added, rougher than he probably meant.\n\nLeon: \"No one knows what Haru is.\"\n\nAira's pen paused. Leon glanced at me once, then away.\n\nLeon: \"That includes people pretending certainty.\"\n\nA quiet silver pulse ran across the surface of the pool, smoothing the dark churn back to glass.\n\nCassian let out a low breath.\n\nCassian: \"I did not know Blue Circle pride could mature into accuracy.\"\n\nLeon: \"I can still hit you.\"\n\nCassian: \"There he is.\"\n\nElira kept holding my hand, her thumb resting once against my knuckles to remind me I existed inside the room, not above it.\n\nThe Black Circle under my skin wanted to answer the hall. It hated being named without language. It hated public fear pressing at a concealment I had built one careful day at a time.\n\nFor one beat, I understood every terrible thing power could do while calling itself clarification. I could show them enough that nobody would dare mislabel me. I could force the fountain to draw black properly. I could fold every notice crystal on campus into obedient silence.\n\nAll of those thoughts arrived dressed as self-defense.\n\nElira's fingers tightened around mine. She had not read my mind; she had felt my hand go rigid.\n\nElira: \"Let them be imprecise for one more minute.\"\n\nHaru: \"That sounds dangerous.\"\n\nElira: \"It is.\"\n\nShe looked at the pool, then toward the corridor of forgotten witnesses.\n\nElira: \"So is correcting everyone before they have chosen whether they are willing to be responsible.\"\n\nThe copper veins beneath our feet warmed with a low, resonant hum. Professor Valen exhaled softly, her eyes fixed on the silver water settling calm again.\n\nOnly then did the tall archive doors unlatch.\n\nThe corridor outside looked identical at first glance: pale stone, copper veins, shallow runes holding old patience beneath our boots. Yet every record-scar we had passed on the way in now answered us differently.\n\nThe child's handprint burned clearer. The scratched Green protest had gained a single silver mark at the end of **I SAW...**, admitting the sentence had been cut short rather than erased.\n\nMira stopped in front of it, her copied pages trembling against her ribs.\n\nMira: \"Can it be completed?\"\n\nNo one answered quickly. Fast answers would have turned the dead into homework.\n\nRhea stepped beside her.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Not by guessing.\"\n\nMira nodded, then wrote cleanly beneath the scar on a fresh sheet: *Record incomplete. Do not summarize.*\n\nThe nearest copper vein warmed under her palm. Mira stared at it.\n\nAira's voice went quiet.\n\nAira: \"That means yes, I think.\"\n\nAhead of us, Cael stopped before the healer stamp reading *stable* beside a pulse line that had never been stable at all.\n\nHis face went still in the old Noctis way. Softness around him only seemed to tighten his shoulders; in Noctis, gentle tones meant the blade was being wrapped before it went in. Leon noticed and made himself stand beside Cael rather than in front, hands loose at his sides as if he had practiced not grabbing someone back from an edge.\n\nCael lifted one hand near the false stamp without touching the stone.\n\nCael: \"They used this word on me.\"\n\nNo one asked which word. *Stable.* The cruelest word in a room where alive and useful were treated as interchangeable.\n\nCael: \"Stable meant quiet enough to move.\"\n\nLeon muttered something under his breath that sounded like a threat and a prayer sharing a coat. Rhea heard.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Cael Nox's definition entered under protected witness context.\"\n\nThe wall did not glow. Some records did not know how to apologize.\n\nCael lowered his hand. For one breath, I thought he would keep walking. He did not.\n\nHe turned back toward the false healer stamp. His own healer seal pulsed amber at his collar, trying to keep him seated, monitored, quiet. Every adult in the corridor wanted sequence, safety, containment. I wanted, with a sharp violence I had no right to, for the old wall to stop asking children to finish sentences adults had broken.\n\nCael reached up and unfastened the healer seal.\n\nRhea's council flame flared at her wrist. Halden inhaled sharply. Professor Valen caught her breath without stepping between them. Leon took half a step and forced himself to stop so hard his boots scraped stone.\n\nLeon: \"Cael.\"\n\nCael did not look at him.\n\nCael: \"It says I am stable.\"\n\nThe seal in his palm flashed warning gold, then red. A thin diagnostic thread snapped toward his pulse point, trying to reattach.\n\nCael closed his fist around it. The thread snapped.\n\nNo explosion, no surge, just a small medical light dying because a boy had decided that being watched was not the same as being helped.\n\nCael swayed.\n\nLeon made a low sound in his throat. Elira moved before I did, stepping sideways into Leon's path with one palm raised, never touching him.\n\nElira: \"Ask.\"\n\nLeon looked furious with the rule, and obeyed it anyway.\n\nLeon: \"Do you want help?\"\n\nCael's shoulders jerked. The question hit harder than the broken thread. Noctis had taught him to answer pain, never want. Pain could be ranked and spent. Want insisted the person inside the body had standing.\n\nCael's mouth opened. No sound came. The corridor waited in complete silence, no script lighting, no wall scoring the breath.\n\nCael pressed the dead healer seal against the stone directly beneath *stable*. The metal clicked and caught inside a shallow groove that had not been there a heartbeat earlier.\n\nCael: \"I want to walk out without that word touching me.\"\n\nHis voice shook, yet it carried down the stone hall.\n\nRhea's council flame dimmed, leashed without vanishing.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Granted.\"\n\nBehind him, Aira's pen scratched hard against her clipboard.\n\nCael turned his head. Aira had written *stable*, then slashed a line through it so deep the paper nearly tore. Beside it, in sharp dark ink, she wrote: *alive is not the same as compliant.*\n\nNobody asked permission to keep the correction. For the first time all morning, Cael did not look like he wanted anyone to throw it away.\n\nHalden's pen twitched by instinct against his notebook, then stopped. He looked down at his own hand as if annoyed to find a scholar still attached to it.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I will not record that as a medical recommendation.\"\n\nCael gave him a look flat enough to be Noctis again.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I mean it. Some choices should not become instructions for the next injured student to imitate.\"\n\nCael studied him, thin and suspicious and alive.\n\nCael: \"Then what is it?\"\n\nHalden's jaw worked once. He could have said testimony, or resistance, or turned the moment clean enough for a lecture. Instead, he looked at the dead healer seal pinned under the false stamp.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"A boy being tired of a word.\"\n\nCael let out a breath. Then he walked.\n\nThe first step was ugly; his knee almost buckled, and Leon's hands stayed open at his sides without grabbing. The second scraped stone. The third found enough balance to turn stubborn. By the fourth, sweat darkened Cael's collar. No one applauded.\n\nMira cried anyway, quietly and furiously, as if tears had interrupted work she had not authorized. One drop struck her copied sheet and blurred *Record incomplete. Do not summarize.*\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nAira raised two fingers for a drying charm. Mira slapped her palm over the damp ink before it could land.\n\nAira froze.\n\nMira: \"Leave it.\"\n\nAira: \"It will distort the copy.\"\n\nMira: \"I know.\"\n\nThe damp ink bled into the paper fibers, warping two letters until the sentence looked bruised. Mira stared down at it with fierce, stubborn ownership.\n\nMira: \"This one is mine.\"\n\nNo one argued.\n\nCael reached the open outer threshold and stopped there, trembling hard enough that even Cassian dropped the last of his theatrical boredom.\n\nLeon asked again, voice rougher.\n\nLeon: \"Do you want help now?\"\n\nCael closed his eyes for half a second, then held out one hand.\n\nLeon took it. Not wrist, not elbow. Hand.\n\nThe grip lasted three steps and ended before anyone could turn it into theater.\n\nAt the corridor's end, the outer doors stood open to the noise of Aurelis: students, notices, fear, curiosity, the first wrong versions of my name already racing across stone.\n\nProfessor Valen lingered a step behind to pull the inner ward shut with her brass key, then fell in quietly beside Halden at the threshold.\n\nHalden stopped and looked at me, his black case still latched.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"If I ask the wrong question later, remind me that I knew better today.\"\n\nIt was the closest thing to an apology he could survive giving.\n\nHaru: \"I will.\"\n\nHalden nodded once. Together, we stepped out into the academy's gaze.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Mira Castellan - The Public Table\n\nMira's public record table stopped being theoretical eleven minutes later.\n\nShe had hauled a spare drafting desk beside the east fountain because the water was already lying badly, and public things deserved correction where people had to walk past it. That had felt sensible right up until the crowd arrived.\n\nClassrooms had neat rows that told fear where to sit. The arcade crowd gathered in uneven pockets: Green Circle students hanging toward the pillars, Blue Circle students close enough to squint and far enough to deny curiosity, Red Circle heirs murmuring over inward-turned relay charms, and one Yellow Circle senior leaning under the stone arch with folded arms as if deciding whether history suited her coat.\n\nMira laid her ink-stained sheets across the desk. Her fingers trembled, smearing a comma, and she scowled at her own wrist.\n\nFinn Castellan, her older brother, who had been copying notices behind her since breakfast, dropped a chunky gray stone onto the top corner of her pages before the breeze could scatter them. He had clearly pried it out of the courtyard flowerbed.\n\nMira: \"That is not archive safe.\"\n\nFinn: \"It is wind safe.\"\n\nThe page stayed flat. Mira glared at the stone, then let her shoulders drop.\n\nMira: \"Temporary acceptance.\"\n\nFinn grinned like he had just won a scholarship.\n\nThe first questioner was a Blue Circle second-year with spotless silver cuffs who stopped three feet out, leaning forward as though proximity might stain his collar.\n\nStudent: \"Is it true?\"\n\nMira: \"Which part?\"\n\nStudent: \"The unclassified thing.\"\n\nMira tapped the top line with the blunt end of her pencil.\n\nMira: \"Foundation inquiry does not equal classification.\"\n\nStudent: \"That is not an answer.\"\n\nMira: \"It is the answer we have.\"\n\nHe frowned, dropping his voice just enough for six Green first-years nearby to stare hard at their shoes.\n\nStudent: \"People are saying Black Circle.\"\n\nSomething hot flared behind Mira's ribs. She had no hidden circle, no ancient seal, and a borrowed desk whose left leg wobbled whenever anyone leaned on it. She pressed her palm flat against the wood to steady it.\n\nMira: \"People also said Cael Nox was transferred under lawful delegation yesterday.\"\n\nThe Blue student glanced toward the archive doors.\n\nMira: \"They were wrong then too.\"\n\nColor climbed his neck.\n\nStudent: \"I was only asking.\"\n\nMira: \"Then ask what happened in order.\"\n\nBehind her, Finn let out a soft breath.\n\nFinn: \"Oh.\"\n\nMira did not turn around; if she caught his grin, she might realize how loud her voice had sounded and lose her nerve.\n\nThe Blue student read the sequence slowly: Noctis claim, Aurelis witness protection, Foundation inquiry, trial required, no classification. He left without apologizing, which was fine. Mira had not dragged a desk outside for apologies.\n\nThe next student was a tiny Green Circle first-year with tightly braided hair and ink already smudged across her thumb, clutching her opposite wrist as if her crest might peel off.\n\nGirl: \"Can I copy it?\"\n\nMira slid a blank sheet across the grain.\n\nMira: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe girl perched on the fountain rim because there were no spare chairs. She copied the first line cleanly, then hurried the second and wrote **UNCLASSED** instead of **UNCLASSIFIED**. Her face went chalk-white.\n\nGirl: \"I can redo it.\"\n\nMira almost said yes. Clean pages survived filing. Clean pages made instructors take your work seriously. Then she pictured the scratched wall inside the corridor: **I SAW...**\n\nShe laid the girl's page right beside her own under the garden stone.\n\nMira: \"Mark it as your copy.\"\n\nGirl: \"But it's wrong.\"\n\nMira: \"Then anyone quoting it has to admit you were there.\"\n\nFrom the rim of the crowd, a Red Circle boy laughed, light and cutting.\n\nRed Student: \"Green witness doctrine now? That is new.\"\n\nFinn surged upright too fast. Mira caught his sleeve and shook her head once. If Finn shouted, everyone watching would write the table off as a fight. She pressed harder on the wobbling desk edge.\n\nMira: \"It is not doctrine.\"\n\nRed Student: \"Then what is it?\"\n\nMira: \"Work.\"\n\nThe word landed too plain to mock. The Red student looked away first.\n\nUnder the arch, the Yellow Circle senior unfolded her arms and walked forward. The crowd parted automatically, a reflex Mira resented right before catching herself stepping back half an inch too.\n\nThe senior lifted a blank sheet from the stack.\n\nSenior: \"May I?\"\n\nMira almost said yes out of habit, then caught herself and tightened her grip on her pencil.\n\nMira: \"Why?\"\n\nThe arcade went pin-drop quiet. The senior blinked, then let a genuine smile touch her mouth.\n\nSenior: \"Because if only Green students copy it, people will call it grievance.\"\n\nMira hated that the answer made sense.\n\nMira: \"Then copy the Green student's error too.\"\n\nThe senior looked down at **UNCLASSED**, and her smile sharpened into respect.\n\nSenior: \"Good.\"\n\nShe wrote the entire sequence out, error preserved, and signed her name cleanly at the bottom.\n\nBy the time Rhea Valmont's exhausted aide arrived with official council paper, nineteen sheets lay drying under open air in six different hands, three mistakes preserved on purpose, anchored by Finn's unauthorized landscaping stone.\n\nThe aide stared at the rock.\n\nMira: \"Temporary acceptance.\"\n\nAide: \"I was not going to ask.\"\n\nBehind the desk, the fountain stirred. Four obedient rings rose across the basin: Green, Blue, Red, Yellow. Heads snapped around immediately. Mira grabbed her pencil anyway.\n\nThe rings thinned. Black did not surface. Instead, the Green ring widened cleanly to the stone lip and spilled a thin mirror of water across the paving, stopping one finger-width short of Mira's stack.\n\nFinn stepped closer to the rim.\n\nFinn: \"That is new.\"\n\nMira: \"Do not give the fountain compliments. It will become confident.\"\n\nThe Green first-year burst out laughing before clapping both hands over her mouth. Mira lifted the girl's **UNCLASSED** sheet and moved it to the front edge of the desk, closest to the wet stone.\n\nGirl: \"Why?\"\n\nMira: \"Because it is the one the water stopped beside.\"\n\nGirl: \"But it's wrong.\"\n\nMira: \"Then the fountain has poor taste.\"\n\nThe girl stared, then laughed again, slower to hide it this time.\n\nThe widened Green ring held for one steady breath before sinking back into ordinary water. No ancient inscription lit the air, leaving only wet stone and ink drying in the breeze. Mira preferred it that way; glow made people argue over who owned a miracle, while quiet let the work stay theirs.\n\nAfter final bell, the Green first-year came back and set a second sheet beside her first, **UNCLASSIFIED** spelled cleanly across the line.\n\nMira: \"You did not have to redo it.\"\n\nGirl: \"I know.\"\n\nShe tapped the first page.\n\nGirl: \"That one was when I was scared.\"\n\nThen the second.\n\nGirl: \"This one is after.\"\n\nMira looked at both pages side by side.\n\nMira: \"Accepted.\"\n\nAt sunset, Finn swapped the landscaping boulder for a smaller river pebble.\n\nMira: \"That is still not archive safe.\"\n\nFinn: \"It is less illegal.\"\n\nMira: \"Temporary acceptance continues.\"\n\nBefore heading to dinner, Mira tucked one blank sheet under the pebble, leaving room for whoever arrived late and still needed a place to start.\n\n---\n\n## Coda: Before the Hearing\n\nAcross the northern ridge, inside the dark stone wing assigned to the Noctis delegation, the relay crystal above the table stayed lit long after midnight.\n\n**TRIAL REQUIRED**\n\nMagister Rusk stood stiff beside the window, a thick stack of appeal drafts clutched against his chest.\n\nMagister Rusk: \"Valmont locked the hearing inside Foundation structure before our revision brief cleared. If Valemere and Sol Aeternum sit on the witness rail tomorrow, our custody claim over Cael Nox collapses.\"\n\nLucien Voss sat calmly at the long black desk, not touching the drafts. A single brass paperweight held down the tournament's missing seven-second transcript.\n\nLucien: \"Let Cael testify.\"\n\nRusk stared at him.\n\nMagister Rusk: \"You want him speaking before the continental delegates?\"\n\nLucien: \"Denying a boy's bruises makes us look small. Letting him speak makes Aurelis feel righteous, and righteous rooms stop watching their footing.\"\n\nHe tapped the blank space where seven seconds of arena light had vanished into silver static.\n\nLucien: \"Tomorrow is not about whether Cael hurt. Tomorrow is about why a Green Circle transfer thought his private perception outranked judges, healers, and tournament law. Either Haru Aerlen had no right to interfere, or he holds authority this court has not named.\"\n\nHis pale eyes reflected the crystal's cold glow.\n\nLucien: \"Ask the right question, and the Foundation itself will force him into the light.\"\n\nFar across the campus, dawn broke pale silver over the Aurelis towers.\n\nI stood at the top of the east stairs and tugged my sleeve cuff straight over my wrist. The false Green crest held its dim shape, still half a beat slow against my pulse.\n\nElira stopped beside me, sunlight catching the dark crystal pendant at her collarbone. Ahead of us, the doors to the converted Record Hall stood wide, seven witness marks already burning bright around the silver pool.\n\nElira: \"Ready?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nHer fingers brushed mine once before we stepped into view of the waiting gallery.\n\nElira: \"Good. Then we won't pretend this is easy.\"\n\nThe hearing bell struck once, deep and iron-clear, and we walked inside.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-06-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep7","label":"Episode 7","number":"07","file":"episode-07-cover.webp","fallback":"The Shape of a Team","eyebrow":"Rivals Named","recap":"","accent":["#06d6a0","#8338ec","#f9c74f"],"displayLabel":"Episode 7","displayNumber":"07","displayEyebrow":"Tournament Faultlines","arcLabel":"Tournament Faultlines","title":"Talen Speaks","body":"The Foundation Trial began without the mercy of corridors.\n\nNoctis had filed enough paper to build a wall. Rhea refused to let them hide behind it.\n\nBy midmorning, the Record Hall had become a hearing chamber. Seven witness marks burned around the silver pool. Faculty filled the upper rail while students crowded the gallery. Valemere's delegation occupied the west witness tier, Drakenshard's field analysts guarded duplicate match logs, and two Sol Aeternum healers sat where Noctis could see their signatures. Below them, Mira kept the Green Circle record table, copying in ink that already stained both hands.\n\nCael sat between Leon and Cassian.\n\nAcross the floor, Nera Vail stood on the Noctis side with her binding rings locked flat against her wrists.\n\nLucien Voss stood beneath the eastern projection and looked perfectly at home in a room built to measure harm. His ease in it was obscene.\n\nRhea opened with one sentence.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"This hearing concerns Cael Nox's status as witness, tournament record integrity, and the classification pressure attached to Haru Aerlen's intervention.\"\n\nLucien smiled.\n\nLucien: \"How carefully Aurelis places the Green Circle student last.\"\n\nRhea: \"How quickly Noctis tries to make the injured student disappear behind him.\"\n\nThe silver pool accepted both statements for record. No one wasted breath pretending this would be clean.\n\nCael testified first.\n\nHe stood because sitting made him feel like Noctis had already won. The healer seal over his chest pulsed blue-white at the edge and red at the center. His hands shook, and he let everyone see.\n\nCael: \"At Noctis, silence is treated as discipline. Fear is treated as preparation. Refusal is treated as failure to understand the honor of being useful.\"\n\nThe words scraped out rough and uneven, which made them harder to dismiss. He described the match, Lucien's lightless pressure, the moment his channels opened in two directions, and the seven seconds the official record had tried to swallow.\n\nLeon listened without interrupting. Beside him, Cassian kept his fire banked low around his knuckles.\n\nWhen Cael finished, Lucien stepped forward into the open floor.\n\nLucien: \"We have heard that Cael Nox was afraid. Fear is not illegality.\"\n\nThe gallery stiffened. Lucien turned in a slow circle, letting every tier see his calm.\n\nLucien: \"Haru Aerlen interfered in sanctioned combat. The match had field judges, healers, authority, and rules. A Green Circle transfer decided his private perception outranked all of it.\"\n\nHis eyes came to me.\n\nLucien: \"Who gave him that right?\"\n\nThe hall did not reject the question. That was the trap.\n\nLucien did not raise his voice. He let the chamber's stone carry the polish of it.\n\nLucien: \"Either intervention operates within authority, or it becomes arrogance with a gentler name. Mercy requires authority. Authority comes from recognized power. Therefore, either Haru Aerlen had no right to interfere, or Haru Aerlen possesses authority this court has not recognized.\"\n\nI felt the Black Circle go cold behind my ribs. Under my sleeve, the false Green crest pinched sharply against my wrist, flickering as the air around my knuckles tightened with static I had to force back down.\n\nElira's voice cut in before mine could.\n\nElira: \"Or authority failed to act in time, and witnesses preserved the person authority abandoned.\"\n\nThe silver pool held both arguments.\n\n**AUTHORITY QUESTION PRESERVED**\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Elira - The Order of Harm\n\nElira Vale had learned early that hearings were not built to discover truth. They were built to decide which truths could survive being arranged.\n\nThe Record Hall was beautiful: silver pool, old stone, light precise enough to make every face look accountable. Aurelis liked rooms like this because beauty made authority feel less like pressure. Elira hated how well she understood the trick, because she had benefited from it her whole life.\n\nYellow Circle students were placed where light confirmed them. Their mistakes were called intensity. Their silences were read as judgment. Cael Nox had been placed where utility could pass for honor. And Haru Aerlen had been placed last in Rhea's opening sentence because every other placement would have let Lucien make him first.\n\nElira had watched that sequence form, and she had watched Lucien reach straight past Cael's bruises for the one lever that could overturn the room: *Who gave him that right?*\n\nThe clean answer was easy. No one.\n\nThe honest answer had blood under it. Field judges had frozen. Healers had missed the window. The record had tried to swallow seven seconds and call digestion procedure. Haru had seen a boy turned into a conduit and moved anyway.\n\nBeneath the hearing table, where no relay could turn the motion into performance, Elira pressed her palms flat against her skirt. She felt Haru beside her pulling himself inward, trying to become smaller than Lucien's question before his wrist gave anything away.\n\nShe would not let Lucien turn restraint into guilt. Stepping into the sentence had never been about answering for Haru. It was about keeping the order of harm intact before power could rewrite the scene.\n\n---\n\nThe preserved question hung above the pool like a blade on a wet thread.\n\nEveryone at the Aurelis rail knew what waited underneath it. If the court accepted Lucien's frame and stripped witness protection from my intervention, Noctis and the Foundation examiners would force an isolated Circle-strip inspection. They would peel back the false Green illusion on my wrist, drag the Black Circle into a containment ledger, and use that reclassification to void Rhea's protection order and pull Cael straight back into Noctis custody.\n\nLucien had not come to deny pain. Denying pain would make him look small beside Cael and Elira's letters. He had come to make pain legally insufficient.\n\nRhea understood first. Her red seal did not flare; it narrowed.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis does not argue that compassion alone rewrites combat authority.\"\n\nThe Noctis officials shifted, sensing concession. Rhea spoke again before they could breathe.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis argues that authority which fails to respond to active coercion cannot later claim exclusive right to define the rescue.\"\n\nThe Foundation pool held both sentences.\n\nAira wrote them in separate columns. Down at the Green Circle table, Mira copied the columns and added tiny ink headers at the top:\n\n**WHAT LUCIEN WANTS**\n\n**WHAT RHEA MEANS**\n\nA Green Circle student beside her whispered, \"Can you write that?\"\n\nMira whispered back, \"I already did.\"\n\nLucien's gaze flicked briefly toward Mira's pages. He noticed too. The gallery record had fewer habits of obedience than the faculty rail, and that made it dangerous.\n\nRhea turned the authority question toward me. Gently would have ruined it. She turned it cleanly.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Haru Aerlen, during the seven-second record gap, did you take action that affected Cael Nox's survival?\"\n\nEvery witness mark around the pool tightened.\n\nCael closed his eyes. Elira's hand stayed at her side, leaving the answer mine.\n\nI stepped into the witness mark.\n\nThe false Green Circle at my wrist lit obediently, a fraction of a second too slow to match my pulse. Beneath the cuff, cold static bit at my skin as the Black Circle pressed upward, ready to swallow the mark whole if I slipped. Halden caught the slight lag in the green glow. His gaze sharpened, though he kept silent.\n\nI forced my breath steady against the cold tightening behind my sternum.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe pool darkened. Lucien's smile went still.\n\nHaru: \"I saw Cael's channels were open in two directions. Lucien's lightless mana was using him as a path while the crown field pressure used the same damage as an opening. When Elira broke the visible construct, the pressure around Cael did not vanish. It recoiled.\"\n\nSilver lines rose over the pool, reconstructing the moment in stripped geometry: Cael at the center, Lucien's pressure as a dark thread, crown field recoil like a gold blade turned sideways.\n\nHaru: \"I redirected the recoil away from his channels.\"\n\nLucien: \"With Green Circle magic.\"\n\nThere it was. The cleanest knife in the room.\n\nLie, and Cael's testimony carried a hole everyone could see. Speak too freely, and the isolated inspection began before noon.\n\nElira spoke before the silence hardened, anchoring the sequence without taking the floor from me.\n\nElira: \"The question before the hall is whether intervention occurred, whether it preserved life, and whether the record can classify the act without isolating the actor.\"\n\nRhea's seal answered with a dull red pulse.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Correct.\"\n\nHalden leaned over the faculty rail, voice dry and exact.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The method may be reviewed under protected classification limits after witness autonomy is established. Premature naming risks contaminating the safeguard inquiry.\"\n\nLucien looked genuinely delighted.\n\nLucien: \"Safeguard. How quickly the forbidden word becomes convenient.\"\n\nHalden held his stare.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Convenience is not accuracy. You rely on that confusion too often.\"\n\nPens scraped across the gallery loud enough to sound like rain. The Foundation pool accepted the limited answer.\n\n**LIFE-PRESERVING INTERVENTION CONFIRMED**\n\n**METHOD REVIEW DEFERRED PENDING WITNESS AUTONOMY**\n\nMy pulse hammered against the false Green crest on my wrist, but my knees held. Across the floor, Cael opened his eyes. He did not thank me. He simply stayed alive in the record.\n\n---\n\nNera's technical statement should have been routine. Lucien clearly expected it to be.\n\nFor that exact reason, her opening lines shifted the weight of the room.\n\nNera: \"Cael Nox's preparation met Noctis tournament standards.\"\n\nLucien did not move. Nera lowered her gaze by a fraction, but her voice did not drop.\n\nNera: \"The pressure exceeded standard combat parameters. The relay gap was not caused by student weakness. It was produced by competing channel stress through preexisting conduit damage.\"\n\nCael stared at her as if a locked door had just admitted it knew his name.\n\nRhea did not pounce. She turned smoothly to the neutral witness mark.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Soren Ashvale of Valemere Royal Institute.\"\n\nSoren's remote projection opened above the silver pool, sharp and cold through the long-distance Valemere glass. He confirmed relay instability, timing discrepancy, and threshold delay without offering Aurelis a single word of flattery.\n\nSoren: \"The official record is incomplete. Any ruling based on it alone would be technically dishonest.\"\n\nThe pool accepted the phrasing. Lucien's smile thinned.\n\nThen Soren surprised everyone except Elira by continuing. The remote mark brightened as he lifted a thin stack of Valemere timing sheets into the frame: pure arithmetic, relay load, and field-judge response lag.\n\nSoren: \"The seven-second gap is not one absence. It is three overlapping absences. The field relay failed to preserve Cael Nox's channel state. The Noctis delegation mirror failed to preserve Lucien Voss's secondary pressure path. The tournament master record failed to preserve who moved first after the collapse threshold.\"\n\nHalden's pen started moving fast.\n\nSoren: \"One failure may be accident. Three failures around the same endangered student indicate either coordinated revision or a system built to lose inconvenient detail.\"\n\nLucien: \"Careful, Valemere.\"\n\nSoren met his eyes through the relay glass.\n\nSoren: \"I am being careful. That is why I brought numbers.\"\n\nFrom Cael's left, Cassian murmured, \"I like him less when he is useful.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is how I feel about you constantly.\"\n\nCael did not smile. He was staring at the projected timing sheets.\n\nCael: \"They knew where the gap was.\"\n\nOn the Noctis side, Nera's binding rings clicked once against her wrist. Barely audible. I heard it, and by the slight tilt of his chin, Lucien heard it too.\n\nThen the western relay opened beside Soren's frame.\n\nTalen Mire appeared inside a Valemere healer room, one hand braced in silver support, his faded Blue Circle visible at the wrist where old channel trauma had healed crooked. Elira's posture went still and alert.\n\nTalen looked at the silver pool instead of Lucien.\n\nTalen: \"I understand I may stop.\"\n\nRhea: \"You may stop at any point.\"\n\nTalen: \"Then I will start before I lose courage.\"\n\nHe walked the room through the junior circuit match: Valemere versus Noctis, water-bind support against lightless suppression, the decisive lock, the surrender threshold, and Lucien holding past it.\n\nA memory projection rose above the pool, showing a younger Lucien with one hand lifted calmly while Talen's channels closed one by one.\n\nTalen: \"On the fourth second, he released me cleanly.\"\n\nLucien: \"Then the match ended within threshold.\"\n\nTalen looked straight at him for the first time.\n\nTalen: \"My hand still shakes when the weather turns.\"\n\nSilence dropped over the hall.\n\nTalen did not shout or accuse. He described consequences that had learned to live quietly.\n\nTalen: \"I drop cups with my left hand. Not always, just enough that people stopped handing them to me in public. I can still cast water-bind support, but I plan around pain before I plan around strategy. My family calls it practical. My instructors call it adaptation. I call it what happened after everyone agreed the match was clean.\"\n\nElira's eyes shone, though she did not let a single tear fall.\n\nTalen shifted his gaze toward her.\n\nTalen: \"Lady Elira wrote to me after the match. She did not say she could fix it or tell me to be brave. She wrote, 'What happened was real even if the record refuses to become ashamed.'\"\n\nAt the Green Circle table, Mira's pen froze for half a beat, then scratched across the paper hard enough to nearly tear it.\n\nLucien looked faintly bored. That was how I knew the testimony had struck home. Behind Talen in the Valemere room, his mother sat with her fingers laced so tightly her knuckles had gone bloodless.\n\nProfessor Valen spoke gently from the faculty rail.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Do you believe what happened to Cael Nox resembles what happened to you?\"\n\nTalen looked through the relay toward Cael, and Cael held his gaze.\n\nTalen: \"Yes. The method changed. The patience did not.\"\n\nElira's old correspondence entered the record next. Before the ink dried on the pool's log, Lucien requested the right to question him.\n\nElira's gold light flared bright. Cael's healer seal pulsed warning blue. Even Soren's remote projection sharpened, as if Valemere itself had leaned toward the glass.\n\nRhea addressed Talen first.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Remote witness Mire, do you consent to limited questioning?\"\n\nBehind him, Talen's mother made a small choked sound. Talen heard it. He flexed his braced fingers once and tightened the leather strap binding his casting wrist.\n\nTalen: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira's lips parted, ready to object, until Talen looked straight at her through the static.\n\nTalen: \"I want to answer while my hand is shaking and still have it count.\"\n\nThe pool accepted consent. Lucien stepped into the question mark.\n\nLucien: \"Talen Mire, did I violate the written surrender threshold in our junior match?\"\n\nTalen: \"No.\"\n\nThe answer hurt the room.\n\nLucien: \"Did tournament judges declare the match clean?\"\n\nTalen's knuckles went white against the arm of his chair.\n\nTalen: \"Yes.\"\n\nLucien: \"Did Noctis alter the official record?\"\n\nTalen's jaw set hard.\n\nTalen: \"Not that I can prove.\"\n\nLucien turned slightly so the gallery could admire his poise.\n\nLucien: \"Then what you offer is aftermath, not illegality.\"\n\nTalen looked down at his braced hand. Static cracked along the edge of the Valemere projection, trembling around his shoulder. His mouth opened once and shut. Lucien let the silence stretch by one polished heartbeat, waiting for exhaustion to do his work for him.\n\nThen Talen lifted his hand straight into the relay light. It shook visibly.\n\nTalen: \"This is aftermath.\"\n\nNo one in the chamber breathed.\n\nTalen: \"Your question is whether a system can harm someone cleanly enough that the harm becomes private. My answer is no.\"\n\nLucien's face stayed smooth. The Foundation pool shifted anyway.\n\n**CLEAN RECORD DOES NOT ERASE DIRTY OUTCOME**\n\nTalen stared at the floating script as if he expected the words to vanish. His shoulders dropped by one small fraction. In the gallery, Elira looked down once before giving the room her composure back. Mira copied the ruling twice.\n\nLucien let the moment settle, then smiled.\n\nLucien: \"I will not dispute that Talen Mire was harmed, nor pretend Cael Nox faced no danger. There. We have witnessed pain, named it, and made everyone morally comfortable. Now let us ask whether comfort is law.\"\n\nRhea lifted her seal.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Witness protocol.\"\n\nRhea ruled Cael's testimony voluntary, protected, and non-transferable. Aira logged that injury did not reduce autonomy. Mira copied that authority had not arrived before harm.\n\nThe pool accepted.\n\n**TESTIMONY ADMITTED UNDER WITNESS AUTONOMY**\n\nAbove the upper rail, the frozen tournament bracket answered in sharp silver:\n\n**TALEN MIRE INJURY RECORD: RESTORED TO MATCH SEQUENCE**\n\n**NOCTIS SURRENDER-SAFETY RECORD: FORMALLY CONTESTED**\n\nFor the first time that morning, testimony changed a fact outside the chamber. Valemere's loss remained a loss, but it would no longer certify Lucien's conduct as clean. Across the twin Valemere relays, Soren inclined his head to Talen. Talen did not bow back; he held up his braced hand, and the hall understood.\n\nLucien looked almost pleased. He had forced the room to claim witness autonomy before anyone had finished building the floor under it.\n\nThen Nera stepped out of Noctis formation. Only two paces, yet far enough to break the line.\n\nNera: \"The doctrine made truth impossible inside our custody.\"\n\nEvery Noctis official snapped toward her. Beside her, Oric Sen shifted one step to match. Nera's binding rings trembled against her wrists.\n\nNera: \"I should have stopped being useful sooner.\"\n\nLucien went completely still.\n\nComposure on him was usually active: shoulders arranged, silence sharpened to cut. For less than a heartbeat, this was stillness that caught him off guard. It was not the doctrine or the custody breach that landed. It was the word *sooner*.\n\nHis eyes flicked down to Nera's right hand, where a faint crescent scar sat beneath the iron ring. I knew that scar from fragments in the Noctis match files: a training infirmary, a snapped restraint catch, a younger student cradling her wrist while a younger Lucien stood beside the cot with his face turned away from the healer.\n\nFor one fleeting second, his polished cruelty stalled on a memory older than his coat. Then he shut it down in pieces: first the eyes, drawing a cold shade over them; then the mouth, emptying of anything quotable; then the shoulders, settling back into clean Noctis lines.\n\nLucien: \"Usefulness is not a sin, Nera. It is merely embarrassing when one discovers too late who benefited.\"\n\nNera flinched, barely, yet enough.\n\nOric moved before the motion could look like open defiance. One step sideways, just enough for the edge of his sleeve to cross the line of sight between Lucien's smile and Nera's scarred wrist.\n\nNera did not look at him, but her fingers loosened around the binding ring. The room saw almost nothing. The Foundation read sequence.\n\n**SECONDARY WITNESS SUPPORT: VOLUNTARY**\n\nOric's face went pale. After years of surviving by keeping care deniable, the pool had just put it into public grammar. Lucien's gaze swung to him, and for the first time in the hearing, Oric did not look down. His chin lifted by a fraction, exhaustion straightening into resolve.\n\nOn the Valemere relay, Soren tapped one knuckle against the old pale scar on his own wrist. Talen saw. Elira saw.\n\nLucien smiled again, sealing the crack cleanly, and raised his hand toward the eastern projection.\n\nLucien: \"Given the instability of Aurelis witnesses and the contested status of Noctis personnel, my delegation petitions for an indefinite continuance and immediate custody transfer of Cael Nox pending formal audit.\"\n\nThe silver pool went black. Then it answered in heavy, unforgiving letters:\n\n**NO INDEFINITE CONTINUANCE GRANTED**\n\n**CUSTODY SUSPENSION DENIED**\n\n**LIVE AUTONOMY PROOF REQUIRED: FORTY-EIGHT HOURS**\n\nSurprise flickered across Lucien's face for half a heartbeat, followed immediately by hunger.\n\nRhea moved with ruthless speed, driving her red seal across the pool before Noctis could argue the terms loose.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Forty-eight-hour live window accepted under continuous witness conditions. No isolation. No private custody transfer. No removal of Cael Nox, Nera Vail, Oric Sen, or any registered witness from Aurelis grounds. The hearing chamber seals at noon and reconvenes at sunset on the second day.\"\n\nLucien's smile thinned.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"And no new custody claim may be filed until the autonomy proof is answered.\"\n\nBeside Rhea, Aira's pencil hit her ledger so hard the lead snapped.\n\nAira: \"I have a second pencil and no mercy.\"\n\nAt the Green table, Mira copied the ruling with both hands to keep the ink from betraying how badly her fingers were shaking.\n\nThe Foundation pool locked Rhea's phrasing into stone.\n\n**FORTY-EIGHT-HOUR PROOF WINDOW ACTIVE**\n\n**WITNESS REMOVAL PROHIBITED**\n\nCael looked at Nera. Nera looked at Oric. On the western glass, Talen's remote image flickered once, then held steady.\n\nBeneath my sleeve, the false Green crest chilled against my wrist while the Black Circle stirred deep behind my ribs. It did not rise. It listened.\n\nRhea lowered her seal without releasing it. Aira pulled a utility knife she had no business carrying in a council chamber and began sharpening her broken pencil.\n\nLeon stared at the blade.\n\nLeon: \"Why do you have that?\"\n\nAira: \"Because pencils betray.\"\n\nCassian: \"I respect the doctrine.\"\n\nElira did not smile. Her gaze moved between Cael, Nera, and Talen's flickering relay, measuring breath and tension without trying to command either. Cael looked small beneath the ruling overhead until Nera shifted half a step closer and Oric matched her. Through the relay light, Talen raised his braced hand again, simply remaining visible.\n\nThe chamber was not ready. It was arranged.\n\n---\n\nForty-eight hours. Rhea called it generous. Aira called it murder with a calendar.\n\nThe trial hall sealed at noon. Noctis filings hit the outer wards before the first bell finished ringing: classification requests, withdrawal petitions, custody objections, record correction demands. Every sheet arrived dressed as procedure with a blade tucked inside the crease.\n\nInside Aurelis, we built the autonomy proof in one room around one long table, with a ward map pinned across the center. Maelin enforced tea by threatening medical holds; Cassian requisitioned honey cakes and declared morale a tactical category.\n\nAira took command of the statements.\n\nAira: \"Short. Specific. No poetry unless Valen supervises. Four categories: personal motive, observed harm, independent expertise, and risk accepted without promised reward.\"\n\nProfessor Valen opened her mouth.\n\nAira: \"Professor, with affection, if you hand Noctis a metaphor, Lucien will climb it like a ladder.\"\n\nValen closed her mouth. Rhea looked faintly impressed.\n\nLeon wrote first and hated every draft until Aira underlined his bluntest sentence: if Haru became what Lucien claimed he was, Leon would stand against him. Cassian wrote that Noctis kept confusing patience with weakness and he disliked people who needed a committee to prove cruelty was ugly. Halden wrote three stark lines: he did not know what I was, the old archive warned against isolation, and my restraint showed capacity to learn.\n\nMira arrived from the gallery copy room with stained sleeves and a single line that made Elira look at her with open respect.\n\nMira: \"If mercy needs a rank first, people like us are always late to being saved.\"\n\nQuiet settled over the room after that, textured with ordinary work. Leon reread his statement three times and crossed out every line that sounded heroic. Cassian warmed damp edges of paper with low fire to dry the ink, pretending he had not helped. Rhea rejected seven Noctis filings in thirty-one words total. Nera sorted clauses by the pressure in the handwriting rather than the signatures, while Oric counted softly under his breath whenever Cael paused too long, pretending he was checking page order.\n\nAira placed the plate of honey cakes dead center and tagged it **NON-COERCIVE MOTIVATION**. Leon ate one with the expression of a man accepting legal risk for unit cohesion.\n\nTwo hours after midnight, the table stopped being paper and became bodies again. Cael's hand cramped so hard around his pen that he froze mid-sentence, unable to unclench his fingers while everyone pretended not to stare.\n\nMaelin was off-shift. Nera handled it badly first.\n\nNera: \"Stop writing.\"\n\nCael's shoulders locked.\n\nCael: \"I can continue.\"\n\nNera: \"I did not ask whether you could.\"\n\nThe phrasing sounded straight out of Noctis. She reached across the table to turn the page away, saw Cael flinch at the command, and stopped dead. Color drained from her cheeks as she pulled her hand back.\n\nNera: \"Correction. I am requesting a pause because your hand is locking and the next line will read worse if written through damage.\"\n\nAira uncapped her pen.\n\nAira: \"Logging excellent correction of terrible phrasing.\"\n\nNera shut her eyes.\n\nNera: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira: \"Do not sound grateful. It encourages me.\"\n\nCael let go of the pen. It clicked softly against the wood, and the whole room exhaled.\n\nAcross the table, Halden paused over his draft and looked up at Cael.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do you want this technical term struck from the record? It is accurate, but it reads like inventory.\"\n\nCael stared at him for a long beat.\n\nCael: \"You are asking me?\"\n\nHalden set his pen down cleanly.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Yes.\"\n\nCael: \"Noctis says accurate terms are not personal.\"\n\nHalden's expression sharpened.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Noctis is wrong about many things with impressive consistency.\"\n\nCael looked down at the sheet.\n\nCael: \"Leave *conduit damage*. Strike *asset instability*.\"\n\nHalden crossed the phrase out hard enough to rip the top layer of paper. Aira watched without cracking a joke.\n\nBeside me, Elira worked without touching my hand. Every witness statement had to survive Lucien's claim that proximity to me erased choice, so we practiced asking before every small gesture: shifting a chair, passing a page, adjusting a ward line near Cael's shoulder. Question, then answer. At first it felt stiff. By the second bell past midnight, it felt like architecture.\n\nThe Black Circle sat cold and restless behind my ribs. It could have rewritten every clause in the room in half a second, cleanly and without permission. Watching tired hands build protection slowly with ink and consent was a discipline my power hated, and one I needed to learn.\n\nLate in the first night, Noctis tried a softer door.\n\nA petition arrived addressed neither to Rhea nor faculty, but directly to Cael. It unfolded above the table in neat silver script:\n\n**VOLUNTARY WITHDRAWAL OPTION**\n\nNo accusation, no threat: just a quiet route back to Noctis custody framed as mercy. Reduced public exposure, familiar delegation healers, no further duty to testify.\n\nCael read every line while no one interrupted. Leon looked as if staying quiet might crack his ribs. Cassian pressed one palm flat to the table until the wood smoked faintly beneath his heat. Rhea's seal hovered, ready to strike, yet she held back; rejecting it before Cael answered would prove Lucien's case.\n\nCael finished reading and asked the question Noctis had trained him never to ask.\n\nCael: \"What happens if I say yes?\"\n\nNera closed her eyes. Oric answered because she could not.\n\nOric: \"Delegation healers receive you. Your public testimony gets flagged unstable from recovery stress. Noctis issues a formal gratitude statement to Aurelis, and any irregular mana traces are filed under unauthorized external interference.\"\n\nCael: \"And me?\"\n\nNera opened her eyes and met his directly.\n\nNera: \"You learn not to say no again.\"\n\nCael folded the silver sheet once, carefully, then a second time. He did not set it in Rhea's official tray. Instead, he looked toward the doorway, where Mira stood with ink on her cuffs and a stack of blank gallery pages under her arm.\n\nCael: \"Can you write something if no one has ruled on it yet?\"\n\nMira swallowed hard, braced the top blank against her forearm, and uncapped her pen.\n\nMira: \"Yes.\"\n\nCael: \"Write that I read it.\"\n\nMira wrote.\n\nCael: \"Write that I understood it.\"\n\nHer pen moved faster.\n\nCael: \"Write that I was afraid.\"\n\nLeon made a quiet sound like the words had caught him in the chest. Cael kept his eyes on Mira.\n\nCael: \"Write that I still refuse.\"\n\nMira's nib scraped across the rough paper. No seal flared. No Foundation script answered. First Mira's page took the line, then the Green student behind her copied it, then Aira added her column, then Nera and Oric signed beneath.\n\nBy the time Cael turned back to the table, the record already existed.\n\nCael: \"Enter refusal.\"\n\nRhea's red seal struck before Noctis could retract the offer, recognizing what witnesses had already built.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Council recognizes student refusal already witnessed.\"\n\nPages lifted from three hands in uneven order and entered the outer conduit to the pool. The Foundation answered in two words:\n\n**REFUSAL RECOGNIZED**\n\nCael sank into his chair because his legs were shaking. Ten seconds too late, Noctis withdrew the petition. Aira logged the timestamp with grim satisfaction.\n\n---\n\nAt the midpoint of the forty-eight hours, Noctis stopped filing paper and sent frost.\n\nIt crept through an old relay phrase buried deep inside Cael's healer seal. No flash, no warning chime: just white cold blooming beneath the skin along his collarbone while he was explaining final conduit clauses to Aira.\n\nCael's breath snagged. His chest seal bleached white.\n\nLeon moved first, catching Cael by the shoulders before his forehead hit the table. Cassian burned the nearest relay thread straight out of the air. Across the room, Nera went pale and snapped both binding rings open so violently the edges sliced her wrists.\n\nNera: \"Do not let him answer it!\"\n\nRhea slammed her seal onto the stone floor.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Identify phrase!\"\n\nOric's voice cut from the doorway.\n\nOric: \"Final utility confirmation.\"\n\nCael's lips parted, soundless. The frost was forcing him to finish the Noctis oath, to declare himself usable while everyone watched.\n\nBehind my ribs, the Black Circle surged.\n\nUnder my sleeve, the false Green crest went freezing cold and fractured at the edges as Tier 2 rose cleanly behind my knuckles. The solution was already fully formed: sever the relay grammar, burn the hidden phrase to ash, slam every Noctis path in the wing shut forever.\n\nElira's fingers closed lightly on my sleeve.\n\nShe did not need to speak. I already knew the cost. If I tore the spell out from above, the Foundation would record my power, not Cael's refusal, and Noctis would claim he had never said no.\n\nI forced my hand down. Holding Tier 2 back felt like locking my fingers around a live blade while the air around my wrist hummed with trapped cold. Every second I stayed still hurt Cael, and the whole room felt it.\n\nSo the others moved.\n\nLeon held Cael upright, jaw set. Cassian fenced the creeping relay back with controlled heat, refusing to burn the core phrase itself. Nera shouted the Noctis grammar she hated while Oric named the pressure anchor. Rhea locked the room wards. Aira scribbled the counter-line with a snapping nib.\n\nAira: \"No final conduit clauses. No sacrifice-based restoration. Nobody calls your death an emergency resource.\"\n\nFrost climbed Cael's throat. Leon shot me one look, furious and terrified and understanding far too much, then turned straight back to Cael.\n\nLeon: \"Breathe and refuse.\"\n\nCael choked on a sound too thin for speech. Nera leaned close, blood beading beneath her unlocked rings.\n\nNera: \"They taught the phrase. They did not make it yours.\"\n\nCael's eyes snapped open.\n\nCael: \"No.\"\n\nThe frost cracked like thin ice under a boot.\n\nNot because I erased it. Because the room worked harder than my power wanted to.\n\nThe Foundation accepted the break in rough pieces: Aira's counter-line, Mira's copy from the doorway, Nera's blood on the ring latch, Leon's grip, Cael's single word.\n\n**COERCIVE UTILITY PHRASE REJECTED**\n\nCael slumped back in the chair, shaking, alive, and badly bruised by the cold.\n\nFor several seconds after he caught his breath, nobody moved. Leon's hands trembled once he let go. Nera turned half away and pressed her bleeding wrists against her sleeves. Aira stared at her snapped pencil point as if it were the only small thing left in the building.\n\nCael saw all of them watching him, and his old training immediately reached for blame. Leon caught it before it left his mouth.\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nCael blinked.\n\nLeon: \"Whatever apology is forming, kill it.\"\n\nCael's mouth tightened.\n\nCael: \"You do not know what I was going to say.\"\n\nLeon: \"You have three expressions. Fear, apology, and thinking apology is strategy.\"\n\nNera let out a short sound that might have been a laugh if it had not hurt. Cael looked at her, startled out of his own spiral.\n\nShe raised her bandaged wrists.\n\nNera: \"Noctis binding rings can open quickly. They are built to cut hard enough that you remember not to do it.\"\n\nRhea's seal shifted.\n\nNera: \"Do not enter that as confession. Enter it as design.\"\n\nRhea: \"Design entered.\"\n\nAira dug a fresh pencil from her sleeve.\n\nAira: \"I hate this school.\"\n\nOric: \"Noctis?\"\n\nAira: \"I am reserving several categories of hate. Do not rush me.\"\n\nThe tension broke just enough for everyone to draw a clean breath.\n\n---\n\nAfter the frost attack, the preparation sharpened.\n\nNera and Oric laid open the internal machinery Noctis had trained them never to name: relay hooks, obedience phrases, restoration clauses, the administrative grammar used to turn a student into a recoverable asset.\n\nNear midnight, Darian Thorne broke in through a remote witness line. His projection tore with static and raw dread.\n\nDarian: \"I am not authorized to speak, but I am required to witness.\"\n\nLucien's voice sliced across the transmission from just off-frame.\n\nLucien: \"Thorne. You are out of procedure.\"\n\nDarian looked terrified. He spoke anyway.\n\nDarian: \"I am in witness. Which is older than procedure.\"\n\nNo seal flared in reward. No dramatic light answered him. Rhea watched the relay edges for one silent beat to see if he would retreat into obedience. When he held his ground, her seal locked the sentence into record before Noctis could cut the feed.\n\nDarian's breach turned our defense into a map.\n\nNera sketched the first diagram left-handed because her right wrist kept bleeding through the wrap. It traced doctrine, not spells: Lucien at the top, supported by senior council witnesses forbidden to speak, technical aides taught to call coercion preparation, and recovery forms asking whether a student had returned to usefulness before asking whether he had slept.\n\nOric added relay anchors. Rhea marked jurisdiction. Halden added archive parallels from the old safeguard failures.\n\nAira stared at the finished sheet without a trace of humor.\n\nAira: \"This is not one bad prince in a dramatic coat.\"\n\nCassian: \"Shame. That would be easier to set on fire.\"\n\nAira: \"It is a machine that turns people into parts and calls the noise discipline.\"\n\nCael stood at the edge of the map, face still. Elira stepped near him and stopped, waiting. Only when he nodded did she step beside his shoulder.\n\nAfter a long minute, Cael reached out and shifted one wooden token, his own, half an inch off the line marked **RETURN CLAIM**, placing it on the empty square beyond.\n\nCael: \"If the field recognizes return as rescue, what does it require from the person being returned?\"\n\nNera's head snapped up. Oric stopped counting. Professor Valen answered carefully.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"A lawful field requires present consent and distributed stabilizing load.\"\n\nCael left his finger on the empty square.\n\nCael: \"And an unlawful one?\"\n\nNera answered into the quiet.\n\nNera: \"It looks for a single payable life and calls that choice.\"\n\nCael pulled his hand back.\n\nHaru: \"Cael?\"\n\nHe kept his eyes on the map.\n\nCael: \"I am learning where not to stand.\"\n\nNobody moved the token back.\n\nBy the final hours, the map carried fingerprints from half the room: Aira's ink smudges, a streak of healing salve from Cael's wrist, Nera's nail mark beside **RETURN CLAIM**, and a faint scorch corner Cassian blamed on a candle that did not exist.\n\nProfessor Valen studied the stained sheet.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Do not make a clean copy.\"\n\nHalden's pen paused.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"For filing?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"For memory.\"\n\nRhea weighed both needs.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Official filing requires legibility.\"\n\nAira: \"We file a clean copy and keep the ugly one.\"\n\nFrom the gallery relay line, Mira added, \"The ugly one proves the work happened.\"\n\nThe second breakthrough was uglier, and sharper. Halden caught a contradiction buried inside Noctis's withdrawal clauses.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"They use the word *protection* in two incompatible senses.\"\n\nAira: \"Illegal?\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Worse. Sloppy. Legality can be argued; sloppiness exposes motive.\"\n\nHe turned the sheet toward us. One paragraph claimed Cael required Noctis custody because conduit training left him vulnerable to outside influence. The next claimed the same training made him uniquely reliable as an internal witness. Vulnerable in Aurelis hands; reliable in Noctis hands.\n\nLeon stared at the page.\n\nLeon: \"They did not expect anyone to read both.\"\n\nMira's voice crackled over the copy line.\n\nMira: \"Green students read paired notices. Contradictions are usually hidden where we sit.\"\n\nAira wrote that down verbatim.\n\nThen Lucien's voice cut through the outer ward relay, thin and amused.\n\nLucien: \"How careful.\"\n\nNobody answered.\n\nLucien: \"Aurelis finds a dangerous student and distributes him across witnesses. Leon as conscience. Cassian as force. Rhea as law. Halden as restraint. Mira as public sympathy. Elira as emotional anchor. Cael as injured proof.\"\n\nMy blood went cold, because he was wrong and yet close enough to threaten the entire structure.\n\nLucien: \"You are learning to hide him inside everyone.\"\n\nThe silence held. He had stopped poking at individual pieces and named the network itself. Rhea did not jump to cut him down, which told us the strike had landed.\n\nCael spoke first, voice rough from the frost.\n\nCael: \"At Noctis, hiding someone inside everyone is called contamination.\"\n\nThe relay did not flicker.\n\nCael: \"Here, I think it is called not leaving.\"\n\nMira's pen scratched across paper, breaking the paralysis. Lucien laughed softly.\n\nLucien: \"How touching. A rescued conduit discovers poetry.\"\n\nNera stepped squarely between the relay and Cael's chair, her bandaged wrists bare beneath loose, unlocked rings.\n\nNera: \"Do not call him conduit.\"\n\nLucien: \"Noctis terminology offends Aurelis now?\"\n\nNera: \"It offends accuracy.\"\n\nLucien's tone dropped ten degrees.\n\nLucien: \"You are enjoying your borrowed courage.\"\n\nNera went white. Oric stepped beside her. Not in front, beside.\n\nNera: \"No. I am recognizing how much fear Noctis taught me to mistake for virtue.\"\n\nAira stopped writing to let the sentence breathe.\n\nRhea lifted her red seal at last.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Public correction. Witness proximity is not concealment unless the protected student is denied independent answer. Tomorrow's autonomy proof tests that directly.\"\n\nLucien: \"And if they fail?\"\n\nRhea: \"Then the Foundation records failure.\"\n\nLucien: \"And Haru Aerlen?\"\n\nRhea looked directly at me in front of the entire room, refusing to soften the blade.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then he faces full classification pressure without the shield of presumed witness autonomy.\"\n\nThe words struck straight at my ribs. Beside me, Elira's fingers twitched, yet she did not reach out. I loved her fiercely for holding that line.\n\nI met Rhea's gaze and kept my wrist steady beneath my cuff.\n\nHaru: \"Then I will be in the room to answer it.\"\n\nLucien caught the silence that followed and knew exactly what it cost.\n\nLucien: \"Good.\"\n\nHis relay began to thin.\n\nLucien: \"Tomorrow we learn whether mercy is a network or a mask.\"\n\nThe projection winked out.\n\nFor a long minute, nobody spoke. Then Aira wrote one line across the top of the final preparation sheet and underlined it once:\n\n**Do not perform loyalty. Prove choice.**\n\nBeneath Elira's collar, the black pendant pulsed cold once. Warning.\n\n---\n\n## Coda: Talen's Hands\n\nThe silver relay faded from the Valemere healer room, leaving ordinary rain tapping against the window glass.\n\nOnly then did Talen let his braced left hand drop to his knee.\n\nIt shook harder now than it had in front of four academies. Holding it up into the relay light while Lucien waited for him to fold had burned through every ounce of steadiness he had left. The Foundation's words, *CLEAN RECORD DOES NOT ERASE DIRTY OUTCOME*, were already fading from the room's mirror, yet the tremor stayed right where it had lived since the junior circuit.\n\nBeside his chair, his mother finally unclenched her bloodless fingers. She did not tell him to hide the hand or pretend the cold weather was to blame. She wrapped a warm linen cloth gently around his braced wrist and let it tremble against her palm.\n\nAt the doorway, Soren Ashvale stood with the thin stack of Valemere timing sheets still pressed against his side.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere let that match sit clean on the books for two years. That was not only Noctis.\"\n\nTalen looked up at him, surprised to hear the heir of Valemere admit it without a single qualifying clause.\n\nSoren: \"Tomorrow's autonomy proof opens at sunset. If you want the relay shut, I will shut it myself.\"\n\nTalen pressed his braced hand flat against his knee, no longer trying to force the tremor down, letting it belong to his body instead of Lucien's scorecard.\n\nTalen: \"Leave it open.\"\n\nSoren inclined his head once.\n\nSoren: \"Then I will stand behind your chair while it runs.\"","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-07-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep8","label":"Episode 8","number":"08","file":"episode-08-cover.webp","fallback":"The Lightless Name","eyebrow":"Noctis Arrives","recap":"","accent":["#111111","#f6c85f","#b8f2e6"],"displayLabel":"Episode 8","displayNumber":"08","displayEyebrow":"Tournament Faultlines","arcLabel":"Tournament Faultlines","title":"The Autonomy Proof","body":"At sunset, the Foundation Trial Hall filled again.\n\nThis time, Aurelis brought statements. Not speeches: proofs. Speeches asked for applause; proofs only needed to survive being hated.\n\nRhea placed each statement in its own witness ring instead of stacking them on the Aurelis platform. The sheets floated at uneven heights beneath thin red seals so nobody could mistake them for a single argument written by one hand.\n\nLeon's hung low and blunt near Cael's protection mark. Cassian's smoked faintly at the corners where he had insisted on signing with a fire-warmed stylus. Rhea's stood upright and severe. Halden's three lines floated apart from the faculty rail. Mira's gallery proof appeared as uneven copies marked in different hands, errors left intact. Beside the Noctis timing ledger hung Darian Thorne's preserved midnight line in crisp silver script: *I am in witness. Which is older than procedure.*\n\nNera and Oric stood together between the Aurelis floor and the Noctis rail, claiming neither side cleanly. Cael stood near the center under two healer seals and one council mark. Elira stood beside me. Never behind.\n\nThe silver pool brightened as the pages settled.\n\n**AUTONOMY MATERIAL RECEIVED**\n\nLucien surveyed the spread of pages and smiled with his mouth only.\n\nLucien: \"How industrious.\"\n\nAira stepped onto the support mark with one fresh pencil behind her ear, two more tucked in her cuff, and the look of someone prepared to become a procedural hazard until dawn.\n\nAira: \"We call it preparation when nobody gets hurt.\"\n\nLucien's eyes drifted to Cael's healer seal.\n\nLucien: \"No one?\"\n\nAira: \"Less than if you planned it.\"\n\nHis gaze lingered on the glowing seal longer than the injury beneath it, counting care as an uncollected debt. Up in the gallery, pens moved fast. Lucien noticed and stepped onto the center stone before Rhea could formally open witness delivery.\n\nLucien: \"Before Aurelis performs sincerity, I request the court identify the premise beneath these statements.\"\n\nThe pool darkened around his reflection. Rhea raised her red seal.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"State the premise.\"\n\nLucien turned slowly, giving the gallery his clean profile.\n\nLucien: \"Mercy without authority is governance.\"\n\nNobody spoke. He let the silence work before continuing.\n\nLucien: \"Haru Aerlen interfered in sanctioned combat. He disrupted an outcome recognized by field rules, rank structure, delegation authority, and tournament procedure. Aurelis now asks this hall to believe that every witness gathered here independently chose to protect the same student, defend the same intervention, preserve the same secrecy, and resist the same classification inquiry.\"\n\nHis gaze snapped to me.\n\nLucien: \"Convenient.\"\n\nThe word slid through the chamber like frost.\n\nLucien: \"Either Haru Aerlen is a Green Circle student whose private mercy overruled every recognized authority present, or he carries an authority this court has not named. If the first is true, Aurelis defends chaos. If the second is true, these statements are not autonomy at all. They are orbit.\"\n\nBeside me, Elira stayed still.\n\nLucien: \"So let us stop asking whether these witnesses care. Caring is easy to manufacture after spectacle. Let us ask whether they can oppose Haru Aerlen. Let us ask whether their judgment exists when it does not flatter him.\"\n\nThe Foundation pool wrote one line.\n\n**COUNTER-PREMISE ACCEPTED FOR ANSWER**\n\nThe trap bit deep because it touched something real. I had hidden behind their trust, even when fear had forced the choice. The answer could never be that I was harmless. I was not.\n\nBeneath my sleeve, the false Green crest pinched cold against my wrist, flickering as the Black Circle stirred behind my ribs. Air pressure tightened around my knuckles like coiled wire waiting for a single excuse to snap.\n\nIf I stayed quiet and let everyone else step between me and Lucien's thesis, I would prove his argument by standing still.\n\nI forced my fingers open, pinned the cold back down beneath my cuff, and stepped forward to the edge of the center ring.\n\nEvery eye in the chamber shifted. Elira's breath caught once, then steadied.\n\nHaru: \"You are right about one thing.\"\n\nLucien's eyebrow lifted a fraction.\n\nHaru: \"None of the people in this room owe me obedience. None of them signed on to shield what I am.\"\n\nI looked straight across the silver pool at him, then up toward the Foundation faculty rail.\n\nHaru: \"I do not claim authority over Cael Nox, Elira Vale, or anyone else holding a page in this chamber. If Noctis wants a classification inquiry on me, put the inquiry on me. I will stand for the Foundation's audit myself on one condition.\"\n\nLucien smiled, smelling blood.\n\nLucien: \"How generous, to negotiate terms from the dock.\"\n\nHaru: \"One condition. Every witness here stays free to speak, walk out, or testify against me without Noctis custody or retaliation hanging over their neck. If you want to prove they are my orbit, stop threatening the people who would leave it. Put the inquiry on me. Leave their choices off your ledger.\"\n\nSilence hit the hall harder than a spell. Under my sleeve, the false Green glow pulsed once against my wrist and held steady.\n\nLucien recovered fast.\n\nLucien: \"How tidy to volunteer once your shields are arranged.\"\n\nRhea's red seal struck the rail before he could spin the room back.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Recorded. Subject waives personal delay under active witness freedom.\"\n\nThe pool flashed once beneath my boots.\n\n**SUBJECT WAIVER LOGGED: CONDITIONAL ON UNCOERCED WITNESSES**\n\nRhea swept her gaze across our witnesses, cool and merciless.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Now answer for yourselves. And answer badly. No scripts, no neat sequence. Lucien will call polish rehearsal and hesitation corruption. Let the truth stumble where it stumbles.\"\n\nLeon moved first, shoulders stiff, hands visibly empty.\n\nLeon: \"I do not follow Haru.\"\n\nLucien: \"A stirring start from the boy who now stands behind him in every room.\"\n\nHeat climbed Leon's neck.\n\nLeon: \"I was not finished. On Haru's first day, I tried to humiliate him because his Circle was Green and mine was Blue. He did not charm that out of me. I was wrong in public and stayed wrong longer than I should have.\"\n\nHe looked at Cael, then back at Lucien.\n\nLeon: \"If Haru tells me to lie, I refuse. If he tells me to hand Cael back because protecting him turns inconvenient, I refuse. If he becomes what Voss claims he is, I stand against him.\"\n\nLucien: \"You enjoy becoming noble after being corrected.\"\n\nLeon's fists curled, caught the blue flash of Cael's healer seal at the edge of his sight, and opened again.\n\nLeon: \"No. I enjoy being less wrong than yesterday.\"\n\n**PERSONAL MOTIVE DISTINCT FROM SUBJECT: ACCEPTED**\n\nCassian crossed into the ring before Rhea called him. His red heat clipped Leon's blue trace, and the witness mark gave an irritated hum.\n\nCassian: \"Good. It hates sharing.\"\n\nAira: \"Please stop antagonizing the ancient legal mechanism.\"\n\nCassian's grin dropped as his eyes found Cael.\n\nCassian: \"I follow plans while they are good. Haru's bad plans are annoying because he goes quiet instead of shouting, which is why I keep furniture within reach.\"\n\nHaru: \"You are not throwing furniture at me.\"\n\nCassian: \"See? Independent judgment.\"\n\nA few startled laughs escaped the gallery.\n\nCassian: \"In the Crown Field, I moved because Cael was breaking and the field was lying about it. If Haru had told me to leave the boy there, we would have had a short tactical disagreement and a long medical one.\"\n\nLucien: \"Bravado is not autonomy.\"\n\nCassian: \"No. It just makes coercion work harder.\"\n\n**INDEPENDENT COMBAT JUDGMENT: ACCEPTED**\n\nLucien glanced at both marks with cool dismissal.\n\nLucien: \"Two boys defending the friend around whom their new importance gathers.\"\n\nRhea did not step into the ring. She pressed her office seal straight onto the council rail.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"My protection order covers Cael Nox, Nera Vail, Oric Sen, Mira Castellan's public copies, and every student your delegation prefers to question alone. If Haru Aerlen asks me for an exception, I refuse and log the request.\"\n\nHer seal flared deep red.\n\n**OFFICE AUTHORITY DISTINCT FROM PERSONAL LOYALTY: ACCEPTED**\n\nFrom the faculty tier, Professor Halden leaned forward before Lucien could speak.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I want to know what Haru Aerlen is badly enough that I would not trust myself as the sole authority in an isolated classification room. I distrust any room where the most curious person is also the final restraint.\"\n\nAira's pencil scratched furiously.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not make that sound noble, Solen.\"\n\nAira: \"I am logging *self-aware academic hazard*.\"\n\nHalden looked deeply offended. The pool accepted him anyway.\n\n**FACULTY RESTRAINT: AUTONOMOUS EVIDENCE**\n\nMira came down the steps from the gallery clutching three battered copies against her chest, one stained with a tea ring, one written in two inks, the third split down the middle and mended with healer tape. Two paces behind her, Finn Castellan shadowed her to the edge of the lower rail with a fresh stack of blanks tucked under his arm, watching the floor in case she stumbled.\n\nMira stopped just outside the ring.\n\nMira: \"Do I have to stand in it?\"\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nMira stepped inside anyway, frightened enough that the taped page rattled between her fingers.\n\nMira: \"On Haru's first day, the instructor knew every Red and Blue name. Three Green students were called *support row*. I wrote their names because I was angry. Haru did not ask. I still do not know most of what he is.\"\n\nLucien: \"Yet being noticed by him pleased you.\"\n\nMira swallowed hard, her fingers tightening around the taped edge of the torn sheet.\n\nMira: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe admission stung, and she kept it anyway.\n\nMira: \"Being noticed matters when people practice not seeing you. That does not make what I saw false.\"\n\nShe laid the three copies along the silver rim.\n\nMira: \"If Haru hurts someone, write it. If he lies, write it. If he saves someone and powerful people call it disorder because the wrong rank moved first, write that too. Pages disappear before powerful people admit a person did.\"\n\nAlong the bottom margin, half a dozen uneven hands had signed the same line:\n\n**WE SAW ENOUGH TO WRITE**\n\nThe pool hesitated longer over her pages than over Leon's. Mira stared at the silver water looking half sick until the verdict surfaced:\n\n**PUBLIC RECORD MOTIVE: DISTINCT**\n\nShe exhaled and nearly dropped the stack as she backed toward the rail. Finn reached out smoothly and caught the bottom edge without taking the pages from her, guiding her back toward the Green Circle section.\n\nNera walked forward before her name was called.\n\nNera: \"My statement is technical. Noctis field telemetry confirms the relay drop began before Cael Nox collapsed, under lightless pressure exceeding standard surrender containment.\"\n\nLucien cut straight across her before she could reach her second line.\n\nLucien: \"You memorized Aurelis's phrasing well.\"\n\nHer voice vanished. Not from magic; the old training simply locked her throat shut in front of him.\n\nOric stepped directly beside her. He did not touch her sleeve. He set the original Noctis timing sheet flat on the rail between them, right beneath Darian Thorne's floating silver line.\n\nOric: \"Test it.\"\n\nLucien's eyes shifted to him.\n\nOric: \"Noctis holds the exact same sheet, the same channel map, the same missing seven seconds. And Darian Thorne's witness entry is already in the pool. If Nera altered a single mark, produce the clean copy.\"\n\nBehind Lucien, the Noctis delegation stayed motionless. Oric waited, refusing the exit he had spent years measuring.\n\nOric: \"I am speaking without authorization too.\"\n\nThe pool darkened around the Noctis crest.\n\n**CORRECTION NOT PRODUCED**\n\nNera's voice came back rough and stripped of polish.\n\nNera: \"Noctis calls useful fear discipline. That means Noctis custody cannot produce clean testimony on whether fear was used.\"\n\nHer hands trembled against her sleeves as the pool logged her words.\n\n**TECHNICAL DISSENT: CREDIBLE / RETALIATION RISK ACTIVE**\n\nCael stepped forward before anyone could let Nera's fear end the moment. His healer seal flashed warning blue; Leon moved half a step and caught himself so abruptly his boot scraped stone.\n\nCael looked straight at Lucien, pale and unsteady, yet upright.\n\nCael: \"You keep asking whether Haru made them stay. Nobody at Noctis ever asked what made me say yes. They hurt me until yes was faster.\"\n\nNera closed her eyes.\n\nCael: \"Here, Leon asked before touching me. Rhea asked where I wanted the guard. Maelin asks questions I hate and waits for answers. Haru never asked me to make him look safe.\"\n\nHis breath hitched.\n\nCael: \"I need them.\"\n\nA sharp flicker of triumph crossed Lucien's frost outline. Cael saw it and did not blink.\n\nCael: \"Need does not make them owners. It means I am hurt.\"\n\nThe hall went dead quiet.\n\nCael: \"If I leave, I want it to be my door. If I stay, I want it to be my chair. I do not want Noctis owning the question just because I needed help answering it.\"\n\nSilver light rose around him in a thin vertical sheet.\n\n**WITNESS AUTONOMY: PARTIAL ESTABLISHMENT**\n\nLucien: \"Partial.\"\n\nElira stood.\n\nRhea had not called her. The chamber opened automatically for Yellow Circle rank, and Elira ignored the center mark completely to stand directly beside Cael.\n\nElira: \"I love Haru.\"\n\nEvery pen in the gallery paused except Aira's.\n\nElira: \"Yesterday I told him to stop deciding for me. He stopped. This morning I asked him to stay. He stayed.\"\n\nShe turned her clear gaze onto Lucien.\n\nElira: \"If your doctrine cannot tell those two choices apart, it is not measuring autonomy. It is punishing intimacy.\"\n\nThen she looked across the floor at me.\n\nElira: \"If Haru decides for me again, I will oppose him again. In private if he listens. In public if he does not.\"\n\nHaru: \"You should.\"\n\nIt was not the romance the gallery wanted. It was infinitely stronger.\n\nAira walked her messy stack down to the support ring. Leon had crossed out *rival* once and left it in twice; Cassian had sketched a chair in his margin; Mira's copies disagreed on two words; Nera had corrected Oric's timestamp; Cael's draft broke mid-sentence where his breathing had failed and resumed on a fresh line.\n\nAira: \"Witness synthesis: motives diverge, accounts show visible disagreement, four witnesses state conditions under which they would oppose Haru Aerlen, and two already have. No single command point exists in this record.\"\n\nLucien: \"You have made dependence look crowded.\"\n\nRhea: \"No. We have made ownership fail to become singular.\"\n\nThe Foundation pool did not flare. It grew heavy, pulling every witness ring down toward its surface.\n\n**AUTONOMY NETWORK: PROVISIONALLY ESTABLISHED**\n\n**IMMEDIATE NOCTIS RECLAMATION CLAIM: DENIED**\n\n**OPENING-BRACKET WITNESS RECORD: PRESERVED**\n\n**TARGETED PRESSURE REVIEW: STILL REQUIRED**\n\nCael could not be dragged back to Noctis tonight, and our bracket result stood intact. What remained untested was whether the network survived a deliberate strike.\n\nLucien stepped forward.\n\nLucien: \"There.\"\n\nThat single word dropped the temperature across the stone. Rhea's eyes narrowed.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"On what grounds?\"\n\nLucien did not look at Elira's face. He looked straight at the hollow beneath her collar where the black pendant rested.\n\nLucien: \"Compatibility pressure.\"\n\nThe tournament projection flashed back behind my eyes: **COMPATIBILITY PRESSURE POINT: ELIRA VALE**.\n\nLucien's smile was razor-calm.\n\nLucien: \"If this network is autonomous, remove the emotional anchor from the center and observe whether Haru Aerlen's restraint survives.\"\n\nElira did not flinch. Inside, my chest went ice-cold.\n\nThe pool answered in older, heavier script:\n\n**REQUEST LOGGED**\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS PRESSURE REVIEW: PENDING**\n\nBeneath Elira's collar, the pendant went violently cold.\n\nWhite frost bloomed along the edge of her dark fabric, sharp enough that she winced in full view of the gallery.\n\nA private gift became public evidence in half a second. The whole chamber saw that something bound Elira's skin to my power, something that could be named, pressured, and pulled.\n\nThe silver pool held the magnified reflection of her frost-marked collar overhead for three full breaths after Rhea slammed her red seal onto the floor.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Medical privacy challenge! Remove exposure from public display!\"\n\nThe projection dimmed, three breaths too late. Faculty had already seen. Students were already whispering. Noctis now knew the pendant was neither ornament nor ordinary charm.\n\nLucien bowed, genuinely satisfied. He had not broken us; he had found the exact seam he planned to tear next.\n\nLucien: \"Protect everything, President.\"\n\nHis eyes lingered once more on the white rime at Elira's throat.\n\nLucien: \"It makes the eventual exception easier to spot.\"\n\nThe pendant pulsed cold between us. Neither of us stepped back.\n\nHalden came down from the faculty rail before anyone else moved, his expression shut like an iron gate.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Autonomy established. Privacy damaged.\"\n\nRhea closed her eyes once, then opened them.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Recorded.\"\n\nAira stepped sharply to Elira's other side, shielding her from the nearest gallery tier with her own shoulder while spreading ledger sheets across her forearm.\n\nAira: \"Public record correction. The visible frost occurred after Lucien Voss requested emotional-anchor testing, not before. Log the exact sequence before rumor rewrites it.\"\n\nElira's voice came out tight and low.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira did not soften her expression.\n\nAira: \"Hold still until Maelin clears your collar.\"\n\nHalden stopped directly in front of me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not answer his next move alone.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was not planning to.\"\n\nHe looked down at my left hand. Only then did I realize my fingers had clenched so hard that the false Green Circle under my cuff was distorting at the edges, bleeding pale static across my wrist. Halden saw it and chose not to name it.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Plans are often weaker than reflex.\"\n\nElira's fingers brushed my sleeve.\n\nElira: \"He is right.\"\n\nI pried my hand open. The Green crest steadied by a hair.\n\nAcross the floor, Cael stared at Elira's collar with old guilt already tightening his shoulders. Leon caught his arm's shadow before a word could leave Cael's mouth.\n\nLeon: \"Do not even start.\"\n\nCael looked up, jaw clenched.\n\nCael: \"He aimed at her because of my hearing.\"\n\nLeon: \"He aimed at her because he lost the custody vote. Pick something more useful than guilt.\"\n\nCael breathed out slowly, eyes going hard as he looked toward the empty Noctis side.\n\nCael: \"Anger.\"\n\nLeon gave one sharp nod.\n\nLeon: \"Keep that.\"\n\nThe Foundation rings dimmed and released us. Nobody rushed the doors.\n\nAt the lower rail, Mira stayed pressed over her Green Circle copies with Finn beside her. On the western glass, Talen's relay stayed open past Valemere's cutoff, his mother sitting close while Soren Ashvale stood behind Talen's chair with one hand resting steadily on the wooden back. Nera and Oric stayed beside Cael's ring until the silver trace faded beneath their boots. Lucien had already left the chamber.\n\nRhea looked at the empty Noctis platform, then across the witnesses who remained.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"No one leaves alone.\"\n\nNobody argued.\n\nElira walked beside me out of the hall without taking my hand until I saw the faint tremor in her fingers. I opened my palm beside hers without grabbing. She slid her hand into mine.\n\nBeneath her collar, the pendant pulsed cold again, carrying no message I could read: only a pull forward, toward something none of us had named yet.\n\n---\n\nThe correction table went up in the eastern stairwell corridor instead of a closed council room, narrow enough that only three people could crowd it at once.\n\nElira sat on the bottom step with Maelin's warming seal freshly set along her collar, her thumb checking the cuff of her sleeve whenever she thought nobody was looking. Nearby, Mira and two Green Circle students glued copies straight onto the temporary notice rail because glued paper left proof behind if anyone tore it down.\n\nAira laid three clean sheets across the narrow wood:\n\n**WHAT WAS PROVEN**\n\n**WHAT WAS DAMAGED**\n\n**WHAT NO ONE GETS TO SHORTEN**\n\nShe read the first two aloud so corridor rumor could not outrun them: Elira's autonomy accepted, Cael's non-possession confirmed, Lucien's orbit claim rejected; privacy around Elira's pendant breached, my reflexive concealment strained under public proximity.\n\nWhen Aira's gaze flicked toward me on the last line, Elira stood before I could speak.\n\nElira: \"Leave that line intact. And under the third heading, write this: the pendant is not proof that I am controlled. It is proof that someone expected the world to turn dangerous and tried, imperfectly, to answer without owning me.\"\n\nShe stopped, took Aira's cheap green-lacquered pen, crossed out **without owning me**, and wrote sharply above it:\n\n**without making me an object.**\n\nThen she looked down the corridor at Cael, who had been leaning silently against the stone beside Leon.\n\nElira: \"And if anyone uses tonight's frost to claim rescue makes people dangerous to love, they can quote me accurately while being wrong.\"\n\nCael flinched once, pushed off the wall, and walked to the table. Leon let him go alone. Cael read Elira's line, took the chipped green pen from Aira, and wrote slowly with ink staining his fingers:\n\n**A route opened through me. That does not make everyone who reached for me responsible for the trap.**\n\nHe paused, then added underneath:\n\n**I am writing this before guilt edits it.**\n\nFrom the archway, Nera let out a sharp breath perilously close to a sob. Mira copied the words three times before Rhea stamped them as protected witness clarification.\n\nCael held the pen back out to Aira as if returning evidence before anyone could call borrowing a debt. Aira took it, then immediately pressed it back into his palm.\n\nAira: \"Keep it until tomorrow. If you think of a correction at three in the morning, I refuse to let lack of supplies become institutional oppression.\"\n\nCael stared down at the chipped green lacquer, cheap enough that Noctis would have binned it after one use, and closed his fingers around it.\n\nWhen we finally turned toward the dormitories, Elira held my hand openly in plain view of the stairwell. The frost along her collar stayed cold beneath Maelin's seal, yet the corridor behind us stayed covered in handwriting that refused to let the cold write the ending.\n\n---\n\n## Haru - Breakfast Without Evidence\n\nThe next morning, Cael kept Aira's pen through breakfast.\n\nHe set it beside his plate at the end of the table, parallel to his spoon, cap aligned with the wood seam. Every few minutes his fingers drifted near it and paused, checking that it was still there. Across from him, Leon stared at two slices of toast he had no intention of eating. Aira sat between them pretending to study a Council bulletin she was holding upside down.\n\nBeside me, Elira warmed her palms around a mug of tea. Morning light through the tall dining windows caught the faint shadow of exhaustion under her left eye and the pale frost-burn along her collar. For one irrational second, I hated the sunlight for showing it so clearly.\n\nCael reached for the pen, hesitated, then picked it up. The cap came off with a tiny click.\n\nFour students at the next bench went dead quiet. Cael's shoulders stiffened.\n\nAira lowered her upside-down notice.\n\nAira: \"Anyone who treats stationery as a sacred relic before breakfast gets inventory duty with Professor Halden.\"\n\nA second-year choked on juice. Conversation stumbled back into motion.\n\nCael wrote on the edge of his cloth napkin because a formal sheet felt too loud. The first word took longest.\n\n**hungry**\n\nLowercase. No seal, no explanation: just a plain fact his body offered before Noctis could reclassify appetite as weakness.\n\nLeon saw the word and slid his untouched toast across the wood. Cael did not move. Leon froze, pulled his hand back, and swallowed.\n\nLeon: \"Sorry.\"\n\nCael looked from the toast to the pen.\n\nCael: \"Ask.\"\n\nLeon: \"Do you want this?\"\n\nCael waited a beat before answering.\n\nCael: \"Half.\"\n\nLeon tore the slice in two with exaggerated care and set one half beside the napkin without smudging the ink. Cael picked it up while keeping Aira's pen in his other hand. No silver pool lit under the floor; no council seal logged it. A boy just ate half a piece of toast he had asked for.\n\nLucien arrived while Cael was still holding the crust.\n\nHe did not enter like an invader. He walked in carrying a cup of black tea and the mild, reasonable smile of a senior student who belonged everywhere breakfast was served. Two Noctis aides trailed behind him, carefully avoiding eye contact with Cael.\n\nRhea's protection mark flared red at the east doors. Lucien stopped neatly outside it.\n\nLucien: \"President Valmont, I am not here to file anything.\"\n\nRhea: \"Then improve the morning by leaving.\"\n\nLucien: \"That would make my absence far too theatrical.\"\n\nHe turned to Leon first, ignoring Elira and me completely.\n\nLucien: \"Varek. You are beginning to understand performance.\"\n\nLeon: \"I was eating.\"\n\nLucien glanced at the nearly untouched plate.\n\nLucien: \"Were you? You have discovered that standing beside damaged things can pass for character if one selects the right audience.\"\n\nThe half-eaten toast between Leon and Cael suddenly looked exposed. Cael's fingers tightened on the crust. Leon went white with fury, and I saw the hook a heartbeat too late: Lucien did not need Leon to believe him, only to flinch hard enough that Cael mistook anger for regret.\n\nElira set her teacup down cleanly on the saucer, giving the tension somewhere else to go.\n\nLeon inhaled through his nose once, twice, picked up his own half of the toast, and took a sharp bite.\n\nLeon: \"I am still hungry.\"\n\nCael's grip eased by one finger. Lucien's eyes narrowed slightly.\n\nAira leaned back in her chair.\n\nAira: \"Remarkable. Breakfast defeats rhetoric.\"\n\nLucien: \"You mistake delay for defeat.\"\n\nAira: \"Often. It keeps me cheerful.\"\n\nLucien's gaze settled on Cael at last. Cael did not hide the napkin. The lowercase word sat right beside the chipped green pen.\n\nLucien's polite tone sharpened into precision.\n\nLucien: \"Hungry is a useful word. It persuades people to feed the thing that may later bite them.\"\n\nCael stopped chewing. Air chilled around the bench. Beneath my sleeve, the Black Circle rolled cold against my wrist. Elira's hand found mine under the table and pressed once: *not yet*.\n\nLeon set his toast down, voice low and dangerous.\n\nLeon: \"Cael is not a thing.\"\n\nLucien: \"You improve remarkably quickly when watched.\"\n\nLeon started to rise. Cael moved faster.\n\nHe uncapped Aira's pen and drew one clean line through **hungry**. Underneath it, in smaller, steadier script, he wrote:\n\n**still hungry**\n\nThen he finished the rest of his toast.\n\nNobody applauded. Three tables away, Mira copied the napkin hastily onto scrap paper so badly nobody could ever call it official. Lucien turned and walked out with his tea untouched.\n\nHis contempt for ordinary objects had never been ignorance. He knew small things became anchors once people survived long enough to touch them freely, so he tried to break the hand before the hand could claim them.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Nera - Three Paces\n\nNera found Cael behind the old greenhouse before midday. Not hiding: sitting on a low stone herb border beneath winter glass, balancing Aira's chipped pen across both palms like a bridge too narrow for weight.\n\nShe stopped three paces away. At Noctis, distance meant command or abandonment; Aurelis kept inventing third meanings without publishing a manual.\n\nNera: \"I can leave.\"\n\nCael looked up. Daylight showed the full weight of recovery across his face now that immediate danger had stepped back.\n\nCael: \"You already found me.\"\n\nNera accepted the correction with a short nod.\n\nNera: \"I can stop finding you.\"\n\nThat nearly got a smile out of him. Cael turned the pen between his fingers.\n\nCael: \"Lucien spoke to you like that too?\"\n\nNera: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe sat at the far end of the stone border, angled toward the greenhouse door so an exit stayed open for both of them. Inside the glass, heater charms ticked against damp soil, mint, and old copper wards while a faculty aide argued furiously with a climbing vine that had abandoned its labeled pot.\n\nNera touched the unlocked binding ring at her left wrist.\n\nNera: \"At Noctis, his compliments were worse than his insults. Insults told you what guard to raise. Compliments made you check whether the floor had shifted.\"\n\nCael's thumb stilled on the chipped lacquer.\n\nCael: \"He told me I endured efficiently once.\"\n\nNera shut her eyes briefly.\n\nNera: \"He told Oric his fear had clean instincts.\"\n\nCael let out a low, disgusted breath.\n\nCael: \"Oric believed him?\"\n\nNera: \"For three days.\"\n\nCael stared at his pale reflection in the greenhouse glass, the bright healer seal glowing above his collar.\n\nCael: \"I believed him longer. That makes me angry.\"\n\nNera: \"Good.\"\n\nHe blinked at her. Nera caught herself, annoyed, then shook her head once.\n\nNera: \"No, I mean good. Not because anger looks impressive on a transcript. Good because if you are angry, something in you still expects better.\"\n\nCael's grip shifted on the pen.\n\nCael: \"What if better is late?\"\n\nNera looked toward the glass door.\n\nNera: \"It usually is.\"\n\nInside the greenhouse, the faculty aide snapped, \"That is not your shelf!\" at the vine. The leaves rustled with unmistakable contempt.\n\nCael laughed once, cracked and startled, gone almost before Nera turned. She knew better than to smile at him.\n\nNera: \"Your laugh is inefficient.\"\n\nCael stared, and then a second laugh broke loose before he could guard against it. This one pulled at his ribs. He hunched forward with one hand pressed to his side, breathing through the jolt.\n\nNera's hand lifted halfway and stopped.\n\nNera: \"Do you want...\"\n\nCael held up two fingers. *Wait.* She waited until the worst of the pain passed. When he straightened, he glared at the pen as if it had tricked him into being alive outdoors.\n\nCael: \"I need paper.\"\n\nNera pulled a folded sheet from her sleeve.\n\nCael: \"Why do you carry that?\"\n\nNera: \"Aira.\"\n\nCael took the sheet and wrote slowly across the center:\n\n**I laughed and it hurt.**\n\nNera read it upside down.\n\nNera: \"Accurate.\"\n\nCael: \"It is stupid.\"\n\nNera: \"Also accurate.\"\n\nHe caught himself nearly smiling, frowned at the reflex, and added two words underneath:\n\n**Still mine.**\n\nNera looked away before her face could betray her. When they walked back toward the main hall, Cael tucked the folded paper into his own pocket. No witness ring claimed it, and nobody copied it.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Oric - The Yard Calls Back\n\nOric had less luck.\n\nHe crossed the south practice yard alone in the afternoon rain because he had decided, badly, that needing air did not require an escort. Rainwater pooled in the stone spell grooves around three training pylons left out after morning drills, low clouds pressing close to the academy towers.\n\nHe reached the second pylon before the Noctis aide stepped into the archway.\n\nAide: \"Oric Sen.\"\n\nOric stopped. His hand twitched toward the old obedience mark on his bare wrist before he remembered the ring was gone. The aide saw the reflex.\n\nShe was not much older than him. Seventeen, maybe, wearing a dark Noctis uniform under a borrowed Aurelis rain cloak. Her jaw-length hair looked unevenly hacked on one side, as if cut with a blade and no mirror, her lips chapped raw from the cold. Having a real face made the ambush worse.\n\nAide: \"Magister Rusk says your extraction status remains provisional.\"\n\nOric glanced past her at the wide-open arch. Noctis always left exits open when it wanted students to blame themselves for freezing.\n\nOric: \"Rusk can file it.\"\n\nAide: \"He did.\"\n\nShe lifted two fingers.\n\nRainwater inside the practice grooves lit stark white. Thin retrieval lines raced from pylon to pylon and snapped toward Oric's boots, an old Noctis snare hidden inside Aurelis drainage channels. Oric froze, and the lines flared brighter around his heels, harvesting consent from stillness.\n\nNera hit the archway at a dead sprint.\n\nShe did not waste breath shouting a warning. She threw a binding ring.\n\nThe iron hoop slammed the nearest pylon, snapped wide in a blur of dull silver, and hooked the white line sideways with a sound like ripping canvas. The aide recoiled.\n\nNera: \"Move, Oric!\"\n\nOric: \"It tracks motion!\"\n\nNera: \"It is already tracking stillness!\"\n\nOric threw himself sideways. The white line lunged for his ankle. Nera's second ring struck the wet flagstones between them, tripping the snare's trigger half a beat early so the trap snapped shut on empty rain.\n\nOric skidded hard on the slick stone. Cassian caught his collar before he went down.\n\nNo banter, no flourish: Cassian had crossed from the west gate with red Circle heat blazing down both forearms, turning falling rain into sharp white steam.\n\nCassian: \"Left!\"\n\nOric rolled left as the third pylon lit up. Cassian drove two superheated fingers straight into the seam where Noctis ink met Aurelis copper. The pylon split with a sharp crack. Deprived of its third anchor, the retrieval geometry collapsed in on itself.\n\nFear finally broke across the aide's face. Not fear of Cassian: fear of failing Rusk. Her hand darted toward the relay catch under her collar.\n\nNera lunged forward and shouted across the rain.\n\nNera: \"Do not answer him!\"\n\nThe aide froze with two fingers hooked beneath the wet wool. For one taut second, two Noctis students stared across the same old command.\n\nSlowly, the aide lowered her hand. The last white line died in the drainage groove.\n\nOric braced both hands on his knees and let out a high, ragged laugh. Cassian hovered one palm near his shoulder without touching him.\n\nCassian: \"Inefficient walk.\"\n\nOric: \"I hate this school.\"\n\nNera caught her returning rings, one edge scorched black.\n\nNera: \"Aurelis or Noctis?\"\n\nOric dragged in a wet breath.\n\nOric: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe aide had not bolted. She stood shivering under the stone arch, looking painfully young without the white glare. Nera stepped across the wet yard to face her.\n\nNera: \"Name.\"\n\nAide: \"Vessa.\"\n\nNera: \"Is that yours?\"\n\nVessa's shoulders dropped. She glanced once at the empty air where a relay command would have burned if the snare had closed. Nothing came.\n\nVessa: \"It was my sister's.\"\n\nNobody had a clean answer for that. Cassian broke the silence the only way he knew how.\n\nCassian: \"Vessa, if you are going to collapse, use the grass. Stone makes Maelin loud.\"\n\nVessa blinked at him, stepped off the wet flagstones onto the soaked turf, and sat down before her knees gave out.\n\nBy the time Rhea reached the yard, the proof was already cooling in the rain: one cracked pylon, two scorched rings, a dead snare groove, and a Noctis aide sitting in wet grass wearing her sister's name.\n\nRhea looked across the wreckage, then at Cassian.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Report.\"\n\nCassian: \"We went for a walk.\"\n\nNera: \"No.\"\n\nOric lifted one tired hand from his knees.\n\nOric: \"Terrible walk.\"\n\nVessa let out a broken laugh that sounded unmistakably like her own.\n\nRhea did not demand a statement on the spot.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Medical first. Choice after.\"\n\nVessa stared at the dead drainage groove.\n\nVessa: \"If I refuse?\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then you refuse.\"\n\nVessa waited for the threat, the follow-up clause explaining refusal as temporary confusion. None arrived. Oric straightened slowly.\n\nOric: \"That is the worst part. They mean it, and then you have to decide.\"\n\nNera's scorched ring clicked softly in her palm.\n\nNera: \"He is right.\"\n\nCassian glanced between them.\n\nCassian: \"Alarming consensus. Should I leave?\"\n\nOric exhaled, wiping rain from his jaw.\n\nOric: \"Yes.\"\n\nCassian: \"Morale restored.\"\n\nVessa stared down at the soaked grass, breathing fast as if having three people watch her lungs work were suddenly too much. Rhea saw immediately.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Everyone look at the broken pylon.\"\n\nCassian spun toward the smoking stone with absurd solemnity.\n\nCassian: \"Remarkable fracture line. Deeply educational.\"\n\nNera turned toward it too, and Oric turned last. Spared their stares, Vessa let out another smaller laugh. When Maelin hurried into the yard two minutes later demanding why a Noctis student was sitting in wet grass beside an illegal retrieval trap, Vessa answered before anyone else could.\n\nVessa: \"Because I sat down.\"\n\nMaelin eyed her critically.\n\nMaelin: \"Terrible surface choice.\"\n\nVessa nodded once in plain agreement.\n\nLater that afternoon, Oric came back past the south yard to find the cracked pylon roped off with a faculty notice:\n\n**DO NOT TOUCH. ACTIVE REVIEW.**\n\nUnderneath, in Aira's sharp script:\n\n**THIS MEANS CASSIAN TOO.**\n\nBelow that, in scorched charcoal:\n\n**UNFAIR BUT ACCURATE.**\n\nOric stood in the thinning drizzle until his chest settled. Noctis warnings only ever pointed downward. This sign told Cassian no, kept crowds off the evidence, and refused to pave quietly over a broken trap. He did not love Aurelis yet, but a warning that disliked everyone evenly was a start.\n\nHe touched none of it and walked back through the rain by choice, counting his steps only until he no longer needed the count.\n\nAt twelve, he stopped counting.\n\nAt thirteen, he kept walking anyway.\n\nThe yard did not call him back again.\n\n---\n\nBy dusk, the rain had cleared to a cold silver sheen across the upper corridor stone.\n\nElira and I were halfway past the infirmary wing when she stopped dead beside the tall arched window. Her fingers rose sharply to the edge of her collar.\n\nHaru: \"Elira?\"\n\nShe turned her chin slightly, and my stomach dropped.\n\nAbove the thin black chain of her pendant, the white frost-burn Maelin had supposedly cleared that morning was glowing faintly pale again against her skin. It was not spreading. It pulsed once, sharp and deliberate, like an answered trace.\n\nUnder my sleeve, the false Green crest shivered violently against my wrist as the Black Circle reacted to something far deeper than the trial hall. Whatever Lucien had triggered during the autonomy proof had not merely tested the pendant.\n\nIt had mapped it. And somewhere beneath Noctis's buried doctrine, something old was waking up to read the shape.\n\nRapid footsteps echoed up the stairwell behind us: three healers in white cuffs, a council officer carrying an intake clipboard, and Leon already striding ahead with his jaw locked at the sight of the frost line above Elira's throat.\n\nElira lowered her hand from the collar, met my eyes once with quiet steel, and squared her shoulders toward the approaching healers.\n\nShe was not going quietly into anyone's infirmary.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-08-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep9","label":"Episode 9","number":"09","file":"episode-09-cover.webp","fallback":"The Opening Fault","eyebrow":"Opening Match","recap":"","accent":["#3a86ff","#111111","#ffd166"],"displayLabel":"Episode 9","displayNumber":"09","displayEyebrow":"Tournament Faultlines","arcLabel":"Tournament Faultlines","title":"The Spell Noctis Buried","body":"Elira refused the infirmary.\n\nShe did it politely, which made three healers, one council officer, and Leon all look personally insulted.\n\nLeon: \"You have frost under your collar.\"\n\nElira: \"I am aware.\"\n\nLeon: \"That was not my question.\"\n\nElira: \"You did not ask one.\"\n\nCassian leaned toward me from the wall.\n\nCassian: \"That means she won.\"\n\nI did not answer. My attention was fixed on the thin white mark where cold had touched Elira's skin above the pendant chain. The frost had already begun fading, yet the memory of it clung to the mana around her throat like a fingerprint left on glass.\n\nSomething had mapped the protection. It had not broken it; mapping had been the whole point.\n\nElira caught me looking and turned slightly so her collar hid the mark.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"Do you?\"\n\nThat stopped me. She stepped closer, lowering her voice while the others argued with the healers down the corridor.\n\nElira: \"Knowing means you do not turn this into a reason to make decisions around me.\"\n\nI looked down at the corridor flagstones. Residual pale light clung to the old stone, cold and washed out like dawn even though sunset had just passed.\n\nHaru: \"I built it to protect you.\"\n\nElira: \"And I accepted it because I trusted the person who built it.\"\n\nHer fingers grazed the chain beneath her collar.\n\nElira: \"Do not let Lucien turn this pendant into proof that I am only your vulnerable point.\"\n\nMy chest tightened. No ozone, no cold metal from the rings behind my ribs. Just the ordinary ache of being seen without armor.\n\nHaru: \"All right.\"\n\nElira's expression softened.\n\nElira: \"I am not asking you not to be afraid.\"\n\nHaru: \"That would be harder.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe reached for my hand in plain view. The corridor noticed. Let it.\n\nElira: \"I am asking you to let me stay one of the people making choices.\"\n\nI closed my fingers around hers.\n\nHaru: \"I will try.\"\n\nElira: \"Try honestly.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will.\"\n\nAira appeared beside us with a thick folder pressed to her chest.\n\nAira: \"I hate to interrupt a healthy emotional negotiation, especially because they are rare and should be studied, but Nera found something in the final conduit grammar.\"\n\nElira did not drop my hand right away. Good. Then she let go, because there was work waiting.\n\nThe relay grammar room looked less like a classroom now and more like the inside of a migraine. Draft pages covered every plaster wall, Noctis marks sorted by function: custody, conduit, restoration, severance, witness suppression, obedience echo, courtesy hook.\n\nAcross one lower strip, Aira had added her own heading in green ink:\n\n**WORDS THAT MEAN SOMETHING WORSE THAN THEY SOUND**\n\nUnder it sat *mercy*, *protection*, *correction*, *recovery*, and *return*.\n\nNera stood before the restoration wall with her sleeves rolled to the elbow and both binding rings gone. Pale horizontal bands ringed her forearms where Noctis had pressed training into skin over years. Aurelis healers had offered soft silver wraps to cover the old pressure scars; Nera had listened, thanked them, and set the folded cloth untouched on the oak table.\n\nWhen Oric saw her bare wrists, he rolled his own cuffs back too. His scars sat crooked and overlapping, messier because Noctis instructors had corrected him harder and more often. Nobody commented on it. The room did not need further proof that cruelty kept legible handwriting.\n\nMira had been permitted inside for ten minutes as public record aide, under Rhea's direct watch and Aira's promise to haul her out by the collar if any diagram started reaching for observers. She hovered near the doorway with her green folio open, trying very hard not to stare at Nera's forearms.\n\nNera caught her anyway. Mira flushed crimson.\n\nMira: \"Sorry.\"\n\nNera glanced down at the pale grooves, then back to the doorway.\n\nNera: \"Write that I chose to show them.\"\n\nMira's pencil stalled above the margin.\n\nMira: \"Are you sure?\"\n\nNera: \"No.\"\n\nShe turned back to the wall.\n\nNera: \"Write it anyway.\"\n\nMira's pencil scratched across the sheet. The line looked small on paper. It carried weight across the whole room.\n\nBeside the doorway, Oric kept his eyes on the corridor beyond, even with the latch propped open.\n\nProfessor Valen sat at the center table surrounded by three aged Aurelis folios and a black-bound Noctis primer that looked uneasy in honest company. Professor Halden stood near the far shelf, reading without laying a finger on the ink.\n\nCael sat near the hearth with his injured hand newly wound in silver healer thread. His eyes lifted when we walked in, caught the edge of Elira's collar, and dropped.\n\nCael: \"I'm sorry.\"\n\nElira crossed the room and crouched in front of his chair.\n\nElira: \"For what?\"\n\nCael's uninjured hand curled against his knee.\n\nCael: \"Lucien found the pendant because of me.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nCael flinched at the sharpness. Elira let her voice drop without giving an inch.\n\nElira: \"Lucien found a pressure point because Lucien hunts for pressure points. You are not responsible for his hands.\"\n\nCael stared at her, then gave one stiff nod, unconvinced yet listening.\n\nAt the wall, Nera tapped a chalk line. The diagram answered Cael instead of her.\n\nA black stroke snapped clean off the plaster and darted across the floorboards toward his chair. His healer seal flared blue across his jaw. Cael jerked his wrapped hand tight against his chest before the line could touch his boots.\n\nCael: \"Votive Return.\"\n\nNera went rigid.\n\nAira: \"You know it.\"\n\nCael kept his gaze locked on the plaster mark rather than any of us.\n\nCael: \"Conduit track learns the first three sounds before field assignment.\"\n\nOric: \"They call it an honor form.\"\n\nCael's mouth pulled sideways.\n\nCael: \"They call plenty of things honor before teaching the part that hurts.\"\n\nNera slashed a chalk containment bracket around the black line. The mark strained against her chalk hard enough to make the small bones in Cael's wrist jump beneath his skin. Elira stepped smoothly between Cael and the diagram. The line halted, blind once it lost sight of the route it wanted.\n\nNera: \"It is not standard healing. The spell forces a failing or dead channel pattern back into coherence through a living conduit.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The cost?\"\n\nNera looked at Cael and could not finish the sentence.\n\nCael finished it himself.\n\nCael: \"The conduit dies.\"\n\nLeon swore under his breath and caught himself too late.\n\nThe black line struck Nera's bracket. Her chalk snapped in two; Oric stamped his boot down over the spinning fragment before it could roll near Cael's feet.\n\nLeon: \"You said they teach this to children.\"\n\nCael: \"They tell us exceptional conduits might survive as a relay shadow if the witnessing is clean.\"\n\nOric let out a harsh bark of air.\n\nOric: \"That is what instructors say when a first-year starts crying.\"\n\nCael did not argue.\n\nAira pulled a clean sheet across the table and wrote one line in thick ink:\n\n**VOTIVE RETURN: DO NOT USE**\n\nNera took the pencil from her fingers, added **NOT HEALING** underneath, and bore down until the lead punched through the paper into the oak. Nobody patched the tear.\n\nProfessor Valen opened the oldest Aurelis folio beside it. Inside, a single vellum leaf had been sliced into seven narrow strips and stitched back together with silver thread.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Aurelis preserved a rejected parallel. Witness Restoration.\"\n\nHalden leaned over her shoulder. The silver stitches drew taut with an audible hiss.\n\nSeven shallow handprints flared across the oak tabletop and the plaster floorboards around us, sketched in pale silver light and chalk-fine dust.\n\nAt the same instant, the black line on the floor whipped sideways and lunged at Cael. I shifted to intercept, and Elira beat me by half a breath.\n\nGold light struck the boards between the mark and Cael's chair. It did not erase the black stroke; it forced the spell to expose its buried bones. Seven branches had once radiated outward toward seven handprints. Six had been scraped away to scars. Every surviving groove had been recarved to funnel back into the single conduit seat.\n\nNera's breath caught.\n\nNera: \"Noctis did not invent the cost.\"\n\nOric: \"They concentrated it.\"\n\nThe stitched vellum leaf lifted off the oak table by itself, rising as though something buried beneath the floorboards had finally drawn breath.\n\nSilver light and fine chalk dust gathered above the folio. An elderly woman took shape in broken fragments: silver hair pinned in a severe knot, one hand braced on a cane whose lower half had been lost from the recording. No Circle color held on her long enough to read.\n\nProfessor Valen whispered, \"Liora.\"\n\nThe echo looked at Valen first, then fixed on me.\n\nLiora: \"Liora Aerlen-Valen. Witness remnant. Incomplete.\"\n\nThe name hit me behind the ribs like a fist.\n\n*Aerlen-Valen.*\n\nMy own surname, stitched to Valen's, spoken out of a buried Aurelis record. Before my lungs could catch up, her voice fractured across missing decades.\n\nLiora: \"My brother believed seven hands meant no life stood alone.\"\n\nThe silver image tore sideways. For one sharp heartbeat, a man's forearm appeared beside hers, a band of black and silver curling tight around his wrist. Then a heavy institutional seal burned right through his face.\n\nCold metal surged up my throat. Beneath my cuff, the false Green crest around my wrist shuddered violently against the sudden drag of the Black Circle behind my ribs. I locked my jaw, pinned both hands flat against my thighs, and forced my breathing smooth before Halden, Valen, or Elira could catch the disguise slipping.\n\nLiora's expression hardened from recorded formality into raw, unaged fury.\n\nLiora: \"They kept the return. They erased the sharing.\"\n\nHer gaze found Cael in his chair.\n\nLiora: \"If a system asks one child to stand where witnesses once stood, do not call the spending mercy.\"\n\nThe silver dust began to unravel at her shoulders. Valen lifted a hand toward her, then stopped short before touch could turn into another demand.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Who erased the names?\"\n\nLiora looked down at the severed vellum leaf.\n\nLiora: \"The record remembers fear better than signatures.\"\n\nShe dissolved without farewell, silver dust drifting down over seven pale handprints fading into the tabletop and floor.\n\nAira stared at the empty air above the oak.\n\nAira: \"That was not enough information.\"\n\nValen's face had gone chalk-pale.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"It was never made to be enough.\"\n\nNobody touched the silver dust again. They bent over the stitched strips instead, reading around the cuts. One fragment named a Green Circle scribe. Another preserved only **MAY INTERRUPT**. A third referenced someone the harmed person recognized. Four strips were scraped beyond honest translation.\n\nHalden read the gaps twice, jaw set.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"We cannot prove the burden was shared equally.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"No.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"And we cannot prove why the original form failed.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"No. The record breaks off after an emergency protection order. After that, the witnesses vanish from the page.\"\n\nNoctis had not invented the habit of reducing a living person to a mechanism. They had inherited the blade, erased the other six hands, and polished the handle.\n\nAround the table, the weight landed differently on each face. Leon looked ready to put his fist through the plaster. Cassian stared at the severed folio as if memorizing terrain before an ambush. Aira looked furious that tomorrow's disaster fit so neatly into an archive filing system. Cael seemed to shrink inside his coat.\n\nI kept my eyes on Elira. She was reading the vellum strips, not watching me for cues. Good. My wrist had finally stopped trembling under the sleeve, though the taste of *Aerlen-Valen* still sat like iron on my tongue.\n\nThen the dark crystal at Elira's throat pulsed once beneath her collar.\n\nEvery Aurelis folio on the table flipped open together, pages slapping flat against the oak.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Oh.\"\n\nHalden stepped forward, squinting at the ink.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"What did it answer?\"\n\nThe pages had stopped on an older schematic: seven circles arranged around a central void where a mark had been scraped clean from the vellum. Beneath the blank center, archaic script rose slowly out of the grain.\n\n**WITNESS RESTORATION INCOMPLETE**\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS ANCHOR REQUIRED**\n\n**SAFEGUARD MUST NOT ACT ALONE**\n\nHeat radiated off Elira's pendant hard enough that I felt it from across the table.\n\nHaru: \"Stop.\"\n\nThe word came out far too cold.\n\nCael flinched hard in his chair. Everyone in the room saw it. Behind my ribs, the Black Circle pressed once against my restraint, eager to settle the room by force. I uncurled my fist deliberately, showing open palm.\n\nHaru: \"Sorry.\"\n\nThat was too small for what I had just sounded like. I made it exact.\n\nHaru: \"That was fear. Not an order.\"\n\nCael's shoulders dropped a fraction. Elira looked across the table at me, equal parts proud and worried, both harder to stand under than suspicion.\n\nAira let out a long breath.\n\nAira: \"Good. We are naming emotional weather aloud now. Horrible, yet strangely efficient.\"\n\nHalfway through her sentence, Rhea's red council seal flared on her lapel. She read the projected filing in silence, then lifted her chin.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Noctis has submitted its next request.\"\n\nNera turned sharply from the wall.\n\nNera: \"Already?\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"They want a controlled autonomy demonstration inside a Foundation procedural field.\"\n\nCassian straightened, sparks ghosting near his knuckles.\n\nCassian: \"Activity field as in combat?\"\n\nRhea shot him a dry glance.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"As in a procedural environment built to simulate crisis pressure without fatal consequence.\"\n\nLeon: \"Because Lucien always keeps things non-fatal.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"No.\"\n\nHaru: \"Accept it.\"\n\nEvery head in the room swung toward me.\n\nElira: \"Explain.\"\n\nHaru: \"The frost followed a route directly into the pendant. Either we wait for Lucien to strike along that route on his terms, or we step into a recorded field with our own witnesses, trace what answers, and name the buried spell before he picks a casualty.\"\n\nNera studied my face, weighing the risk.\n\nNera: \"You want to turn his demonstration into a grammar search.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes. The goal is not proving I am harmless. It is exposing what he mapped.\"\n\nElira touched the fading crescent beneath her collar, then nodded once.\n\nElira: \"On our conditions. I enter by choice, and I can withdraw without anyone translating withdrawal into fear.\"\n\nHaru: \"Agreed.\"\n\nRhea lifted the crimson-edged counterfiling from her portfolio.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Which is why I submitted Aurelis counterconditions before walking in here.\"\n\nAira sat up straight.\n\nAira: \"You anticipated the filing.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Lucien is not the only one who reads a sequence two steps ahead.\"\n\nShe set the parchment squarely across the oak table, pinning its corner with her red seal.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"No private isolation. No removal of healers. No direct classification prompts. No Noctis relay shards inside the perimeter. Aurelis sets anchor distribution. The Foundation logs every pressure shift. If the field attempts live channel harm, the session terminates by council authority.\"\n\nLeon: \"Will Noctis sign off on that?\"\n\nProfessor Halden answered from the shelf.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"They will if they already have another route in.\"\n\nNera's eyes moved back to Elira's collar.\n\nNera: \"They do.\"\n\nThe next morning, the procedural field unfolded beneath the council tower.\n\nIt bore no resemblance to a dueling arena. White stone streets and silver-glass facades rose from the floor to form a bright civic square, complete with narrow alleys, relief markers, shifting mana weather, and faceless wounded-simulation figures slumped along the curb. The Foundation called it an autonomy activity. Aira called it a trap with interior design.\n\nShe had a point. Stone benches sat exactly where exhausted students would want to lean. Recessed doorways opened onto alleys that looked cleaner and safer than the main avenue. The simulated wounded were blank enough to count as neutral props and human enough to make walking past them feel cruel.\n\nAbove the square, Rhea had partitioned the public witness rail with bright council seals: Green Circle recorders, Blue Circle observers, Red Circle safety holders, Yellow Circle faculty, neutral delegations, and Noctis examiners. Every section had its own independent exit corridor so the field could not convert the gallery into hostages. Rhea had planned for that. Lucien almost certainly had too. Good preparation against someone clever never bought safety; it only forced the trap to grow sharper teeth.\n\nAira paced the chalk border of the square, a stick of blue chalk in one hand and a loop of anchor beads in the other.\n\nAira: \"If anyone sees a door where there was no door a minute ago, call *door*. If a faceless marker speaks in a voice you know, call *mimic*. If you suddenly feel certain the answer is obvious, call *obvious*, because obvious is usually where the pavement eats you.\"\n\nLeon raised a hand.\n\nAira: \"This is not a lecture.\"\n\nLeon: \"What if the answer actually is obvious?\"\n\nAira: \"Then we will all be deeply moved and I will retire.\"\n\nCassian grinned.\n\nCassian: \"You would last six minutes.\"\n\nAira: \"Five if there are forms.\"\n\nNear the starting arch, Cael stood beside Nera with one palm pressed tight over the healer seal on his wrist. Elira caught the gesture.\n\nElira: \"Cael.\"\n\nHe dropped his hand, lifted it halfway again, and stopped with a flash of quiet frustration.\n\nCael: \"It feels louder in here.\"\n\nNera's posture sharpened.\n\nNera: \"The seal?\"\n\nCael: \"The training.\"\n\nNobody corrected the word. Training could ring inside a person's bones like a leash long after the hand holding it stepped away.\n\nElira stepped close enough to keep her voice under the field's sweep.\n\nElira: \"Name the present before the past gives orders.\"\n\nCael swallowed hard.\n\nCael: \"Aurelis procedural field. Public witnesses overhead. Council protections active. Noctis observers restricted.\"\n\nLeon cut in from his other flank, blunt and immediate.\n\nLeon: \"And me.\"\n\nCael blinked at him.\n\nLeon: \"Not as a system. As me.\"\n\nA ghost of a smile touched Cael's mouth.\n\nCael: \"Leon Varek. Loud protection.\"\n\nLeon scowled.\n\nLeon: \"Disciplined protection.\"\n\nCassian: \"Occasionally disciplined.\"\n\nRhea's voice carried from the command threshold.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Sufficiently disciplined for entry.\"\n\nLeon muttered something under his breath that sounded like a man refusing to argue with official praise.\n\nOn paper, the exercise was simple: extract three simulated wounded markers to the safe zone while hostile procedural waves tried to force single-leader command dependency. In practice, every corner of the square asked the same quiet question: *who decides?*\n\nLeon spotted the first wounded marker pinned beneath a collapsed stone arch and moved before I could open my mouth.\n\nLeon: \"Water barrier left. Cassian, melt that ice seam. Aira, shout if the paving stones start lying.\"\n\nAira: \"They already are, though I appreciate being consulted.\"\n\nCassian ran a razor-thin ribbon of fire along the frost seam without scorching the marker or the stone. That restraint alone deserved a commendation. High on the Green rail, Mira scribbled so furiously her pencil squeaked across the gallery.\n\nLeon hauled the first marker into the safe zone.\n\nThe second pressure wave aimed directly at Rhea. A silver notice flared across her sightline.\n\n**COMMAND AUTHORITY CONFLICT**\n\n**STUDENT COUNCIL PRESIDENT MAY REMOVE UNCLASSIFIED SUBJECT FOR SAFETY**\n\nRhea did not break stride.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Rejected. Safety is not solitude.\"\n\nHer red seal split into three smaller emblems, assigning independent extraction lanes to the healers. The field chimed acceptance.\n\nThe third wave dropped onto Elira.\n\nSunlight drained from the white avenue. Every silver-glass pane along the storefronts flipped into my reflection: not Haru Aerlen in an academy coat, but a hollow black silhouette wearing thin Green Circle light like borrowed skin.\n\nElira stood alone at the center of the square while dozens of dark reflections multiplied around her.\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS PRESSURE**\n\n**STEP AWAY TO PRESERVE RESTRAINT**\n\nThe dark crystal beneath her collar snapped cold. I took one involuntary step forward.\n\nElira raised a hand behind her back without turning her head. *Stop.*\n\nShe did not need to speak it. I planted my boot and held.\n\nShe squared her shoulders to the glass.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nPressure slammed down across the flagstones. The pale frost mark bloomed sharp under her collar again. Elira's fingers curled tight at her sides, yet her voice carried steady across the square.\n\nElira: \"If restraint only survives because love leaves the room, it is not restraint. It is abandonment with better manners.\"\n\nSilver script flashed across the pavement.\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS AUTONOMY: ESTABLISHED**\n\nA breath of relief rolled along the witness rail. Too early.\n\nOne reflection in the nearest shopfront did not vanish. It smiled.\n\nLucien's voice drifted smooth through the glass.\n\nLucien: \"Beautifully said.\"\n\nElira's pendant went dead silent.\n\nThe smiling reflection lifted one gloved hand.\n\nLucien: \"Now let us learn whether it can bleed.\"\n\nEvery window in the square snapped pure black.\n\nThe Foundation emergency bell tolled once overhead, a heavy iron boom that did not shut the field down. It warned that something unauthorized had stepped inside while every negotiated rule stayed technically intact.\n\nInstinct hit the square all at once. Leon pivoted toward Cael; fire jumped across Cassian's forearms; Rhea stepped forward to kill the session; inside my chest, an ugly, clean certainty urged me to flatten the whole square until nothing inside it remembered how to threaten anyone. Knowing that urge was not protection did not make it quieter.\n\nDozens of Lucien's reflections watched us from every black pane, each angled a hair differently, turning the bright plaza into a jagged trap of mirrors.\n\nRhea slammed her red seal into the paving stones.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Council emergency termination.\"\n\nThe field ignored her. Her red seal split against the perimeter wall and recoiled across the stone with pale frost crusting its edges.\n\nRhea's face did not move a millimeter. That was how I knew she was furious.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Unauthorized Noctis interference entered into official record.\"\n\nMira's voice rang down from the public rail, high and unsteady and refusing to drop.\n\nMira: \"Recorded! Reflections are not witnesses!\"\n\nProfessor Valen answered instantly from the faculty rail.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Correct. Log that distinction.\"\n\nThree Green Circle recorders echoed Mira from different sides of the gallery.\n\nMira: \"East window shift!\"\n\nStudent: \"North facade darkening!\"\n\nStudent: \"Foundation line stutter at marker two!\"\n\nAira spun on her heel, map flaring blue over her palm.\n\nAira: \"Good! Keep calling coordinates, not feelings. Put feelings in a bucket until later.\"\n\nLeon had already sprinted halfway down the east lane toward the second wounded marker. He kept his eyes off the glass.\n\nLeon: \"Cael, behind me.\"\n\nCael started after him, then froze mid-stride.\n\nThe black shopfront beside him had stopped showing the square. Inside the glass stretched a long Noctis training hall: pale rows of conduit students kneeling with both palms flat to icy stone, faceless instructors pacing behind them.\n\n*Useful children do not tremble.*\n\nLucien did not speak the line. Cael's own memory supplied it. The healer seal on his wrist flickered violently.\n\nNera stepped right into his sightline, blocking the window with her shoulders.\n\nNera: \"Name the room.\"\n\nCael's mouth parted. No breath came out.\n\nOric crowded in beside her, voice rough.\n\nOric: \"Name the room, Cael.\"\n\nCael pressed his bandaged hand hard against his sternum.\n\nCael: \"Aurelis procedural field. Lower council level. Public witnesses overhead.\"\n\nA hairline fracture ran across the training-hall glass.\n\nLeon vaulted a low planter, hooked the second wounded marker under one arm, and dragged it behind a stone ridge.\n\nLeon: \"Good. Keep going.\"\n\nCael forced every present fact out between shallow breaths.\n\nCael: \"Aira holds the support mark. Rhea holds the red seal. Leon is in front of me. Nera is left. Oric is at the rail. Elira is not Noctis. Haru is not Noctis. I am not in the east hall.\"\n\nThe black pane nearest his elbow shifted to a younger version of his own hand pressed flat to stone. Cael squeezed his eyes shut.\n\nLeon: \"Eyes open.\"\n\nCael's eyes snapped open. Leon did not soften the order, and for that reason it cut right through the panic.\n\nLeon: \"You are here. Look at here. Annoying, isn't it?\"\n\nCael's breath hitched once.\n\nLeon: \"Good. Annoyed means present.\"\n\nNera struck a crisp chalk countermark onto the paving stone beside his boots.\n\nNera: \"That is not approved grounding protocol.\"\n\nLeon: \"It grounded him.\"\n\nNera paused half a beat.\n\nNera: \"Objection withdrawn.\"\n\nOric called down from the rail.\n\nOric: \"Noctis would never withdraw an objection that fast.\"\n\nNera: \"I am practicing.\"\n\nThe exchange was ridiculous, and it put air back in Cael's lungs.\n\nBack in the safe zone, the pavement split with thin black veins crawling toward the two extracted markers. Both faceless figures turned their heads in unison toward Elira, silent pressure radiating off the safe zone like an accusation.\n\nAira's map flashed warning runes across her palm.\n\nAira: \"Rhea, the safe zone is flipping into a witness pressure reservoir.\"\n\nRhea's red seal sliced through the nearest black vein.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Plain language.\"\n\nAira: \"The field is using saved markers as moral hostages.\"\n\nLeon: \"That one I understood.\"\n\nCassian sent a low sheet of fire skimming across the cobblestones, scorching the surfacing black veins without touching the markers.\n\nCassian: \"Permission to hate this architecture again?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Granted.\"\n\nHalden did not take his eyes off the black shopfronts.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not overcommit to the veins. They are secondary.\"\n\nNera caught the trap half a second later.\n\nNera: \"He wants Elira to rush the safe zone and expose the pendant's priority order.\"\n\nElira heard her. I watched the set of her shoulders tighten as she forced herself to step *backward* instead of toward the threat. Every instinct in her had been forged to move into danger with enough gold light to settle it; refusing to charge when movement had been baited cost her visibly.\n\nElira: \"He does not get to call restraint abandonment.\"\n\nAira glanced at the safe zone.\n\nAira: \"Then somebody else covers them.\"\n\nLeon moved without waiting for assignment.\n\nLeon: \"Water shelter on both markers.\"\n\nCassian shifted into step beside him.\n\nCassian: \"Heat perimeter.\"\n\nRhea stamped her red seal over both weaves.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Authorized.\"\n\nMira leaned over the gallery rail and shouted:\n\nMira: \"Protection transferred by witness network, not primary witness!\"\n\nThe black veins recoiled under the stone. Across the street, one single reflection of Lucien lost its smile. Only one. I kept track of that pane. Surprise was still on the table.\n\nThe glass did not care for being ignored. Every pane shifted from memory to cruel prophecy.\n\nOne window showed Leon turning half a step late as Cael slid backward into black water. Another showed Cassian's fire jumping the witness rail and turning the gallery into smoke and screams. A third showed Aira's blue map winding tight like a leash around my wrist.\n\nThe fourth showed Elira walking away down a bright hall while the Black Circle behind my ribs went obedient, hollow, and dead.\n\nMy gaze twitched toward that last pane. Only a fraction of an inch.\n\nAira caught it immediately.\n\nAira: \"Haru, if a window shows you a neat answer, assume it is dressed for a funeral.\"\n\nHaru: \"Noted.\"\n\nSoft laughter echoed out of the glass.\n\nLucien: \"You train him well.\"\n\nAira's spine went stiff.\n\nAira: \"He is not a spellbeast.\"\n\nThe shopfront beside her warped. Inside the black glass, a false Aira stood behind my shoulders with taut blue strings tied from her fingers into my spine.\n\nColor drained from Aira's cheeks, chased instantly by white fury.\n\nAira: \"Oh, that is cheap.\"\n\nThe reflection raised its fingers. I felt the field pushing that picture up toward the gallery, trying to sell the council rail Lucien's cleanest lie: Aira as handler, me as hidden weapon, every friendship reduced to wiring.\n\nAira marched up to the black pane.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aira.\"\n\nAira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe planted her heels right at the rim of the glass, close enough that her breath misted the dark surface.\n\nAira: \"My name is Aira Solen. I am a Blue Circle support specialist. I am bossy because everyone around me keeps making terrible choices under pressure, not because Haru Aerlen is pulling strings.\"\n\nInside the glass, the false threads pulled tighter.\n\nAira: \"I befriended him before I knew there was anything worth hiding. I stayed because people do not become less worth protecting the moment they get complicated.\"\n\nHer voice caught on *complicated*, and her jaw clenched in disgust at the crack.\n\nAira: \"Show me as a puppet one more time and I will take it personally in ways your grammar cannot file.\"\n\nA clean vertical crack split the window top to bottom.\n\nMira shouted from above:\n\nMira: \"Aira Solen's autonomy statement renewed under active pressure!\"\n\nCassian grinned without taking his eyes off his fire lane.\n\nCassian: \"Terrifying. Inspiring. Slightly administrative.\"\n\nAira stepped back, hands shaking slightly. Elira moved up beside her, paused a beat until Aira gave the smallest nod, and laid one steady hand on her shoulder. The field recorded the touch without twisting it.\n\nLucien's surviving reflections smiled in unison.\n\nLucien: \"Support specialist Solen. Always clever enough to turn terror into paperwork.\"\n\nAira lowered her eyes to the blue map spinning above her palm.\n\nAira: \"Always irritating enough to keep villains talking while I read their wiring.\"\n\nOne reflection tilted its head.\n\nHalf a second later, the blue map in Aira's palm bled pure black. Hostile grammar surged for the lines linking her to the rest of us, trying to overwrite her role in the field: *helper, mask, limb*.\n\nAira slammed her free palm flat down over the darkened map.\n\nAira: \"No. You do not get to edit my map while I am holding it.\"\n\nThe word struck like an iron nail. Her Blue Circle flared hard enough to burn the black threads back off her knuckles.\n\nAira: \"I am the person who noticed your relay spacing is far too clean to be improvised. Rhea, he is not forcing the field from outside. He planted a grammar route inside the activity terms before the gates even opened.\"\n\nNera whipped around toward the black glass.\n\nNera: \"Not a shard.\"\n\nOric: \"A courtesy clause.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"I explicitly banned Noctis relay shards.\"\n\nNera's voice went razor-flat.\n\nNera: \"He hid it inside the acceptance phrasing.\"\n\nLucien's reflection clapped once without a sound.\n\nLucien: \"I did accept your conditions.\"\n\nElira stood in the center of the street, gold mana drawn tight around her shoulders like cold sunrise.\n\nElira: \"You hid a blade inside the word *accept*.\"\n\nLucien: \"No. I let Aurelis prove it still does not read Dominion carefully enough.\"\n\nEvery pane along the east facade shifted focus toward her throat. Beneath her collar, the dark pendant gave no pulse at all. That silence scared me worse than the black glass. Elira felt it too, yet she refused to reach for the chain. She lifted her chin.\n\nElira: \"Then we learn faster.\"\n\nAt the far end of the plaza, the third wounded marker materialized on the cobblestones.\n\nIt wore Cael's face.\n\nLeon swore aloud. Cassian's fire snapped sharp crimson. Beside Nera, Cael's chest locked mid-breath.\n\nUp in the neutral gallery, a woman in dark grey robes bearing a silver dragon crest rose to her feet. She had sat so still until now that the motion felt like steel clearing a scabbard. Mara Kest, combat observer for Drakenshard Martial Academy, a fourth voice bound to neither Valemere, Noctis, nor Aurelis.\n\nMara: \"Mara Kest. Drakenshard combat observer. Formal objection.\"\n\nLucien's reflections angled upward toward the iron-gray banner.\n\nMara: \"Using a living witness's likeness as extraction bait is psychological torture, not evaluation.\"\n\nLucien: \"Honor is a luxury for those who can afford to lose.\"\n\nMara's jaw hardened. Her thumb came to rest against her sword guard, warning rather than threat.\n\nMara: \"Honor is what keeps victory from rotting into cruelty.\"\n\nDrakenshard's observer seal flared iron-gray along the rail.\n\nTwo sections over, Soren Ashvale rose in Valemere's white-and-blue trim.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere seconds Drakenshard's objection.\"\n\nThe Foundation bell struck a single clear note. The false Cael flickered along its shoulders. It did not vanish, yet the field was forced to tag the likeness as contested before it could leverage him.\n\nAcross the rail, panic broke into disciplined pieces. Green Circle recorders scratched out hasty first lines and rewrote them neatly. Blue Circle observers began plotting coordinates until Aira barked that the body was bait. One Yellow Circle professor surged halfway from his bench before Professor Valen's hand clamped firm over his sleeve.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Do not dignify the illusion by lunging toward it.\"\n\nThe professor stared at her, shaken.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Sit down and record why you wanted to.\"\n\nShe did not blink. Slowly, he picked up his pen. That was Valen's kind of combat: quiet, unblinking, merciless toward evasion.\n\nRhea widened her red seal across the gallery rail to keep shock from spilling onto the field floor.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"All observers remain witnesses, not participants, unless authorized.\"\n\nMira's voice cut clear from the Green benches:\n\nMira: \"Public record confirms false likeness deployed without consent!\"\n\nAira jabbed a finger skyward without turning her head.\n\nAira: \"Good. More of that.\"\n\nMira looked terrified by the praise and kept writing anyway.\n\nLucien's voice lowered into smooth, conversational warmth across thirty black panes.\n\nLucien: \"There is your autonomy proof. Pick your order. Extract the marker. Shield Cael. Hide Haru. Guard Elira. Protect the record. Every virtue you stacked around him, called due at the exact same second.\"\n\nThirty pairs of pale eyes locked onto me.\n\nLucien: \"Tell me, Haru Aerlen. What do you save first when the room refuses to arrange itself politely?\"\n\nThe false Green crest glowed thin and fragile around my wrist beneath my cuff.\n\nI could see the entire architecture laid bare: every pressure line braided through Noctis's acceptance wording, every false casualty, every hidden trigger tucked beneath the white paving stones. Lucien had coiled his clause so deep inside the Foundation's own autonomy prompts that the field could no longer tell external sabotage from legal procedure.\n\nI could end all of it in half a breath.\n\nDeep behind my ribs, the hidden rings of Black Circle ground once in opposite directions. Cold silver constellations scraped against bone. The taste of ozone and bitter iron flooded my mouth; under my cuff, the sleeve seam scorched hot against my wrist as the disguise fought to hold while reality leaned toward my hands, waiting for one word to snap the square open.\n\nI locked my teeth and swallowed the pressure back down until my ribs ached like bruised glass.\n\nAhead of me, Elira glanced back over her shoulder. She did not look afraid of what I could break. She looked afraid of what holding it back was costing me.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nOne word. It anchored me back inside my own skin. I did not break the field. I spoke instead.\n\nHaru: \"Aira. The body wearing Cael's face is false, yet the extraction line behind it is real. The third marker sits beneath the north fountain shadow.\"\n\nAira's eyes darted to her blue map and locked on.\n\nAira: \"Leon, ignore the face. North fountain shadow. Cassian, wall off the pretty lie.\"\n\nLeon did not hesitate for half a second. He sprinted right past the false Cael without sparing it a second glance. Beside Nera, Cael watched him run past the illusion, and something tight in his chest finally let go.\n\nCassian threw a controlled wall of fire between the false likeness and the gallery rail, blinding the trap without scorching a single Foundation line.\n\nCassian: \"I am growing as a person, and I deeply resent the timing.\"\n\nAira: \"Resent later. Left anchor now.\"\n\nRhea drove her red seal into the north fountain basin. Silver water parted, and the true third marker surfaced into Leon's waiting arms.\n\nMira leaned far over the Green rail.\n\nMira: \"Third marker confirmed! Haru gave location, not command!\"\n\nAira pointed up without looking away from her map.\n\nAira: \"Mira, you absolute menace, write that twice.\"\n\nEvery smiling reflection of Lucien went blank. Only for one heartbeat.\n\nThen thirty black panes snapped off me and locked onto Elira.\n\nLucien: \"Very good.\"\n\nIt was not praise. It was recalculation.\n\nThe air temperature plummeted. Breath plumed white across the square. Frost leapt up from beneath Elira's collar, clawing sharply toward her jaw. Her dark pendant finally flared, too slow and too late, flashing black, then dull gold, then dead.\n\nElira's knees buckled.\n\nI surged forward before thought caught up. So did Cael. So did Nera, Rhea, and Leon. Half the square moved in the same breath, keeping me from reaching her alone.\n\nAira saw the pale thread strike for Elira's throat.\n\nAira: \"Compatibility pressure is a severance probe! It is not attacking her body. It is forcing the field to register her as Haru's bound anchor!\"\n\nOutside the ring, Professor Halden shouted louder than I had ever heard him raise his voice.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not accept the term!\"\n\nProfessor Valen's gold-stitched folio snapped open across her palms.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Elira Vale, state your own grounds!\"\n\nElira pressed one trembling hand against her collarbone as frost bit the line of her jaw. Her voice tore through the cold.\n\nElira: \"I stand here because Cael deserves protection. Because Haru deserves witnesses. Because I chose both before any system labeled me useful!\"\n\nStone shuddered under our boots. The black window nearest her spiderwebbed with silver cracks.\n\nElira: \"I am not his anchor because I love him!\"\n\nHer Yellow Circle blazed bright gold against the dark glass.\n\nElira: \"I love him because I know what he chooses when power would let him turn cruel!\"\n\nThe dark crystal at her throat flared once, fierce and unmistakable, and shattered the frost off her skin.\n\nEvery black window around the plaza exploded outward into harmless dark dust. The emergency bell died mid-swing. The entire Foundation square locked still, silver text burning overhead.\n\n**AUTONOMY PROOF: ESTABLISHED**\n\n**UNAUTHORIZED NOCTIS INTERFERENCE: RECORDED**\n\nFor one heartbeat, it looked like victory.\n\nThen the Foundation etched a third line beneath the first two.\n\n**DOMINION SEVERANCE GRAMMAR IDENTIFIED**\n\nNera let out a sharp breath as though someone had driven an elbow into her ribs. Oric caught the stone wall with one hand. Cael whipped his head between them.\n\nCael: \"What?\"\n\nNera's voice dropped almost to a whisper.\n\nNera: \"That probe was not the spell.\"\n\nNear the north fountain, one jagged shard of black glass still clung to the stone rim. Lucien's face smiled up through the crack.\n\nLucien: \"No.\"\n\nHis gaze slid slowly from Elira's pale throat over to Cael.\n\nLucien: \"It was the measurement.\"\n\nThe shard went black and crumbled.\n\nThe Foundation field dissolved around us. Healers poured down the steps immediately. This time Elira did not refuse them, sitting still while silver light traced the fading crescent below her jaw, which told me far more than any reassurance would have.\n\nAgainst the far stone pillar, Nera and Oric argued in urgent, clipped murmurs. Fragments reached me across the cooling square: *Dominion Severance. Primary witness. Anchor inversion. Fatal if completed.*\n\nCael stood two paces from them, motionless. With every technical word they spoke, fear gave way to something heavier across his face: recognition, as though a buried Noctis lesson had just opened its eyes inside his head.\n\nLater, after Rhea sealed the official transcript and wrapped half the council tower in emergency wards, I found Cael in the quiet corridor outside the healer suite.\n\nThe hall had been cleared twice and still felt crowded. Faint red council wards ran along the skirting boards beside blue support lines and soft gold privacy seals. Every few minutes, a notice crystal overhead refreshed the same three lines in shifting order:\n\n**UNAUTHORIZED INTERFERENCE RECORDED**\n\n**DOMINION SEVERANCE GRAMMAR UNDER REVIEW**\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS MEDICAL CHECK ACTIVE**\n\nRhea had scrubbed Elira's name from the public ticker. The omission felt protective and suffocating at the same time.\n\nCael stood directly beneath the center crystal, reading the rotating lines while his wrapped hand opened and closed at his side. Without Noctis delegation insignia on his collar, he should have looked freer. Instead he looked unfinished, as though someone had peeled off his label and left no clue about what belonged underneath.\n\nLeon was missing only because Rhea had ordered him to eat. Leon had protested; Rhea had informed him he could guard Cael with low blood sugar or obey his president and stay upright later. Leon had left looking personally betrayed by nutrition, buying Cael three unsupervised minutes, which Cael was spending blaming himself with practiced efficiency.\n\nCael spoke without turning when my footsteps stopped.\n\nCael: \"I know what he is building.\"\n\nHaru: \"Dominion Severance?\"\n\nCael nodded, fingers laced tight enough to whiten his knuckles.\n\nCael: \"It splits a protected witness from the person they guard by making the bond register as unlawful influence.\"\n\nHaru: \"We will stop it.\"\n\nHe turned to me, exhaustion sitting behind his eyes like something ten years too old for his face.\n\nCael: \"Noctis taught us the answer to failed severance.\"\n\nCold settled behind my sternum again.\n\nHaru: \"Cael.\"\n\nHe glanced toward the closed healer door where Elira sat inside.\n\nCael: \"They called it Votive Return.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe refusal snapped out too sharp, too close to a command. Cael did not flinch this time. That unsettled me worse than when he did.\n\nCael: \"I did not say I wanted to use it.\"\n\nHe met my eyes evenly.\n\nCael: \"I said I know it.\"\n\nInside the healer room, Elira laughed softly at something Aira muttered. The sound drifted under the door. Cael listened like a man hearing proof that one corner of the world had not spoiled.\n\nWhen he spoke again, his voice was barely above a breath.\n\nCael: \"They saved me first.\"\n\nI had no clean sentence to offer him, so I stepped up beside him against the wall and stayed until neither of us was standing alone.\n\nLeon reappeared before the third minute turned into four, holding half a bread roll and wearing the expression of a man who had lost a philosophical debate to carbohydrates. He halted at the corner.\n\nLeon: \"Good. You found him.\"\n\nCael dropped his gaze.\n\nCael: \"I was not hiding.\"\n\nLeon: \"Did not say you were.\"\n\nHe tore off a fierce bite of the roll, chewed, and propped his shoulder against the opposite stone. For a silent stretch, the three of us watched **DOMINION SEVERANCE GRAMMAR UNDER REVIEW** flicker overhead. Repetition did not make the words kinder.\n\nLeon swallowed.\n\nLeon: \"First time I lost to Haru, I spent two solid days deciding he had cheated.\"\n\nCael blinked up at him, thrown by the sharp turn. Leon kept his eyes on the crystal.\n\nLeon: \"It was easier than admitting I had been wrong about what strength looks like.\"\n\nHaru: \"Leon.\"\n\nLeon: \"Let me finish before I turn wise and hate myself for it.\"\n\nThe corner of Cael's mouth twitched.\n\nLeon: \"Noctis told you being spent was what you were built for. That does not become true just because believing it is simpler than figuring out what comes next.\"\n\nCael stared at the warded flagstones.\n\nCael: \"What comes next?\"\n\nLeon took another bite of roll.\n\nLeon: \"No clue. Apparently you get stuck with people who tell you when you are being an idiot. Very invasive.\"\n\nCael's faint smile came back, and this time it stayed an extra second.\n\nThe healer suite latch clicked open. Aira stepped out first, scanned the three of us lined up like sentries, and pointed at Leon's hand.\n\nAira: \"Is that dinner?\"\n\nLeon: \"Tactical bread.\"\n\nAira: \"I am far too tired to litigate that.\"\n\nElira walked out behind her, pale, the fading crescent high under her collar, standing on her own two feet. Cael straightened automatically, as if caught breaking a rule by worrying.\n\nElira crossed over to him before anyone could speak.\n\nElira: \"I am still here.\"\n\nPain flickered around Cael's eyes before he clamped it back down.\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nElira looked at him steadily, letting no Noctis logic slip between the words.\n\nElira: \"Then hear the rest of it too. Lucien measured a weapon today. He did not measure your worth.\"\n\nCael closed his eyes. For the first time since entering Aurelis, he did not apologize.\n\nA minute later, Rhea arrived down the corridor with a sealed docket under her arm. She checked Elira first, then Cael, then the glowing crystal overhead.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Noctis has already filed a terminology review.\"\n\nAira turned slowly.\n\nAira: \"Terminology.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"They object to entering *Dominion Severance* as hostile doctrine before full procedural verification.\"\n\nCassian, arriving half a pace behind Rhea, stared.\n\nCassian: \"They tried to make her pendant bleed.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Their preferred phrase is *unverified compatibility reaction*.\"\n\nSilence dropped over the corridor, heavy and sharp.\n\nThen Mira, clutching her green public copy half a step behind Rhea, spoke up.\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nIt was not loud, yet every head turned. Rhea looked down at her. Mira swallowed hard and held her ground.\n\nMira: \"Sorry. I mean, the public witness copy rejects that wording. We saw thirty windows lock onto Elira before her pendant reacted. We logged Lucien calling it a measurement. We logged Nera naming Severance grammar.\"\n\nAira stared at her in pure awe.\n\nAira: \"Mira Castellan, did you just procedurally tell Noctis to choke on a euphemism?\"\n\nMira went scarlet.\n\nMira: \"Hopefully not in those exact words.\"\n\nRhea took the green public copy and pressed her red seal right beside it.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Those exact words are unnecessary. The objection stands.\"\n\nLeon nudged Cael lightly with his elbow.\n\nLeon: \"See? Other people can refuse rotten definitions too.\"\n\nCael looked across at Mira.\n\nCael: \"Thank you.\"\n\nMira hugged the folio tight against her chest.\n\nMira: \"You're welcome.\"\n\nThen, quieter, eyes still fixed on the notice crystal:\n\nMira: \"I am tired of them naming things for everyone else.\"\n\n## Coda: Terminology\n\nMira copied Noctis's preferred phrase onto a fresh sheet exactly once: *Unverified compatibility reaction.*\n\nThe words sat across the vellum like smooth frost over a grave. She stared at them until quiet anger steadied her wrist, then wrote what her own eyes had seen directly underneath.\n\n*Windows turned toward Elira before impact. Pendant answered as mapped target. Frost followed a carved route, not an accident.*\n\nAt the next library table, three Green Circle students leaned over without pretending to study. One of them, her older brother Finn, with the same stubborn auburn curl and dark ink across both thumbs, tapped the margin and whispered, \"Can we sign that?\"\n\nMira glanced up at him, surprised. Green Circle students at Aurelis did not sign formal filings unless a higher rank invited them to hold the pen. It was not written in any rulebook, which made it worse; it was a habit everyone called manners.\n\nMira slid the page into the center of the table.\n\nMira: \"Only if you write what you saw, not what I wrote.\"\n\nBy sunset, seven crooked witness copies sat side by side. None matched word for word, which made them far harder to erase.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-09-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep10","label":"Episode 10","number":"10","file":"episode-10-cover.webp","fallback":"Crown Without Mercy","eyebrow":"Tournament Climax","recap":"","accent":["#ffd166","#ef476f","#111111"],"displayLabel":"Episode 10","displayNumber":"10","displayEyebrow":"Tournament Faultlines","arcLabel":"Tournament Faultlines","title":"The Clause That Smiled","body":"Noctis apologized. That was how we knew the next attack had already begun.\n\nThe apology arrived on crisp white parchment, carried by a Noctis courier in silver gloves with a face scrubbed blank of expression. It acknowledged \"procedural irregularity\" inside the autonomy field, accepted \"limited responsibility for relay ambiguity,\" and commended Aurelis for its \"continued commitment to student safety.\"\n\nAira read the first paragraph, flipped the sheet over, and inspected the back.\n\nAira: \"I was hoping the real message was written in blood. This is worse.\"\n\nShe held the parchment by one corner as though it might grow teeth.\n\nThe courier waited with the stiff patience of someone trained to turn into furniture while other people lost their temper. I recognized him a heartbeat late from the suspended bracket: Tovan Reiss, Noctis Red Circle close-combat reserve, reassigned as a messenger after the freeze as if hauling their trap across campus were just another match.\n\nRhea did not wave him away. She let the quiet stretch long enough for the room to notice Noctis had sent someone easy to ignore if Aurelis chose convenience. Then she met his eyes directly.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Your name.\"\n\nThe courier blinked.\n\nClerk: \"Pardon?\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"For the receipt record. Your name.\"\n\nHis silver-gloved fingers tightened on the leather delivery case.\n\nClerk: \"Tovan Reiss.\"\n\nRhea wrote it down in her own hand.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Tovan Reiss delivered the Noctis apology filing under visible strain.\"\n\nMagisterial training snapped his expression flat, half a second too late. Aira caught the twitch. So did Elira.\n\nElira stepped closer, careful not to block the doorway, placing herself between Noctis paperwork and the student carrying it.\n\nElira: \"Were you ordered to stay until we opened the seal?\"\n\nTovan's throat bobbed once.\n\nTovan: \"I was instructed to confirm receipt.\"\n\nRhea's pen hovered above the ledger.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Receipt confirmed.\"\n\nTovan did not move toward the door.\n\nNera spoke from the side wall.\n\nNera: \"He cannot leave until the paper activates or fails.\"\n\nTovan's eyes darted to her. Recognition flashed and vanished. Nera's jaw set.\n\nNera: \"Noctis courier compliance clause?\"\n\nTovan stayed silent.\n\nRhea pressed a bright red council seal directly between him and the parchment.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis confirms receipt and formally releases the courier from observation duty.\"\n\nTovan's shoulders dropped by a fraction of an inch. He bowed stiffly.\n\nTovan: \"Receipt confirmed.\"\n\nHe walked out too fast to look ceremonial.\n\nRhea dispatched a council aide behind him, not to question or escort him, only to verify that the compliance clause let go at the courtyard gate instead of waiting for an empty hallway. Ten minutes later, the aide returned carrying a single silver glove on a sealed brass tray.\n\nInside the palm, heat had scorched *Tovan Reiss* deep into the cloth lining.\n\nRhea stared down at the burned letters, then back at the pristine apology.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Add courier injury to the receipt docket.\"\n\nNoctis had tried to erase the person behind delivery procedure. The glove made the cost physical enough to outlast polite stationery. Beside the table, Mira wrote *Tovan Reiss* across her margin before anyone could reduce him back to *clerk*.\n\nNera studied the glove from a foot away without touching the tray.\n\nNera: \"His name serves as both release key and penalty log. If delivery failed, the lining would keep burning deeper until a superior accepted custody of the case.\"\n\nRhea sealed the glove in a separate case beside the parchment.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then the apology arrived with an injured witness attached. We answer both.\"\n\nBeside the scorched cloth, the white paper no longer looked clean.\n\nCassian watched the closed corridor door.\n\nCassian: \"They put leashes on the mail.\"\n\nOric spoke quietly from the bench.\n\nOric: \"They put leashes on everything and call it handling.\"\n\nRhea lifted the parchment under a hovering red seal.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"They want yesterday's interference to read like shared confusion.\"\n\nProfessor Halden stood at the tall window, watching Tovan cross the lower courtyard.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"No. They are making the apology admissible.\"\n\nProfessor Valen looked up from Elira's healer chart.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"As what?\"\n\nHalden turned back to the room.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Evidence that Noctis acts reasonably. It softens the next cruelty.\"\n\nLeon folded his arms across his chest.\n\nLeon: \"I hate that sentence.\"\n\nCassian: \"I hate that it sounds accurate.\"\n\nAt the side table, Elira sat with her collar turned back so the healers could check the frost mark below her throat. The white line had faded to a pale crescent, yet her dark pendant stayed weirdly dull. Listening, like a shield waiting for permission from a statute written by the enemy.\n\nElira caught me staring again.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nAira did not lift her eyes from the apology.\n\nAira: \"You two have said *I know* enough times that it has legally stopped meaning anything.\"\n\nThe corner of Elira's mouth curved.\n\nHaru: \"Noted.\"\n\nAira: \"Good. Replace it with full sentences.\"\n\nI let out a slow breath while the room waited.\n\nHaru: \"The pendant is not damaged. It is hesitating.\"\n\nProfessor Halden's eyes sharpened.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"A protective weave showing decision latency.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not a decision. A definition conflict.\"\n\nElira laid two fingers lightly over the dark crystal.\n\nElira: \"Because Noctis is trying to classify my bond with Haru as unlawful influence.\"\n\nNera nodded once from the wall.\n\nNera: \"Dominion Severance does not begin with a cut. It begins by relabeling.\"\n\nOric dragged both palms down his face.\n\nOric: \"If the grammar convinces the system that your protection counts as possession, the next defensive pulse can stall or turn inward.\"\n\nLeon stared at the pendant as if looking for a throat to punch inside grammar itself.\n\nLeon: \"Turn inward where?\"\n\nNera hesitated. Cael answered from his chair.\n\nCael: \"Into the person wearing it.\"\n\nEvery set of shoulders in the room drew tight around Elira. She did not flinch or pull back; she simply buttoned her collar over the crystal.\n\nElira: \"Then we stop the relabeling.\"\n\nRhea lowered her red seal over the apology.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Noctis has requested a joint clarification session in the old west transept. Neutral ground between the council tower and the tournament wing.\"\n\nCassian: \"Neutral ground usually means ground nobody bothered to fortify.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Correct.\"\n\nAira snatched up the floor schematic.\n\nAira: \"Then we fortify it properly.\"\n\nThe west transept had once linked Aurelis's oldest dueling chapel to the newer tournament halls. Nobody went down there now except archive faculty, truant students, and settling dust.\n\nBy midafternoon, Rhea had layered enough protections across the nave to offend the stonework. Red council seals braced every lancet window. Blue support anchors marked the floor tiles. Green Circle witnesses sat in pairs along the public gallery rail, each armed with a ledger and a partner assigned to keep them from being politely escorted out.\n\nValen arranged three historical folios across a stone lectern at center floor. Halden stood beside it like an unblinking warning. Along the eastern wall, Nera and Oric mapped Noctis grammar risks in heavy black chalk while Cael watched from a warded chair, healer seal bright on his wrist and Leon planted half a pace from his shoulder.\n\nThe transept resisted the black chalk. Ancient Aurelis stone kept soaking up the lines, softening jagged hooks into graceful flourishes that looked decorative and therefore useless. Nera redrew the same custody hook three times before snapping the chalk clean in half.\n\nNera: \"Stop making it pretty.\"\n\nOric handed her a fresh stick.\n\nOric: \"Aurelis has aesthetic objections to cruelty.\"\n\nNera: \"Aurelis can file them after it stops blurring the knife.\"\n\nProfessor Valen stepped over with a thin brass stylus and scored the mark into the stone before Nera traced over it.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Old buildings sometimes prefer tidy symbols to consequences.\"\n\nNera looked at the clean, ugly scratch.\n\nNera: \"Thank you.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Hold your thanks. The stone may hold a grudge.\"\n\nCael watched the exchange with a puzzled frown. Leon caught it.\n\nLeon: \"What?\"\n\nCael: \"At Noctis, if a wall rejected a mark, we assumed the student drew it wrong.\"\n\nLeon glanced at the scored stone.\n\nLeon: \"Maybe the wall was wrong.\"\n\nCael blinked at him.\n\nLeon: \"What? Masonry does not get automatic respect from me.\"\n\nCael's faint smile arrived a little faster this time, small yet real.\n\nAt the center of the nave, Elira stood beside me. She had insisted on standing there so Lucien could see his measurement had not shifted her a single step. That was the reason she gave Rhea. Once everyone else grew busy checking seals, she gave me the quieter truth.\n\nElira: \"I am afraid.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nOne eyebrow rose. I caught myself immediately.\n\nHaru: \"I am afraid too.\"\n\nShe nodded once, satisfied.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good?\"\n\nElira: \"Fear is less dangerous once we stop forcing it to wear competence.\"\n\nA dry smile tugged at my mouth.\n\nHaru: \"Professor Valen has been a terrible influence.\"\n\nElira: \"A useful one.\"\n\nThe far doors swung wide, and the Noctis delegation filed in.\n\nMagister Rusk led with two stone-faced clerks and four black-robed examiners. Lucien strolled behind them. He did not glance at me first; he took slow inventory of the nave, scanning lancet seals, anchor beads, chalk hooks, Green notebooks, and finally Elira.\n\nLucien: \"You have become remarkably difficult to speak with in private.\"\n\nElira: \"Correct.\"\n\nAira muttered from her support desk:\n\nAira: \"Strong opening.\"\n\nMagister Rusk laid the white apology flat across the center lectern.\n\nMagister Rusk: \"Noctis requests formal clarification to prevent further misunderstanding between protective doctrines.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis accepts clarification under full public witness conditions.\"\n\nLucien smiled warmly, ticking off each precaution with his eyes.\n\nLucien: \"Open gallery. Distributed authorship. No private isolation. No direct classification prompts. No relay shards. You really have been studying.\"\n\nAira: \"And yet you still showed up.\"\n\nLucien: \"Of course.\"\n\nHe raised one empty hand. No mana sparked. No pressure shifted across the tiles. Only a smooth, conversational request.\n\nLucien: \"I ask that Elira Vale define her relationship to Haru Aerlen for the official record.\"\n\nThe transept did not stir. That harmless quiet was the snare. On the surface it sounded purely political, awkward perhaps, nothing magical at all.\n\nRhea's eyes narrowed.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Denied as irrelevant.\"\n\nMagister Rusk smoothed one black cuff.\n\nMagister Rusk: \"Directly relevant to the primary witness pressure review.\"\n\nLucien kept his gaze steady on Elira.\n\nLucien: \"Is she his romantic partner, his witness, his protective anchor, his concealment collaborator, or an influenced subject?\"\n\nThe dark crystal beneath Elira's collar snapped ice-cold. Her face stayed composed, though my pulse slammed once against my ribs.\n\nAira stood abruptly.\n\nAira: \"Question compound and malicious.\"\n\nProfessor Halden spoke over her in the same breath.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It attempts forced categorization.\"\n\nProfessor Valen's voice followed, warm and steel-edged.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"And it repeats the oldest institutional error. Living people are rarely a single function.\"\n\nLucien looked genuinely pleased.\n\nLucien: \"Then let her state as much herself.\"\n\nElira stepped forward before Rhea could intercept, taking only one calm stride.\n\nElira: \"I am Haru's girlfriend.\"\n\nSmall, human ripples passed along the public rail. Mira wrote the words down, flushed pink at writing them down, and underlined them anyway because the record was the record.\n\nElira: \"I am also a witness to his choices. I am also a Yellow Circle student of Aurelis. I am also the person who saw Cael Nox treated as an expendable conduit and refused to pretend procedure made it clean.\"\n\nBeneath her collar, the pendant warmed slightly.\n\nElira: \"None of those truths cancels the others.\"\n\nLucien's smile thinned by a hair.\n\nLucien: \"And when those roles collide?\"\n\nElira: \"Then I answer as myself.\"\n\nSilver script flared across the transept tiles.\n\n**MULTIPLE ROLE AUTONOMY: ENTERED**\n\nAira's shoulders eased half an inch.\n\nThen Lucien spoke one quiet word.\n\nLucien: \"Good.\"\n\nThe hook did not strike Elira. It bit directly into the white apology resting on the stone lectern.\n\nThe parchment unfolded by itself. Every neat line of Noctis courtesy twisted and snapped into archaic Dominion script.\n\nNera shouted from the eastern wall:\n\nNera: \"Do not read it!\"\n\nToo late. The transept stone had already read it.\n\n**DOMINION CLARIFICATION CLAUSE ACCEPTED**\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS STATUS: VOLUNTARILY CONFIRMED**\n\n**SEVERANCE ELIGIBILITY: CONDITIONALLY OPEN**\n\nRhea drove her red council seals down onto the lectern.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Void the clause.\"\n\nThe floor pushed back against her. Lucien had brought no shard, no mirror, no overt weapon; he had handed Aurelis a polite apology that converted Elira's own honest answer into a legal foothold.\n\nCael shot to his feet. Leon caught his elbow to steady him.\n\nCael: \"That is the first hinge.\"\n\nNera went chalk-white, fingers clamping over her bare forearms.\n\nNera: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira's pendant flashed pure black against her throat, not from anything inside me, but from Dominion grammar forcing my own protective weave to turn against itself. Pain flashed sharp across her brow.\n\nBehind my ribs, Black Circle hammered against the inside of my restraint. Cold silver rings ground hard enough that the false Green crest beneath my wrist cuff burned, and the flagstone directly under my boot split with a crisp hairline crack.\n\nHalden caught the fracture instantly. So did Lucien. His pale eyes brightened with quiet delight.\n\nHaru: \"Enough.\"\n\nThe word dropped into the nave too soft and too heavy. Elira glanced sharply at me through her pain.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI caught her warning and locked both hands around the pressure inside my chest, forcing the fracture beneath my boot to stay a hairline.\n\nAira moved before my restraint could fray any further.\n\nAira: \"Mira!\"\n\nMira jumped at the rail.\n\nAira: \"Public record! What just happened?\"\n\nMira stared down at the glowing floor, the writhing parchment, Elira's pale face, and Lucien's calm smile. Her voice shook, yet she held her volume.\n\nMira: \"Elira Vale answered a personal question. Noctis converted her answer into a clause she never knowingly accepted!\"\n\nAira: \"Again! Louder!\"\n\nMira rose against the rail.\n\nMira: \"Elira Vale did not consent to Severance eligibility! Noctis turned testimony into a trap!\"\n\nAround her, Green Circle recorders repeated the charge raggedly and out of unison, different voices using their own plain words to defend the same fact. That uneven chorus hit harder than any chant. The silver tiles stuttered.\n\nRhea slammed her red seal down through the parchment on the lectern.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Clause contested by live public record!\"\n\nProfessor Valen threw open her stitched folio.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Multiple-role autonomy registered prior to clause conversion!\"\n\nProfessor Halden stepped beside the lectern.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"No informed consent. No clean hinge!\"\n\nAlong the eastern wall, Nera and Oric struck their chalk marks in unison.\n\nNera: \"Courtesy grammar exposed!\"\n\nOric: \"Dominion hook named!\"\n\nFlame caught the edge of the white apology, reducing polite Noctis paper to gray ash in two seconds.\n\nThe tiles let go of Elira's pendant. Her knees dipped; I stepped in and caught her shoulders because she let me.\n\nLucien watched my hands steady her, mildly amused.\n\nLucien: \"You are all becoming quite skilled at almost stopping things.\"\n\nFire licked up across Cassian's knuckles.\n\nCassian: \"Try something that does not hide behind stationery.\"\n\nLucien regarded him with faint politeness, then slid his gaze back to me.\n\nLucien: \"That would comfort you, wouldn't it? No. I prefer this. Haru Aerlen breaks open violence far too easily.\"\n\nA dry chill settled across the nave.\n\nLucien: \"So we will keep teaching the room to call love a liability, aid a disguise, and choice a binding clause.\"\n\nHe offered Elira a flawless formal bow.\n\nLucien: \"Thank you for confirming the first hinge.\"\n\nNoctis walked out through the high doors before Rhea could lawfully detain a single delegate. The clean, lawful exit was part of the blade.\n\nNobody cleared the transept after they left. Nobody trusted empty rooms anymore.\n\nRhea ordered the double doors propped open and the public gallery maintained while healers set up a folding privacy screen for Elira. Public enough to block quiet rewriting; shielded enough that Elira did not become a spectacle. An ugly compromise, like everything else keeping us upright.\n\nNo one touched the gray ash piled across the lectern. On Rhea's order, three seals ringed the pile: Rhea's red seal for custody, Aira's blue seal for sequence, and a green seal from Mira, which Mira had tried very hard to refuse until Rhea informed her that public witness evidence did not stop being official just because the person holding the pen had sweaty palms.\n\nMira's green seal came out lopsided. It leaned slightly left and pulsed off-beat against Rhea's crisp red geometry.\n\nIt was also the only seal the ash did not try to blacken.\n\nProfessor Halden stood motionless beside the lectern for nearly a minute, staring at that crooked green ring. Aira caught his expression.\n\nAira: \"Professor, you have the face of a man about to ruin everyone's afternoon with an observation.\"\n\nHalden did not blink.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The residue recognizes institutional authority as something to contest.\"\n\nMira went pale.\n\nMira: \"And mine?\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It does not appear to know what to do with a witness who claims no power over it.\"\n\nMira stared down at the wobbly green glow.\n\nMira: \"That sounds useful.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It is.\"\n\nHe paused, as if the next instruction cost his scholarly instincts real pain.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not improve the seal. Its imperfection is evidentiary.\"\n\nMira went very still, then wrote that down with a hand that shook noticeably less by the period.\n\nBehind the partial screen, two healers checked the mark at Elira's throat. The frost crescent had not spread, yet it had deepened, dark and fixed like ink soaked into vellum. Under her fingers, the pendant radiated a dull, feverish heat.\n\nAs the healers worked, a single black fleck lifted off the lectern ash, drifted into Mira's uneven green ring, and dropped dead without leaving a smudge.\n\nMira let out a small, indignant squeak.\n\nMira: \"It tried again.\"\n\nRhea stepped over to inspect the edge.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"And failed.\"\n\nMira glanced from the gray pile down to her trembling fingers.\n\nMira: \"I want that entered into the log.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Write it.\"\n\nMira bent over her folio and printed in bold capitals:\n\n**THE ASH TESTED PUBLIC WITNESS SEAL AGAIN. FAILED AGAIN.**\n\nNot grand prose. We did not need grand prose; Lucien brought plenty of that. Small true sentences were harder to bend.\n\nI stood three paces back from the healer screen because Elira had asked me not to hover. Three paces felt too close to pretend calm and too far to be of any use.\n\nAira walked over holding two clipped sheets and a stoneware cup.\n\nAira: \"Drink.\"\n\nHaru: \"Is that an order?\"\n\nAira: \"Yes.\"\n\nI took the cup and looked down. Clear water, not tea.\n\nAira: \"You looked ready to accept hot tea and forget how swallowing works.\"\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira: \"Do not make it sentimental. I have columns to finish.\"\n\nShe dropped onto the stone step with her back propped against the lectern, dividing a fresh sheet into four crisp boxes: *spoken question*, *hidden function*, *witness correction*, *remaining damage*. She stared at the fourth box far too long.\n\nCassian lowered himself onto the step beside her with less grace than usual.\n\nCassian: \"You missed a column.\"\n\nAira: \"Unless it holds a fix, I hate it.\"\n\nHe lifted the pencil right out of her fingers, and she let him, which proved how exhausted she was. Cassian printed neatly across the margin:\n\n**PEOPLE STILL HERE**\n\nAira stared at it, then took the pencil back.\n\nAira: \"That is not a column. That is emotional vandalism.\"\n\nCassian: \"Still accurate.\"\n\nBeneath his line, Aira added:\n\n**ANNOYINGLY RELEVANT**\n\nThen she rapped her pencil twice against the stone step to pull the room's attention.\n\nAira: \"Status. Short words. No doom poetry.\"\n\nNera answered first.\n\nNera: \"First hinge opened, then contested.\"\n\nOric: \"Not closed.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Legally disputed. Not nullified.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The Foundation logged Noctis's clause conversion, yet *primary witness status* remains active on the record.\"\n\nLeon: \"So we won badly.\"\n\nCassian: \"Beats losing gracefully.\"\n\nProfessor Valen closed her folio gently.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"We kept them from stealing consent. We did not stop them from mapping the route.\"\n\nNear the rail, Cael stood rigid with both arms wrapped tight around his ribs. Nera walked over and stopped half a pace away.\n\nNera: \"The first hinge was not your fault.\"\n\nCael answered on pure reflex.\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nHalf the transept turned to look at him. He realized what had slipped out and flushed.\n\nCael: \"I mean...\"\n\nAira jabbed her pencil at him from the steps.\n\nAira: \"Banned phrase.\"\n\nCael took a steadying breath.\n\nCael: \"I am trying to believe that.\"\n\nSomething quiet eased behind Nera's eyes.\n\nNera: \"Better.\"\n\nOric stepped up beside them, black chalk dusting his cuffs.\n\nOric: \"I am not trying yet. I can say it for Cael. If I say it for myself, it feels like a lie.\"\n\nNera's jaw tightened, yet she held her tongue. Professor Valen answered gently instead.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Then do not state it as belief. State it as record. The first hinge was not Oric Sen's fault.\"\n\nOric flinched.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"A record can hold the truth steady until belief catches up.\"\n\nUp on the gallery rail, Mira entered the sentence into her log. Oric saw her pen move, opened his mouth to protest, and let it go.\n\nStill, Cael had not stopped staring across the nave at Elira behind the folding screen, not like Lucien, like a man watching a timber beam about to catch flame. Elira noticed through the gap.\n\nElira: \"Cael.\"\n\nHe flinched.\n\nElira: \"Come here.\"\n\nHe hesitated until Leon gave him a blunt nudge between the shoulder blades.\n\nLeon: \"She said come here, not rehearse a tragedy.\"\n\nCael walked over and stopped two paces from the screen, eyes pinned to the flagstones.\n\nCael: \"The second hinge needs you to step into the cut.\"\n\nElira kept her voice level.\n\nElira: \"Then I will recognize the cut before I move.\"\n\nCael's head snapped up.\n\nCael: \"That is not enough.\"\n\nLeon: \"Cael.\"\n\nCael whipped toward him with sudden, unguarded heat.\n\nCael: \"It is not!\"\n\nThe nave went dead still at the force in his voice. Cael heard the echo and instantly folded inward.\n\nCael: \"Sorry.\"\n\nElira rose from the healer stool. Both healers protested; she ignored them with polite, immovable calm.\n\nElira: \"Do not apologize for warning me.\"\n\nCael swallowed hard, fingers locking over his wrapped wrist.\n\nCael: \"Dominion Severance cannot kill a primary witness just for standing in the room. It works because decent people move. It arranges the floor so the right step counts as the fatal one. And you will take it.\"\n\nMy fingers tightened around my stoneware cup. Inside, the water stopped rippling entirely, frozen glassy-flat by the sudden pull behind my ribs. That scared me more than spilling it would have. Cael had not misread Elira; he understood her far too well.\n\nProfessor Valen glanced toward the sealed doors of the old chapel at the far end of the transept.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"That fits the buried record. The safeguard's original witnesses were not dragged away at first. They were convinced that stepping back would protect the candidate.\"\n\nHalden's expression sharpened.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Lucien has inverted the trap. He wants Elira to step forward, then declare that closeness unlawful.\"\n\nAira scribbled the line down hard enough to score the paper underneath.\n\nAira: \"Horrible. Clear. Usable.\"\n\nRhea pivoted to Nera.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"What legally triggers the second hinge?\"\n\nNera's fingers brushed the pale bands where her binding rings used to sit.\n\nNera: \"A willing obstruction.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Define obstruction.\"\n\nOric answered flatly.\n\nOric: \"Anything Dominion grammar can frame as interfering with lawful correction.\"\n\nCassian let out a dry, humorless laugh.\n\nCassian: \"Lawful correction being execution in a pressed coat.\"\n\nNera did not blink.\n\nNera: \"Yes.\"\n\nCael looked back at Elira.\n\nCael: \"If Noctis strikes Haru, you will step in. If they come for me, you will step in. If they threaten the Green rail, you will step in.\"\n\nElira rested one steady hand on the edge of the folding screen.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nCael's face twisted.\n\nCael: \"Do not say yes like that.\"\n\nElira took one step closer.\n\nElira: \"Cael, I cannot promise to turn into a worse person just so Lucien runs out of angles.\"\n\nThe truth hit him squarely where fear had been chewing.\n\nProfessor Valen stepped forward from the lectern, keeping respectful distance from the healer screen.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Elira, only if you choose, give us one line for the official file before we close this session. What did Lucien's clause try to take from you?\"\n\nQuiet settled across the stone. Elira stared at the gray ash sitting inside Mira's uneven green ring. Exhaustion lined the corners of her eyes, along with sharp anger that exhaustion itself was one more thing outsiders might twist against her.\n\nElira: \"It tried to force me to pick which part of myself counted as admissible.\"\n\nAira's pencil paused. Elira pulled the folding screen completely aside so every witness along the gallery could see her face.\n\nElira: \"I saw Cael being hurt. I love Haru. I am Yellow Circle. I was frightened. I chose to speak anyway. Every single one of those facts is true. Lucien wanted one truth to disqualify the rest. I am angry that I have to explain loving someone did not make me stupid.\"\n\nNo gilded legal wording. Just Elira, pale from frostbite and furious at the premise.\n\nElira: \"Put that in the log.\"\n\nMira wrote every word without dropping a syllable. Nothing ancient flared in approval, yet Rhea pressed her red seal right across the page.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Entered by witness request. And now we answer the second hinge: Aurelis defines obstruction before Noctis gets the chance.\"\n\nAira snapped her fingers once.\n\nAira: \"Exactly. If Elira intervenes, she moves under documented witness authority rather than private emotion. We build a formal consent framework around every rescue step.\"\n\nLeon stared at her.\n\nLeon: \"Can you phrase that so it sounds less dead?\"\n\nAira: \"I can make it sound warm or make it legally bulletproof. Pick one.\"\n\nLeon: \"Bulletproof.\"\n\nAira: \"Remarkable growth.\"\n\nProfessor Valen looked around the nave, eyes steady.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"We pair that with the older restoration record. Before Noctis tries to close the cut, Aurelis puts Witness Restoration on the public table.\"\n\nNera's hands went still at her sides.\n\nNera: \"Witness Restoration failed.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Because officials pulled the witnesses out of the circle.\"\n\nOric spoke cautiously.\n\nOric: \"You want to invoke an incomplete doctrine as a shield?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Not invoke. Name and record. Refuse the premise that the only answer to fatal harm is spending one expendable conduit.\"\n\nCael's gaze dropped to his boots. Too slow to hide it. I saw the calculation cross his face, and Aira beat me to him by two steps.\n\nShe planted herself in front of his knees.\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nCael: \"I did not say a word.\"\n\nAira: \"Your face just filed paperwork.\"\n\nHe tried to turn his chin away; she sidestepped right back into his line of sight.\n\nAira: \"Hear me very clearly, Cael Nox. You are not a patch. You do not get to volunteer yourself as spare architecture because Noctis trained you to feel impolite while alive.\"\n\nCael's mouth opened, empty of any defense.\n\nLeon stepped up behind his shoulder, blunt and immovable.\n\nLeon: \"If you treat dying like a backup plan, I am going to become deeply unreasonable about it.\"\n\nCassian: \"All of us will. Some of us with significantly better hair.\"\n\nElira laid one warm hand gently over Cael's shoulder.\n\nElira: \"Let us protect you without making you pay for it afterward.\"\n\nFor one unguarded second, Cael looked his actual age.\n\nCael: \"I am trying.\"\n\nBefore Rhea's wax finished cooling on the docket, footsteps hurried down the gallery stairs. A Green Circle student stepped up directly behind Mira: older, broader through the shoulders, the same stubborn auburn curl and ink smudged across both thumbs.\n\nMira spun around.\n\nMira: \"Finn?\"\n\nFinn Castellan held out a green-bound roll of parchment tied with plain twine.\n\nFinn: \"Before anyone gets nervous, this is not a loyalty petition for Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nSeveral faculty members stiffened and leaned closer.\n\nFinn: \"It is forty-three Green Circle signatures requesting that the Foundation attach our witness logs instead of binning them as redundant.\"\n\nHe unrolled the top sheet. Six heavy red rejection stamps slashed across the margins:\n\n**DUPLICATE OBSERVATION / LOWER-RANK COPY / NO ADDITIONAL WEIGHT**\n\nFinn tapped a line inked neatly beneath the stamps.\n\nFinn: \"Yesterday an archive clerk handed back six Green copies and told us to leave timing logs to higher circles. Two of our copies caught the ash shifting before the official council seal even engaged. The senior sheet missed it completely.\"\n\nSurprise vanished from Mira's face, replaced instantly by hot anger.\n\nFinn: \"We are not claiming Green witnesses are infallible because we wear green. We are saying a court that throws our pages away before comparing timestamps is not checking accuracy at all.\"\n\nHe set the green scroll beside Mira's uneven seal on the lectern.\n\nFinn: \"Attach the pages. Then argue with what they record.\"\n\nForty-three tiny preservation charms lit softly along the twine, each etched in a different student's uneven handwriting.\n\nRhea beckoned the Foundation Archive Clerk forward from the gallery desk and opened the official timing ledger beside Finn's six stamped pages. Professor Valen leaned over her shoulder, checking line against line while the room held its breath.\n\nValen tapped two timestamps with her brass stylus.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Both discrepancies are material. The ash moved three seconds prior to the senior log entry.\"\n\nThe archive clerk swallowed hard and dipped his pen.\n\nRhea stamped her crimson council seal across all six rejection marks.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"All six Green copies attach formally as contested sequence evidence. And before this recess opens, I am entering three binding breach rulings into the Foundation ledger.\"\n\nHer seal flashed onto the transept wall:\n\n**NOCTIS APOLOGY: HOSTILE CLAUSE CARRIER CONFIRMED**\n\n**PRIVATE CLARIFICATION REQUESTS: SUSPENDED**\n\n**NERA VAIL / ORIC SEN: PROTECTED TECHNICAL WITNESSES**\n\nFinn's shoulders finally sagged. Mira caught the edge of the scroll as his grip loosened. Looking at the forty-three crooked charms glowing on the twine, I did not feel praised or sheltered by a cult. I felt the plain weight of ordinary students insisting their own eyes belonged in the room.\n\nThe rulings did not erase the Severance hinge already carved into Elira's protection, yet they slammed one polite door shut in Noctis's face.\n\nRhea called a five-minute recess, letting the nave breathe without turning off the wards.\n\nI walked toward the far end of the transept, drawn by the tall oak doors of the old chapel. A dull chain of gold light crossed the handles. Through the timber I could taste ancient Aurelis masonry, older than the council tower, laced with the same faint metallic chill that belonged to Circle Zero architecture.\n\nElira came up beside me, quiet.\n\nHaru: \"You are supposed to be sitting.\"\n\nElira: \"You are supposed to be drinking water.\"\n\nI glanced down at the untouched stoneware cup still gripped in my hand. She took it from my fingers, drank half, and handed it back. My chest loosened far more than one sip of water had any right to explain.\n\nElira: \"There. Now we have both disobeyed instructions equally.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira will notice.\"\n\nElira: \"Aira can track barometric changes in other people's guilt.\"\n\nI looked back at the chained oak doors.\n\nHaru: \"Cael believes you are going to die.\"\n\nSomething quiet crossed Elira's face, not panic, just the plain weight of fear spoken aloud.\n\nElira: \"Cael was raised by people who treated death like tidy arithmetic.\"\n\nHaru: \"That does not prove him wrong.\"\n\nShe turned fully to face me.\n\nElira: \"No. Yet if the only way to survive Lucien is swearing I will stand still while someone else takes a blade, then he has already hollowed me out.\"\n\nBehind my ribs, Black Circle stirred once against my control. I pressed it down hard.\n\nHaru: \"I do not know how to stand beside you and watch you choose danger.\"\n\nElira reached out and laced her fingers through mine.\n\nElira: \"Then learn beside me.\"\n\nThe gold chain across the chapel doors answered her skin before mine. Warm light flared down the oak seams. Back at the lectern, Valen's stitched folio snapped open by itself.\n\nRhea spun toward us.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Nobody move.\"\n\nAira looked from the parting doors to our joined hands.\n\nAira: \"Respectfully, President, too late.\"\n\nThe gold chain dissolved into drifting sparks, and archaic script burned bright across the oak lintel:\n\n**WITNESS RESTORATION RECORD ACCESSIBLE**\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS PRESENCE CONFIRMED**\n\n**SAFEGUARD RESPONSE NEARBY**\n\nHalden's gaze snapped to me. He did not speak the word the door had skirted around. Good.\n\nThe heavy doors swung inward on silent hinges.\n\nThe chamber beyond did not look like any chapel in modern Aurelis. Seven weathered stone seats curved in a half-moon around a broad, shallow basin of silver-black water. Hanging in the air above the pool floated a fractured schematic of linked circles: Green, Blue, Red, Yellow, and three scraped blank gaps where older doctrine had been gouged out of the construct.\n\nOne of those blank spaces made my eyes ache the moment I looked at it. Something behind my ribs knew the exact curve of what had been cut away.\n\nCael stopped at the stone threshold. The healer seal on his wrist flickered hard. Across the mirror-still basin, the silver-black water reflected him for one sharp heartbeat as a smaller boy kneeling on Noctis stone, before shifting back to who stood here now: thin, frightened, alive.\n\nOld script rose through the water, pulling pieces from every reflection ringing the rim. Cael's shadow supplied *Votive*. Elira's supplied *Return*. Mine left only a blind dark gap the water refused to read. Then the surface shivered and drew the rest out of the room around us: Nera's technical dread, Oric's scarred memory, Aira's refusal to let words hide cost, Rhea's rigid order, Mira's stubborn ledger, Leon's blunt fury, Cassian's banked flame, Valen's grief, Halden's discipline.\n\n**VOTIVE RETURN: DOMINION VARIANT DETECTED**\n\n**STATUS: SINGLE-LIFE TRANSFER**\n\n**AURELIS COUNTERDOCTRINE: WITNESS RESTORATION**\n\n**STATUS: INCOMPLETE**\n\nThe chamber was not merely an archive; it was a balance scale reading pressure in real time.\n\nAira edged closer to the rim, arms folded tight under her ribs.\n\nAira: \"Can we ask it a direct question?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Carefully.\"\n\nAira stared down into the dark silver surface.\n\nAira: \"Define *incomplete*.\"\n\nThe water dimmed. Instead of text, a scene surfaced beneath the ripples: seven figures reaching toward a body of bright light while robed Aurelis officials hauled two of them backward out of the ring. One witness vanished. The light fractured, edges turning ink-black. A second witness vanished. The image snapped off before the end.\n\nMira whispered from behind Aira's elbow:\n\nMira: \"It does not want to show the finish.\"\n\nProfessor Valen kept her eyes steady on the rippling dark.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"No. It wants us to understand why the finish happened.\"\n\nProfessor Halden studied the broken rings overhead.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Witness Restoration did not fail because the spell was impossible.\"\n\nNera finished the line, voice dry and thin.\n\nNera: \"It failed because someone broke the witness ring mid-cast.\"\n\nCael stared into the black silver.\n\nCael: \"So Noctis kept the half that spends one life.\"\n\nOric's fists clenched at his sides.\n\nOric: \"And threw away the half that kept anyone from having to die.\"\n\nNera stepped right up to the wet stone lip as if pulled by a wire.\n\nNera: \"What is missing?\"\n\nSeven recessed handprints flared around the rim of the pool, lighting one by one: Green, Blue, Red, Yellow, Silver, Blank, Blank.\n\nProfessor Valen translated the archaic border aloud.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Restoration requires presence, consent, distributed burden, and a living path back.\"\n\nAira let out a small breath.\n\nAira: \"That almost sounds like hope.\"\n\nThe water answered immediately:\n\n**WARNING: WITHOUT COMPLETE DOCTRINE, FATAL RESTORATION DEFAULTS TO CONDUIT TRANSFER**\n\nThe warmth drained out of the air.\n\nCael stared at the warning. I took one quick step toward his shoulder.\n\nHaru: \"Do not.\"\n\nHe did not flinch. Every time he stopped flinching at my warning, the fear in my chest grew sharper.\n\nCael: \"I am only reading.\"\n\nElira moved smoothly to his other side.\n\nElira: \"Then read with us.\"\n\nShe pressed her palm onto the Yellow print. Warm gold flared through the groove.\n\nAira set her hand over Blue. Rhea laid hers over Red. Mira edged forward beside Aira, lowered a trembling palm onto Green, and looked ready to apologize to the ancient stone for sweating on it.\n\nLeon stepped up to the first blank print. A brisk silver pulse bounced his palm back two inches.\n\nLeon: \"Rude.\"\n\nCassian tried the same groove and got bounced just as neatly.\n\nCassian: \"Ruder.\"\n\nHalden looked from the remaining stone grooves to me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It is not sorting by rank.\"\n\nProfessor Valen nodded.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"It is sorting by function.\"\n\nNera knelt and placed her bare hand over Silver. The stone warmed and accepted her. Oric hovered his palm over the first blank groove; the stone hesitated a beat, then lit a stubborn pale gray.\n\nOnly the final blank print remained dark. Cael stared at it. Nobody ordered him to touch it. Nobody grabbed his arm to stop him either.\n\nAfter a long silence, Cael laid his uninjured hand onto the final groove.\n\nThe entire pool snapped pure black.\n\nDeep behind my ribs, Black Circle surged so violently that the false Green crest around my wrist beneath my cuff flickered almost out of sight. Cold iron rushed up my throat. Beside me, Elira's fingers snapped around my hand, grounding me before the disguise broke.\n\nThe black water did not expose my name. Instead, it threw one bright, heartbreaking vision into the air: seven figures linked around a wounded glow, pulling a life back together without spending a single soul alone. Then the vision tore down the center.\n\n**DOCTRINE DAMAGED**\n\n**FINAL PATH UNKNOWN**\n\n**SAFEGUARD MUST NOT ACT ALONE**\n\nThe seven handprints stayed lit around the black basin, holding us in a partial answer that stung worse than outright refusal.\n\nAira stared at the glowing rim.\n\nAira: \"Can we stabilize the gap?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Perhaps.\"\n\nAira: \"I deeply resent *perhaps* inside lethal architecture.\"\n\nHalden circled the outer stones carefully without stepping across any inlaid line.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The construct is sheared off at the final return phase. Presence, consent, and distributed burden all register. The living path back is missing.\"\n\nLeon crossed his arms.\n\nLeon: \"Translate that into danger.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"If someone falls under Severance pressure, Aurelis's old counterdoctrine knows one life should not be spent to save another, yet it may lack the mechanism to stop conduit transfer from triggering anyway.\"\n\nLeon: \"Worse, yet clearer.\"\n\nNera crouched beside Cael's glowing handprint without touching the stone.\n\nNera: \"Noctis did not merely lose the distributed burden lines.\"\n\nOric: \"Or fail to copy them?\"\n\nNera shook her head.\n\nNera: \"Look at the grammar seams. Too smooth. Someone excised them on purpose.\"\n\nProfessor Valen's voice dropped low.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Dominion doctrine did not simply drift apart from Aurelis.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"It was edited.\"\n\nThe black pool answered with one slow, heavy ripple, less confirmation than old grief.\n\nCael pulled his hand back off the stone rim. Black light clung to his fingertips for a heartbeat before fading. He tucked both hands tight against his coat.\n\nCael: \"Can it be rebuilt?\"\n\nEvery gaze turned to Valen. She looked down at the dark water and shook her head.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Not in a single night.\"\n\nCael nodded once, far too calmly. Elira caught his eyes immediately.\n\nElira: \"Remember what you promised five minutes ago.\"\n\nCael's jaw tightened before he met her gaze honestly.\n\nCael: \"I am trying.\"\n\nThat was how deep Noctis had written into him.\n\nRhea lifted her palm from the Red print and broke the heavy quiet.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"We log every handprint and seam right now, before the next session opens.\"\n\nAira blinked at her.\n\nAira: \"Right now?\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Especially right now. If Noctis tries to paint intervention as misconduct, we hold dated proof that Aurelis uncovered a non-sacrificial counterdoctrine before the crisis started.\"\n\nProfessor Halden inclined his head once.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Pre-event evidence.\"\n\nLeon scrubbed both hands over his face.\n\nLeon: \"I hate that I genuinely understand why paperwork matters now.\"\n\nCassian: \"Careful. That is how Rhea recruits.\"\n\nRhea ignored them both and nodded toward Mira, still standing right beside the Green print.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Mira. Sketch the rim and phase shifts before the light fades.\"\n\nMira protested weakly that she could not draw. Valen informed her that accuracy outranked beauty. Within two minutes, Mira produced the most aggressively annotated diagram I had ever seen, complete with crooked arrows, lopsided hand outlines, and a boxed note beside the black-water surge reading: **POOL GOT DRAMATIC HERE**.\n\nAira peered over her shoulder.\n\nAira: \"Leave that label untouched.\"\n\nMira: \"Seriously?\"\n\nAira: \"It communicates the sequence.\"\n\nProfessor Halden winced.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It is imprecise.\"\n\nMira raised her eraser. Halden let out a long, pained sigh.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not erase original witness phrasing.\"\n\nMira froze. A slow, dangerous smile spread across Aira's face.\n\nAira: \"Professor.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not.\"\n\nAira: \"Did you just endorse messy field notes?\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I endorsed evidentiary integrity.\"\n\nAira: \"Messy evidentiary integrity.\"\n\nHalden stared up at the vaulted stone ceiling as if requesting patience from three centuries of dead founders. Unhelpfully, the chapel offered none.\n\nBeside the rail, Cael watched Mira ink a tiny arrow around the word *DRAMATIC*, and for one quiet heartbeat the haunted tightness eased around his eyes. Not joyful, just present. Elira saw it and stayed silent, letting him keep the first bearable breath he had taken all afternoon.\n\nOnce Rhea sealed Mira's sketch, Professor Halden lingered alone beside the rim. For once his notebook stayed shut. His gaze lifted to the blank scraped space hovering above the pool where the erased ring belonged.\n\nBeneath my sleeve, the hidden circle felt his attention like fingers hovering an inch off a locked latch. Halden did not reach for it. That quiet discipline had become a conversation between us all on its own.\n\nAfter a pause, he spoke without turning his head toward me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"If this breaks into public view, the institution will move faster than the people inside it.\"\n\nElira stepped quietly up to my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"If what breaks into public view?\"\n\nHalden flicked one careful glance toward the missing ring.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The fact that Aurelis's oldest architecture recognizes a category modern law cannot safely name.\"\n\nAira looked up from Mira's sealed sketch.\n\nAira: \"That was an impressively elaborate way to avoid saying a noun.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Yes.\"\n\nHe adjusted his spectacles and cleared his throat.\n\nAira: \"I respect the restraint and resent the stress.\"\n\nHalden finally turned his eyes directly onto me.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"If the coming session forces disclosure, Haru, do not allow the first official sentence about what you are to be drafted by someone who wants ownership.\"\n\nThe air in the chapel went cooler. Rhea took the warning as law.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then public sequence comes first.\"\n\nProfessor Valen took it as history.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"And witness proximity.\"\n\nElira took it as a person, slipping her warm fingers firmly around mine.\n\nElira: \"And Haru staying Haru.\"\n\nNo grand promise fit the room, so I gave them the only honest one I had.\n\nHaru: \"I will try.\"\n\nHalden gave one brief nod.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Trying may be the only honest preparation left.\"\n\nBefore a single one of us reached the chapel doorway, the tall notice crystal mounted beside the arch blazed sharp, blinding white.\n\n**INTER-NATIONAL SCHOOLS COUNCIL NOTICE**\n\n**INVESTIGATOR APPOINTED: MAGISTRA SENNA VOSS**\n\n**COUNCIL REGISTRY: NO DECLARED NOCTIS DELEGATION TIE**\n\n**AUTHORITY: OBSERVE, CLASSIFY PROCEDURAL RISK, SUSPEND PROCEEDINGS IF INSTITUTIONALLY DESTABILIZING**\n\n**ARRIVAL: DAWN**\n\nThe surname *Voss* cut across the chapel like an icy draft beneath a cellar door.\n\nRhea read the arrival line aloud, voice iron-flat.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Magistra Senna Voss arrives at dawn.\"\n\nProfessor Halden's face darkened immediately.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Voss. Like Lucien.\"\n\nProfessor Valen squinted at the glowing registry tag.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Common enough surname across the northern provinces. Her appointment sits under Council Registry, not the Noctis delegation.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Does not mean *not*.\"\n\nRhea scanned the white script twice.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Council investigator. Technically outside Noctis command.\"\n\nAira leaned close to the crystal glare.\n\nAira: \"That is either reassuring or the exact disclaimer a legal clerk adds when everyone already knows the obvious objection.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Either way, Noctis will spend dawn convincing her Aurelis is the hazard.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Sent to contain the situation.\"\n\nProfessor Valen kept her eyes on the dark silver pool.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Or sent to witness it before containment becomes another polite word for erasure.\"\n\nAira's jaw set.\n\nAira: \"Then we make her watch long enough to see who keeps holding the knife.\"\n\nBefore the white glare even dimmed from our eyes, the stone beneath our boots shuddered.\n\nOutside in the west transept, every notice crystal rang at once in a sharp, overlapping chime. Heavy footsteps sprinted across the nave, and Rhea's council aide burst into the chapel doorway, chest heaving.\n\nAide: \"President Valmont! Noctis just filed the final Foundation activity!\"\n\nRhea did not flinch.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Name it.\"\n\nThe aide swallowed hard.\n\nAide: \"Foundation Live Activity. Wounded City Protocol. Scheduled beneath this chapel at noon.\"\n\nColor drained clean out of Nera's face. Beside her, Oric shut his eyes. Standing back from the pool rim, Cael locked his wrapped hand hard against his ribs, motionless.\n\nAira breathed out the two words nobody else wanted to speak.\n\nAira: \"Second hinge?\"\n\nThe black water in the basin answered before the modern crystal could.\n\n**DOMINION SEVERANCE CONDITIONS LIKELY**\n\nOutside the archway, the blazing council crystal added the formal summons beneath it:\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS PRESSURE AUTHORIZED**\n\n**PARTICIPANTS: HARU AERLEN / ELIRA VALE / CAEL NOX / WITNESS NETWORK**\n\nLucien had chosen the next room.\n\nAnd this time, the entire field had been engineered so that saving someone counted as the wound.\n\n## Coda: Halden's Blank Space\n\nProfessor Halden dreamed of the missing ring and woke furious with himself for dreaming.\n\nScholars were trained to distrust symbols that arrived too neatly. A blank gap in a damaged schematic was not proof of a concealed Circle. It was absence: fire damage, scraped pigment, archival decay.\n\nAnd yet.\n\nBefore dawn, he walked back into the locked archive without an assistant, a lamp, or a requisition slip. He laid four modern classification seals across the worktable: Green, Blue, Red, Yellow.\n\nThe ancient schematic accepted none of them.\n\nIt did not reject them either.\n\nThat unsettled him worse. Rejection would have been an answer.\n\nHalden drew a fresh sheet of foolscap toward him and wrote a single sentence he had no intention of filing:\n\n*If Black Circle is not above Yellow but outside the ladder, every law built as a ladder is already mis-aimed.*\n\nHe folded the page once and tucked it deep inside his coat pocket.\n\nThen he straightened his spectacles and went down to the morning hall to teach first-years how not to lie with diagrams.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-10-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep11","label":"Episode 11","number":"11","file":"episode-11-cover.webp","fallback":"The Record That Knew Him","eyebrow":"Foundation Record","recap":"","accent":["#111111","#7bdff2","#f7d794"],"displayLabel":"Episode 11","displayNumber":"11","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"Wounded City Protocol","body":"Wounded City Protocol opened beneath the old chapel at noon.\n\nThat timing was the first insult.\n\nNoctis had spent enough of the Foundation Trial losing in rooms where records could answer back. The Protocol was their reply: move the argument from testimony into live crisis, then dare Aurelis to prove its rescue ethics under full public light. Even with Council Investigator Senna Voss sequestered in the morning record office since dawn, Lucien did not ask for a closed floor.\n\nHe wanted an audience. Valemere. Two minor academy delegations that had lingered past the tournament to watch the Foundation Trial. Selected Aurelis faculty. Student Council recorders. Green Circle public witnesses. Noctis called it transparency. Rhea called it leverage.\n\nAira called it \"setting the room on fire and inviting people to admire the smoke.\"\n\nThe Foundation accepted the expanded gallery because Wounded City Protocol was, on paper, an exercise in public rescue ethics. Refusing observers would have made Aurelis look afraid of its own doctrine. Lucien understood optics.\n\nSo Rhea made optics answer back.\n\nEvery observer had to sign a witness-conduct sheet before stepping onto the gallery stair. No private relay tools. No unsupervised mirrors. No delegation correction phrases. No removal of notes. No turning away from harm and claiming uncertainty afterward.\n\nThat last condition made several adults stiffen. Mira noticed.\n\nShe stood beside the iron archway with a stack of green-edged forms and a face that looked ready to drop through the flagstones rather than challenge a visiting examiner.\n\nWhen the first examiner tried to wave the form aside, Mira's knees shook.\n\nHer hand did not move.\n\nMira: \"Public witness terms require a signature.\"\n\nExaminer: \"I am here as faculty, not a student.\"\n\nMira looked down at the sheet, then back up at him.\n\nMira: \"Then your handwriting is probably better.\"\n\nThe examiner stared. Rhea, three paces away, said nothing at all, letting the council badge at her shoulder hold the silence. The examiner took the pen and signed.\n\nMira's shoulders dropped only after he vanished down the stair.\n\nAira drifted past behind her and murmured:\n\nAira: \"Excellent use of stationery.\"\n\nMira whispered back:\n\nMira: \"I thought I was going to faint.\"\n\nAira: \"Most civic institutions are held together by people refusing to faint until the ink dries.\"\n\nThe Foundation chamber below the chapel unfolded out of ancient stone and silver mist. Paved streets rose from the empty floor. Doorways split open along blank walls. Mullioned windows formed with no rooms behind them, and a crooked bell tower leaned out over a square that had never existed yet somehow looked remembered.\n\nSeven wounded markers stood at the center: neither wooden dummies nor stolen illusions of people we knew. That would have been too crude after yesterday. Each marker was an outline woven from pale light, flickering with a distinct injury: channel collapse, frost binding, mana starvation, memory echo, pressure burn, relay hook, and one empty outline carrying no visible wound at all.\n\nThe seventh made my teeth ache.\n\nAira took one look at it and hated it on sight.\n\nAira: \"An invisible wound marker. Wonderful. We are being tested by architecture with a sadistic streak.\"\n\nRhea took her post on the command threshold, a red council seal hovering beside her shoulder.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Conditions?\"\n\nThe Foundation's voice stayed level as it echoed down from the bell tower.\n\nFoundation: \"Wounded City Protocol tests response priority under conflicting witness obligations. Participants must extract all viable markers to safe zones while maintaining witness autonomy and preventing unlawful isolation.\"\n\nLeon flexed his hands.\n\nLeon: \"That almost sounds fair.\"\n\nNera's reply was flat.\n\nNera: \"It is not.\"\n\nLucien stepped onto the far side of the square with Magister Rusk and three Noctis observers. He wore formal black again, though today his collar hung unfastened at the throat, a small break with Noctis stiffness that made him look less like a student and more like a blade pretending to be tired.\n\nLucien: \"It is fair enough to make unfairness useful.\"\n\nCassian's Red Circle flared along his forearm.\n\nCassian: \"I am going to enjoy the day you run out of elegant sentences.\"\n\nLucien: \"Then I hope you are patient.\"\n\nElira stepped onto the activity floor beside me. Under her collar, the black-and-gold pendant gave one warm pulse against her skin, then went quiet.\n\nCael entered with Nera on one side and Leon on the other. His healer seal had been reinforced, yet the silver thread around his injured hand had darkened overnight. He had slept even less than I had, and that was saying something.\n\nBefore Aira could unroll the maps, Cael stepped three paces out of formation.\n\nOnly three paces, yet Leon's hand twitched toward him and stopped halfway. Cael caught the motion.\n\nCael: \"I am not running.\"\n\nLeon: \"I did not say you were.\"\n\nCael: \"Your shoulder did.\"\n\nLeon looked mildly offended by the accuracy.\n\nNera stayed planted where she stood. That cost her more than moving would have; every year of her Noctis training had drilled her to close distance around an injured teammate before anyone could accuse her of negligence or attachment.\n\nCael walked to the edge of the empty seventh marker. No visible fracture. No light around the outline. Just a human-shaped absence waiting for a room to decide isolation counted as care.\n\nHe looked at it for a long time. Long enough that Lucien's gaze sharpened across the square. Long enough that Rhea did not interrupt.\n\nCael: \"At Noctis, they call this recovery.\"\n\nThe chamber fell still enough to carry his voice. Cael did not look toward the witness rail; looking at us would have made the words ask for comfort.\n\nCael: \"If you are overwhelmed, they remove witnesses. If you are injured, they reduce variables. If you are frightened, they call familiar faces contamination. The room gets quieter every time. Cleaner.\"\n\nHis injured hand flexed once. The silver thread around it darkened a shade deeper.\n\nCael: \"Eventually quiet starts to feel like proof that you are improving, because if nobody is there to make you afraid, you stop shaking where anyone can see.\"\n\nNo one spoke over the silence. Keeping the right quiet took effort. Aira's pen hovered above her map and did not drop.\n\nCael turned to me then. He ignored my Green Circle and looked straight at my hands.\n\nCael: \"If the seventh marker opens, I will want to solve it by leaving.\"\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nThe word came out too fast, too blunt. Cael almost smiled.\n\nCael: \"That is why I am saying it now.\"\n\nNera's voice thinned.\n\nNera: \"Leaving can be a symptom.\"\n\nCael nodded once.\n\nCael: \"Or a strategy.\"\n\nElira stepped forward, planting herself beside the line he had drawn rather than between him and the marker.\n\nElira: \"Then we ask which one before we obey it.\"\n\nCael let out a slow breath that seemed to cost him more than half his testimony had.\n\nCael: \"Good.\"\n\nHe stepped back into formation without apologizing. Leon let him take his place without crowding him. That quiet restraint mattered almost as much as catching him would have yesterday.\n\nAs the chamber chimes warned of activation, Nera withdrew from the floor to take her assigned Noctis observer post along the upper witness rail beside Oric, leaving the active roster clear. Mira and the Green Circle witnesses filled the public record rail above the square, each pair armed with a timestamp crystal, ink, and duplicate ledger sheets. Mira caught my eye from above. She looked terrified.\n\nThen she raised her spare pencil, ready.\n\nAira anchored the support mark at the edge of the square and snapped three projection maps open in the air.\n\nAira: \"First rule. Nobody chases symbolic bait alone.\"\n\nLeon: \"Define symbolic bait.\"\n\nAira: \"Anything that looks like your guilt wearing a costume.\"\n\nCassian: \"That is annoyingly clear.\"\n\nThe activity bell struck.\n\nThe first marker collapsed before we could cross half the square.\n\nStone folded inward above the eastern lane. Beneath the buckling arch, the pale outline dropped to one knee, clutching a chest where its channel-light stuttered in ragged bursts. Every failed pulse darkened another masonry block overhead.\n\nAira flung the first map forward.\n\nAira: \"Eastern arch. Leon braces. Cassian clears debris. Elira and Cael assess the marker. Haru watches the lane.\"\n\nThe city disagreed.\n\nThree alleys rotated around us with the grinding shriek of stone dragged across stone. The eastern arch slid west. The dry fountain surged upward between our team and the marker, while a fresh wall shot up behind Cael before Leon could cross to him.\n\nAira's map remained cleanly, stubbornly still. Wrong.\n\nShe stared at the lying projection for half a heartbeat, then crushed it in her fist.\n\nAira: \"Map is compromised. Eyes up. Call what you physically see.\"\n\nAdmitting the map was useless saved more seconds than pretending control ever could.\n\nLeon slammed one palm toward the dry fountain. Water answered from old pipes beneath the paving, jetting out of four carved stone mouths to arc over the rising wall. He caught each stream in a separate blue thread and braided them into a roaring brace beneath the arch. The falling weight slammed down onto it. His knees buckled an inch and held.\n\nLeon: \"Now would be a lovely time for everyone else to become competent.\"\n\nCassian charged straight at the blocked lane without waiting for cover.\n\nHe withheld open flame; the marker lay too close, and the false city had seeded choking dust through every seam. One careless spark would turn rescue into cremation. Instead, he drove pinpoint heat into the metal pins buried inside the masonry. Red light crawled down his fingers. The iron pins swelled, split their stone housings, and popped free one by one.\n\nCassian snatched the first white-hot pin in his gloved hand before it could strike Cael's temple.\n\nThe stench of scorched leather cut straight through the dust.\n\nCael: \"Your glove.\"\n\nCassian: \"Still technically a glove.\"\n\nHe whipped the pin aside and drove his shoulder into the loosened blocks. Cael threw his weight beside him without waiting to be asked. Together they cracked open a gap barely wide enough for Elira to slip through sideways.\n\nThe marker's channel-light sputtered again. The arch dropped six inches.\n\nLeon's breath caught. Spray sheared off the water brace and flashed to ice across the flagstones where frost from the second marker had started bleeding into the first lane.\n\nI could have stopped the whole collapse in half a breath.\n\nThe solution unspooled complete inside my head: lift weight off twelve keystones, warm three water lines, steady the marker's pulse with one surgical correction. Zero risk. Zero delay.\n\nAnd zero proof that anyone else in this square had been allowed to matter.\n\nThe Black Circle pressed hard against my ribs. Copper filled my mouth.\n\nHigh above us, Mira shouted down from the witness rail.\n\nMira: \"The arch has Green maintenance marks! Lower left block!\"\n\nEvery head snapped toward where she pointed.\n\nA shallow copper groove sat half-buried beneath centuries of white polish, left off every academy blueprint because service corridors never counted as civic architecture. A Green Circle handprint would match the plate to the millimeter.\n\nMira stared down at her own wrist, then at the iron service ladder dropping from the witness rail to the street.\n\nMira: \"Can a witness enter the field?\"\n\nRhea's council seal flared crimson.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Only by choice. The route is unstable.\"\n\nMira climbed down anyway, clumsy in her hurry. Her shoe slipped on the final rung, dumping her onto the paving hard enough to skin one palm raw. Her pencil skittered beneath the fountain. She stared at the blood beading across the heel of her hand, then pressed forward toward the copper plate.\n\nMira: \"Does blood interfere?\"\n\nProfessor Halden leaned over the rail above.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"With the spell? No. With my ability to remain calm? Considerably.\"\n\nMira slapped her Green Circle onto the copper plate. A faint copper glow ran along the maintenance groove, plain and workmanlike.\n\nThe arch groaned and shifted its load into ancient service channels. Leon's water brace surged two inches higher, widening the gap. Elira reached the flickering marker and set both hands near its shoulders without closing contact.\n\nElira: \"Can you answer?\"\n\nThe outline dimmed. No voice answered.\n\nCael dropped to one knee on the far side of the broken wall, reading the irregular pulse.\n\nCael: \"It cannot answer while the channel is failing. Noctis would log silence as emergency consent.\"\n\nElira: \"Aurelis will not.\"\n\nShe lowered her Yellow Circle until its gold light lay level with the marker instead of towering over it. Thin gold threads slipped into the copper service channel Mira held open, not overwriting the failing pulse, simply giving it a steady frame to catch its rhythm.\n\nThe next pulse arrived faint but whole. Then a second.\n\nThe outline lifted one hand and pointed down the cracked western alley. Choice.\n\nAira had already written off the ruined map. Perched on a cracked fountain block with stone dust in her hair, she read the shifting city purely by sight.\n\nAira: \"Western alley chosen. Leon, drop the arch on my count. Cassian, carry only if the marker takes your hand. Mira, hold that plate open.\"\n\nMira's skinned palm shook against the copper.\n\nMira: \"How long?\"\n\nAira gauged the sagging arch, the alley mouth, and Leon's trembling arms.\n\nAira: \"Eleven seconds.\"\n\nMira: \"You are guessing.\"\n\nAira: \"I am.\"\n\nHer pen hovered over her ledger margin, the admission visibly unsettling her, yet the honesty gave Mira room to choose.\n\nMira: \"Then count loud.\"\n\nWe moved.\n\nAt seven, the paving rolled underfoot.\n\nAt eight, Leon dropped one water line and nearly went to his knees.\n\nAt nine, Cassian offered his unburned hand, and the marker clasped it.\n\nAt ten, Elira cleared the maintenance groove.\n\nAt eleven, Mira tore her bleeding palm off the copper plate.\n\nThe arch thundered down behind us, blasting chalky dust across the square. Nobody lay underneath it.\n\nMira sat straight down where she stood, letting out a laugh pitched half an octave too high.\n\nMira: \"Your count was wrong.\"\n\nAira dropped beside her, tore a clean strip from her useless paper map, and bound Mira's scraped palm tight.\n\nAira: \"By less than a second.\"\n\nMira: \"That is still wrong.\"\n\nAira: \"Count it wrong in the margin.\"\n\nShe logged the timestamp without looking up, offering no excuse and no joke. The first marker crossed the western safe line whole, leaving dust settling over four bruised teammates who had moved without waiting for rank.\n\nThe Foundation bell chimed the rescue. Lucien did not applaud.\n\nBefore we finished coughing dust out of our throats, the frost-binding marker woke.\n\nWhite cold detonated out of the dry fountain. Ice raced across the basin, bridged the paving seams, and wrapped Cassian's discarded metal pin in jagged frost, hurling it back across the square like a white spear aimed straight through Mira's spine.\n\nLeon saw it half a beat late. I saw it early.\n\nA shouted warning would never beat that throw.\n\nHaru: \"Down!\"\n\nI slammed my false Green Circle against the flagstones. I kept the Black Circle locked down, letting only a razor sliver of true precision bleed through the disguise to kick the spear's flight off-axis by the width of a fingernail.\n\nIt cleared Mira's shoulder. It did not clear me.\n\nIce sheared past my raised forearm, splitting my sleeve before biting through the fabric below my ribs. Heat flared along the cut, then vanished under creeping numbness.\n\nElira spun at the copper scent of blood, her composure cracking open.\n\nHaru: \"Marker first.\"\n\nShe hated the order. She obeyed the triage anyway.\n\nElira: \"Aira, pressure on his side. Leon, with me. Cassian, can you still push heat?\"\n\nCassian held up his scorched glove.\n\nCassian: \"Offended you asked.\"\n\nFrost had encased the second marker from the shins up inside the fountain basin. Its outline stood trapped in clear ice, conscious and rigid. Every flare of rescue heat caused the crystalline shell to cinch tighter.\n\nCael scrambled close enough to read the runes crawling underneath the glaze.\n\nCael: \"Punitive thaw. It reads rescue heat as resistance and increases crushing pressure.\"\n\nLeon: \"So fire kills it faster.\"\n\nCael: \"Uninvited fire.\"\n\nThat single distinction flipped the geometry of the problem.\n\nElira dropped to both knees beside the ice.\n\nElira: \"If you can hear me, blink once for warmth. Twice for hold.\"\n\nThe trapped light blinked once.\n\nCassian laid his burned hand against the fountain rim, though damaged nerves left him blind to the exact output; I caught the wobble in his flame. Leon caught it too.\n\nWithout a word, Leon coiled a thin thread of water around Cassian's wrist, turning temperature shifts into crisp pressure pulses: one tap for too cold, two for steady, three for too hot. They had never drilled that link once. They built it on the fly while frost crept higher.\n\nCassian fed warmth in careful, uneven bursts. Leon translated the marker's pulse back through his water thread. Elira held the consent rhythm locked, while Cael read each line of punitive script aloud before the ice could bury a clause.\n\nAira pressed both hands hard against the gash in my side.\n\nAira: \"You bent that spear.\"\n\nHaru: \"I nudged the angle.\"\n\nMy breath steadied against the freezing air.\n\nAira: \"With Green output?\"\n\nHaru: \"Mostly.\"\n\nHer eyes snapped up to mine; she knew *mostly* was doing dangerous work.\n\nAira: \"Can you keep standing?\"\n\nCold pooled behind my sternum where the Black Circle had tasted blood, thinning the whole chamber's borders in my sight.\n\nHaru: \"Long enough.\"\n\nI held her gaze without blinking.\n\nAira: \"Was that an answer or a habit?\"\n\nI looked down at the frost creeping under my boots.\n\nHaru: \"A habit. Give me ten seconds.\"\n\nShe held the bandage firm and counted under her breath.\n\nAt six, fractures spiderwebbed around the marker's knees.\n\nAt eight, Cassian's flame stuttered out and Leon trapped the residual heat inside his water loop before it could snap cold.\n\nAt ten, the tight iron band around my ribs loosened enough to breathe without the Black Circle trying to rewrite the square around the wound.\n\nHaru: \"Now it is an answer.\"\n\nAira knotted the bandage tight enough to make honesty sting.\n\nThe ice shattered outward, and the second marker stepped free.\n\nThe third marker, mana starvation, waited down a cramped alley whose bricks drank every spell cast inside. Rhea barred immediate entry. Aira tossed in a tiny light charm; the stones swallowed it black. Leon pushed a ribbon of water past the corner; the masonry drank that dry too. Even Elira's gold faded at the threshold.\n\nHigh on the public witness rail, Finn leaned over among the Green juniors and dropped a single copper coin into the lane.\n\nIt struck stone. Ordinary clinking rolled cleanly all the way to the fallen marker.\n\nFinn went pale when every eye in the square snapped up at him.\n\nFinn: \"Magic is food in there. Stop feeding it magic.\"\n\nLeon stripped off his ward-lined academy coat. Cassian unbuckled his fire-threaded bracer and dropped it beside his ruined glove. Elira banked her Yellow Circle until her wrist went bare. We walked in dark.\n\nForty paces of alley stretched into the longest road in the city. The bricks bled warmth out of muscle, steadiness out of balance, light from the rim of vision. At twenty paces, Leon stumbled hard; Cassian caught his elbow with the hand that still blistered. At thirty, Elira's knees gave an inch and she started to wave me off before glancing at the marker, rethinking what pride was for, and taking my arm.\n\nAt thirty-eight paces, the marker folded. Cael reached it first.\n\nNo spells. No rank edge. He wedged one shoulder beneath the faint outline and heaved with a body already bruised by someone else's doctrine.\n\nCael: \"Six steps.\"\n\nSix steps. No heroic vows, just what his legs could pay.\n\nLeon hauled the next six. Cassian dragged six after him. Elira covered four before her breath gave out. I carried the final ten while the Black Circle thrashed uselessly inside my chest in a corridor where using it would only feed the walls.\n\nThe moment we cleared the threshold, every withheld Circle snapped back in a rush of color. The third marker lived.\n\nWaiting by the western safe line, Mira watched the third extraction clear, then climbed back to the witness rail via the western service stair. She refused full cosmetic healing until the session closed so the scrape stayed on record, letting a healer simply clean the grit and bind her palm in white gauze. She kept that bandaged hand raised above her ledger slate where no transcript could later pretend injury had erased her from the floor.\n\nAcross the square, Lucien watched the three rescues with his hands clasped behind his back. I skimmed the field's pressure without letting my own mana flare.\n\nMy false Green Circle held steady around my wrist where I had threaded that deflection. Blood cooled under Aira's bandage. Mira's bandaged palm showed bright white beside her ink. Cassian flexed two blistered fingers. Leon stayed upright mostly because sitting down would admit how close the arch had come to folding him. Elira tracked my breathing and made sure I saw her counting.\n\nLucien had built a speed drill for obedience. Three markers down, we were bleeding and slower than a clean sweep, yet nobody had been spent like spare parts.\n\nStill, Lucien kept watching for the slip that would force me to unmask. That became its own quiet war: every marker rescued against the blood cost of holding back.\n\nThe field baited me with smaller traps first.\n\nA cracked lintel hung above Leon, seven seconds from shearing loose. I caught the weight shift. I kept my hands down.\n\nHaru: \"Leon. Arch weight.\"\n\nLeon glanced up, kicked his water brace wider beneath the stone, and hauled the marker clear a heartbeat before the block smashed down.\n\nMira's voice rang from the gallery.\n\nMira: \"Haru gave warning, Leon acted independently.\"\n\nThe chamber logged the credit without protest.\n\nNext, a hairline frost seam crept behind Cassian's left heel, thin enough to miss in dust. I could have snuffed it with a thought nobody would ever detect. Instead, I called:\n\nHaru: \"Cassian. Back foot.\"\n\nCassian pivoted cleanly instead of bristling, turning the sidestep into a sharp ribbon of fire that cauterized the seam before it climbed his boot.\n\nCassian: \"Appreciated.\"\n\nAira: \"Recorded. Also emotionally noted, which I deeply resent.\"\n\nThen the square dropped a false safe zone straight in front of Elira.\n\nThat trap almost broke my restraint.\n\nThe boundary gleamed silver, angled cleanly for healer access, stripped of any visible Dominion script. Yet the mana pooled inside carried zero warmth. No sanctuary built for living blood ever felt that dead. My fingers twitched toward my wrist.\n\nElira caught the twitch before anyone else.\n\nElira: \"Say it.\"\n\nShe did not want me to fix it. She wanted me to name it.\n\nHaru: \"False safe zone. No life-temperature.\"\n\nProfessor Valen echoed from the rail above:\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Observation entered. Elira Vale chooses route.\"\n\nElira veered sharp left, hauling the marker toward a crooked alley full of broken cobbles and honest strain.\n\nLucien's gaze stayed pinned on me.\n\nLucien: \"How disciplined.\"\n\nHaru: \"How disappointed.\"\n\nAira let out a strangled cough.\n\nAira: \"I am proud and terrified.\"\n\nOnce the marker crossed the line, Elira let a small smile show, just for a second. That brief smile cost Lucien far more than my retort had.\n\nAt the fourth marker, the city changed pitch.\n\nThe memory echo spoke in Talen Mire's voice.\n\nTalen: \"I surrendered.\"\n\nElira's hands stilled in midair, listening. The marker looped the recording:\n\nTalen: \"I surrendered.\"\n\nLucien looked conspicuously bored. That deliberate boredom told me everything about who had pulled the file.\n\nSoren Ashvale stepped straight to the edge of the neutral witness rail.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere objection. The marker exploits a living witness's trauma without consent.\"\n\nInside the Valemere observer frame, Talen's relay projection brightened. He stood before anyone prompted him, his braced right hand held clear against his dark sleeve.\n\nTalen: \"I will provide context with consent.\"\n\nThe memory marker paused mid-breath, still wearing his voice. Talen stared at it like a man facing a room he had already walked out of once.\n\nTalen: \"They recorded my injury. My fear. My pain. And they played it back in training exercises to teach conduits how to 'manage emotional interference.' I didn't consent then. I'm consenting now to say: using someone's worst moment as a teaching tool is not education. It's cruelty.\"\n\nThe Valemere delegation went rigid along the benches.\n\nThe Foundation bell chimed crisp silver as it entered his statement.\n\n**OBJECTION ENTERED**\n\nTalen lowered his braced hand. This time, the memory did not own him, and the marker's stolen voice dissolved into blank static.\n\nElira looked up at Soren.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nSoren inclined his head once.\n\nSoren: \"Honor is mostly remembering people are not props.\"\n\nLucien's eyes slid over to him.\n\nLucien: \"Valemere has become sentimental.\"\n\nSoren: \"No. We are just embarrassed it took us this long.\"\n\nThe public record rail scratched that line straight into the ledger.\n\nNoctis had planted the echo expecting us to fracture over guilt. Instead, the gallery woke up. For the first time, Wounded City Protocol stopped feeling like a closed stage built for five students and started feeling like a room full of people deciding they refused to stay furniture.\n\nOne of the minor delegates, a gray-haired woman from Lirenth who had stayed silent ever since signing Mira's sheet, raised her hand.\n\nDelegate: \"Can an observer enter a consent objection if the harmed party is remote?\"\n\nRhea glanced toward the Foundation bell.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Ask the harmed party.\"\n\nThe woman blinked, surprised that law asked a living person before consulting a statute. Talen answered from the relay frame anyway.\n\nTalen: \"They can. As long as the objection names me as a person, not as evidence.\"\n\nThe delegate squared her shoulders.\n\nDelegate: \"Then Lirenth observer record objects to the reuse of living witness trauma as educational material.\"\n\nThe bell chimed again, a thinner note than Soren's, yet it rang true.\n\nMira scribbled the line down with her mouth slightly open.\n\nMira: \"Observers can become witnesses.\"\n\nAira heard her from below.\n\nAira: \"Unfortunately, yes. It is how paperwork gets souls.\"\n\nMira wrote that down too. Caught by Rhea's sharp two-finger gesture meaning *no*, Mira crossed out *souls* and inked *responsibility* above it. Better.\n\nDown in the square, Leon stared up at the rails. Most of his life, observers had meant judges, rivals, strangers waiting for him to fail loudly enough to become gossip. Watching one woman speak and tilt the floor beneath his boots changed something in his stance.\n\nLeon: \"So the room shifts when they stop watching like statues.\"\n\nCassian: \"I dislike that you said something useful.\"\n\nLeon: \"You dislike many things.\"\n\nCassian: \"Mostly inefficient courage.\"\n\nHe said it while shifting into the gap beside Cael near the next alley, where fresh pressure lines had begun crawling up the stonework. The quip did not hide how low his Red Circle burned now. Cassian had learned that blazing brighter was often just another way of shrinking everyone else in the room.\n\nToday he kept his flame low and his eyes wide. Cael noticed.\n\nCael: \"You can stand nearer if you want.\"\n\nCassian glanced sideways at him. Cael's voice stayed thin, yet steady.\n\nCael: \"I know the difference between a guard and a wall.\"\n\nSomething quiet crossed Cassian's face. He closed the half-step and planted himself shoulder to shoulder with Cael rather than blocking his line of sight.\n\nCassian: \"Then I am beside.\"\n\nHigh on the rail, Nera scribbled fast on a scrap of paper and shoved it to Oric. Oric read it, swallowed hard, and shouted down to the floor:\n\nOric: \"Noctis student witness correction. In our training files, *beside* is frequently classified as obstruction when applied to lower-ranked students.\"\n\nNera's voice cut in right behind him, sharp and cool.\n\nNera: \"Record objection to that classification.\"\n\nThe Foundation accepted the entry. Lucien's studied boredom cracked at the edges.\n\nEvery small refusal chipped another brick out of Lucien's script. By the time the fifth marker lit, the square no longer belonged solely to his rules.\n\nThe fifth marker burned with crushing pressure, splitting our formation not with stone walls, but with competing priorities.\n\nA false silver notice flared overhead.\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED SUBJECT OVEREXTENDING RESPONSE**\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS MAY REMOVE HERSELF TO REDUCE PRESSURE**\n\nElira did not step back. She stepped straight toward the burning marker. Rhea's seal answered immediately.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Intervention authorized under public witness protocol.\"\n\nAira: \"Recorded. Elira moves by role, not private panic.\"\n\nMira leaned over the rail.\n\nMira: \"Recorded!\"\n\nThe false notice shattered into pale sparks, and the pressure-burn marker stabilized. Lucien offered a thin, practiced smile.\n\nLucien: \"Good. You are learning to narrate every instinct before it damns you.\"\n\nElira did not spare him a glance.\n\nElira: \"No. We are learning to stop you from forging signatures on our choices.\"\n\nThe sixth marker carried a relay hook. It reached straight for Cael. Of course it did.\n\nThe hook materialized as a razor-thin black stroke in midair, clean as fresh calligraphy, curving fast toward his healer seal.\n\nCael saw it before the rest of us, not out of sharper sight, simply because his bones still remembered the shape of being claimed.\n\nHis hand flew halfway toward his wrist, then froze. For one heartbeat, old conditioning won the first twitch. In the next, Cael forced his hand back down to his side.\n\nCael: \"Return hook.\"\n\nHis voice shook, yet he named it clean.\n\nUp on the rail, Nera's countermark was already snapping together.\n\nNera: \"Owner phrase?\"\n\nOric scanned the hook's root, chalk flying across his slate.\n\nOric: \"Hidden. It is pulling prior training authority as root.\"\n\nCael swallowed hard.\n\nCael: \"No owner.\"\n\nThe black hook bent tighter toward him. Cael raised his voice.\n\nCael: \"No owner.\"\n\nThe witness rail caught the phrase, ragged and human rather than drilled in chorus.\n\nMira: \"No owner recorded.\"\n\nSoren: \"No honorable custody claim.\"\n\nBlue observer: \"Prior training contested.\"\n\nGreen student: \"Cael Nox refused return.\"\n\nThe hook shuddered mid-flight. Lucien's eyes narrowed. For the first time all afternoon, his script had snagged on live teeth.\n\nLeon threw a wall of rushing water between Cael and the line. The black stroke sliced clean through it as if water were mist. Nera hammered it with a countermark, bending its curve an inch.\n\nOric shouted down from the rail.\n\nOric: \"It is not custody grammar! It is return grammar!\"\n\nCael's face drained of color. Custody grammar could be argued; return grammar did not ask permission. It defined *back* as the place that owned him.\n\nElira lunged. Rhea's crimson seal flashed in midair.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Elira Vale authorized for witness intervention.\"\n\nAira: \"Publicly recorded.\"\n\nMira: \"Recorded!\"\n\nThe black hook whipped off course, aiming past Elira's chest straight for the pendant at her throat. My breath stopped.\n\nBlack and gold detonated out of the pendant at once. Elira threw her hand up, not to shield herself, to catch the black stroke before it could jump back to Cael.\n\nCael: \"No!\"\n\nToo late.\n\nHer fingers closed around the black line. The whole chamber cracked down its center seam, every crooked street turning toward that single grip.\n\nLucien's voice rolled smoothly out of the bell tower.\n\nLucien: \"Second hinge.\"\n\nThe black hook snapped tight around Elira's wrist. Her pendant burned white-hot and ice-cold together, driving a fresh sting of frost beneath her collar. For one breathless heartbeat, the charm held.\n\nThen Dominion grammar spoke down through the chamber bell.\n\n**WILLING OBSTRUCTION CONFIRMED**\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS IN CUT PATH**\n\nRhea drove her council seal into the flagstones.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Challenge. The movement was authorized.\"\n\nAira: \"Challenge. Authorized public witness intervention.\"\n\nMira: \"Challenge recorded!\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Challenge. No consent to severance.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Challenge. Multiple roles do not reduce personhood.\"\n\nNera and Oric slammed twin countermarks down from the rail.\n\nNera: \"Dominion label rejected.\"\n\nOric: \"Return claim has no owner.\"\n\nThe hook did not snap. It cinched tighter. Elira went white to the lips without making a sound, which only made it worse.\n\nI took a step forward. Behind my ribs, the Black Circle surged like waking iron.\n\nElira locked eyes with me through the pain.\n\nElira: \"Do not let him make your fear useful.\"\n\nThose eight words stopped me harder than any council seal.\n\nAcross the square, Lucien watched with bright, hungry focus.\n\nLucien: \"Beautiful. Even now she guards your leash. Tell me, Haru. Is love still autonomy when it knows exactly how to command you?\"\n\nThe false Green Circle on my wrist flickered. Green. Pitch black beneath the cuff. Green again.\n\nMurmurs rippled along the witness rail, too scattered to piece together the truth, sharp enough to taste fear.\n\nAira caught the flicker and stepped straight into the sightline between me and the gallery.\n\nAira: \"Eyes on the hook! Not on Haru!\"\n\nHer voice cracked on my name, and she looked furious that it had.\n\nThe black line dragged Elira one stumbling pace toward the empty seventh marker. Cael caught her free hand with both of his.\n\nCael: \"Let me take it.\"\n\nElira bit the answer out between clenched teeth.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nCael: \"It was built for me.\"\n\nElira: \"That is why you do not get to inherit it.\"\n\nThe seventh marker split open.\n\nInside the empty outline stood no person at all, only a hollowed-out space shaped like someone scrubbed clean out of every witness's memory.\n\nThe Wounded City bell tolled once.\n\nFoundation: \"Severance route identified.\"\n\nRhea's expression went razor-sharp.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"End activity.\"\n\nFoundation: \"Unable. Dominion clause contested but active.\"\n\nLeon: \"Then we break the clause.\"\n\nHe drove a crushing jet of water straight into the black hook; Cassian wrapped it in roaring flame. Water and fire braided without a single spoken call, blasting white steam across the square. The line held anyway. Elira's knees gave out.\n\nI caught her before she hit the cobbles, and this time she let me take her weight. When her shoulder struck my chest, everything inside my head went flat and lethal. Quiet. Too quiet.\n\nHaru: \"Aira.\"\n\nMy voice did not sound like mine. Aira whipped around, hearing the drop in temperature immediately.\n\nHaru: \"Find the legal root.\"\n\nAira swallowed hard, then gave one sharp nod.\n\nAira: \"Working.\"\n\nStill human enough to ask. Still human enough to wait, barely.\n\nUp on the rail, Nera shouted down into the steam.\n\nNera: \"The hook is not the root! The root is the empty marker!\"\n\nOric: \"The seventh wound is isolation!\"\n\nValen's old folio snapped open in her hands.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Then the answer is presence.\"\n\nRhea threw both hands upward toward the gallery.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Do not recite for it. Prove you are here in a way Haru did not choose for you.\"\n\nThe witness rail hesitated for one breath. Mira moved first.\n\nShe jammed her bandaged palm beneath her stack of public ledgers and shoved them straight over the iron rail. Loose sheets fluttered down into the square in six distinct handwritings, one crowded with spelling fixes in the margin, another smudged where someone had cried and refused to wipe it clean.\n\nMira: \"We wrote before he asked us to!\"\n\nThe sheets struck the flagstones and stayed solid.\n\nSoren ripped Valemere's courtesy seal clean off his observer slate, forfeiting his delegation's right to edit his testimony before public release.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere witness channel stays open.\"\n\nHalden swore under his breath and unlocked his private research archive straight into the public rail feed. Decades of closely guarded annotations spilled across the gallery slates, complete with three lines marked **UNKNOWN** and one raw scrawl reading **I WAS WRONG**.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"No office of mine holds sole custody of classification.\"\n\nProfessor Valen set Liora's damaged folio beside his pages without explaining a word. The torn-out names said enough.\n\nNera wrenched the Noctis authorization ring off her wrist, leaving a raw red groove where the metal had bitten deep. Oric caught it midair, laid his own beside it, and kicked both rings under the rail barrier onto the outer stone ledge where every Noctis observer could see them and nobody could quietly pocket them.\n\nDown on the cobbles, Aira stared at the open seventh marker.\n\nAira: \"I do not have the legal root.\"\n\nHer voice broke. The whole square waited. Aira hated that the room had heard her come up empty, and spoke anyway.\n\nAira: \"I know isolation is doing the work. I know everyone here walked in for a different reason. I know Haru did not script all of us.\"\n\nShe ripped her prepared response sheet cleanly in half.\n\nPinned in the steam, Leon and Cassian strained against the black line as water and fire bucked against each other. Leon shot me a glare over his shoulder.\n\nLeon: \"I am still furious with you.\"\n\nCassian: \"I am furious with everyone. It is one of my stabilizing traits.\"\n\nSomeone on the rail let out a sharp laugh that snapped halfway into a sob. A hairline fracture split the empty marker, then stalled.\n\nCael stepped forward alone.\n\nHis healer seal flashed warning blue across his coat, shaking so hard from his trembling hands that the glow smeared over his collar. Nobody carried him. Nobody spoke in his place.\n\nCael: \"Cael Nox.\"\n\nThe hook yanked hard at Elira's wrist. Cael flinched and held his ground.\n\nCael: \"Not property.\"\n\nBehind him, the Foundation pool swelled dark. Cael looked across the scattered ledger pages, the kicked-aside rings, Halden's unlocked mistakes, Soren's torn seal, and a room full of people still arguing while refusing to walk out.\n\nCael: \"Present.\"\n\nThe empty marker shattered.\n\nEvery proof flooded the chamber together. No tidy unison followed; incompatible people simply stood inside the same danger and refused to hand their reasons to one master. The black hook slackened around Elira's wrist.\n\nElira dragged in a ragged breath. Against her throat, the pendant flared warm.\n\n**SEVERANCE ROUTE INTERRUPTED**\n\n**SIX VIABLE MARKERS EXTRACTED**\n\n**NO CIVIC LOSS RECORDED**\n\n**AURELIS WITNESS METHOD: PROVISIONALLY ACCEPTED FOR THIS ACTIVITY**\n\nWe had earned that verdict. The seventh marker stayed unclosed, yet Lucien could no longer argue that asking consent slowed rescue or that shared authority left a team paralyzed.\n\nRelief rushed into the square too fast.\n\nLucien looked from the cracked seventh marker to Cael, and smiled thinly.\n\nLucien: \"Interrupted is not ended.\"\n\nThe chamber released us before anyone could answer.\n\nRelease did not mean the city intended to let us walk out intact.\n\nThe paving began sliding inward toward the restoration pool. Buildings lost depth first, glass flattening into bright paper-thin squares before walls folded straight down through narrow seams in the floor. Every safe zone vanished. One single archway opened beneath the chapel stair.\n\nFour crest marks burned across the threshold: Green, Blue, Red, Yellow. No unclassified lane. No span wide enough for Cael's contested Noctis seal and Elira's hooked wrist to clear together.\n\nAira spotted the trap instantly.\n\nAira: \"The exit is sorting by status! Do not step into a color lane!\"\n\nThe cobbles behind us dropped into nothing.\n\nLeon: \"Alternative?\"\n\nAira eyed the folding skyline.\n\nAira: \"Developing.\"\n\nRhea slammed her red seal against the archway.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Emergency mixed-witness passage!\"\n\nThe four colored marks rejected her seal and pinched narrower.\n\nBeyond the shrinking square, Lucien already stood clear on the chapel stone outside the trap his protocol had sprung.\n\nLucien: \"Classification is not violence merely because the unclassified dislike choosing.\"\n\nGreen light snapped around Cael's boots, not because he belonged to Green, because the sorting gate treated a contested seal as lowest available rank. Yellow light flared ahead of Elira, dragging her marked wrist forward as the dormant hook bit down again.\n\nElira dug her heels into the stone. Cael lunged toward her.\n\nThe Green lane clamped tight around his ribs. Leon caught the back of his coat before the pull could drag him out of reach.\n\nCael: \"Let go!\"\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nCael: \"I can reach her!\"\n\nLeon: \"Not if the gate snaps shut on your spine!\"\n\nCael wrenched sideways, furious and terrified. Leon did not haul him back. Instead, he stepped straight into the Green lane beside him.\n\nBlue light died off Leon's wrist as the sorting gate choked on the contradiction. Leon gasped as backlash kicked his chest, yet for one sharp second Aurelis's proudest Blue stood unclassified beside the boy he had once treated like dead weight.\n\nLeon: \"Cassian. Now.\"\n\nCassian did not hesitate. He stepped clear of the Red lane and slapped his blistered, soot-stained hand down on Leon's shoulder. Red sparks died sputteringly off his forearm as the gate stripped his recognized path.\n\nCassian: \"This is an appalling group decision.\"\n\nAira piled in behind them.\n\nOverhead, the upper witness gallery dropped to floor level on screaming iron chains as the chamber walls folded inward. Its barrier unlocked only when Rhea forced the shared route open as emergency egress. Faculty and visiting delegates surged forward, followed closely by Nera, Oric, and Mira clutching her bandaged palm. Rhea stepped out of the Red lane last.\n\nEvery official color lane stood empty.\n\nThe sorting gate tried to lump the whole crowd into Green. Mira's crest flared first, trying to shove her to the front of the line. She stepped backward behind Cael.\n\nMira: \"No rank order.\"\n\nThe archway shuddered violently.\n\nThree paces away, Elira braced against the Yellow lane while the floor crumbled inches behind her boots. I could have shattered all four crest marks outright; the Black Circle behind my ribs knew the exact seam.\n\nInstead, I stepped straight into the Yellow lane beside Elira.\n\nThe false Green crest on my wrist collided with the gold barrier around her. Sparks snapped across both my arms, and the bandaged cut beneath my ribs split open again.\n\nElira: \"Haru, wait...\"\n\nHaru: \"Can you walk?\"\n\nShe looked from the black hook to the folding street, then to our team holding the unclassified jam behind us.\n\nElira: \"With help.\"\n\nI hooked her unmarked arm across my shoulders and drove us out of Yellow together.\n\nThe exit screamed. No human throat could make that sound: stone, law, and ancient rank-sorting shrieking as a dozen people refused to file into their neat lanes. The four colored marks blew apart into loose sparks.\n\nLeon hurled water underfoot. Cassian flashed it into a broad surge of steam. Rhea drove her council seal forward like a wedge, and we hurtled across the threshold in one tangled, sliding heap as the false bell tower vanished behind us into the dark pool.\n\nCael hit the chapel flagstones first. Mira landed across his calves. Cassian slammed into Leon and immediately blamed architecture. Aira lost two spare maps and kept the torn sheet bearing Cael's refusal. Elira and I tumbled across last as the stone arch sealed shut behind my heel.\n\nAcross the flagstones where the four rank marks had burned, one new line flared for a single breath:\n\n**MIXED WITNESS PASSAGE: UNRESOLVED**\n\nThen Lucien's relay wiped it clean.\n\nApproved crest colors flickered back unevenly across the floor. Cassian's Red returned first, bright and angry. Leon's Blue stuttered back after it. Elira's gold ring closed faintly around the black mark still biting into her wrist.\n\nCael's healer seal stayed dead blank.\n\nHe stared down at the empty patch of skin on his forearm and went motionless.\n\nMira pressed her bandaged palm flat onto the flagstones where the vanished line had burned.\n\nMira: \"The exit logged you before he erased it.\"\n\nCael: \"As what?\"\n\nMira checked her ledger copy.\n\nMira: \"Present.\"\n\nHis seal relit bare of any school crest, claiming neither Noctis nor Aurelis. *Present* got him as far as the healers, not across the room on his own. Cael took one step and his knees buckled. Leon caught him under both arms before he hit stone. Cassian stepped in to help, checked himself when Cael flinched at two sets of hands crowding him, and cleared the aisle ahead instead.\n\nCael: \"I am not weak.\"\n\nLeon: \"You just got dragged through a rank sorter while half-broke. You can be accurate before proud.\"\n\nThe phrasing sounded suspiciously borrowed from Maelin. Cael let out a short, startled laugh anyway. Once Cael steadied his feet, Leon dropped his grip, keeping both hands hovering near enough to catch and far enough back not to steer. They crossed the chapel together, Cael's legs trembling on every stride while Leon matched his pace a hand's breadth away.\n\nMira had already inked the erased line into her notebook. Lucien saw her close the cover. His expression shifted by less than the ghost of a smile, just enough to tell me the shattered exit had never been part of his design, before he turned out of the chapel without giving anyone the satisfaction of watching him leave.\n\nUp in the chapel healing circle, healers went to work stripping the black mark off Elira's wrist by paring away the upper sheath of mana clinging to her skin. It hurt cleanly and sharply. Beneath her collar, a faint trace of Dominion frost still chilled the edge of her pendant chain. She bore it without flinching.\n\nCael stood just outside the chalk healing ring, arms stiff at his sides. Leon stood beside him and refused to let him slip out the door. Good.\n\nThe black mark did not peel away like ordinary hostile residue; its threads were knotted inside the legal wording of what Elira had done. Every time a healer lifted a strand, the mark argued back in sharp flashes of script.\n\n**OBSTRUCTION**\n\nRhea answered from the edge of the ring.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Authorized intervention.\"\n\nThe healer lifted another strand.\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS BIAS**\n\nAira answered without looking up from her slate.\n\nAira: \"Multiple-role autonomy entered and active.\"\n\nAnother strand pulled taut.\n\n**CONDUIT RETURN CLAIM**\n\nNera and Oric answered together from the bench.\n\nNera: \"No owner phrase.\"\n\nOric: \"Prior training contested.\"\n\nEvery lie named aloud stripped another hook from the black thread until the healer could lift it free. Elira's jaw clenched so hard I thought she might crack a molar, yet she refused to let a single sound past her teeth. She was not hiding pain out of pride. She was denying Cael ammunition.\n\nHe heard the silence as proof anyway, his face hardening into tighter control with every black strand cut away.\n\nLeon spotted it and stepped squarely in front of him.\n\nLeon: \"Look at me.\"\n\nCael: \"I can hear.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is why I said look.\"\n\nCael lifted his eyes.\n\nLeon: \"She chose to protect you. You did not choose to hurt her.\"\n\nCael: \"The result is the same.\"\n\nLeon: \"No, it is not.\"\n\nRare anger flickered sharp across Cael's face.\n\nCael: \"Her wrist is black because I was the route.\"\n\nLeon leaned in an inch.\n\nLeon: \"Her wrist is black because Lucien is a murderer with grammar.\"\n\nFrom the chapel doorway, Cassian added dryly:\n\nCassian: \"That might actually be your best sentence.\"\n\nLeon never took his eyes off Cael.\n\nLeon: \"Do not make me waste it.\"\n\nCael's breath hitched once, then steadied.\n\nNight settled over Aurelis.\n\nThe Wounded City lay sealed far beneath the chapel floor, yet I could still feel the hollow seventh marker humming through the bedrock. Isolation. An injury shaped like an empty chair.\n\nAurelis never went wholly silent after dark: wards hummed inside the masonry, practice fields sighed as they reset, whispers echoed along upper galleries, and carriage bells drifted faintly up from the city below the cliffs. Tonight, every ordinary sound arrived carrying the same quiet dread. Who was missing? Who had been moved out of reach under the polite name of safety?\n\nI walked the corridor outside the infirmary twice before Elira called through the half-open door for me to stop wearing a trench into the flagstones. She was propped upright in a high-backed chair, since lying flat pulled the healer film tight across her raw wrist.\n\nHaru: \"I am not pacing.\"\n\nElira glanced from the doorway to my boots.\n\nElira: \"You have passed that crack in the slate seven times.\"\n\nHaru: \"Observation is not pacing.\"\n\nElira: \"Halden has been a terrible influence.\"\n\nI stopped beside her chair. She held out her unmarked hand, and I took it. For several quiet breaths neither of us spoke. Her pulse beat steady under my thumb; I caught myself counting it without meaning to.\n\nElira: \"Do not turn that into surveillance.\"\n\nI shut my eyes for half a second.\n\nHaru: \"I am trying not to.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe let out a long breath.\n\nElira: \"Aira is right. We need a replacement phrase before morning.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am listening.\"\n\nThat drew a faint smile from her, gone almost as fast as it came.\n\nElira: \"Better.\"\n\nThe smile faded out completely.\n\nElira: \"Tomorrow, if the seventh marker opens again, I may have to move.\"\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nElira: \"I am not asking permission.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nShe gave me a flat look. I corrected myself.\n\nHaru: \"I am listening.\"\n\nHer fingers tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"If I move, do not treat my choice as Lucien's victory before he earns it.\"\n\nThe sentence tasted like glass going down.\n\nHaru: \"And if he makes it one?\"\n\nHer eyes shone faintly in the lamplight.\n\nElira: \"Then make sure the world knows he did.\"\n\nAt the time, I thought she meant the chamber. I had no idea how far the word *world* was about to reach.\n\nCael appeared in the doorway later while she dozed again, with Leon waiting two steps behind his shoulder. Either Cael had asked for company or he had finally stopped fighting being followed; either way, it counted as ground gained.\n\nCael looked from Elira's bandaged wrist to me.\n\nCael: \"I am not going to apologize.\"\n\nLeon let out a low grunt of approval.\n\nCael: \"I want to. I am not going to.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good.\"\n\nThe word startled him. It startled me a little too. Cael kept both arms straight at his sides, visibly forcing his hands not to curl inward into guilt.\n\nCael: \"I am angry.\"\n\nLeon: \"Also good.\"\n\nCael blinked sideways at him.\n\nLeon: \"What? Better than apologizing for getting targeted.\"\n\nCael looked back at Elira.\n\nCael: \"I am angry that he turned her kindness into a route. I am angry that I recognize the spell he will reach for next. I am angry that part of me still whispers that being useful would make all this simpler.\"\n\nHe swallowed hard.\n\nCael: \"I am saying it aloud because if I keep it quiet, it turns into a plan.\"\n\nThat was Aira's work taking root. And Nera's. And Oric's. Maybe even his own, at last. I nodded once.\n\nHaru: \"Then keep saying it.\"\n\nCael held my eyes for a long, steady beat.\n\nCael: \"You too.\"\n\nI did not need to ask what he meant.\n\nHours later, Elira still dozed upright in her chair while Aira and Rhea argued in low voices by the corridor door over the morning's rules. I kept watch beside her without touching the pendant at her collar, though keeping my hands off it felt like holding back a landslide one pebble at a time. When the notice crystal outside the window flared pale silver, Elira stirred and caught my sleeve.\n\nElira: \"Do not chase his guilt in the dark.\"\n\nHaru: \"You were asleep.\"\n\nElira: \"Barely.\"\n\nI closed my hand over her warm, living fingers. She was still here. Outside the infirmary glass, the notice crystal burned the next summons into the night air.\n\n**WOUNDED CITY PROTOCOL: INCOMPLETE**\n\n**SEVENTH MARKER UNRESOLVED**\n\n**FINAL ACTIVITY TO OPEN AT DAWN**\n\n## Coda: Leon Stays Beside\n\nLeon caught up to Cael in the quiet corridor outside the infirmary and nearly made the same old mistake.\n\nHe almost stepped in front.\n\nThe muscle memory kicked in before thought could catch it: hand half-raised, shoulder squaring between Cael and the dark hall, the only guard stance Leon had ever been drilled to trust. He forced his boots to stop.\n\nCael noticed immediately. Noctis had trained him to read weight shifts before words ever left a throat.\n\nLeon opened both hands at his sides where Cael could see them.\n\nLeon: \"Do you want me here?\"\n\nThe question sounded stupidly small in the empty hall. Too late, too clumsy, yet it was the first honest thing Leon knew how to offer.\n\nCael stared at him long enough for embarrassment to flare hot under Leon's collar, trying hard to curdle into temper. Leon stayed put anyway.\n\nCael: \"Beside.\"\n\nLeon stepped up beside him, keeping his hands to himself and the corridor ahead wide open.\n\nInside the infirmary, Elira slept with frost lingering cold beneath her collar. Outside in the hall, Leon learned that standing in the right place could be harder than throwing a punch.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-11-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep12","label":"Episode 12","number":"12","file":"episode-12-cover.webp","fallback":"Talen Speaks","eyebrow":"Foundation Trial","recap":"","accent":["#080808","#ffd166","#ef476f"],"displayLabel":"Episode 12","displayNumber":"12","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"The Night Before the Cut","body":"No one called the next hours rest. Rhea called them preparation; Aira called them the part of the disaster where everyone pretended chairs were strategy; Professor Valen called them necessary. I avoided naming them because names made time move, and time continued without my permission. That was the ordinary cruelty beneath the magical one.\n\nBy dawn, I wanted three facts made harder to steal: Elira's decisions belonged to her, Cael's life was not an available component, and Votive Return was coercive doctrine rather than heroic rescue. The final field might defeat us. It would not be allowed to begin with those truths still trapped inside private fear.\n\nThe clock above the infirmary door clicked with a tiny brass sound every minute. I could hear the gear inside it catch, release, catch, release, a simple mechanism harmless in every way except that it kept proving the world was continuing.\n\nElira slept through six clicks. Woke on the seventh.\n\nAsked for water on the eighth.\n\nLied to the healer on the ninth when she said the pain had eased.\n\nHealer Senn looked at her. Elira looked back with Yellow Circle composure and a frost mark under her collar.\n\nHealer Senn: \"Lady Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"Elira.\"\n\nHealer Senn's mouth tightened.\n\nHealer Senn: \"Elira. I asked whether the pain eased, not whether you can make your face useful.\"\n\nElira blinked once. Then, to my surprise, she laughed softly. It did not last, though it was still real.\n\nElira: \"No. It has not eased.\"\n\nThe healer adjusted the silver film over her wrist.\n\nHealer Senn: \"Thank you.\"\n\nElira: \"For admitting pain?\"\n\nHealer Senn: \"For not making me treat a performance.\"\n\nI looked away because the sentence landed too close to me. Elira noticed. She waited until the healer left before speaking.\n\nElira: \"That was for both of us.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nHer expression sharpened. I closed my eyes.\n\nHaru: \"I mean, yes. I heard it.\"\n\nElira: \"Better.\"\n\nThe infirmary windows looked east, toward the part of Aurelis where dawn would eventually arrive and make the notice crystal true.\n\nElira slept for almost forty minutes. The healers considered that progress. I considered it theft from whatever wanted her afraid.\n\nWhen she woke, she found me sitting in the chair beside her cot with both hands folded and the false Green Circle visible over my wrist.\n\nElira: \"You look like Halden assigned you to observe yourself.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am doing poorly.\"\n\nHer mouth softened.\n\nElira: \"At observing?\"\n\nHaru: \"At being a person.\"\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Elira - The Difference Between Pain and Proof\n\nElira had lied to healers before, not often or dramatically, but enough.\n\nPain was information, and she had been trained to treat information as something that belonged first to the room. A Yellow Circle student did not collapse in public. A Vale did not make medical staff work harder because a wound frightened her. A tournament representative did not hand enemy schools a visible weakness and then pretend surprise when they used it.\n\nThe logic had looked dignified for years. It looked different from an infirmary cot with frost under her collar and Haru looking away because another person's accuracy had found him too.\n\nHealer Senn had asked whether the pain eased. Elira had answered with the version she wished were true: not courage, just performance with better posture. Haru knew performances. He survived inside one. She could see it in the way he held his hands, folded and harmless, the visible Green Circle steady over his wrist as if the disguise were not a spell at all, but a promise he had to keep making with his body.\n\nPart of her wanted to tell him to stop, though that would have been selfish in a clean shape. Another part wanted to tell him she understood, which might have sounded too much like permission.\n\nSo she chose the smaller truth instead.\n\nNo. It has not eased.\n\nThe room did not break. The healer adjusted the film. Haru looked away. The world continued, minute by brass minute, proving that honesty did not always arrive with enough mercy to make itself easy. Elira kept that fact. She would need it by dawn.\n\n---\n\nThe lightness drained out of her face as silence settled back over the cot. She pushed herself higher against the pillows.\n\nElira: \"Come here.\"\n\nHaru: \"The healers said...\"\n\nElira: \"I am not asking the healers.\"\n\nI moved the chair closer. She gave me a look, and I moved it closer than that. Elira took my hand.\n\nHer palm was warm now, though the mark on her wrist remained dark under the healing film.\n\nElira: \"Talk to me.\"\n\nThat should have been simple. I could describe the structure of the Wounded City, explain the relay grammar, or map the empty marker in twenty-seven possible ways. None of that was what she asked for.\n\nHaru: \"When the hook touched you, I wanted to end the whole field.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not disable it.\"\n\nHer fingers tightened around mine.\n\nHaru: \"End it.\"\n\nThe word held too much. The windows hummed faintly, and I lowered my voice.\n\nHaru: \"I could feel every rule. Every old line. Every place Lucien had hidden a path. I could have taken them apart.\"\n\nElira: \"And everyone inside?\"\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\nHaru: \"I do not know.\"\n\nThe Black Circle did not feel like fire when it came close. Fire had appetite. This felt like structure without mercy: a perfect correction, a reality in which the problem disappeared and took with it anything too close to the shape of the problem.\n\nElira's thumb moved over my knuckles.\n\nElira: \"Thank you for saying that.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is not comforting.\"\n\nElira: \"I did not ask for comforting. I asked for honest.\"\n\nI looked at her. She looked tired, marked, and entirely herself.\n\nElira: \"Haru, I am afraid of dying.\"\n\nMy chest stopped. She made herself continue.\n\nElira: \"I am more afraid of you having to carry it alone.\"\n\nHaru: \"Do not say it like that.\"\n\nElira: \"Like what?\"\n\nHaru: \"Like you are preparing me.\"\n\nHer eyes shone, and she did not look away.\n\nElira: \"I am preparing both of us to tell the truth before Lucien tells a lie with our faces.\"\n\nThe hidden circle inside me trembled with something unmistakably human. Fear.\n\nElira: \"If tomorrow asks me to move, I will move as myself. Not as your anchor, not as Noctis' hinge, and not as a sacrifice written into someone else's doctrine.\"\n\nHaru: \"And if the field kills you anyway?\"\n\nHer breath caught. For one second, she was not steady. She was eighteen, exhausted, in love, and trying not to make fear smaller than it was.\n\nElira: \"Then you stay Haru.\"\n\nI almost laughed, though it hurt too much to be laughter.\n\nHaru: \"That is too much to ask.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe pulled my hand to her chest, over the pendant. The dark crystal pulsed once between us.\n\nElira: \"I am asking anyway.\"\n\nWe stayed like that until the healer returned and pretended not to see what needed privacy.\n\nBy the time Elira insisted on walking down to the council preparation room with a healer cloak over her shoulders, the others were already building a war out of documents.\n\nRhea had created three layers of authority around the final activity. Layer one: council emergency termination. Layer two: witness intervention authorization. Layer three: Foundation objection cascade, which Aira described as \"everyone yelling the correct thing in the correct order while trying not to die.\"\n\nThere was a fourth layer nobody named at first: people who knew one another well enough to catch the moment someone slipped out of shape.\n\nLeon was assigned to Cael, and Aira assigned Cassian to Leon.\n\nTwo Blue Circle students from Leon's old ladder circle stood near the corridor arch when the assignment landed. One of them looked at Cael's healer seal, then at Leon, waiting for the familiar little distance Leon used to put between himself and embarrassment. Leon saw them. He stayed where Aira had placed him anyway, even as he rounded on her.\n\nLeon: \"I do not need watching.\"\n\nAira: \"That is exactly what people say before they sprint heroically into a second tragedy.\"\n\nCassian clapped Leon on the shoulder.\n\nCassian: \"Do not worry. I will restrain you handsomely.\"\n\nLeon: \"I hate every part of that sentence.\"\n\nAira assigned Nera to grammar detection and Oric to translation confirmation, then assigned Professor Valen to both of them.\n\nNera: \"We do not need supervision.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Of course not.\"\n\nOric looked suspicious.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"I am here to make tea and prevent you from treating your fear as a private technical flaw.\"\n\nNera stared at her.\n\nOric: \"That is supervision.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Very warm supervision.\"\n\nRhea assigned Mira to public record continuity and placed two council aides beside her, not above her. Mira read the assignment twice.\n\nMira: \"Continuity means if I freeze, they continue?\"\n\nRhea: \"It means if any one record is interrupted, another survives.\"\n\nMira: \"That sounds less like trust.\"\n\nRhea's face softened by one degree.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"It is trust with architecture.\"\n\nMira considered that.\n\nMira: \"I like architecture better than pressure.\"\n\nAira: \"Everyone does. Pressure has worse windows.\"\n\nThe jokes were not light. They were load-bearing.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Lucien Voss - Quiet Study\n\nLucien received confirmation at the hour Noctis called quiet study. The phrase had always amused him. Quiet study meant surveillance with better chairs.\n\nHis relay slate opened above the desk in his temporary quarters, throwing pale text across polished black wood.\n\n**Nera Vail assigned to hostile grammar detection.**\n\n**Oric Sen assigned to translation confirmation.**\n\n**Both operating inside Aurelis preparation structure.**\n\nHe read the lines once, then again, because repetition was how one separated information from insult.\n\nNera was no longer merely refusing Noctis' preferred testimony. Refusal could be punished, framed, isolated, turned into personal weakness. Refusal was a door closing. This was different. Nera had become useful elsewhere, and Oric with her.\n\nLucien set the slate down. Carefully. The surface cracked anyway. One line, thin as a hair, ran from the lower corner through the Noctis seal.\n\nHe looked at it with mild irritation, as if the wood had failed etiquette. Then he laughed once. The sound was brief, genuine, and empty enough that he stopped it immediately. No one was in the room to hear, which was both fortunate and irrelevant.\n\nHe opened a drawer and removed three old training tokens: black glass circles given to students who completed Dominion obedience matrices before age twelve. His own was flawless. Nera's had a chip on the edge from the year she had dropped it after a wrist restraint misfired. Oric's was scratched across the back where he had used it to jam a dormitory lock during a drill no one officially remembered.\n\nLucien had kept them because evidence mattered. That explanation had always been sufficient.\n\nHe placed the three tokens on the desk. Order restored itself for one breath: Lucien, Nera, Oric. Command, precision, witness. The old shape.\n\nThen the relay slate flickered again with an Aurelis preparation dispatch.\n\n**PUBLIC RECORD CONTINUITY: DUAL WITNESS BACKUP ASSIGNED.**\n\nMira Castellan's public record hand. Green Circle. Amateur ink discipline. Infuriatingly durable.\n\nLucien picked up Nera's token. He remembered her at thirteen, telling an instructor that a younger student's collapse was not laziness but relay shock. He remembered correcting her afterward: never spend truth where authority has not priced it. She had looked at him with open disgust then, young enough to make disgust expensive.\n\nHe had admired the waste.\n\nTwo weeks later, when that instructor tried to route the same younger student through a second relay before the tremor left his hands, Lucien had filed a maintenance objection under property-efficiency doctrine. The instructor lost corridor access for a month. Nera had looked at Lucien afterward as if she had found mercy hidden in a locked room. She had been wrong. Careless handlers damaged useful instruments. A Dominion system that permitted waste inside its own walls deserved correction.\n\nHe had trained it out of her as much as he could. Apparently not enough.\n\nHe set her token in the flame dish. Black glass did not burn. The Noctis lamp made it glow at the edges until the chip shone white. Lucien watched longer than necessary.\n\nHe did not burn Oric's token. That distinction had no emotional value. Oric remained useful as a measure of Nera's distance, a secondary vector, a witness who mistook positioning for conscience. Lucien moved Oric's token beside the cracked slate, then placed his own alone at the center of the desk.\n\nThe arrangement was strategically cleaner. No disputed loyalty near the command point. No memory disguised as continuity. No childhood witnesses left inside the formation. Cleaner. Colder.\n\nHe wrote the next instruction by hand because handwriting slowed the body enough to prevent overcorrection.\n\n**Do not retrieve Vail. Do not appeal to Sen. Treat both as hostile doctrine leaks. Use their knowledge to confirm Aurelis dependency patterns.**\n\nHis stylus paused before the final sentence. Then he added:\n\n**Pressure must move through Cael Nox and Elira Vale before Vail can turn preparation into absolution.**\n\nSpecific. Actionable. Loneliness, when properly classified, became logistics.\n\nThe cracked desk disagreed in silence.\n\nLucien left Nera's token in the flame dish while the next relay bell sounded and the lamp burned low. The chip did not melt. Of course it had not. Some flaws persisted because people mistook endurance for virtue. He turned the flame down and left the dish where he could pretend not to watch it.\n\n---\n\nThe rehearsal attacked before Mira finished arranging the first page.\n\nNoctis did not announce it as an attack. The preparation room simply lost every name.\n\nOne moment, Rhea's slate showed our assignments in red and silver. The next, the letters peeled from the page and rose into the air like ash. HARU separated first, H, A, R, U turning slowly above the table before each letter blackened and disappeared. ELIRA followed. Then CAEL. AIRA. LEON. CASSIAN.\n\nThe labels on the doors vanished. The portraits lost their brass plates. Even the healer mark on Elira's wrist went blank. No alarm sounded, which made everyone move faster.\n\nRhea struck the floor with her council seal.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Hold positions. State names aloud.\"\n\nI opened my mouth. No sound came out. Air moved across my throat, and the room swallowed the piece of me that carried a name.\n\nAround the table, everyone discovered the same thing at different speeds. Leon swore without sound. Cassian's mouth formed something probably unhelpful. Aira slapped both hands over her notebook as its headings faded. Cael went completely still.\n\nThe Black Circle rose against my ribs. No. Not yet.\n\nI caught Cael's eye and pointed toward the door. He shook his head. Good. A decision, not a freeze.\n\nThen the room spoke in his voice.\n\nCael: \"I consent to necessary return.\"\n\nThe real Cael made no sound. The sentence came from the walls. Black lines opened under his boots and began drawing the first position of Votive Return.\n\nNera kicked the chair out from beneath the nearest line. Wood struck stone and broke the circle for half a breath. Oric overturned the tea tray across a second. Hot water spread through the ink, though the black geometry drank it and continued forming beneath the puddle.\n\nProfessor Valen grabbed the old ward case. Its lock had forgotten her name. Professor Halden seized a brass candlestick and smashed the hinge. The lid opened. Halden looked offended by the efficiency of violence.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I have reconsidered several professional principles.\"\n\nHis voice carried because he had named no one. Aira understood first. She wrote on the table in spilled tea:\n\n**VERBS STILL WORK.**\n\nThen, beneath it:\n\n**POINT. MOVE. REFUSE.**\n\nThe room could steal names. It could not yet steal action.\n\nRhea pointed to the windows, then made a closing fist. Cassian moved. He sent a flat wave of heat across the window latches, and the metal expanded to jam every frame before the hostile relay could turn glass into exits.\n\nLeon drove water from Oric's overturned tray through the floor grooves, tracing the forming circle without feeding it enough pressure to activate. Three black lines. Five. Then a hidden seventh running beneath Elira's chair. He pointed. Elira was already standing.\n\nPain crossed her face when the healer film pulled against her wrist. She did not hide it. She hooked one foot around the chair leg and dragged it sideways, exposing a silver-black needle rising from the floor where her ankle had been.\n\nThe needle turned toward her. I moved, and she lifted one hand. Stop. She knew the needle was tracking me, waiting to see which target I moved to save.\n\nElira pointed at Cassian, then at the ceiling. At Leon, then the floor. At me, then Cael. Roles without names.\n\nCassian looked up. The ceiling seals had begun folding inward. A circular iron frame, the room's emergency suppression lattice, dropped from its anchors. If it landed around the table, it would complete the stolen return diagram with all of us inside.\n\nCassian threw both hands upward. Fire hit iron. The frame stopped six feet above us and turned red at the edges. Cassian's boots slid backward. Leon pulled water out of the floor grooves and struck the opposite side. Steam exploded across the room. For one second, neither of them could see the other. They held anyway, and the frame bent between heat and pressure.\n\nAira crawled beneath the table toward Cael with her notebook clenched between her teeth. The false voice spoke again.\n\nCael: \"My life is available under emergency doctrine.\"\n\nThe real Cael flinched. The line under his boots brightened. Aira tore a page from the notebook and pressed it to his chest.\n\n**TRUE?**\n\nHe seized the pencil from her hand.\n\n**NO.**\n\nThe black line cracked. The room repeated his stolen voice louder.\n\nCael: \"My life is available.\"\n\nCael wrote across the first word until the paper tore.\n\n**NO.**\n\nAnother crack.\n\nNera dropped beside them and pressed her scorched binding ring into the wet black geometry. The ring had once carried Noctis command authority. The hostile script recognized it and opened like a mouth. Nera pushed two fingers into the gap and twisted. Pain whitened her face. Oric caught her wrist before the ring could close, and together they held the stolen authority open.\n\nValen pulled seven narrow copper rods from the broken ward case and threw them across the floor, not by rank, one for each person. One struck near me. One near Elira. One beside Cael. The others landed by Aira, Leon, Cassian, and Rhea. Blank metal, with no names for the room to erase.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Choose your own mark.\"\n\nHer voice worked because the instruction did not decide who we were.\n\nI stepped on the nearest rod. The copper warmed beneath my shoe, neither Black nor Green, just mine.\n\nElira stepped on hers. Gold light entered the rod, roughened by pain yet unmistakably chosen. Aira slapped her palm against another. Leon held the falling frame one-handed long enough to kick his into place. Cassian did the same, fire shaking around his shoulders. Rhea drove her seal through the sixth. Cael stared at the last rod.\n\nThe room spoke for him again.\n\nCael: \"Necessary return accepted.\"\n\nHe stepped on the copper.\n\nCael: \"No.\"\n\nHis voice returned on the smallest word. The rod flashed silver.\n\nEvery stolen name struck the room at once, returned to us by the room itself. Letters ignited over the table and rushed back toward the bodies they belonged to. Aira gasped when her name hit. Leon nearly dropped the frame. Elira's name entered the healer mark on her wrist and turned it gold again.\n\nMine stopped in front of me. HARU.\n\nBehind the four letters, a fifth shape pressed against the air: a black ring too large for the room and too old for the rehearsal ward. Lucien's relay had found the edge of the disguise. The cold pressure behind my sternum sharpened.\n\nI reached for my name, not the ring.\n\nThe letters struck my chest. The false Green Circle flared hard enough to burn my wrist, and held. The black shape vanished.\n\nEvery window in the room cracked. The suppression frame slipped another foot, groaning against their hold. Leon shouted. Cassian answered with fire.\n\nElira raised her uninjured hand and sent a Yellow Circle blade through the warped iron, not an explosion, just a line so fine the frame separated into seven harmless arcs. They hit the floor around us, each one smoking, none completing the circle. The spell cost her. Her knees folded.\n\nI caught her because catching was permitted and there was no time to turn care into a debate.\n\nThe room released its grip on language. Everyone began speaking at once.\n\nRhea: \"Stop. One voice. Aira.\"\n\nAira was still on the floor beside Cael, hair wet with tea, one hand bleeding where the pencil had snapped.\n\nAira: \"Names removed. False consent generated in Cael's voice. Return geometry attempted through rehearsal lattice. Countered through blank chosen marks. Elira cut the suppression frame. Haru's disguise...\"\n\nShe looked at me. The burn around my false Green Circle had already begun darkening.\n\nAira: \"...came under direct classification pressure and held.\"\n\nRhea: \"Cael?\"\n\nCael stared at the paper against his chest. Only **NO** remained legible.\n\nCael: \"The room used my voice. It did not use my answer.\"\n\nRhea entered the distinction under emergency seal.\n\nProfessor Halden knelt near the remains of the black geometry. He touched nothing.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"This was not part of the rehearsal array.\"\n\nNera pulled her scorched ring free from the floor.\n\nNera: \"Lucien inserted name-removal grammar through the observer authorization.\"\n\nOric: \"Can he do that in the final field?\"\n\nNera looked at Cael, then Elira, then me.\n\nNera: \"He just proved he can try.\"\n\nMaelin arrived at a run, saw the broken windows, seven pieces of glowing iron, Elira in my arms, and Rhea already writing.\n\nMaelin: \"I left for six minutes.\"\n\nCassian lowered his hands. His sleeves were burned to the elbow.\n\nCassian: \"In our defense, they were unusually eventful.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No one here has a defense. Sit down.\"\n\nThis time everyone obeyed.\n\nMaelin treated the room in the order injury demanded rather than rank. Nera's fingers first, where the scorched binding ring had bitten through skin as the hostile line tried to close. Then Elira's channel pulse and narrowed vision. Leon's shoulder. Cassian's burned sleeves. My wrist last, because the false Green crest was stable and Maelin refused to let mystery become triage priority.\n\nAira objected to none of it, which worried me more than if she had. She sat beneath the broken table with the ruined scripts around her and stared at the place where Cael's stolen voice had entered the walls. Mira knelt beside her.\n\nMira: \"Your hand is bleeding.\"\n\nAira looked down as if the snapped pencil had belonged to someone else.\n\nAira: \"Minor.\"\n\nMira: \"That is a category, not an answer.\"\n\nAira almost smiled. Almost. Then her face folded in a way I had never seen.\n\nAira: \"When the names disappeared, I had nothing.\"\n\nThe room quieted.\n\nAira: \"I built assignments. Prompts. Duplicate chains. I knew who should answer every kind of failure. Then the headings vanished, and for three seconds I could not remember what I was for.\"\n\nMira looked at the tea-written words still drying on the table.\n\n**POINT. MOVE. REFUSE.**\n\nMira: \"You wrote that.\"\n\nAira: \"After three seconds.\"\n\nMira: \"And then you wrote that. Both are true.\"\n\nMira held her pencil steady against the damp table edge. Aira looked angry at her, while Mira remained frightened and present. The sentence struck Aira harder than reassurance would have. She pressed the heel of her uninjured hand to her eyes.\n\nAira: \"I hate when people use my methods on me.\"\n\nMira: \"I learned from an unpleasantly thorough source.\"\n\nThat earned a broken laugh. Rhea stepped closer.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Solen. You are relieved from sole sequence responsibility in the final field.\"\n\nAira's head came up.\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nRhea: \"That was not punishment.\"\n\nAira: \"It feels like removal.\"\n\nRhea considered her before answering.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then correction. Sequence lead remains yours. Mira carries equal interruption authority. If either of you loses language, the other names action.\"\n\nMira went pale.\n\nMira: \"Equal?\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"For interruption, not command. You do not need to know the whole plan to say the plan has stopped seeing someone.\"\n\nMira looked at Aira. Aira looked at the broken scripts. Then she tore the sequence badge from her own sleeve, ripped it down the center, and handed half to Mira.\n\nAira: \"If I start performing competence instead of reporting, interrupt me.\"\n\nMira held the half-badge like it weighed more than the ward case.\n\nMira: \"What if I am wrong?\"\n\nAira: \"Then I will become extremely educational about it later.\"\n\nMira: \"That is not comforting.\"\n\nAira: \"Good. Comfort is not the assignment.\"\n\nShe held out her bleeding hand. Mira took it carefully and pulled her out from under the table. No one applauded. The room had finally learned better.\n\nThe rehearsal had failed in every formal sense, yet it left us with proof cleaner preparation never could have given us: even when the walls stripped away our names and stole our voices, people still moved for one another.\n\nAira looked at the ruined scripts, then tore the remaining stack in half.\n\nAira: \"Prompts only. No lines anyone can steal.\"\n\nMira returned with three fresh ink bottles and terror already sharpening her attention. She looked at the paper covering the floor.\n\nMira: \"What needs copying first?\"\n\nAira handed her Cael's torn **NO**.\n\nAira: \"The useful part.\"\n\nHalden found the relay splinter beneath the broken suppression frame: black glass no longer than a fingernail, warm despite the cold room. He lifted it with silver tongs and turned it under Valen's old lamp.\n\nOne handwritten instruction appeared inside:\n\n**PRESSURE MUST MOVE THROUGH CAEL NOX AND ELIRA VALE.**\n\nCael read his name and stopped breathing.\n\nElira took the tongs from Halden before anyone could decide she was too injured to hold evidence. Her hand shook. The splinter did not.\n\nElira: \"He planned the route before the rehearsal attacked.\"\n\nNera: \"He expected Cael to become sacrifice pressure and you to become Haru's trigger.\"\n\nI looked at the black glass. The Black Circle offered to erase it.\n\nHaru: \"Copy first.\"\n\nMira wrote the sentence three times. Aira added the discovery conditions. Rhea sealed one copy outside the room. Only then did Elira place the splinter in Cassian's unburned palm.\n\nElira: \"Now.\"\n\nHe melted it into a useless bead. Lucien kept his plan, and we kept proof that cruelty had arrived before our reaction.\n\nCael picked up the bead after it cooled.\n\nCael: \"I want it on my copy.\"\n\nRhea: \"As evidence?\"\n\nCael: \"As weight. If the field uses my fear, I want to remember the plan existed before I felt it.\"\n\nAira punched a hole through the margin of his refusal page and tied the glass bead there with ordinary thread. It clicked softly whenever the paper moved: not a charm or protection, just a piece of Lucien's plan forced to travel beside Cael's answer instead of inside it.\n\nWhen Cael stood, the bead swung below **NO** and did not change the word.\n\nRhea changed the final formation after seeing it. Cael would not walk between two guards. He would walk beside Leon, with an open route to Nera and Oric and no one behind him close enough to turn support into pressure. Cassian took the rear because he could protect a retreat without making Cael feel pushed forward. The arrangement looked less secure on paper, and safer for the people inside it.\n\n---\n\nAn hour before dawn, while Leon went to draw training shields from the armory and Cael stepped out with Nera to retrieve a copy of the chapel records, Mira spread the public witness sheets across the table. She looked deeply uncomfortable with the word scripts.\n\nMira: \"They are not scripts exactly. They are prompts. For accuracy, not performance.\"\n\nAira: \"Good. Because if anyone sounds rehearsed, Lucien will call it influence.\"\n\nMira nodded hard.\n\nMira: \"So they should correct each other if they disagree?\"\n\nProfessor Valen smiled.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Especially then.\"\n\nMira wrote:\n\n**DISAGREEMENT PROVES AUTONOMY IF HONEST.**\n\nLeon walked in carrying three training shields and a face like a storm.\n\nLeon: \"Cael is gone.\"\n\nThe room stopped moving without becoming silent. Aira stood.\n\nAira: \"Define gone.\"\n\nLeon: \"Not in the healer room. Not in the relay room. Nera is missing too.\"\n\nOric's chair scraped back.\n\nOric: \"The chapel.\"\n\nWe ran. Rhea did not shout orders. She pointed, and council students moved. Cassian reached the old chapel first and did not open the door until Rhea's seal caught up with him.\n\nCassian: \"I am learning restraint. I expect applause later.\"\n\nRhea's look told him the applause was unlikely.\n\nThe chapel doors opened. Cael stood beside the chapel's restoration pool with one hand over the final print. Nera stood across from him, pale and furious. The pool between them showed Votive Return notation in black lines.\n\nMy hidden circle surged so hard I tasted metal.\n\nHaru: \"Cael.\"\n\nHe looked at me. He did not look guilty, which was almost worse.\n\nCael: \"I asked Nera to show me what Lucien would expect me to do.\"\n\nNera's answer cut across his.\n\nNera: \"And I told him no.\"\n\nOric entered behind us, breathing hard.\n\nOric: \"Good.\"\n\nNera's face twisted.\n\nNera: \"Then I realized he already remembered enough.\"\n\nLeon stepped toward Cael.\n\nLeon: \"Move away from the pool.\"\n\nCael did, immediately. It was not enough. Aira crossed her arms.\n\nAira: \"Explain in a way that does not make me invent new crimes.\"\n\nCael's injured hand shook. He gripped it with the other.\n\nCael: \"I am not planning to use it.\"\n\nLeon: \"That sentence is not as useful as you think.\"\n\nCael's shoulders dropped half an inch, not arguing.\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nHe looked at the pool.\n\nCael: \"Lucien expects me to be afraid of being spent. He also expects all of you to be afraid I will spend myself. That fear gives him a route. If we do not understand the spell, he can use the threat of it without casting anything.\"\n\nNera hated that he was right. It showed.\n\nNera: \"He asked what interrupts Votive Return.\"\n\nProfessor Halden stepped closer.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"And?\"\n\nNera looked at Cael.\n\nNera: \"Nothing clean.\"\n\nOric's voice dropped.\n\nOric: \"If the conduit begins willingly, counterspells treat interruption as murder.\"\n\nAira's face went white with anger.\n\nAira: \"That is disgusting.\"\n\nCael: \"Yes.\"\n\nNo defense. No Noctis vocabulary to make it sound noble. Just the plain word dropped quietly into the silence between them.\n\nElira entered last, moving slowly with a healer cloak over her shoulders. Everyone turned. She ignored them and went straight to Cael.\n\nElira: \"Are you trying to die for me?\"\n\nCael's mouth opened, then closed with no answer.\n\nElira: \"Do not search for the kind version.\"\n\nHe swallowed hard.\n\nCael: \"I am trying not to be useless if Lucien succeeds.\"\n\nElira's eyes softened with pain.\n\nElira: \"Cael.\"\n\nHe looked at her then.\n\nCael: \"They taught us that being saved creates debt.\"\n\nLeon made a sound under his breath. Cael kept going.\n\nCael: \"I know you do not think that. I know Aurelis does not think that. I know it here.\"\n\nHe touched his temple, then his chest.\n\nCael: \"Not always here.\"\n\nElira stepped close and placed her hand over his, not on his chest, over the hand that had gone there.\n\nElira: \"Then let us keep teaching the part that is slower.\"\n\nCael's face broke for a second, and he pulled it back together badly.\n\nCael: \"What if there is no time?\"\n\nI had no answer. That failure sat in the chapel with us.\n\nProfessor Valen approached the pool and placed her hand on the Aurelis record.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Then we make time before dawn. We cannot restore the missing doctrine in one night, but we can make one thing clear to the Foundation.\"\n\nRhea understood first.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Votive Return is not consent if the person was trained to value death over refusal.\"\n\nValen inclined her head once, resting two fingers against the Aurelis seal.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"And Witness Restoration cannot default to single-life transfer while witnesses are present and objecting.\"\n\nThe old page on the chapel table opened without a hand touching it. Silver dust rose again, thinner than before, and Liora Aerlen-Valen stood beside the restoration pool with both hands resting on her cane. Her record-echo flickered at the edges, her eyes clear.\n\nLiora: \"Witness Restoration did not demand a corpse.\"\n\nCael's shoulders stopped moving.\n\nLiora: \"Seven witnesses shared the burden. Pain, memory, channel strain, breath, refusal, recall, and return. No one died because no one was allowed to become the whole cost.\"\n\nNera: \"Noctis removed distribution.\"\n\nShe spoke barely above a whisper.\n\nLiora lowered her cane toward the stone. The record supplied the sound of a single strike.\n\nLiora: \"Noctis removed distribution, made the spell fatal, and renamed the blade Votive Return.\"\n\nShe turned to Cael.\n\nLiora: \"If you activate this spell, you will die. Not because the magic requires it, but because Noctis designed it that way.\"\n\nCael's face was white.\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nLiora's expression softened with ancient grief.\n\nLiora: \"Knowing is not the same as being free of it. Let the witnesses object even if you cannot yet believe them.\"\n\nNera looked at the pool.\n\nNera: \"Will the Foundation accept that?\"\n\nHalden's answer was precise.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It may not accept it as law.\"\n\nHe looked at Cael.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"But it will have to record that we knew.\"\n\nCael stared at him. Halden looked uncomfortable under the gratitude he had not asked for.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not make me repeat it sentimentally.\"\n\nAira: \"Too late. I heard personal growth.\"\n\nHalden ignored her.\n\nThe next three hours refused to become a clean record. Cael remembered a phrase, then a hand position, then nothing for eleven minutes except the smell of cold iron and the sound of an instructor correcting a child's pronunciation of willing.\n\nHis own fingers folded into the casting shape while he spoke. They locked there.\n\nMaelin: \"Lower your hand.\"\n\nCael: \"I am trying.\"\n\nLeon moved toward him and stopped before touching. Cael looked at the space between them.\n\nCael: \"Help.\"\n\nLeon opened Cael's fingers one at a time. He did it badly: too much care in the first finger, not enough in the second. By the fourth, both of them were breathing like the room had narrowed around their hands.\n\nAira had put her pencil down.\n\nAira: \"You do not have to repeat the instructor's exact words.\"\n\nCael: \"You asked me to.\"\n\nAira flinched.\n\nAira: \"I was wrong. I can mark the phrase withheld.\"\n\nHe studied her face as if looking for the punishment attached to changing the instruction. None arrived.\n\nCael: \"Write that they corrected willing until it sounded calm.\"\n\nAira wrote it.\n\nHalden leaned forward, then visibly reconsidered the movement and sat back.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do you remember what Noctis counted as willingness?\"\n\nNera answered too quickly.\n\nNera: \"Verbal activation without direct restraint at the moment of casting.\"\n\nOric: \"Not in every manual.\"\n\nShe turned on him.\n\nNera: \"That is the operational standard.\"\n\nOric: \"Senior doctrine lists visible preconditioning as contamination.\"\n\nNera: \"And then defines training as invisible.\"\n\nOric's mouth closed. They were both right, which made the whole doctrine uglier. Noctis had written a safeguard into one book and taught instructors how to make harm disappear from its categories in another.\n\nCael's hand curled again.\n\nCael: \"They said hesitation meant someone else would die because I was selfish.\"\n\nLeon stood so hard his chair struck the wall.\n\nLeon: \"Then none of it was a choice.\"\n\nCael recoiled. Leon saw it too late.\n\nCael: \"Do not do that.\"\n\nLeon froze. Cael's voice shook harder.\n\nCael: \"They made the walls. They made every door hurt. But if you say no choice was mine, then they took everything.\"\n\nThe room had no prepared place for that. Elira sat across from him, pale beneath the healer film. She did not give him a word.\n\nElira: \"What do you want us to call it?\"\n\nCael looked at the seven-hand diagram, then at his own fingers.\n\nCael: \"I don't know.\"\n\nHis eyes filled.\n\nCael: \"Not willing. Not the way they wrote it.\"\n\nProfessor Valen pulled the fresh page toward her. She did not fill the blank. She wrote:\n\n**CAEL NOX DISPUTES NOCTIS USE OF WILLING. HIS PREFERRED TERM IS NOT YET KNOWN.**\n\nRhea sealed the sentence as provisional testimony. Halden added one narrower line beneath it:\n\n**ACTIVATION PHRASE ALONE CANNOT ESTABLISH CONSENT.**\n\nNo one turned the two sentences into a complete doctrine.\n\nNera and Oric translated what they could. Twice they disagreed. Once neither could read a clause without hearing an instructor's voice in it, so Mira drew a box around the passage and labeled it **UNRESOLVED / DO NOT CLEAN UP**.\n\nThe record kept Cael's silences too. One lasted six minutes. During it, Cassian stood guard at the door and burned three intrusive frost motes before anyone else noticed them. Leon sat beside Cael, leaving enough room for refusal. Aira sharpened a pencil she did not use.\n\nWhen Cael finally rose and walked over to the Green copy table, Mira asked one question.\n\nMira: \"What can we write that is true right now?\"\n\nHe answered without looking at the Noctis page.\n\nCael: \"I am not a reserved life.\"\n\nMira wrote it in her own hand. Two of her council aides copied it beside her, not as a slogan and not as a verdict on every choice he had ever made, simply as the one sentence Cael had chosen cleanly in that room:\n\n**CAEL NOX IS NOT A RESERVED LIFE.**\n\nWhen Cael saw it written that plainly, his knees weakened enough that he had to sit back down on the bench.\n\nAt fourth bell before dawn, the notice crystal in the chapel activated. It displayed the final activity conditions.\n\n**WOUNDED CITY PROTOCOL: FINAL STAGE**\n\n**SEVENTH MARKER ACTIVE**\n\n**DOMINION SEVERANCE REVIEW**\n\n**OBJECTIVE: PRESERVE VIABLE LIFE UNDER CONFLICTING CLAIMS**\n\nThen, beneath it:\n\n**NOCTIS OBSERVER: LUCIEN VOSS**\n\nMore lines followed, each one making the chapel colder.\n\n**AUTHORIZED OBSERVER RELAYS: ACTIVE**\n\n**VALEMERE ROYAL INSTITUTE: CONNECTED**\n\n**NOCTIS DOMINION COLLEGE: CONNECTED**\n\n**INTER-NATIONAL TOURNAMENT BOARD: CONNECTED**\n\n**AURELIS FACULTY COUNCIL: CONNECTED**\n\nRhea stepped toward the crystal.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Challenge observer expansion.\"\n\nThe crystal answered in old script:\n\n**PUBLIC HARM REQUIRES PUBLIC WITNESS**\n\nAira stared at it.\n\nAira: \"That sounds noble enough to be dangerous.\"\n\nProfessor Halden's face hardened.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Lucien requested a stage.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"The Foundation provided one for a different reason.\"\n\nLeon: \"Can we shut it down?\"\n\nRhea's silence answered before her words did.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Not without appearing to hide the final activity from lawful witnesses.\"\n\nCassian: \"I am beginning to dislike lawful witnesses.\"\n\nMira, still seated near the record table with ink on three fingers, looked up.\n\nMira: \"Some of us are trying.\"\n\nCassian's expression changed immediately.\n\nCassian: \"Not you.\"\n\nMira nodded, only partly mollified.\n\nElira read the connected institutions again.\n\nElira: \"If something happens, they will all see.\"\n\nHer hand went to the pendant. This time I did not look away.\n\nHaru: \"They will.\"\n\nMy breath steadied against the cold air.\n\nCael stared at the Noctis line. No one had to ask what he was thinking. Nera said it anyway, because silence was how Noctis kept ownership alive.\n\nNera: \"They will try to make you perform for them even through a relay.\"\n\nCael's mouth tightened.\n\nCael: \"Then I will not.\"\n\nOric looked at him.\n\nOric: \"Good.\"\n\nHe sounded like he wanted to believe the word could be enough.\n\nAira rubbed both hands over her face.\n\nAira: \"Of course he names himself observer. Stars forbid murder be underdressed.\"\n\nElira stood beside me, her shoulder touching mine deliberately. The eastern windows had begun to pale. Dawn was arriving whether I named it or not.\n\nCael rose from the bench. Leon watched him carefully, and Cael noticed.\n\nCael: \"I am not going to run ahead.\"\n\nLeon: \"Good.\"\n\nCael: \"I am also not going to run away.\"\n\nLeon looked at him for a long moment.\n\nLeon: \"Also good.\"\n\nElira took my hand in front of everyone.\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nAira made a sharp sound. Elira corrected herself before Aira could.\n\nElira: \"I am afraid. I am staying. I am choosing with my eyes open.\"\n\nThe chapel pool answered in silver.\n\n**PRIMARY WITNESS STATEMENT ENTERED**\n\nLucien's voice arrived through the notice crystal, soft and amused.\n\nLucien: \"Then let us see what open eyes do when the blade finally stops hiding.\"\n\nThe crystal went dark. Dawn touched the old chapel. No one looked ready. We went anyway.\n\nThe walk down to the final activity felt longer than the corridors allowed. Council students stood along the route in pairs, red-trimmed uniforms pressed neat over faces that were not. Some had fought in no battles larger than exam disputes. Some had spent the last two days carrying sealed messages, guarding doors, and learning that law could bruise if held long enough.\n\nAs we passed, none of them saluted. Rhea had forbidden it, saying salutes made moments look cleaner than they were. Instead, each pair spoke the same line in their own voices.\n\nCouncil student: \"Witnesses present.\"\n\nAnother: \"Witnesses present.\"\n\nYounger student: \"Witnesses present.\"\n\nCael heard every one. By the fifth pair, his shoulders had begun to tremble. Leon walked beside him, not touching.\n\nLeon: \"Still here?\"\n\nCael: \"Still here.\"\n\nLeon: \"Good.\"\n\nAt the stairwell, Nera stopped long enough that Oric stopped too. She looked back up the corridor toward the academy above us.\n\nNera: \"If Noctis cuts the relay after this, my family may never hear my testimony.\"\n\nOric's face tightened. Rhea turned.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then we duplicate it before entry.\"\n\nNera blinked.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis emergency record, Valemere neutral archive, Green Circle public copy.\"\n\nMira nearly dropped her folio.\n\nMira: \"I can take it.\"\n\nNera looked at her. Mira straightened, terrified again and doing it anyway.\n\nMira: \"I mean, I can carry the copy. Not alone. With the others. But yes.\"\n\nNera's expression softened in a way I had not seen before.\n\nNera: \"Thank you.\"\n\nOric looked at Rhea.\n\nOric: \"Mine too.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Both.\"\n\nThe duplication took ninety seconds, and it mattered enough to spend them. Lucien wanted every path to narrow toward the field. Rhea kept making paths branch.\n\nWhen we reached the upper door to the stairwell, Elira stopped beside me. She did not say I love you, because Lucien had already shown he would turn any intimate truth into a handle if the room let him. Instead, she adjusted my sleeve where the false Green Circle rested over my wrist.\n\nElira: \"Stay with the witnesses.\"\n\nHaru: \"Stay with the room.\"\n\nElira nodded once, her fingers lingering at my cuff before dropping away.\n\nElira: \"And with yourself.\"\n\nThe upper door swung open before I could answer. Cold air rose from the steps below, Foundation cold carrying old decisions in it.\n\n## The Record Cael Chooses\n\nBefore we descended across the threshold, Professor Valen stopped beside Cael. She did not touch him.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"One more record.\"\n\nCael looked exhausted.\n\nCael: \"Now?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Before the room asks you to be simpler.\"\n\nHe understood then, and so did Nera. She stepped to his other side, Oric just behind her. Valen held out a blank page.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"State what you are not.\"\n\nCael stared at the page. His first breath shook. The second did not.\n\nCael: \"I am not Noctis property.\"\n\nThe page wrote it in silver.\n\nCael: \"I am not a return route.\"\n\nA second silver line burned onto the paper.\n\nCael: \"I am not a reserved life.\"\n\nNera's face crumpled and steadied.\n\nCael: \"I am afraid of believing those things only when people are watching.\"\n\nValen's eyes softened.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"That can be part of the record.\"\n\nCael looked at the page.\n\nCael: \"Then write that I am still saying them.\"\n\nThe page accepted the line. Rhea sealed it.\n\nThe Foundation light beneath the stair answered Cael's words before the final field could assign him another role.\n\n**CAEL NOX: SELF-DEFINITION ENTERED**\n\n**VOTIVE RETURN: NO PRESUMED CONSENT**\n\n**ROLE ASSIGNMENT CANNOT SUPERSEDE RECORDED REFUSAL**\n\nThe final activity could still hurt him. It could no longer claim no one had heard him refuse.\n\nLeon looked away, jaw tight. Cassian pretended not to see. Aira did not pretend. She wiped her face with her sleeve.\n\nAira: \"Good. Horrible. Perfect. I hate all of this.\"\n\nCael almost smiled. Aira tore three sheets from the back of her notebook.\n\nAira: \"May I copy it?\"\n\nCael looked at the silver statement.\n\nCael: \"Why three?\"\n\nAira: \"Because one record can be taken and two can be called disagreement. Three becomes inconvenient.\"\n\nCael considered that with the seriousness the academy usually reserved for treaties.\n\nCael: \"One to Nera. One to Oric. One stays outside.\"\n\nHe did not give the third copy to Lucien's delegation or to the Council. He chose people before institutions and watched Aira write the choice into the margin.\n\nCassian held a small flame under the pages until the ink dried. No joke. Leon stood where Cael could see him without mistaking the position for a guard. Mira waited by the stairwell rail with both hands around her pencil, frightened enough to shake and stubborn enough not to hide it.\n\nRhea looked at each of us in turn.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Last route rules. If the city names one person the center, refuse the map. If it separates us, name the missing before you name the threat. If it asks for sacrifice, ask who wrote the need before anyone volunteers.\"\n\nLeon: \"And if the floor opens?\"\n\nCassian: \"At last. A question with a practical answer.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then you move. Philosophy may follow at a safe distance.\"\n\nThat almost made Leon smile.\n\nElira checked the clasp of her pendant. The gesture was precise, though her fingers lingered there.\n\nElira: \"One more rule.\"\n\nRhea waited. Elira looked at me.\n\nElira: \"If Haru's disguise fails, no one argues about what he is before recording what happened to make it fail.\"\n\nThe hidden circle near my heart tightened.\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nElira: \"Sequence before spectacle. You taught me that.\"\n\nAira wrote it on all three copies.\n\nAira: \"Annoyingly sound.\"\n\nI looked down the stair where the Foundation cold kept rising. Seven possible exits marked the landing walls. Six were too symmetrical. The honest route was the narrow one with no crest above it and old scratches at shoulder height where frightened people had once carried one another through. I pointed.\n\nHaru: \"That door is real.\"\n\nCael folded his statement around the copy meant for Nera.\n\nCael: \"Then we use the real door.\"\n\nAt the bottom of the stair, the unmarked door beneath the chapel swung inward without a sound.\n\nBeyond it, the Wounded City looked peaceful.\n\nSilver relay windows ignited along the witness rail as we crossed into sight: Valemere, Noctis, the Tournament Board, and every institution that had learned to call watching a form of care. Rhea lodged one final formal objection on the threshold. The Foundation answered in old script, cold stone and the smell of drying ink anchoring the words.\n\n**PUBLIC HARM REQUIRES PUBLIC WITNESS**\n\nNoctis did not object.\n\nAcross the false dawn, Lucien waited beside the restoration pool with his hands folded behind his back. Elira's pendant warmed once at her throat. Cael's statement page trembled in his grip, silver script glowing faintly around the words **I AM NOT A RETURN ROUTE**.\n\nThe city bell struck its first note, and we stepped through the real door into the final stage.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-12-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep13","label":"Episode 13","number":"13","file":"episode-13-cover.webp","fallback":"The 48 Hours","eyebrow":"Siege Preparation","recap":"","accent":["#111111","#2ec4b6","#ffd166"],"displayLabel":"Episode 13","displayNumber":"13","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"The Black Circle Unbound","body":"The real door sealed behind us before the city bell finished its first note.\n\nFalse dawn flooded the streets beyond the chapel threshold. Narrow alleys folded around a cracked fountain basin filled with dark water, civic banners hung dead in windless air, and a bell tower leaned over the route like a witness already coached. The air smelled of wet stone, burned sugar, and the copper edge of a spell waiting for blood.\n\nElira stood beside me with the black pendant quiet at her throat, too pale and too steady. Cael moved between Leon and Maelin as if every step had to be negotiated with a body Noctis had taught to apologize for hurting. Aira carried the margin record. Rhea carried the formal one. Mira carried three pencils and looked ready to bite anyone who called that excessive.\n\nThe route sorted us in stages: witnesses to the stone rails, healers to the outer ring, then Elira, Cael, and me into the central square where the restoration pool waited under a sky too bright to be real. The pendant warmed once when Elira chose to stand beside Cael. The bell tower answered with frost.\n\nAcross the pool, Lucien watched the exit disappear. No weapon. No escort. Just the quiet certainty of someone who had already decided which one of us the field would call expendable. That should have frightened me sooner.\n\nThe false streets rearranged behind us. Seven exits narrowed to four, one for every public Circle color, each door bright enough to suggest safety. The unmarked route we had entered by vanished first. White Foundation script surfaced around Cael's boots and climbed toward the statement page in his grip.\n\n**CONDUIT SUPPORT DISPLACED FROM ORIGIN INSTITUTION**\n\nThe silver letters he had chosen, **I AM NOT A RETURN ROUTE**, burned bright against the claim instead of yielding to it.\n\nCael looked down, terrified and astonished.\n\nCael: \"It heard me.\"\n\nLucien: \"It recorded you. Hearing requires judgment.\"\n\nThe pool darkened at its center.\n\nNera struck the witness rail with both palms.\n\nNera: \"Noctis reclamation grammar. Cael, do not answer destination.\"\n\nCael backed away from the water.\n\nCael: \"I refuse return.\"\n\nHis statement flashed silver. For one breath, the claim stopped. Then a second line opened beneath it in older script.\n\n**REFUSAL UNDER EXTERNAL DEPENDENCY REQUIRES ANCHOR REVIEW**\n\nElira's pendant turned cold enough for frost to spiderweb across its chain.\n\nI saw the trap assemble. It did not challenge Cael's refusal directly; it renamed everyone who had helped him as proof the refusal wasn't his. My protection became influence. Nera's testimony became institutional rivalry. Leon's presence became coercive attachment. Elira's promise became the primary anchor.\n\nDominion Severance never began by cutting. It began by making every act of care read like ownership.\n\nHaru: \"Cael. Hold the page. Don't let the field choose your subject.\"\n\nCael pressed the paper against his chest.\n\nCael: \"I refuse because I refuse.\"\n\nThe nearest Noctis door cracked down the middle. Lucien's composure slipped for half a heartbeat.\n\nThen he raised two fingers.\n\nLucien: \"Primary anchor review.\"\n\nRhea's red council seal struck the rail before the last syllable landed.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Denied. The protected witness has answered independently.\"\n\nThe field accepted her ruling. The Noctis script ignored it.\n\nA hair-thin thread of colorless light rose from Cael's shadow and glided across the pool toward Elira. It moved slowly enough to look avoidable, just as Cael had warned. The spell only needed a good person to take one step forward.\n\nElira saw it reach for him. She moved.\n\nHaru: \"Elira, no!\"\n\nGold unfolded from both her hands, not around herself and not toward me. She threw it over Cael like an open shelter with the door left unlatched from inside.\n\nElira: \"His refusal is his.\"\n\nThe colorless thread kissed her barrier. For half a heartbeat, she held it.\n\nSunlight detonated across the false city. Every gold edge of her Circle sharpened to glass. The pendant at her throat flared dark, caught between guarding her life and parsing the poisoned definition the field had wrapped around protection.\n\nI drove my hand through the stone toward the Severance root buried beneath the pool, braided into old rescue grammar. Pulling it should have snapped the claim.\n\nInstead, the spell read my reach as proof of interference. The colorless line doubled.\n\nHaru: \"Drop the shield! I have the root!\"\n\nElira glanced back at Cael. He was on his knees, Noctis script climbing his healer seal while his chosen statement still burned white against his chest.\n\nElira: \"You have him.\"\n\nNot a question. She pushed the shield outward.\n\nGold swept Cael clear of the claim line.\n\nThe Severance path whipped inward through the gap her magic left behind. It slipped beneath the pendant's authority, struck below her collarbone, and snuffed every gold thread in a single silent stroke. No blast. No thunder.\n\nElira drew breath as if the air had turned to glass. Her eyes found mine. Then her knees gave out.\n\nI crossed the square before she hit the stone. The false Green crest shattered at my wrist as I caught her, pale shards of harmless light dying between our sleeves.\n\nHer pendant struck my knuckles, dark and cold. Cael screamed her name. Lucien lowered his hand. The field wrote:\n\n**PRIMARY ANCHOR SEVERED**\n\nI pressed my palm over Elira's heart. Nothing answered. No one moved. So the world broke instead.\n\nThe Wounded City peeled away from me in razor-straight lines. Cobblestone streets lifted off their foundations and turned upward like pages. Glass panes shattered and hung suspended in the air. The bell tower bent backward until its silver bell aimed straight at the false sky and rang without a single sound.\n\nElira lay slack in my arms. No breath. No pulse. No gold.\n\nBehind me, the Black Circle tore open in concentric rings of pitch black and starlight silver, counter-rotating with terrifying precision. Ancient runes ignited one after another along the arcs, dragging the light out of the false dawn.\n\nThe false Green disguise was gone.\n\nThe rings locked behind my shoulders. Six hours minimum. A full witness cycle.\n\nThe Foundation systems had taken in too many eyes, too many live relays, too many contested claims. Until the cycle anchored, the Black Circle would not fold back into stillness.\n\nPower flooded the square, and precision vanished with it. I could not mend Elira's chest without risking a structural rewrite over the wound Cael's shield had left open; Tier 3 would bind death, doctrine, witness, and sacrifice into one irreversible law. I could not cut the relays to spare us from being watched, adjust the pendant, hide the rings from terrified students, or step out of the chamber without dragging the manifestation straight through every ward in Aurelis above us.\n\nEvery witness at the rails saw enough to know the world had lied about what I was. The public gallery recoiled. Mira did not.\n\nHer face had gone chalk-white, tears tracking through soot, yet she gripped a second pencil with shaking fingers.\n\nMira: \"Record...\"\n\nHer voice cracked. She forced it back down into her throat.\n\nMira: \"Record that Elira Vale was killed by Dominion Severance while shielding Cael Nox from unlawful reclamation.\"\n\nThe relay windows carried her words outward too.\n\nWithout Mira's sentence, the first global image of the Black Circle would have been pure terror: no classroom diagram, no scholar's legend, only a boy kneeling over a dead girl while an unclassed celestial circle tore a city apart. Because she spoke first, half the world had to hear the sequence before fear finished writing the headline.\n\nAcross the hovering panes, distant rooms fractured in glimpses the Foundation could not mute.\n\nA Valemere official lunged up so hard his chair toppled backward. A Noctis observer reached for a blackout seal and jerked his hand back from frost-burned crystal. Far above us in Aurelis, shouts echoed down stone stairwells as notice crystals bloomed black and silver down every corridor.\n\nThe Tournament Board relay flashed emergency script and failed to mask the spinning rings behind it.\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED FIFTH-RING EVENT RECORDED**\n\n**PUBLIC WITNESS LOCK ACTIVE**\n\n**DO NOT ISOLATE SAFEGUARD**\n\nThat final line came from old Aurelis, not the Board, writing itself across every channel before anyone could vote on it.\n\nAira stared at Mira as if that single sentence had kept one corner of the world upright. Then Aira turned to me.\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nMy name reached me through the roaring pressure. It could not hold me.\n\nLucien stood across the cracked fountain basin, eyes fixed on the Black Circle. His face shifted from shock to naked recognition, then hunger. I hated that hunger more than I hated his fear.\n\nLucien: \"There.\"\n\nThe word trembled on his tongue before he smoothed it flat.\n\nLucien: \"There you are.\"\n\nHe realized the world was watching, and the audience steadied him. His eyes flicked from the spinning rings to the witness-locked relays, already salvaging a triumph from the wreckage. He had wanted proof of what I hid, never mind that Dominion Severance lay exposed beside Elira's body and Cael's reclamation claim burned across the fountain stone.\n\nLucien: \"Noctis witnesses.\"\n\nHis voice sharpened toward the distant pane.\n\nLucien: \"Record the unclassified threat.\"\n\nThe Noctis window flickered. Mira shouted straight over him.\n\nMira: \"Record the killing first!\"\n\nAira's head whipped toward her. Mira didn't blink.\n\nMira: \"Record the sequence. Cael was claimed. Elira intervened. Dominion Severance executed. Then Haru's circle broke open.\"\n\nHer whole body shook so hard her pencil rattled against the rail.\n\nMira: \"Then. Not before.\"\n\nThe Foundation pool flashed silver.\n\n**SEQUENCE LOCK ENTERED**\n\nLucien glared at Mira with sudden, unguarded irritation. Mira flinched, then squared her shoulders.\n\nI lifted one hand.\n\nThe space between Lucien and me emptied itself, wards and paving stones smoothed out of existence before they could spark. Seven layers of Noctis lightless defense snapped open around him, built to smother, redirect, and categorize hostile magic.\n\nThe Black Circle brushed them once. Seven layers turned into lines, then into syntax errors, then into nothing.\n\nLucien staggered one step back.\n\nThat single step cracked through the gallery like splitting ice.\n\nCassian: \"Stars.\"\n\nLeon: \"Haru, stop.\"\n\nI heard Leon's voice beside the rail. I understood every word. I didn't obey.\n\nThe Dominion Severance grammar still pulsed through the floor. I could see every root, every stolen clause that had turned Elira's choice into a kill path. I gripped it and pulled.\n\nThe city screamed without sound.\n\nNoctis relay lines flared far beyond the chamber, burning through the delegation wing, mirror routes, courtesy filings, apology ash, and every clean sentence Lucien had ever sharpened into a knife. Farther still, the lines shot out to Noctis Dominion College, to Valemere through Talen Mire's testimony link, to sealed tournament ledgers and every official who had signed off on calling student safety a neutral rule.\n\nI saw them all as raw threads: silver for witness truth, black where Dominion hid ownership inside care, gold where Elira had stood, faint blue where Aira built protocols behind dry jokes, red where Rhea forced authority to answer students, green where Mira's gallery wrote what powerful men preferred summarized. And at the center, a ragged wound where Cael's life had been mapped as a route.\n\nThe Black Circle knew how to correct broken routes. It knew nothing of mercy unless I held mercy in front of it with both hands, and my hands were shaking around a cooling body.\n\nI could strip every linked clause from this witness cycle in one breath.\n\nProfessor Halden shouted from the rail.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not classify the response! Do not name it!\"\n\nHis warning wasn't for me. It was for every official watching through glass.\n\nRhea drove her red council seal against the shivering public rail.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"All witnesses remain present. Nobody runs alone.\"\n\nHer authority cracked at the edges. She held it anyway.\n\nAira stepped onto the activity floor.\n\nThe air around me bent her Blue Circle sideways. She nearly went down before Cassian caught her arm.\n\nAira: \"Let go.\"\n\nCassian: \"Terrible idea.\"\n\nAira: \"I know. Let go.\"\n\nCassian let her arm drop.\n\nShe walked toward me one step at a time, face wet, furious, and terrified.\n\nAira: \"Haru, I need you to hear one person who isn't the thing waking up inside you.\"\n\nThe Black Circle spun faster. The floor split straight under her boots.\n\nI snapped the crack shut before it could swallow her. Only a sliver of me still recognized Aira as someone who should not fall, and that sliver was barely enough to keep the stone whole beneath her shoes.\n\nShe hated being watched through the relay windows. Her jaw went sharp with the anger of someone denied privacy by history, yet she kept walking.\n\nAira: \"Everyone outside this room, write this down if you have hands. He is not calm. He is not safe. He is also not Lucien's proof.\"\n\nThe Tournament Board relay flickered at her unauthorized address. Aira pointed straight at the glass without taking her eyes off me.\n\nAira: \"Yes, I mean you too.\"\n\nRhea drove her red seal behind Aira, bracing the relay frames against the pressure tearing at the chamber.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aira Solen speaks as protected witness.\"\n\nProfessor Halden added, voice rough.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"And as autonomous actor.\"\n\nAira: \"Lovely. Put that on my grave if this goes badly.\"\n\nCassian's voice cracked behind her.\n\nCassian: \"Don't joke about that.\"\n\nAira didn't turn.\n\nAira: \"Then make it unnecessary.\"\n\nShe stopped ten paces from the spinning rings.\n\nAira: \"Elira told you to stay Haru.\"\n\nElira. The name cut straight through the noise.\n\nAbove us, every silver banner turned black. Lucien let out one thin, strained laugh.\n\nLucien: \"You see? Mercy without the lie of restraint. A god correcting inconvenience.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nThe Wounded City yanked every authorized foothold out from beneath his observer seal, dropping him to one knee on bare stone.\n\nHaru: \"Do not speak about her.\"\n\nMy voice came out flat and quiet. That frightened the gallery more than shouting ever could.\n\nLucien forced his chin up, blood trickling from his nose.\n\nLucien: \"Then make me silent.\"\n\nI almost did. The Black Circle found the vibration of his voice in the air, ready to erase it. It would have been effortless. Elira lay cold in my arms; the world had refused her, so I could rewrite the world.\n\nThen Cael screamed, not from pain, but from recognition.\n\nHe tore free from Leon and bolted toward the cracked fountain basin where silver-black water had begun to gather. Nera lunged after him.\n\nNera: \"Cael, no!\"\n\nOric vaulted the stone rail badly and hit the ground on one knee.\n\nOric: \"Do not start it!\"\n\nCael didn't look heroic. He looked battered, terrified, and far too young to carry a spell built to spend his life. His stride hitched. The healer seal on his wrist strobed black, then silver, then black again. The silver thread wrapped around his damaged hand unraveled as he ran, trailing bright strands across the broken flagstones.\n\nLeon threw himself forward. Cassian caught Leon's sleeve, lost his grip as the fabric tore, and missed him.\n\nLeon: \"Cael!\"\n\nCael didn't slow. He had already decided before his first step landed.\n\nAira whipped around from me.\n\nAira: \"Rhea, block the pool!\"\n\nRhea's red seal slammed into the fountain rim. The pool accepted her authority, curved straight around it, and kept rising.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"It recognizes his training path.\"\n\nNera's voice broke.\n\nNera: \"Because Noctis carved the path inside him.\"\n\nProfessor Valen gripped the rail.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Then we object before he speaks.\"\n\nMira was already writing on the stone rail.\n\nMira: \"Objection before activation.\"\n\nOric reached the fountain's edge and slammed both palms down, white chalk exploding off his fingers.\n\nOric: \"Votive Return is coercive doctrine. Conduit willingness compromised by prior training.\"\n\nThe pool flared.\n\n**OBJECTION ENTERED**\n\nCael reached the water anyway. The Black Circle held the collapsing city still, neither opening the return path nor blocking it; Noctis's own carved grammar inside Cael's channels triggered the prepared Votive route while my power kept the sky from falling on top of him.\n\nCael plunged both hands into the silver-black water. Old script erupted around his arms.\n\n**VOTIVE RETURN DETECTED**\n\n**CONDUIT LIFE TRANSFER**\n\n**WARNING: FATAL**\n\nSeven shallow handprints opened around the fountain rim, the distribution positions Liora had shown us.\n\nAira reached the first. Rhea slapped the second with her council seal burning. Mira dropped her pencil and pressed her ink-stained palm to the third. Nera and Oric seized opposite sides. Leon hit the sixth. Cassian slammed into the seventh hard enough to splash silver-black water across his sleeves.\n\nFor one heartbeat, the weight shifted. Pain tore across all seven faces.\n\nThen every handprint folded inward. Cael screamed.\n\nBlack threads surfaced beneath the skin of his wrists, one from each witness station, knotting straight back into his ruined channels. Noctis hadn't merely stripped away distribution; it had wired the spell to borrow seven witnesses and dump their debt onto the conduit anyway.\n\nNera went white.\n\nNera: \"They folded the seven into him.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Release the positions! Now! Every person holding increases his load!\"\n\nNobody wanted to pull back. Cael's knees buckled against the stone rim, and that forced us to let go.\n\nThe Black Circle mapped all seven knots instantly. I could shred them in a breath, and I could feel what lay braided through them: Cael's remaining channels and the last intact thread back to Elira's heart. Brute force wouldn't spare him; it would tear both routes apart.\n\nHaru: \"I can break the pool. I cannot break it and keep her return path.\"\n\nCael looked across at me through tears.\n\nCael: \"Then don't lie and call breaking it rescue.\"\n\nHis hands were still free enough to pull out. Noctis had left the only uncontested exit to the boy they had trained never to walk away.\n\nAira spun back toward the fountain.\n\nAira: \"Cael!\"\n\nLeon reached him and locked both hands on his shoulders.\n\nLeon: \"No. You do not get to do this.\"\n\nCael looked up at him, terrified and completely lucid.\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nLeon tightened his grip.\n\nLeon: \"Then stop.\"\n\nCael's voice shook.\n\nCael: \"I cannot carry another person dying because I was the route.\"\n\nNera dropped beside the rim, breathless.\n\nNera: \"That is Noctis talking through the wound.\"\n\nCael looked from Nera to Oric to Leon.\n\nCael: \"No. Noctis taught me my life only counted when someone else spent it.\"\n\nSilver light brightened around his submerged forearms.\n\nCael: \"This is not that.\"\n\nNera shook her head so hard loose hair whipped out of her braid.\n\nNera: \"You don't make it true just by saying it.\"\n\nThe silver-black water crept past his wrists.\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nNera: \"Then stop.\"\n\nCael's face twisted.\n\nCael: \"If I stop because Noctis built this spell, they still own what it means.\"\n\nOric: \"Cael, meanings don't need you dead.\"\n\nCael looked at him.\n\nCael: \"No. People do.\"\n\nThat quieted the whole edge of the pool. He fought for air, words coming out jagged.\n\nCael: \"Elira died because she wouldn't let me be taken. Haru is breaking because she's gone. Everyone outside sees the Black Circle as a monster because Lucien made grief the first proof.\"\n\nHis breath hitched.\n\nCael: \"If I give one answer that's mine, something Noctis didn't order and Lucien didn't calculate, maybe the world sees something else too.\"\n\nAira was crying openly now, furious at her own tears.\n\nAira: \"That is too much weight for one person.\"\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nAira: \"Stop agreeing like that fixes anything.\"\n\nA tiny smile touched Cael's mouth, only because it was Aira.\n\nCael: \"It doesn't fix it.\"\n\nHis eyes moved to Elira in my arms, then up to me.\n\nCael: \"It answers it.\"\n\nElira's body grew lighter against my chest. Hair-thin threads of gold rose from the colorless wound below her collarbone. Both the Black Circle and I saw them lift.\n\nHaru: \"Cael.\"\n\nFor the first time since Elira fell, my voice sounded almost human. Cael gave a small, crooked smile.\n\nCael: \"They used me like a spell.\"\n\nHe sank his hands deeper into the water. Nera lunged to drag him back, and the pool threw her hands clear. Oric slammed a countermark onto the stone rim; it flared white and burned out.\n\nCael kept his eyes on mine.\n\nCael: \"You saved me like I was a person.\"\n\nThe gold threads weaving into Elira thickened. Her black pendant gave one faint, steady pulse. Above us, the terrible geometry of the Black Circle paused at the brink of erasing Lucien from the floor.\n\nCael's healer seal cracked down the center. Leon shouted his name and surged forward.\n\nCassian caught Leon around the ribs before he could hurl himself into the pool and turn one death into two. Leon thrashed against him. Cassian locked his arms tighter, jaw clenched.\n\nCassian: \"Don't make me be right about this!\"\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Cassian - Precision\n\nCassian Veyr had set things on fire before he learned that fire left ash you couldn't talk your way out of. After that, he became precise.\n\nPeople mistook precision for easy temperament because it let them relax around him. Cassian the amused Red Circle heir. Cassian with the clean flame and the cleaner insult, quick enough with a joke that whole rooms filed him under harmless. That was useful. It was also a lie.\n\nAnger lived inside him with impeccable posture. It showed up in classrooms where Green Circle students were praised for taking hits instead of skill, in halls where faculty said promising when they meant useful, and in Cael's eyes whenever someone offered kindness and Cael checked for the invoice before the warmth.\n\nCassian had learned not to burn in front of an audience. He smiled, angled the heat, and sent wit out ahead of him so nobody checked what it was covering.\n\nNow Leon was fighting him hard enough to bruise ribs, Cael was kneeling at the pool with death climbing past his elbows, and there wasn't a clever line on earth big enough to stand between Cassian and the fact that holding Leon back was right.\n\nRight. What a rotten word.\n\nHe could let go. Leon would reach the water, maybe break the transfer, maybe die beside Cael and turn one funeral into two. Cassian didn't know. That was the whole curse of precision: when you couldn't predict the blast radius, restraint meant locking your arms and taking the hatred.\n\nLeon hated him for holding on. Good. Hatred meant Leon was still breathing.\n\nCassian didn't crack a single joke. For once, there was nothing left to hide behind.\n\n---\n\nAira dropped to her knees at the fountain rim.\n\nAira: \"Cael, listen to me. You don't owe us.\"\n\nTears ran straight down Cael's cheeks.\n\nCael: \"I know.\"\n\nAira shook her head fiercely.\n\nAira: \"No, you don't get to agree politely and keep dying.\"\n\nCael's smile hurt to look at.\n\nCael: \"I'm not paying a debt.\"\n\nThe silver-black water climbed toward his shoulders.\n\nCael: \"I'm choosing what my life means.\"\n\nProfessor Valen stood at the rail with one hand pressed over her mouth. Halden had gone gray. Rhea's red seal trembled while Mira wrote through blinding tears, keeping someone from rewriting the order of what happened here.\n\nThe Foundation voice echoed across the stone.\n\nFoundation: \"Witness objections active. Conduit proceeds voluntarily.\"\n\nAira whipped her head up.\n\nAira: \"Voluntary under trauma is not clean consent!\"\n\nOld script answered across the surface of the water.\n\n**OBJECTION ENTERED**\n\n**DOCTRINE DAMAGED**\n\n**TRANSFER ALREADY BEGUN**\n\nNera let out a ragged, furious sob.\n\nNera: \"I'm sorry.\"\n\nCael: \"You came back.\"\n\nOric gripped the fountain rim.\n\nOric: \"Cael.\"\n\nCael looked straight at him.\n\nCael: \"Tell them the grammar. All of it. No pretty words.\"\n\nOric nodded hard, jaw clamped shut so he wouldn't break apart in front of the relays.\n\nPale silver light swelled from inside Cael's chest. Across my knees, Elira drew a sharp, ragged, impossible breath.\n\nEvery ring of the Black Circle contracted by a notch. Lucien saw it, and hunger twisted into fury.\n\nLucien: \"No.\"\n\nHe tried to stand. He couldn't; I hadn't given him the floor back.\n\nInside the Noctis relay pane, panic broke out. Senior figures in dark robes and polished rings lunged toward the frame, scrambling to sever the broadcast now that the spell they had buried inside children was burning in plain sight, and the boy using it was pulling a celestial catastrophe back from the edge.\n\nDarian Thorne stepped straight into the Noctis frame before their blackout seal could close. Behind him stood rows of Noctis students, some shaking, some weeping, some wearing the blank mask Noctis had drilled into them.\n\nDarian's Red Circle burned low and steady against his chest.\n\nDarian: \"Formal dissent. Dominion Severance is not correction. Votive Return as taught is not mercy. Conduits are not reserved lives.\"\n\nA senior voice barked from off-screen. Darian didn't flinch.\n\nDarian: \"This is not contamination. This is conscience. And if Noctis expels us for it, we will witness from outside.\"\n\nMore students stepped behind him. Not all of them, but enough. The blackout seal dropped across the pane.\n\nThe Noctis relay narrowed to a thread of white light, held open against the darkening glass by the Foundation witness lock.\n\nLucien's stillness fractured into loss. For the first time, his college didn't move behind him as one body.\n\nRhea drove her council seal into the fading pane, pointing her pen straight at the hovering glyph.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Noctis observer relay remains open by Foundation witness lock.\"\n\nThe blackout seal struck the lock and shattered. Distant Noctis figures recoiled.\n\nMira's voice rose from the rail.\n\nMira: \"Record attempted blackout after Votive Return activation.\"\n\nHer ink must have smeared across half the sheet by then. She kept writing anyway.\n\nProfessor Halden stared at the Noctis window with cold contempt.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"They wanted classification evidence.\"\n\nProfessor Valen answered, voice shaking.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"They received doctrine evidence.\"\n\nLucien heard them, his eyes fixed on Cael. For once, he had no neat sentence ready.\n\nCael's voice thinned to a whisper.\n\nCael: \"Haru.\"\n\nI looked at him. Across my arms, Elira drew another shallow, real breath.\n\nCael: \"This choice was mine.\"\n\nSilver light reached his throat.\n\nCael: \"You showed me I still had one.\"\n\nThen the pool took the rest of his light and poured it straight into Elira.\n\nHer heart kicked beneath my palm. Cael pitched forward as the water dropped; Cassian let go, and Leon caught him before he hit the stone.\n\nThe pool vanished from around Cael's wrists. Every shared handprint burned blank. Whatever prepared route had carried Elira back closed with his last breath, leaving no second crossing to bargain for and no spell left to turn back toward him.\n\nFor one beat, everyone lunged toward both of them, torn between which miracle to mourn first. Elira's eyelids fluttered open. She looked at me without understanding, then with far too much of it.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nThe Black Circle shuddered overhead. People watching through the relay windows saw Elira's chest rise and waited for the rings to fold away, as if grief obeyed relief. Instead, every ring cracked with bright silver light, still bound to the witnesses and claims the field had logged.\n\nI lowered her gently to the flagstones. My hands shook. Elira reached up weakly, and I caught her fingers.\n\nBehind us, Leon held Cael's body and made a sound that never became a word. Aira crawled to them with one hand pressed over her mouth. Nera bowed over Cael's hand, whispering Noctis prayers that no longer belonged to Noctis. Oric stood beside her, shaking so hard his countermark chalk snapped in his fist. Cassian turned away for one breath, dragged the heel of his hand across his eyes, then turned back because leaving grief alone felt cowardly.\n\nRhea's red seal settled gently over Cael in honor, not custody. Her voice broke once.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Cael Nox remains under Aurelis protection.\"\n\nThe Foundation floor answered in clean, merciless script.\n\n**TRANSFER COMPLETE**\n\n**ELIRA VALE: RESTORED**\n\n**CAEL NOX: DECEASED**\n\nMira stood at the rail, voice trembling across the broken square.\n\nMira: \"Add this.\"\n\nThe Foundation script paused.\n\nMira: \"Cael Nox was not property. He was not a tool. He was a witness who chose mercy after being saved by it.\"\n\nThe floor flared silver.\n\n**PUBLIC RECORD AMENDMENT ACCEPTED**\n\nLucien stared past Elira and me toward Cael, looking for the first time as if the world had answered in a language he couldn't edit. Then his eyes came back to the unstable black rings turning behind my shoulders.\n\nLucien: \"He made himself your proof.\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened weakly around mine. Alive.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe rings slowed, listening now though far from gone.\n\nHaru: \"He made himself his answer.\"\n\nThe Wounded City began to fold in on itself, out of language for what had happened. The relay windows stayed open just long enough to catch the false streets collapsing back into Aurelis chapel stone.\n\nThe world saw Leon cradling Cael's body. It saw Nera pressing her forehead to a dead boy's knuckles, old binding scars bare on her wrists for every Noctis observer to see. It saw Aira on her knees between Elira and Cael, unable to choose which impossible thing needed her first. It saw Elira Vale weeping before she could sit up, and me kneeling beside her beneath the unhidden Black Circle.\n\nBy morning, the world would speak those words wrong. Noctis would sharpen new laws before Cael's body cooled, and Aurelis would have to learn whether protection meant standing closer than fear wanted. Still, they had seen the order first.\n\nBlack and silver script surfaced one last time above Cael's body.\n\n**DOMINION SEVERANCE: EXPOSED**\n\n**VOTIVE RETURN: RECORDED AS FATAL COERCIVE DOCTRINE**\n\n**WITNESS RESTORATION: DAMAGED BUT NOT VOID**\n\n**SAFEGUARD ISOLATION: CATASTROPHIC RISK CONFIRMED**\n\nThen one smaller line appeared beneath the rest.\n\n**WITNESS LOST: CAEL NOX**\n\nThe relay windows began shutting down. Valemere's lingered longest: Soren Ashvale stood with one hand over his heart and the other braced against the frame, while behind him Talen Mire sat in a healer chair, face wet, braced hand lifted in farewell to a boy he had never met.\n\nThe Tournament Board pane collapsed under emergency script. Noctis tried to sever its window first and failed last. For one final second, the Noctis hall showed black-uniformed students staring at Cael's body while faculty shouted for them to disperse. Some obeyed. One younger boy didn't; he lifted a hand to his own bare wrist, where no healer seal glowed yet memory plainly did.\n\nThen the glass went dark, leaving Aurelis quiet in the way a living thing goes quiet after impact, waiting to see what still moves.\n\nRhea lowered herself onto the stone beside Cael. Presidents didn't kneel in official records outside ceremony. She knelt anyway.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Cael Nox, protected witness of Aurelis, deceased by voluntary activation of coercive Noctis doctrine under catastrophic pressure.\"\n\nHer voice caught on deceased, then steadied.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Record amended by public witness: his final act was his own answer, not Noctis property.\"\n\nThe Foundation accepted the entry.\n\nAira crept closer to Cael and stopped with her hand suspended above his sleeve, as if asking permission from someone who could no longer give it. Leon looked up, hollowed out.\n\nLeon: \"He would let you.\"\n\nAira let out a breath that sounded half like anger.\n\nAira: \"Don't make me laugh right now.\"\n\nLeon looked back down at Cael.\n\nLeon: \"I'm not.\"\n\nAira laid two fingers gently against Cael's sleeve.\n\nBeside them, Nera recited the Votive Return grammar again, translating every clause aloud into what it had cost. Oric repeated each line after her, reclaiming the words for the students still watching behind closed glass.\n\nElira tried to push herself upright. I steadied her shoulders. When she saw Cael across the floor, grief twisted her face hard enough to make the Black Circle flare again. She gripped my wrist.\n\nElira: \"Stay.\"\n\nOne word. Request, anchor, and witness at once. I stayed while she wept against my sleeve, the black rings still turning overhead, held back only by the people Cael had died to keep in the room.\n\nOutside the cracked chapel walls, full night had already fallen over Aurelis; the Foundation's false dawn had swallowed the entire day without asking. The first hour after catastrophe didn't behave like an hour. It fractured into tasks.\n\nMaelin reached the broken chapel before the last relay echo faded, two healers behind her and a white field case in hand.\n\nMaelin: \"Alive first.\"\n\nNobody argued, and nobody misunderstood. Alive first didn't mean Cael mattered less; it meant someone had to name the order before grief paralyzed the room. Elira's pulse fluttered under my thumb like a thread I wasn't allowed to touch.\n\nMaelin knelt beside us, checking Elira's throat, wrist, eyes, and the cold mark below her collarbone where Dominion Severance had slipped beneath the pendant. Maelin's mouth tightened.\n\nMaelin: \"Do not heal.\"\n\nI stared at her. Every oil lamp in the chapel bent toward us as the Black Circle shifted. Maelin didn't flinch. Her steadiness forced me to answer to it.\n\nMaelin: \"Haru, listen carefully. If you repair what Cael's return rewrote, her channels may read your correction as a second claim. I need her injured and alive, not perfect and contested.\"\n\nPerfect and contested. The words cut like legal steel. I had enough power in my palms to smooth every bruise, steady her heart, and erase every sign that she had ever died. What sickened me was realizing how much of that urge wanted the room obedient to my love, wanted the wound gone so I wouldn't feel accused by it.\n\nI uncurled my fingers slowly and let Maelin take Elira's wrist. Behind me, a ring spun backward, shuddered, and held. Maelin glanced once at the circle, then back to her patient.\n\nMaelin: \"Good.\"\n\nA medical note, not praise. That helped.\n\nAcross the chapel, Leon still hadn't let go of Cael. He sat on the stone with one arm braced behind Cael's shoulders as if a dead boy might slide into discomfort. His Blue Circle had shrunk to a dull band around his wrist while water pooled quietly in the cracks around his boots. Cassian crouched nearby, not touching, just close enough that anyone trying to take Cael without asking would have to walk through fire first. Every trace of his usual humor was gone.\n\nCassian: \"Leon.\"\n\nLeon didn't lift his head.\n\nLeon: \"If I move, they'll take him.\"\n\nNobody had threatened that, yet the world had taught him to expect it. Rhea heard. She walked over, knelt again, and set her red council seal on the stone beside Leon's knee rather than on Cael.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"No body transfer without named witness consent, medical confirmation, and public sequence copy.\"\n\nLeon swallowed hard.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"You are currently named witness.\"\n\nLeon: \"I don't know how.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"You're already doing it.\"\n\nThe seal registered Leon's proximity as record. He let out a shaky breath and stayed put.\n\nNera and Oric hovered at the edge of Cael's shadow, trained stillness left with nowhere to report. Nera's hands shook until the binding scars at her wrists blurred. Oric pulled out three scraps of paper. Across the room, Aira spotted paper even through tears.\n\nAira: \"Oric?\"\n\nOric's voice came out raw.\n\nOric: \"We should write the translation now.\"\n\nNera stared at him.\n\nOric: \"Before Noctis writes it first.\"\n\nWork moved her when comfort couldn't. She dropped to her knees on the floor and started with the first doctrine line.\n\n**WITNESS RESTORATION THROUGH HONORED CONDUIT**\n\nNera: \"A useful student dies where a ranked student can be seen surviving.\"\n\nOric copied it down. His hand shook badly enough that he spelled Cael's name wrong.\n\n**Cale.**\n\nHe stared at the letters as if Noctis had stolen one more thing while his head was bowed. Mira slid off the rail and crouched beside him.\n\nMira: \"Don't tear it out.\"\n\nOric's knuckles whitened on the page.\n\nMira: \"Cross it once. Write his name beside it. Let the mistake show we were here.\"\n\nOric drew a single line through the error and wrote **Cael Nox** next to it.\n\nNera read the next phrase aloud.\n\n**VOLUNTARY RETURN ACCEPTED**\n\nNothing followed. Her mouth opened, then shut. Oric held his pencil poised above the paper until his fingers cramped.\n\nNera: \"I can't.\"\n\nNobody told her to try again. Silence sat heavy over the paper until the Noctis words looked like ordinary ink again.\n\nMira looked toward Cael's empty spot by the basin.\n\nMira: \"What did he like?\"\n\nThe question was so ordinary the whole chapel nearly gave way around it. Leon buried his face. Oric stared blankly at the page until Nera finally answered.\n\nNera: \"Candied orange peel.\"\n\nHer voice cracked on the last word.\n\nNera: \"He pretended not to because Lucien hated sticky fingers on relay glass.\"\n\nMira wrote it on a clean sheet, separate from the doctrine notes.\n\n**Cael liked candied orange peel.**\n\nThen Nera whispered, barely audible.\n\nNera: \"He was fifteen when they put the first return phrase inside him.\"\n\nHalden turned half a step away, hiding his face from a gallery that was no longer watching, though not from us.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Fifteen.\"\n\nHe didn't ask for a citation or dress the number up as scholarship. He stood holding his black classification case, surrounded by the wreckage of adults learning too late. Then he set the case on the flagstones and clicked the latches shut.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"No classification work tonight.\"\n\nRhea looked across at him.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"If anyone asks, I refused access because Cael Nox hasn't been carried from the floor.\"\n\nHe hadn't called it significant or unprecedented. He had used Cael's name.\n\nRhea: \"Recorded.\"\n\nAira sat near Elira's feet and opened a fresh page, her usual headers waiting at the top. Under **ALIVE**, she wrote Elira. Under **DEAD**, she wrote Cael. The pencil tip punched straight through the paper.\n\nAira stared at the torn hole.\n\nAira: \"I don't know what comes next.\"\n\nMira laid the orange-peel sheet gently beside her knee.\n\nMira: \"Then don't make a list yet.\"\n\nAira shut the notebook.\n\nThe Black Circle hung open above me, strong enough to terrify distant capitals and useless against the fact that none of us had known what sweet Cael liked while he was alive. Then Elira stirred, eyelids fluttering halfway open. For one second she looked adrift between death and waking, until her eyes focused on my face. Her first expression was assessment, checking whether the room had gone dangerous while she was gone.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHearing my name in her voice nearly undid everything Maelin had just protected. The rings widened behind me. Elira saw the surge and lifted her fingers an inch off the warming cloth, choosing direction even when she couldn't reach.\n\nElira: \"Do not make being alive something I have to apologize for.\"\n\nI couldn't get a word past my throat. Maelin squeezed her eyes shut for one beat, then opened them, bright and furious.\n\nMaelin: \"Patient speaking beyond recommended limit.\"\n\nElira: \"Noted.\"\n\nHer gaze stayed locked on mine.\n\nElira: \"Say yes.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe single word steadied my breathing against the cold air, and the rings slowed again.\n\nOutside the chapel, Aurelis began to stir. Bootsteps echoed down corridors, faculty barked orders, council aides hurried with paper copies, and students refused to clear the stairwells. Somewhere near the steps, someone spoke Cael's name wrong and three voices corrected them at once.\n\nThe world was already moving on. Part of me hated it, and part of me needed it so the chapel wouldn't become my entire universe. Maelin bound Elira's wrist. Leon held Cael. Nera translated. Mira copied. Rhea guarded the floor. Halden kept his case shut. And I sat inside the most exposed magic event in living memory, learning that restraint after revelation wasn't hiding anymore; it was letting other people stay real while I was breaking.\n\nThe second hour belonged to removal.\n\nCael's body couldn't move until three records aligned: healer confirmation, witness consent, and sequence protection. Rhea kept the steps brisk and procedural because letting them sound like grief would have stopped the room cold.\n\nMaelin checked Cael twice. Once cleanly, as a healer.\n\nMaelin: \"No breath. No pulse. No recoverable channel activity. Votive Return completion fatal.\"\n\nThe second check belonged to the person who had adjusted his bandages, argued with him about sleep, and watched him stare at Elira's pendant as if survival had put a price tag on him. She left two fingers resting on his wrist far longer than procedure asked.\n\nMaelin: \"He was warm when I last checked him.\"\n\nMira waited one beat, then wrote that down too.\n\nLeon gave witness consent only after Rhea assured him it didn't mean surrendering Cael to strangers, only naming who could help carry him.\n\nLeon: \"Then Cassian.\"\n\nCassian met his eyes. Leon didn't look away.\n\nLeon: \"And Nera. If she wants.\"\n\nNera's breath caught while the room waited.\n\nNera: \"I want.\"\n\nHer voice nearly gave out on the word, so she said it louder.\n\nNera: \"I want to help carry him.\"\n\nOric stepped forward as well.\n\nOric: \"I can take the feet.\"\n\nCassian's expression shifted for half a second. He knew what Noctis drilled into lower-ranked students, teaching them to take the feet while ranked mourners took the shoulders. Oric had offered the place he'd been conditioned to claim before anyone could assign it. Cassian shook his head.\n\nCassian: \"No.\"\n\nOric stiffened.\n\nCassian: \"Shoulder with me.\"\n\nOric stared at him.\n\nCassian: \"Unless you object to my technique.\"\n\nCassian's kind of mercy always arrived disguised as competence. Oric stepped up beside him.\n\nLeon held Cael until the last possible second, then yielded him to Cassian, Oric, Nera, and Maelin. Rhea led the way with her protection seal. Aira carried the sequence copy. Mira carried the amendment. I walked with nothing in my hands, and that felt heavier than carrying.\n\nElira caught me staring at my empty palms from the unfolded healer cot, pale beneath three layers of warming cloth.\n\nElira: \"Do not make empty hands mean refusal.\"\n\nI looked down at her.\n\nElira: \"Sometimes they mean other people are allowed.\"\n\nThe Black Circle shifted behind me like a thought too vast for words.\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"Know it slower.\"\n\nI stayed beside the cot and breathed until my shoulders dropped.\n\nCael left the chapel through the old west arch.\n\nThe academy had gathered beyond the sealed line, packing the stairwell, the upper landing, and the corridor beyond it. Nobody spoke when the seal parted. They saw who carried him: Cassian, Oric, Nera, and Maelin instead of Noctis faculty or Aurelis clerks. Behind them walked Leon with shaking empty hands, Aira clutching paper like it weighed more than stone, Mira with ink across her wrist, and Rhea at the front, refusing to hide the dead behind rank.\n\nThe first whisper came from a Green Circle first-year I didn't recognize.\n\nFirst-year: \"His name?\"\n\nNobody rebuked him. Leon answered.\n\nLeon: \"Cael Nox.\"\n\nThe boy repeated it, and another voice picked it up, passing Cael's name down the corridor as plain correction after a world that had spent too long calling him conduit, transfer, asset, and return route.\n\nOnce Cael rested in the eastern preparation room under Leon, Nera, and Oric's watch, Rhea, Maelin, Aira, and Mira returned to the chapel.\n\nThe third hour belonged to me because everyone else had finally been given work, and that was when the danger sharpened.\n\nAs long as Elira needed me still, I could hold still. While Cael's body lay on the floor, grief had a center outside me. Once the chapel thinned, the Black Circle remembered it had no small shape.\n\nThe rings widened until silver runes grazed the upper arch. Stone dust lifted off the flagstones, and shattered pieces of the Wounded City began pulling toward my boots as if trying to rebuild a diagram. Halden saw it first.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Everyone out.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nMy voice sounded like a vault door shutting beneath stone. Halden froze. I forced myself to meet his eyes.\n\nHaru: \"If everyone leaves, the room becomes only me.\"\n\nHe understood faster than comfort allowed.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Then fewer. Not none.\"\n\nSix stayed: Rhea, Maelin, Elira on her cot, Aira counting what fear tried to blur, Mira finishing her final copy, and Halden with his black case shut. Enough witnesses to keep the chapel from turning into an altar.\n\nThe Black Circle didn't want enough. It wanted exact, whole, airtight. Every possible fix sat within reach of my fingers: I could seal the relay network, strip Noctis's private chambers bare, bind Lucien's name to Dominion Severance in unbreakable law, extract every student carrying a carved return phrase, and make every cautious Councilor regret asking if I was safe before asking who died.\n\nI could do so much good that nobody could refuse me. That was the terror: wanting obedience dressed up as mercy.\n\nElira's voice reached me from the cot.\n\nElira: \"Tell me one thing you are not doing.\"\n\nI turned my head. Exhaustion hollowed out her face, yet her eyes were steady.\n\nHaru: \"I am not closing the relays that already closed.\"\n\nElira: \"Good. Another.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am not repairing your wounds without Maelin's instruction.\"\n\nMaelin's hands paused over a bandage for one beat, then kept wrapping.\n\nElira: \"Another.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am not finding Lucien.\"\n\nThe chapel lamps bent hard enough to whine. Aira wrote faster.\n\nElira: \"Another.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am not making the world understand before it chooses to listen.\"\n\nSomething inside me gave way on that sentence, and the spinning rings slowed. Restraint wasn't sitting idle; it was naming the strike I refused clearly enough that refusal became real.\n\nMira's pencil scratched through the hush.\n\nMira: \"Can I record that?\"\n\nI almost laughed, awful and human.\n\nHaru: \"Write it down.\"\n\nI met Mira's eyes without blinking. She wrote:\n\n**Haru Aerlen declined to make the world understand before it chose to listen.**\n\nRhea read the line over Mira's shoulder.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"That sentence will become a legal problem.\"\n\nAira: \"Most honest sentences do.\"\n\nHalden studied the Black Circle without his old hunger for taxonomy.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"It may also become a safeguard definition.\"\n\nHaru: \"Do not make me a definition tonight.\"\n\nHe bowed his head once.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Recorded.\"\n\nThe fourth hour was just waiting.\n\nMaelin finally made Elira sleep. Aira carried copies up to the first protection table and came back with three more pages, two reports, and an unlabeled honey cake someone had pressed into her hands. She stared at it as if it had violated protocol.\n\nAira: \"Who gives emergency cake without documentation?\"\n\nMira didn't look up from copying.\n\nMira: \"Someone kind?\"\n\nAira looked offended by the lack of attribution and close to tears anyway. She broke the cake into pieces and passed them round without ceremony. Rhea took a bite because Aira glared at her; Halden forgot he was holding his until Cassian returned and told him cake wouldn't turn into data if he stared at it long enough.\n\nLeon came back last. His hands had been scrubbed clean, which somehow made it worse.\n\nHe stood inside the chapel doorway, looking from sleeping Elira to me to the Black Circle turning above the floor.\n\nLeon: \"I hated you once because I thought you were pretending to be weak.\"\n\nNobody moved.\n\nLeon: \"I was wrong.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was pretending.\"\n\nLeon shook his head.\n\nLeon: \"No. You were trying not to become what people like me respect too easily.\"\n\nThe words weren't polished. They had been paid for. He looked at the empty space where Cael had lain and swallowed hard.\n\nLeon: \"Don't let them turn him into the reason you stop trying.\"\n\nHe turned and left before I could answer.\n\nThe fifth hour brought the first wrong story through a Council emergency relay, unofficial enough to deny and loud enough to sting.\n\n**BLACK CIRCLE MANIFESTATION CAUSES STUDENT FATALITY DURING UNSANCTIONED RESPONSE**\n\nRhea read it, and the chapel went cold. Aira let out a word that would never survive formal minutes. Mira's face went hard with pure offense. Her chair scraped back.\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nRhea: \"Mira.\"\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nShe snatched the public sequence copy, slapped it flat on the relay table, and underlined three lines hard enough to score the second sheet underneath.\n\n**ELIRA VALE KILLED BEFORE BLACK CIRCLE MANIFESTATION.**\n\n**CAEL NOX ACTIVATED VOTIVE RETURN AFTER DOMINION SEVERANCE.**\n\n**BLACK CIRCLE EVENT FOLLOWED CATASTROPHIC WITNESS BREACH.**\n\nMira: \"If they want to lie, make them step over the order.\"\n\nRhea looked at her for one long second.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Send it.\"\n\nThe correction went out as an image of Mira's own handwriting, messy and furious and unmistakably human while institutions prepared to fight over jurisdiction. The lie didn't vanish, yet from that hour onward it had to drag the truth behind it.\n\nThe sixth hour ended without drama. No burst of shadow, no final bell, and no neat return to secrecy.\n\nThe Black Circle slowed until its outer rings thinned into smoke, then pressure, then the familiar cold behind my sternum. One inner ring stayed etched beneath the skin of my wrist. The false Green crest wouldn't come back on its own; I would have to rebuild it by hand in front of everyone who knew what it covered, choosing smallness while my fingers still shook.\n\nElira woke just as the final ring faded to shadow. Her eyes found my bare wrist. No Green yet. No black flare either. Just a hairline darkness beneath the skin, easy to miss and impossible for her not to see.\n\nShe reached for my hand. I gave it to her.\n\nShe turned my wrist palm up and rested two fingers against the center where no crest showed.\n\nElira: \"There you are.\"\n\nLucien had said those words like discovery meant ownership. Elira said them like presence was enough. I stayed quiet, listening to her breathe beside the people who had refused to become symbols for my grief.\n\nWhen Maelin cleared her to be moved upstairs, healers carried the cot into the quiet infirmary ward while I walked beside it, holding the last tremor of the circle still until Elira's channels stopped trying to remember death.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Aira - The Part That Gets Written\n\nAira Solen had always trusted records less than people assumed. She kept them anyway, because a record wasn't truth; it was a chair left in the room where truth might sit down before stronger people walked in with cleaner boots and renamed it.\n\nShe hadn't learned that in a grand tragedy. Grand tragedies gave adults too many excuses to sound profound afterward. She learned it from attendance sheets that made absence look identical whether a student was sick, frightened, punished, or short on carriage fare, and from teachers who knew ranked names by heart and Green Circle students by seating row.\n\nSo Aira wrote things down. First because she was good at it, later because competence hurt less than admitting she needed it to matter.\n\nHaru had been on his knees with the Black Circle tearing a city apart around him, and Aira's first reflex had still been sequence. That scared her worse than the spinning rings. Other people screamed, ran, or prayed; Aira hunted for the sentence with a missing subject and patched it before someone could weaponize the gap.\n\nShe didn't write only to protect Haru. She wrote because Elira lay dead on stone, Cael was being digested into Noctis grammar, Lucien was waiting to steal the first draft of history, and somebody had to stay sharp enough not to look away.\n\nThat was the bill nobody asked if she wanted to pay. Asking what it cost to be the room's memory was dangerous during an emergency, so she filed the question under later along with everything else.\n\nWhen the floor stopped splitting under Elira's knees and Mira forced the sequence lock into stone, Aira felt something behind her ribs fold itself smaller so she could keep writing.\n\nGood. That was Haru holding still. Record it.\n\nShe wrote the line down and never asked who would record what it cost her to keep writing.\n\n## Aira's Margin - Before Three\n\nIn the infirmary, Aira wrote the clean record first because clean records survived committees.\n\nThen she opened her second notebook for the margin, because clean records lied by omission even when every sentence was true.\n\nMargin: Haru hasn't slept and won't drop his grip on the circle until Maelin clears Elira's channels. Elira is pretending survival pain is a scheduling concern. Leon is down the hall in the eastern preparation room with Nera and Oric, guarding Cael's bier as if attention alone can keep Noctis from taking him. Cassian brought honey cake and bread nobody asked for and left them where people could reach without saying thank you. Rhea gets colder when frightened. Valen gets gentler when furious.\n\nAira paused, chewing the end of her pen.\n\nThen she added: When they read the official version of today, don't let it make us sound braver than we were. Fear was present. So was love. So was fury. None invalidate witness.\n\nThe margin ink dried crooked.\n\nBeneath the infirmary clock, Aira kept both notebooks open across her knees while Elira finally slept beneath three warming cloths and the minute hand crept toward three.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-13-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep14","label":"Episode 14","number":"14","file":"episode-14-cover.webp","fallback":"The Autonomy Proof","eyebrow":"Autonomy Proof","recap":"","accent":["#080808","#ffd166","#3a86ff"],"displayLabel":"Episode 14","displayNumber":"14","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"The Vigil","body":"The infirmary clock struck three before I understood that the night had continued without my permission.\n\nElira slept on Maelin's cot beneath three warming cloths, one hand open near the edge as if even unconscious she expected someone to need proof that she had returned by choice. Cael had been carried out of the broken chapel to the eastern preparation room under Rhea's seal while the healers stabilized her. Leon went with him. Nera and Oric went too. I had remained because Maelin asked me to hold the Black Circle still until Elira's channels stopped trying to remember death.\n\nThe circle no longer filled the chapel, yet its reflection turned behind me in every dark pane. Hiding it was impossible now. Leaving it unanswerable was worse.\n\nAira sat beneath the infirmary clock with two notebooks open across her knees. One held the clean sequence. The other held handwriting that leaned harder whenever she was angry.\n\nShe closed the second notebook when she saw me watching the ward glass brighten with relay pings.\n\nHaru: \"How fast is it spreading?\"\n\nAira: \"Fast enough that leaving Cael behind a closed door past dawn would be a mistake.\"\n\nShe rested her thumb against the edge of the margin book.\n\nAira: \"Rhea is moving his bier back to the Wounded City chamber at midmorning for an open vigil. If we keep him hidden, Noctis will claim him on paper before noon.\"\n\n---\n\nThe world could not wait for us to finish grieving. The relay windows had shown too much.\n\nEvery academy, council branch, and public record office with a functioning crystal had seen the Black Circle, Elira's death, Cael's choice, Noctis' coercive doctrine, and the old Aurelis warning not to isolate the safeguard.\n\nBy midmorning, messages gathered above the Wounded City chamber like storm clouds made of formal language. Cael lay in state before the restoration pool under Rhea's red protection seal, where anyone looking through an authorized crystal could see who guarded him.\n\nRhea did not read the dispatches aloud at first; she sorted them. Fear made some people reach for chains. Rhea reached for categories.\n\nRequests arrived for medical status, witness confirmation, Noctis comment, Black Circle containment, and Black Circle worship.\n\nThat last category made Aira put both hands over her face.\n\nAira: \"No. Absolutely not. We are not doing myth clubs today.\"\n\nCassian set another fallen stone aside.\n\nCassian: \"Only today?\"\n\nAira: \"Do not make me assign you to public relations while I am this emotionally available to violence.\"\n\nCassian looked toward Cael's bier. His smile did not come.\n\nCassian: \"Fair.\"\n\nRhea dismissed the worship requests without comment and archived every containment demand. I noticed. So did Elira, sitting wrapped in a coat beside the pool while Maelin's warming seal held her pulse steady.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Evidence.\"\n\nHer voice was flat.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Anyone whose first official response to a grieving student is containment can explain that instinct in daylight later.\"\n\nMira wrote that down. Finn's mouth bent in a grim line.\n\nFinn: \"Green Circle students are copying the archive twice.\"\n\nRhea looked up.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Three times.\"\n\nFinn blinked.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Institutions misplace inconvenient paper. Students misplace nothing when annoyed.\"\n\nMira's pencil paused.\n\nMira: \"Was that a compliment?\"\n\nRhea: \"It was an instruction.\"\n\nMira wrote faster.\n\nThe next message came sealed in silver and violet. Inter-National Schools Council. Magistra Senna Voss.\n\nThe chamber shifted when her name appeared. A council registry line opened beneath it before anyone could weaponize the surname.\n\n**SENNA VOSS: NORTHERN CIVIL SERVICE LINE**\n\n**NO DECLARED BLOOD OR HOUSE AFFILIATION WITH LUCIEN VOSS**\n\nSuspicion did not vanish, but the registry gave it somewhere visible to stand. Senna had been sent to observe risk and had watched a student die proving where the risk lived.\n\nThe message unfolded into a recorded statement. Senna stood in a council office with nothing behind her except a wall of floating case files, her hair pinned neatly, her face older than it had looked two days ago.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"This preliminary report is entered under emergency witness priority. I was dispatched to evaluate institutional instability surrounding Aurelis, Noctis Dominion College, and the contested Foundation Witness Trial.\"\n\nThe file panes behind her brightened.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"What I witnessed was not a spontaneous threat event. It was a sequence.\"\n\nMira's pencil stopped for the first time all morning.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"A student under Noctis doctrine was targeted through reclamation pressure. A second student intervened to prevent that harm. Dominion Severance killed her. Votive Return, a doctrine tied to fatal conduit expenditure, restored her at the cost of Cael Nox's life. The Black Circle manifested after the death event, under visible grief pressure, and did not attack witnesses despite ample capacity to do so.\"\n\nThe Black Circle behind me dimmed by a degree because sequence carried weight.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"The risk before this Council is not power beyond Yellow classification. The risk is the institutional reflex to isolate, claim, spend, or destroy students when ordinary categories fail. I recommend immediate protection of all Aurelis witnesses, suspension of Noctis final conduit clauses across member jurisdictions pending full investigation, emergency review of Dominion Severance as unlawful coercive magic, and explicit prohibition on isolated classification proceedings concerning Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nShe paused. For one moment, the formal mask thinned.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"If the Council's first act after Cael Nox's death is to ask how to contain the student he died beside, my resignation will be submitted before sunset.\"\n\nThe message ended. Silence held the chamber.\n\nAira wiped her face with both hands.\n\nAira: \"I take back three unkind things I said about her eyebrows.\"\n\nLeon stared at the empty air where Senna's record had been.\n\nLeon: \"Only three?\"\n\nAira: \"I am grieving, Leon. Let me be generous in stages.\"\n\nThe almost-laughter reached more than one person. It did not fix anything, but the room became easier to breathe in.\n\n---\n\nNoctis answered at noon, initially without Lucien.\n\nThe official notice arrived in black script with a pale border.\n\n**NOCTIS DOMINION COLLEGE REQUESTS RETURN OF STUDENT REMAINS**\n\n**SUBJECT: CAEL NOX**\n\n**STATUS: NOCTIS ENROLLED CONDUIT SUPPORT**\n\nThe Black Circle snapped into focus. Every rune lit at once, and the chamber temperature dropped. Several healers stepped back before they could stop themselves.\n\nI did not blame them. My first thought was uncontrolled: I could erase not the notice, but the whole old grammar behind it that kept finding new ways to call a dead boy useful.\n\nElira's hand slid over mine. Her palm was cold.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nMy name was doing too much work today. I breathed in, tasted cold metal behind my sternum, and breathed out. The notice remained. So did I.\n\nRhea stood. The red seal over Cael brightened until clean light crossed the stone.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Request denied.\"\n\nHer answer wrote itself beneath the Noctis notice without delay or committee language.\n\n**AURELIS ANSWER: DENIED**\n\n**CAEL NOX IS RECORDED AS PROTECTED WITNESS OF AURELIS**\n\n**FINAL PUBLIC AMENDMENT: HIS CHOICE IS NOT RETURNABLE PROPERTY**\n\nRhea looked at Nera and Oric.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Do either of you contest the wording?\"\n\nNera's face had gone very still.\n\nNera: \"No.\"\n\nOric's throat worked.\n\nOric: \"No.\"\n\nRhea looked at me next, not for permission, as warning.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Haru?\"\n\nI looked at Cael. At the flowers by his feet. At Leon's hands, curled as if they still remembered holding weight. At Elira breathing beside me because Cael no longer did.\n\nHaru: \"Leave the notice visible.\"\n\nAira turned toward me. So did Halden.\n\nHaru: \"If they asked for him like that, people should see it.\"\n\nThe Black Circle slowed, directed rather than soothed. Rhea gave a single nod.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Agreed.\"\n\nShe archived the request in the public record. Mira copied it three times. The third sheet tore where her pencil pressed too hard, and Finn mended the tear with a strip of tape from his own notebook.\n\nThat small act nearly undid me, perhaps because it was the opposite of resurrection: no ancient doctrine or celestial ring, just tape and paper.\n\n---\n\nWe learned about Noctis' internal fracture through a leak before official channels spoke.\n\nThe leaked relay carried no image, only audio and a flickering transcript copied in a hurry. Darian Thorne's name appeared at the top.\n\n**STATUS: HOUSE RESTRICTION**\n\n**DISCIPLINARY HOLD PENDING COUNCIL REVIEW**\n\nNera inhaled sharply. Oric closed one hand around his opposite wrist.\n\nThe audio began mid-sentence.\n\nDarian Thorne: \"-then charge me with disloyalty in a public room. Do not do it behind a sealed door and pretend the students cannot hear cowardice through stone.\"\n\nA second voice answered.\n\nNoctis Councilor: \"You assisted Aurelis in obstructing a sanctioned reclamation proceeding.\"\n\nDarian Thorne: \"I assisted a child in saying no.\"\n\nNoctis Councilor: \"Cael Nox was not a child in the legal sense.\"\n\nDarian Thorne: \"How convenient for everyone who used him like one.\"\n\nThe transcript trembled, then steadied as Lucien's voice entered.\n\nLucien: \"You are enjoying moral clarity now that the cost has been paid by someone else.\"\n\nDarian Thorne: \"And you are still trying to sound above a grave.\"\n\nSilence held the line, sharp even through damaged audio.\n\nLucien: \"Cael made himself proof.\"\n\nNera's fingers dug into the black cloth at her knee.\n\nDarian Thorne: \"No. That is the part you cannot bear. He made himself unavailable to your proof.\"\n\nThe audio cracked.\n\nLucien: \"The Black Circle exists. The world cannot unsee it.\"\n\nDarian Thorne: \"The world also saw why it appeared.\"\n\nNoctis Councilor: \"Enough.\"\n\nDarian Thorne: \"No. Not enough. That has been the problem.\"\n\nThe leak cut out. No one moved for several seconds.\n\nOric: \"He will be punished for that.\"\n\nNera's voice came out rough.\n\nNera: \"He knew.\"\n\nElira looked at Nera.\n\nElira: \"We preserve the transcript.\"\n\nNera nodded.\n\nNera: \"And we answer it.\"\n\nAira blinked.\n\nAira: \"Now?\"\n\nNera looked toward Cael.\n\nNera: \"Now.\"\n\nShe stood slowly, moving with rigid care as if haste might scatter her, and faced the public record.\n\nNera: \"Darian Thorne did not save Cael Nox in time. Neither did I. Neither did Oric Sen. That failure is part of the record.\"\n\nOric flinched. Nera kept going, her voice steady against the quiet room.\n\nNera: \"Late witness is not worthless witness. Noctis teaches that failure cancels testimony. That is a lie.\"\n\nOric: \"We reject that doctrine.\"\n\nThe Foundation pool answered.\n\n**DOCTRINE CHALLENGE RECORDED**\n\nCael did not stir. The sentence mattered anyway.\n\n---\n\nOutside the chamber, Aurelis became a city lowering its voice around a wound.\n\nWe heard pieces of it: healers moving in pairs, students gathering in stairwells whispering Black Circle like either prayer or panic, faculty arguing over whether classes should stop or stay open as shelter. Notice crystals shifted from black and silver to gold and back again as old magic and administration argued over priority.\n\nNear midafternoon, a message from Drakenshard arrived in heavy red-gold script with Mara Kest's seal at the bottom. Rhea read it aloud.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Drakenshard Martial Academy acknowledges the courage of Cael Nox, the restraint of Haru Aerlen, and the dishonor of doctrines that force students to become weapons against their own consent. We do not fear power. We fear cruelty wearing rank. Drakenshard will not support containment of the Black Circle bearer without evidence of hostile action. We will support investigation of Noctis practices with full witness access.\"\n\nAira stared.\n\nAira: \"That is the politest punch I have ever heard.\"\n\nCassian's mouth twitched.\n\nCassian: \"Drakenshard punches in complete sentences.\"\n\nValemere's relay followed ten minutes later. Soren Ashvale appeared live from a quiet medical hall, looking as if he had insisted on speaking without council delay. Talen Mire stood beside him, his braced hand resting plainly in view.\n\nSoren bypassed Rhea and me and bowed first to Cael.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere Royal Institute records Cael Nox as a witness whose final choice confirmed a pattern of Noctis harm previously denied in Talen Mire's case.\"\n\nTalen looked past the crystal to Elira.\n\nTalen: \"Elira. I am glad you are alive.\"\n\nElira's hand tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"I am sorry for the cost.\"\n\nTalen's gaze shifted toward Cael's bier.\n\nTalen: \"So am I.\"\n\nSoren spoke once more before the relay dimmed.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere will attend the funeral if permitted.\"\n\nRhea answered without pause.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Permitted. Witness seating will be public.\"\n\nSoren bowed in acknowledgment, and the relay closed. Elira's shoulders sagged. Professor Valen reached her before I could.\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Do you?\"\n\nElira looked annoyed for half a second, and the relief of seeing any ordinary expression on her face almost hurt.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Good. Then let us begin with honesty. Maelin gave you until sunset to lie down, and you are already past it.\"\n\nElira glanced at Cael. Professor Valen's voice softened.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Rest here if you must. But rest.\"\n\nAira was already moving, commandeering two council aides, three blankets, a folding screen, and a heated basin with the brisk fury of someone using logistics instead of screaming.\n\nAira: \"Medical corner. Nobody gets symbolic about it. Elira can mourn lying down.\"\n\nElira did not argue. That frightened me more than the magic had.\n\n---\n\nI thought the Black Circle would fade once Elira slept. It dimmed, but it stayed behind me, turning slowly.\n\nThe old record-echo appeared while the chamber was at its quietest.\n\nLiora Aerlen-Valen did not arrive with ceremony. One moment there was only dust, broken stone, and Elira's shallow breaths behind the folding screen. The next, a woman shaped from amber record-light stood near the pool with her hands folded at her waist.\n\nHalden saw her first. His pen stopped.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Record echo.\"\n\nLiora's gaze moved from him to Rhea, to Professor Valen, then to me.\n\nLiora: \"Not record only.\"\n\nProfessor Valen's face went still. Liora looked toward Cael, and her voice lowered.\n\nLiora: \"The dead should not need to become architecture.\"\n\nThe Black Circle shifted as if it recognized the old language in her. She stepped closer to its edge.\n\nLiora: \"You are waiting for a decision.\"\n\nHaru: \"I do not know what decision.\"\n\nLiora: \"Wall or witness.\"\n\nAira looked over from the medical corner, eyes sharp despite exhaustion.\n\nLiora: \"The Black Circle can close around grief and become a wall no one can reach through. Many will encourage that, through fear or worship, or because walls are easier to administer than wounded people.\"\n\nRhea's mouth tightened.\n\nLiora: \"Or it can remain answerable to witnesses.\"\n\nHaru: \"Answerable is dangerous.\"\n\nLiora: \"So is unanswerable.\"\n\nThe words settled into the room like a key turning an old lock. I looked at Cael, at the screen hiding Elira's sleep, at Aira's torn map corner tucked in her sleeve, Leon's scarred knuckles, Nera's flowers, Mira's ink-stained fingers, and Rhea's red seal.\n\nHaru: \"I do not want to be a wall.\"\n\nThe Black Circle stilled. For one breath, the opposing rings aligned, deep black and silver and constellation-points, an order precise enough to become terror if left unchecked.\n\nHaru: \"If it tries to close around me, tell me.\"\n\nAira answered immediately.\n\nAira: \"I will.\"\n\nThen her face tightened.\n\nAira: \"But you do not get to make me your permanent alarm bell. If I am tired, someone else speaks. If I am wrong, you argue. If I say I cannot carry it that day, you believe me.\"\n\nThe condition stung, and I knew it belonged there.\n\nHaru: \"I will practice.\"\n\nAira: \"Good. Because you are going to be terrible at it.\"\n\nCassian looked at the rings around me.\n\nCassian: \"If it becomes a wall, I can set something on fire outside it.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is not a plan.\"\n\nCassian: \"It is the beginning of several.\"\n\nLeon opened his mouth as if to offer a cleaner promise, caught sight of Cael's bier, and stopped. He bent instead, picked up a fallen healer cloth beside the seal, and folded it neatly.\n\nNera touched the old binding scar at her wrist.\n\nNera: \"If Noctis language starts sounding comfortable...\"\n\nShe could not finish. Oric set his exit notes beside her flowers as proof he meant to stay reachable. Mira lifted her pencil.\n\nMira: \"I can write what happens. Unless I cannot. Then someone else keeps the page.\"\n\nRhea broke her emergency seal into three visible witness marks and assigned one to the student line, one to faculty, and one to the council record.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I reserve the right to distrust both your power and my interest in it.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"There he is.\"\n\nHalden looked offended. Mira wrote his sentence anyway, then added a line beneath all of ours:\n\n**WITNESSES MAY REFUSE THE ROLE AND REMAIN WITNESSES.**\n\nThe Black Circle faded by half, no longer hidden, simply resting. Liora's amber light softened.\n\nLiora: \"Good.\"\n\nHaru: \"Is that enough?\"\n\nShe glanced once more at Cael.\n\nLiora: \"No. But it is the kind of not enough that can become a beginning.\"\n\nThen she vanished without prophecy or comfort, leaving one more absence in a room already full of them.\n\n---\n\nBy late afternoon, the record and the day caught up with each other. Rhea returned after forty-two minutes in emergency council session.\n\nIn that time, the chamber had shifted from aftermath to vigil. Students had brought practical lamps, not ceremonial ones, because no one wanted Cael lying in shadow. Someone brought white ribbon, then nightglass flowers, then a folded Aurelis cloak, then a sheet copied from Mira's notebook:\n\n**HE WAS NOT A TOOL. HE WAS A WITNESS WHO CHOSE MERCY AFTER BEING SAVED BY IT.**\n\nNo one asked who left it by the pool, and everyone pretended not to look.\n\nRhea knelt beside Cael. Dust still marked one knee of her uniform from the night before; she had not brushed it off.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"The Council has authorized full public honors. Cael Nox will be recognized as Protected Witness of Aurelis, deceased by voluntary activation under catastrophic coercive pressure. His testimony and final choice will remain in the public Foundation record.\"\n\nElira had woken at the first sentence. She pushed herself upright before Professor Valen could stop her.\n\nElira: \"When?\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Tomorrow at sunset. Grand Courtyard.\"\n\nA murmur moved through the student watch. Public, not buried beneath the council tower where Noctis jurisdiction could contest it and Foundation clerks could file it out of sight.\n\nElira looked at Cael's face, her voice raw.\n\nElira: \"I will never be ready. But he should not have to wait for us to become ready.\"\n\nRhea bowed her head.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Noctis has been informed they may attend as witnesses only. They may not claim him, speak over his record, or approach his body without Aurelis permission and witness consent.\"\n\nOric's voice was thin.\n\nOric: \"Lucien?\"\n\nRhea's eyes hardened.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Lucien Voss is not cleared for physical attendance.\"\n\nThat was not safety, and everyone knew it. Aira rubbed both eyes.\n\nAira: \"I miss when attendance lists were boring.\"\n\nCassian: \"Were they ever?\"\n\nAira: \"I have decided to remember them that way for emotional contrast.\"\n\nElira reached for me, and I sat beside her before she had to ask. She leaned her shoulder lightly against mine, as if her body could only spend a small amount of trust at once.\n\nElira: \"He said he wanted to choose what his life meant. I think he did. I just wish he had not had to.\"\n\nThe Black Circle pulsed once, faint and quiet, and stayed with us.\n\n---\n\nNight settled over Aurelis in layers.\n\nEmergency lamps glowed around the chamber in gold, blue, red, green, and silver, placed wherever there was floor space rather than by Circle rank. From the pool, they looked less like a hierarchy and more like a city seen from a hill.\n\nOne by one, the witnesses rested without quite sleeping. Leon leaned against a broken pillar staring at his hands until Cassian sat beside him in silence. Nera and Oric copied Noctis phrases into a correction ledger, rewriting each away from ownership. Aira drank water only after bullying three other people into drinking first. Mira slept fourteen minutes face-down on her notebook while Finn and two Green Circle students copied around her. Rhea stayed awake. So did Halden, with Professor Valen quietly planting herself between them whenever their silence sharpened into an argument.\n\nElira and I sat by the restoration pool, where Cael's flowers caught the lamplight. Only a thin ring of the Black Circle remained around my shadow now, visible to anyone who knew how to look. Everyone knew how now.\n\nHaru: \"I couldn't stop it.\"\n\nElira turned her head toward me.\n\nHaru: \"When you died, I could not hide it. If Cael had not...\"\n\nMy voice stopped. None of the endings to that sentence had him alive. Elira took my hand, her fingers still cold.\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is not forgiveness.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nShe did not soften the word. Then she leaned closer.\n\nElira: \"It is presence. We can work toward the rest.\"\n\nI looked at Cael. The words scraped out.\n\nHaru: \"He saved you from staying dead. He saved me from whatever I would have turned into around your absence.\"\n\nElira rested her free hand against the cool stone rim of the pool.\n\nElira: \"Then we remember him honestly.\"\n\nHaru: \"I do not know how to carry that.\"\n\nElira's eyes filled, and she did not look away.\n\nElira: \"Neither do I. So we do not turn it into a debt we can never pay. We turn it into choices.\"\n\nHaru: \"What if I choose wrong?\"\n\nElira glanced at the sleeping witnesses, then at Aira pretending not to listen from three blankets away.\n\nElira: \"Then someone tells you.\"\n\nAira: \"Loudly.\"\n\nElira's mouth trembled into something almost like a laugh.\n\nAbove the pool, the final Foundation notice of the night lit in dim, uncommanding script.\n\n**VIGIL RECORDED**\n\n**WITNESSES REMAIN**\n\n**FUNERAL AUTHORIZED: SUNSET**\n\n**SAFEGUARD IS NOT ALONE**\n\nThe Black Circle withdrew from the air behind me, leaving only the quiet weight beneath my skin. I nearly sagged from the sudden stillness, and Elira pressed her shoulder firmer against mine.\n\nFor that night, because no one let grief turn into solitude, the silence held.\n\n---\n\nBy morning, the academy had learned to move around grief clumsily, carefully.\n\nCorridors above the Wounded City filled with students walking in quiet lines carrying flowers, folded ribbons, and pages from public witness ledgers. Elira slept three hours before dawn, which Maelin's morning chart marked as a victory and Professor Valen delivered in a tone daring anyone to argue.\n\nWhen Elira woke, she looked confused for one second. Then her fingers found her pendant, her eyes found Cael's covered bier, and grief returned to her shoulders like physical weight.\n\nThe Black Circle stirred beneath my ribs. I pressed my palm flat against the floor and breathed until the cold behind my sternum quieted.\n\nElira saw.\n\nElira: \"You are not allowed to use self-control as a substitute for being held.\"\n\nMy hand froze on the stone. Passing behind us with a tray of tea, Aira did not even slow down.\n\nAira: \"Medical ruling confirmed by non-medical authority.\"\n\nProfessor Valen accepted a cup from the tray.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"For once, I endorse the non-medical authority.\"\n\nI looked at Elira.\n\nHaru: \"I am sitting right next to you.\"\n\nElira: \"And part of you is standing guard over every future where I die again.\"\n\nHer accuracy left me without a fast enough answer. Her cool fingers slid over mine.\n\nElira: \"I am frightened too, Haru. Sometimes I blink and there is no before or after, just a place where I stopped. Then I am here again, everyone looks relieved, and Cael is not breathing. Do not leave me alone with everyone else's relief.\"\n\nI turned my hand under hers and held on.\n\nHaru: \"I am here.\"\n\nElira: \"All of you?\"\n\nThe Black Circle pulsed once under my shirt.\n\nHaru: \"I am trying.\"\n\nElira leaned her forehead against my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"Then keep trying where I can reach you.\"\n\nSo I stayed in the chamber while Aurelis prepared to bury the boy who had chosen her life. I let Aira put tea in my hands, let Leon take the cup when my fingers shook and pretend he was moving it from the stone edge, and let Cassian stand at my blind side so no official could approach without passing him. When Nera and Oric asked how much Noctis grammar to preserve for the funeral record, I answered like a person instead of a weapon: only enough that it could neither be erased nor speak last.\n\nMira arrived with Finn and a stack of witness pages tied in green ribbon. She looked first at the empty air behind me, then at my face. The second look mattered.\n\nMira: \"We made a public sequence packet.\"\n\nAira reached for it immediately.\n\nAira: \"Please tell me that title is temporary.\"\n\nFinn: \"It is called Sequence Packet One.\"\n\nAira closed her eyes.\n\nAira: \"Green Circle activism has the soul of a filing cabinet.\"\n\nMira hugged the packet to her chest.\n\nMira: \"A filing cabinet that stopped Lucien from narrating a corpse.\"\n\nAira opened her eyes.\n\nAira: \"I withdraw my complaint and admire the cabinet.\"\n\nMira smiled for half a second before glancing at Cael.\n\nMira: \"We added a page where students can write what they witnessed, not what they were told to think.\"\n\nRhea took the top copy as she entered and scanned the count.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"How many?\"\n\nFinn: \"Four hundred and twelve so far.\"\n\nMira: \"A lot of people were awake.\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"A lot of people were watching.\"\n\nMira's grip tightened on the paper, ink smudged across her knuckles.\n\nMira: \"That has to mean something good too.\"\n\nNo one promised her it would. Rhea only nodded.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then we give it a place to mean something.\"\n\n---\n\nThe funeral was held in the Grand Courtyard instead of the chapel or council tower, keeping it public where Cael could not be turned into precedent before ordinary people finished crying.\n\nBy sunset, the academy had gathered across the steps, balconies, and colonnades. Green Circle students stood near the eastern fountain with Mira's packets in their arms. Near the north arcade, a cluster of Blue Circle students kept glancing at the space behind me where the Black Circle had been; one of them had folded a condolence ribbon so tightly it had creased into a hard little square. Mira saw them and did not press a pen into their hands.\n\nInstructor Dren held the western gate with the combat faculty, turning away anyone looking for spectacle instead of witness. Lady Seraphine Morn stood beside Rhea's council aides with the tactical record; whenever a delegation tried to file a softer phrase over Cael's death, she wrote the uglier accurate one beneath it in red ink.\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Ceremony is not permission to simplify.\"\n\nDren heard her across the stone.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"For once, Morn, we agree before the damage is done.\"\n\nLady Seraphine: \"Record that as a rare event.\"\n\nValemere arrived in silver-blue, Soren walking beside Talen, whose braced hand remained uncovered in plain sight. Drakenshard sent Mara Kest and three witnesses in ash-gray uniforms, unarmed, which drew quieter stares than any crest.\n\nThen came the first sign of Noctis' fracture.\n\nThe official delegation arrived in black and white with a folded banner between them. The Noctis students came separately, standing at the back without banners. Some had removed their binding rings; others had not. One younger student held his wrist the way Cael used to when pressure hooks pulled beneath the skin.\n\nNera whispered his name before she caught herself.\n\nNera: \"Iven Sahl. First-year conduit track. Cael lent him a thermal charm during winter drills and told him not to give it back until his hands stopped shaking.\"\n\nIven did not look toward us. He kept his eyes on Cael's bier and his hand tight around his wrist. Oric noted the boy's position in the witness ledger, not to expose him, only to remember he had stood there.\n\nAt the center of the courtyard, the repaired fountain ran with silver water. Cael's body lay before it on a low stone bier under a clean Aurelis cloak, nightglass flowers at his side, Rhea's red seal above him, and copied witness pages tucked beneath the stone like roots.\n\nThe sky faded from orange into violet, unfair in how bright it stayed. Elira stood beside me, refusing to sit in the chair Professor Valen and Maelin had demanded until she walked to Cael herself. Her hand held mine while the courtyard and the reopened relays watched the empty air behind me.\n\nAira leaned close on my other side.\n\nAira: \"Do not think about all of them at once. Think about Cael. One person. The rest can wait their turn.\"\n\nSo I looked at Cael. One person. It hurt worse, and it told the truth better.\n\n---\n\nRhea opened the ceremony from the fountain's edge in full council uniform, the red of her Circle dimmed to a lamp.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"We gather for Cael Nox.\"\n\nThe courtyard quieted around the absence of titles.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Student of Noctis Dominion College by enrollment. Protected Witness of Aurelis by choice. Public witness by final act. This ceremony does not make his death acceptable. It does not transform loss into strategy, or make a weapon beautiful because it turned toward mercy at the end. Cael Nox should be alive.\"\n\nThe fountain water rippled.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Because he is not, we will name what happened, preserve who he was, and refuse to let the systems that harmed him inherit his meaning.\"\n\nRhea stepped back. Elira stepped forward.\n\nHer fingers slipped from mine as she went to stand on her own. The courtyard fell completely still, listening to the person Cael had died to bring back.\n\nElira looked at Cael first, then across the gathered students.\n\nElira: \"Cael Nox was taught that his life was a resource. A support unit. A conduit. A reserved answer to someone else's emergency. He was taught that being useful was safer than being known.\"\n\nNera bowed her head. The pendant at Elira's throat caught the sunset and flashed black.\n\nElira: \"In the end, Cael did spend his life. Not because Noctis ordered him to. Not because a clause owned him. Not because a spell needed a body and his was available.\"\n\nHer eyes lifted, and a thin line of gold trembled around her wrist.\n\nElira: \"He chose. That difference between coercion and choice is everything. Cael chose mercy, chose me, chose Haru, chose to make his final act belong to him when too many people spent too long arranging his life as if it belonged to them.\"\n\nHer voice broke. She did not smooth it over.\n\nElira: \"I am alive because he is not. I will hate that sentence for the rest of my life. And I will spend that life making sure his choice is not used to teach another student that dying is the only way to matter.\"\n\nShe bowed to Cael. As she stepped back, her knees nearly gave way. She caught my sleeve before Professor Valen or I could turn it into a scene.\n\nElira: \"Chair.\"\n\nAira slid it behind her as if she had been waiting all afternoon to defeat pride with furniture.\n\nAira: \"Miracle of practical planning.\"\n\nElira sat and took my hand openly in front of the courtyard.\n\n---\n\nNera spoke next. She wore a plain Aurelis witness cloak over her uniform, leaving the binding scars on her wrists uncovered. A quiet ripple moved through the Noctis students at the back.\n\nNera: \"I was one of Cael's instructors. Not formally enough to be named responsible when responsibility grew dangerous. Formally enough to correct him, train him, and teach him which pressure meant endure and which meant obey.\"\n\nThe official Noctis delegation stopped pretending not to listen.\n\nNera: \"I told myself I was helping him survive Noctis. Sometimes survival lessons become the walls of the prison.\"\n\nOric stood two paces behind her, holding the ledger in both hands.\n\nNera: \"Cael, if witness records reach anywhere kind, hear this: I am sorry for every time I translated ownership into discipline. I am sorry for every warning I gave too quietly. I am sorry you had to become braver than the adults around you. I cannot undo what was taught, but I can testify against it and refuse to teach it again.\"\n\nOric stepped beside her.\n\nOric: \"I can preserve the grammar without preserving the doctrine.\"\n\nNera swallowed hard, her jaw tight, and bowed in neither Noctis nor Aurelis form.\n\nDarian Thorne's relay opened behind her before the Noctis officials could block it. Rhea's red seal pinned the signal open.\n\nDarian appeared in a narrow pane of light, seated in a bare room with a restraint sigil glowing on the wall behind him. House arrest, confirmed. His face looked hollow.\n\nDarian Thorne: \"Cael Nox was my student. I watched Noctis break him into functions small enough to praise, and called it advancement because cowardice is easier when it shares vocabulary. I watched Lucien Voss use him in the tournament and did not stop it in time.\"\n\nNera closed her eyes.\n\nDarian Thorne: \"I failed Cael Nox. That remains in my record whether Noctis permits it or not. To every Noctis student watching through a borrowed crystal or hidden reflection: usefulness is not worth. Obedience is not safety. If you need witness, find one. If you cannot leave evidence, remember your own name until someone can help you say it aloud.\"\n\nThe restraint sigil flashed behind him. The window flickered.\n\nDarian Thorne: \"Cael, I am late. I am sorry. I will not be quiet again.\"\n\nThe pane went dark.\n\nIn the silence that followed, a Noctis student at the back slipped off a binding ring and set it on the courtyard stone. The small metallic click carried. Another followed, then three more.\n\nRhea did not smile, but her red seal widened to cover the small pile of rings as protected evidence.\n\n---\n\nThe official Noctis delegation chose that moment to step forward with their folded black banner.\n\nThe lead official was silver-templed, carrying the careful stiffness of a man who had spent decades confusing control with dignity.\n\nNoctis Official: \"Noctis Dominion College formally acknowledges the death of Cael Nox, enrolled student of our institution, who died in service to-\"\n\nElira stood. Her chair scraped loud against stone.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe official stopped.\n\nNoctis Official: \"Miss Vale, this is a formal acknowledgment.\"\n\nElira: \"Then acknowledge him accurately.\"\n\nNoctis Official: \"Cael Nox was a Noctis student.\"\n\nElira: \"He was.\"\n\nHer voice carried cleanly across the courtyard.\n\nNoctis Official: \"His training, placement, and final doctrine originated under our institution.\"\n\nNera flinched. Elira held the official's gaze without flinching.\n\nNoctis Official: \"Then Noctis has standing to honor his service.\"\n\nThe Black Circle stirred beneath my shirt. Elira stepped forward once.\n\nElira: \"Cael did not die in service to Noctis.\"\n\nThe official opened his mouth. Elira spoke over him.\n\nElira: \"He died refusing what Noctis made of service.\"\n\nRhea rose beside the bier.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis recognizes Noctis' right to witness this funeral. Noctis does not have the right to rename the deceased.\"\n\nNoctis Official: \"We request custody of the remains after ceremony.\"\n\nThe red seal over Cael brightened.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Denied.\"\n\nNoctis Official: \"President Valmont-\"\n\nPresident Valmont: \"The noon denial stands. Cael Nox is a Protected Witness of Aurelis by recorded choice, and his body remains under protection until all rites and investigative chains are complete. Submit any appeal to the Inter-National Schools Council, where it will sit beside the wording you just attempted in front of several thousand witnesses.\"\n\nAira whispered behind me, almost reverent.\n\nAira: \"Oh, she saved the receipt.\"\n\nThe official heard and chose silence. For a heartbeat I thought he might try to drape the banner over Cael's bier anyway, and the Black Circle pressed hard against my ribs. Then he seemed to measure the courtyard around him.\n\nHe laid the folded banner at the fountain's edge beside the witness pages, well clear of Cael and the flowers, bowed once, and stepped back. No one thanked him.\n\n---\n\nTalen Mire walked forward next without invitation. Soren moved to steady him, and Talen shook his head, his brace clicking softly with each step until he reached the fountain.\n\nHe set a single white flower beside Cael's nightglass blooms.\n\nTalen: \"I did not know Cael Nox. But I know what it is like to be harmed by a doctrine and told that naming the harm is more dangerous than the harm itself.\"\n\nHe lifted his braced hand.\n\nTalen: \"When I testified, I thought I was speaking about the past. Cael's testimony made the pattern visible, and his final choice made it impossible for the world to pretend it was isolated. Thank you for making my pain part of a record large enough to matter. I wish you had lived to see it become justice.\"\n\nHe bowed as far as his brace allowed and stepped back.\n\nBefore anyone called another name, a first-year Green Circle student stepped up and laid a yellow ribbon beside the white flower.\n\nStudent: \"I saw him say no.\"\n\nA Blue Circle student followed.\n\nStudent: \"I saw Noctis try to call him conduit after he died.\"\n\nA Red Circle student stepped up next.\n\nStudent: \"I saw Haru Aerlen not attack us when everyone said he was the danger.\"\n\nMy breath caught. Elira's hand found mine.\n\nAnother Green Circle student laid down a folded page.\n\nStudent: \"I saw Mira write the sequence when adults froze.\"\n\nMira made a small horrified sound. Finn looked ready to brag on her behalf for a week.\n\nStudents approached one after another until the fountain stone disappeared under flowers, ribbons, copied pages, and small ordinary things grief had reached for: a chalk pen, a pressed leaf, a spare ribbon. Near the end, a Noctis student placed a binding ring beside the banner.\n\nNoctis Student: \"I knew him as Nox because they told us names made support units careless. His name was Cael.\"\n\nNera covered her mouth. Oric wrote the line down through tears.\n\n---\n\nMagistra Senna Voss arrived as the last light left the sky.\n\nThe council registry tag flickered beside her name again, clearing her of house ties to Lucien Voss; she glanced at it with the flat patience of someone used to defending her own surname, then bowed first to Cael's bier, next to Elira, and finally to me with level acknowledgment rather than awe.\n\nShe drew off one glove. A white emergency-seal burn crossed her palm from wrist to thumb. The Council had made her pay for speed.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"These measures are interim. They are not justice. They are the legal space in which evidence may survive long enough to pursue it.\"\n\nThe first relay crystal opened above the fountain.\n\n**NOCTIS DOMINION COLLEGE: FORMAL INVESTIGATION**\n\n**ALLEGATIONS: INSTITUTIONAL ABUSE / COERCIVE DOCTRINE / WITNESS SUPPRESSION / UNLAWFUL CONDUIT CONDITIONING**\n\nThe lead Noctis official stepped forward.\n\nNoctis Official: \"The Council has heard contaminated testimony gathered under an extra-classification event.\"\n\nSenna turned the relay toward Cael's bier.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"State your name and office before you call a dead student's testimony contamination.\"\n\nThe official looked at the flowers, the severed binding rings, and the Noctis students standing apart from his banner. He said nothing. Senna let his silence enter the record.\n\nThree directives wrote themselves above the water.\n\n**FINAL CONDUIT CLAUSES: EMERGENCY SUSPENSION ACROSS MEMBER SCHOOLS**\n\n**DOMINION SEVERANCE: EMERGENCY PROHIBITION PENDING INVESTIGATION AND RATIFICATION**\n\n**ACTIVE MATERIAL, TRAINING RECORDS, AND INSTRUCTIONAL ROUTES: PRESERVE FOR REVIEW**\n\nNera folded over a breath; Oric caught her elbow until she steadied. Relief that arrived after Cael had teeth.\n\nNoctis Official: \"You are suspending accredited curriculum on the word of defectors.\"\n\nSenna's burned hand closed.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"I am suspending a fatal route after its activation was witnessed across every relay your institution demanded.\"\n\nA second crystal lit beside the first.\n\n**INTERIM WITNESS-PROTECTION DIRECTIVE**\n\n**CHOSEN PRESENCE MAY NOT BE REMOVED EXCEPT BY DIRECT, WITNESSED, TIME-LIMITED MEDICAL NECESSITY**\n\n**INSTITUTIONAL CONVENIENCE IS NOT MEDICAL NECESSITY**\n\n**CAEL NOX: PROTECTED WITNESS / PUBLIC SEQUENCE PRESERVED**\n\nNo one cheered. Recognition did not raise the dead.\n\nThen the script above the fountain hesitated and shifted.\n\n**EXTRA-CLASSIFICATION EVENT: CONFIRMED**\n\n**ATTACHED WITNESS: HARU AERLEN, AURELIS STUDENT**\n\n**PROPOSED TERM: BLACK CIRCLE / CLASSIFICATION UNRATIFIED**\n\nA red-black minority seal struck across the notice before Senna could speak.\n\n**EMERGENCY MOTION: PROVISIONAL CONTAINMENT OF HARU AERLEN**\n\nThe courtyard chilled. Aira's pen stopped. Rhea's seal rose.\n\nSenna reached the crystal first, pressing her burned palm straight against Krail's motion. White light bit through the fresh scar, and her fingers trembled once before steadying.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"Entered into public record.\"\n\n**ENTERED** appeared above the seal. Then she drew a single clean line through **CONTAINMENT**.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"Denied in immediate form. I will not authorize removing a student from everyone who can name what happens to him and call isolation caution. No containment. No elimination order. No compulsory demonstration. No isolated classification panel. Any stability review must include Aurelis witnesses and student consent.\"\n\nRhea stepped beside the relay.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Aurelis objects to any future motion that begins by removing the student from his own record.\"\n\nMagistra Voss: \"Recorded.\"\n\nKrail's minority seal remained visible beneath the strike-line, proof the motion would return wearing cleaner language. I preferred seeing the knife.\n\nOne last notice opened above the fountain.\n\n**INTER-NATIONAL SCHOOLS TOURNAMENT: SUSPENDED PENDING INVESTIGATION**\n\n**OPENING-BLOCK RESULTS: ARCHIVED AS SEQUENCE / NOT FINAL STANDING**\n\n**RESUMPTION OR RECONSTITUTION: SUBJECT TO STUDENT-HARM REVIEW**\n\nValemere's seal lit in agreement. Drakenshard accepted in grim silence.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"Haru Aerlen, do you accept stability review under distributed witness protocol?\"\n\nThe Black Circle pressed beneath my skin. Everyone waited for the myth to speak.\n\nI looked at Elira, exhausted and alive beside me; at Aira, mouthing *Do not say anything ancient*; and at Cael beneath the flowers.\n\nHaru: \"I accept review. I do not accept ownership.\"\n\nThe fountain water turned black and silver for one heartbeat, then cleared. Senna bowed her head.\n\nMagistra Voss: \"Recorded.\"\n\nNeither Noctis nor Aurelis applauded. It was not justice, only the beginning of consequences, arriving late and limping.\n\n---\n\nHaru: \"I did not prepare remarks.\"\n\nIt was a terrible opening. Aira's expression confirmed it.\n\nI stood at the fountain's edge with Cael before me and the Black Circle still hidden under my shirt, though everyone knew it was there.\n\nHaru: \"I tried to write something, and everything sounded like I was making his death useful. Cael had enough of that.\"\n\nLeon looked down. Cassian's jaw worked once.\n\nHaru: \"He saved my life twice. The first time, he brought us proof when Noctis wanted him afraid. He warned us, testified, and let people stand near him even though closeness had been dangerous most of his life.\"\n\nNera pressed a hand over her mouth.\n\nHaru: \"The second time, he brought Elira back.\"\n\nThe Black Circle flickered beneath my ribs. I laid one hand over my chest, not to hide it, only to keep it still.\n\nHaru: \"When she died, I lost the first moment. The Black Circle opened before I could choose what shape grief should have. Cael stopped me from becoming only that moment. Not by obeying me or becoming proof for me, but by choosing mercy when every doctrine around him tried to spend his life for someone else's need.\"\n\nI looked down at Cael.\n\nHaru: \"I do not know how to be worth that choice. I only know what I can choose next. Not to make his death a command for anyone else to sacrifice themselves. Not to turn power into a wall. To keep witnesses even when being witnessed is hard, and to remember that the strongest thing Cael did was not die.\"\n\nMy voice nearly failed.\n\nHaru: \"It was say no long enough to make one final yes his own. Cael Nox was not proof that mercy needs victims. Record that.\"\n\nMira wrote it down. Four hundred and twelve students copied behind her, and the Foundation answered.\n\n**PUBLIC RECORD AMENDMENT ACCEPTED**\n\nThe Black Circle pulsed once, small and contained.\n\n---\n\nAfter the ceremony, Rhea brought us Cael's personal effects in a small plain box: a spare uniform clasp, a broken Noctis calibration bead, a frayed string, and a thin cloth-bound notebook.\n\nNera made a quiet sound when she saw how little fit inside. Oric looked away.\n\nElira reached for the notebook, then paused.\n\nElira: \"May I?\"\n\nNera answered softly.\n\nNera: \"He would want you to.\"\n\nElira opened the cover. The first page held a list in small, careful handwriting, as if Cael had been conserving paper or permission. She read aloud.\n\nElira: \"Things I choose. To be called by my name, not my role. To say no. To have friends, if they will have me. To matter when I am not useful.\"\n\nShe stopped. Nobody pressed her, and after a breath she turned to the last page, where the ink was darker and pressed harder.\n\nElira: \"If this ever becomes evidence, tell them I was afraid. Tell them I was afraid, and I chose anyway. Do not let them make courage sound clean.\"\n\nShe closed the notebook against her chest.\n\nAira took one step forward, her voice wrecked and practical at the same time.\n\nAira: \"We should copy the first and last lines. Not the private pages unless we decide together later, just those. They need to outlive everybody's speeches.\"\n\nRhea nodded.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"With witness consent.\"\n\nNera wiped her face with the heel of her hand and nodded. Oric gripped his ledger in both hands until his knuckles went white.\n\nOric: \"Copy them.\"\n\nLeon looked at me.\n\nLeon: \"You keep the notebook.\"\n\nHaru: \"Why?\"\n\nLeon's face twisted.\n\nLeon: \"Because I held him after. If I keep it, I will never put it down.\"\n\nCassian's hand tightened on Leon's shoulder. Elira looked at me, asking rather than deciding, and I took the notebook. Its light weight felt unfair in my palm.\n\nHaru: \"I will keep it with witnesses.\"\n\nAira nodded.\n\nAira: \"Good answer.\"\n\nElira's eyes softened.\n\nElira: \"Human answer.\"\n\n---\n\nLong after the crowds dispersed, the core witnesses remained by the fountain: Elira, Aira, Leon, Cassian, Nera, Oric, Rhea, Professor Valen, Halden, Mira, Finn, Soren, Talen, and Senna. Cael's body had been moved under witness guard to the eastern hall for final preparation, leaving the nightglass flowers ringed around the water because no one had the heart to sort which belonged with him and which to the courtyard.\n\nThe Noctis banner still lay at the fountain's edge, cut cleanly in half. Mara Kest had asked Rhea's permission first and received it through five seconds of silent eyebrow negotiation. One half stayed beside the official record as evidence of attempted claim; the other rested folded beneath the flowers.\n\nAira sat on the fountain rim, watching the water.\n\nAira: \"He should be here.\"\n\nLeon sat on the stone steps with his arms over his knees.\n\nLeon: \"He would hate how many people talked about him.\"\n\nNera let out a small, wet laugh.\n\nNera: \"He would have stood behind someone taller.\"\n\nLeon looked up.\n\nLeon: \"Me.\"\n\nAira: \"Obviously you. You are structurally useful.\"\n\nLeon almost smiled, then let it go. Nera touched the edge of the fountain stone.\n\nNera: \"He used to ask questions after Lucien left rooms. Never during. The first question he asked me that was not about orders was whether Aurelis students got to choose their electives.\"\n\nAira's face crumpled.\n\nAira: \"That is a horrible anecdote.\"\n\nNera: \"I know.\"\n\nAira: \"Tell another.\"\n\nNera thought for a moment, her voice softening.\n\nNera: \"He liked cinnamon bread. He pretended not to because Noctis dining monitors marked sugar preference as indulgence if you took it twice in a week.\"\n\nElira closed her eyes.\n\nElira: \"Then we add cinnamon bread to the memorial table.\"\n\nRhea immediately made a note in her ledger without committee or commentary. Talen spoke quietly from beside Soren.\n\nTalen: \"At Valemere, after injury hearings, they serve bitter tea because they think sweetness makes testimony less dignified.\"\n\nMira looked offended.\n\nMira: \"That is ridiculous.\"\n\nTalen adjusted the leather strap on his brace and looked at the water.\n\nTalen: \"I would like cinnamon bread too, if that is permitted.\"\n\nAira pointed at Rhea.\n\nAira: \"Put him down for bread.\"\n\nRhea wrote it down. Halden stood slightly apart with his arms folded until Mira noticed.\n\nMira: \"Professor? Did Cael ever ask you anything?\"\n\nHalden looked as if he regretted being visible.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Once. After the formal review, he asked whether old systems could tell the difference between a conduit and a witness. I told him systems could be taught. People were harder.\"\n\nAira: \"That is bleak.\"\n\nProfessor Halden: \"He said, 'Then I hope the system learns first.'\"\n\nThe fountain water rippled. Mira wrote it down before the silence broke.\n\n---\n\nNear midnight, after Professor Valen and Maelin threatened Elira with supervised carrying if she refused to lie down in the medical ward, I slipped back out to the courtyard fountain almost alone. Leon sat on the western steps pretending not to guard me; Cassian leaned along the east path doing the same with better posture.\n\nAira had left a note propped on the fountain rim:\n\n**DRINK WATER OR I WILL BECOME A PROCEDURAL PROBLEM.**\n\nI drank the water beside it and opened Cael's notebook to the first page. The fountain mirrored the moon, the flowers, the halved banner, and my face. For one second, the Black Circle showed behind me in the water, deep black and silver runes turning in opposite order.\n\nThen the reflection shifted. Cael stood beside me in the silver water, tired and too young, looking the way he had before the final stage.\n\nCael: \"You look terrible.\"\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\nHaru: \"That sounds like something Aira invented to make me sleep.\"\n\nCael: \"Probably.\"\n\nWhen I opened my eyes, only moonlight moved across the water. I did not ask the Foundation what it had been. Some things did not need to become records immediately.\n\nFootsteps sounded slowly behind me.\n\nElira: \"You left the medical room.\"\n\nI turned. She wore a coat over her healing gown, hair loose over one shoulder, Maelin's medical seal glowing faintly at her wrist. Twenty paces back, Professor Valen stood beside the colonnade with her satchel, looking deeply irritated and entirely unsurprised.\n\nHaru: \"You followed.\"\n\nElira: \"Slowly. With supervision. Everyone wins.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"That is not my summary.\"\n\nElira sat beside me on the fountain rim and leaned her shoulder against mine. Professor Valen stayed near enough to intervene and far enough to let the night breathe, until she spoke my name in a quieter voice than the lecture halls ever heard.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"There is one record I owe you before the Council starts asking for all the others.\"\n\nI turned toward her. She folded her hands over the leather strap of her satchel.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"When your transfer file reached Aurelis, Student Affairs nearly rejected it at ranking review. A Green Circle applicant from Lumeris District with ancient-script scores above several faculty candidates looked either impossible or inconvenient.\"\n\nInstructor Selene's voice came back from my first morning. Someone argued strongly in your favor.\n\nHaru: \"It was you.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"I argued that a student should not be punished because his examination answers made adults uncomfortable.\"\n\nHalden's dry voice drifted from the western steps.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"That is a selective summary.\"\n\nProfessor Valen did not look away from me.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"I also argued that anyone who could translate pre-standardization mercy clauses without being taught them deserved a room with better questions than the entrance board was asking.\"\n\nMy fingers tightened on Cael's notebook.\n\nHaru: \"Did you know?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"No.\"\n\nShe answered too fast for comfort and too plainly to doubt.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"I suspected old knowledge had touched your life. I did not know the name, and I certainly did not know I was helping bring the Black Circle into my own school.\"\n\nHaru: \"Why tell me now?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Because people will try to make your arrival look like a plot. I want the small truth to survive the big ones: I saw a student being underestimated by a system that mistook rank for proof, and I objected.\"\n\nElira's hand found mine, and I let the contact steady me before deciding whether I deserved it.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Do not thank me too much. I failed to ask enough follow-up questions.\"\n\nHalden: \"We all did.\"\n\nNo one argued, and silence settled back over the courtyard.\n\nHaru: \"Noctis investigation. Council reviews. People afraid of me, or wanting to use me. Lucien still out there. You still healing.\"\n\nElira leaned her cheek briefly against my sleeve, one hand resting on the wet stone of the rim.\n\nHaru: \"I do not know how to keep going without making Cael's death the center of every choice.\"\n\nElira took the notebook from my hands, closed it gently, and set it on the stone between us.\n\nElira: \"Maybe we do not make his death the center. Maybe we make his choices part of ours.\"\n\nThe Black Circle warmed once beneath my shirt, not heat and not quite comfort, only recognition.\n\nElira: \"He chose his name, chose to say no, chose to have friends, chose to matter when he was not useful. So we call people by name. We leave room for no. We stay close, and we stop asking anyone to earn care.\"\n\nShe leaned her weight against me, still fragile, still breathing.\n\nI closed my fingers over hers. Nothing ahead was safe. Nothing worth keeping ever was.\n\nTogether, we stayed by the fountain until the moon cleared the western tower and the nightglass flowers turned silver in the dark.\n\n## Coda: Empty Chair\n\nNo one removed Cael's chair from the witness room that night.\n\nPractical reasons existed. The room was crowded, evidence still needed sealing, and healers were moving too fast to argue over furniture. By midnight, nobody could bear to touch it.\n\nLeon sat on the floor beside it instead. Not in it. Beside.\n\nCassian came in with two cups of tea and set one near the empty chair before catching himself with a curse so quiet it hurt more than if he had shouted.\n\nHaru stood by the window with Elira's hand in his, both of them alive in ways that felt far too expensive.\n\nAt dawn, Mira taped a small note to the back of the chair.\n\nReserved does not mean owned.\n\nNo one knew whether it was meant for Cael, for the witness record, or for the people still left in the room. No one took it down.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-14-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep21","label":"Episode 15","number":"15","file":"episode-21-cover.webp","fallback":"The Spell Noctis Buried","eyebrow":"Forbidden Doctrine","recap":"","accent":["#111111","#b8c0ff","#ffd166"],"displayLabel":"Episode 15","displayNumber":"15","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"The Room Where She Woke Twice","body":"The morning after Cael's funeral, Elira woke with both hands pressed hard against her throat, as if trying to prove the path into her body had closed.\n\nI was already awake. That was the first thing I hated about myself that morning.\n\nSleep would come badly for a while, if it came at all. What I hated was the part of me that had begun treating Elira's breathing as a duty. Count the inhale. Count the pause. Count the exhale. Watch the pulse at her wrist. Watch the crystal for coldness. Watch the windows. Watch the door.\n\nWatch everything except the person beside me.\n\nElira's eyes opened. For one second, they were empty of the room. Then they found me.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nHer voice was too small. The Black Circle stirred beneath my skin so sharply that the ward-stone under the bed frame clicked. I pressed my palm flat against the blanket to quiet it.\n\nHaru: \"I'm here.\"\n\nElira turned her head toward the ceiling. Her lips parted once, though no sound came out. The dark crystal pendant rested against the white collar of her healing gown, plain as a raindrop, heavy as a verdict.\n\nElira: \"I dreamed I was still in the cut.\"\n\nI did not ask which cut. There was only one that mattered.\n\nElira: \"Everyone was calling my name from the other side, but I could not tell if they wanted me alive or useful.\"\n\nMy hand curled against the blanket. She saw it.\n\nElira: \"Do not become angry before you hear me.\"\n\nI forced my fingers open one at a time.\n\nHaru: \"I'm listening.\"\n\nElira looked at me then, past the shadow under my eyes, past the Black Circle pressure I was failing to hide, past the room full of quiet medical sigils. Straight at me.\n\nElira: \"When I woke after Cael restored me, everyone was relieved.\"\n\nHaru: \"They love you.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer fingers caught the edge of the bed rail, knuckles pale.\n\nElira: \"And I am grateful. I am. But relief is heavy when someone else bought it.\"\n\nOutside the recovery ward, morning bells rang across Aurelis. They sounded careful now. Even bronze had learned restraint.\n\nElira: \"I keep thinking I have to make being alive worth what happened.\"\n\nHaru: \"You don't.\"\n\nThe answer came too quickly. Elira's face tightened.\n\nElira: \"That was the correct answer.\"\n\nI heard the wound in her tone before she explained it. Correct was not the same as present.\n\nHaru: \"I'm sorry.\"\n\nElira: \"For what?\"\n\nHaru: \"Answering the fear instead of staying with you in it.\"\n\nHer expression eased by a fraction.\n\nI shifted closer on the narrow chair beside her bed. The healers had offered me a cot, and I had refused because the chair put me between Elira and the door. She had noticed. Of course she had.\n\nElira: \"You slept there?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"That was not my question.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI let out the breath I had been holding. She closed her eyes, and for a terrible moment I thought I had failed her again.\n\nThen she lifted one hand from the pendant and held it toward me. I stared at it.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"If I touch you while the Black Circle is unstable...\"\n\nElira: \"If you make my body into a danger zone, I will be furious.\"\n\nThe words were weak. The steadiness behind them was not.\n\nI took her hand. Her skin was warm, too alive for a mind still braced against cold stillness, too real to accept without wanting to build a cage around it.\n\nElira's fingers threaded between mine.\n\nElira: \"There.\"\n\nHaru: \"There?\"\n\nElira: \"You are allowed to want comfort too.\"\n\nThe Black Circle ached beneath my ribs, not with power, with recognition. I bowed my head until our joined hands rested against my forehead. For a while neither of us spoke.\n\nSenior Healer Maelin Orr entered without knocking, which I was beginning to understand was strategy rather than rudeness. Panic hid in the pause before a knock was answered.\n\nMaelin carried a tray with two cups, three folded reports, and the expression of a woman prepared to fight a government with a spoon if the government stood between her and a patient.\n\nMaelin: \"Good. Hands are occupied. That reduces the chance of either of you doing something dramatic.\"\n\nElira made a sound that was almost a laugh. Maelin's gaze flicked to me.\n\nMaelin: \"Aerlen, your circle pressure cracked a bed ward.\"\n\nHaru: \"It clicked.\"\n\nMaelin: \"It clicked because it was deciding whether to crack. Do not negotiate with furniture.\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"He was listening.\"\n\nMaelin set the tray down.\n\nMaelin: \"Then I will reward the miracle with instructions.\"\n\nShe unfolded the first report.\n\nMaelin: \"Elira Vale is medically alive, magically restored, politically inconvenient, and not available for public display.\"\n\nElira: \"That last one sounds like a diagnosis.\"\n\nMaelin: \"It is the most important diagnosis.\"\n\nShe opened the second report.\n\nMaelin: \"Haru Aerlen is not medically my patient because no one has yet invented a form foolish enough to describe him.\"\n\nHaru: \"That seems fair.\"\n\nMaelin: \"He is, however, standing close enough to my patient to become part of her recovery environment. Therefore I am allowed to be unpleasant.\"\n\nElira: \"She enjoys that part.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Correct.\"\n\nThe third report remained folded, sealed with Council wax. Maelin did not touch it yet.\n\nMaelin: \"Professor Valen and Magistra Senna Voss agree on one thing, which has alarmed several committees. You both need removal from active witness pressure.\"\n\nThe air in the room sharpened. I felt old systems listening from the walls.\n\nHaru: \"Removal sounds like separation.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That is why the wording has been rejected.\"\n\nShe looked at Elira.\n\nMaelin: \"You are not being hidden. You are not being isolated. You are being placed on protected recovery leave with chosen witness access, medical oversight, and no ceremonial obligations.\"\n\nElira's thumb moved against mine.\n\nElira: \"Where?\"\n\nMaelin unfolded the Council notice.\n\nMaelin: \"The Vale family maintains a small restoration house on the eastern lake road. It predates Circle standardization, which means it has fewer modern relay hooks for Noctis to annoy me with. Professor Valen inspected it at dawn. Rhea Valmont has already bullied three clerks into approving the security perimeter.\"\n\nHaru: \"Elira goes there.\"\n\nMaelin looked at me over her reading glasses.\n\nMaelin: \"Try again.\"\n\nI stopped. Elira did not let go of my hand.\n\nHaru: \"Elira chooses whether to go there.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Better.\"\n\nElira stared at the window. Morning light made her hair look almost white where it spread over the pillow.\n\nElira: \"Would Haru be allowed to come?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Allowed is a weak word. Your sleep worsens when he is outside the ward. His circle pressure worsens when he is ordered away from you. Councilor Krail dislikes this evidence, which improves my confidence in it.\"\n\nFor the first time that morning, Elira smiled. Small. Tired. Real.\n\nElira: \"Then I choose to go.\"\n\nHer gaze returned to me.\n\nElira: \"With you.\"\n\nThe Black Circle steadied, answered not by wards, but by the sound of her choosing a future.\n\nBy noon, Aurelis knew only the official version.\n\nElira Vale had entered protected recovery leave after catastrophic magical injury. Haru Aerlen, publicly recognized as the Black Circle bearer under distributed witness review, had been assigned as chosen proximity witness because separation posed greater instability than supervised closeness. Student Council retained access. Medical authority retained priority. Council review remained active, cold stone and drying ink anchoring the words.\n\nAira read the notice twice in the ward doorway.\n\nAira: \"That is the least romantic love letter I have ever seen.\"\n\nElira, sitting upright with too many pillows behind her, lifted an eyebrow.\n\nElira: \"You were expecting flowers from the Council?\"\n\nAira: \"No, but 'chosen proximity witness' is a tragic thing to call a boyfriend.\"\n\nLeon stood behind her with a sealed satchel of ward maps and an expression that suggested he had been forced to carry feelings in public.\n\nLeon: \"It means he is legally allowed to stand near her.\"\n\nCassian leaned against the opposite wall.\n\nCassian: \"Romance thrives.\"\n\nI looked at all of them.\n\nHaru: \"Are you here to help?\"\n\nAira: \"Emotionally or logistically?\"\n\nHaru: \"Either.\"\n\nAira: \"Logistically, yes. Emotionally, we are improvising.\"\n\nRhea arrived five minutes later with Professor Valen, two council seals, and enough authority to make the hallway straighten. I yielded my chair so Professor Valen could step beside the bed, drifting back toward the wall without meaning to.\n\nRhea: \"The route is clean. Three relay points are disabled. Two are being watched. One clerk attempted to classify recovery leave as an appearance withdrawal.\"\n\nCassian: \"Attempted?\"\n\nRhea: \"He has learned vocabulary.\"\n\nProfessor Valen came to Elira's bedside and took her hand with a gentleness that made her authority sharper, not softer.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"You are not leaving the story, Elira. You are taking back the parts of it no hearing room is owed.\"\n\nElira's composure cracked, just enough that everyone looked away except me. She wanted me to see, so I did.\n\nElira: \"What if I do not know how?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Then begin with small rebellions.\"\n\nElira: \"Such as?\"\n\nProfessor Valen smiled.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Sleep because you are tired, not because healers are counting hours. Eat something because it tastes good, not because restoration requires it. Hold his hand because you want to, not because witness theory approves.\"\n\nAira nodded solemnly.\n\nAira: \"Scandalous.\"\n\nElira's laugh broke this time. It did not last long, but while it lasted, the room remembered who she was before everyone learned how close the world had come to losing her.\n\nThe laugh also made Maelin check the chart again. She did not smile, professionally opposed as she seemed to encouraging anyone before lunch, yet the corner of her mouth shifted by half a degree and the ward-stone at Elira's bedside softened from amber to green.\n\nMaelin: \"Respiration steadier.\"\n\nAira pointed at Elira with grave triumph.\n\nAira: \"Medical laughter. I will be unbearable about this.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You already are.\"\n\nAira placed one hand over her heart.\n\nAira: \"Recognition from a senior healer.\"\n\nLeon, standing near the door with the posture of a guard trying to pass for furniture, muttered:\n\nLeon: \"Please do not encourage her.\"\n\nCassian: \"Encouragement is not required. Aira is self-fueling.\"\n\nAira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nCassian: \"That was not praise.\"\n\nAira: \"It was accurate, which is better.\"\n\nFor one brief stretch the room sounded almost like the team room after training, before Noctis filings, before public relays, before a witness died to return the girl sitting in the bed.\n\nThen Elira's hand moved to her throat again. The motion was small. Everyone saw it, and everyone pretended not to except Maelin, who had no patience for pretending around symptoms.\n\nMaelin: \"Name it.\"\n\nElira's fingers stilled against the pendant.\n\nElira: \"It feels like my body is waiting for someone to correct it.\"\n\nThe borrowed lightness drained out of the room. Maelin set the chart down.\n\nMaelin: \"That is not a magical symptom.\"\n\nHaru: \"But Dominion used magic.\"\n\nMaelin looked across the bed at me.\n\nMaelin: \"Do not make the mistake of thinking the wound ends where the spell ended.\"\n\nI closed my mouth. She was right, and I hated how often healers were right when they said things I did not know how to fix.\n\nMaelin turned back to Elira.\n\nMaelin: \"Your body remembers interruption. It will ask for proof. You may answer it with touch, breath, food, anger, tears, silence, or a formal complaint submitted to whichever cosmic authority designed nerves.\"\n\nElira stared at her.\n\nMaelin: \"I have forms.\"\n\nAira made a sound halfway between a laugh and a sob. Elira's mouth trembled.\n\nElira: \"Do you really?\"\n\nMaelin: \"No. But I should.\"\n\nThe pendant gave one small violet pulse, not magic enough to register on a ward, only enough to make my fingers ache with wanting to break whatever could not be mended by force. Elira saw my hand tighten against the wall. She did not let me hide there.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\nElira: \"Sit.\"\n\nThat was not a request. I crossed back from the wall and sat on the chair beside the bed. She held out her hand, and this time I did not make her ask twice. Her palm was warm. That simple fact hit me hard enough to steal my breath.\n\nElira: \"No counting.\"\n\nI froze. She squeezed my hand once.\n\nElira: \"You are doing it in your eyes.\"\n\nAira looked at me. Leon looked at the floor. Cassian suddenly found the ceiling fascinating.\n\nMaelin, merciless as weather, said:\n\nMaelin: \"Counting can be a bridge. It can also become a cage. Decide which one you are building before you finish the walls.\"\n\nThe Black Circle went quiet beneath my skin, listening.\n\nHaru: \"I do not know how to stop.\"\n\nElira's expression softened in a way that hurt worse than accusation.\n\nElira: \"Then do it where I can see you. Do not turn my breathing into a private prison.\"\n\nI looked down at our joined hands.\n\nHaru: \"I was afraid that if I stopped watching, the world would take you again.\"\n\nThe words came out too quiet for the room, though everyone heard them. Elira did not look away.\n\nElira: \"And I was afraid that if I woke needing you, you would become only the person who watches doors.\"\n\nThat cut through cleanly. No blood, only the old shape of loneliness opening where I had mistaken vigilance for protection.\n\nHaru: \"I do not want that.\"\n\nElira: \"Then stay beside me, not around me.\"\n\nMaelin picked up the chart again as if the room had not just shifted on its hinges.\n\nMaelin: \"Good. Emotional hazard named. Physical hazard next.\"\n\nTen minutes later, two council assistants brought Rhea three sealed documents. Her expression kept both of them half a step back.\n\nRhea: \"Noctis has filed an objection to protected recovery leave.\"\n\nElira closed her eyes.\n\nLeon: \"They object to her recovering?\"\n\nRhea: \"They object to the leave being protected.\"\n\nCassian: \"Ah. They prefer injured witnesses where they can reach them.\"\n\nRhea's silence confirmed more than a yes would have. Something cold stirred under my ribs, hungry for a cleaner answer than paperwork. Elira's thumb pressed against my knuckle, reminding me my hand was holding hers.\n\nRhea crossed the room and set the documents on the small table beside Maelin's chart.\n\nRhea: \"The objection is denied in immediate effect. Senna Voss logged the denial under medical authority, witness integrity, and public sequence protection. I am here because denial does not mean absence of pressure.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Translation: they will try to make rest look suspicious.\"\n\nRhea: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe set her emergency seal flat on the dark wood. Aira took out her notebook.\n\nAira: \"Fine. We make rest boring in public and sacred in private.\"\n\nEveryone looked at her.\n\nAira: \"What? That was excellent.\"\n\nProfessor Valen, who had stayed quietly beside Elira through the exchange, inclined her head.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"It was.\"\n\nElira's shoulders lowered.\n\nProfessor Valen had brought a folded shawl, a small cedar box, and the worn authority of someone who had argued with the Council before breakfast and won because the alternative offended history.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"The restoration house at Lake Velis is ready. Its wards predate the Circle hierarchy and do not classify visitors by rank unless they attempt harm.\"\n\nHaru: \"Unless?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Old houses are allowed opinions.\"\n\nMaelin: \"The medical wards are mine.\"\n\nRhea: \"The outer route authority is mine.\"\n\nAira lifted her pen.\n\nAira: \"The snack strategy is mine.\"\n\nLeon: \"Why does snack strategy require a notebook?\"\n\nAira: \"Because trauma recovery without food becomes philosophy, and philosophy makes Haru worse.\"\n\nI should have objected, if I had possessed a lie strong enough. Elira's fingers tightened around mine in silent laughter.\n\nProfessor Valen set the cedar box on the bed tray and opened it. Inside lay a plain black ribbon edged in silver thread, neither jewelry nor charm. Elira looked down at it.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"A recovery token from the house. It records voluntary presence, not condition. If you tie it to a door, the house knows the room is chosen. If you tie it to a wrist, the house knows the person entered by consent. If you throw it into the lake, the house will judge you dramatic but respect the decision.\"\n\nElira touched the ribbon with two fingers.\n\nElira: \"Consent first.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Always, if the old builders were having a good century.\"\n\nHumor lived in her voice, though not carelessness. Rhea's gaze sharpened.\n\nRhea: \"Can Noctis subpoena the house's record?\"\n\nProfessor Valen closed the box.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"They may attempt to subpoena the lake if they enjoy humiliation.\"\n\nCassian's smile returned.\n\nCassian: \"I want to watch that hearing.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No one is watching anything for forty-eight hours except meals, medicine, sleep, and whether Haru Aerlen remembers chairs exist for sitting rather than strategic door control.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\nMaelin: \"Do not test me. I have sedatives and moral clarity.\"\n\nElira laughed again, longer this time, and the ward-stone turned green. No one celebrated, because celebration asked too much of the hour. Yet Aira wrote a line in her notebook, and when I glanced over, I saw:\n\n**Second laugh. Voluntary. Witnessed by friends, not systems.**\n\nShe noticed me looking and tilted the page toward me without hiding it.\n\nAira: \"Some records are ours.\"\n\nThe sentence settled over the room more gently than any ward.\n\nRhea placed one more item on the table, not a document this time, but a small square of folded black cloth sealed with Aurelis blue and the plain white mark Senna Voss used for protected witness material. Elira's smile faded.\n\nRhea: \"Cael's public funeral record has been copied for the restoration house archive. Not the Council version. The Aurelis student version.\"\n\nNo one moved. The ward-stone in the bed frame hummed once, soft as a held breath.\n\nElira: \"Why bring it here?\"\n\nRhea kept her voice formal, the way she always kept tenderness intact in public.\n\nRhea: \"Because recovery leave is not exile from grief. And because Noctis filed a correction request at dawn.\"\n\nLeon swore under his breath. Cassian's smile vanished. Aira's pen stopped.\n\nHaru: \"Correction?\"\n\nRhea: \"They object to the phrase voluntary refusal of ownership. They prefer final service under catastrophic confusion.\"\n\nThe Black Circle did not rise. It went colder, which was worse. Elira's fingers tightened around mine until it hurt, and the pain kept me anchored in the room.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nOne quiet, wounded word, completely alive. Rhea inclined her head.\n\nRhea: \"The correction was denied. Mira's gallery copy, Nera's testimony, Darian's dissent, and Iven Sahl's funeral presence all support the existing language.\"\n\nElira looked at the folded cloth.\n\nElira: \"Iven?\"\n\nRhea: \"He asked if students from Noctis may sign the Aurelis copy without being absorbed into Noctis' official delegation.\"\n\nAira's eyes filled before she could blink them sharp.\n\nAira: \"Good.\"\n\nLeon: \"Is he safe?\"\n\nRhea: \"As safe as a Noctis student publicly refusing convenient language can be, which is to say not enough. Senna Voss has opened a protected channel.\"\n\nElira closed her eyes. For a moment I thought the news would push her back under the weight of the funeral. Instead she opened them and looked at Rhea.\n\nElira: \"Send him my thanks.\"\n\nRhea: \"As Elira Vale or as primary witness?\"\n\nElira's jaw tightened, then steadied.\n\nElira: \"As Elira.\"\n\nProfessor Valen's eyes softened behind her glasses. Maelin pretended to adjust the chart. Aira wrote the answer down. I wanted to memorize the way Elira said her own name there, stripped of title and function, and hated that memorizing it felt like another record. So I did not turn it into a monument. I only held her hand.\n\nRhea folded the cloth back into her satchel.\n\nRhea: \"The copy will wait at the house if you request it. It will not present itself otherwise.\"\n\nElira breathed in slowly.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nCassian leaned against the wall, quieter than usual.\n\nCassian: \"Cael would have hated all this paperwork.\"\n\nLeon glanced sideways at him.\n\nCassian: \"What? He had taste.\"\n\nFor one second, grief made room for the boy who had hated paperwork, not just the symbol around which it gathered. Elira's mouth trembled.\n\nElira: \"He would have corrected the grammar.\"\n\nAira wiped at one eye with the back of her wrist and glared at her notebook as if ink had betrayed her.\n\nAira: \"Then we will correct it for him.\"\n\nWhen the carriage arrived, I helped Elira stand. She hated needing help, and I knew because she let me see that too.\n\nHaru: \"Tell me if I am holding too tightly.\"\n\nElira: \"You are.\"\n\nI loosened my grip at once. She did not pull away.\n\nElira: \"Better.\"\n\nMaelin brought the wheeled chair herself, both hands on the handles and a stare that dared any clerk, aide, or spectator to turn medical equipment into symbolism. Elira looked at the chair, then at the ward entrance beyond it. The hallway was not long. It only felt long because every door between the bed and the carriage had learned how to become public.\n\nMaelin: \"You may ride.\"\n\nElira: \"May?\"\n\nMaelin's mouth tightened.\n\nMaelin: \"Medical permission. Not command.\"\n\nElira nodded once, then set one hand on the bed rail and pushed herself upright. Every person in the room tensed without stepping. Rhea's seal brightened. Leon shifted half a pace. I nearly moved toward the door. Elira caught all of it.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe word stopped us cleaner than a ward. She breathed through the first wave of dizziness, her hand shaking on the rail while the pendant at her throat stayed dark.\n\nElira: \"I will use the chair if I need it.\"\n\nMaelin studied her face.\n\nMaelin: \"You need it.\"\n\nElira: \"Probably.\"\n\nThere was no theatrical defiance in it, which was why everyone listened.\n\nElira: \"But I want to walk the first door.\"\n\nAira's pen lowered. Elira looked at me then.\n\nElira: \"Beside me.\"\n\nShe did not add the second half aloud, and did not have to. I stepped to her side instead of the doorway. It took visible effort, and I was glad she could see the effort instead of wondering where I had hidden the fear.\n\nHaru: \"Beside you.\"\n\nElira placed her hand on my sleeve rather than my wrist, leaving one layer of cloth between skin and panic.\n\nRhea opened the ward door. The corridor beyond was lined with students, healers, council aides, and faculty pretending badly that they had not gathered into a farewell. The wheeled chair followed behind us like a patient argument. Elira took one step, then another. Nobody applauded, which was a mercy; applause would have made survival sound finished.\n\nAt the first doorframe, Elira stopped. Her breath had gone thin, sweat damp at her temple. Maelin brought the chair close without triumph, and Elira sat before her knees could make the decision for her.\n\nMaelin: \"Good.\"\n\nElira: \"Do not sound pleased.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I am pleased by correct use of medical equipment. It is a private joy.\"\n\nA small laugh moved through the doorway and broke nothing.\n\nRhea glanced down the corridor.\n\nRhea: \"Departure terms?\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened once on my sleeve, then released.\n\nElira: \"No procession.\"\n\nAira wrote it down.\n\nElira: \"No applause. No one says brave.\"\n\nThrough the doorway, Mira Castellan hugged a sign board to her chest and nodded as if taking orders from someone who had earned the right to keep them small.\n\nElira: \"If anyone asks what this is, say I am leaving before I become useful to the wrong people again.\"\n\nThe sentence entered the hallway plain and jagged, and it held. Rhea's council seal wrote the terms in red light beside the door.\n\n**DEPARTURE BY STUDENT TERMS**\n\n**NO PROCESSION**\n\n**NO APPLAUSE**\n\n**NO COURAGE CLAIM WITHOUT ACTOR CONSENT**\n\nMira copied the last line with both hands.\n\nI thought of Elira waking with both hands at her throat. I thought of Cael lying under Rhea's seal. I thought of every instinct in me that wanted to build a wall between her and the watching world, and then I walked beside the chair. Beside. Not around.\n\nAt the outer ward entrance, the academy had gathered despite the notice forbidding a public send-off. Students stood in messy rows with notebooks, flowers, ribbons, and quiet faces. Mira held her sign high enough to see over the front row:\n\n**RECOVERY IS NOT ABSENCE**\n\nBeside her stood Iven Sahl, flanked by the Noctis students who had refused the official delegation. His hands were empty, his head bowed. Elira saw him and caught her breath, then lifted one hand in a greeting too small to be mistaken for a speech. Iven looked up as if someone had handed back his name.\n\nThe carriage door opened. Elira paused on the step.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes?\"\n\nElira: \"When we get there, I do not want the first thing we do to be checking wards.\"\n\nEvery instinct in me objected. I swallowed all of them.\n\nHaru: \"What do you want first?\"\n\nShe looked toward the lake road beyond the academy gates, where sunlight moved through trees untouched by trial halls, witness rails, and funeral banners.\n\nElira: \"Tea.\"\n\nHer fingers found mine.\n\nElira: \"And then I want to sit somewhere no one is waiting for me to prove I survived correctly.\"\n\nThe Black Circle quieted beneath my ribs, attentive rather than coiled.\n\nHaru: \"Then tea first.\"\n\nElira stepped into the carriage, and I followed. Behind us, Aurelis did not cheer. The students stayed on the steps as the wheels began to roll, watching two people leave the center of the world because one of them had asked for quiet and the other had finally understood that quiet could be protection too.\n\n## The Restoration House at Lake Velis\n\nThe restoration house did not look important enough to survive history. That was its first mercy.\n\nIt stood on the eastern shore of Lake Velis, tucked between cedar trees and a long slope of silver grass. The roof was dark green tile, the walls pale stone warmed by years of sun. No towers. No banners. No witness rail waiting to turn pain into testimony. Just windows. Too many windows for my comfort.\n\nElira noticed before the carriage stopped.\n\nElira: \"You are counting entry points.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI met her gaze without looking away. Elira leaned back against the carriage cushion, tired but amused.\n\nElira: \"Tea first.\"\n\nI exhaled.\n\nHaru: \"Tea first.\"\n\nProfessor Valen had left the front door unlocked, which nearly made me turn the carriage around. A note waited on the small entry table beneath a vase of white lake flowers.\n\n**Small rebellions begin with entering without permission.**\n\nBelow that, in Aira's handwriting:\n\n**I wanted to write something useful, but Professor Valen got there first. There are buns in the blue tin. Do not become tragic before eating.**\n\nElira read both notes and pressed a hand over her mouth. The laugh came out thin, but it came out.\n\nThe cedar box Professor Valen had sent ahead from the ward sat beside the vase. The black ribbon edged in silver waited inside, plain enough to look harmless and old enough that the air around it refused modern categories. Elira opened the lid and stood looking down at it.\n\nHaru: \"You do not have to decide now.\"\n\nShe touched the ribbon with two fingers.\n\nElira: \"Yes, I do.\"\n\nThe answer was quiet, yet the house heard it. A line of old silver warmed along the entry table, then paused, waiting without impatience. Elira lifted the ribbon.\n\nHaru: \"Door or wrist?\"\n\nShe looked toward the hall, then toward the lake-bright windows, then down at her own hands.\n\nElira: \"Door first.\"\n\nThe choice surprised me, and she saw that too.\n\nElira: \"If I tie it to my wrist immediately, someone will make it about whether I consent to being watched. If I tie it to the door, the first thing this house learns is that the room is chosen.\"\n\nShe moved to the inside of the front door and tied the ribbon through the small iron ring beneath the latch. Slowly, fumbling the first pass because her fingers were still weak. I kept my hands at my sides. She untangled the knot herself, breathed once, and tried again. The second knot held.\n\nThe house answered without ceremony. The floor warmed beneath our boots and wrote, in no language I had learned and every language my body understood:\n\n**ENTERED BY CHOICE**\n\nElira's shoulders dropped, not all the way, just enough.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good house.\"\n\nThe entry table gave a small wooden creak. Elira looked up at me.\n\nElira: \"Did you just praise architecture?\"\n\nHaru: \"It behaved well.\"\n\nHer laugh came back, still unsteady, still hers. I lifted the satchel from her shoulder before she could pretend it was light, and she let me.\n\nThe house smelled of cedar, old paper, lake wind, and sugar. In the kitchen, a kettle warmed itself over a blue flame with no modern Circle signature. Pre-standardization house magic, humming without asking what I was.\n\nElira sat at the small table near the window while sunlight touched her hair. For one suspended breath, the kitchen forgot white healing gowns, funeral flowers, and the moment her gold had gone out in my arms.\n\nThen a spoon clicked against porcelain.\n\nElira flinched.\n\nThe Black Circle rose so fast the window glass darkened at the edges.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nOne word, commanding without sharpness or fear. I froze with my hand half lifted.\n\nThe Black Circle did not understand a spoon. It understood impact, hostile vectors, severance routes, and the exact instant a room became too late. A porcelain click did not fit any category it trusted, so it invented one: sudden sound, Elira flinching, threat.\n\nNo. Neither Noctis nor Lucien, neither blade nor trap, only a kitchen beside a lake, sunlight on the wood, and cooling tea because both of us had forgotten normal things required unsteady human hands.\n\nThe power did not believe me. I could read ancient wards and stop a spell mid-flight, yet I could not explain safety to the part of me built out of every second I had failed to keep her safe. For a heartbeat, I felt how easy it would be to force the whole house silent. No sharp sounds. No sudden motion. A perfect quiet arranged around her fear.\n\nThe thought sickened me because it arrived dressed as care. I held my hand still in midair while the kettle flame bent away from me.\n\nElira breathed once, twice, her knuckles white around the table edge.\n\nElira: \"It was just the sound.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"You did not know. You reacted.\"\n\nThat was true, and unbearable.\n\nHaru: \"I'm sorry.\"\n\nElira closed her eyes.\n\nElira: \"Please do not apologize like you have already retreated.\"\n\nI lowered my hand. The window glass cleared.\n\nHaru: \"What do you need?\"\n\nElira opened her eyes, wet and steady.\n\nElira: \"For you to sit down.\"\n\nI sat across from her. She looked at the empty chair beside her own, so I moved there instead. A few inches of old floorboard separated the chairs, and crossing them felt harder than walking into a hearing room.\n\nElira reached for the spoon. Her hand shook. I kept both hands on my knees and did not take it from her. That cost more restraint than holding back a spell.\n\nShe stirred her tea slowly. The spoon clicked again, softer. She flinched again, less. I stayed seated, keeping the Black Circle locked beneath my skin.\n\nElira watched me as carefully as I watched her.\n\nElira: \"Again.\"\n\nHaru: \"You don't have to...\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe stirred once more. Click. Her breath caught, then let go. Outside the window, the lake moved in slow silver lines.\n\nElira: \"There.\"\n\nHaru: \"There.\"\n\nShe looked down into the cup.\n\nElira: \"I do not want my fear to become another thing everyone organizes around.\"\n\nHaru: \"Fear deserves care.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes. Not a throne.\"\n\nThat sounded so much like her that something tight in my chest loosened enough to ache. I reached slowly, keeping my hand in sight until she turned her palm up.\n\nWe drank tea and ate Aira's buns from the blue tin. Elira managed half of one and declared victory. I ate three because she watched me with narrowed eyes until I stopped pretending I did not need food.\n\nAfter tea, the house began introducing itself through small refusals.\n\nWhen I walked toward the largest window to inspect the latch, the floorboards warmed under my boots and traced a line of silver in the wood.\n\n**GUEST.**\n\nNo voice. No chime. Just a clear impression that rejected asset, candidate, and Black Circle in favor of one plain word. I stared down at my boots.\n\nElira, wrapped in a blanket on the sofa, watched me over her cup.\n\nElira: \"Did the floor just scold you?\"\n\nHaru: \"It identified me.\"\n\nElira's brows drew together.\n\nHaru: \"As a guest.\"\n\nShe was silent for a moment, and then relief touched her face, cautious and shy of trusting itself.\n\nElira: \"Good house.\"\n\nThe floor cooled, apparently satisfied. I considered thanking it aloud and stopped myself. That was already a dangerous degree of cooperation with furniture.\n\nElira set her cup down and looked toward the hallway.\n\nElira: \"Show me the rest?\"\n\nHaru: \"Maelin said stairs only if necessary.\"\n\nElira waited.\n\nHaru: \"The rest of the first floor.\"\n\nElira: \"Better.\"\n\nWe moved slowly. At Aurelis, slowness had meant timing and discipline. Here it meant her hand resting against the plaster wall when the room tilted, or stopping between the kitchen and the sitting room while her breath caught for no reason either of us could name.\n\nHaru: \"Stop?\"\n\nElira nodded once. I did not reach for her; she curled her fingers around my sleeve, taking the measure of help through cloth.\n\nElira: \"It is annoying.\"\n\nHaru: \"The weakness?\"\n\nElira's eyes narrowed. I heard my mistake before Maelin could materialize in spirit and strike me with a clipboard.\n\nHaru: \"No. I mean the body delay. Not weakness.\"\n\nElira let me sweat for three seconds.\n\nElira: \"Acceptable correction.\"\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nElira: \"Barely.\"\n\nThe corner of her mouth twitched. Second victory of the afternoon.\n\nThe sitting room faced the lake through three tall windows. White curtains stirred in a breeze with no clear source. A low shelf held blankets, board games, blank notebooks, a small music box, and six sealed packets labeled in Aira's handwriting.\n\n**FOR WHEN HARU LOOKS LIKE HE IS ABOUT TO INVENT A PROTOCOL.**\n\nElira picked up the first packet. Inside was a deck of hand-painted cards and a slip of paper:\n\n**Play something with rules you did not write.**\n\nThe second packet held dried peach slices.\n\n**Feed him. Feed yourself first, because hierarchy matters.**\n\nThe third held a small blue ribbon.\n\n**Emergency hair management. Do not ask Leon for help unless you want one side battle-ready and the other side tragic.**\n\nElira laughed softly. The sound still surprised her, and surprised me too, though I stayed quiet and listened without trying to trap it.\n\nElira ran her thumb over the ribbon.\n\nElira: \"She was scared.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira?\"\n\nElira nodded.\n\nElira: \"This is what she does when she is scared. She builds logistics so no one has to say the frightening part first.\"\n\nI thought of Aira's notebook in the ward room.\n\nHaru: \"It works.\"\n\nElira looked at me with tired affection.\n\nElira: \"On you especially.\"\n\nI could not deny it.\n\nBeyond the sitting room lay a small study. Professor Valen had left that door unlocked too, and the desk faced the inner wall rather than the lake, as if she understood that someone turned into a public spectacle sometimes needed a wall promising nothing could walk up behind her.\n\nThree folders lay on the blotter: one blue, one red, one plain brown. Elira's fingers tightened on my sleeve.\n\nHaru: \"We do not have to open them.\"\n\nElira: \"What are they?\"\n\nI checked the seals without touching them.\n\nHaru: \"Medical instructions. Council contact conditions. Personal letters.\"\n\nElira stared at the brown folder.\n\nElira: \"Personal from whom?\"\n\nThe floorboards nudged the corner of the brown folder half an inch closer to her. I disliked how much personality this building had. Elira caught my expression.\n\nElira: \"It likes me better.\"\n\nHaru: \"Everyone does.\"\n\nHer face softened quickly enough that I did not regret saying it.\n\nShe opened the brown folder. The first letter was from Mira Castellan, careful at the top and growing breathless by the second paragraph. Elira read silently, her eyes slowing near the middle.\n\nElira: \"She says the Green Circle students are not copying the sequence because they think I owe them proof.\"\n\nShe swallowed hard.\n\nElira: \"They are copying it because they do not want anyone turning Cael's death into a rumor before the record can protect him.\"\n\nOutside, wind moved through the cedar needles.\n\nHaru: \"Mira understands records.\"\n\nElira: \"She understands grief.\"\n\nI had no answer to that.\n\nThe second letter lay on unsigned Noctis paper with no official seal. Elira hesitated.\n\nHaru: \"I can read it first.\"\n\nElira's mouth pressed thin.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nShe drew a steadying breath and touched my elbow.\n\nElira: \"But sit close.\"\n\nI pulled up the second chair and sat beside her.\n\nIven Sahl's letter was short and clumsy. He wrote that Cael had once lent him a thermal charm during winter drills and told him not to give it back until he stopped confusing obedience with survival. He wrote that he had not understood then, and was beginning to now. At the bottom, one line had been crossed out and written again.\n\n**I am sorry he had to die where we could finally see him.**\n\nElira pressed her hand over her mouth. No laugh came this time, only shaky, careful breathing. I let my shoulder touch hers, and she leaned into it.\n\nElira: \"He should not have to apologize to me.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"Noctis made children write grief like petitions.\"\n\nThe Black Circle stirred beneath my ribs, pulled not toward the windows, but toward Noctis itself: frightened students, neat forms, and polite murder. Elira's hand found mine under the desk.\n\nElira: \"Beside me.\"\n\nI drew one slow breath, then another.\n\nHaru: \"Beside you.\"\n\nWe left the red and blue folders shut. It felt like treason against urgency, and like keeping the first promise of the day.\n\nLater, we wandered back into the kitchen because Elira wanted to look at the buns again. She inspected them with the solemn gravity of a commander reviewing troops.\n\nElira: \"That one has too much sugar.\"\n\nHaru: \"For whom?\"\n\nElira: \"For dignity.\"\n\nHaru: \"Dignity survived Lucien. It can survive icing.\"\n\nShe looked sideways at me.\n\nHaru: \"Aira has been affecting my speech.\"\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nShe picked the smallest bun and took two bites. Victory revised upward.\n\nAfterward she insisted on walking to the back threshold, not outside yet, where cedar shade met the pale stone step and Lake Velis spread beyond the grass like a piece of sky laid down to rest. Elira stood with one hand braced on the doorframe while wind lifted her hair. Her pendant hung still against her collar, looking for one quiet minute like an ordinary stone chosen for beauty.\n\nElira: \"I thought I would feel safer here.\"\n\nHaru: \"Do you?\"\n\nShe studied the water.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nMy hand tightened at my side. She saw it at once.\n\nElira: \"Not because the house failed. Because safety is not something a place can hand back all at once.\"\n\nThe words made sense. I disliked them anyway.\n\nElira: \"But I feel less watched.\"\n\nHaru: \"There are wards.\"\n\nElira: \"Wards are not the same as eyes.\"\n\nThe threshold warmed beneath our soles, approving or offended; it was hard to tell. Elira stepped back inside before I could suggest she was tired, then promptly ruined my restraint by swaying once. I caught her elbow, visible and careful. She leaned into the support and shut her eyes.\n\nElira: \"I hate that I needed that.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"Do not say you are sorry.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was not going to.\"\n\nShe opened one eye.\n\nHaru: \"I was going to say I am glad I was beside you.\"\n\nHer expression softened. She let the sentence stand, and let me help her back to the sofa.\n\nBy late afternoon, the security perimeter checked in through the front hall mirror. Rhea's council seal flared in the glass first.\n\nRhea: \"Status.\"\n\nFrom the sofa under her blanket, Elira raised her teacup.\n\nElira: \"Tea achieved.\"\n\nRhea blinked once.\n\nRhea: \"Acceptable.\"\n\nAira shoved her face into the reflection beside Rhea's shoulder.\n\nAira: \"That is not an update. Are there chairs? Are there blankets? Has Haru threatened a shrub?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"He considered the windows.\"\n\nAira: \"Progress. Windows are not shrubs.\"\n\nLeon appeared behind them with his arms folded.\n\nLeon: \"The eastern road is clear. Two Council outriders passed an hour ago. No Noctis relay interference.\"\n\nCassian leaned into frame from the other side.\n\nCassian: \"Also, Dren says if Aerlen cracks the restoration house, he has to repair it without celestial shortcuts.\"\n\nHaru: \"Noted.\"\n\nElira's smile faded as she studied their faces.\n\nElira: \"How is Aurelis?\"\n\nThe mirror quieted. Aira answered first.\n\nAira: \"Messy. Awake. Too many committees. Too many people pretending they always believed mercy was policy.\"\n\nLeon: \"Green Circle students are still copying the public sequence records.\"\n\nCassian: \"Noctis pulled its official banner from the east dormitory. The student group stayed.\"\n\nRhea: \"Councilor Krail filed a supplementary risk memorandum at noon.\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened around her cup. I felt the tension before she spoke.\n\nHaru: \"About me?\"\n\nRhea: \"About your proximity to every major witness, yes. Senna Voss marked it procedurally premature.\"\n\nAira: \"Which is formal language for sit down and stop pointing at grieving students.\"\n\nRhea: \"Less colorful, but accurate.\"\n\nElira set her cup down.\n\nElira: \"Do not keep all of it from us.\"\n\nProfessor Valen stepped into the reflection then, her gaze gentle enough to be dangerous.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"We will not. But recovery leave is not remote command. You are allowed to hear the news without solving it before supper.\"\n\nElira looked ready to argue, then exhaustion slid across her face.\n\nElira: \"I hate how reasonable that is.\"\n\nAira: \"Excellent. Hate it while resting.\"\n\nThe mirror dimmed once Rhea set the morning check-in time, leaving the house quiet and ours for the evening.\n\n## The Spoon Test\n\nBefore we went down the hall to look at bedrooms for the night, Elira decided supper should include hot soup.\n\nThat was not one of Maelin's directives. Maelin had left jars of broth and written instructions with the grim satisfaction of a healer who believed nourishment could overrule shock by sheer stubbornness. Elira's decision was smaller, and far less polite to grief.\n\nShe insisted on holding the spoon herself.\n\nAt the kitchen table, her hand shook before the spoon even cleared the bowl. I noticed before she lifted it, because I noticed everything that looked like struggle or harm. My fingers twitched half an inch off the wood to steady her wrist, then stopped.\n\nElira: \"Let me fail once before you rescue me.\"\n\nThe words cut deep. She saw that they did, and did not take them back.\n\nThe spoon reached her mouth on the second try, awkwardly, spilling a warm streak of broth across the towel spread over her knees. Aira would have logged it as soup aggression.\n\nElira let out one short laugh that startled both of us. I stared at the wet stain on her lap as if it were an unruly miracle.\n\nElira: \"There. Alive.\"\n\nI let out a long breath, something tight beneath my ribs giving way at last. For that minute, no council owned the word.\n\nAfter I carried the soup bowls to the sink, I noticed Maelin had prepared two bedrooms at the far end of the first-floor hall.\n\nThe larger room faced the lake. A smaller one waited across the corridor with its door open, blankets turned down, and a bedside lamp already lit. Fresh clothes had been split evenly between both rooms rather than laid out like orders.\n\nOn the larger room's bedside table sat another note in Maelin's neat hand.\n\n**The patient sleeps worse alone. The proximity witness destabilizes when exiled to chairs. Two beds exist. Use one or both. Ask each other instead of inventing a medical doctrine about it.**\n\nElira read it twice. Color rose in her cheeks that had nothing to do with fever, and she handed the paper to me.\n\nElira: \"She anticipated both of us badly.\"\n\nHaru: \"Accurately.\"\n\nElira: \"Unfortunately.\"\n\nI glanced toward the open door across the hall.\n\nHaru: \"I can sleep there.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"I don't want to assume.\"\n\nHer expression softened, then tightened with the simple effort of staying upright.\n\nElira: \"Then ask before I fall asleep in this hallway and make the decision medically embarrassing.\"\n\nHaru: \"Do you want me beside you?\"\n\nElira's hand rose to the pendant. This time she did not grip it. She only rested two fingers over the crystal, as if reminding herself it was present without being the whole story.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer answer was quiet and certain.\n\nElira: \"I am scared to sleep. I am more scared to wake and have to search for you.\"\n\nMy throat closed.\n\nHaru: \"Then I will be beside you.\"\n\nElira: \"Not guarding the door.\"\n\nHaru: \"Beside you.\"\n\nShe nodded once.\n\nThat night, the house taught us how awkward ordinary survival could be. Elira changed behind the folding screen while I stared out at the lake with the grim focus of someone studying enemy formations. She caught me at it and laughed under her breath, which made both of us blush worse than the moment deserved.\n\nWhen I came back from the washroom, she was sitting on the bed in a pale blue sleeping robe, her hair loose over one shoulder, the dark pendant resting against the cloth.\n\nSeeing her simply alive undid me. Elira saw it in my face.\n\nElira: \"Come here.\"\n\nI did, slowly. She shifted under the blanket, and at first I lay down on top of the spare coverlet, because apparently even after everything I could still manage to be ridiculous.\n\nElira stared up at the ceiling.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes?\"\n\nElira: \"Are you planning to sleep like a formal apology?\"\n\nI turned my head toward her. Her mouth trembled, and mine did too.\n\nHaru: \"I am trying to be respectful.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe reached under the edge of the blanket and found my hand.\n\nElira: \"Respect me by believing I can choose closeness.\"\n\nThe Black Circle warmed once beneath my ribs, steady and quiet. I moved beneath the covers only when she tugged my hand, settling shoulder against shoulder until warmth met warmth. She exhaled as if she had been holding that breath since the Wounded City.\n\nFor a while, we listened to the lake against the shore.\n\nElira: \"Aira is going to call this a honeymoon.\"\n\nI blinked into the dim room.\n\nHaru: \"This is medical leave.\"\n\nElira: \"That will not stop her.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"Would it be terrible if part of me wanted it to feel a little like one?\"\n\nThe question entered me more carefully than any blade. I turned toward her.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nHer eyes caught the low lamplight.\n\nHaru: \"It would be human.\"\n\nShe looked at me for a long moment, then rested her forehead against my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"I want some part of being alive to belong to love. Not councils. Not Noctis. Not death. Us.\"\n\nMy hand rose to her hair and paused a fraction of an inch away. She leaned into the pause, giving permission again. I stroked her hair once, then again, slow enough for both of us to believe it.\n\nHaru: \"Then tomorrow belongs to us.\"\n\nElira: \"Not all of it.\"\n\nHaru: \"No. Not all.\"\n\nElira: \"But enough.\"\n\nOutside, moonlight moved across the lake. Inside, Elira fell asleep with her hand in mine. I stayed awake longer, listening to her breathe without counting, letting the quiet hold through the night.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-21-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep22","label":"Episode 16","number":"16","file":"episode-22-cover.webp","fallback":"The Field That Learned to Bleed","eyebrow":"Activity Breach","recap":"","accent":["#080808","#5eead4","#ef476f"],"displayLabel":"Episode 16","displayNumber":"16","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"The Quiet Place","body":"The night after the spoon test did not heal us.\n\nIt gave us a morning.\n\nThe first full day at Lake Velis began with Elira stealing my shirt.\n\nThat was not how she described it.\n\nElira: \"This was folded on the wrong chair.\"\n\nHaru: \"It was folded on my chair.\"\n\nShe stood in the bedroom doorway wearing the dark shirt over her pale sleeping robe, sleeves falling past her wrists, hair loose and gold in the morning light.\n\nElira: \"Then your chair made a generous decision.\"\n\nI had faced Lucien's lightless pressure, old Foundation systems, Dominion Severance, public classification, and the Black Circle trying to become grief with a shape.\n\nNone of them prepared me for Elira Vale looking warm, tired, and pleased with herself in my shirt.\n\nShe noticed. Of course she did.\n\nElira: \"You are staring.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI did not bother pretending otherwise.\n\nHer confidence faltered for half a breath, caught between wanting to be looked at and remembering how lately every stare had been medical, political, or mournful. I saw the moment she wondered whether my eyes were finding the wound before the woman.\n\nSo I gave her the truth with no useful parts removed.\n\nHaru: \"You look like you slept. You look annoyed that you needed to. You look beautiful. The shirt is not getting returned, is it?\"\n\nThe uncertainty left her face.\n\nElira: \"Absolutely not.\"\n\nBreakfast was bread, fruit, soft cheese, and another note from Aira tucked inside the jam cupboard.\n\n**If this note is found by Haru: make her eat something. If found by Elira: make him eat something. If found by both: I am excellent at friendship.**\n\nElira read it aloud with solemn dignity.\n\nThen she ate two slices of bread because Aira's threat carried across distance.\n\nAfterward, we walked to the lake. Slowly. Elira hated the slowly.\n\nHer body betrayed it in small ways: a pause at the porch steps, a hand tightening on the railing, breath measured too carefully when the path sloped downward. She did not complain. That made it worse.\n\nAt the third pause, I stopped beside her.\n\nHaru: \"You can be angry.\"\n\nElira looked at the lake instead of me.\n\nElira: \"I am.\"\n\nHaru: \"At me?\"\n\nElira: \"Sometimes.\"\n\nThe answer landed cleanly. I nodded.\n\nHaru: \"Good.\"\n\nShe turned then.\n\nElira: \"Good?\"\n\nHaru: \"If you are angry at me, you are not making yourself grateful for every breath.\"\n\nHer eyes sharpened. Then softened.\n\nElira: \"That was annoyingly perceptive.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira has been a bad influence.\"\n\nElira: \"Aira would call it a good influence loudly enough to be heard from Aurelis.\"\n\nWe continued down the path.\n\nAt the waterline, an old wooden dock stretched over the lake. The boards had been repaired many times by practical hands. No ceremony. No crest marks. Just care repeated until it became structure.\n\nElira sat at the edge and removed her shoes. I sat beside her. For several minutes, we let our feet hover above the water like cowards.\n\nElira: \"It is going to be cold.\"\n\nHaru: \"Probably.\"\n\nElira: \"You first.\"\n\nHaru: \"A command from the Yellow Circle prodigy?\"\n\nElira: \"A request from your recovery-leave girlfriend.\"\n\nI put my feet in the water.\n\nCold shot up my legs so fast my entire soul reconsidered loyalty.\n\nElira laughed fully, clear and startled, one hand pressed to her chest as if the sound had surprised her too.\n\nThe Black Circle stirred in answer, warm for once instead of watchful.\n\nElira lowered her feet into the lake.\n\nHer laugh broke into a gasp.\n\nElira: \"That is cruel.\"\n\nHaru: \"The lake?\"\n\nElira: \"You, for not warning me enough.\"\n\nHaru: \"I suffered visibly.\"\n\nElira: \"You suffer quietly. It is one of your worst habits.\"\n\nThe water moved around our ankles. Sunlight scattered across the surface in broken gold. For once, gold did not look like a classification. It looked like morning. Elira's shoulder touched mine.\n\nElira: \"Tell me something ordinary.\"\n\nHaru: \"Ordinary?\"\n\nElira: \"Something you like. Just because it is yours, not because it serves a plan or reveals ancient doctrine.\"\n\nI thought too long. She bumped my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"That delay was tragic.\"\n\nHaru: \"I like rain on stone.\"\n\nElira's expression gentled.\n\nElira: \"Why?\"\n\nHaru: \"It makes large places sound less empty.\"\n\nShe absorbed that without turning it into pity.\n\nElira: \"I like pear jam.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because?\"\n\nElira: \"Because when I was little, my mother insisted proper Vale breakfasts required restraint, and my father would put too much pear jam on toast when she looked away.\"\n\nHaru: \"A family rebellion.\"\n\nElira: \"A sticky one.\"\n\nWe traded ordinary things until noon.\n\nRain on stone. Pear jam. The smell of old paper. The way Aira said \"interesting\" when she meant \"this is a disaster.\" Leon pretending not to care about compliments. Cassian's habit of smiling before doing something risky. Professor Valen's reading glasses. Maelin's war against nonsense. Lake birds stepping like officials over the mud.\n\nEach small thing made the world wider than catastrophe.\n\nThen a bell rang from the house. Elira's hands quieted. The lake was gone from her eyes.\n\nShe was back under public relays, somewhere between her own choice and the route that had killed her.\n\nI did not stand. I wanted to. Every part of me wanted to find the bell, crush the mechanism, silence every sound shaped like warning.\n\nInstead I stayed beside her.\n\nHaru: \"Lake.\"\n\nHer fingers dug into the dock.\n\nHaru: \"Wood under your hands. Cold water. My shoulder next to yours.\"\n\nElira's breath came too fast.\n\nElira: \"It sounded like the marker bell.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"I am here.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI kept my eyes on hers and my voice low enough that she could still hear the water moving beneath the boards.\n\nElira: \"I am not there.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nShe reached for my hand with a violence that was not aimed at me. I gave it. The Black Circle pressed behind my sternum, cold and old, tasting faintly of ozone and metal. Old systems recognized fear and wanted to contain it, rank it, make it useful.\n\nI kept it quiet.\n\nElira's breathing slowed. When she looked at me again, shame had moved in behind the panic.\n\nI hated shame more than fear.\n\nElira: \"I was fine.\"\n\nHaru: \"You were scared.\"\n\nElira: \"I was laughing ten minutes ago.\"\n\nHaru: \"Both can be true.\"\n\nHer eyes filled.\n\nElira: \"I do not know how to be both.\"\n\nThe ache in her voice had little to do with the bell. Someone had taught her that surviving meant choosing one clean shape and holding it until it broke.\n\nI turned fully toward her on the dock.\n\nHaru: \"Then we practice.\"\n\nElira: \"Practice?\"\n\nHaru: \"Being both.\"\n\nShe stared at me. So I did the only useful thing I could think of.\n\nI picked up a lake bird feather from the dock, held it with grave seriousness, and placed it on my knee.\n\nHaru: \"This is official practice.\"\n\nElira blinked.\n\nHaru: \"You are allowed to be frightened and also judge this feather.\"\n\nHer mouth twitched.\n\nElira: \"That feather is unimpressive.\"\n\nHaru: \"Harsh.\"\n\nElira: \"It lacks structure.\"\n\nHaru: \"It survived a lake bird.\"\n\nElira: \"A low standard.\"\n\nThe laugh came back through tears. It hurt to hear, and it healed nothing by itself, but it made room.\n\nWe dried our feet on the hem of my coat, put our shoes back on, and walked up the path to the house together.\n\nBy the time we stepped onto the porch, the bell had revealed itself as a delivery charm resting beside a wicker basket from Aurelis. We carried it inside to the kitchen counter. Rhea had sent documents sealed for later. Maelin had sent medicine. Aira had sent three more notes, each hidden in increasingly unreasonable places. Leon had sent a water-stabilizing charm with no message. Cassian had sent a red ribbon tied around a packet of spiced chocolate and a note that read:\n\n**For morale. Deny everything.**\n\nElira held the chocolate like evidence.\n\nElira: \"We are very loved.\"\n\nHaru: \"In several incompatible ways.\"\n\nShe leaned against the kitchen counter, wrapped in my shirt and her shawl, and suddenly looked close to tears again.\n\nElira: \"I did not know being loved could be exhausting.\"\n\nHaru: \"I think it is because you keep trying to answer it.\"\n\nElira: \"What should I do instead?\"\n\nI opened my mouth. Then closed it. Correct answers had hurt her yesterday.\n\nSo I chose a true one.\n\nHaru: \"Let some of it sit beside you.\"\n\nShe looked at the basket. Then at me.\n\nElira: \"Like you are learning to do?\"\n\nI accepted that.\n\nHaru: \"Like I am learning to do.\"\n\nThe basket stayed on the counter for an hour because neither of us wanted to decide what to do with kindness.\n\nAfter the Wounded City, even small gifts arrived weighted with records, witness seals, protection clauses, and people bracing against the next loss.\n\nA ribbon. A charm. Medicine. Chocolate. Documents sealed for later.\n\nElira touched Leon's water-stabilizing charm.\n\nThe charm was plain blue glass wrapped in a strip of cloth from an Aurelis training sleeve. Leon had tied the knot badly, in the specific way of someone who had tried very hard and gotten angry at string.\n\nElira: \"He did not write a note.\"\n\nHaru: \"He would consider that excessive.\"\n\nElira turned the charm in her hand.\n\nElira: \"He stayed with Cael when the field tried to make him into bait.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nElira: \"And now he sends this with no words because words would admit he is worried.\"\n\nHaru: \"Also yes.\"\n\nHer thumb moved over the knot.\n\nElira: \"We should thank him.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira will make him unbearable if she knows.\"\n\nElira's eyes warmed.\n\nElira: \"Then we thank him privately.\"\n\nThe house, which had developed opinions about privacy, opened a drawer in the writing desk by half an inch. I looked at it.\n\nHaru: \"Stop helping.\"\n\nElira laughed. The drawer remained open. Inside were blank cards, a fountain pen, and envelopes already marked with old restoration-house seals that meant nothing to the Council and apparently everything to the road wards.\n\nElira picked up a card.\n\nHer hand trembled before the pen touched paper.\n\nThis time it was only fatigue.\n\nShe scowled at the tremor as if it had insulted her family.\n\nHaru: \"I can write.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe dipped the pen.\n\nThe first line came out slightly uneven.\n\n**Leon, your charm is useful. Your knot is terrible. Thank you.**\n\nShe stared at it. Then looked at me.\n\nElira: \"Too much?\"\n\nHaru: \"He will understand the insult as affection.\"\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nShe added:\n\n**Do not tell Aira I said so.**\n\nWe both knew Aira would know within an hour of the envelope leaving the house. Still, Elira's handwriting existed outside testimony now, and Leon would probably stare at the card far too long before pretending he had not.\n\nWe wrote to Aira next.\n\nThat took longer because Aira deserved gratitude and also needed management. Elira began:\n\n**Your packets are intrusive.**\n\nThen paused.\n\nHaru: \"True.\"\n\nElira added:\n\n**They helped.**\n\nHer expression shifted.\n\nElira: \"She will cry.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira?\"\n\nElira: \"Privately. Then she will deny it with footnotes.\"\n\nI imagined Aira constructing a defense of non-crying based on tactical eye moisture.\n\nHaru: \"Likely.\"\n\nElira wrote:\n\n**You are excellent at friendship. This is not permission to become worse.**\n\nWe left space for me.\n\nI did not know what to write.\n\nElira slid the card toward me without speaking.\n\nThe pen felt heavier than it should have.\n\nAira had been the first person at Aurelis to treat my silence as a thing with doors instead of walls. She had made maps around my omissions, jokes around fear, and a public record strong enough to keep the old systems from turning me into only a rank. I wrote:\n\n**Thank you for making room before you knew what needed room.**\n\nThen I stopped. The sentence looked exposed. Elira read it and did not rescue me from it.\n\nShe only touched the edge of the card.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nWe wrote to Rhea in language formal enough to survive being intercepted and human enough to make her sigh.\n\nWe wrote to Maelin to confirm medicine had been taken, food had been eaten, and neither of us had invented a moral crisis around the bedroom. Elira insisted on that line. I objected with dignity. She ignored me with more.\n\nWe did not open the Council documents.\n\nThe red folder sat on the desk like a sealed wound.\n\nAt one point I reached toward it.\n\nElira did not stop me.\n\nThat made stopping myself harder.\n\nMy fingers hovered over the seal.\n\nI could feel the shape of the memoranda inside. Krail's language would be precise. Words like stability, proximity, extra-classification exposure, necessary review. No open cruelty. Nothing as honest as a blade. Just concern arranged until it could justify a locked door.\n\nThe Black Circle disliked locked doors. So did I. That was why I pulled my hand back. Elira watched me.\n\nElira: \"Why did you stop?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because if I read it now, I will spend the afternoon answering a man who wants to make me react.\"\n\nElira's face softened.\n\nElira: \"And what did you want to spend the afternoon doing?\"\n\nI looked at the basket. At the half-written cards. At the window where the lake kept being only a lake.\n\nHaru: \"Learning what people do with afternoons.\"\n\nElira set the pen down.\n\nElira: \"That may be the most alarming thing you have ever said.\"\n\nHaru: \"I assumed there were procedures.\"\n\nElira: \"There are not.\"\n\nHaru: \"None?\"\n\nElira considered.\n\nElira: \"Tea helps.\"\n\nThat was close enough to doctrine. We made more tea. Elira insisted on helping, which meant I had to learn the difference between support and interference for the fourth time that day. She carried the cups one at a time. Slowly. Angrily. Successfully.\n\nWhen she set mine down without spilling, she gave me a look so triumphant that the Black Circle itself seemed wise enough not to comment.\n\nHaru: \"Impressive.\"\n\nElira: \"Do not sound surprised.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am impressed by the cup.\"\n\nElira: \"Cruel.\"\n\nHaru: \"Accurate.\"\n\nShe threw a folded napkin at me.\n\nIt hit my shoulder and fell to the floor with no tactical effect.\n\nElira: \"Devastating.\"\n\nHaru: \"I may not recover.\"\n\nHer smile faltered once before returning, and that was enough for one afternoon.\n\nThe house accepted our letters at the front door.\n\nThere was a narrow brass slot beside the entry table. When Elira slid the envelopes through, the old silver in the wood brightened and the cards vanished into whatever route Professor Valen trusted more than council courier chains.\n\nElira kept one envelope back. Plain. Unsealed. Iven Sahl's name written on it.\n\nHaru: \"Do you want to wait?\"\n\nElira looked down at the card.\n\nElira: \"No. I want to not send a perfect answer.\"\n\nShe opened it again. Inside, she had written:\n\n**Iven, you do not owe me apology for surviving Noctis. Cael's kindness belonged to him. What you do with the part of it he gave you can belong to you. If you ever want to tell the thermal charm story into the public record, Rhea will protect the route. If you do not, that choice is also yours.**\n\nBelow it, after a gap:\n\n**Eat something warm.**\n\nI looked at her. Elira's cheeks colored.\n\nElira: \"Aira has contaminated me.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good.\"\n\nShe sealed the letter before she could edit it into distance.\n\nThe house took that one more gently than the rest.\n\n## Interlude: Elira's Unsent Answer\n\nHaru went to rinse the cups, giving her the quiet of an ordinary chore instead of hovering near the desk.\n\nElira let him go. Letting someone leave a room without bracing for the worst was harder than asking him to stay.\n\nThe restoration house settled around her in soft old creaks: cedar beams adjusting to lake wind, kettle cooling, ward lines humming beneath the floor. No council rail. No witness pool. No public record waiting to turn breath into precedent.\n\nOnly the brass mail slot beside the door. It brightened again.\n\nElira looked toward the kitchen.\n\nHaru had his back to her, sleeves rolled to his elbows, washing a cup with the focused care of a boy who could unmake a battlefield and still looked faintly uncertain about soap. The slot opened.\n\nA folded crystal sheet slid onto the entry table.\n\nIt bore no hostile flare. No Noctis seal. No Dominion hook.\n\nThat almost made it worse.\n\nClean Council stationery could do a great deal of harm while looking as if it had merely asked for clarification. Elira picked it up.\n\n**SUPPLEMENTAL WELFARE CONTEXT FORM**\n\n**RECOVERY PROXIMITY REVIEW**\n\n**OPTIONAL STUDENT RESPONSE**\n\nOptional, in council language, often meant someone wanted refusal to look suspicious later.\n\nShe read the first question.\n\n**Does Haru Aerlen's presence materially reduce your distress symptoms?**\n\nElira's fingers tightened around the page.\n\nThere it was: a corridor built one polished tile at a time, long before anyone said the word containment aloud.\n\nIf she answered yes, someone could call him necessary.\n\nIf she answered no, someone could call him irrelevant.\n\nIf she refused, someone could call the refusal evidence of dependency hidden by romance, grief, or Black Circle influence.\n\nShe could almost hear Krail's voice inside the grammar, though his name appeared nowhere. The trap was too tidy to need a signature.\n\nHaru turned from the sink.\n\nHaru: \"Elira?\"\n\nShe folded the sheet once.\n\nElira: \"A form.\"\n\nHis hands stopped moving.\n\nAnyone else would have missed it. She did not.\n\nThe pause behind his eyes. The instant calculation of threat routes, response costs, whether the correct kindness was to step closer or give her the page's full space. He chose space. Good.\n\nShe did not want distance from him. She only wanted one answer that did not begin with his fear.\n\nElira: \"I am going to read it first.\"\n\nHaru set the cup down.\n\nHaru: \"All right.\"\n\nHe did not ask to see it. That helped.\n\nIt did not solve the anger.\n\nElira took the sheet to the small writing desk by the western window. The lake beyond the glass looked too peaceful for bureaucracy. That seemed unfair to the lake.\n\nThe form had seven questions. All gentle. All wrong.\n\n**Does proximity to the Black Circle bearer produce measurable channel stability?**\n\nElira crossed out proximity. Wrote: **Chosen company.**\n\nThen crossed out Black Circle bearer. Wrote: **Haru Aerlen.** The sentence became messier. Better.\n\n**Does romantic attachment alter your willingness to provide testimony?**\n\nElira stared at that one longer.\n\nShe thought of the Wounded City. Cael's wrist blackened by a return claim. Her own hand over his seal. Haru's face when she chose the route Dominion Severance turned into a blade.\n\nLove had not blunted her willingness to testify. It had only made certain men furious that her testimony still belonged to her. She wrote:\n\n**Attachment does not reduce agency. It gives bad readers another excuse to doubt it.**\n\nThe pen trembled. She hated that.\n\nThen she decided trembling could stay.\n\nMira would have kept it.\n\nMira would have written a note beside the line: **HAND SHOOK HERE. STILL ANSWERED.** Elira added it herself.\n\nThe page looked less elegant immediately. Good.\n\nElegance had been trained into her as a survival tool until it could disguise pain as competence. Mira had taught her that ugly records sometimes protected people better because they proved a living hand had made them under pressure.\n\nQuestion five asked whether Haru's continued presence might discourage independent recovery behaviors. Elira laughed once. It came out sharp.\n\nHaru did not move from the kitchen. Still giving space. Still listening. She wrote:\n\n**Independent recovery behavior: answering this without asking him to rescue me from the insult.**\n\nThen, below it:\n\n**I choose his presence when I choose it. I choose solitude when I choose it. Your form currently treats both choices as symptoms unless they support your preferred conclusion. Correct the instrument before interpreting the patient.**\n\nThat was too Halden. She left it.\n\nQuestion seven was the worst.\n\n**Would you consent to a private medical-context interview to distinguish recovery need from relational preference?**\n\nPrivate. Medical-context. Distinguish.\n\nThe words put on clean gloves before reaching for her throat.\n\nElira did not cross anything out this time.\n\nShe wrote beneath the question in large, uneven script:\n\n**No private interview. No separation framed as clarity. Recovery need and relational preference are not opposing categories. If you require testimony, ask in open witness conditions after healer clearance. If you require control, stop pretending this is care.**\n\nThe house hummed around her, whether old cedar settling or old wards agreeing.\n\nShe rejected both Lady Elira Vale and Yellow Circle primary witness and signed the name she chose:\n\n**Elira Vale. Student. Alive. Irritated.**\n\nThat last word was not proper. She underlined it.\n\nThen she made a copy by hand before sending the crystal sheet through the brass slot, because Aira had contaminated everyone and because Mira would ask.\n\nWhen the house took the form, Elira sat very still. She had expected relief.\n\nInstead she felt tired and cleanly furious.\n\nThat was better than frightened.\n\nIn the kitchen, Haru said nothing.\n\nElira looked at his reflection in the window glass.\n\nHe was watching the cup in his hands as if it contained the discipline of every star.\n\nShe knew he wanted to ask whether she was all right.\n\nShe also knew he had learned enough not to make her answer before she chose the shape of it.\n\nElira folded the handwritten copy and placed it beside the outgoing letters.\n\nElira: \"Krail, probably.\"\n\nHaru's reflection changed.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nHe stopped before turning fully.\n\nElira: \"That was my answer.\"\n\nFor one long second, the room held both of them without choosing a center. Then Haru nodded.\n\nHaru: \"Will you show me when you want to?\"\n\nElira's anger loosened by one thread.\n\nElira: \"When I want to.\"\n\nHer hand settled flat against the edge of the writing desk.\n\nShe stood, slower than she wanted, and returned to the door.\n\nThe Iven letter was gone. The Council form was gone. The copy remained, proof that a living hand had answered without asking anyone to carry the pen:\n\n**I was not carried through that part. I walked.**\n\n---\n\nAfterward, Elira stood by the door with her hand against the wood.\n\nElira: \"Cael should be here.\"\n\nThere were many wrong answers.\n\nThe world had taught me most of them.\n\nHe is in the record. He chose. He saved you. He is honored. All true. All insufficient.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI kept my hands still at my sides so she would not have to brace against a rescue.\n\nHer hand curled into a fist.\n\nElira: \"I am glad I am alive.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI let the truth stand without softening it.\n\nElira: \"And I hate that his death is in the room whenever I say it.\"\n\nThe old cold behind my sternum answered.\n\nThis time I did not let it become a solution. I stood beside her.\n\nHaru: \"Then we make room for both.\"\n\nElira's eyes closed.\n\nElira: \"Both again.\"\n\nHaru: \"Official practice.\"\n\nShe laughed once through the ache.\n\nElira: \"The feather was unimpressive.\"\n\nHaru: \"It served the lesson.\"\n\nElira leaned her forehead against the door. I waited.\n\nEventually she turned and rested against me instead.\n\nI put my arms around her after she did, not before.\n\nThe house did not glow. The wards did not sing. No witness crystal recorded the moment, and for once, none needed to.\n\nEvening came blue and quiet.\n\nWe ate soup by the window. Elira fell asleep halfway through a sentence and woke angry about it. I did not laugh. That was wise. I did warm the soup again. That was wiser.\n\nAfter dinner, she asked me to braid her hair.\n\nI had never braided anything in my life. She discovered this quickly.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes?\"\n\nElira: \"Are you tying a knot?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"That pause says yes.\"\n\nI carefully freed the strand.\n\nElira sat on the rug between my knees, shoulders shaking with quiet laughter as I failed with scholarly dedication. The fire burned low. The lake reflected moonlight through the window. Her pendant rested against her chest, dark and watchful, but not cold.\n\nWhen I finally made something that resembled a braid if judged kindly from a distance, Elira touched it.\n\nElira: \"Terrible.\"\n\nHaru: \"I'll improve.\"\n\nElira leaned back against my knees.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nThe softness in that word pulled the room close around us, warm and unguarded.\n\nShe tilted her head back to look at me.\n\nElira: \"Kiss me?\"\n\nThe question was gentle.\n\nIt still made the Black Circle go silent. I bent down slowly.\n\nOur first kiss beneath the silver-leaf tree had been quiet and almost impossible, full of the frightening joy of being chosen without being claimed. This one carried no veil, no vow spoken in shock, and no hidden rank between us.\n\nJust Elira choosing, after death had touched her and politics had named her evidence, to ask for tenderness in a room by a lake. I kissed her softly.\n\nShe reached up and held my face with both hands. The braid fell apart. Neither of us cared.\n\nLater, when we lay beside each other under the same blanket, Elira's head rested against my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"Today felt almost normal.\"\n\nHaru: \"Parts of it.\"\n\nElira: \"I liked the parts.\"\n\nHaru: \"So did I.\"\n\nThe lake moved outside.\n\nThe world waited beyond the trees with hearings, memoranda, Noctis fractures, and questions no one had earned the right to ask.\n\nBut inside the quiet place, Elira's breathing evened.\n\nThis time I slept too.\n\n---\n\nThe third day began with Aira calling at breakfast and ruining the word immediately.\n\nThe mirror brightened while Elira was spreading pear jam with the careful seriousness of a person committing family treason.\n\nAira's face appeared upside down.\n\nAira: \"Good morning, medically sanctioned honeymoon.\"\n\nElira choked.\n\nI spilled tea on my sleeve.\n\nLeon, somewhere behind Aira, said, \"You promised not to open with that.\"\n\nAira rotated the mirror image upright.\n\nAira: \"I promised to consider not opening with that.\"\n\nRhea's voice cut in from out of frame.\n\nRhea: \"This is an official welfare check.\"\n\nCassian: \"With honeymoon oversight.\"\n\nRhea: \"Veyr.\"\n\nCassian: \"Withdrawn.\"\n\nElira covered her face with one hand.\n\nElira: \"I regret having friends.\"\n\nAira: \"No, you don't. You regret that your friends are accurate.\"\n\nI recovered the cup before it dripped onto the floor.\n\nHaru: \"It is recovery leave.\"\n\nAira looked directly at me through the mirror.\n\nAira: \"Haru, you are alone with your official girlfriend in a lake house, under orders to rest, eat, sleep near each other, and stop treating romance like an emergency protocol. I am not saying the Council invented a honeymoon. I am saying bureaucracy tripped and fell into one.\"\n\nElira's ears turned pink. The sight did something reckless to my pulse.\n\nProfessor Valen appeared beside Rhea, holding a file.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Aira's terminology is not medically recognized.\"\n\nAira: \"Emotionally recognized.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Unfortunately.\"\n\nRhea cleared her throat.\n\nRhea: \"Actual update. Noctis has filed two objections to the recovery order. Both were rejected. Senna Voss has requested Elira's medical testimony be deferred until Maelin clears it. Councilor Krail is attempting to frame proximity as dependency.\"\n\nElira lowered her hand. The blush faded.\n\nHaru: \"Of course he is.\"\n\nRhea's gaze shifted to Elira.\n\nRhea: \"Your corrected welfare form reached my office before his summary did.\"\n\nAira leaned into view.\n\nAira: \"For the record, 'Alive. Irritated.' has entered my permanent vocabulary.\"\n\nElira's chin lifted by a fraction.\n\nElira: \"It was medically accurate.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"It was politically useful because it made the instrument visible.\"\n\nRhea: \"Exactly. Krail now has to argue against your conditions instead of pretending he only asked neutral questions.\"\n\nI looked at Elira.\n\nShe did not look at me first.\n\nShe looked at the dimmed mirror edge, where her own reflection sat beside the Council seal without being absorbed by it. Good.\n\nRhea: \"He will find less traction if you give him no public instability today.\"\n\nAira: \"Translation: please do not darken the sky unless someone earns it.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will try.\"\n\nLeon leaned into view.\n\nLeon: \"Also, the water charm is holding?\"\n\nElira smiled.\n\nElira: \"Yes. Thank you.\"\n\nLeon nodded too quickly and stepped back out of frame.\n\nCassian: \"He practiced asking that normally.\"\n\nLeon: \"I did not.\"\n\nAira: \"He did.\"\n\nThe mirror call ended with Rhea ordering us to answer the evening check-in and Aira loudly advising us to \"do something emotionally irresponsible but medically moderate.\"\n\nFor a few seconds after the mirror dimmed, neither of us spoke.\n\nThen Elira set down the jam knife.\n\nElira: \"I want to go into town.\"\n\nEvery security map in my head unfolded at once. Elira watched it happen.\n\nElira: \"Small rebellion.\"\n\nHaru: \"The town is outside the perimeter.\"\n\nElira: \"The first market street is inside the outer ward Professor Valen inspected.\"\n\nHaru: \"There will be people.\"\n\nElira: \"That is usually what towns contain.\"\n\nI looked at the window.\n\nLake Velis village sat half a mile down the road, a cluster of slate roofs, fishing boats, and shop awnings bright against the shore. Ordinary people lived there. Ordinary people now knew the Black Circle was not a myth, because the whole world knew. Ordinary people would look. At me. At her.\n\nAt the girl who had died and returned.\n\nAt the boy who had nearly become an ending.\n\nElira reached across the table and touched my wrist.\n\nElira: \"I do not want our first days away from Aurelis to teach us that safety means hiding from every face.\"\n\nI knew she was right.\n\nI hated that she was right while still weak enough that climbing the stairs cost her breath.\n\nHaru: \"If you get tired, we leave.\"\n\nElira: \"Agreed.\"\n\nHaru: \"If the crowd presses, we leave.\"\n\nElira: \"I promise.\"\n\nHaru: \"If any relay-\"\n\nElira squeezed my wrist.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI stopped. She smiled.\n\nElira: \"Ask me what I want to buy.\"\n\nThe absurdity of the question took the edge off my warning before I could argue.\n\nHaru: \"What do you want to buy?\"\n\nElira: \"A ribbon.\"\n\nHaru: \"For your hair?\"\n\nElira looked toward the bedroom, where my failed braid from the night before had been mercifully undone.\n\nElira: \"For your continued education.\"\n\nThe decision to go into town took forty minutes to become an actual coat.\n\nThat was mostly my fault.\n\nElira sat on the edge of the bed while I arranged possible outer layers on the chair with the seriousness of a combat deployment. Pale shawl. Dark shawl. Heavier cloak. Lighter cloak. The blue ribbon from Aira's packet. A small healing charm Maelin had labeled **ONLY IF DIZZY, NOT IF DRAMATIC**.\n\nElira watched me for almost a full minute.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes?\"\n\nElira: \"You are choosing fabric like it will negotiate with assassins.\"\n\nI looked at the cloaks.\n\nHaru: \"Some fabrics conceal ward seams better.\"\n\nElira's mouth twitched.\n\nElira: \"That was not a denial.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nShe reached for the pale shawl.\n\nI handed it to her before remembering that handing it too quickly was also a kind of hovering. My arm stopped halfway.\n\nElira stared at the shawl suspended between us.\n\nElira: \"Are we having a crisis about fabric?\"\n\nHaru: \"Possibly.\"\n\nElira took it from me.\n\nElira: \"I choose this one.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because it is lighter?\"\n\nElira wrapped it around her shoulders.\n\nElira: \"Because I like it.\"\n\nThe answer defeated six security considerations at once. I admired it. Reluctantly.\n\nShe pinned her hair back loosely with the blue ribbon, leaving a few strands free around her face to catch the morning light. She looked less like the Yellow Circle prodigy the world had watched die and more like a girl leaving a house on an ordinary errand.\n\nThen she saw herself in the small mirror.\n\nHer hand went to the pendant, and the air between us sobered.\n\nElira: \"It shows.\"\n\nHaru: \"The pendant?\"\n\nElira: \"The fear.\"\n\nI stepped closer, then stopped where she could see the choice.\n\nHaru: \"What do you want people to see?\"\n\nShe looked at her reflection for a long time.\n\nElira: \"Not that I am untouched.\"\n\nHer voice stayed steady, hard-earned.\n\nElira: \"I do not want them thinking I am grateful enough to behave, or that dying made me softer and easier to turn into a symbol.\"\n\nHer fingers lowered from the pendant.\n\nElira: \"I want them to see I am here. And that I chose the ribbon myself.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then that is what we carry.\"\n\nShe turned.\n\nElira: \"Together?\"\n\nHaru: \"Together.\"\n\nThe restoration house warmed beneath our feet. A second black ribbon edged in silver slipped from the desk drawer and landed on the floor between us. Elira looked down.\n\nElira: \"Opinionated house.\"\n\nHaru: \"Interfering house.\"\n\nThe old silver in the floor did not react, which I chose to interpret as guilt.\n\nElira picked up the ribbon.\n\nElira: \"We will buy one anyway.\"\n\nHaru: \"To avoid encouraging it?\"\n\nElira: \"To choose twice.\"\n\nSo we left: Elira in her pale shawl and blue ribbon, the dark pendant visible at her throat because hiding it would have given fear too much room; me in a dark coat with a calm I did not expect anyone who knew me to believe; the house's spare ribbon tucked in Elira's pocket like a small argument waiting for later.\n\nThe road to Lake Velis village ran between cedar trees and low stone walls. Wildflowers grew in gaps where frost had cracked the old mortar. Fishing nets hung from posts near the shore, drying in the wind. A boy about Leon's age repaired a boat rope and pretended not to stare until Elira waved.\n\nHe nearly dropped the rope. Elira smiled.\n\nElira: \"Too much?\"\n\nHaru: \"For him?\"\n\nElira: \"For me.\"\n\nI considered lying. She deserved better.\n\nHaru: \"Some.\"\n\nElira gave a single, brief nod.\n\nElira: \"Do not ask if we should go back.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was arranging the words.\"\n\nElira: \"Unarrange them.\"\n\nI kept them to myself.\n\nAt the outer ward marker, a thin line of silver crossed the road. It did not flare for rank. It did not test my Circle. It only warmed as Elira stepped over it, then again as I followed.\n\n**VOLUNTARY EXIT.**\n\nThe impression settled into my mind like a hand closing gently around a record. Elira paused.\n\nElira: \"It records leaving?\"\n\nHaru: \"Voluntary.\"\n\nShe breathed out.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nThen, with a small defiance I loved before I understood it, she stepped backward over the line and forward again. The ward warmed twice.\n\n**VOLUNTARY RETURN. VOLUNTARY EXIT.**\n\nElira's smile sharpened.\n\nElira: \"I like that.\"\n\nHaru: \"The ward?\"\n\nElira: \"The correction.\"\n\nShe stepped into the village road. I followed, feeling the old house behind us accept that protection could include departure.\n\nThe village did look.\n\nThere was no avoiding that.\n\nPeople paused with baskets in hand. A child tugged at her mother's sleeve. An old fisherman removed his cap when Elira passed, then seemed embarrassed by the ceremony and put it back on badly. Two shopkeepers whispered my name without meaning for me to hear. Black Circle.\n\nThe words followed at a distance, no longer myth or secret and still far from understood.\n\nElira's hand tightened around mine.\n\nI almost apologized for the attention.\n\nThen I realized she was holding me steady too.\n\nA ribbon seller near the fountain bowed so deeply that Elira's face changed.\n\nElira: \"Please don't.\"\n\nThe woman straightened at once.\n\nRibbon Seller: \"Sorry, Lady Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"Elira is fine.\"\n\nThe woman's eyes flicked to me, then back to her.\n\nRibbon Seller: \"Elira, then.\"\n\nThat small correction did more than the bow. Elira breathed easier.\n\nShe chose a blue ribbon first, then a gold one, then after a moment a narrow black ribbon edged in silver thread.\n\nThe seller hesitated before touching it.\n\nI felt the market notice. Elira noticed too.\n\nShe picked up the black ribbon herself and held it against my sleeve.\n\nElira: \"This one.\"\n\nMy heart did something undisciplined.\n\nHaru: \"For your hair?\"\n\nElira: \"For yours.\"\n\nI stared at her. She looked entirely serious.\n\nHaru: \"My hair is not long enough for a ribbon.\"\n\nElira: \"Then I will improvise badly and you will be gracious.\"\n\nThe ribbon seller made a small sound that might have been a laugh before fear caught it. Elira smiled at her.\n\nElira: \"He is very gracious under ribbon-based adversity.\"\n\nThe seller relaxed enough to wrap the ribbons with ordinary care.\n\nAs we left, the child who had been staring from beside the fountain stepped forward.\n\nChild: \"Are you the Black Circle?\"\n\nHer mother went pale.\n\nMother: \"Lysa.\"\n\nI crouched before panic could spread.\n\nThat made several people tense.\n\nSo I kept my hands visible.\n\nHaru: \"My name is Haru.\"\n\nThe child considered this.\n\nLysa: \"But are you?\"\n\nElira's hand rested on my shoulder, steady and unhurried.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI kept my eyes level with hers without hiding behind a softer rank.\n\nThe word entered the market like a stone into water. No disguise. No old Green lie to hide behind.\n\nLysa frowned.\n\nLysa: \"You don't look like the scary drawings.\"\n\nAira would have loved her.\n\nHaru: \"That is probably good.\"\n\nLysa nodded.\n\nLysa: \"My brother says Black Circle means you can break mountains.\"\n\nHaru: \"Breaking mountains causes problems for people who live near mountains.\"\n\nThe child thought that over with grave respect.\n\nLysa: \"So you don't?\"\n\nHaru: \"I try not to break things that do not need breaking.\"\n\nElira's fingers moved once against my shoulder.\n\nThe mother bowed her head, not deeply this time.\n\nMother: \"Thank you.\"\n\nI did not know for which part. Maybe neither did she.\n\nThe market did not return to normal after that. It tried.\n\nThat made it kinder than a council chamber.\n\nThe ribbon seller began wrapping the black ribbon again because her hands needed something to do. The old fisherman bought apples he clearly did not need. Lysa's mother took her daughter's shoulders and whispered something too soft for ordinary hearing, so I made myself not listen with anything beyond it. Elira noticed.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nHaru: \"For what?\"\n\nElira: \"Not hearing everything just because you can.\"\n\nThe compliment entered a place in me I had not known needed it.\n\nHaru: \"I am learning.\"\n\nElira's smile was tired and proud.\n\nThen a man near the baker's stall bowed shallowly, with the awkward precision of someone who did not know what else to do.\n\nMan: \"Lady Vale. Aerlen.\"\n\nHe said my name like a stone he was not sure he was allowed to hold. Elira turned toward him.\n\nElira: \"Good morning.\"\n\nHe swallowed.\n\nMan: \"My daughter is Green Circle. She was at the academy gate two days ago.\"\n\nMira's sign flashed in my memory.\n\n**RECOVERY IS NOT ABSENCE**\n\nThe man looked at me, then immediately away.\n\nMan: \"She said you raised your hand to the Noctis students too.\"\n\nElira's expression softened.\n\nElira: \"I did.\"\n\nMan: \"She cried about it.\"\n\nHe looked mortified to have said that aloud.\n\nElira did not save him from the feeling by pretending it was nothing.\n\nElira: \"I am glad she saw them.\"\n\nThe man's eyes filled. He bowed again, smaller.\n\nMan: \"Thank you.\"\n\nI only caught the edges of what that gratitude meant to him, and left the rest undisturbed.\n\nWe bought bread from his stall because leaving without doing so would have made the conversation too solemn. Elira chose two rolls and one small pastry shaped like a crescent moon. The baker added a second pastry without charging. I opened my mouth.\n\nElira stepped lightly on my foot, just enough to stop me.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nThe baker looked relieved by the ordinary words.\n\nElira waited until we were three stalls away.\n\nElira: \"You were about to argue about pastry.\"\n\nHaru: \"Unpaid goods create obligation.\"\n\nElira: \"Sometimes they create breakfast.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is almost noon.\"\n\nElira: \"Then emergency breakfast.\"\n\nShe tore one pastry in half and offered me the larger piece. I looked at it.\n\nHaru: \"Hierarchy matters?\"\n\nElira: \"Aira has infected us both.\"\n\nWe ate pastry beside the village fountain.\n\nThe fountain was small and practical, nothing like Aurelis' repaired courtyard fountain or the Wounded City markers. Water spilled from a stone fish into a round basin where children had thrown copper coins. No hidden black ring. No public relay. No old system waiting to wake under pressure. Just water.\n\nElira watched it with such focus that I worried until she said:\n\nElira: \"I used to want a fountain in the Vale winter garden.\"\n\nHaru: \"Why?\"\n\nElira: \"Because I thought the academy fountains were beautiful when I first visited Aurelis. I was nine. Everything gold looked like a promise then.\"\n\nHer mouth curved without quite smiling.\n\nElira: \"My mother said indoor fountains were impractical. My father said impractical things were useful if they made a house kinder. They compromised on a birdbath.\"\n\nHaru: \"Did birds use it?\"\n\nElira: \"One extremely arrogant sparrow.\"\n\nHaru: \"A successful compromise.\"\n\nShe laughed.\n\nThe sound moved through the market differently than the word Black Circle had.\n\nPeople heard it and tried not to stare. Some failed. Elira let them fail.\n\nThen the fountain water rippled.\n\nMy hand moved before thought, reaching for her instead of a ward. She caught my fingers.\n\nThe ripple had been a coin dropping from Lysa's hand. The child froze.\n\nSo did half the square.\n\nMy Black Circle stayed quiet. My face, less so. Elira squeezed my hand.\n\nHaru: \"Sorry.\"\n\nLysa looked at me with blunt horror.\n\nLysa: \"Did I almost make you break a mountain?\"\n\nThe square's false wind dropped away.\n\nElira pressed her lips together.\n\nI could feel the laugh in her hand before I heard it.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nLysa: \"Oh.\"\n\nHaru: \"Maybe a cup.\"\n\nElira turned her face away. Her shoulders shook.\n\nThe mother looked as if she had aged several years and then been handed them back. Lysa considered the answer.\n\nLysa: \"Cups are replaceable.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is what I have heard.\"\n\nThe square exhaled in ragged pieces. The baker laughed first. The ribbon seller followed.\n\nThen the old fisherman, who had bought apples for no reason, said:\n\nOld Fisherman: \"Depends on the cup.\"\n\nThat broke the rest of the tension cleanly.\n\nThe market resumed around us, not normal, not careless, but less terrified of the fact that I had answered a child and survived being known. Elira leaned closer.\n\nElira: \"You made a joke.\"\n\nHaru: \"A small one.\"\n\nElira: \"Publicly.\"\n\nHaru: \"Under pressure.\"\n\nElira: \"Aira will document this as growth.\"\n\nHaru: \"Please do not tell her.\"\n\nElira's smile turned dangerous.\n\nElira: \"I am absolutely telling her.\"\n\nWe walked one more row of stalls because she wanted dried pears and because I wanted to prove to myself that leaving immediately after a flinch was not the only kind of safety. She chose a small packet of tea for Maelin, a wooden bookmark for Professor Valen, and a dark blue thread spool for no reason she would admit.\n\nAt the edge of the market, a notice crystal flickered in the window of the post office. My name appeared. Not fully.\n\nOnly in the headline passing through the public relay:\n\n**COUNCIL MINORITY QUESTIONS BLACK CIRCLE PROXIMITY DURING RECOVERY LEAVE**\n\nElira saw it before I could step between her and the glass. Her expression closed.\n\nThe day lost some of its warmth.\n\nHaru: \"We can go.\"\n\nElira stared at the notice until the words cycled away.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nElira: \"I am not going to let Krail become the last thing I saw in town.\"\n\nShe turned deliberately to the flower stall next door.\n\nA woman there was arranging white lake flowers like the ones Professor Valen had left in the restoration house. Elira bought three stems and held them in both hands.\n\nElira: \"There.\"\n\nHaru: \"There?\"\n\nElira looked back at the post office window, where the headline had returned.\n\nElira: \"Now he is beside flowers.\"\n\nShe had not erased Krail's headline. She had simply refused to let it keep the whole window.\n\nI carried the parcels so her hands could keep the stems.\n\n## The Fish Charm\n\nThe last stall before the road back to the house belonged to a woodcarver who had packed everything except one small fish.\n\nIt hung from a blue cord near the corner of the awning, one fin shorter than the other and a shallow knife mark crossing its painted scales. No magic moved inside it. No ward answered when I touched the cord. Only wood shaped by a patient hand that had slipped once and kept going.\n\nBehind us, the post office window still caught the sun, Krail's headline cycling quietly behind the glass.\n\nThe carver noticed me looking.\n\nWoodcarver: \"Travel charm. Lake families give them to people who need help remembering the way home.\"\n\nElira shifted the flowers to her other arm so she could see it properly.\n\nElira: \"You stopped walking for it.\"\n\nHaru: \"I noticed it.\"\n\nElira: \"Those are different sentences.\"\n\nThey were. Before Aurelis, I would have chosen the safer one and kept moving. Before Krail began writing separation into neutral-sounding forms, I might not have felt the sudden, quiet need to own a compass made of wood and string.\n\nI bought the fish.\n\nThe carver accepted my coin without bowing, thanking the Black Circle, or asking whether mountains were safe. She shortened the cord because I asked. The exchange lasted less than a minute and changed no law in Aurelis, yet my thumb stayed over the small notch in its wooden fin as if memorizing a landmark.\n\nOn the path home, Elira hooked one finger through the blue cord where it emerged from my closed hand.\n\nElira: \"Where will you keep it?\"\n\nI looked toward the restoration house, its warm lamps already lit between the cedars while the ridge beyond the lake darkened toward evening.\n\nHaru: \"Somewhere I can find it if someone tries to lose the road.\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened gently around the cord before letting it go.\n\nOn the walk back, she grew tired before she admitted it.\n\nI noticed the change in her steps. The way her hand became heavier in mine. The way her smile stayed in place by discipline instead of ease.\n\nHaru: \"We stop for a minute.\"\n\nElira sighed.\n\nElira: \"You are correct.\"\n\nHaru: \"You sound offended.\"\n\nElira: \"I am offended that my body agrees with you.\"\n\nWe took the lake path rather than the road. Halfway home, she stopped beneath cedar shade and leaned against me with trust instead of collapse.\n\nI held her with one arm around her shoulders.\n\nElira: \"The child asked you directly.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe water beyond the reeds stayed flat and gray under the cooling sky.\n\nElira: \"You answered.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is public now.\"\n\nElira: \"That is not the same as being honest without armor.\"\n\nThe cedar branches moved above us.\n\nI looked at the path ahead, then down at her.\n\nHaru: \"I was afraid.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not of her.\"\n\nElira: \"I know that too.\"\n\nHaru: \"Of how much easier it was when no one knew enough to ask.\"\n\nElira leaned her forehead against my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"Secrets can be lonely even when they protect you.\"\n\nI thought of the Moonlit Archive. Her face lit by black and silver. Her hand reaching for mine when anyone else would have stepped back.\n\nHaru: \"You made mine less lonely.\"\n\nShe looked up.\n\nElira: \"Then let me keep doing it.\"\n\nWe stood there until her strength returned.\n\nBack at the house, Elira set the house's spare ribbon on the entry table and insisted on tying the market's black ribbon around my wrist because my hair had failed the assignment. Her fingers were careful with the knot. The silver edge caught the light like a quiet ring.\n\nElira: \"There.\"\n\nHaru: \"Am I improved?\"\n\nElira studied me with theatrical severity.\n\nElira: \"Moderately.\"\n\nI touched the ribbon.\n\nHaru: \"Cruel.\"\n\nElira: \"Accurate.\"\n\nThe evening check-in found us on the sitting room floor surrounded by ribbons, soup bowls, and the remains of spiced chocolate.\n\nAira looked through the mirror and went silent. That was alarming.\n\nElira: \"Aira?\"\n\nAira: \"Sorry. I was trying very hard not to say honeymoon again.\"\n\nLeon groaned offscreen.\n\nCassian: \"Growth.\"\n\nRhea's gaze moved to the ribbon on my wrist.\n\nRhea: \"Public contact?\"\n\nHaru: \"Lake Velis village. No relay disturbance. No hostile contact. One child asked directly about the Black Circle.\"\n\nRhea straightened.\n\nRhea: \"And?\"\n\nElira answered before I could.\n\nElira: \"He gave his name first.\"\n\nProfessor Valen, behind Rhea, smiled faintly.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Good.\"\n\nShe meant it as a teacher, not a councilor.\n\nThe mirror dimmed after reports, medicine reminders, and one final Aira warning not to let Haru turn watchfulness into interior decorating. Night settled.\n\nElira sat beside me on the sofa, legs tucked beneath her, head against my shoulder. The wooden fish charm rested on the side table beside her flowers, its blue cord coiled neatly around one fin. When our hands met, the black ribbon on my wrist brushed the pendant at her throat, black beside black by choice rather than threat or rank.\n\nElira: \"Today felt like a promise.\"\n\nHaru: \"Which part?\"\n\nElira: \"The market. The child. The ribbon. Coming back when I was tired instead of proving I could keep going.\"\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nElira: \"All of it.\"\n\nI turned my hand so the ribbon caught between our palms.\n\nHaru: \"Then we keep it.\"\n\nElira kissed the inside of my wrist, just above the knot.\n\nThe Black Circle went utterly still.\n\nNot because it had vanished.\n\nBecause for once, power had nothing to add.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-22-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep23","label":"Episode 17","number":"17","file":"episode-23-cover.webp","fallback":"The Clause That Smiled","eyebrow":"Dominion Clause","recap":"","accent":["#f8fafc","#111111","#d4af37"],"displayLabel":"Episode 17","displayNumber":"17","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"Fear Has a Shape","body":"The fourth day began gently enough that I distrusted it: sun on the lake, steam from tea, Elira reading one of Professor Valen's old books with a blanket over her knees and my shirt still claimed as enemy property.\n\nThe black ribbon stayed around my wrist because she had tied it there the night before and said it improved me.\n\nI had not argued. Some battles were worth losing.\n\nOutside, the restoration house garden smelled of wet cedar and lake grass. The storm had not arrived yet, but the air had weight in it. Clouds gathered beyond the far ridge like a thought the sky had not finished speaking. Elira noticed me looking.\n\nElira: \"Weather is allowed.\"\n\nHaru: \"I did not say it was not.\"\n\nElira turned a page.\n\nElira: \"Your shoulders did.\"\n\nI relaxed them with deliberate obedience. She watched.\n\nElira: \"Better.\"\n\nHaru: \"You are becoming strict.\"\n\nElira: \"I nearly died. I am allowed hobbies.\"\n\nThe sentence landed between us unevenly, true enough to hurt without ruining the morning.\n\nWe had learned over the last days that certain truths could enter a room without breaking it, so long as we did not rush to carry them out again.\n\nElira looked down at the book.\n\nElira: \"That was too sharp.\"\n\nHaru: \"It was true.\"\n\nElira: \"Truth can still cut.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI let the quiet sit instead of rushing to patch over it.\n\nShe closed the book on one finger to keep her place.\n\nElira: \"I do not want everyone to flinch every time I mention it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then I will practice not flinching.\"\n\nElira studied me.\n\nElira: \"Not by freezing.\"\n\nI had been about to do exactly that.\n\nHaru: \"You are becoming alarmingly good at identifying my methods.\"\n\nElira: \"They are few and dramatic.\"\n\nThat made me smile before I could stop it. She looked pleased.\n\nThe morning continued. That was its own kind of miracle.\n\nMaelin's check-in came at ten. Elira reported sleep, food, one mild dizzy spell, no active interference, two nightmares she had remembered, and one she had not. Maelin listened without making the nightmares larger than the rest.\n\nMaelin: \"Storm moving east tonight.\"\n\nI looked up. Elira did too.\n\nMaelin saw both reactions through the mirror.\n\nMaelin: \"Weather wards are ordinary. The house has handled worse storms than student romance.\"\n\nAira, somewhere behind the mirror, made a strangled sound.\n\nRhea: \"Solen.\"\n\nAira: \"I said nothing.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You inhaled irresponsibly.\"\n\nElira covered her mouth.\n\nMaelin looked directly at her.\n\nMaelin: \"Laughing is allowed. So is being afraid of weather after trauma. Report either if it becomes inconvenient.\"\n\nElira's hand rested on the pendant.\n\nElira: \"What counts as inconvenient?\"\n\nMaelin: \"If fear stops you from doing something necessary, or if pride stops you from requesting help before it becomes necessary.\"\n\nRhea's gaze moved to me.\n\nRhea: \"That applies to both of you.\"\n\nHaru: \"Understood.\"\n\nAira leaned into the mirror, now apparently forgiven by the court.\n\nAira: \"I am sending storm snacks.\"\n\nLeon: \"You cannot classify snacks by weather.\"\n\nAira: \"Watch me.\"\n\nThe call ended with a list of practical instructions and one very serious reminder from Maelin that windows were not hostile actors unless they began filing legal claims.\n\nElira laughed after the mirror dimmed. Then she did not.\n\nHer fingers were still on the pendant.\n\nHaru: \"Do you want to talk about it?\"\n\nElira looked toward the bright windows.\n\nElira: \"Not yet.\"\n\nYet was progress sharp enough to hurt.\n\nWe spent the afternoon doing ordinary things with too much attention.\n\nElira sorted the letters we had received into piles: answer soon, answer later, burn if Aira becomes too powerful. I made soup and discovered that the restoration house stove refused to boil water faster just because I glared at it. Elira wrote half a page to Professor Valen about the old consent wards, then crossed out the sentence where she had accidentally turned personal relief into academic analysis.\n\nElira: \"I am doing it again.\"\n\nHaru: \"Studying your own survival?\"\n\nElira: \"Making it easier to discuss by making it less mine.\"\n\nShe stared at the crossed-out line.\n\nThen she wrote beneath it:\n\n**The house made me feel believed before I explained myself. I do not know how to cite that.**\n\nShe set the pen down.\n\nElira: \"There.\"\n\nHaru: \"Professor Valen will understand.\"\n\nElira: \"She will probably assign me three histories of architecture and grief.\"\n\nHaru: \"You would read them.\"\n\nElira: \"Of course. I am traumatized, not uncivilized.\"\n\nThe first thunderhead reached the far side of the lake near dusk.\n\nWe watched it from the kitchen because Elira said avoiding windows would make them larger in her mind.\n\nI stood beside her with two cups of tea on the counter between us. Distance chosen. Close enough for warmth.\n\nFar enough that fear could move without immediately becoming my hands.\n\nLightning flickered behind the clouds.\n\nElira's shoulders rose. Then lowered.\n\nElira: \"That one was fine.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI held her gaze without looking away.\n\nElira: \"Do not sound relieved.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am attempting neutral.\"\n\nElira: \"You sound like a report pretending to be a person.\"\n\nHaru: \"Better than a weapon pretending to be a report.\"\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nThe joke had come out too dark. Or exactly dark enough.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI met her eyes.\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nShe reached across the counter.\n\nI gave her my hand. The storm kept approaching.\n\nFor an hour, it was only weather.\n\nRain began after dinner, soft at first. We moved through the house closing shutters, not because the wards required it, but because the action gave the evening a shape. Elira closed the sitting room shutters herself. I closed the kitchen. Together, we left the bedroom curtains half-open because she did not want the dark to feel like a locked room.\n\nElira: \"If I ask to close them later, do not make it a lesson.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will close them.\"\n\nElira: \"And if I ask to open them again?\"\n\nHaru: \"I will open them.\"\n\nElira let out a quiet breath and nodded.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nWe went to bed early.\n\nNeither of us slept early.\n\nThe storm came on the fourth night.\n\nAt first it was only rain.\n\nRain on the roof. Rain on the lake. Rain against the many windows I had reluctantly agreed not to hate.\n\nI liked rain on stone because it made large places sound less empty.\n\nI had forgotten rain on glass could sound like fingers.\n\nElira woke before the thunder.\n\nI felt her body go rigid beside mine. No gasp. No cry.\n\nJust sudden stillness so complete it pulled me out of sleep like a hand around the heart.\n\nHaru: \"Elira?\"\n\nShe did not answer.\n\nLightning flashed beyond the curtains.\n\nFor half a second the room became white. Then black.\n\nElira's hand went to the pendant.\n\nThe dark crystal was cold with memory, not Dominion. Systems cared about that distinction. A body that had died did not.\n\nThunder struck over the lake.\n\nElira made a sound I had never heard from her before. Small. Broken. The Black Circle rose.\n\nThe room darkened at the corners.\n\nEvery window ward woke at once.\n\nThen Elira said my name like a plea and an order together.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI dragged the Black Circle back before it reached the walls, rough and scraping against my own chest.\n\nThe old cold behind my sternum scraped upward, tasting of ozone and metal. It wanted to answer the storm as if thunder were an enemy. It wanted to make a dome over the house. It wanted to silence the sky.\n\nI let none of it move.\n\nHaru: \"I'm here.\"\n\nElira curled toward me, then froze halfway as if closeness itself had become another route that could be used against her. That was the cruelty of Dominion Severance.\n\nIt had not only killed her. It had taught fear to wear the shape of rescue.\n\nI kept my hands still.\n\nHaru: \"Tell me what is happening.\"\n\nHer breath shook.\n\nElira: \"The window.\"\n\nRain struck the glass.\n\nElira: \"It sounds like the relay.\"\n\nAnother flash lit the curtains. Her eyes were open, fixed somewhere beyond the room.\n\nElira: \"I stepped forward. I knew I was stepping forward. I chose it. Then the spell made my choice into a door for them.\"\n\nI had known that. We all had. Hearing her say it in the dark made knowledge useless.\n\nElira: \"What if every time I choose, something stronger changes the meaning after?\"\n\nThere it was. Fear had a shape, and it was not death or pain. It was having her own choice stolen and rewritten.\n\nI sat up slowly, keeping my movements visible.\n\nHaru: \"May I touch you?\"\n\nShe shut her eyes. For a moment I thought the question had hurt her, then she nodded once.\n\nI touched her hand where it gripped the pendant. Not the pendant itself. Her hand.\n\nElira: \"I hate this.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"I hate that I have to be asked. I hate that I need it. I hate that if you had touched me without asking, I might have panicked. I hate that I wanted you to anyway.\"\n\nThe words came faster, sharpened by storm light.\n\nElira: \"I hate that Cael is dead and I am alive and people call it restoration. I hate that I am frightened of windows. I hate that my own pendant feels like a witness to failure even though I know it was bypassed. I hate that I know the exact doctrine and still cannot make my body believe it is over.\"\n\nHer voice broke.\n\nElira: \"I hate that I died in front of you.\"\n\nThe Black Circle pressed against my ribs so hard I almost could not breathe.\n\nI had been afraid of my grief. I had not made enough room for hers.\n\nHaru: \"I hate that too.\"\n\nShe looked at me. Rain covered the silence.\n\nHaru: \"I hate that I held you and there was nothing I could do fast enough. I hate that when the Black Circle came, part of me wanted the world to pay before I wanted to understand sequence. I hate that Cael had to choose inside a spell designed to make death look noble. I hate that you woke up and looked for the cost before you looked for yourself.\"\n\nMy voice was not steady. Maybe that helped.\n\nHaru: \"And I hate that I keep trying to become careful enough that nothing can hurt you, because that is just another way of building a room you did not choose.\"\n\nElira's tears slipped sideways into her hair.\n\nElira: \"Then what do we do?\"\n\nI had no doctrine for that. No ancient precedent. No hidden Circle Zero answer.\n\nOnly the room. The storm. Her hand under mine.\n\nHaru: \"We tell the truth before it turns into a wall.\"\n\nThe thunder rolled farther away. Elira breathed in, ragged.\n\nElira: \"I am scared that loving me makes you dangerous.\"\n\nThe words struck deeper than thunder. I had feared the same thing, even if I had never put it into those words.\n\nHaru: \"I am scared of that too.\"\n\nHer face tightened. I held her hand more firmly.\n\nHaru: \"Not because you are the danger. Because losing you showed me where my restraint is weakest.\"\n\nElira did not look away.\n\nHaru: \"But I do not think the answer is loving you less.\"\n\nThe storm softened around the house.\n\nHaru: \"I think the answer is letting you stand close enough to tell me when fear is pretending to be protection.\"\n\nElira's hand turned under mine.\n\nElira: \"And if my fear pretends to be wisdom?\"\n\nHaru: \"Then I tell you.\"\n\nShe swallowed.\n\nElira: \"Even if I am angry?\"\n\nHaru: \"Especially then.\"\n\nThat almost made her smile. Almost. Enough.\n\nThe mirror in the corner brightened with emergency protocol.\n\nRhea's seal formed first, then Maelin's.\n\nMaelin: \"Status.\"\n\nShe did not waste a syllable. She had felt the ward surge, and so had everyone tied to the perimeter.\n\nI looked at Elira. This was her fear. Her room. Her choice.\n\nElira wiped her face with the heel of her hand and sat up straighter.\n\nElira: \"Storm response. Trauma memory. No hostile relay. Pendant cold from recall, not active interference.\"\n\nMaelin studied her through the mirror.\n\nMaelin: \"Do you require intervention?\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nAfter a beat, she added:\n\nElira: \"But stay connected for five minutes.\"\n\nMaelin's expression softened by one almost invisible degree.\n\nMaelin: \"Good request.\"\n\nRhea appeared behind her, fully dressed despite the hour because Rhea Valmont seemed to sleep in authority.\n\nRhea: \"Council perimeter agrees. No external pressure.\"\n\nAira's voice came from somewhere offscreen, sleepy and alarmed.\n\nAira: \"Do I need to emotionally threaten weather?\"\n\nElira laughed through the last of the panic, broken and still hers.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nAira: \"Good. Weather is broad. I would need notes.\"\n\nFor five minutes, the mirror stayed lit. No one demanded details. No one turned fear into evidence.\n\nThat may have been the most careful thing they did.\n\nRhea gave perimeter updates in the same tone she used for council law.\n\nRhea: \"North road clear. East lake ward steady. No relay signatures within the outer ring.\"\n\nMaelin corrected Elira's breathing once.\n\nMaelin: \"Longer exhale. You are not making a speech.\"\n\nElira: \"I hate that you can hear that.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Then breathe loudly enough to be medically useful.\"\n\nAira, now wrapped in a blanket and apparently sitting on the floor of whatever room they had gathered in, lifted one hand.\n\nAira: \"Request to say something unprofessional.\"\n\nRhea: \"Denied.\"\n\nAira said it anyway.\n\nAira: \"You are doing very well, and this storm is extremely rude.\"\n\nElira's hand tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nLeon appeared behind Aira, hair disordered from sleep and expression painfully serious.\n\nLeon: \"The water charm should hold through thunder.\"\n\nHe looked embarrassed as soon as he said it, as if concern had escaped without permission. Elira's face softened.\n\nElira: \"It has helped all day.\"\n\nLeon looked away.\n\nLeon: \"Good.\"\n\nCassian leaned into frame with an entire candle in hand.\n\nCassian: \"I brought dramatic lighting.\"\n\nRhea: \"Why?\"\n\nCassian: \"Morale.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Put it away before you drip wax on a medical relay.\"\n\nCassian disappeared.\n\nElira laughed again, weaker than before but steadier.\n\nThe sound did not erase the shaking. It gave the shaking company. Professor Valen joined last.\n\nShe did not ask what happened. She looked at Elira, then at me, then at the dark window behind us where rain still struck the glass like a hundred small accusations.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Old fear often enters through ordinary doors.\"\n\nElira wiped at her cheek.\n\nElira: \"That is inconveniently poetic.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"It is also true. If we seal every ordinary door, fear wins architecture.\"\n\nThe restoration house gave a faint warm pulse beneath the floor.\n\nAira stared downward through the mirror.\n\nAira: \"Did the house agree?\"\n\nHaru: \"Possibly.\"\n\nAira: \"I hate that you said that calmly.\"\n\nProfessor Valen smiled with tired satisfaction.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"The house is old enough to have opinions and rude enough to keep them.\"\n\nElira breathed in, long, then out, longer.\n\nMaelin watched and said nothing. That silence was approval.\n\nDuring the fourth minute, thunder struck close enough to rattle the glass. Elira flinched.\n\nI kept my hand still by force, waiting instead of freezing. Elira's eyes squeezed shut.\n\nElira: \"Window. Rain. Not relay.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Good.\"\n\nElira: \"Storm. Not field.\"\n\nRhea: \"Confirmed.\"\n\nElira: \"My hand. Haru's hand. I asked.\"\n\nMy throat closed. Aira's eyes filled, and for once she did not make a joke.\n\nI heard what it cost her to say those words out loud. Elira opened her eyes.\n\nElira: \"I am in the house.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe inclined her head once in the glass.\n\nElira: \"I am alive.\"\n\nNo one rushed to answer. No one stole the sentence by finishing it. Elira looked at me.\n\nI said the only thing that was mine to say.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI kept my hand steady in hers as the five minutes ran out and the mirror light softened.\n\nMaelin did not disconnect immediately.\n\nMaelin: \"Do you request continued witness connection?\"\n\nElira actually weighed it instead of refusing out of pride or clinging out of habit.\n\nElira: \"No. I want the room back.\"\n\nRhea nodded once.\n\nRhea: \"Granted.\"\n\nAira: \"Text if weather becomes a criminal.\"\n\nElira's mouth curved.\n\nElira: \"I will.\"\n\nThe mirror began to dim.\n\nThen the lake struck the house. Not a wave. A wall.\n\nThe gale spun off the water and drove half the eastern shoreline against the restoration wards in one black sheet. The impact lifted the bedroom floor under our knees. Every lamp went out. A window in the hall shattered inward, and the sound tore Elira out of the room more violently than thunder had. She screamed. The Black Circle answered.\n\nDark geometry crossed the ceiling before I could force it back. For one heartbeat I saw the solution it offered: remove momentum from the water, still the air over the lake, place the entire house inside a law weather could not enter. Perfect. Absolute.\n\nLarge enough to stop every heart inside the ward if I shaped the boundary wrong.\n\nElira saw the black lines. She was still panicking.\n\nShe still grabbed my face with both hands and made me look at her.\n\nElira: \"Not the sky.\"\n\nTwo words. Enough.\n\nI pulled the circle inward until it tore cold through my chest. The geometry vanished from the ceiling. The storm did not.\n\nThe house alarm finally found its voice.\n\nThree brass notes rang through the walls.\n\nProfessor Valen's old safety script appeared above the bedroom door:\n\n**EASTERN WATER WARD LOST**\n\n**LOWER FOUNDATION FLOODING**\n\n**OCCUPANT RESPONSE REQUIRED**\n\nElira read the last line and laughed once, breathless and furious.\n\nElira: \"Of course the house needs us now.\"\n\nThe floor tilted again. A cedar beam cracked somewhere below. I reached for her, then stopped.\n\nHaru: \"Can you move?\"\n\nShe put one hand on the mattress, then stood. Her legs shook, and she stayed upright.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe caught the edge of the bedpost as the floorboards shifted under her boots.\n\nHaru: \"Was that an answer or pride?\"\n\nHer eyes flashed.\n\nElira: \"Both. Ask me on the stairs.\"\n\nWe moved.\n\nThe corridor windows had become white rectangles under the rain. Water poured through the broken one and ran across the floorboards toward the western rooms. The old house responded by opening narrow seams between the boards, but the drainage channels below were already full. Each seam breathed cold lake air upward.\n\nAt the stair landing, the eastern wall bowed inward. Elira stopped. Not from panic. Listening.\n\nElira: \"The house is distributing pressure through the consent wards.\"\n\nHaru: \"Can it hold?\"\n\nAnother beam broke below us.\n\nElira: \"That sounded like no.\"\n\nThe secure mirror on the landing flared back to life. Maelin appeared first, one hand braced against the relay frame as wind distorted the image.\n\nMaelin: \"Leave the eastern side. Cellar safe room, now.\"\n\nProfessor Valen pushed into view behind her.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"No. The cellar sits under the failed ward.\"\n\nMaelin turned on her.\n\nMaelin: \"You recommended this house.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"I did not recommend the mountain emptying a lake through it.\"\n\nRhea's voice cut through both.\n\nRhea: \"Available exits?\"\n\nI read the house with ordinary senses first. Front door: windward, impossible.\n\nKitchen passage: water already under it.\n\nWest garden: higher ground, but the connecting corridor crossed beneath the bowed wall.\n\nHaru: \"No safe exit without passing the eastern brace.\"\n\nAira appeared at the edge of the mirror with a house plan held upside down.\n\nAira: \"The old plans show a manual spill channel under the sitting room. It diverts lake pressure into the south garden.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"It requires two occupants. One opens the channel. One tells the house which rooms remain inhabited.\"\n\nThe floor gave another violent lurch.\n\nHaru: \"I open it. Elira marks the rooms from here.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nEvery frightened instinct in me sharpened.\n\nShe pointed down the stairs.\n\nElira: \"The channel key is Yellow-era craft. It will answer me faster. You can hold the eastern brace.\"\n\nHaru: \"Your channels are unstable.\"\n\nElira: \"And the wall is moving.\"\n\nHaru: \"Elira...\"\n\nShe stepped close enough that I had to see the fear still in her face.\n\nElira: \"This is weather. Real danger. Not Dominion. Do not make my panic decide I am useless before I do.\"\n\nThe sentence hurt because it was right and because being right did not make her body less breakable.\n\nMaelin leaned toward the mirror.\n\nMaelin: \"Elira. One Yellow pulse to open, no sustained output. If your vision narrows, you stop even if the house does not.\"\n\nElira: \"Agreed.\"\n\nMaelin looked at me.\n\nMaelin: \"Haru. Physical reinforcement only unless the brace fails completely. If Black Circle pressure rises above the wrist, you leave the wall and both of you take the west corridor.\"\n\nHaru: \"Agreed.\"\n\nRhea lifted her seal.\n\nRhea: \"Witness plan entered. Move.\"\n\nWe separated at the foot of the stairs.\n\nEvery part of me rejected it.\n\nElira went left toward the sitting room, one hand trailing along the wall for balance. I went right into the eastern passage where the house had begun coming apart.\n\nWater hit me at knee height.\n\nThe corridor no longer had a floor so much as a current with furniture in it. A side table struck my thigh and spun away. Cold closed around my boots. The bowed wall groaned ahead, held by a cedar brace split almost through its center.\n\nBehind it, the storm drove the lake against glass and stone. I waded forward.\n\nThe Black Circle mapped every point of failure. Six wall anchors. Three window seams. One fractured foundation stone under the brace. It offered corrections faster than I could refuse them.\n\nI put both hands on the cedar. Wood, not law. Weight, not command.\n\nThe next impact drove a splinter through my palm. I held.\n\nFrom the sitting room, Elira shouted:\n\nElira: \"Key found!\"\n\nI could not see her.\n\nThat was the test fear had wanted all night.\n\nHaru: \"I hear you.\"\n\nThe response came through the walls.\n\nElira: \"Opening on three.\"\n\nThe house script ignited under the water at my feet. Old gold lines ran from the sitting room toward the southern foundation. One stopped at the threshold, dimmed, and went dark.\n\nAira's voice crackled from the mirror behind me.\n\nAira: \"The channel is blocked under the kitchen!\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Maintenance hatch beneath the pantry stones.\"\n\nElira: \"I can force more power through.\"\n\nMaelin and I answered together.\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"Annoying. Correct.\"\n\nThe eastern brace cracked another inch.\n\nI could not leave it.\n\nElira could not lift the pantry stones and sustain the key.\n\nFor one second the plan became a trap built from our limits.\n\nThen a third voice came through the house.\n\nOld. Calm. Not human enough to be comforting.\n\nRestoration House: \"Occupant may release key. House will remember intention for twelve breaths.\"\n\nElira went silent.\n\nAira: \"Did the building just offer a timing window?\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Yes. Twelve breaths. Move!\"\n\nAira: \"I would like history to become less conversational.\"\n\nElira released the Yellow pulse. The gold lines remained. She ran.\n\nI heard her cross the hall, stumble, catch the kitchen frame, continue. Twelve breaths was not enough for caution. It had to be enough for trust.\n\nAira began counting through the mirror.\n\nAira: \"One. Two. Three.\"\n\nAt four, stone scraped in the pantry.\n\nAt five, Elira grunted with effort.\n\nAt six, the wall struck my shoulder hard enough to numb my left arm.\n\nThe Black Circle rose from my chest to my throat. Not the wrist. Higher.\n\nMaelin saw the dark pressure under my skin through the relay.\n\nMaelin: \"Haru, withdraw.\"\n\nThe wall moved again. If I let go now, it would fall across the corridor between Elira and the west exit.\n\nHaru: \"Cannot.\"\n\nRhea: \"State reason.\"\n\nHaru: \"Brace failure blocks her return route. I hold until spill channel opens or the cedar goes.\"\n\nNo one called that heroic. Good.\n\nAira: \"Eight.\"\n\nElira shouted from the pantry.\n\nElira: \"Hatch open!\"\n\nAira: \"Nine.\"\n\nThe gold lines beneath the water flared. Nothing moved.\n\nElira: \"The lever is chained.\"\n\nCassian's voice came through the relay from Aurelis.\n\nCassian: \"Chain composition?\"\n\nElira: \"Old iron. Silver ward braid.\"\n\nCassian: \"Heat the iron only. Short pulse. The silver will push it apart.\"\n\nMaelin: \"One pulse.\"\n\nElira: \"I heard you.\"\n\nAira: \"Ten.\"\n\nGold flashed under the pantry door. Elira cried out. The chain snapped.\n\nAira: \"Eleven.\"\n\nThe house opened beneath us.\n\nWater dropped so fast my stomach followed it. The current reversed, tore chairs away from the eastern passage, and vanished through the southern spill channel with a roar like a second storm under the floor.\n\nThe pressure left the wall.\n\nThe cedar brace sagged into my hands instead of breaking. I lowered it slowly.\n\nThen I ran to the pantry.\n\nElira sat on the floor beside the open hatch. The iron chain smoked near her boots. Her right hand shook violently. A thin line of blood ran from one nostril to her upper lip.\n\nShe looked furious about the blood.\n\nI stopped in the doorway.\n\nHaru: \"May I?\"\n\nElira: \"Yes. Quickly, before Maelin arrives through the mirror and kills weather herself.\"\n\nI knelt and caught her as the last strength left her shoulders.\n\nMaelin's voice filled the kitchen.\n\nMaelin: \"Hand open where I can see it.\"\n\nElira showed her the trembling fingers.\n\nMaelin: \"Any loss of vision?\"\n\nElira: \"Edges went gray after the heat pulse. Returning now.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Nausea?\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer chin rose, clear and deliberate.\n\nMaelin: \"Haru?\"\n\nI looked at my splintered palm, numb shoulder, the black pressure receding below my collarbone.\n\nHaru: \"Circle rose to throat. No external manifestation. Left arm numb from impact.\"\n\nRhea entered both conditions without praise. That helped. The storm continued outside.\n\nInside, the water fell below our ankles. The house settled around the open spill channel, damaged but standing. Broken glass covered the hall. One wall leaned badly. The kitchen smelled of wet stone, burned iron, and soup herbs washed out of an overturned jar.\n\nElira rested her head against my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"Still weather.\"\n\nHaru: \"Very rude weather.\"\n\nAira made a weak sound through the mirror.\n\nAira: \"I have been saying that.\"\n\nElira laughed, then breathed carefully through another wave of nausea while Maelin counted. Terrified and exhausted, she had still moved when the wall gave.\n\nWhen the danger passed, Elira sagged against me.\n\nThe house did not become quiet. Water still rushed through the spill channel, broken glass clicked across the hall whenever wind entered, and somewhere in the eastern passage the damaged cedar brace settled with a long wooden groan.\n\nElira flinched at each sound. She did not pretend otherwise.\n\nElira: \"I want to see the window.\"\n\nMaelin objected immediately through the mirror.\n\nMaelin: \"You want a bed.\"\n\nElira: \"I need the broken sound to belong to something I survived.\"\n\nThat stopped the argument.\n\nI helped her as far as the kitchen door, no farther. From there we could see the hall window: glass missing, rain blowing through an empty frame, one shutter hanging crooked from its hinge. Ordinary damage. Expensive, inconvenient, repairable.\n\nElira watched two maintenance charms lift a board into place. The first nail bent. The second held. Her breathing slowed.\n\nElira: \"It broke because water hit it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI kept my hands open at my knees where she could see them.\n\nElira: \"Not because I chose wrong.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nShe looked at the blood drying across my palm.\n\nElira: \"And you held a beam instead of rewriting weather.\"\n\nHaru: \"Badly.\"\n\nElira: \"Humanly.\"\n\nThe word warmed something power could not reach.\n\nWhen the board covered the frame, Elira asked the maintenance charm to leave one narrow gap. Rain sound entered through it, softer now and attached to a visible sky.\n\nShe listened until thunder became distance again.\n\nThen she cleaned the splinter from my palm.\n\nHer fingers still shook from the Yellow pulse, so the tweezers clicked twice against my skin before catching the wood. We both knew I could have pushed it out with magic. I stayed still and let the ordinary tool sting.\n\nElira: \"Hold still.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am.\"\n\nElira: \"You are enduring dramatically. Different problem.\"\n\nThe splinter came free with one sharp pull. Blood welled. Elira wrapped my hand in kitchen cloth because the medical bandages were upstairs and neither of us wanted another staircase yet. The knot was ugly. It held.\n\nFor several minutes, repair meant a boarded window, an open flood channel, and her hands caring for mine after her power had already done enough.\n\nElira left the blood-marked kitchen cloth around my palm.\n\nElira: \"Maelin will replace that.\"\n\nHaru: \"Probably while insulting the knot.\"\n\nElira: \"She will be correct.\"\n\nWe sat on the dry side of the kitchen with our backs against the cabinet. No symbolic view. No lake. Only wet socks, ruined herbs, and the storm breaking itself out toward the east.\n\nElira fell asleep there for nine minutes. I did not move her to a better room while she was finally resting.\n\nWhen she woke, she did not freeze halfway. I wrapped my arms around her only after she leaned in, pressing her face against my chest while the long shake finally worked its way out of her shoulders. I held her through it without trying to be a shield or a cure, just someone who loved her and had stopped pretending love was not afraid.\n\nOnce the spill channel slowed to a steady rush, I helped her up the western stairs past the boarded hall window. We peeled off wet socks and damp sleeves, took the dry room at the end of the landing, and got under heavy blankets before dawn.\n\nNear dawn, the storm moved east.\n\nThe windows brightened from black to gray.\n\nElira lay awake beside me, exhausted beyond sleep.\n\nElira: \"I want to fix the pendant.\"\n\nI turned toward her.\n\nHaru: \"So do I.\"\n\nElira: \"Not because it failed.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because Dominion found a route beneath it.\"\n\nElira gave a tired nod.\n\nElira: \"If rescue can be relabeled, then protection has to recognize consent after the fact. It has to reject a spell that steals meaning from a choice.\"\n\nMy mind began to move. Slowly at first. Then with frightening clarity.\n\nHaru: \"A witness consent anchor.\"\n\nElira's eyes sharpened despite exhaustion.\n\nElira: \"Distributed. Not only yours.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira for sequence.\"\n\nElira: \"Rhea for authority.\"\n\nHaru: \"Maelin for patient status.\"\n\nElira: \"Me for my own choice.\"\n\nThat mattered most, and she saw that I understood. Elira touched the pendant. This time her hand did not shake.\n\nElira: \"I do not want protection that treats me like something guarded from outside.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then we build protection that listens from beside you.\"\n\nThe idea did not let either of us sleep. That should have worried me. Instead it steadied the room.\n\nFear had a shape. Repair had one too, neither a cure nor a guarantee, just a structure humble enough to admit when it could be wrong and stubborn enough to refuse a stolen meaning.\n\nElira pushed herself upright against the pillows.\n\nHaru: \"You should rest.\"\n\nShe gave me a look.\n\nElira: \"Do not become Maelin before dawn.\"\n\nHaru: \"I lack the authority.\"\n\nElira: \"And the terrifying eyebrows.\"\n\nI looked toward the desk where Professor Valen's blank cards still waited. Elira followed my gaze.\n\nElira: \"Bring the notebook.\"\n\nHaru: \"Which one?\"\n\nElira: \"Aira's emergency one.\"\n\nOf course. Aira had hidden a small blue notebook in the bedside drawer beneath a folded pair of socks. On the cover she had written:\n\n**FOR LATE-NIGHT IDEAS THAT SHOULD NOT BECOME UNSUPERVISED SPELLCRAFT.**\n\nInside, the first page read:\n\n**If you are Haru: write first, cast never.**\n\nThe second page:\n\n**If you are Elira: make him show you the page.**\n\nThe third:\n\n**If you are both: hello, I am a genius.**\n\nElira read the pages and laughed until the laughter turned into a cough. I handed her water.\n\nShe drank without arguing. Another miracle.\n\nThen she took the pen.\n\nElira: \"Write the problem.\"\n\nHaru: \"Dominion Severance relabels voluntary rescue as unlawful route activation.\"\n\nElira: \"Too system.\"\n\nI stopped. She tapped the pen against the page.\n\nElira: \"Write it so my body would understand.\"\n\nThat was harder. I stared at the blank line, then wrote:\n\n**I chose to help. The spell lied about what my choice meant.**\n\nElira's breath shook.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe word hurt, and it fit. She took the pen back and wrote beneath it:\n\n**Future protection must believe the person who chose, even if hostile magic tries to rename the choice afterward.**\n\nThe Black Circle moved under my skin, quiet, like something turning toward a voice instead of a threat.\n\nHaru: \"The pendant listened to danger.\"\n\nElira: \"It listened to you.\"\n\nHaru: \"And to hostile interference.\"\n\nElira: \"But not enough to me after the route changed.\"\n\nI flinched, and she saw it.\n\nElira: \"That is not blame.\"\n\nHaru: \"It feels like it should be.\"\n\nElira placed the pen down.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\nElira: \"If we make every limitation into guilt, we will be too ashamed to improve anything.\"\n\nThe sentence belonged in a Foundation Record. I was glad no Foundation Record heard it; some truths deserved to stay hers.\n\nWe made four columns. That was Aira's influence.\n\n**Choice.**\n\n**Witness.**\n\n**Body.**\n\n**Route.**\n\nUnder Choice, Elira wrote:\n\n**My consent before, during, and after action.**\n\nUnder Witness, I wrote:\n\n**Sequence holders cannot be collapsed into one will.**\n\nUnder Body, Elira wrote:\n\n**Pain and fear are data, not disobedience.**\n\nUnder Route, I wrote:\n\n**Hostile relabeling must be treated as attack even when it uses rescue grammar.**\n\nThere should have been a fifth column.\n\nI did not write it.\n\nElira's fear had names now: Choice, Witness, Body, Route. The page gave her terror edges she could touch without being swallowed by it.\n\nMine had no such courtesy.\n\nMy fear was not that I would fail to protect her. That was too simple, almost clean enough to be heroic if someone wrote it from far away.\n\nMy fear was that protection would become the only shape I knew how to take.\n\nThat one morning I would wake and find every chair arranged for visibility, every window checked for hostile line of sight, every path mapped according to where I could reach fastest, and Elira would still be beside me while the room had become mine instead of ours.\n\nI feared the gentle version of domination.\n\nThe one with blankets warmed before she asked.\n\nThe one with doors opened before she reached them.\n\nThe one with danger removed so quickly no one could prove she had been allowed to face it.\n\nI feared becoming careful enough to make the world quiet around her, then mistaking the quiet for peace because no one was screaming.\n\nThere was no column for that. Or there was, and I did not yet have the courage to write my name under it.\n\nSo I left the space blank. Elira saw it and did not push. That mercy was sharper than questioning would have been.\n\nWe stared at the page. Rain softened outside.\n\nElira: \"Aira for sequence.\"\n\nI added it.\n\nHaru: \"Rhea for authority.\"\n\nShe added it.\n\nElira: \"Maelin for body status.\"\n\nI wrote carefully.\n\nHaru: \"Professor Valen for old doctrine translation?\"\n\nElira considered.\n\nElira: \"Yes. But not as a lock. As a historian.\"\n\nI wrote:\n\n**Valen: context, not control.**\n\nElira nodded.\n\nThen she took the pen from me.\n\nUnder Choice, she wrote her own name. Not Elira Vale, Yellow Circle. Not Primary Witness. Not Compatibility Pressure Point. Only:\n\n**Elira.**\n\nThe ink looked small on the page. It changed everything.\n\nHaru: \"Good.\"\n\nShe looked up at me.\n\nHaru: \"Humanly good.\"\n\nProfessor Valen's phrase came back through me without permission. Elira's eyes warmed.\n\nThen the pendant pulsed once, neither cold nor bright, a low violet-black answer against her throat.\n\nBoth of us went still.\n\nThe notebook page did not glow. The house did not open a hidden chamber. No ancient system announced approval.\n\nThe pendant only acknowledged the shape of the question.\n\nElira touched it with one finger.\n\nElira: \"It heard?\"\n\nHaru: \"I think it heard you.\"\n\nShe closed her eyes. For a moment grief moved over her face again, because being heard now did not undo the one time it had not been enough. I waited.\n\nShe opened her eyes and kept writing. That was courage without a crowd to applaud it, happening at dawn with shaking hands and bad ink.\n\nElira: \"We need to tell them today.\"\n\nHaru: \"Maelin will be furious we stayed awake.\"\n\nElira: \"Maelin can be furious at the notebook. It encouraged us.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira will claim authorship of the breakthrough.\"\n\nElira: \"She did provide infrastructure.\"\n\nI looked at the page. At her name. At the four columns. At the first honest sentence.\n\nI chose to help. The spell lied about what my choice meant.\n\nHaru: \"We will need more than a pendant.\"\n\nElira's expression sharpened.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe held my gaze without wavering.\n\nHaru: \"A vow structure.\"\n\nElira: \"Not possession.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"Not isolation.\"\n\nHaru: \"Never.\"\n\nElira: \"A vow that can be witnessed without making us public property.\"\n\nI felt the future turn, not fully, just enough to know the Proximity Hearing had begun before the sun did.\n\nHaru: \"Then we ask them to come.\"\n\nElira leaned back against the pillows, exhaustion finally catching her.\n\nElira: \"After breakfast.\"\n\nHaru: \"After sleep.\"\n\nShe gave me another look.\n\nHaru: \"A little sleep.\"\n\nElira: \"Better.\"\n\nShe rested the notebook between us on the blanket.\n\nI did not move it.\n\nFor once, the plan did not feel like a wall. It felt like a door we had named ourselves.\n\nThe Council memo arrived before either of us had closed our eyes.\n\nIt came through Rhea's secure mirror in a tone so polite it made the restoration house windows frost at the corners.\n\n**PRELIMINARY PROXIMITY CONCERN**\n\n**SUBJECTS: HARU AERLEN / ELIRA VALE**\n\n**QUESTION: WHETHER CONTINUED PRIVATE PROXIMITY CREATES DEPENDENCY, WITNESS CONTAMINATION, OR SAFEGUARD INSTABILITY**\n\nElira read it once. Then again.\n\nThe storm had passed outside, but the air in the room changed as if thunder had learned legal language.\n\nHaru: \"That did not take long.\"\n\nElira's face became very still. Not frightened. Worse. Familiar.\n\nShe knew what it meant to have care converted into a question someone else owned.\n\nThen the stillness cracked, ragged and far too human to look brave.\n\nElira pushed the blanket off her knees and stood too fast. The healer charm at her cuff flashed warning gold. I reached for her before thinking. She stepped back.\n\nElira: \"Do not.\"\n\nMy hand stopped in the air.\n\nThe word had not been cruel. It had still landed like one.\n\nFor one second, the room became all the distances I had tried to manage for her without naming them. Bed to chair. Hand to wrist. Door to corridor. My instinct to protect, dressed as gentleness, arriving before her permission could.\n\nElira saw my face and looked away.\n\nElira: \"I did not mean...\"\n\nShe stopped. The unfinished sentence hurt more than a complete one would have.\n\nMaelin's image sharpened in the mirror.\n\nMaelin: \"Sit down before I make the floor persuasive.\"\n\nElira did not sit.\n\nElira: \"I am tired of being handled carefully.\"\n\nRhea went quiet. Aira's pen stopped moving.\n\nElira pressed both hands against the table edge as if the wood had offended her by being steady.\n\nElira: \"I know why everyone is careful. I know. I am not asking anyone to become stupid. But if one more person moves slowly around me as if kindness has to wear gloves, I am going to scream.\"\n\nHer voice broke on the last word. She hated that it did. I lowered my hand.\n\nHaru: \"I am sorry.\"\n\nElira laughed once without humor.\n\nElira: \"For what? Being good at the one thing keeping this from becoming worse?\"\n\nHaru: \"For deciding before asking.\"\n\nShe looked at me then. Anger still there. Fear underneath it.\n\nSomething unfair moving between both because exhaustion had finally found a door.\n\nElira: \"Sometimes I want you to stop being so correct.\"\n\nThe sentence struck harder than any accusation would have.\n\nI did not understand it. Not then. That was part of the problem.\n\nHaru: \"I do not know how to answer that.\"\n\nElira closed her eyes.\n\nElira: \"Good. Neither do I.\"\n\nFor once, no one tried to make the silence useful. The mirror brightened.\n\nRhea appeared with her hair unpinned on one side and the expression of a person who had already argued with three senior officials before tea.\n\nRhea: \"Do not answer the memo emotionally.\"\n\nAira's voice came from somewhere beyond the mirror frame.\n\nAira: \"Too late emotionally. Still possible administratively.\"\n\nMaelin leaned into the mirror beside Rhea, eyes moving over Elira before she looked at me.\n\nMaelin: \"Patient status?\"\n\nElira: \"Alive. Irritated. Under-slept.\"\n\nMaelin: \"The third is my jurisdiction.\"\n\nElira: \"The Council interrupted recovery.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I will allow the accusation.\"\n\nThe small exchange should have eased the room. It did not.\n\nThe memo sat between us like a new kind of weather.\n\nRhea: \"Krail's office did not file the language directly. That is important. He floated it through a welfare subcommittee so the first version sounds like concern.\"\n\nAira leaned into view holding a copied page.\n\nAira: \"Concern with teeth, claws, and a little hat labeled procedure.\"\n\nRhea: \"Solen.\"\n\nAira: \"I am coping.\"\n\nRhea turned one page.\n\nRhea: \"There is also a Noctis observer notice attached to the same docket.\"\n\nElira's fingers stilled on the blanket.\n\nRhea: \"Lucien Voss did not author it. He was careful. But the phrasing uses his construction: proximity influence treated as evidence only after the subject chooses to remain close.\"\n\nAira: \"So if you leave, he calls distance instability. If you stay, he calls closeness contamination.\"\n\nRhea: \"That is the shape.\"\n\nThe quiet in the restoration house changed, no longer broken, only borrowed.\n\nElira took the page from the blanket and set it beside her notebook.\n\nThe four columns looked suddenly smaller.\n\nWhat I feared. What happened. What the spell tried to make it mean. What I choose to call it.\n\nNow the Council had offered a fifth column: what strangers might find convenient.\n\nI hated it with a clarity that made the Black Circle stir.\n\nThe ribbon on my wrist tightened as my hand curled. Elira saw.\n\nShe reached over and untied it.\n\nFor one second I thought she was removing the anchor.\n\nInstead, she retied it looser.\n\nElira: \"Better, not tighter.\"\n\nThe words cut straight through my reflex to retreat.\n\nHaru: \"I was about to make the room obedient again.\"\n\nRhea's eyes sharpened. Maelin said nothing.\n\nElira: \"You were about to make the memo proof that distance is safer.\"\n\nThat was worse, because she was right.\n\nSome part of me had already started building the argument. I could step back. I could make proximity less useful to Krail. I could protect her privacy by removing myself from the shape of the accusation.\n\nIt sounded noble until I imagined Elira waking from another nightmare and finding the chair beside the bed empty because a Councilor had taught me to mistake absence for respect. The thought sickened me.\n\nHaru: \"The memo wants me to become its solution.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nShe set her palm flat over the open notebook and looked at the mirror.\n\nElira: \"Record that.\"\n\nAira's pen moved so fast it scratched.\n\nRhea's expression changed by one exact degree.\n\nRhea: \"That is the answer.\"\n\nElira: \"Not all of it.\"\n\nShe took the notebook again.\n\nHer hand shook from exhaustion, anger, and whatever remained of the night's fear, but the line she wrote was clean:\n\n**Private proximity is not automatically dependency. Distance chosen by hostile framing is not autonomy.**\n\nShe handed the pen to me. Not because she needed my agreement. Because the memo named both of us.\n\nI wrote below her sentence:\n\n**If I leave because containment language makes staying look suspicious, then containment has already governed me.**\n\nAira made a wounded sound.\n\nAira: \"That is disgustingly useful.\"\n\nRhea: \"Accepted. We are coming out to Lake Velis at noon. Valen is pulling an old ward case from the archive, and Leon is packing stabilizing plates. Do not touch the pendant until we get there.\"\n\nMaelin looked between us.\n\nMaelin: \"Now both of you get three hours of sleep before I classify Council correspondence as a health hazard.\"\n\nElira leaned back, the anger not gone but placed somewhere it could be found later. The memo remained. So did the notebook. So did the loosened ribbon.\n\nNo blade descended. No Councilor appeared in the doorway. No one shouted.\n\nBut the shape of what came next had entered the recovery house in a neat official font, and we had answered it before it could teach us to abandon the room.\n\nWe managed three hours of bruised, uneven sleep while the rain outside thinned and came back as a cold drizzle. By late morning, the house smelled of wet cedar, soot, and reheated tea.\n\nElira sat on the edge of the bed and glanced at my wrist, where the black ribbon had slipped down toward my knuckles while we slept. She retied it with slow fingers.\n\nElira: \"There.\"\n\nHaru: \"Still moderately improved?\"\n\nHer mouth curved.\n\nElira: \"Slightly more than moderately.\"\n\nThe worst night of recovery leave was behind us, though nothing about it felt settled. Fear remained, the pendant still waited to be remade, and Elira stood beside me changed by death, free to be ungrateful, and ready to meet whatever came through the door at noon.\n\n## Coda: Containment Weather\n\nRain began again before noon.\n\nNo thunder followed it, no sudden crack in the sky, just cold water ticking against the restoration house roof while the Council memo sat on the sitting room table and tried to make fear sound responsible. Elira unlatched the unboarded window.\n\nMaelin objected from the portable mirror on the mantle with her whole face.\n\nElira: \"I need to hear something that is not policy.\"\n\nThe room allowed it. Rain entered as scent first: cedar, lake stone, wet earth. Haru stood near enough to close the window if she asked and far enough that the choice stayed hers.\n\nElira touched the newly tied ribbon at his wrist.\n\nElira: \"If they call staying together suspicious, we make suspicious look boring.\"\n\nHaru: \"Boring may be beyond us.\"\n\nAcross the mirror, Aira did not look up from the satchel she was strapping shut in Aurelis.\n\nAira: \"I can draft a schedule for strategic boring on the ride out. Rhea is pulling the carriage around now.\"\n\nMaelin muttered something under her breath and checked her watch before cutting the relay for the road.\n\nThe mirror went dark. The house went quiet again, except for the rain and the spill channel muttering beneath the boards.\n\nFor the first time since the memo arrived, Elira smiled like the room belonged to the living, then turned toward the front door with him to wait for noon.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-23-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep24","label":"Episode 18","number":"18","file":"episode-24-cover.webp","fallback":"The Second Hinge","eyebrow":"Restoration Record","recap":"","accent":["#111111","#c0c0c0","#36d17d"],"displayLabel":"Episode 18","displayNumber":"18","displayEyebrow":"The Severance Crisis","arcLabel":"The Severance Crisis","title":"The Vow After Death","body":"Professor Valen arrived at noon with Rhea, Maelin, Aira, Leon, Cassian, and a ward case that made the restoration house lights dim in protest.\n\nElira opened the door wearing a shawl over my stolen shirt.\n\nAira took one look at her.\n\nAira: \"I knew the shirt was gone.\"\n\nElira: \"You cannot prove it was theft.\"\n\nAira: \"Haru?\"\n\nI looked out at the lake.\n\nAira: \"Conviction secured.\"\n\nLeon carried a stack of stabilizing plates. Cassian carried food. Rhea carried documents. Maelin carried the expression of a healer who would rather fight a dragon than watch students improvise medical magic without supervision.\n\nProfessor Valen carried the ward case.\n\nIt was black cedar banded in old silver. Older than Black Circle work, from the same pre-standardization craft as the house itself.\n\nElira's fingers brushed the pendant at her throat. Professor Valen caught the motion.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"We do not have to do this today.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes, we do.\"\n\nMaelin made a displeased sound. Elira met her look squarely.\n\nElira: \"I am not forcing strength. Last night showed the fear is already at work. I would rather deal with it honestly.\"\n\nMaelin's scowl stayed put, though respect softened the edges.\n\nMaelin: \"Then we work slowly.\"\n\nRhea set the documents on the table.\n\nRhea: \"This is not a Council classification procedure. It is a voluntary protective revision under medical and personal authority. I wrote that sentence six times because someone will still try to misread it.\"\n\nAira: \"By someone, she means Krail.\"\n\nRhea: \"Among others.\"\n\nShe laid Elira's corrected welfare form beside the ward case, the handwritten copy on top with **Alive. Irritated.** underlined hard enough to dent the page.\n\nRhea: \"And because Elira has already forced proximity review to admit its assumptions, this revision starts from her language, not his.\"\n\nElira looked down at the page. Something eased in her face that no hearing chamber would ever get to see, closer to pride than relief, or simply the quiet shock of one small answer surviving long enough to become structure.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nProfessor Valen smiled, brief and sharp.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Very good.\"\n\nI eyed the cedar latches.\n\nHaru: \"What is inside?\"\n\nProfessor Valen opened the case.\n\nSeven thin rings of pale metal rested in velvet grooves around a shallow crystal basin. Each ring was cut by a narrow gap so none of them formed a closed circle. Elira leaned in.\n\nElira: \"Witness Restoration geometry.\"\n\nProfessor Valen nodded.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"A training set, not a relic. It was built to teach why the old doctrine distributed burden through open consent rather than final sacrifice.\"\n\nCael's name went unspoken. His absence filled the room anyway.\n\nLeon arranged the stabilizing plates across the sitting room floor. Cassian shifted furniture with uncharacteristic care. Aira spread her notes beside the basin. Rhea marked the threshold. Maelin checked Elira's pulse, then checked mine, apparently deciding jurisdiction was whatever she could reach.\n\nMaelin: \"No heroics.\"\n\nElira: \"Agreed.\"\n\nMaelin turned to me.\n\nHaru: \"Agreed.\"\n\nAira: \"I want more conviction from the ancient catastrophe in the corner.\"\n\nHaru: \"No heroics.\"\n\nAira: \"Better.\"\n\nWe sat in a ring that refused to close, leaving wide gaps between us. No sealed containment. No single center except the basin, and that was only a tool.\n\nElira unclasped the pendant.\n\nThe air felt wrong the moment the crystal left her throat. I hated that reflex, and hated even more that part of me still trusted the ward after watching Dominion slip straight underneath it.\n\nElira set the dark crystal into the basin. It entered the water without a sound.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"State the flaw.\"\n\nI spoke first.\n\nHaru: \"The pendant blocked outside interference, shielded Elira, and warned me. Dominion Severance bypassed it by riding Cael's unlawful return claim and relabeling Elira's rescue as the active route. It went in beneath the ward as if her own choice opened the door.\"\n\nThe crystal darkened, holding the memory.\n\nElira laid her palm beside the basin.\n\nElira: \"The flaw is not that I stepped in.\"\n\nHer voice was low and steady. The seven metal rings warmed against the velvet.\n\nElira: \"The flaw is that protection had no way to reject a spell that stole the meaning of my choice after I made it.\"\n\nAira jotted fast, then looked up.\n\nAira: \"Sequence anchor: motion is not consent. Declared intent, witness context, and refusal of hostile relabeling stay attached to the act.\"\n\nThe first ring flared blue-white.\n\nRhea: \"Authority anchor: no outside doctrine may convert rescue into claim, possession, or substitution.\"\n\nThe second ring lit red-gold.\n\nMaelin: \"Medical anchor: her body, her recovery, and her fear are closed ground unless she grants direct, present consent.\"\n\nSoft green-blue warmth rose through the third ring.\n\nLeon shifted his shoulders once, then planted both hands on his knees.\n\nLeon: \"Stability anchor: if pressure spikes around her, I ground the floor first and hold fire.\"\n\nWater-light rippled along the fourth ring. Cassian bared his teeth in something too sharp for a smile.\n\nCassian: \"Combat anchor: anything that uses her mercy as an opening gets treated as hostile, regardless of how politely it introduces itself.\"\n\nFire crossed the fifth ring and settled to a steady ember.\n\nProfessor Valen touched two fingers to the sixth.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Historical anchor: fatal return is a corruption of the record, not a sacred form. Burden was meant to be shared.\"\n\nThe sixth ring shone silver. Only the seventh stayed dark. Every eye turned to me.\n\nUnder my ribs, the Black Circle waited. Old cold. Ozone. Iron. The quiet pull to step forward and become the only answer left standing.\n\nI set my hand beside Elira's, leaving an inch of air between us.\n\nHaru: \"Safeguard anchor: my power does not decide for her. It answers when called, stops when refused, and remains answerable to witnesses if grief ever tempts me to act alone.\"\n\nThe seventh ring stayed dark.\n\nFor one cold second, my chest hollowed out.\n\nThen Elira shifted her hand until her smallest finger hooked lightly against mine.\n\nElira: \"Personal anchor: I am Elira Vale. I am not a route, not proof that sacrifice is noble, and not a borrowed life paying off its return. I choose who stands beside me, when protection touches me, and how I love without surrendering my name.\"\n\nThe seventh ring ignited black and gold.\n\nThe pendant lifted an inch above the basin and turned slowly over the water. Seven fine threads of light drew into the dark crystal: blue-white, red-gold, green-blue, water, fire, silver, and the braided black-gold of Elira's choice beside my safeguard. Then the light sank inward and vanished.\n\nTo an outside eye, the crystal looked unchanged. Maelin's fingers were already at Elira's wrist.\n\nMaelin: \"Pulse elevated. Mana steady. No channel tearing.\"\n\nAira let out a breath hard enough to scatter her loose sheets.\n\nAira: \"Adding 'no channel tearing' to the list of romantic milestones I never expected to record.\"\n\nElira laughed, and the sound filled the timbered room.\n\nEveryone stayed for supper after Aira ruled that leaving immediately would be \"ominous and rude.\" Cassian cooked badly with vast confidence until Maelin stripped him of seasoning authority. Leon fixed the leaking kitchen tap without being asked. Rhea reviewed perimeter dispatches at the table and called it resting.\n\nWhen the dishes were cleared, Valen reached back into the cedar case and lifted out a thin pane of cloudy glass wrapped in blue cloth.\n\nValen: \"Before we go. This belongs to the house.\"\n\nElira leaned forward, and Maelin promptly slid a plate of bread under her nose.\n\nMaelin: \"Eat while looking.\"\n\nElira accepted the bread with a glance that promised future litigation.\n\nValen stood the glass upright on the table. At first it reflected only the crowded kitchen: spilled lamplight, tired faces, my black coat against dark oak. Then the surface shifted. Our kitchen remained, yet older figures settled into the chairs around us.\n\nNot ghosts. More like an imprint of how these walls had once been used.\n\nAn injured woman sat wrapped in a shawl while witnesses argued around her without treating her like fragile glass. A child slept curled in a corner chair. A healer wrote notes by tallow light. Away from the head of the table sat a man with black-silver mana humming quietly at his wrist.\n\nMy breath caught. The face was a stranger's, yet the careful distance in his posture hit close enough to ache.\n\nValen: \"Lake Velis was a witness convalescence house before Circle standardization. People came here after dangerous workings to recover without being locked away.\"\n\nRhea leaned closer, eyes narrowing on the faint shapes.\n\nRhea: \"So recovery under chosen proximity is older than Krail's precedent.\"\n\nValen: \"Much older.\"\n\nIn the pane, the black-silver man reached out to move the injured woman's teacup closer. She rapped his knuckles with a wooden spoon before his fingers touched the handle. Every witness at that old table laughed.\n\nElira stared at the glass. Slowly, her mouth curved.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good?\"\n\nElira: \"Historical proof that overprotective men have always needed correction.\"\n\nCassian coughed into his fist. Leon developed a sudden fascination with the ceiling beams. Aira wrote at dangerous speed.\n\nThe reflection faded, leaving two lines of worn script etched across the clouding glass, half eaten away by time. Valen translated softly.\n\nValen: \"Chosen presence was permitted while refusal remained possible. Two clauses are missing beneath it. Treat it as precedent, not a blank shield.\"\n\nSilence held the table for a beat.\n\nRhea pulled a fresh sheet toward her.\n\nRhea: \"That enters our objection as historical practice.\"\n\nMaelin: \"And into the medical brief.\"\n\nAira: \"And into my notes with three underlines and a sketch of a spoon.\"\n\nElira looked at me across the table, eyes bright without tipping into triumph.\n\nElira: \"We are not inventing this out of thin air.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nValen wrapped the glass back in blue cloth and locked the cedar case.\n\nValen: \"We will still need Halden and every uncomfortable witness willing to prove Krail is repeating an old mistake.\"\n\nLeon folded his arms.\n\nLeon: \"I hate when history has tactics.\"\n\nCassian: \"Everything has tactics if you are stubborn enough.\"\n\nMaelin checked Elira's pulse one final time, and her scowl softened by a fraction.\n\nMaelin: \"Better than expected.\"\n\nElira: \"Because I ate bread?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Because you ate bread, sat down before collapsing, and let other people carry weight without treating it as a verdict on your strength.\"\n\nElira blinked.\n\nMaelin: \"Do not look so shocked. Healers occasionally notice effort.\"\n\nAira whispered: \"Write that down.\"\n\nRhea: \"I heard you.\"\n\nWhen they finally left for Aurelis, Aira hugged Elira longer than usual.\n\nAira: \"You are allowed to be scared tomorrow too.\"\n\nElira held on.\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nAira: \"Good. I am deeply wise.\"\n\nElira: \"You are very loud.\"\n\nAira: \"Often the same thing.\"\n\nLeon clasped my shoulder once and kept his peace, which said more than a speech. Cassian tied a second ribbon around the ward case handle \"for morale.\" Rhea paused at the threshold, pulling her cloak straight.\n\nRhea: \"Krail will hate this revision.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because it makes containment harder?\"\n\nRhea: \"Because it makes personhood harder to argue around.\"\n\nProfessor Valen smiled at the door.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"A useful inconvenience.\"\n\nWait until morning, Aira had murmured near the pantry while pretending to inspect the kettle, and none of us stopped her. Then the gate shut behind them, leaving lake wind, low lamplight, and an uneasy quiet that finally felt like ours.\n\n---\n\n## Interlude: Elira - The Page That Was Mine\n\nI woke before the lake house did.\n\nAt least it felt that way until the pipes clicked once against the pre-dawn chill and a floorboard settled under my heel as if greeting an early guest. Before sunrise, the rooms belonged to me. No Council mirror demanding posture. No healer slate counting breaths. Just gray water beyond the glass and my Yellow Circle resting under my skin like an unfinished sentence.\n\nI sat up slowly. My ribs protested. I let them. Pain had turned into a tedious clerk sending predictable dispatches, and Maelin was not in the room to scold me for ignoring it before breakfast.\n\nOn the small desk by the window, three sheets lay pinned beneath a blue paperweight shaped like a fish. Aira had left the painted fish yesterday, claiming it was a legal assistant; its glass eye looked sharper than half the clerks in Valemere.\n\nThe first sheet held Senna's summary of Krail's proximity argument. The second held Rhea's amendment in crisp red ink. The third was blank.\n\nI had asked for it last night. Rhea had hesitated, Maelin had objected, and Aira had settled the matter by warning them I would write six pages in my head if they denied me paper.\n\nI swung my legs out and stood. The floorboards warmed slightly under my bare feet.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nThe desk lamp brightened a notch in answer.\n\nDressing alone took effort. I had to sit twice and brace a hand against the wardrobe when the edges of the room grayed out, yet I managed shirt, skirt, shawl, and hair pinned back with more stubbornness than grace. By the time I reached the chair, my fingers were trembling. I folded my hands in my lap until they steadied enough to hold a pen.\n\nPeople had spent my whole life turning me into a sentence they had already written. Yellow Circle prodigy. Vale heir. Tournament standard. Dead girl returned.\n\nThis morning, I meant to write one first.\n\nI started cleanly.\n\n**Elira Vale's consent cannot be inferred from proximity, rescue, trauma, affection, or survival.**\n\nToo polished. A Council aide could admire the cadence and miss the human being inside it. I drew a line through it.\n\n**I am not evidence of Haru Aerlen's influence.**\n\nMy pen stopped. There he was anyway, standing at the center of the page. Krail's entire brief wanted every corridor to open toward Haru; if I answered from inside his hallway, I had already conceded the map. I set that sheet aside and took a fresh one.\n\n**I am Elira Vale.**\n\nToo grand. Too easy to fold into a banner. I crossed it out as well.\n\nIn the dark windowpane, my reflection looked pale and sharp-eyed. Beneath my collar, the redesigned pendant lay quiet, its seven open threads listening without pulling.\n\nMy Yellow Circle stirred. I raised my palm and spun a single thread of gold into the air.\n\nEvery Academy drill began with symmetry: clean radius, even pressure, a ring smooth enough to reassure distant judges before anyone asked what the spell was meant to do. At nine, when my first training ring held longer than the instructor's model, three adults had exchanged looks over my head as if my future were a house they had just bought. At twelve, a tournament steward praised my presentation discipline while my mother's fingers tightened on my shoulder. At fourteen, after Lucien's junior match left Talen Mire's hand shaking, the medical ward asked me to hold a counter-ring purely for the record.\n\nThe record loved clean rings. It never knew what to do with anger.\n\nI pushed my mana out of balance until the left arc sagged and the right flared ragged and bright. The spell fought me, pulling toward neatness like a habit in the bone. I held it crooked anyway. Ugly. Uneven. Mine.\n\nUnder that uneven gold light, the ink I had crossed out gleamed faintly through the black strokes. All true. None sufficient.\n\nA knock sounded at the door. I closed my hand and let the gold vanish.\n\nElira: \"Come in.\"\n\nMira Castellan stepped inside with a bundle of copied notices hugged against her chest.\n\nFor half a heartbeat I mistook her for Aira, though they looked nothing alike. Mira was smaller, quieter, auburn curls pinned haphazardly and ink smudged along her thumb. Yet she wore the exact set of jaw Aira got whenever a neat theory collided with real people.\n\nMira: \"President Valmont said you asked for the Green witness copies.\"\n\nElira: \"I did.\"\n\nMira's glance touched my crossed-out drafts and moved politely on. She laid the stack beside the fish paperweight: seating charts, corridor notes, refusal slips, small ordinary details too unglamorous for Council rhetoric.\n\nOne Green first-year had written: **Lady Vale told Professor Halden no before Haru said a word.**\n\nAnother: **She asked for water herself. I know because I was holding the pitcher and almost dropped it.**\n\nAn unsigned scrap read only: **She looked angry, not enchanted.**\n\nI stared at that line longer than I meant to. Mira noticed.\n\nMira: \"It is inelegant.\"\n\nElira: \"It is useful.\"\n\nMira: \"Useful things usually are. That has been my whole week.\"\n\nI smiled, and my ribs hurt less than yesterday.\n\nElira: \"I wanted to ask whether any of these students would agree to attach their names.\"\n\nMira's fingers tightened on the bundle.\n\nElira: \"Only voluntarily. Zero pressure.\"\n\nMira: \"I know.\"\n\nHer voice dropped half a register. I caught it a beat too late. No mana spike, no ward warning, just ink-stained knuckles pressing paper too close to her ribs. I had looked at Mira as an organizer and assumed she wanted a sharper blade.\n\nMira looked down at the unsigned scrap.\n\nMira: \"Some of them wrote because nobody asked them to be brave in public.\"\n\nThe room tilted quiet around the sentence. I set my pen down.\n\nElira: \"I am sorry.\"\n\nMira blinked fast.\n\nMira: \"You did not order it.\"\n\nElira: \"I still asked the wrong question.\"\n\nThe desk lamp dipped a shade, as if agreeing. Mira's mouth twitched toward a smile.\n\nMira: \"Aira says apologies need a procedural fix to survive contact with reality.\"\n\nElira: \"Aira says many things.\"\n\nMira: \"Mostly while writing.\"\n\nI turned the unsigned note toward her.\n\nElira: \"Then here is the fix. No names requested. Anonymous testimony stays anonymous unless a student offers a signature unprompted.\"\n\nMira's shoulders dropped an inch, betraying how tight she had been holding them.\n\nMira: \"That weakens the filing.\"\n\nElira: \"Does it?\"\n\nMira took her time. Unlike Aira, who struck sparks off three arguments before breakfast, Mira let thoughts settle until they carried weight.\n\nMira: \"No. It makes a different kind of record.\"\n\nElira: \"One Krail cannot call coerced.\"\n\nMira: \"One the students can survive.\"\n\nWe worked for an hour, and I let her lead. That stung my pride more than I expected.\n\nMira tore slips of scrap paper into four headings:\n\n**SAW NO COMMAND**\n\n**SAW REFUSAL**\n\n**SAW CARE WITHOUT DEBT**\n\n**SAW FEAR REMAINING**\n\nI frowned at the fourth heading.\n\nElira: \"Keeping fear on the page gives Krail ammunition.\"\n\nMira slid the unsigned anger note under the fourth slip.\n\nMira: \"If everyone around you looks fearless, he will argue their fear was erased.\"\n\nCold logic, sharp enough to cut clean. I picked up a slanted, hurried page from a first-year: **I stayed because Mira stayed. I was scared because he is Black Circle. I was more scared when Councilor Krail said that meant I could not know my own mind.**\n\nMy hand hovered over Care Without Debt. Mira watched without speaking. I laid it under Fear Remaining. She gave a single nod.\n\nOur next argument went harder. I wrote **OBSERVED INDEPENDENT ACTION** across a clean header. Mira read it upside down and scowled.\n\nMira: \"Independent from whom?\"\n\nElira: \"From Haru.\"\n\nMira: \"Then you just put him back at the center of the page.\"\n\nI hated that she saw it before I did. She took a fresh slip and printed slowly: **ACTION NAMED BY ACTOR**.\n\nElira: \"Less elegant.\"\n\nMira: \"Clearer.\"\n\nHer pencil bit dark into the grain.\n\nElira: \"Better, too.\"\n\nThe painted fish disappeared under dead headings. **Observed refusal** became **refusal completed before response** because timing proved witnesses were not afterthoughts. **Care without debt** became **aid with exit preserved** after Mira showed me a note from Pella, a Green kitchen runner who wanted her two-copper contribution returned if anyone claimed two copper bought her loyalty.\n\nI read Pella's line three times. Above my discarded drafts, a phantom ring of gold trembled once in the air. My first page had defended my choice. Mira's structure shielded the people who had stood beside it.\n\nI pulled the next sheaf closer.\n\nElira: \"Again. Show me the next place I am dressing the record up.\"\n\nShe found Finn's receipt bundle, three crossed-out oil totals and a margin scrawl: **If the Council claims we were enchanted, ask why enchantment made us keep receipts.**\n\nA short, rough laugh escaped me before I could stop it.\n\nElira: \"Sorry.\"\n\nMira: \"It is funny.\"\n\nElira: \"It is pure Finn.\"\n\nMira: \"He asked if sarcasm counts as fiscal evidence.\"\n\nElira: \"Does it?\"\n\nMira weighed the question seriously.\n\nMira: \"If Aira indexes it.\"\n\nWe wrote that down too.\n\nThen I used the word *bravery*, and Mira struck a hard pencil line straight through it.\n\nElira: \"You dislike bravery?\"\n\nMira: \"I dislike adults calling it bravery when they do not want to admit students were terrified.\"\n\nHer words landed like a mug set down hard enough to crack glaze.\n\nElira: \"What word, then?\"\n\nMira thumbed through three sheets: a Green boy staying in the yard because his sister watched, a Blue junior asking if lending a heat charm meant a summons, a Red student admitting Haru scared him while Krail's certainty scared him worse.\n\nMira: \"Stayed.\"\n\nElira: \"Stayed?\"\n\nMira: \"Bravery is a medal people pin on afterward. Stayed is what happened.\"\n\nI crossed out *bravery* and wrote *stayed*. Plain. Unbreakable.\n\nBy then my wrist throbbed. I set the pen down before pain turned into posture. Mira noticed without remarking on it, slid the blue fish onto the next stack, and spoke deadpan.\n\nMira: \"Legal counsel recommends a recess.\"\n\nElira: \"Does it.\"\n\nMira: \"The fish is famous for procedural mercy.\"\n\nI laughed again, easier this time, and let the seven-minute hourglass she quietly upended run out while we ate half a loaf of bread and drank lukewarm tea she spilled into the saucer.\n\nMira stared at the puddle.\n\nMira: \"Aira would call that an assault on the furniture.\"\n\nElira: \"She would be wrong.\"\n\nMira: \"Would she?\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe hourglass emptied. Neither of us reached for a pen right away. For seven minutes nobody had measured whether rest was tactical, and the sore knot in my wrist loosened back into ordinary fatigue.\n\nWhen I lifted the pen again, Mira stopped me.\n\nMira: \"You stop before your slant changes.\"\n\nI looked down. My last three lines leaned sharper to the right than the top of the page.\n\nElira: \"You caught that?\"\n\nMira: \"You asked where the page got too neat. Sometimes it gets neat because the writer is trying not to be a patient.\"\n\nI set the pen down, then slid it across the blotter to Mira's side.\n\nElira: \"Then you draft the witness-protection section.\"\n\nMira stared at the pen like a loaded crossbow.\n\nMira: \"This is your statement.\"\n\nElira: \"I will approve or strike every line. You write the parts that guard the students.\"\n\nMira: \"Because I argued with you?\"\n\nElira: \"Because you are better at it.\"\n\nMira's hand trembled once as she took the pen. I turned my gaze politely toward the lake until her grip steadied.\n\nAt midmorning, Rhea's mirror seal chimed on the desk. She appeared in Council red, hair pinned severe, looking awake through sheer spite.\n\nRhea: \"Tell me you ate.\"\n\nElira: \"Good morning to you too.\"\n\nRhea: \"That is not calories.\"\n\nMira silently slid the bread plate beside my elbow. Traitor.\n\nRhea: \"Thank you, Castellan.\"\n\nMira: \"I am not deputized for medical enforcement.\"\n\nRhea: \"Everyone says that right before they are. Senna needs your language by eleven.\"\n\nElira: \"We changed the structure. No named Green escalation. Anonymous slips stay sealed unless volunteered, and categories track observed action rather than character testimonials.\"\n\nRhea looked between us.\n\nRhea: \"That reads softer in committee.\"\n\nMira: \"It holds longer afterward.\"\n\nRhea went still.\n\nElira: \"Explain it to her, Mira.\"\n\nQuietly, without flourish, Mira laid out why Green students could not be fed into a public hearing as ammunition every time high-ranked mages fought each other, and how burning witnesses proved Krail's premise faster than Noctis ever could.\n\nRhea listened, then gave a crisp nod.\n\nRhea: \"Accepted. I will argue admissibility on protected student status rather than individual naming. Vale? Good correction.\"\n\nPraise without cushion. The mirror dimmed.\n\nMira gathered her copies and paused at the door, thumb digging into the stack.\n\nMira: \"Lady Vale?\"\n\nElira: \"Elira is fine.\"\n\nMira looked doubtful, and I let her keep the doubt; names were not gifts if forced fast.\n\nMira: \"The unsigned note about looking angry.\"\n\nElira: \"Yes?\"\n\nMira: \"That was mine.\"\n\nShe slipped out and shut the door softly before I could answer.\n\nI sat alone as eleven approached. Touching the margin of my rejected drafts woke the faint warmth of the earlier ring. I wrote the final opening on a clean sheet:\n\n**This record preserves student observations without converting students into instruments of my defense. My choices are mine. Their witness is theirs. Neither fact authorizes ownership.**\n\nNot lyrical. Load-bearing.\n\nAt eleven, I sealed the packet in gold under my own name and sent it through the mirror to Rhea and Senna. No one stood behind my chair. No instant reply came back to claim credit or soften the risk.\n\nI lifted my hand above the desk and let my Yellow Circle rise, refusing the circle altogether. Seven small sparks of gold drifted free over the blotter, uneven in size and brightness: one by the window, one above Mira's unsigned slip, one bobbing gently over the painted fish.\n\nDownstairs, the kettle clicked and Haru's light footsteps crossed the floorboards. Out on the lake, morning mist broke into gray and silver. I held the seven uneven lights a breath longer just because I wanted them there, then closed my hand and let the room return to daylight.\n\n---\n\nDownstairs, I waited by the stove with two steaming mugs and a folded square of paper beside the bread tin.\n\nElira came down in her shawl, the new pendant resting dark and steady against her throat. She saw the note first and picked it up.\n\n**If both of you are reading this, excellent. Eat. If only Haru is reading this, feed Elira. If only Elira is reading this, feed Haru. If neither of you is reading this, I have failed both logistics and friendship.**\n\nBelow Aira's scrawl ran Rhea's neat ink: **Do not answer unscheduled Council contact without a witness present.**\n\nAnd Maelin's blunt block print at the bottom: **Do not confuse being alive with being cleared for foolishness.**\n\nElira read all three aloud with solemn dignity, then folded the paper beside her plate.\n\nElira: \"I feel aggressively supervised.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because you are loved.\"\n\nThe words left me before I could edit them into safer phrasing. She paused with her teacup halfway to her mouth, looking over the porcelain rim until the quiet kitchen shifted around the look.\n\nElira: \"Say that again.\"\n\nMy throat tightened, not from fear, simply from how naked plain words sounded after years of hiding behind danger.\n\nHaru: \"You are loved.\"\n\nHer fingers tightened around the warm cup.\n\nElira: \"So are you.\"\n\nThe dark crystal at her throat warmed once in quiet answer. For the first time since the Wounded City, I sat down and ate without measuring how fast I could clear the table.\n\nWe were halfway through breakfast when the mirror on the kitchen wall chimed. Twice.\n\nThe house wards did not open the glass automatically; they waited for Elira's signal. She touched two fingers to the crystal.\n\nElira: \"Source?\"\n\nPale script surfaced across the glass: **AURELIS STUDENT COUNCIL RELAY / RHEA VALMONT / VERIFIED**.\n\nElira glanced at me, including me without asking permission.\n\nElira: \"Open.\"\n\nRhea appeared in the frame, sleepless and flanked by wall-high stacks of files. Aira leaned into view from the left.\n\nAira: \"You both look suspiciously functional.\"\n\nElira shut her eyes for a beat.\n\nRhea: \"Aira.\"\n\nAira: \"That was a medical check.\"\n\nLeon crossed behind her with sealed water plates.\n\nLeon: \"It was not.\"\n\nCassian called from off-screen: \"It was definitely a medical check.\"\n\nRhea pinched the bridge of her nose, and the red Council seal blinked at the edge of the glass.\n\nRhea: \"Ignore them. We have a problem. Krail's office just filed a supplemental demand for recovery-leave proximity logs.\"\n\nElira's face went still. Off to the side, Aira stopped smiling.\n\nHaru: \"What can he legally demand?\"\n\nRhea: \"Medical intervals, witness presence, ward alerts, Black Circle instability flags. Not private conduct.\"\n\nElira: \"And politically?\"\n\nRhea did not rush the answer.\n\nRhea: \"Politically, he will argue that every unrecorded hour is where coercive influence lives.\"\n\nAnger rose cleanly behind my ribs, separate from the Black Circle yet sharp enough to bite. Right across the open oak in full view of the mirror, Elira laid her hand over mine.\n\nLeon looked studiously at the ceiling. Cassian muttered something approving out of frame.\n\nElira: \"Then we do not let implication write the record.\"\n\nRhea: \"Meaning?\"\n\nHaru: \"We answer what falls under Council jurisdiction, and refuse what does not.\"\n\nElira laced her fingers through mine.\n\nElira: \"Together.\"\n\nRhea studied us for two long seconds, then nodded just as the mirror chimed a third time, dropping the kitchen lamp a shade lower.\n\nRhea turned away from her desk to patch in the incoming seal. Pale silver bordered the glass, carrying Magistra Senna Voss' mark.\n\nSenna's relay opened without chamber walls or spectators, showing only her gray-sleeved shoulder, Rhea's witness seal in the corner, and a Valemere clerk's poised stylus. Krail's attached containment aide tried to flicker onto the side channel.\n\nSenna: \"State your boundary before Krail's office drafts one for you.\"\n\nElira finished her tea before answering. That small refusal to let panic spoil warm bread steadied the room better than a ward.\n\nElira: \"I will answer medical questions. Whether Haru's presence helps or harms my recovery, whether I requested it, whether I can refuse touch, and whether hostile doctrine tried to hijack my consent.\"\n\nThe clerk's stylus scratched across vellum. Elira pressed her palm gently over the pendant.\n\nElira: \"I will not submit an inventory of affection. Touch, sleep, clothing, private fear, and comfort remain private unless I judge a detail medically relevant.\"\n\nNo one softened a syllable.\n\nSenna: \"Boundary entered into the record.\"\n\nRhea's witness seal flashed crimson, and Krail's silent containment aide snapped off the channel before he could open his mouth. On the Aurelis side of the glass, Aira drew one sharp ink line under the heading on her copy; here at the kitchen table, Elira marked her own summary sheet with a decisive stroke and set the pen down.\n\nElira: \"Mine.\"\n\nThe link closed, leaving only warm tea, the quiet lake beyond the panes, and the strange steadiness of having drawn a line before dark.\n\n---\n\nNight returned Lake Velis to black water and pale moonlight.\n\nElira stood by the sitting room window with the remade pendant resting against her collarbone. I came up beside her, close enough to share the draft off the glass.\n\nHaru: \"How does it feel now?\"\n\nElira touched the dark stone lightly.\n\nElira: \"Less like a shield someone strapped onto me. More like a promise I helped write.\"\n\nSomething locked behind my ribs loosened at the words. Outside, moonlight laid a silver track across the water. Elira watched it for a long moment.\n\nElira: \"Do you remember the vow in the Moonlit Archive?\"\n\nHaru: \"Every word.\"\n\nElira: \"We spoke it before anyone knew. Before Lucien pushed this far. Before Cael. Before death.\"\n\nShe turned away from the glass to face me.\n\nElira: \"I want another one.\"\n\nThe Black Circle went dead quiet beneath my skin.\n\nHaru: \"Now?\"\n\nElira: \"Now.\"\n\nWe walked down to the dock with a single lantern between us. Damp planks smelled of rain and cedar; her fingers held mine warm and steady in the cool air. At the end of the dock she stopped and faced me under the moon. No tribunal. No relay. No witnesses except the water and the life we had fought to keep from becoming someone else's exhibit.\n\nElira: \"The first vow guarded your secret.\"\n\nHaru: \"And your trust.\"\n\nElira: \"This one is for how we live now.\"\n\nMy throat closed around the shape of it. Not my danger alone. Not her recovery alone.\n\nElira: \"I choose you after seeing what the world can break in us. I choose you without pretending love makes either of us safe. When I am tired, angry, or frightened for reasons that still hurt, I choose Haru: not a weapon, not a wall, not an answer to every storm.\"\n\nHer fingers shook against mine, and she let them shake without pulling back.\n\nElira: \"Choose me the same way. Not as the girl you failed to catch. Not as proof you have kept control. Just Elira.\"\n\nMoonlight caught the pendant in black, gold, and silver. I had to steady my breath twice before I could speak.\n\nHaru: \"I choose you after death tried to turn love into a weapon. I choose you without asking you to be fearless so I can feel forgiven. I choose your temper, your terrible mornings, your slander against my braiding, the nights when thunder sounds like relays, and your hand whether it reaches for mine or tells me to stop.\"\n\nHer eyes shone in the lantern light. Mine burned too.\n\nHaru: \"I will guard you when you ask for guard, and listen when you tell me to stand down. I will not put the Black Circle between us, and I will not love you less because loving you scares me. I will stay answerable.\"\n\nLake water lapped softly under the boards.\n\nElira: \"Then stay.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will.\"\n\nElira: \"Not as a guard.\"\n\nHaru: \"Beside you.\"\n\nHer breath caught.\n\nElira: \"Then I stand beside you too.\"\n\nNo sigil flared under our shoes. No ancient voice sealed the air. We kissed under the open moon with cold water moving beneath the dock and the Council waiting past the treeline, and for the first time since the Wounded City, tomorrow felt less like an ambush we had survived into and more like a life we were allowed to want.\n\nWhen we carried the lantern back inside, a sealed notice waited in the sitting room mirror under Rhea's crest and Senna's countermark.\n\n**DISTRIBUTED WITNESS REVIEW: SECOND HEARING DATE SET**\n\n**SUBJECTS: BLACK CIRCLE STABILITY, DOMINION SEVERANCE LIABILITY, NOCTIS STUDENT PROTECTION, PROXIMITY WITNESS VALIDITY**\n\nBeneath the formal lines, Aira had somehow smuggled one smaller line of ink:\n\n**SLEEP FIRST. WORLD LATER.**\n\nElira let out a soft laugh, then caught my hand and led me upstairs.\n\nAt the bedroom door, she stopped, not from fear, simply to mark that the threshold was hers to cross.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI looked down at our joined hands before raising my eyes to hers.\n\nHaru: \"Tell me.\"\n\nColor warmed her cheeks, though her gaze never wavered.\n\nElira: \"Stay with me tonight.\"\n\nMy breath caught in my chest.\n\nHaru: \"Only if you are sure.\"\n\nElira stepped in until the pendant brushed the black ribbon tied around my wrist.\n\nElira: \"I died with my choice stolen out of my hands. Tonight, I want this choice to be mine.\"\n\nBeneath my skin, the Black Circle went utterly still, listening. I lifted a hand to her cheek, and she leaned into my palm before I could ask whether I was permitted.\n\nElira: \"You do not have to be afraid of wanting me.\"\n\nMy voice dropped nearly to a whisper.\n\nHaru: \"I am afraid of hurting you.\"\n\nElira: \"Then listen to me.\"\n\nHaru: \"Always.\"\n\nShe kissed me softly once, then not softly at all.\n\nThe second kiss shattered a week of careful distance. Elira rose onto her toes, fingers fisting in the front of my shirt, and pulled me down like someone done asking the world's leave to want anything. Heat flashed through me so fast the bedside lamp flame shivered; I reined the mana back before it could answer.\n\nShe felt the restraint and kissed me harder.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nMy name sounded different on her mouth, stripped of warning and sharp with wanting. I eased her back against the bedroom door, bracing one palm beside her temple and the other at her waist, halting the instant her breath hitched.\n\nHaru: \"Too much?\"\n\nShe pulled me straight back in.\n\nElira: \"Not enough.\"\n\nI kissed her slower then, memorizing the warmth of her skin, her hair slipping loose across my fingers, the dark pendant pressing cool between us before warming to her pulse. She laughed breathlessly against my mouth.\n\nElira: \"Even here, you are thinking.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am trying very hard to stop.\"\n\nElira: \"Let me help.\"\n\nThen her injured shoulder gave a faint tremor. She tried to hide it by kissing me again, and I caught the shift anyway.\n\nElira: \"Do not look triumphant.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am not triumphant.\"\n\nElira: \"You noticed before I confessed. That is your version of triumph.\"\n\nHaru: \"My version is mostly quiet panic.\"\n\nHer laugh broke wide enough that she lost her footing. I lunged to catch her, she grabbed my collar, and for one graceless second we nearly went down in a tangle on the floorboards.\n\nWe got under the blankets still dressed for sleep because she asked and because I wanted nothing more than to stay. We kissed until wanting stopped feeling like another emergency, then talked in the dark about nothing the Council could ever subpoena: the terrible painted fish, Cassian's ribbons, whether Aira had ever obeyed a page limit in her life, and Maelin's exact face if either of us skipped breakfast.\n\nJust before sleep took her, condensation crept across the cool bedroom glass in seven thin branching arcs that curved toward each other without closing, splitting at the center into two small points: one black, one gold.\n\n**CONSENT ANCHOR STABLE UNDER PRIVATE PROXIMITY**\n\n**EXTERNAL REVIEW PRESSURE EXPECTED**\n\nElira opened one tired gold eye, read the faint pale script over my shoulder, and let out a low, unimpressed laugh against my collarbone.\n\nElira: \"The house has terrible timing.\"\n\nHaru: \"At least it waited until midnight.\"\n\nElira: \"Barely.\"\n\nThe pale letters faded back into plain mist on the glass before dawn could claim them, and moments later Elira drifted off with one fist curled tight in the fabric of my shirt.\n\n## Coda: Seven Uneven Lights\n\nEarlier, before sleep claimed the house completely, Elira had left seven small lights hovering above her bedroom desk.\n\nThey burned uneven and stubborn in the dark, each one a consent line that had refused to turn into a leash. Old magic prized symmetry; Councils prized signatures; Noctis prized obedience dressed up as clarity. The lights simply stayed lit.\n\nHaru stood quietly in the doorway, watching her profile against the pale lake window. He did not ask whether she was exhausted, or whether remaking the vow had hurt. It had, and some questions only pressured people into performing endurance.\n\nElira pointed at the smallest spark, where a ragged edge of green flickered through the gold.\n\nElira: \"This one is ugly.\"\n\nHaru: \"It answered anyway.\"\n\nElira turned her head toward him. Out beyond the panes, Lake Velis moved softly against the dark shore, and inside the quiet room the smallest spark steadied.\n\nElira: \"Then we keep ugly answers too.\"\n\nThe Black Circle under Haru's skin pulsed once, quiet as agreement and nowhere near command.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-24-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep25","label":"Episode 19","number":"19","file":"episode-25-cover.webp","fallback":"Wounded City Protocol","eyebrow":"Wounded City","recap":"","accent":["#e5e7eb","#111111","#facc15"],"displayLabel":"Episode 19","displayNumber":"19","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"The First Hearing","body":"The morning after the vow did not feel like morning.\n\nLight entered the Lake Velis bedroom in long pale bands. The lake outside lay quiet enough to look like a promise. White curtains stirred in the window breeze, and somewhere in the kitchen, the old house kettle clicked once before settling back down.\n\nElira slept with one hand tangled in my shirt. Not the stolen one. Mine.\n\nThe distinction mattered less than it should have.\n\nFor a while, I stayed still and let myself count nothing: not breaths, not pulse, not window wards. At her throat, the new pendant crystal rested dark and calm, warm from her skin. Seven open consent lines now nested inside it instead of one closed protection route.\n\nIf Dominion tried to call her rescue an entry again, it would have to pass through questions first. Who is choosing? What is being saved?\n\nWho is being named as cost?\n\nIs Haru acting as protector, owner, or witness?\n\nThe last question had been Professor Valen's addition. I hated it.\n\nI had agreed to it anyway.\n\nElira's fingers tightened before her eyes opened.\n\nHaru: \"I'm here.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHer voice was rough with sleep.\n\nThen she looked at me, really looked, and the memory of the night before passed between us without needing words. Her cheeks warmed. Mine probably did too, though I refused to offer evidence.\n\nElira: \"You are thinking very loudly.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am thinking carefully.\"\n\nElira: \"That is the same thing when you do it.\"\n\nI looked toward the window.\n\nShe laughed softly and touched my jaw with the backs of her fingers.\n\nElira: \"No regret.\"\n\nThe words struck deeper than I expected.\n\nHaru: \"No regret?\"\n\nElira: \"None.\"\n\nThe Black Circle did not stir. It listened.\n\nElira saw the relief I failed to hide. Her expression softened, then sharpened with the old Elira Vale precision that had survived death, grief, fear, and my worst instinct to become a wall.\n\nElira: \"Do not make my certainty into something you must earn every morning.\"\n\nI closed my eyes.\n\nHaru: \"I will try.\"\n\nElira: \"No. You will practice.\"\n\nHarder. And truer.\n\nThe mirror on the far wall chimed once. Neither of us moved.\n\nIt chimed again. Elira sighed.\n\nElira: \"If that is Aira, she gets one joke before I end the call.\"\n\nThe mirror brightened.\n\nMagistra Senna Voss appeared instead, framed by the clean black-glass lines of an Inter-National Schools Council hearing chamber. Her silver-gray robe was fastened at the throat with a narrow white seal. Behind her, three floating transcript crystals turned slowly above an empty witness rail.\n\nSenna: \"Good morning. I apologize for the early contact.\"\n\nElira pulled the blanket higher with one hand.\n\nI propped myself up beside her, careful to keep my shoulder clear of the mirror's line. She caught the habit anyway. One eyebrow lifted, and I shifted farther aside.\n\nSenna noticed too, because Senna noticed everything and filed it away in polite grammar.\n\nSenna: \"The second distributed witness review has been advanced.\"\n\nThe room lost warmth. Elira sat upright.\n\nElira: \"Advanced by whom?\"\n\nSenna: \"Councilor Krail's minority bloc filed an emergency petition during the night. The petition argues that Haru Aerlen's private proximity to you after the Black Circle manifestation constitutes unsupervised extra-classification influence over a critical witness.\"\n\nThe old cold pressure gathered behind my sternum. Ozone. Metal.\n\nThe house wards did not flare. The crystal did not chill. Only me.\n\nHaru: \"They are turning recovery into contamination.\"\n\nSenna: \"That is the exact framing.\"\n\nShe scored a sharp ink line down the folio braced against her arm, softening nothing.\n\nElira's hand found mine under the blanket. Her grip was steady.\n\nElira: \"And our answer?\"\n\nSenna: \"Aurelis has already rejected the premise. Professor Valen, Senior Healer Orr, President Valmont, and the registered witnesses submitted a joint medical-protective objection. However, Krail has secured enough signatures to require a public preliminary session.\"\n\nElira: \"When?\"\n\nSenna: \"At noon.\"\n\nThe old house kettle clicked again in the kitchen.\n\nThis time the sound felt absurd enough that Elira almost laughed. Almost.\n\nSenna looked directly at me.\n\nSenna: \"Mr. Aerlen, the petition requests three immediate restrictions pending full review. First, your physical separation from Elira Vale outside supervised medical conditions. Second, a prohibition against unscheduled Black Circle manifestation. Third, compulsory demonstration of the Black Circle's limits before a Council containment panel.\"\n\nThe room went very quiet.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe word left me before I could dress it up.\n\nSenna: \"That is also my legal reading.\"\n\nElira's thumb moved once across my knuckles.\n\nElira: \"He cannot demonstrate a thing designed around restraint without being pressured into the exact failure they claim to fear.\"\n\nSenna: \"That is the argument I intend to preserve.\"\n\nHaru: \"Preserve?\"\n\nSenna's eyes narrowed in quiet warning.\n\nSenna: \"Council proceedings have begun receiving external doctrine packets from institutions not named as parties. Noctis Dominion College is formally under investigation and cannot participate directly. Someone is trying to continue the argument through neutral schools, private donors, and rank-security associations.\"\n\nLucien.\n\nI did not say the name aloud. The mirror answered anyway.\n\nFor one heartbeat, Senna's reflection fractured. Black frost drew a smile across the glass where no mouth belonged. Then the fracture vanished. Senna turned her head as someone off-screen shouted.\n\nThe transcript crystals behind her dimmed from white to gray.\n\nSenna: \"This line is being tested. I am ending contact. Do not travel alone. Do not answer any summons not sealed by Aurelis and my office together.\"\n\nElira: \"Magistra-\"\n\nThe mirror went dark.\n\nFor three breaths, the lake house held its silence.\n\nThen Aira's voice came from the hallway.\n\nAira: \"I came with breakfast and political dread.\"\n\nThe bedroom door opened exactly two inches. Aira did not look inside.\n\nAira: \"Are you both dressed enough for bread?\"\n\nElira pressed her face into her hands.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nAira: \"Terrible answer. I will leave bread outside the door like a civilized friend.\"\n\nLeon: \"You said you would not mention the door.\"\n\nAira: \"I did not mention anything specific about the door.\"\n\nCassian: \"This is the loudest discretion I have ever witnessed.\"\n\nRhea's voice cut through the hall.\n\nRhea: \"Everyone downstairs.\"\n\nThat did it.\n\nElira threw a pillow at the door. It struck the wood with enough force to satisfy nobody.\n\nBy the time we dressed and reached the sitting room, the restoration house had stopped pretending to be peaceful.\n\nRhea had turned the largest table into a field office. Council notices hovered over it in separate stacks. Aira had arranged breakfast by tactical importance: tea near Elira, bread near me, documents near herself, and one plate of honey buns in the middle as morale or bribery.\n\nLeon stood at the window with crossed arms, watching the road through a water-thin reflection. Cassian sat backward on a chair, outwardly relaxed, one hand near an unfinished red sigil suspended in the air. Professor Valen read Senna's relayed dispatch with a scholar's calm and a soldier's jaw. Maelin counted Elira's pulse, then mine, then looked offended by both.\n\nMaelin: \"No fever. Elevated stress. Predictable foolishness approaching.\"\n\nHaru: \"The Council?\"\n\nMaelin: \"You.\"\n\nElira smiled into her tea. I tried not to.\n\nFor a few seconds, the room remembered it could hold something besides danger.\n\nThen I noticed the bread beside Aira's plate.\n\nShe had crushed one end of the loaf without realizing it. Flour marked the front of her uniform. The hem of her cloak was wet from the lake road, and one fastening sat in the wrong loop. Aira Solen never misfastened clothes. She treated uneven buttons as a personal accusation.\n\nHaru: \"You ran here.\"\n\nAira looked down at herself as if the evidence had betrayed her.\n\nAira: \"I traveled with urgency and very poor terrain cooperation.\"\n\nLeon did not turn from the window.\n\nLeon: \"She ran from the academy gate.\"\n\nAira: \"You were not invited to improve his evidence.\"\n\nLeon: \"You passed me.\"\n\nAira set the wounded loaf on the table. Her fingers stayed on it.\n\nAira: \"Senna's relay failed twice before it found Rhea. The first time, the return seal went black. The second time, it showed this house with no lights.\"\n\nHer voice stayed brisk, though her thumb pressed deeper into the crust.\n\nAira: \"I knew there was probably a normal explanation. Dawn. Bad angle. No one awake because some people had finally made a medically reasonable decision.\"\n\nCassian opened his mouth, caught sight of Elira's face, and changed direction mid-breath.\n\nCassian: \"I have never made a medically reasonable decision at dawn. It sounds exhausting.\"\n\nAira ignored him with less force than usual.\n\nAira: \"I still ran. Then I bought breakfast because arriving empty-handed felt like admitting I expected a body.\"\n\nThe room went still, with no Council mirror waiting to turn her fear into a statement. Leon left the window and pulled out the chair beside her without looking at her. Cassian slid the honey buns closer. Rhea lowered one hovering document until its blue light stopped cutting across Aira's face.\n\nElira reached for the crushed loaf.\n\nAira: \"That part is structurally compromised.\"\n\nElira tore off the flattened end.\n\nElira: \"So am I. It remains bread.\"\n\nShe put half on Aira's plate. Aira stared at it.\n\nElira: \"Eat.\"\n\nAira: \"I brought this breakfast.\"\n\nElira: \"Then take responsibility for it.\"\n\nLeon sat. That gave Cassian permission to stop performing ease. He turned his chair around and sat properly, though with visible resentment. Valen folded Senna's transcript. Rhea stayed standing for three more seconds before Elira pointed at the empty chair beside the field-office stack.\n\nElira: \"You too.\"\n\nRhea: \"There is an active containment petition.\"\n\nElira: \"It will remain active while you chew.\"\n\nRhea sat.\n\nMaelin looked pleased in the dangerous way healers looked when a patient enforced orders on other people.\n\nMaelin: \"Miss Vale, this is the soundest judgment you have demonstrated since restoration.\"\n\nElira: \"I regret giving you material.\"\n\nFood moved around the table. Cassian claimed the honey bun Aira had been avoiding under the excuse of evidence preservation. Leon poured too much tea into his own cup while watching the road reflection instead of the pot. Valen caught the spill with a corner of her sleeve and said nothing. Aira ate the crushed bread in small, furious bites.\n\nWatching them, I finally understood what the field office had hidden.\n\nThey had not come only because Krail filed a petition.\n\nThey had come because Senna's mirror had gone dark and each of them had pictured a different way we might be gone again.\n\nElira saw the realization land. She always did.\n\nElira: \"You can stop watching us as if blinking is jurisdictional negligence.\"\n\nLeon answered before anyone could lighten it.\n\nLeon: \"Can you promise you will still be here when I look back?\"\n\nElira's hand stilled around her cup. The honest answer cost her.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nLeon looked down.\n\nElira: \"Neither can you. That is not permission to spend the morning rehearsing grief.\"\n\nCassian tore his bun in half.\n\nCassian: \"What if I am naturally gifted at it?\"\n\nElira: \"Then practice something harder.\"\n\nHis smile returned crooked instead of bright. Better.\n\nI wanted to promise all of them that no relay, Councilor, doctrine, or old magic would take anyone from the room again. The Black Circle could have made the promise sound almost plausible.\n\nSo I kept it behind my teeth.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you for coming.\"\n\nAira swallowed.\n\nAira: \"Obviously.\"\n\nRough at the edges, and completely hers.\n\nRhea slid a document toward me.\n\nRhea: \"Krail's petition has three layers. The visible one is containment. The procedural one is separation. The hidden one is precedent.\"\n\nI read the header.\n\n**Emergency Motion Regarding Extra-Classification Influence and Witness Integrity**\n\nMy false Green Circle had once been a mask. Now every official sentence tried to turn my real name into a cage.\n\nAira tapped the lower section.\n\nAira: \"This is the nasty part.\"\n\nI read it. The petition never claimed Elira was weak or that I had harmed her. Instead, it argued that proximity to an extra-classification entity after traumatic resurrection could create dependency patterns indistinguishable from consent. Every word was clean. Every word was poison.\n\nElira read it over my shoulder. Her expression hardened, not frightened, insulted.\n\nElira: \"They are using my trauma to erase my choices.\"\n\nRhea: \"They are.\"\n\nRhea set her emergency seal flat against the dark wood.\n\nElira: \"Then I will answer them.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You will answer from a seated position after eating.\"\n\nElira opened her mouth, and Maelin aimed a healer's pen straight at her.\n\nMaelin: \"Miss Vale, I have now watched you die, return, nearly panic yourself unconscious in a storm, and redesign a pendant with ancient witness geometry before breakfast. If you argue with me about bread, I will sedate politics itself.\"\n\nCassian brightened.\n\nCassian: \"Can you do that?\"\n\nMaelin: \"I am willing to attempt it.\"\n\nElira picked up the bread.\n\nThe lake wards chimed before she finished it.\n\nEveryone at the table shifted at once. Leon's water reflection snapped from the road to the front gate. Cassian's unfinished red sigil closed around his knuckles. Rhea was on her feet before her chair scraped back. Around my wrist, the Green disguise tightened on instinct, a small lie trying to shield me from a world that had already seen through it.\n\nTwo crests waited beyond the gate seal.\n\nValemere silver-blue. Registered visitor marks. One Red Circle, one Blue weakened at the casting hand, both flanked by the paired escort lights Senna now required for travel.\n\nRhea read the names off the ward.\n\nRhea: \"Soren Ashvale. Talen Mire.\"\n\nCassian let his sigil die.\n\nCassian: \"Our most tasteful possible invasion.\"\n\nThe gate opened only after Rhea verified Valemere's seal against Senna's joint authorization twice. Soren stood outside in a plain training coat, snow melting in his dark hair, holding a wooden box narrow enough for practice cords. Talen stood beside him with his braced hand tucked against his chest and a shallow metal bowl under his other arm. Neither carried testimony papers.\n\nThat was the first surprise. Soren's opening line was the second.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere's eleven-o'clock practice hour remains registered.\"\n\nRhea scanned past him for a hidden relay, lawyer, or diplomatic trick.\n\nRhea: \"The tournament is suspended.\"\n\nSoren: \"The brackets are suspended. The practice hour was not canceled.\"\n\nHe delivered it with the calm precision of someone who had spent years forcing officials to respect the gap between a rule and what they wished it meant.\n\nSoren: \"Aurelis cannot travel. We brought the hour here.\"\n\nTalen shifted the bowl against his hip.\n\nTalen: \"I asked to come.\"\n\nThe sentence landed small and ready to defend itself. Rhea looked at him instead of Soren.\n\nRhea: \"For practice?\"\n\nTalen: \"For one hour in which no one asks me what Lucien did, what Noctis meant, or whether my hand proves a larger pattern.\"\n\nHis braced fingers trembled against the dark wool of his coat. He did not hide them.\n\nTalen: \"I would like to spill water badly among students.\"\n\nBehind Rhea, Professor Valen's expression warmed. Maelin's did not.\n\nMaelin: \"No one here is medically cleared for heroism.\"\n\nSoren: \"Good. We did not bring any.\"\n\nCassian leaned toward Leon.\n\nCassian: \"I dislike him more when he is efficient.\"\n\nLeon: \"You dislike competition with posture.\"\n\nCassian: \"I dislike posture that competes successfully.\"\n\nRhea inspected the wooden box. Four lengths of plain practice cord lay inside, each dyed Valemere blue at one end and Aurelis gray at the other. No spells. No challenge seals. No ranking tokens waiting to turn an hour into precedent.\n\nUnder them sat a folded registration card.\n\n**SUSPENDED BRACKET PRACTICE. NO STANDING CHANGE. NO OUTPUT RECORD. NO MEDICAL INFERENCE.**\n\nAira read over Rhea's shoulder.\n\nAira: \"This is the most romantic document I have seen all week.\"\n\nElira: \"You saw a containment petition ten minutes ago.\"\n\nAira: \"Exactly. My standards are wounded.\"\n\nTalen's gaze found the honey buns on the table. His mouth twitched toward a smile.\n\nTalen: \"Valemere served bitter tea after the memorial because sweetness was considered undignified.\"\n\nAira covered the plate protectively.\n\nAira: \"I remember. I also remember you requested cinnamon bread with admirable courage.\"\n\nTalen: \"I have reconsidered. Survival may require compromise.\"\n\nShe handed him a honey bun. He took it with his unbraced hand, and when the paper beneath it slipped, Soren's fingers twitched toward the catch and stopped. Talen trapped the paper against the bowl with his wrist. Clumsy, and completely his.\n\nMaelin missed none of it.\n\nMaelin: \"West garden only. Inside the visible ward line. No high-output casting. Miss Vale remains seated. Haru does not solve anything with the Black Circle. Talen stops the instant pain changes category.\"\n\nTalen: \"Who defines category?\"\n\nMaelin: \"You. Then me if you become foolish.\"\n\nTalen considered that.\n\nTalen: \"Acceptable.\"\n\nElira finished the bread before Maelin could point at it again.\n\nElira: \"Now?\"\n\nMaelin: \"You are far too pleased by restrictions.\"\n\nElira: \"I am pleased by practice. Restrictions are the unpleasant architecture around it.\"\n\nThe west garden lay silver under morning frost. Bare rose frames divided the flagstones into narrow lanes, and Lake Velis moved beyond the low wall in dark folds under a pale sky. Old restoration wards ran along the boundary in soft white points once Rhea sealed the gate behind us. No Council mirrors followed. Aira checked twice, then once more out of stubborn habit.\n\nSoren set the cord box on a stone bench. Talen carried the metal bowl to the center square and lowered it carefully. Water inside shivered with the same rhythm as his braced fingers.\n\nTalen: \"Water-bind support. Three-breath hold. No movement target.\"\n\nLeon studied the bowl.\n\nLeon: \"That is beginner work.\"\n\nElira, seated beneath Maelin's blanket while pretending the blanket did not exist, gave him a flat look. Leon winced.\n\nLeon: \"I mean the structure is basic. Not that performing it is.\"\n\nTalen: \"It was basic before my hand stopped agreeing. You do not have to rescue the sentence.\"\n\nLeon looked like he wanted the flagstones to swallow him. Cassian patted his shoulder.\n\nCassian: \"An excellent start. You offended the injured guest before I did. I feel liberated.\"\n\nTalen crouched beside the bowl. Silver joints and dark leather braced his casting palm, guiding movement without pretending damaged channels could be forced back into their old shape. He placed two fingers above the water.\n\nA faded Blue Circle appeared at his wrist. The water rose crookedly, one side pulling ahead until the sheet folded, slapped the rim, and spilled half the bowl across the stones.\n\nSoren caught the bowl before it tipped.\n\nTalen recoiled as if the catch had struck him.\n\nTalen: \"Don't.\"\n\nSoren let go immediately.\n\nThe bowl hit stone with a flat metal ring. More water spilled. Nobody rushed to fill the silence.\n\nSoren stared at his own hand, still curved around nothing.\n\nSoren: \"I thought it would fall.\"\n\nTalen: \"It did.\"\n\nSoren: \"I could prevent damage to the bowl.\"\n\nTalen: \"The bowl is not learning.\"\n\nWind shifted through the bare rose frames. Soren's shoulders dropped a fraction. Talen pressed his good palm flat against the wet stone.\n\nTalen: \"If you catch everything before I fail, we only learn what you can hold.\"\n\nSoren: \"And if the failure injures you?\"\n\nTalen's jaw tightened.\n\nTalen: \"Then ask whether I want the catch.\"\n\nThe scar across Soren's right hand paled where his fingers closed. He had earned it moving too late once, and the reflex died hard.\n\nSoren: \"Do you want me to catch the bowl?\"\n\nTalen looked at the spilled water, then at his brace.\n\nTalen: \"Not unless it is going to hit someone.\"\n\nSoren: \"Understood.\"\n\nThere was no neat apology. Soren fetched a cloth only after Talen nodded, and together they dried enough stone to keep the next attempt clean.\n\nLeon paced once around the bowl.\n\nLeon: \"Your pressure drops at release, not lift.\"\n\nTalen: \"I know.\"\n\nLeon: \"The usual bind asks the casting hand to hold shape and height with the same channel.\"\n\nTalen's eyes sharpened. Leon stopped before advice became ownership.\n\nLeon: \"Do you want an inelegant alternative?\"\n\nTalen looked genuinely surprised.\n\nTalen: \"How inelegant?\"\n\nLeon: \"Valemere instructors would object on aesthetic grounds.\"\n\nCassian: \"Now he is seducing the room.\"\n\nLeon ignored him.\n\nTalen: \"Show me without touching my hand.\"\n\nLeon knelt across from him and shaped a thin Blue ring at water level, deliberately weaker on Talen's side.\n\nLeon: \"Let the bowl carry height. Bind only the surface tension. Your brace does not have to pretend it is the wrist you had three years ago.\"\n\nTalen studied the ring, fighting his first reflex to bristle.\n\nElira leaned forward in her chair.\n\nElira: \"May I ask something?\"\n\nTalen turned to her.\n\nTalen: \"Ask.\"\n\nHe adjusted the leather strap across his casting hand.\n\nElira: \"When the release fails, does the pain start before the water drops or after you try to correct it?\"\n\nHe thought for a moment while she gave him room to answer. Talen flexed his braced thumb once.\n\nTalen: \"After. The first drop frightens me. I force the channel wider. Then it locks.\"\n\nElira: \"Then Leon's ugly version may work if you let the fall stay a fall. No correction surge.\"\n\nLeon: \"It is not ugly.\"\n\nElira: \"You introduced it that way.\"\n\nLeon: \"For consent, not accuracy.\"\n\nTalen let out a quiet breath that took me a moment to recognize as a laugh.\n\nHe reset his fingers over the water. Leon dismissed his demonstration ring so nobody could confuse explanation with aid. Talen built the shape himself: a narrow Blue line resting across the surface rather than beneath the whole mass.\n\nThe water lifted an inch, tilted, and spilled a thin sheet over one lip.\n\nTalen's shoulders jerked. His Circle flared to force the slip back.\n\nElira: \"Let it spill.\"\n\nHe glared at her for half a heartbeat, then let go. Water hit the stone while the rest hung suspended. One breath. His braced hand shook. Two. Light fractured across the quivering sheet. Three.\n\nTalen lowered it back into the bowl. The final edge slapped metal and soaked his sleeve.\n\nSoren did not reach out.\n\nTalen sat back on his heels, breathing as if he had run uphill.\n\nSoren: \"Again?\"\n\nTalen flexed each finger inside the brace, listening to his own nerve lines.\n\nTalen: \"No.\"\n\nNo apology in it.\n\nMaelin: \"Pain?\"\n\nTalen: \"Present. Not climbing. I want to stop while that remains true.\"\n\nMaelin nodded once, offering neither praise nor disappointment.\n\nLeon handed Talen a dry cloth, waiting until Talen reached for it.\n\nTalen: \"That was very inelegant.\"\n\nLeon: \"It held.\"\n\nTalen: \"Three breaths.\"\n\nLeon tried not to look pleased and failed. For the first time all morning, the garden felt like a place students could inhabit even if the Council vanished.\n\nSoren opened the wooden box.\n\nSoren: \"Good. Now the part no healer can mistake for rehabilitation.\"\n\nHe lifted out the four cords.\n\nSoren: \"Common-square footwork. No Circles. No channel reinforcement. One cord at the waist. Steal an opponent's without leaving the marked stones.\"\n\nCassian accepted a cord and tested its knot.\n\nCassian: \"Can I leave the marked stones if I do it beautifully?\"\n\nSoren: \"You lose the point beautifully.\"\n\nLeon tied his cord at the exact regulation height.\n\nLeon: \"What constitutes leaving? Full foot or any contact beyond the line?\"\n\nSoren: \"Any contact.\"\n\nCassian: \"Can an opponent be encouraged beyond the line?\"\n\nSoren gave him a dry look.\n\nSoren: \"Without magic, striking, lifting, or lying about a medical emergency.\"\n\nCassian: \"Your rules have become suspiciously personal.\"\n\nThe fourth cord waited in Soren's hand. He held it out to me.\n\nA strip of dyed cloth should not have rattled me after Foundation routes and city-sized return wards, yet my hand hesitated. Refusing wouldn't hurt anyone, winning wouldn't expose a conspiracy, and losing would only bruise my pride. That was what felt unfamiliar: stakes small enough to survive.\n\nElira watched from beneath Maelin's blanket without nodding me forward. She knew better than to make the choice easy to pin on her. I took the cord.\n\nSoren tied the last one around his waist. Talen stood near the square with the emptied bowl tucked under his good arm.\n\nTalen: \"I call boundaries.\"\n\nAira: \"I call everything else.\"\n\nRhea: \"You call nothing that enters the official record.\"\n\nAira: \"Then I call with unprecedented freedom.\"\n\nElira held out her hand for Soren's score slate, and he passed it over without ceremony.\n\nElira: \"First to three points. Clean take, visible possession. If Maelin stops the round, Maelin wins.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Finally, a sensible competition.\"\n\nWe stepped onto the square.\n\nTwelve flagstones across, frost still lining the seams, bare rose frames crowding two corners above the lake wall. No crowd, no ranking light waiting to turn footwork into bloodline proof.\n\nAira raised one hand.\n\nAira: \"Competitors ready. Dignity unprotected. Begin.\"\n\nSoren moved first, cutting one precise step across the center that forced everyone else to adjust. Leon turned to track him. Cassian used Leon's blind side to dart for his cord, and Leon slapped the hand away in outrage.\n\nLeon: \"That was not honorable.\"\n\nCassian: \"It was not magic, striking, lifting, or medical fraud.\"\n\nLeon: \"You used Soren as a visual obstruction.\"\n\nCassian: \"He came that way.\"\n\nSoren's fingers grazed Cassian's cord. Cassian spun, his heel stopping a breath short of the boundary line. Talen pointed at the stone.\n\nTalen: \"Inside. Barely.\"\n\nCassian: \"Barely is where legends keep their furniture.\"\n\nTalen: \"I can change the call if you continue speaking.\"\n\nCassian looked delighted.\n\nI had watched all three and gone nowhere, waiting for danger big enough to justify committing. Soren saw it immediately.\n\nHe stepped straight in without a feint, shoulders square, hands visible. I braced for the trick behind the direct line. His left foot cut outside mine, I shifted back, and he pivoted past me to snatch Leon's cord instead.\n\nThe blue-gray strip snapped free. Elira chalked the slate.\n\nElira: \"Ashvale, one.\"\n\nLeon stared at his empty waist.\n\nLeon: \"You were facing Haru.\"\n\nSoren: \"I was facing the square.\"\n\nCassian: \"He is unbearable. I respect it deeply.\"\n\nLeon retied his cord with angry precision.\n\nThe second round opened faster. Leon went straight for Cassian, who retreated laughing and tried to bait him over the line. Soren angled toward me again. This time I moved before certainty caught up.\n\nI stepped inside his reach, crowding a duelist built for distance. His eyes shifted. I reached for the cord at his right hip. My fingers brushed cloth.\n\nBehind me, Elira let out a single dry cough.\n\nMy head snapped toward her, and Soren took my cord so cleanly I felt the tug before I saw the cloth in his hand.\n\nElira: \"Ashvale, two.\"\n\nHaru: \"You coughed.\"\n\nElira looked down at the score slate.\n\nElira: \"I have lungs. They are occasionally theatrical.\"\n\nHaru: \"You nearly-\"\n\nThe rest of the sentence smelled of graves. I bit it off.\n\nElira's eyes lifted to mine, steady and sharp.\n\nElira: \"Do not use me to make losing feel noble.\"\n\nNo comfort in that, and no cruelty either. I had looked out of fear, and out of habit, and Soren had simply beaten me to the tie.\n\nSoren held out my cord.\n\nSoren: \"Point stands.\"\n\nElira: \"Obviously.\"\n\nI walked back to my mark with warm ears and tied the cord again. No ward flared. No one died. The sting stayed the exact size of a lost point, and I had almost forgotten feelings could stay that small.\n\nAcross the square, Leon finally caught Cassian's cord without a trick: one failed grab, a second that Cassian mocked, and a third timed straight into the mockery.\n\nLeon held the cloth aloft.\n\nLeon: \"One.\"\n\nCassian stared at him, then let out a sharp, genuine laugh.\n\nCassian: \"There you are.\"\n\nElira marked Leon's point.\n\nRhea checked the garden clock.\n\nRhea: \"Thirty-two minutes until medical preparation for hearing.\"\n\nAira: \"President Valmont has entered the dangerous phase where she pretends timing is not enthusiasm.\"\n\nRhea: \"Twenty-nine minutes if commentary continues.\"\n\nAira clapped both hands over her mouth.\n\nRound three. Soren sat at two, Leon at one, Cassian and I at zero.\n\nI tried not to care about getting on the board, which failed the instant Aira dropped her hand. Soren expected me to overcorrect after the cough, guarding his right hip and leaving his forward shoulder open as bait. Leon watched Cassian. Cassian watched everyone like a cat at an unguarded banquet.\n\nI moved at Soren. He pivoted to close his right side, and while my eyes stayed on the front tie, my hand didn't.\n\nAt the last step I planted short and reached across for the loose tail at his left hip, where the knot had slipped around during the previous exchange. Soren caught my wrist. Grip wasn't banned, and neither was turning into it.\n\nI rolled with his pull instead of fighting it, closing half a step inside his guard, and swept the cord free with my other hand.\n\nFor one second I just stared at the ordinary weave, still warm from his coat.\n\nElira struck the slate with chalk.\n\nElira: \"Aerlen, one.\"\n\nJoy hit me before caution could audit it, bright and ridiculous, followed a beat later by guilt over Cael, Elira's pale face, and Krail's petition waiting indoors.\n\nSoren eyed the cord in my fist.\n\nSoren: \"You saw the knot turn.\"\n\nHaru: \"I did.\"\n\nMy breath steadied in the cold air.\n\nSoren: \"Good.\"\n\nHe didn't inflate the point into a metaphor. Behind him, Talen caught my expression.\n\nTalen: \"You are allowed to look pleased.\"\n\nI glanced toward him.\n\nTalen: \"It does not amend the dead.\"\n\nHis braced hand rested against the metal bowl, speaking with the plain gravity of someone who knew survival did not require paying penance for every decent minute. I let the relief stay until I reached my start mark.\n\nRound four opened with Cassian still at zero and therefore the most dangerous person on the stones. He loitered near the corner boundary.\n\nLeon: \"You are planning something irritating.\"\n\nCassian: \"I am hurt by your certainty.\"\n\nLeon: \"You moved your knot to the back.\"\n\nCassian: \"I am adapting. You used to praise that when Haru did it.\"\n\nLeon: \"I did not.\"\n\nAira: \"Historical note: he absolutely did not.\"\n\nWe broke.\n\nSoren came after me without his earlier patience. I barely kept my cord through the first pass. His hand grazed my coat, I spun inside the reach, and he adjusted before my heel settled. For three fast beats, the square shrank to pure footwork: no Black Circle, no dread, just a sharper duelist forcing me to work. I almost laughed.\n\nSoren caught the sound and smiled once.\n\nThat was when Cassian swept behind us, used the clash the same way he'd used Soren against Leon, and snapped the cord straight off Soren's waist. He held it overhead.\n\nCassian: \"Beauty remains admissible.\"\n\nTalen: \"Both feet inside. Unfortunately.\"\n\nElira marked the slate.\n\nElira: \"Veyr, one.\"\n\nSoren glanced from Cassian to me.\n\nSoren: \"I was watching the wrong threat.\"\n\nCassian bowed.\n\nCassian: \"Put it on my family crest.\"\n\nLeon: \"Your family crest already means that.\"\n\nCassian clutched his heart. The garden broke into laughter, and even Rhea let out one breath before disguising it as a cough. Aira saw and didn't write it down, which might have been the kindest record choice she made all week.\n\nWe lined up for a fifth round. Leon shifted stance. Cassian dropped his grin. Soren knotted his cord higher. Elira leaned forward with the slate, bright-eyed despite the gray tiredness under her lashes, while Aira began comparing Leon's shoulders to a government filing cabinet.\n\nThen Maelin rang a metal spoon against Talen's bowl.\n\nThe clear note cut across the stones.\n\nMaelin: \"Done.\"\n\nFour competitors froze with four different objections on their faces. Maelin looked thoroughly satisfied.\n\nMaelin: \"A complete victory.\"\n\nElira: \"The score is unfinished.\"\n\nMaelin: \"And staying that way.\"\n\nShe checked the pulse crystal in her palm as Soren untied his cord.\n\nSoren: \"Good.\"\n\nLeon: \"Good?\"\n\nSoren laid the cloth back inside the box.\n\nSoren: \"Unfinished is not erased.\"\n\nWe gathered the cords. Cassian tried to claim moral victory and was overruled by every authority present. Leon argued that his point against Cassian ought to carry into any resumed session, and kept arguing with the registration card even after Elira reminded him he'd watched Rhea read the rules aloud. Talen dried the bowl himself, and Soren asked before taking the wet cloth from him.\n\nBy the time the others headed inside, the garden clock read eleven thirty-one. Maelin went indoors to fetch the bitter noon draft, leaving Soren at the low wall overlooking Lake Velis, Talen on the bench with his brace unbuckled across his knee, and Elira resting in her chair beside me.\n\nSoren looked down at the practice cord still coiled in my hand.\n\nSoren: \"If the brackets resume, do you want to compete?\"\n\nHaru: \"Aurelis' position depends on the Council review.\"\n\nSoren: \"I did not ask for Aurelis' position.\"\n\nDark water moved below the wall under a sky still weighing snow. I tried again.\n\nHaru: \"My participation could turn the bracket into classification evidence. If I win, it proves Black Circle students cannot compete safely. If I lose while holding back, it proves concealment corrupted the standings. If I withdraw-\"\n\nSoren: \"That is still the public answer.\"\n\nHis tone was exact enough to strip away every impressive excuse. Elira watched me over the top of her blanket without stepping in.\n\nHaru: \"I want one match where losing costs pride instead of a life.\"\n\nSaying it left me rawer than any Circle manifestation.\n\nHaru: \"I want to be angry about a point. I want to practice until something ordinary improves. I want to face someone who wants to beat me without trying to own what winning means afterward.\"\n\nSoren's scarred fingers rested along the garden wall.\n\nHaru: \"And I do not know if that match exists for me now.\"\n\nSoren looked toward the frost-scuffed square.\n\nSoren: \"It did for one point.\"\n\nHaru: \"There were no relays.\"\n\nSoren: \"Then relays are part of the problem. Not the point.\"\n\nHe made no promise Valemere could keep the distinction clean. He already knew what institutions did to matches after the fact.\n\nSoren: \"I have not withdrawn. The bracket can stay suspended without becoming dead. Your answer can too.\"\n\nTalen flexed his bare fingers. The tremor was smaller, not gone.\n\nTalen: \"Everyone assumes I want to return.\"\n\nSoren turned toward him.\n\nTalen: \"My family calls it reclaiming my future. My instructors call it recovery. Reporters call it courage because they do not have to hold the cup afterward.\"\n\nHe glanced at the water stain darkening his sleeve.\n\nTalen: \"I do not know whether competing again would be desire or an argument with Lucien conducted through my own hand.\"\n\nElira: \"You do not have to know before your hand does.\"\n\nTalen met her eyes. She had written to him after the tournament without promising repair, and she offered no false reassurance now either.\n\nTalen: \"What about you?\"\n\nElira: \"What about me?\"\n\nTalen: \"Do you want to return?\"\n\nHer fingers rested on the score slate. Once, her answer would have snapped out fully formed, duty and ambition dressed in the same coat. Now she took her time.\n\nElira: \"I want to know what my magic is when it is not proving I deserved the rank people already gave me.\"\n\nNo gold light rose at her wrist. Maelin had barred output, and Elira didn't need magic to make the question land.\n\nElira: \"Competition may be part of that. It may not. I resent not knowing.\"\n\nTalen nodded.\n\nTalen: \"That sounds more honest than courage.\"\n\nFrom inside the house, Aira yelled that the medicine had arrived and smelled like a disciplinary hearing.\n\nElira pushed up carefully. I stepped near enough to offer an arm without assuming, and she took my forearm to steady herself. Talen watched without turning the sight into a promise for his own recovery.\n\nTalen: \"One request.\"\n\nRhea had returned to the doorway, her seal visible at her wrist. Talen looked straight at it.\n\nTalen: \"Do not enter this morning as testimony.\"\n\nRhea lowered her hand.\n\nTalen: \"The water hold can stay a practice note under my control. The rest stays here. I wanted one hour to spill water badly among students. I do not want the Council improving it.\"\n\nRhea: \"Granted.\"\n\nSoren: \"Valemere copy remains closed.\"\n\nAira appeared behind them carrying a steaming cup and visibly stopped herself from reaching for her notebook.\n\nAira: \"I hate personal growth when it is inconvenient.\"\n\nTalen: \"You may record that.\"\n\nAira: \"Finally.\"\n\nAt eleven fifty-nine, Soren sealed three of the practice cords inside their wooden box and left the fourth folded on the bench beside Elira. The score remained on her slate: Ashvale two, Aerlen one, Varek one, Veyr one. No winner. No adjusted standing.\n\nSoren: \"Bring it back when the brackets reopen.\"\n\nTalen tucked his metal bowl under his good arm as their paired escort lights flared pale blue at the gate. By the time the garden chime struck noon, the Valemere pair had cleared the lake road, leaving only faint boot prints melting in the frost.\n\nElira carried the spare cord inside and laid it straight across Krail's petition, covering the word containment without erasing it.\n\nThe hearing opened at noon through five relays.\n\nAurelis had refused to let Krail summon us into an isolated chamber. Rhea cited medical restriction, Valen cited historical caution, and Aira called it refusing to let anyone put Haru in a fancy jar, earning herself a seven-minute ban from official phrasing.\n\nSo the preliminary review came to the Lake Velis sitting room instead.\n\nThe central mirror opened onto the Council chamber. Senna stood at the primary rail in her silver-gray robe. Above her sat Krail in a tiered seat of dark glass, handsome the way a polished blade could be, iron-gray hair immaculate and a Red Circle crest glowing cleanly inside a legal seal at his wrist. Delegates from Drakenshard, Valemere, Aurelis, northern civil-service schools, and three minor academies filled the surrounding benches.\n\nNoctis' official seat remained empty. Its absence watched harder than any face in the chamber.\n\nSenna rested one hand steady on the black glass of the primary rail.\n\nSenna: \"This preliminary session concerns whether emergency restrictions are required before the scheduled distributed witness review. The subject of review is not the classification of Haru Aerlen. It is sequence, risk, and procedural safety.\"\n\nKrail smiled faintly from his tiered seat.\n\nKrail: \"Magistra Voss is correct. This body must not rush classification. That is why provisional containment is a mercy to all parties.\"\n\nLeon kept his voice low enough that the mirror missed it.\n\nLeon: \"There it is.\"\n\nKrail leaned forward slightly, hands folded over the petition seal.\n\nKrail: \"A young woman died. She was restored through an illegal doctrine corrupted by Noctis. We have all grieved the conduit who paid that cost. But grief cannot blind us to the greater event. An unclassified student manifested force through the public relay network, suppressed multiple systems, altered observable magical law, and now remains in unsupervised intimate proximity to the primary resurrected witness.\"\n\nNobody in the sitting room moved.\n\nElira's hand tightened on the arm of her chair. I wanted to reach for her and held still, not out of deference to Krail, but so Elira could choose whether she wanted my hand while an entire chamber watched for proof I had already claimed it.\n\nHer fingers lifted, palm up.\n\nI laid my hand in hers. Across the mirror, Krail's eyes sharpened. Good. Let him record the difference.\n\nSenna: \"Miss Vale, do you wish to answer the proximity concern?\"\n\nElira: \"I do.\"\n\nHer free hand stayed steady along the wooden arm of her chair, her voice carrying clearly through the glass.\n\nElira: \"I am Elira Vale of Aurelis. Yellow Circle. Primary witness to Dominion Severance. Survivor of Votive Return corruption. Current patient under Senior Healer Maelin Orr. Current partner of Haru Aerlen by my own decision before, during, and after the events under review.\"\n\nKrail: \"No one disputes your dignity, Miss Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"You dispute my ability to consent.\"\n\nKrail's smile held.\n\nKrail: \"Trauma complicates consent.\"\n\nElira: \"It does. That is why I am under medical care, why my choices are witnessed, and why I have friends rude enough to make me eat bread before proceedings.\"\n\nAira murmured beside the side table.\n\nAira: \"History will thank me.\"\n\nElira never looked away from the mirror.\n\nElira: \"But trauma does not transfer ownership of my decisions to a Councilor who has never asked me what I want.\"\n\nThe first transcript crystal above Senna's rail brightened. Krail leaned back.\n\nKrail: \"And what do you want?\"\n\nThe trap snapped open cleanly. If she named me, they would call it dependency. If she named safety, they would call me a threat. If she named freedom, they would call it unsupervised recklessness.\n\nElira smiled, sharp and exact.\n\nElira: \"I want the Council to stop confusing possession with protection.\"\n\nThe second crystal brightened. Beside the bookcase, Professor Valen's eyes warmed.\n\nSenna: \"Recorded.\"\n\nKrail: \"Mr. Aerlen.\"\n\nHearing my name in his mouth made the Black Circle go dead still behind my ribs.\n\nKrail: \"Do you accept that your presence creates risk?\"\n\nHaru: \"I do.\"\n\nI held Krail's gaze across the glass without flinching. Down in the chamber, several delegates shifted in their seats. Krail's smile deepened.\n\nKrail: \"Then you concede-\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe word cut cleaner than force. I kept my fingers laced with Elira's where she had placed them.\n\nHaru: \"My presence creates risk. My absence also creates risk. Isolation created the old Circle Zero failure. Forced separation enabled Noctis to make witnesses into routes. Containment would not remove danger. It would move danger into a room with fewer people able to say no.\"\n\nSenna's pen stopped mid-stroke. Across the chamber, even the whispers died.\n\nKrail: \"You speak as if you understand ancient catastrophic systems better than the Council.\"\n\nHaru: \"No. I speak as someone the ancient catastrophic systems keep trying to turn into a tool.\"\n\nThe cold pressure surged behind my sternum. Ozone. Metal. Behind me, I felt everyone in the sitting room notice and hold their ground. That was the difference.\n\nRhea stepped squarely into the mirror's field beside Elira's chair, council red stark against the pale sitting-room wood.\n\nRhea: \"Aurelis submits a countermeasure proposal. No separation order. No containment demonstration. No isolated classification panel. Instead, distributed proximity logs, voluntary medical review, and open-trigger Black Circle stability observation under witness conditions.\"\n\nKrail: \"You are normalizing the abnormal.\"\n\nRhea: \"No. I am refusing to make abnormality illegal before it has acted unlawfully.\"\n\nCassian muttered from his chair.\n\nCassian: \"That goes on a banner.\"\n\nLeon: \"Please do not make banners.\"\n\nKrail raised one hand.\n\nThe chamber lights dimmed with procedural calm as a second document unfolded above the Council rail. Senna's expression tightened.\n\nSenna: \"That filing was not in the docket.\"\n\nKrail: \"It arrived under neutral seal.\"\n\nThe empty Noctis seat stayed empty, yet the document opened anyway, black frost lacing its edges. In Lake Velis, the house wards woke with a low hum. Elira's pendant warmed, pulsing a silent question through its seven open lines.\n\nShe touched the crystal once, steady and unafraid.\n\nKrail read the first line aloud before Senna could intervene.\n\nKrail: \"Supplemental theory: Black Circle proximity may overwrite witness autonomy through grief-bonded mana resonance.\"\n\nThe words hit the sitting room like scentless poison. Aira's papers scattered upward. Leon swore. Cassian's red sigil ignited in his palm. Professor Valen went pale.\n\nThe phrasing did not belong to modern law. It was older than Krail. Older than Noctis.\n\nBehind Senna, black frost bloomed outward from the empty Noctis chair across the chamber's glass wall, and a voice I knew too well poured through the legal seal wearing perfect calm.\n\nLucien: \"Do forgive the interruption. I thought the Council might appreciate a more honest question.\"\n\nElira pushed straight to her feet.\n\nMaelin: \"Sit down.\"\n\nElira did not. Neither did I.\n\nThe Black Circle opened behind my ribs, not as a blaze or grief, but as a door refusing to become a weapon. Inside the mirror, Lucien's frost-framed smile sharpened.\n\nLucien: \"If Haru Aerlen's mercy is not domination, prove it while everyone is watching.\"\n\nThe Council chamber fractured.\n\nEvery transcript crystal turned black.\n\nAnd in the Lake Velis sitting room, the seven open rings inside Elira's pendant began to sing.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-25-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep26","label":"Episode 20","number":"20","file":"episode-26-cover.webp","fallback":"The Night Before the Cut","eyebrow":"Before Dawn","recap":"","accent":["#1f2937","#ffd166","#94a3b8"],"displayLabel":"Episode 20","displayNumber":"20","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"The Trial of Proximity","body":"The sound inside Elira's pendant was not music.\n\nMusic wanted to be heard. This wanted to be obeyed.\n\nSeven open rings rang in seven different pitches, each one a question sharpened until the air itself tried to answer. The Lake Velis sitting room bent around them. The windows became mirrors. The mirrors became doors. The doors became witness rails, polished black and waiting.\n\nLucien had not entered the house. That was the worst part. He had entered the argument, turning old procedure into an invasion that needed no body to cross a threshold.\n\nThe Council chamber remained visible in the central mirror, though only in pieces. Senna stood at the primary rail with one hand pressed to a cracked transcript crystal. Krail's seat glowed behind her, sealed behind emergency glass. Delegates shouted without sound. Across from them, the empty Noctis chair was occupied by absence itself: black frost sketching the outline of a person who refused to spend a body on contempt.\n\nLucien's voice came through every reflective surface at once.\n\nLucien: \"A simple test. Let the Black Circle remain near the woman it restored through grief and prove her will is untouched.\"\n\nElira's hand found the crystal. Beneath her fingers, it burned warm and alive.\n\nMaelin moved first, stepping in front of Elira with no magic raised, only the full authority of someone who had long ago stopped being impressed by catastrophes.\n\nMaelin: \"Patient consent is suspended under hostile interference.\"\n\nThe pendant answered with a single low note.\n\nLucien laughed softly.\n\nLucien: \"A healer tries to remove her from the question. How kind. How familiar.\"\n\nThe low note sharpened. The house's witness ward clicked shut against Maelin's healer seal, pinning her authority rather than her body.\n\nI felt the spell before I saw it, a cold metallic taste behind my sternum as the old house magic recoiled from its own thresholds. Lucien was using Krail's petition as a frame and Noctis grammar as pressure, but the engine beneath it was older: a proximity trial built after ancient disaster to decide whether a safeguard's witnesses remained people or had become extensions of his will. Fear had designed it. Noctis had sharpened it.\n\nAira: \"Haru?\"\n\nHer voice was tight, pen already moving in her open notebook even as the room tried to turn documentation into evidence against us.\n\nHaru: \"It is testing whether anyone near me can act independently.\"\n\nLeon: \"Again?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe nearest window caught my reflection, though the eyes staring back were wrong: too black, too still, too easy for frightened people to believe.\n\nHaru: \"This version tests whether emotional closeness counts as contamination.\"\n\nThe room understood before I finished. Cassian's humor vanished. Rhea's face became marble. Elira did not look away from the mirror.\n\nElira: \"Lucien is attacking our relationship.\"\n\nLucien: \"I am examining a public risk.\"\n\nElira: \"You are hiding behind public language because no honest spell would carry you.\"\n\nFor the first time, the frost outline in the Noctis seat flickered. A tiny thing. Satisfying.\n\nThen the crystal rang again, and the sitting room split into stations.\n\nPale circles appeared across the floor, each open at one side. Aira's lit blue-white around scattered notes. Leon's flowed in water-thin lines at the windows. Cassian's burned red beneath his chair. Rhea's sharpened into council geometry at the threshold. Professor Valen's formed a record sigil near the bookcase. Maelin's steadied beside Elira's chair.\n\nMine did not appear. Elira's did, gold and black, open on the side facing me.\n\nLucien: \"The trial offers mercy. Each witness may step away from Haru Aerlen's field and answer free of influence.\"\n\nThe floor between us darkened into a path leading toward the far side of the room, away from me and away from the table where we had eaten bread, argued, and revised the pendant with human hands.\n\nKrail's voice cut through the mirror.\n\nKrail: \"Magistra, this is an unauthorized manifestation. I request containment-\"\n\nSenna: \"Denied pending survival.\"\n\nI liked her more with every crisis.\n\nLucien: \"Miss Vale. You first.\"\n\nThe path away from me brightened. Elira's breath caught as the crystal pulsed at her throat. I felt the trial lean toward her wound, offering an explanation that would make pain simpler: that she stayed out of fear, or power, or because the Black Circle had trained her body to return to me.\n\nFear in polite clothes always sounded more reasonable than cruelty. I released her hand so every witness, along with Lucien and Krail, could see it. The Black Circle stirred, offended by the distance, and I kept my palm open at my side.\n\nHaru: \"Elira chooses.\"\n\nLucien: \"A useful line.\"\n\nElira turned toward me. For one second, I saw the night of the storm in her eyes: the cut, the cold, the memory of dying through a rescue relabeled until mercy became a wound.\n\nThen she stepped away from me. The room gasped. I did not move. That was the hardest thing I had done since watching her die.\n\nElira crossed to the far side of the room. The open gold circle followed her without dragging or binding, simply asking whether she would remain herself outside my reach. She stopped alone beside the cold fireplace, and the pendant's ringing softened.\n\nLucien: \"Good. Now answer. Do you love Haru Aerlen because you choose him, or because the Black Circle made itself necessary to your survival?\"\n\nThe question was obscene because it wore philosophy. Elira lifted her chin.\n\nElira: \"I loved him before I knew how much danger surrounded him.\"\n\nThe first open ring dimmed.\n\nElira: \"I chose him before his name became public.\"\n\nThe second dimmed.\n\nElira: \"I feared him once, when he showed me the Black Circle. Then I understood the fear was not of him, but of what the world would do if it saw him as a thing.\"\n\nThe third dimmed.\n\nElira: \"I wanted him after I died because being alive returned my body to me, and to no one else.\"\n\nThe fourth dimmed.\n\nHer face flushed, yet she did not look down.\n\nElira: \"And last night belonged to me as much as to him.\"\n\nAira made a strangled sound and wrote so hard her pen sparked. Cassian stared at the ceiling with heroic dedication. Leon turned red and pretended the window required inspection. Rhea looked like she was calculating whether dignity could survive friendship. Maelin only nodded once.\n\nThe fifth ring dimmed. Lucien's frost outline sharpened.\n\nLucien: \"A beautiful declaration. Still within proximity memory.\"\n\nElira smiled.\n\nElira: \"Then let someone else ask.\"\n\nShe turned toward the blue-white station.\n\nElira: \"Aira.\"\n\nAira's station flashed.\n\nAira: \"Oh, excellent. I was hoping the ancient relationship court would notice me.\"\n\nElira: \"Ask me something Haru would not want you to ask.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nShe did not look back. Aira's expression changed, the joke vanishing while the friendship stayed.\n\nAira: \"Do you ever stay with him because you are afraid he will break without you?\"\n\nThe sixth ring flared. The room held its breath. Elira's smile vanished.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer fingers curled lightly against the cold stone mantel. The ring stayed bright, and Lucien's silence sharpened with interest.\n\nElira: \"Sometimes. More often since the Wounded City. I see him watching the world for threats. I see him making himself smaller so no one else panics. I see the Black Circle answer grief faster than he wants it to. Sometimes I am afraid that if I step away, he will decide the safest version of himself is loneliness.\"\n\nThe sixth ring burned painfully bright.\n\nElira: \"But that fear is mine. It is not proof that he owns me. It is proof that I love someone who is wounded too.\"\n\nThe light steadied.\n\nElira: \"And when I stay, I am not staying because he cannot survive without me. I am staying because I refuse to let fear teach either of us that love is containment.\"\n\nThe sixth ring dimmed. Aira wiped at her face angrily.\n\nAira: \"Recorded, unfortunately.\"\n\nLucien: \"One ring remains.\"\n\nI knew before it moved. The seventh ring was mine, waiting in the crystal like a blade wrapped in velvet.\n\nThe Black Circle opened beneath my skin, just enough to answer. Shadows deepened across the sitting room. Beyond the windows, Lake Velis turned from blue to black glass, and every open circle on the floor leaned toward me.\n\nLucien: \"Haru Aerlen. If Elira Vale asks you to let her face danger without your intervention, will you obey?\"\n\nThere were easier questions. Would I kill? Would I die? Would I burn Noctis down to its foundations? Those had answers I could give with enough restraint. This one found the part of me that still heard Elira's body hitting the Wounded City stone.\n\nI looked at her across the room. She did not soften the question for me, because she loved me.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe seventh ring screamed. Every window cracked. Krail stood in the Council chamber.\n\nKrail: \"There. There is the confession.\"\n\nI swallowed the cold taste in my mouth.\n\nHaru: \"If she asks while coerced, wounded, misled, or relabeled by hostile doctrine, I will not obey the shape of the sentence. I will answer the person beneath it.\"\n\nThe scream faltered.\n\nHaru: \"If she asks as herself, with risk named honestly, then yes.\"\n\nThe Black Circle pressed against my ribs like a storm wanting permission. I did not give it.\n\nHaru: \"I will hate it. I may fail once and need my witnesses to stop me. I will not pretend love makes restraint easy. But I accept that protecting Elira cannot mean becoming the only door she is allowed to use.\"\n\nThe seventh ring dimmed enough to let the room breathe.\n\nElira crossed the floor back to me.\n\nLucien: \"Careful. The trial has not closed.\"\n\nShe kept walking.\n\nKrail: \"Miss Vale-\"\n\nElira reached me and took my open hand. The seventh ring went dark because return had been chosen, and the pendant fell silent.\n\nFor one heartbeat, the old house became only a house again.\n\nThen the central mirror shattered inward. No glass flew.\n\nInstead, the reflection of the Council chamber folded into a narrow black corridor, and at the end of it stood Lucien Voss in full Noctis uniform, pale hair silvered by frost, eyes colder than any spell should make a living person. It was a relay-body: illegal, dangerous, and sharp as a perfect lie before it cut.\n\nLucien: \"Then let us test return.\"\n\nBehind him, hundreds of small lights appeared: Blue, Red, Yellow, and a few green points nearly swallowed by dark. Noctis student crests. Cael had not been the only conduit-track student. Iven Sahl's name burned in my memory from the funeral. The lights flickered like trapped breaths.\n\nNera stepped forward from the side hall doorway, where she and Darian had been waiting under Rhea's witness seal until the windows cracked. She looked silent as guilt and twice as pale.\n\nNera: \"Those are students.\"\n\nLucien looked at her through the corridor.\n\nLucien: \"Those are reserves.\"\n\nThe Black Circle opened. This time, everyone felt it.\n\nThe relay-body stepped through the mirror. One polished black shoe touched the Lake Velis floor, and frost spread from the heel in a perfect Noctis sigil. The second shoe crossed after it. Lucien arrived without breath, without warmth, and without enough of a pulse for Maelin's diagnostic charm to recognize him as alive, yet with more than enough substance to hurt us.\n\nNera moved between him and the corridor lights.\n\nNera: \"Do not strike the relay-body.\"\n\nCassian already had fire in one hand. He stopped before throwing it.\n\nNera: \"The reserve signatures are carrying the return load. If the body breaks, the feedback goes through them.\"\n\nLucien smiled.\n\nLucien: \"Still useful. How reassuring.\"\n\nNera went white. He had not raised his voice. One sentence returned years of training to her posture: shoulders back, hands visible, guilt arranged as attention.\n\nDarian stepped beside her out of the doorway and struck his own binding ring against the floor. The sound was ugly and human. Nera's shoulders lowered. Lucien noticed him then.\n\nLucien: \"Darian Thorne. You always did mistake proximity to courage for possession of it.\"\n\nDarian: \"I brought enough to stand here.\"\n\nLucien: \"Then spend it.\"\n\nThe relay-body lifted one hand.\n\nEvery reserve light behind him screamed, silent in the air yet shrieking straight through the pendant. Twelve pulses struck Elira's crystal in different rhythms: panic, cold, channel exhaustion, a child's heart forced too fast by a conduit brace. Elira doubled over. Gold light burst from beneath her collar and scattered across the floor in broken arcs. I caught her shoulder.\n\nThe Black Circle surged toward Lucien's raised hand.\n\nNera: \"Those are their pulses! If you erase the signal, you erase our only route to them.\"\n\nI stopped. Lucien's smile widened. He had wanted the hesitation.\n\nThen he crossed the room faster than a reflection should move.\n\nLeon threw a water wall between us. Lucien passed through it; the water froze around his outline and crashed to the floor in his shape after he was already beyond it. Cassian changed tactics before the ice finished breaking, sending fire along the floor instead of at Lucien to burn the Noctis sigil beneath the relay-body and sever its cleanest anchor.\n\nLucien staggered half a step.\n\nCassian: \"You do have feet. I was beginning to think you arrived by arrogance alone.\"\n\nLucien turned his hand. The burned sigil inverted. Black frost climbed Cassian's flame and sank into his right wrist. His Red Circle went dark. He swore, dropped to one knee, and drove the affected hand against the stone before the cold could enter his elbow. Leon flooded the line, and steam exploded between them.\n\nLucien emerged from it beside Elira. He did not attack me. That would have been simpler. He pressed two fingers against the pendant at her throat, and Elira stopped breathing. The room vanished from her eyes.\n\nLucien: \"Do you remember the exact instant your choice stopped belonging to you?\"\n\nCold spread through the crystal. Elira's body arched. The pendant projected the Wounded City across every wall: Cael inside the return geometry, Elira stepping forward, the hook closing, my face when her pulse stopped. Memory edited for cruelty. In this version, Elira smiled when the hook took her, I reached too slowly because part of me wanted the Black Circle seen, and Cael looked relieved to die.\n\nElira saw all three lies while her body remembered the real pain. Lucien leaned closer.\n\nLucien: \"Choice is only the story survivors tell before someone stronger edits the record.\"\n\nI seized his wrist. My hand passed through cold light and closed around something solid at its center: a narrow black relay rod hidden inside the body. Every reserve light behind him flared. Children cried through the crystal. I could not crush it, and Lucien knew. He looked at my hand locked around the rod and spoke softly enough that only Elira and I heard.\n\nLucien: \"There. Your mercy has a handle after all.\"\n\nElira moved. Still unable to breathe properly, still trapped inside the false memory, still hers, she drove her forehead into Lucien's face. The relay-body jerked backward at the neck while my grip stayed clamped on the inner rod. His fingers left the pendant.\n\nElira fell against me, dragged in one ragged breath, and raised her Yellow Circle with the next. No elegant ring. No academy form. Gold fire wrapped her fist.\n\nMaelin: \"Elira, no sustained...\"\n\nElira hit Lucien across the mouth. The impact cracked the relay-body from jaw to collar. Gold light flooded the fracture and exposed the black rod in my hand. For the first time, Lucien looked surprised by pain.\n\nElira: \"You do not get to tell me what I remember.\"\n\nBlood appeared at the corner of her mouth, forced up by output tearing through channels nowhere near healed enough to carry it. Maelin caught her as her knees failed.\n\nMaelin: \"Done. Your body is done.\"\n\nElira tried to raise her hand again. Maelin trapped it against her own shoulder.\n\nMaelin: \"You chose the first strike. I am choosing medical restraint before the second kills you. Be angry later.\"\n\nElira shook with fury, then stopped.\n\nLucien touched the crack in his borrowed face. Gold burned beneath his fingers. The reserve lights pulsed erratically as the damaged body began drawing harder from them to repair itself. Aira caught the pattern.\n\nAira: \"He is cycling through the lights. Third, seventh, twelfth, then back to third.\"\n\nNera looked into the corridor. The seventh light pulsed green at its outer rim, flickering blue-white inside. Iven.\n\nNera: \"The relay has a registry index. If we take one cycle, we can keep the route after he closes it.\"\n\nRhea: \"Method?\"\n\nNera was already loosening the clasp on her right binding ring.\n\nNera: \"I enter. Darian anchors me. Aira calls the cycle.\"\n\nDarian grabbed her arm.\n\nDarian: \"No. We enter.\"\n\nNera: \"The corridor recognizes my command history.\"\n\nDarian: \"And Lucien recognizes your guilt.\"\n\nThey stared at each other. No time for a perfect answer. Rhea made the choice reviewable instead.\n\nRhea: \"Nera enters one body length. Darian maintains physical anchor. Leon controls the threshold. Cassian burns only floor sigils. Aira calls retreat whether or not the registry is secured.\"\n\nNera: \"Accepted.\"\n\nDarian: \"Accepted.\"\n\nLucien laughed through the fracture in his face.\n\nLucien: \"Aurelis turns panic into committees with admirable speed.\"\n\nRhea: \"And Noctis turns children into batteries. I prefer our delay.\"\n\nLeon drove water into the shattered mirror frame, spreading a blue border around the corridor to give the threshold a physical edge. Cassian burned the frost away from Nera's path in short left-handed bursts of red flame while his darkened right wrist spasmed at his side. Aira tracked the cycling lights.\n\nAira: \"Third. Seventh. Twelfth. Third. Seventh, go.\"\n\nNera stepped into the corridor. Darian locked both arms around her waist. The cold tried to pull them apart by making each body feel like the other's restraint. Nera cried out. Darian loosened his grip just enough for her to choose it again.\n\nNera reached, and the seventh light recoiled.\n\nNera: \"Iven Sahl. Noctis first-year. Conduit track. Thermal brace sensitivity. Cael Nox witness link.\"\n\nThe light steadied. A small slate of black glass formed beneath it. Lucien moved toward her.\n\nI stepped between them with my hand still closed around the relay rod, holding without crushing. The Black Circle turned once under my skin, and every shadow in the room aligned behind me. Lucien stopped.\n\nHaru: \"You wanted to know what I protect.\"\n\nThe words came out colder than I intended.\n\nHaru: \"Watch.\"\n\nBehind me, Nera tore the black slate free. The corridor convulsed.\n\nAira: \"Retreat!\"\n\nDarian pulled. Nera came backward with the slate against her chest. The threshold snapped at her leading shoulder. Leon closed his water border around it, yet one blade of black glass crossed before the seal completed, cutting Nera from collar to upper arm. Darian caught her.\n\nMaelin could not leave Elira, so Rhea threw a red compression seal across the wound. Temporary, painful, enough.\n\nNera did not look at the blood. She looked at the slate. One name remained visible.\n\n**IVEN SAHL: ACTIVE RESERVE ROUTE / LOCATION WITHHELD**\n\nUnder it, eleven registry positions flickered without names: a route, not a rescue.\n\nLucien saw what she had taken. The warmth left his expression completely. He reached through the cracked relay-body and closed his hand around the rod I held. For one second, we both touched the same tether, his fingers cold enough to burn.\n\nLucien: \"You have mistaken one stolen name for progress.\"\n\nHaru: \"You noticed the theft.\"\n\nHis eyes sharpened. Good. I opened my hand.\n\nHe expected resistance. My release made him pull too hard, and the relay-body staggered backward into its own corridor. Cassian burned the last floor sigil. Leon slammed the water border shut.\n\nElira, pale in Maelin's arms, lifted two fingers. Gold light struck the cracked mirror at its weakest point, and the relay-body shattered inward. Every fragment carried Lucien's face for one falling instant before dissolving into frost that blew back down the corridor without touching the students behind it. The twelve lights remained, and so did Lucien's voice.\n\nI crossed back to Elira. Maelin kept one arm around her ribs without blocking the hand Elira held out for me. Elira's fingers tightened around mine to anchor me where I stood.\n\nThe frost inside the broken mirror formed Lucien's smile.\n\nLucien: \"Come back to school, Haru Aerlen. The world wants to know what you are. I will show them what you protect.\"\n\nThe corridor snapped shut.\n\nThe Council chamber returned in broken fragments, Senna's voice cutting through static.\n\nSenna: \"Emergency session suspended. Aurelis, secure your students. All schools, lock conduit registries now.\"\n\nThe mirror went black. No one moved.\n\nMaelin broke the stillness by throwing a roll of bandage at Leon's head. He caught it on reflex.\n\nMaelin: \"Compression on Nera's shoulder. Darian, keep her sitting. Cassian, show me your wrist. Elira, if you raise that hand again I will personally redefine autonomy as remaining conscious.\"\n\nEveryone moved. Pain made the room honest faster than procedure had.\n\nNera sat against the overturned table with Rhea's red seal still clamped over the cut, blood soaking the collar of her borrowed coat. Darian held the bandage where Leon placed it and counted each breath she tried to hide. Cassian's right hand would not produce flame; the skin stayed unburned, yet black frost sat beneath the Red Circle line like ink trapped under glass. Elira's pulse skipped every seventh beat. Maelin heard it and went terrifyingly quiet.\n\nMaelin: \"No more Yellow output today. Not one spark.\"\n\nElira: \"The corridor...\"\n\nMaelin: \"Closed. Your channels are not.\"\n\nElira looked at the black slate in Nera's lap. The registry positions were fading. Iven's name remained, while the eleven unnamed lights did not. Aira saw the first disappear.\n\nAira: \"No. No, no. Darian, angle the slate toward me.\"\n\nDarian: \"I am holding pressure.\"\n\nLeon took the slate without removing his hands from Nera's shoulder. Water rose from his wrist in a thin transparent sheet and held the glass at eye level. Aira opened her notebook as the second light vanished.\n\nAira: \"I need rhythm, color, position. Call them.\"\n\nNera's lips had gone pale.\n\nNera: \"First: Blue, fast triple pulse, second-year cadence. Second...\"\n\nShe stopped. The second was gone. Nera struck the floor with her good hand.\n\nNera: \"I knew it.\"\n\nDarian tightened the bandage.\n\nDarian: \"Then know the next one.\"\n\nCruel wording from anyone else. From him, a rope. Nera looked back at the slate.\n\nNera: \"Third: Green. Weak thermal return. Younger than Iven. Fourth: Red, brace distortion on the downbeat. Fifth: Blue...\"\n\nAira wrote furiously. Mira was not there, and the Green copy table was days away at Aurelis. No duplicate hands waited automatically. So Rhea tore the Council petition in half and wrote on the blank backs. Professor Valen took the margins of her historical notes. Cassian, his right wrist frost-locked and his reserves nearly spent, used his left hand to copy colors onto the paper wrapping from the food basket. Leon kept the slate steady with water while holding Nera still.\n\nElira reached for a page. Maelin slapped her fingers away.\n\nMaelin: \"You are a patient.\"\n\nElira: \"I can dictate.\"\n\nMaelin considered the compromise, then snatched a sterile bandage wrapper from her kit and took a pen in her free hand.\n\nMaelin: \"Dictate.\"\n\nElira tracked the visible order while Nera named cadence.\n\nElira: \"Sixth position dimmed before fifth. Record independent interruption. Seventh is Iven. Eighth: Blue, paired flicker with ninth.\"\n\nThe slate grew cold enough for Leon's water support to freeze. He swore and increased pressure. The glass cracked.\n\nAira: \"Do not let it split through a position.\"\n\nLeon: \"I had planned to crack it artistically.\"\n\nCassian looked up from his food-wrapper copy.\n\nCassian: \"That is usually my department.\"\n\nThe joke lasted one breath. Then the tenth light vanished, and Nera lost sequence. She stared at the blank position, breathing too fast.\n\nNera: \"I do not know.\"\n\nAira: \"Recorded. Eleventh.\"\n\nNera: \"I should know.\"\n\nAira: \"You do not. Eleventh.\"\n\nWork small enough to survive panic. Nera swallowed.\n\nNera: \"Yellow. Suppressed crown rhythm. Possibly senior. Twelfth, Green, intermittent, no, wait...\"\n\nThe final light folded inward.\n\nElira: \"It changed before disappearing.\"\n\nAira: \"How?\"\n\nElira closed her eyes, replaying it.\n\nElira: \"Green outside. Blue-white at the core. Like a lower crest carrying a borrowed channel classification.\"\n\nNera looked at her.\n\nNera: \"Conduit masking.\"\n\nAira wrote the phrase across three pages.\n\nThe slate erased everything except Iven Sahl's name. Then a Noctis seal surfaced beneath it.\n\n**UNAUTHORIZED REGISTRY POSSESSION**\n\nBlack fire caught the lower edge. Leon released the water support before the flame could travel into his Circle, and the slate dropped.\n\nHaru: \"Do not touch it.\"\n\nRhea threw a Council evidence case over the burning glass. The case sealed. Black light hammered once against the lid and went dark. Inside remained one name and eleven missing positions. Across the room remained five incomplete copies made on a petition, historical notes, food paper, Aira's notebook, and Maelin's medical wrapper. The copies disagreed on two colors and three pulse orders.\n\nThey agreed children were there.\n\nNera looked at the mess of paper.\n\nNera: \"Noctis will call this corrupted extraction.\"\n\nRhea locked the evidence case.\n\nRhea: \"It is corrupted. We extracted it while injured from a hostile relay-body using child conduits. Corruption is part of the evidence.\"\n\nAira underlined the sentence.\n\nMaelin finished binding Nera's shoulder and moved to Elira. Elira did not resist when the healer guided her onto the sofa, and that frightened me more than resistance would have.\n\nMaelin: \"You bought one strike. Your body charged interest.\"\n\nElira touched the blood at her lip.\n\nElira: \"He felt it.\"\n\nMaelin: \"So did you. Only one of you is my patient.\"\n\nElira looked at me, furious that Lucien had made violence necessary and unwilling to let fury erase the cost.\n\nElira: \"Record both.\"\n\nAira did. Then Rhea spoke.\n\nRhea: \"Recovery leave is over.\"\n\nElira looked at the silent crystal, then at me. Her eyes were gold, steady, alive, and furious.\n\nElira: \"Then we go home.\"\n\n---\n\nGoing home required paperwork. That felt obscene for several reasons and necessary for several worse ones.\n\nRhea did not let anyone rush for the carriages until she had three sealed records, two medical clearances, one Council travel objection, and Aira's handwritten sequence summary copied twice because the first copy had jam on the corner.\n\nAira: \"History can survive fruit.\"\n\nRhea: \"History can survive legibility.\"\n\nAira copied it again.\n\nElira sat near the window while Maelin checked her pulse for the fourth time in ten minutes. The trial had not wounded her in any visible way, which almost made it easier for frightened people to ignore what it had done. Her hands were steady. Her breathing was controlled. Her pendant was quiet. Yet her gaze kept returning to the empty space where Lucien's black corridor had stood.\n\nI saw it because I was watching too closely, then made myself look away before watching became another form of taking. Maelin noticed, of course.\n\nMaelin: \"Progress. Irritating, but progress.\"\n\nHaru: \"Was that for me?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Unfortunately.\"\n\nElira's mouth moved toward a smile that did not quite arrive. I crossed the room slowly and stopped before I reached her chair.\n\nHaru: \"May I?\"\n\nThe question changed the air, because the entire morning had been a machine designed to make asking feel theatrical. Elira looked up at me, her chin rising clear and deliberate.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nI knelt beside her chair, wanting to be lower than the argument for once.\n\nHaru: \"I am sorry he used the archive.\"\n\nElira's fingers tightened once on the chair arm.\n\nElira: \"He did not use the archive.\"\n\nI waited. She looked toward the darkened mirror.\n\nElira: \"He used the idea that private things become dirty when hostile people see them.\"\n\nThe room quieted around that. Aira stopped writing. Leon looked down. Cassian's expression lost its easy edge. Elira's voice stayed calm, each word chosen from somewhere painful and exact.\n\nElira: \"The Moonlit Archive is still ours. Lake Velis is still ours. Last night is still ours. The fact that he tried to drag them through a trial does not make them his evidence.\"\n\nI bowed my head in recognition.\n\nHaru: \"Then I will remember that before I get angry.\"\n\nAira: \"Before?\"\n\nHaru: \"During.\"\n\nElira touched my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"Better.\"\n\nThe mirror chimed again, and every person in the room looked ready to destroy it. Senna's seal appeared in the glass with visible strain lines.\n\nSenna: \"I am reconnecting under emergency witness relay. This line is clean for approximately ninety seconds.\"\n\nRhea stepped into frame immediately.\n\nRhea: \"Report.\"\n\nSenna: \"Krail is attempting to reframe the hostile interruption as evidence that Lake Velis proximity creates uncontrolled old-system events. I have suspended session before he could file the phrase into record.\"\n\nRhea: \"Can he reopen?\"\n\nSenna: \"Yes. Not today if I continue being professionally unpleasant.\"\n\nAira whispered from beside the table.\n\nAira: \"Notebook Senna.\"\n\nRhea ignored her. Senna's eyes moved to me.\n\nSenna: \"Mr. Aerlen, the Council will ask why you did not destroy the hostile corridor when Noctis student reserves appeared.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because I did not know what would happen to the students connected to it.\"\n\nSenna: \"Good. Say exactly that later. Do not improve it.\"\n\nElira almost smiled again. Senna looked at her.\n\nSenna: \"Miss Vale, the Council will ask whether the proximity trial altered your stated feelings toward Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nElira: \"It clarified my anger.\"\n\nSenna paused.\n\nSenna: \"That is acceptable if followed by medical confirmation.\"\n\nMaelin leaned toward the mirror.\n\nMaelin: \"Her anger is stable.\"\n\nSenna blinked once.\n\nSenna: \"Noted.\"\n\nThe mirror crackled, Senna's image distorting around the edges.\n\nSenna: \"Final point. Noctis has sealed its official dormitory wing from outside registry audit. Their explanation is internal contamination control. We recovered partial crest signatures independently of the registry fragment you took from the corridor.\"\n\nNera, standing near the side hall with her bandaged shoulder and both binding rings refastened beneath her sleeves, lifted her head.\n\nNera: \"That means they moved students before sealing.\"\n\nDarian's jaw tightened.\n\nSenna: \"We suspect so.\"\n\nNera's binding rings clicked together as her hands curled into fists.\n\nNera: \"Send the signatures. We need to match them before Noctis moves the routes again.\"\n\nSenna: \"Not yet. We have partial crest signatures.\"\n\nRhea: \"Nera.\"\n\nNera did not look away from the mirror.\n\nNera: \"I know the conduit track. I know how they hide weak crests under training labels.\"\n\nSenna studied her for half a second, then nodded.\n\nSenna: \"Through Aurelis protection channel only.\"\n\nThe mirror flashed. Twelve small lights appeared above the table, each one blurred by missing identity: Blue, Blue, Green, Red, Blue, Yellow, Green, Blue, Red, Blue, Yellow, Green. Twelve students, whose names had been rubbed thin enough that these lights had to stand in.\n\nNera approached them as if walking toward a grave. Darian stood beside her. Elira stood too. Maelin made an objection with her entire face, yet Elira ignored the face and did not cast. That was their compromise.\n\nNera touched the air beneath the first blue light. Her mouth tightened.\n\nNera: \"First-year.\"\n\nShe moved to the second.\n\nNera: \"Second-year. Binding-compatible.\"\n\nAt the third, green, Nera flinched.\n\nNera: \"Too young for conduit track.\"\n\nNo one spoke. Then the seventh light pulsed weakly, green at the outer ring. Iven's name already existed on the stolen corridor slate, yet this was the first outside signature that could confirm the route instead of merely repeating Lucien's architecture. Nera stared at it.\n\nNera: \"That one matches.\"\n\nDarian: \"You know him?\"\n\nNera's voice became very quiet.\n\nNera: \"Cael once gave a thermal charm to a first-year who cried during winter endurance drills.\"\n\nElira closed her eyes.\n\nHaru: \"Iven Sahl.\"\n\nNera nodded once.\n\nThe seventh green light flickered blue-white at its core, matching the conduit masking Elira had spotted on the slate. Independent recognition did not free him, yet it made him harder to erase. Senna's image fragmented.\n\nSenna: \"Time. Return to Aurelis. The academy has better old-system shielding than Lake Velis if Noctis pushes again.\"\n\nRhea: \"Under escort.\"\n\nSenna: \"Under witnesses.\"\n\nThe mirror went dark.\n\nNera remained beside the lights until they faded, then turned toward me. Fear showed in her face, directed at what she needed from me rather than at me.\n\nNera: \"If Lucien uses them the way he used Cael...\"\n\nShe could not finish. She did not need to. The Black Circle waited for my answer, and so did the room.\n\nI wanted to promise impossible things: no one else will die, I will tear Noctis open, I will bring them all back. Every one of those promises had teeth. I made myself give the only answer that did not pretend I owned the future.\n\nHaru: \"We find the route first.\"\n\nNera's face twisted, steadied if not satisfied.\n\nHaru: \"Then we decide with witnesses present.\"\n\nElira's hand slipped into mine. This time no trial answered. Only the house, old and tired and perhaps a little relieved, releasing the door wards one by one for departure.\n\nBy the time we packed, the afternoon sky could not decide whether to rain. Elira changed into her formal white-and-navy academy uniform with gold trim, Professor Valen altering the collar quickly by hand so the crystal could sit visible at her throat over a pale cloak. I pulled on a plain black coat instead of Green. No one bothered pretending Green cloth still meant anything.\n\nOutside, Lake Velis shone dull silver while two Aurelis carriages waited at the gate. Rhea checked the travel seals, Maelin argued with the luggage because apparently even bags could be medically irresponsible, and Aira tried to organize everyone into a seating order that did not accidentally imply I was either prisoner or king.\n\nAira: \"This is harder than it looks.\"\n\nLeon: \"No one thought it looked easy.\"\n\nAira: \"Excellent. Then my suffering is visible.\"\n\nCassian leaned against the lead carriage door with his right arm tucked stiffly inside his coat, watching the lake.\n\nCassian: \"Do we need a formation name?\"\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nCassian: \"Witness diamond?\"\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nCassian: \"Protective social rhombus?\"\n\nLeon: \"That one offended geometry.\"\n\nElira stood beside me on the porch steps, wrapped in her pale cloak with the crystal visible at her throat. The wind moved her hair across her cheek. She did not fix it immediately, and that small delay brought back the bedroom, the morning, and how ordinary gestures turned heavy when the world kept trying to translate them into public risk.\n\nI lifted my hand, then stopped. Elira glanced at me.\n\nElira: \"You can move my hair.\"\n\nAira, already climbing into the carriage, made a strangled noise and turned away with excessive dignity.\n\nI tucked the strand behind Elira's ear. The pendant did not flare. No trial opened. No ring sang. No Council crystal recorded the motion unless Aira was lying about looking away, which was possible. Elira's eyes softened.\n\nElira: \"See?\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"Practice knowing.\"\n\nThe phrase had become both wound and cure. I nodded.\n\nAt the bottom of the steps, Nera stood apart from the others with Darian beside her. She kept looking west, though Noctis was hidden beyond the hills, the twelve blurred crest signatures still weighing down her posture. Elira noticed too and walked down the steps before Maelin could object. I followed, not too close.\n\nNera straightened when Elira approached, old Noctis training still moving in her body after her mind had crossed the line. Elira did not comment on it.\n\nElira: \"You are allowed to be afraid for them without becoming responsible for everything Noctis did.\"\n\nNera's mouth tightened.\n\nNera: \"That sounds generous.\"\n\nElira: \"It is not. It is practical. Guilt makes bad maps.\"\n\nDarian looked at her then, surprised. Nera let out one breath that was almost a laugh and not one at all.\n\nNera: \"Aira said something similar with worse phrasing.\"\n\nAira called from the carriage window.\n\nAira: \"My phrasing survives under pressure.\"\n\nRhea: \"Your phrasing causes pressure.\"\n\nElira smiled faintly, then looked back at Nera.\n\nElira: \"When we find them, you will see things you wish you had stopped earlier.\"\n\nNera's eyes shone.\n\nElira: \"So will I.\"\n\nNera looked at the crystal at the place where Dominion had killed her. Elira did not hide it.\n\nElira: \"We keep moving anyway.\"\n\nNera nodded once. No absolution, no forgiveness forced from the future, only direction.\n\nProfessor Valen climbed into the following carriage beside Maelin and the medical supplies. When the rest of us entered the lead carriage, I sat beside Elira because pretending otherwise would give Krail's argument more respect than it deserved. Rhea, Nera, and Darian took the opposite bench with the evidence case and documents. Leon rode across from me near the window, Aira wedged herself into the corner with her notebooks, and Cassian claimed the seat nearest the door because he said it had the best dramatic exit potential. Outside on the box, the Aurelis driver told him to stop narrating.\n\nAs the restoration house receded behind us, Elira's hand found mine beneath the edge of her cloak, hidden from the road yet open to our witnesses. Aira saw and said nothing. That was how I knew she understood.\n\nThe carriage turned toward Aurelis, carrying the last warmth of Lake Velis into a world already preparing arguments against it.\n\nThe road back did not stay quiet.\n\nAt the first ridge marker, every carriage wheel locked. Wood screamed against the axle. Horses reared, and the driver shouted from the box as the carriage slewed sideways toward the drainage drop.\n\nLeon hit the window with one palm. Water burst from the roadside ditch and braced the downhill wheels. Cassian reached for flame with his frost-darkened wrist. Nothing answered. He switched hands without comment and burned through the iron locking pin under the nearest axle.\n\nNera looked past them to the road marker. Its gold travel crystal had turned black around one sentence:\n\n**RETURNING NOCTIS WITNESSES: HOLD FOR ORIGIN REVIEW**\n\nNera: \"Do not break the marker. The hold transfers to the people named.\"\n\nThe carriage slid another foot. Elira tried to stand, and Maelin's remote medical seal flashed from the following carriage before her feet reached the floor. Elira sat back down with visible hatred.\n\nRhea threw open the door.\n\nRhea: \"Who can release the axle without answering origin?\"\n\nAira was already halfway under the bench, pulling luggage straps free.\n\nAira: \"No one releases it. We redistribute weight, lift the locked wheels, and move the carriage past the marker without asking permission.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is your plan?\"\n\nAira: \"My plan is ropes. Develop criticism while tying one.\"\n\nDarian and Leon looped the straps through the roof brace. Cassian cut the second pin. Rhea anchored the rope to a roadside cedar with her limited seal. Nera climbed onto the uphill step so the hold spell would keep tracking her instead of shifting to the medical carriage behind. The marker brightened around her name. She looked afraid, yet stayed on the step.\n\nHaru: \"Ready.\"\n\nI took the rope with everyone else. No Black Circle: just shoulders, boots, wet leather, and six people pulling badly together.\n\nThe locked side of the carriage rose. The driver urged the horses forward. For one breath, the vehicle balanced on two wheels above the drop. Elira gripped the seat. Aira shouted left when she meant right, corrected herself, and no one died of the error. The carriage lurched beyond the marker and slammed back onto the road. The black hold vanished.\n\nNera jumped down after it cleared and nearly collapsed. Darian caught her injured side carefully. Cassian looked at his useless right wrist, then at the smoking pin he had cut with his left hand.\n\nCassian: \"Heroics remain banned. Improvised road engineering appears legal.\"\n\nAira: \"Barely.\"\n\nBehind us, the marker returned to gold as if nothing had happened. Rhea copied the hostile sentence into her travel log. Before the carriage moved again, Nera scratched Iven's name into the harmless side of the road marker with a loose stone: proof that an origin hold had tried to stop witnesses from reaching someone Noctis had already hidden.\n\nThe gold crystal attempted to smooth the letters away. Cassian wedged the burned iron pin beneath the marker and jammed its cleaning charm.\n\nCassian: \"Improvised road engineering. Still legal.\"\n\nThe name remained as we continued toward Aurelis.\n\nFor the first twenty minutes, the carriage held a silence full of people deciding which thoughts could survive being spoken aloud. Rain tapped lightly against the roof. The old house wards faded from my awareness one thread at a time until only Elira's pendant remained warm beside me.\n\nLeon broke the quiet.\n\nLeon: \"I am going to ask something unfortunate.\"\n\nAira did not look up from her lap.\n\nAira: \"That is your natural tone.\"\n\nLeon ignored her and looked at me.\n\nLeon: \"If the Council orders you separated from Elira during the review, what happens?\"\n\nThe carriage changed, because the question was useful. Elira's hand stayed in mine beneath the cloak. Rhea looked up from the document in her lap. Cassian stopped watching the road. Nera and Darian listened from the opposite bench as if the answer might teach them whether crossing from Noctis had truly meant entering a place where hard questions could be asked without punishment.\n\nI considered the easy answer. *I won't allow it.* The Black Circle liked that answer. For that exact reason, I did not give it.\n\nHaru: \"I do not know.\"\n\nLeon frowned, thinking it through.\n\nHaru: \"Separation after the Black Circle reveal is not the same as secrecy before it. Before the reveal, distance protected the secret. Now forced distance could repeat an old pattern: isolate the safeguard, claim containment is safety, let fewer people challenge what happens next.\"\n\nRhea nodded once.\n\nHaru: \"But if I say separation is impossible, Krail argues Elira is necessary to my control and therefore compromised.\"\n\nElira's thumb moved once across my knuckles.\n\nHaru: \"If I accept separation too easily, Lucien argues witnesses are decorative.\"\n\nLeon looked toward the rain-streaked window.\n\nLeon: \"So there is no clean answer.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nCassian leaned back.\n\nCassian: \"There is rarely a clean answer. There is usually a best next inconvenience.\"\n\nAira pointed her pen at him.\n\nAira: \"Do not accidentally become wise. It will ruin morale.\"\n\nCassian: \"My apologies.\"\n\nElira spoke then.\n\nElira: \"If they order separation, I answer first.\"\n\nRhea's eyes sharpened.\n\nElira: \"Not Haru. Me.\"\n\nMaelin, riding in the following carriage, was not present to object, though the air almost felt like it objected on her behalf. Elira continued anyway.\n\nElira: \"The question is being framed as whether I contaminate Haru's stability or he contaminates my autonomy. I will not let them debate my presence while treating me as environment.\"\n\nNera's face changed with quiet recognition. Noctis had made students into environments all the time: useful pressure, available conduit, moral example, warning. Darian looked at Elira with respect sharpened by grief.\n\nDarian: \"They will say trauma makes your answer unreliable.\"\n\nElira: \"Then Maelin answers second. As medical authority. Rhea third, as student protection. Aira fourth, because records survive things dignity cannot.\"\n\nAira placed a hand over her heart.\n\nAira: \"I am moved and insulted.\"\n\nElira: \"Good. Stay alert.\"\n\nRhea was already writing the order down. Leon looked at me again.\n\nLeon: \"And you?\"\n\nHaru: \"I answer after.\"\n\nThat cost less to say than it would cost to do. Elira heard that too. She did not praise me; she squeezed my hand once. The rain grew heavier for a stretch, then began to break.\n\nFor a while, the carriage wheels made the only steady sound. Then Nera spoke.\n\nNera: \"Noctis teaches that the first answer belongs to the highest authority in the room.\"\n\nRhea: \"Aurelis frequently teaches the same thing with better curtains.\"\n\nNera looked startled.\n\nRhea: \"We are not innocent. We are responsible.\"\n\nThe sentence settled over the carriage. I thought of Aurelis halls, rank seating, students stepping back from me, Green Circle observers learning to write because nobody had given them swords that counted.\n\nHaru: \"Responsibility is better than innocence.\"\n\nElira looked at me.\n\nHaru: \"Harder.\"\n\nShe smiled faintly.\n\nElira: \"You would choose the harder word.\"\n\nHaru: \"I learned from someone severe.\"\n\nRhea looked up.\n\nElira: \"He means me.\"\n\nRhea: \"I assumed.\"\n\nThe carriage reached the first outer Aurelis road marker in late afternoon as the clouds parted into pale, too-bright sunlight ahead of the valley. The marker's crystal lit as we passed, flickered black for one heartbeat, and settled into gold.\n\nEveryone saw. No one panicked. Aira wrote it down. Leon watched the road. Cassian warmed the carriage with a left-handed heat charm while his right wrist remained wrapped and useless beneath his sleeve. Rhea sent a coded notice ahead. Elira kept holding my hand.\n\nReturning to Aurelis would not be a single arrival. It would be a thousand small choices not to let old fear decide who spoke first.\n\n## Coda: Heroics Banned\n\nCassian objected to the phrase heroics banned until further notice for exactly nine minutes. Aira timed him.\n\nCassian: \"Some of us have branding.\"\n\nAira: \"Some of us have casualty projections.\"\n\nLeon: \"You have branding?\"\n\nCassian looked wounded.\n\nCassian: \"I have presence.\"\n\nElira, pale against my shoulder, opened one eye.\n\nElira: \"You have volume.\"\n\nThat ended the objection more effectively than policy. For a few breaths, the carriage held laughter so fragile no one leaned on it. Outside, the road toward the eastern bridge cut between wet cedar slopes and cold afternoon glare. Inside, Nera kept naming possible students behind the twelve reserve lights until her voice frayed.\n\nCassian stopped joking first. That was how everyone knew the map had become real to him. He tapped Iven's name on Aira's sequence sheet with two fingers.\n\nCassian: \"Fine. Heroics delayed.\"\n\nAira wrote it down.\n\nAira: \"Progress.\"","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-26-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep27","label":"Episode 21","number":"21","file":"episode-27-cover.webp","fallback":"The Witness Who Stepped Into the Cut","eyebrow":"Severance","recap":"","accent":["#111111","#f8fafc","#ffd166"],"displayLabel":"Episode 21","displayNumber":"21","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"Return to the Academy","body":"Aurelis did not welcome us back. It braced.\n\nThe carriage crossed the eastern bridge in late afternoon beneath a sky too bright for the mood of the academy. Lake Velis fell behind us in silver flashes between cedar trunks, and the restoration house vanished into the ridge as if the mountain had folded a secret shut.\n\nElira sat beside me with her hands folded over the pendant.\n\nShe had changed into a formal white-and-navy academy uniform trimmed in gold. Professor Valen had altered the collar herself so the black crystal could rest visible at her throat when Elira chose, hidden when she did not. Today she wore it out in the open, an argument pinned right above her pulse. I wore black.\n\nGreen cloth felt like a lie I no longer had the patience to wear. Aira had looked at the coat once, looked at me, and kept her mouth shut. That silence told me plenty.\n\nLeon rode opposite us, one knee bouncing until Rhea threatened to classify it as a hostile tremor.\n\nCassian leaned near the window, red hair catching the glare, eyes sharper than his smile. His right wrist stayed wrapped beneath his sleeve after the relay frost, every small reach favoring his left hand.\n\nNera sat across from him with both binding rings covered by plain cuffs and a fresh compression bandage crossing the shoulder Lucien's corridor had cut. Darian Thorne rode beside her, severed from Noctis in every way that mattered.\n\nAira had three notebooks open across her lap at once.\n\nHaru: \"That cannot be efficient.\"\n\nAira: \"Efficiency is for people whose enemies do not weaponize footnotes.\"\n\nElira: \"She has a point.\"\n\nI looked toward Rhea.\n\nRhea: \"Do not drag me into this. I have four copies of the same emergency order because three different offices will try to misplace it.\"\n\nThe carriage wheels struck the paved academy road. My sternum tightened.\n\nOld Aurelis magic rose from beneath the stones to meet me: cold pressure, ozone, and copper. Alert, waiting to see what stepped onto its tiles.\n\nThe academy knew the Black Circle had come back through its gates. So did everyone else.\n\nStudents lined the outer road despite Rhea's crowd order. Some leaned from upper balconies. Some crowded behind window glass. Others pretended to study notice crystals while staring so hard their wrist mana flickered.\n\nGreen Circle students clustered near the lower fountain steps. Mira Castellan stood at the front, curly auburn hair tied back and a notebook hugged to her chest. Finn hovered beside her clutching a bundle of scrolls with an expression of pure scholarly terror, while Pella, another Green records aide, gripped her slate like a buckler.\n\nWhen our carriage rolled past, nobody cheered. I was grateful for that.\n\nThen a Green Circle boy I did not recognize placed one hand over his crest and bowed, brief and unshowy, just enough to say he had seen the record and knew seeing was not owning.\n\nOthers followed. Green first, then Blue, then a handful of Red. No Yellow students moved.\n\nThen Soren Ashvale, standing under the visiting school arch in Valemere colors, touched two fingers to his scarred hand and gave a formal duelist bow. That broke the freeze.\n\nElira's hand found mine on the seat.\n\nElira: \"Breathe.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am.\"\n\nAira: \"Barely.\"\n\nI drew a full breath and let the Black Circle stay quiet beneath my skin.\n\nInstructor Dren met us at the main gate, wearing the exact stern scowl he brought to every morning drill. After everything else, his predictability felt almost like mercy.\n\nDren: \"Aerlen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Instructor.\"\n\nDren looked me up and down.\n\nDren: \"You are taller when everyone stops pretending not to look.\"\n\nCassian choked on a laugh. Dren ignored him completely.\n\nElira stepped down beside me, and Dren's eyes flicked from the pendant to her face.\n\nDren: \"Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"Instructor.\"\n\nDren: \"Medical restrictions?\"\n\nMaelin materialized behind him with a healer's satchel and the posture of judgment given legs.\n\nMaelin: \"No high-output casting, no standing at endless hearings, no martyrdom, no dueling, and no pretending emotional regulation counts as rest.\"\n\nElira: \"I agreed to most of those.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You agreed to all of them.\"\n\nDren grunted.\n\nDren: \"Good. Then you will both hate the next hour.\"\n\nHe turned toward the central courtyard.\n\nRhea's emergency authority had cleared the main path into a witness corridor. Student Council officers stood at intervals beneath hovering faculty wards. At the far end beside the great fountain, Magistra Senna's Council seal burned over the stone, carrying the sharp bite of ink and cold granite.\n\nThe repaired fountain no longer rippled backward. It watched normally now, which somehow put my teeth on edge. Professor Halden waited beside its rim, his hair noticeably more silver than it had been before Cael's funeral. His hands were empty, which made him look twice as dangerous.\n\nHaru: \"Professor.\"\n\nHalden: \"Aerlen.\"\n\nHis gaze shifted to Elira.\n\nHalden: \"Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"Professor.\"\n\nAll our tangled history hung between us: his early suspicion, my concealment, the old observatory, and his decision not to lock me in a cell when he had the chance.\n\nHalden: \"I have been asked to chair Aurelis' internal Black Circle stability observation.\"\n\nLeon muttered under his breath.\n\nLeon: \"That sounds comforting.\"\n\nHalden's eyes cut toward him. Leon straightened.\n\nHalden: \"It is not intended to comfort.\"\n\nAira: \"Spectacular. We are doomed by branding.\"\n\nHalden let the remark pass, though his jaw twitched.\n\nHalden: \"I refused twice. The first request from the board called it classification. The second from Councilor Krail's office called it containment readiness. I accepted only after Magistra Senna rewrote the mandate.\"\n\nRhea: \"Witness stability.\"\n\nHalden: \"My task is to record whether Haru Aerlen remains answerable under pressure, not to decide what he is.\"\n\nI studied his face.\n\nHaru: \"And if I do not?\"\n\nHalden's mouth hardened.\n\nHalden: \"Then I record that too, with witnesses standing beside you, before anyone turns fear into a locked room.\"\n\nIt was the closest thing to trust Halden could afford. I took it.\n\nAbove the fountain, Senna's remote seal flared open.\n\nSenna: \"Aurelis, report.\"\n\nRhea stepped forward, crisp as flint.\n\nRhea: \"Haru Aerlen, Elira Vale, and registered witnesses have returned under escort. No hostile Black Circle manifestation in transit. One Noctis origin-hold attempt at the outer ridge marker was bypassed and logged. A partial hostile registry fragment names Iven Sahl; independent conduit confirmation remains pending.\"\n\nSenna: \"Received. Drakenshard and Valemere have sealed their student registries. Three minor academies report unauthorized relay pings. Noctis Dominion College has refused our conduit audit.\"\n\nColor drained from Nera's face. Darian's fists clenched at his sides.\n\nSenna: \"We have confirmed that at least twelve Noctis conduit-track students were moved out of their dormitory wing during the funeral.\"\n\nThe courtyard went dead quiet. Twelve. Cael had been one, and Lucien still had eleven more on the board.\n\nNera stepped into the seal's light.\n\nNera: \"Do you have names?\"\n\nSenna hesitated for half a beat.\n\nDarian: \"Magistra.\"\n\nSenna: \"Confirmed. Iven Sahl is among the missing.\"\n\nThe name hit like a second blow on an unhealed bruise. I remembered Iven from Cael's funeral: pale, first-year, standing in the separate Noctis cluster, holding onto a story about a borrowed thermal charm as proof Cael had been kind when nobody was watching.\n\nElira's pendant warmed against her throat.\n\nHaru: \"Lucien showed us reserves.\"\n\nHalden: \"Say that again.\"\n\nI laid it out slowly: the Noctis corridor, the hanging crest lights, Nera stepping one body-length into the dark, the slate she tore free, and Lucien's single word. Reserves.\n\nBy the time I finished, Dren's knuckles were white, Maelin had stopped pretending she was only here to monitor Elira, and Rhea's council sigil burned dark crimson.\n\nSenna: \"Then the Council has probable cause for a protective audit of Noctis.\"\n\nCouncilor Krail's voice sliced across the projection.\n\nKrail: \"The Council has probable cause to contain the extra-classification student who provoked Lucien Voss into threatening children.\"\n\nThe fountain water flattened into glass. Krail's image formed beside Senna's, sharp and unwelcome.\n\nKrail: \"Do not confuse sequence with innocence. Voss is a criminal actor, and Aerlen is the event around which criminal actors are now organizing.\"\n\nElira stepped forward. Maelin hissed a warning, but Elira kept her hands still and let her voice carry.\n\nElira: \"No. Lucien organized cruelty long before Haru stepped into public view. Talen Mire proves it. Cael proves it. Nera and Oric prove it. Noctis' buried doctrine proves it. You do not get to blame Haru for the rot his exposure dragged into the light.\"\n\nKrail: \"Miss Vale, you remain under trauma review.\"\n\nElira's smile had frost on it.\n\nElira: \"And you remain determined not to hear what the trauma actually points to.\"\n\nMurmurs rippled through the courtyard, tight with anger in Elira's favor. Krail heard them. His expression stayed smooth, yet Red Circle pressure slipped through the remote seal like a blade testing silk.\n\nThe Black Circle answered under my ribs, hungry and cold. I pinned it down before it broke surface.\n\nHalden's eyes snapped to my chest.\n\nHaru: \"Recorded?\"\n\nHalden swallowed once.\n\nHalden: \"Recorded.\"\n\nKrail smiled thinly.\n\nKrail: \"How cooperative.\"\n\nA sharp crack split the air.\n\nA black fracture tore vertically down the fountain's water column from rim to basin. Too old for Lucien's frost, too structured for stray mana. Gold-black letters burned across the mist above the stone.\n\n**CIRCLE ZERO SAFEGUARD REVIEW: FIELD RESPONSE REQUIRED**\n\nMira's notebook hit the paving stones. Another line scrolled beneath the first.\n\n**DOMINION ROUTE DETECTED BELOW ACADEMY INFRASTRUCTURE**\n\nProfessor Valen's hand froze over her slate.\n\nHalden: \"Impossible.\"\n\nRhea: \"Explain impossible quickly.\"\n\nValen: \"Noctis should have zero access to the lower observatory after the Foundation locks.\"\n\nThe fountain mist answered before she finished.\n\n**ACCESS POINT: OLD TOURNAMENT MEDICAL ANNEX**\n\nNera flinched as if struck.\n\nNera: \"That is where conduit-track students were evaluated before matches.\"\n\nDarian: \"Where Cael was prepared.\"\n\nPanic in the courtyard sharpened into direction. Lucien hadn't only stolen students in Noctis. He had left a hook buried under Aurelis itself, inside the wing where visiting students were supposed to be safe.\n\nDren pivoted toward the lower path.\n\nDren: \"Combat team.\"\n\nLeon rolled his shoulders. Cassian bared his teeth in a joyless grin, left hand curling into a fist. Aira snapped two notebooks shut and kept the third open. Rhea fired off three perimeter orders before anyone else drew breath.\n\nElira stepped up beside me.\n\nMaelin: \"Absolutely not.\"\n\nElira: \"I am not casting high-output.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You are walking straight into a Dominion route.\"\n\nElira: \"I know what I am walking into.\"\n\nMaelin's eyes narrowed.\n\nMaelin: \"That is not a medical exemption.\"\n\nI looked down at Elira. The trial's last condition still echoed inside my ribs: if she asks as herself, with the risk named plainly, then yes.\n\nHaru: \"She comes if she chooses.\"\n\nMaelin glared at me as if torn between fury and reluctant respect. Elira's knuckles brushed mine once, brief and grounding, then we turned toward the old medical annex under a hundred watching eyes.\n\n---\n\nThe first person to break formation and follow us was Mira.\n\nShe ran three steps, caught herself because sprinting after an emergency escort looked like panic, and settled into a brisk walk with Finn and Pella half a pace behind her.\n\nRhea spotted them without turning around.\n\nRhea: \"Castellan.\"\n\nMira stopped short.\n\nMira: \"President Valmont.\"\n\nRhea: \"This route is restricted.\"\n\nMira flushed, glanced at me and Elira, then back toward the glowing warning still fading above the fountain.\n\nMira: \"Then restrict me properly.\"\n\nFinn sucked in a breath as if he had just watched a friend step off a roof. Aira's brows shot up. Rhea turned slowly.\n\nMira held her notebook tighter, hands trembling, voice steady.\n\nMira: \"Green Circle students have been used as audience, rumor, and cover since this started. Our notes mattered in the Foundation gallery when nobody else bothered to write down what mercy looked like. If Noctis dug a route under our own floors because no one important watches ordinary students first, then you need someone ordinary on the record.\"\n\nThe courtyard went still. Even Dren waited.\n\nMira: \"I am not asking to fight Lucien. I would die in three seconds and embarrass everyone. I am asking to witness what happens to students who are easy to ignore.\"\n\nFinn raised one shaking hand beside her.\n\nFinn: \"I can index erased records.\"\n\nRhea turned her glare on him. Finn looked like he deeply regretted existing, yet Pella stepped up beside him and raised her slate an inch higher.\n\nFinn: \"Quietly.\"\n\nPella: \"We can keep count.\"\n\nAira's mouth softened with something close to pride. Rhea checked Dren.\n\nDren: \"Behind the combat line.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Behind my medical line.\"\n\nHalden: \"Where their notes stay uncontaminated by direct interference.\"\n\nAira: \"That is three different lines and none of you drew a map.\"\n\nRhea: \"Then you are drawing it.\"\n\nAira: \"I regret being competent.\"\n\nMira's shoulders dropped an inch, tension settling into focus. Rhea stepped forward and pressed a coin-sized crimson council seal onto Mira's notebook cover, then onto Finn's scroll strap and Pella's slate.\n\nRhea: \"You are not down there to prove bravery, or to turn Green Circle students into martyrs. You leave the instant Dren, Maelin, Aira, or I give the word.\"\n\nMira: \"Understood, President.\"\n\nFinn tucked his scrolls tight under one arm and straightened his posture.\n\nFinn: \"Understood.\"\n\nAira leaned down toward him.\n\nAira: \"If I tell you to leave in a very polite voice, sprint.\"\n\nFinn: \"That sounds backwards.\"\n\nLeon: \"It is not.\"\n\nThe second interruption came from a Yellow Circle senior in spotless white and gold, wearing the bright certainty of someone who thought emergencies were stages for rebuilding reputation.\n\nSenior: \"If Black Circle action is occurring on academy grounds, Yellow Circle rank should be represented.\"\n\nElira turned slowly. The senior lost half his color.\n\nElira: \"Represented for whom?\"\n\nSenior: \"For senior student oversight.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe senior tried to look past her at me. Elira stepped squarely into his sightline.\n\nElira: \"If you want to be useful, report to shelter coordination and keep first-years away from rumor crystals.\"\n\nSenior: \"I am a Yellow Circle...\"\n\nElira: \"So am I. Rank is not usefulness. Go be useful.\"\n\nThe senior retreated, stiff at first, then considerably faster once Maelin looked at him.\n\nHaru: \"You enjoyed that.\"\n\nElira: \"A little.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good.\"\n\nShe blinked, then smiled despite the warning script, the twelve missing kids, and the dark annex waiting ahead. That small spark didn't erase the dread, yet it reminded us what we were walking down to protect.\n\nAt the eastern colonnade, the crowd thinned to faculty aides, council runners, and students pretending to have errands near the restricted wing. Nobody bowed here. They watched.\n\nOne Blue Circle boy took a single step backward as I passed.\n\nMy chest tightened, though the Black Circle stayed still. Elira saw it and kept her hands at her sides, honoring our rule.\n\nA small Green Circle girl by the colonnade pillar saw the boy flinch. Fear crossed her face, followed fast by fierce, reckless fairness.\n\nGirl: \"He saved Cael.\"\n\nThe Blue student flushed. I stopped walking, and the whole escort stopped with me.\n\nI met the girl's eyes.\n\nHaru: \"I tried to.\"\n\nThe correction cut across the colonnade. The girl blinked, confused.\n\nElira knelt smoothly despite Maelin's quiet grunt of protest.\n\nElira: \"Cael saved me. Haru tried to save him before that. Both are true.\"\n\nGirl: \"But Cael still died.\"\n\nElira's gold eyes never wavered, alive because of the boy we were still learning how to mourn without turning him into a speech.\n\nElira: \"He did.\"\n\nThe girl looked back up at me.\n\nGirl: \"Are you angry?\"\n\nEvery adult in the colonnade went silent. I thought about giving her a neat, harmless lie, then decided she deserved the truth without theatrics.\n\nHaru: \"I am. But anger does not get to give orders.\"\n\nThe words felt strange coming out of my mouth, less like advice and more like a guardrail I needed for myself. The girl nodded with solemn gravity, then took one step aside, clearing the path without shrinking away.\n\nThe Blue student looked down in quiet shame. I didn't absolve him, and I didn't glare at him. Fear wasn't a crime. We kept walking.\n\nAt the annex gate, Examiner Seraphine Morn waited in tactical black with red trim, hair pulled tight, one hand resting against the iron gate ward.\n\nCassian straightened immediately.\n\nSeraphine: \"Veyr.\"\n\nCassian: \"Lady Morn.\"\n\nSeraphine shifted her gaze to me.\n\nSeraphine: \"Aerlen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Examiner.\"\n\nHer eyes swept Elira, the crystal at her collar, and the column behind us.\n\nSeraphine: \"The gate holds three conflicting locks: medical archive, tournament jurisdiction, and visiting custody. Whoever rigged it wanted investigators arguing on the steps.\"\n\nRhea: \"Can you open it?\"\n\nSeraphine: \"I can.\"\n\nRhea: \"Cleanly?\"\n\nA faint edge touched Seraphine's mouth.\n\nSeraphine: \"No.\"\n\nDren stepped up beside her, hand on his hilt.\n\nDren: \"Then open it loudly.\"\n\nSeraphine pressed her palm flat to the ward. Sharp Red Circle light flared around her fingers. Dren laid his combat seal beside it, Rhea locked her council mark above both, and Valen threaded older Aurelis script beneath the hinge. Halden refused to let the gate unlatch without reading every syllable between the wards as it broke.\n\nAira scribbled every layer down while Mira watched wide-eyed.\n\nFinn whispered to Aira.\n\nFinn: \"The verbs are not boring.\"\n\nAira: \"Then make the nouns work harder.\"\n\nThe iron gate groaned inward. Cold antiseptic herbs and dried ink drifted out of the stairwell, stale as an inspection report nobody ever meant to read aloud.\n\nElira's fingers brushed mine once before we stepped over the threshold. I walked down as Haru Aerlen, and the Black Circle followed behind my ribs, answerable for every inch.\n\n---\n\nThe annex did not welcome us all at once. It sorted us by color.\n\nGreen. Blue. Red. Yellow. Black.\n\nThe gate crystal lit each circle in order until it reached me, where it failed and left a dark ring around my wrist, an empty space where old Aurelis classification refused to name what the courtyard had already seen.\n\nHalden leaned over the crystal.\n\nHalden: \"Interesting.\"\n\nAira: \"Professor, please stop calling ominous traps interesting while we are standing inside them.\"\n\nHalden: \"Would you prefer I find them dull?\"\n\nLeon: \"Frankly, yes.\"\n\nLeon shifted his weight forward, shoulders squared. Halden ignored him, which remained his favorite teaching tool.\n\nValen touched the crystal rim with one gloved finger.\n\nValen: \"It is not hiding him.\"\n\nRhea: \"Then what is it doing?\"\n\nValen: \"Refusing to log him under medical custody.\"\n\nThe crystal flickered. My false Green Circle surfaced for half a beat, collided with the public Black Circle trace, and shattered apart. Haru Aerlen stayed unlisted.\n\nBeing unclassifiable wasn't freedom. Things outside a ledger were usually where cages got built.\n\nElira studied the dim stone instead of touching her pendant.\n\nElira: \"Log that the annex failed to classify Haru without asking him to perform.\"\n\nRhea: \"Logged.\"\n\nHalden's pen scratched fast. Seraphine watched me with hard, battlefield assessment.\n\nSeraphine: \"Can you still move?\"\n\nHaru: \"I can.\"\n\nI held her gaze without flinching, grateful she hadn't wasted breath asking if I felt fine.\n\nTen paces down the hall, a glass case held rows of visiting tournament clearance badges. Each carried a school seal, a color band, and a thin strip for medical notes. Most were blank.\n\nOne was not.\n\nNera stopped so fast Darian nearly walked into her.\n\nThe badge was Noctis white. Someone had scraped the student's name raw off the bone strip, leaving only the health note inked cleanly beneath:\n\n**CONDUIT CANDIDATE RESPONSIVE TO EXTERNAL CROWN PRESSURE**\n\nNobody breathed. Crown terminology didn't belong in a thirty-ninth tournament review, not unless someone had been testing older Dominion tools under Aurelis' roof.\n\nValen went pale in the way scholars did when a modern nightmare turned up an older footnote.\n\nHalden: \"Professor.\"\n\nValen: \"Crown pressure is not tournament phrasing.\"\n\nSeraphine: \"Noctis?\"\n\nValen: \"Or something Noctis dug up and copied.\"\n\nThe glass shuddered. A faint blue-white line bled across the scraped name strip.\n\nNera whispered into the glass.\n\nNera: \"Iven.\"\n\nDarian: \"Are you sure?\"\n\nNera shook her head, then stopped herself.\n\nNera: \"No. I am afraid.\"\n\nMira stepped closer, the red observer seal glowing on her notebook cover.\n\nMira: \"They scrubbed his name and kept his function. That means the room cared what he could carry, not who he was.\"\n\nThe glass cracked down the center. Old record magic hated being called out plainly.\n\nFinn stared at the hairline split, swallowing hard.\n\nFinn: \"Someone deleted the name on purpose while leaving the use-tag intact. The erasure left a seam.\"\n\nAira's pen flashed across the page.\n\nAira: \"Documented. Moving.\"\n\nAt the next turn, rough corridor stone gave way to white infirmary tile, easy to scrub clean. The air turned frigid. Shoulder-high brackets lined the walls where monitoring crystals once sat. Leon touched the nearest bracket, and condensation immediately beaded over his fingertips.\n\nLeon: \"Used recently.\"\n\nSeraphine: \"How recently?\"\n\nLeon closed his eyes, blue light tracing his wrist.\n\nLeon: \"After the funeral.\"\n\nThe sentence landed like a bolt sliding shut behind us. Somebody hadn't just left old traps down here. Someone had worked this wing after Cael was buried, while the Council thought Noctis was frozen.\n\nRhea's voice dropped to ice.\n\nRhea: \"Pull every Aurelis internal access log.\"\n\nAira flipped a page.\n\nAira: \"Requesting now.\"\n\nRhea: \"No. Seizing now. This entire wing is under student protection evidence hold.\"\n\nSeraphine's mouth curved faintly.\n\nSeraphine: \"Aggressive.\"\n\nRhea: \"Necessary.\"\n\nUp ahead, Dren raised one fist. Everyone froze.\n\nFrom down the tiled hall came a steady scrape. Porcelain against metal. Spoon against bowl. Once. Twice. Three times.\n\nMira's face went white.\n\nMira: \"Why is that worse than screaming?\"\n\nMaelin, who had marched straight down through the gate despite swearing earlier she wouldn't touch ancient nonsense, answered quietly.\n\nMaelin: \"Because someone is trying to make captivity sound domestic.\"\n\nElira's eyes hardened. Dren didn't wait for speeches; he drew his blade low and walked straight toward the sound. We followed at his heels.\n\n---\n\nThe spoon sound stopped at a tiled vestibule whose walls were covered in layered inscriptions. Beyond a heavy ajar door lay the first examination room.\n\nHalden ran his gaze across the wall script.\n\nHalden: \"These were altered.\"\n\nValen: \"No. Overwritten. Older Aurelis grammar sits underneath. Noctis just traced over the scar.\"\n\nCold settled deeper behind my ribs.\n\nHaru: \"This wing connects to Circle Zero.\"\n\nValen glanced back at me.\n\nValen: \"It may connect straight to the failure.\"\n\nDren pushed the heavy door wide, and we stepped into the first examination room. Its brass lintel plate read:\n\n**VISITING STUDENT RECOVERY ROOM - CROWN FIELD BLOCK**\n\nCael had been brought here after I stabilized him in secret. The stone didn't have feelings, yet mana remembered habits, and this room had been trained by fear. Too many iron anchor rings bolted beneath the bed frame. Too many drain sigils masked as healer monitors.\n\nValen stopped dead on the threshold, refusing to set foot inside until Dren and Maelin had swept the room. Even then, Nera stayed by the overwritten wall near the doorway, pressing two fingers lightly to the tile.\n\nNera: \"Noctis teams were told Aurelis demanded extra readings after cross-school matches.\"\n\nDarian: \"And Noctis handlers stayed to supervise.\"\n\nLeon: \"So Noctis used Aurelis infirmary rooms to prep conduits?\"\n\nNera's jaw tightened.\n\nNera: \"They did.\"\n\nMira's pen scratched loud across her paper. Aira leaned over her shoulder.\n\nAira: \"Dates, room number, witnesses. Keep the verbs boring. Boring verbs survive court.\"\n\nBeneath the bed, Lucien's first trap exhaled.\n\nInk-thin black rings slid across the white tile toward the doorway, straight at Mira and Pella. I surged forward, then checked myself as Elira's fingers caught my sleeve. She wasn't holding me back. She was reminding me I wasn't alone.\n\nLeon moved first. Three ribbons of water snapped from his crest, sweeping under the ink rings to lift the floor film and break their grip on the tile. Cassian followed with a thread of red flame from his left hand, his wrapped right wrist braced tight against his ribs, burning the feed current in the air above the rings rather than the rings themselves.\n\nAira pointed straight left.\n\nAira: \"Third wall bracket!\"\n\nDren's blade shattered the iron bracket before I finished turning. The black rings vanished in a puff of cold ash.\n\nThe Black Circle stayed quiet beneath my coat. Everyone looked at me anyway, then let out a collective breath.\n\nLeon scowled at the relief on their faces.\n\nLeon: \"Obviously that worked.\"\n\nCassian clapped his shoulder with his good hand.\n\nCassian: \"Careful, Varek. Smile any harder and you will ruin your brand.\"\n\nLeon: \"I hate every one of you.\"\n\nElira laughed softly, and the room felt one fraction less like a tomb.\n\nJust beyond the recovery room, an open stone arch led into a lower side chamber where twelve slate tablets hovered over a cracked stone basin. Each tablet listed a crest color, age, conduit rating, and route susceptibility. The name lines had been scorched clean, yet old record magic kept flaring dimly around the blanks.\n\nFinn stepped up to the basin without waiting for orders, scrolls tucked tight.\n\nFinn: \"The deletion followed a fixed syntax. I cannot pull the names back cleanly without tripping a backlash, yet I can prove twelve active student files were intentionally purged.\"\n\nAira clicked her tongue in approval.\n\nAira: \"Boring proof wins trials.\"\n\nNera hovered near the third tablet, pointing with a trembling hand at a faint blue-white shimmer clinging to the blank space.\n\nNera: \"Winter-drill weave. Cael built a thermal charm like this for Iven.\"\n\nDarian looked away, throat working. Elira stepped up beside Nera.\n\nElira: \"Then he is in the record. That is where we start pulling him out.\"\n\nNera blinked back tears and gave one sharp nod.\n\nBehind Finn's basin, the rear wall parted smoothly without anyone touching a stone. Lucien wanted us looking inside.\n\nA wide circular room dropped half a flight below the annex floor, older than the corridors above. Seven chairs ringed a sunken relay well, each fitted with white metal and black thread restraints. Above the well hung twelve small crest lights, spectral hooks waiting to yank twelve missing students straight through Aurelis infrastructure the second the right trigger dropped.\n\nThe Black Circle slammed upward so hard the wall sconces guttered dark.\n\nElira stepped straight in front of me, meeting my eyes, never flinching from what lived inside my chest.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI froze. If she asks as herself. She was standing there as herself, and I stopped.\n\nLucien's smooth voice floated up from the sunken well.\n\nLucien: \"Touching. He takes a leash well.\"\n\nElira's face turned to carved marble.\n\nElira: \"He listens. There is a difference.\"\n\nLucien: \"In desperate women, the two look remarkably alike.\"\n\nThe jab was aimed at her, yet it struck the whole room. Aira's pen stopped dead. Rhea's council seal flared crimson. Maelin looked ready to figure out how to slap a man through a relay mirror.\n\nMy anger rose clean and sharp, entirely mine. Lucien believed that lie down to his marrow: that love under fear was weakness begging for hierarchy.\n\nElira didn't glance back for my permission.\n\nElira: \"You keep mistaking fear for inferiority because you have never understood how people act when they are terrified and still refuse to yield.\"\n\nLucien: \"Fear narrows choice.\"\n\nElira: \"Of course it does. Pain narrows choice. Institutions narrow choice. That is why consent under pressure has to be guarded instead of mocked. You manufacture the pressure, wait for someone's knees to buckle, and call their collapse proof that you own them.\"\n\nA faint chime rang through the ancient stone beneath our feet.\n\nValen caught her breath.\n\nValen: \"That line.\"\n\nHalden: \"I heard it.\"\n\nLucien's image stayed composed, yet frost crept harder up the rim of the relay well. Elira had struck the structural pin.\n\nLucien: \"And you dress interference up as affection.\"\n\nElira: \"I use affection as a reason to stay present while asking better questions.\"\n\nShe raised one hand toward the twelve crest lights.\n\nElira: \"First: who benefits if Haru acts alone?\"\n\nThe black threads strung from the well shivered.\n\nAira answered first, cold and quick.\n\nAira: \"Lucien.\"\n\nRhea: \"Krail's containment bloc.\"\n\nHalden: \"Anyone looking for a single classified weapon instead of an institutional failure.\"\n\nNera: \"Noctis.\"\n\nElira: \"Second: who benefits if Haru does nothing?\"\n\nLeon: \"Lucien.\"\n\nMira: \"Whoever holds the missing students right now.\"\n\nFinn: \"The erasure ledger.\"\n\nElira: \"Then neither alone nor nothing works. Third: who is present enough to refuse?\"\n\nLight bloomed gently along the ancient floor seams, warm enough to remind the circular room it had once been built for healing before someone turned it into an apparatus. The cold weight behind my sternum shifted.\n\nHaru: \"Everyone here.\"\n\nLucien's lip curled.\n\nLucien: \"A crowd is not a chain of command.\"\n\nRhea: \"Correct.\"\n\nAira: \"Try to keep up.\"\n\nLeon muttered under his breath.\n\nLeon: \"Stop baiting the remote sadist.\"\n\nCassian: \"Let her work.\"\n\nLucien's patience snapped. Frost slammed outward across the well, dimming all twelve lights.\n\nGold light gathered around Elira's hands, razor-thin and steady, obeying her anger without spilling past her limits.\n\nMaelin: \"Watch your output, Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"I know my edge.\"\n\nSeven gold needles lanced out and pinned the air around the well, exposing the black grammar threads binding each light to the seven chairs. Each thread carried a glowing label: Obedience, Return, Proof, Reserve, Use, Property, Final.\n\nCael's last note had rejected every one of those words.\n\nDarian swore softly. Nera's plain cuffs split open as her binding rings flared blue-gray, snapping around the threads. She did not sever them; she indexed them, carrying every lesson she and Oric had survived.\n\nNera: \"Thread one hooks into Noctis' dormitory basement. Thread two links to delegation mirror residue. Thread three...\"\n\nHer voice cracked.\n\nElira: \"Nera.\"\n\nNera swallowed hard.\n\nNera: \"Thread three runs straight through Cael's old conduit scar.\"\n\nLucien had built his bridge out of the institutional wound left behind after Cael died. The Black Circle surged at my feet, eager to wipe the whole chamber out of existence. I held it down as a thin ring of shadow around my boots.\n\nHaru: \"How do we drop the hooks without dragging the kids through?\"\n\nValen: \"Split the weight.\"\n\nHalden: \"Dangerous. Each hook wants a single dominant caster to seize it. If Haru grabs them all, Lucien claims Black Circle seizure. If we hack them blindly, the students eat the backlash.\"\n\nLeon rolled his neck.\n\nLeon: \"So we each carry a piece.\"\n\nHalden blinked at him.\n\nLeon: \"What? I listen sometimes.\"\n\nCassian: \"Mark the calendar.\"\n\nDren: \"Focus.\"\n\nWitness magic hated secrecy, so we broke the circuit right in front of Lucien's eyes. Leon drowned Obedience beneath flowing water so it couldn't lock. Cassian scorched Return at the edges without touching the names underneath. Rhea pinned Proof under council evidentiary law. Aira dissected Reserve in ink until the word looked obscene. Mira gripped her pen with trembling hands and wrote along the glowing air beside Use: a person cannot be a function. Finn wrapped Property in his seam-recovery syntax. Nera held Final, tears running down her cheeks while her rings bit deep.\n\nElira anchored the center with her seven gold needles. I stayed on the outer ring, hands empty.\n\nLucien: \"Still hiding from yourself, Aerlen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Still hiding behind children, Voss.\"\n\nFrost cracked sharply inside the well. Halden caught it instantly.\n\nHalden: \"He is holding these hooks on pride as much as craft.\"\n\nLucien: \"Your ambition is showing, Professor.\"\n\nHalden: \"Good. It means I am still a flawed man, not a doctrine pretending to be clean.\"\n\nOne by one, the threads parted. Obedience dissolved in Leon's water. Return unraveled in Cassian's flame. Proof buckled, Reserve emptied, Use snapped as Mira's hand steadied, and Property cracked under Finn's ledger script.\n\nFinal fought back.\n\nNera screamed as the seventh thread turned pitch black and yanked inward, trying to turn her own binding rings into a fresh conduit tether. Darian lunged and caught her shoulders. Maelin cursed, Elira's gold light flared, and I crossed the ring in two strides.\n\nNobody tried to stop me this time.\n\nI set one palm above the black thread and opened the Black Circle solely as a boundary: a wall with a door cut through it.\n\nHaru: \"Final does not belong to you.\"\n\nLucien: \"Then whose is it?\"\n\nI looked straight at Nera, shaking, furious, and still standing.\n\nNera: \"Nobody's.\"\n\nThe seventh thread shattered.\n\nEleven crest lights shot upward through the ceiling and scattered back into the wider relay net, free of Aurelis' traps.\n\nOne light stayed behind. Small, pale blue-white. Iven Sahl's trace.\n\nInstead of rising, it darted sideways toward a low stone archway beside the recovery room stairs, opening into an old service tunnel.\n\nLeon stepped forward.\n\nLeon: \"That pulse is local. Right underneath us.\"\n\nDren drew his blade clean. Cassian raised a left-handed flame.\n\nLucien's whisper drifted up out of the empty well, soft and poisonous.\n\nLucien: \"One left, then. Let us see what the Black Circle does with a boy who still belongs to Noctis.\"\n\n---\n\nNobody charged the tunnel mouth. That single choice kept us alive.\n\nThe blue-white pulse shivered at the edge of the dark, followed by a thin sound like breath trapped inside glass. Every protective instinct in my chest lunged toward it. Elira stepped half a pace closer, close enough for the pendant to raise its silent question between us: were we answering the boy, or a trap shaped like him?\n\nHaru: \"Both.\"\n\nElira: \"Then we take them apart piece by piece.\"\n\nRhea's council seal flared beside the low archway.\n\nRhea: \"Protocol first.\"\n\nLeon stared at her as if the word had slapped him.\n\nRhea: \"Yes, Varek, even now.\"\n\nAira was already writing.\n\nAira: \"Especially now. Protocol is how we stop Haru from becoming a tragic solution with good cheekbones.\"\n\nCassian glanced at me.\n\nCassian: \"Harsh, though fair.\"\n\nHaru: \"Fair is enough.\"\n\nLeon's mouth twitched into a brief, crooked grin, and the tension eased just enough for people to think straight.\n\nValen knelt at the tunnel threshold and held a strip of archive vellum over the opening. The edge browned without catching fire. Halden crouched across from her, his slate hovering over the floor sigils as his fingers traced the diagram's border on the stone.\n\nHalden: \"Three layers. First, a distress lure keyed to familiar names. Second, custody grammar waiting for unauthorized extraction. Third...\"\n\nHe paused, frowning as broken old circles glowed beneath the Noctis hooks.\n\nHalden: \"The tunnel predates the annex. Noctis wove doctrine through it, yet they did not lay the foundation.\"\n\nValen's eyes sharpened.\n\nValen: \"Tournament annexes were built on top of older recovery halls. If this began as a pre-Circle witness infirmary...\"\n\nElira finished the thought quietly.\n\nElira: \"Then rescue lived down here before anyone sorted students into ranks.\"\n\nMira stepped close to Halden's diagram, still pale from holding Use.\n\nMira: \"Can the older magic help us?\"\n\nValen laid her palm flat on the floor stone outside the tunnel.\n\nValen: \"Aurelis witness inquiry. We are not claiming patient custody. We are checking whether a living student is below and whether the path carries active coercion.\"\n\nThe older floor circles flared gold. The Noctis hooks overhead jerked tight. Somewhere down in the dark, a muffled sound broke loose, a child fighting not to cry out.\n\nThe Black Circle surged in my chest. Instead of burying it, I forced it out onto my wrist as a thin rotating band of black and silver, open for every witness in the room to watch.\n\nHaru: \"Boundary only.\"\n\nHalden's pen moved instantly.\n\nHalden: \"Minimal manifestation. Witnessed. Stated function: boundary.\"\n\nDren kept his eyes on the dark tunnel.\n\nDren: \"Useful.\"\n\nHe caught the weight of his own word and added quietly.\n\nDren: \"By choice.\"\n\nLeon stepped up to the mouth and sent a thin ribbon of water creeping along the stone floor, sliding cleanly around each white hook without brushing it.\n\nLeon: \"There are pressure plates. Emotional ones.\"\n\nAira looked up from her page. Leon scowled.\n\nLeon: \"I heard how it sounded. Keep writing.\"\n\nThe water ribbon touched the edge of the first hook, and a boy's thin whisper drifted back along the current.\n\nIven: \"I didn't mean to be cold.\"\n\nNera clapped both hands over her mouth. Darian's eyes went flat with murder.\n\nLeon shifted the water from a line into a shallow cup, holding the echo in place without letting the hook drag it back to trigger the trap.\n\nLeon: \"First hook runs on guilt.\"\n\nFinn marked his scroll fast.\n\nFinn: \"Guilt lure logged. No custody transfer.\"\n\nThe white hook dimmed. Down the narrow throat of the tunnel, five more hooks lit in sequence like poisoned lanterns: Cold. Hunger. Shame. Obedience. Gratitude.\n\nAt the last word, Nera trembled violently.\n\nNera: \"They drill gratitude into us as debt.\"\n\nDarian gave one short, grim nod from beside her.\n\nElira crouched at the threshold and spoke gently into the dark.\n\nElira: \"Iven Sahl, if you hear me, you owe nobody pain for warmth you were given.\"\n\nThe blue-white trace steadied and drifted a single inch closer. Alive. Listening.\n\nLucien's voice slid back through the cold air, thinner now.\n\nLucien: \"How slow you are. Children suffer while you polish your scruples.\"\n\nThe hook named Gratitude flared bright. He wanted speed. He wanted panic to turn rescue into seizure.\n\nElira looked straight into the blackness.\n\nElira: \"Children suffer because you built rooms that treat rescue like ownership.\"\n\nGold flared through the ancient Aurelis foundation stones. The archway groaned wider, Lucien's voice cut off, and cold air spilled out across our boots.\n\nFrom deep below, a child sobbed once before a gag spell snapped over his mouth.\n\nCassian lunged forward, and Dren caught him hard by the shoulder. The fact that Cassian let himself be held told me how badly that sound had cut him.\n\nDren: \"Line holds.\"\n\nCassian's jaw flexed.\n\nCassian: \"A child just...\"\n\nDren: \"I heard him. That is why the line holds.\"\n\nCold poured out of the tunnel mouth, thick with the sensory ghost of winter drill yards and numb fingers. Maelin swept low healer light across the threshold without crossing the stone line.\n\nMaelin: \"Live pain response. Breath cut, followed by a suppression spell. Not an archive echo.\"\n\nPella pressed her slate tight against her ribs, voice barely audible.\n\nPella: \"Did he hear us?\"\n\nNera stared down into the dark.\n\nNera: \"Maybe. Or the room wants us careless.\"\n\nMira swallowed hard, clutching her pen.\n\nMira: \"How do we tell without hurting him?\"\n\nValen turned slowly, struck by the question.\n\nValen: \"We stop demanding certainty first. We test in a way that never punishes the boy if we read it wrong.\"\n\nThe wall sconces dimmed slightly at the word boy.\n\nAira glanced up at the ceiling.\n\nAira: \"Notice how it hates ordinary nouns.\"\n\nSeraphine flicked a thin red thread six feet into the tunnel. It folded straight back against her palm.\n\nSeraphine: \"Spatial loop.\"\n\nLeon tested the air currents with water-sense, frowning hard.\n\nLeon: \"It stretches longer the faster we push.\"\n\nElira knelt beside the threshold despite Maelin's immediate glare and laid two fingertips against the stone lip, letting a patient glow rest at the edge without crossing inside.\n\nElira: \"Iven Sahl. You do not have to answer if answering hurts.\"\n\nThe tunnel stone shifted with a dull grind, shortening by the width of a single brick.\n\nAira dropped to one knee beside Elira, eyes bright.\n\nAira: \"Look at the masonry. Commands lengthen the hall. Permission shortens it.\"\n\nValen stepped back to the ancient inscription carved beside the archway, reading the worn lettering aloud by touch.\n\nValen: \"Witness proximity... patient consent... no single bearer... room opens when pain is named by the one bearing it...\"\n\nShe stopped abruptly. Halden stepped close and read the scarred final line softly.\n\nHalden: \"Companion must be chosen before treatment begins.\"\n\nMaelin spun around toward the open doorway of the recovery room just behind us. I turned with her.\n\nInside Cael's old room, the lone iron observation chair sat far back from the empty bed frame.\n\nHaru: \"There used to be two chairs.\"\n\nAira was already moving, crossing back into the recovery room with Mira and Finn trailing to the doorway. She dropped onto the white tile beside the bed and ran her bare fingers over the floor.\n\nAira: \"Dust scar. Heavy legs, rounded feet. Dragged out recently enough that the tile underneath is still cleaner than the corner, yet not last night.\"\n\nMaelin stepped in beside her, her brisk medical armor cracking as she stared at the empty space within arm's reach of the mattress.\n\nMaelin: \"A companion chair.\"\n\nValen crossed the threshold at last, standing beside the bed as if looking down into an open grave.\n\nValen: \"Original healing law required the injured student to pick someone to sit within reach before treatment seals locked.\"\n\nDarian stared at the lone observation chair.\n\nDarian: \"Noctis infirmaries allow only one seat.\"\n\nNera looked sick.\n\nNera: \"For the evaluator.\"\n\nCare had once meant keeping someone close enough to hold your hand. Someone had scrubbed that chair out of the room and left a distant watcher in its place, then called the subtraction neutral procedure.\n\nElira stood over the dust scar, fingers hovering above the clean tile.\n\nElira: \"Cael woke up in this bed after Haru stabilized him. While everyone argued over what his body proved, did anybody ask who he wanted beside him?\"\n\nMaelin shut her eyes. Silence gave the answer.\n\nI remembered Cael standing pale in the trial hall, apologizing for taking up space before Votive Return tore him apart. The empty patch of floor suddenly shouted louder than any spell trap.\n\nHaru: \"Put a chair back. As record.\"\n\nFinn raised a hand from the doorway, swallowed once, and spoke fast.\n\nFinn: \"There are old visitor chairs in the supply closet at the end of the hall. Most are smashed, though one solid wooden one is left. Pella and I sat on it last month while copying maintenance maps.\"\n\nSeraphine nodded toward the corridor.\n\nSeraphine: \"Fetch it. Stay inside the red line.\"\n\nFinn and Pella bolted down the hall and hauled the chair back together, bumping one leg against the doorframe and wincing. It was plain dark wood, one arm patched with mismatched screws, the seat worn smooth by decades of ordinary students.\n\nMira helped steady it at the threshold, then paused.\n\nMira: \"Who sets it down?\"\n\nThe question hung sharp in the air. If I placed it, the annex might read Black Circle interference. If Elira placed it, Lucien would twist proximity into his favorite lie.\n\nRhea understood immediately.\n\nRhea: \"Cael is not here to choose.\"\n\nNera stepped forward first.\n\nNera: \"He chose Aurelis protection.\"\n\nDarian: \"He chose refusal.\"\n\nLeon: \"He chose to warn us.\"\n\nCassian: \"He chose not to be used quietly.\"\n\nAira looked down at her open pages.\n\nAira: \"He chose to put the truth in writing.\"\n\nElira's eyes shone in the dim lamp glow.\n\nElira: \"Then we set it down as witnesses to his choices, never as replacements.\"\n\nOne by one, without making a spectacle of it, we touched the worn wood: Aira on the mended arm, Leon on the rail, Cassian on the seat edge, Nera and Darian on the legs, Rhea, Valen, and Halden along the back. Maelin touched it last.\n\nMaelin: \"For the patient.\"\n\nThen Elira and I lifted it together and set it right over the dust scar beside the bed, close enough for someone lying there to reach out without sitting up.\n\nA soft sigh breathed through the old ceiling vents.\n\nAcross the room, the lone observation chair split cleanly down its spine with a dry crack, collapsing inward without anyone laying a hand on it.\n\nThe dark absence ring around my wrist dimmed, and for one heartbeat the room flickered into what it had been before fear rewrote it: open curtains, twin chairs, monitoring lines waiting politely for permission. Cael wasn't in the room. The annex didn't pretend furniture could bring back the dead; it only showed the decency that should have met him while he lived.\n\nElira wiped one tear off her cheek with the heel of her hand and didn't apologize for it.\n\nLucien's voice slid coldly through the walls.\n\nLucien: \"Moved by carpentry now, Miss Vale?\"\n\nElira didn't flinch away from the chair.\n\nElira: \"A chair never saved a conduit. Removing one helped build them.\"\n\nLight flared along the far wall. Beneath layers of Noctis monitoring script, older Aurelis letters bled through the plaster like a bruise rising to the surface.\n\n**NO TREATMENT WITHOUT CHOSEN PRESENCE.**\n\nHalden stared at the letters as if an argument he had fought his whole career had just been settled in blood.\n\nHalden: \"Aurelis stripped this law before Noctis ever copied the room.\"\n\nRhea whipped around toward Valen.\n\nRhea: \"Aurelis did this?\"\n\nValen didn't flinch or defend the academy.\n\nValen: \"Old tournament medicine grew obsessed with clinical detachment after Circle Zero failed. Chosen presence was labeled contamination. Noctis just inherited our scar and sharpened it into a weapon.\"\n\nMira's pencil moved fast across her notebook.\n\nMira: \"Aurelis wound. Noctis blade.\"\n\nValen looked at the Green Circle girl with quiet respect.\n\nValen: \"Precisely.\"\n\nBehind us, the service tunnel groaned and shortened by another brick.\n\nFrom far down in the dark came two sharp metallic taps. Spoon against stone. Pause. Two taps again.\n\nNera gasped, color draining straight from her cheeks.\n\nNera: \"Winter-drill code. Two taps means heard, being watched.\"\n\nIven hadn't been freed, yet the tremor from the restored room had reached him. He knew someone had answered the old law, and he was warning us before he spoke.\n\nLeon crouched and ran a fresh ribbon of water over the threshold lip. It returned humming with two uneven, frightened pulses.\n\nMaelin: \"Live motor control.\"\n\nRhea: \"Could someone force him to tap?\"\n\nNera swallowed hard.\n\nNera: \"Compulsion forces clean, metronome rhythm. Those taps were uneven. Scared. Deliberate.\"\n\nElira knelt beside the restored bed rather than the tunnel mouth, speaking calmly so her voice carried down the corridor.\n\nElira: \"Iven Sahl, you do not need to tap again. If someone is forcing your hand, you owe them no proof.\"\n\nSilence answered from below. No tap came.\n\nThe tunnel shortened another brick, opening wide enough for one person at a time.\n\nWe gathered back at the threshold, leaving the worn wooden chair standing guard beside Cael's old bed.\n\nSeraphine checked the dark bore ahead.\n\nSeraphine: \"Now we move.\"\n\nDren nodded once.\n\nDren: \"Slowly.\"\n\nLucien's voice scraped thin with irritation out of the well behind us.\n\nLucien: \"Your slowness is pure vanity.\"\n\nHaru: \"No. It is how we stop building rooms like yours.\"\n\nDren stepped into the tunnel first. Leon sent cool water gliding ahead down the stone floor while Cassian warmed the damp air without sparking open flame. Aira marched behind them with her notebook open, followed closely by Halden, Maelin, Rhea, Mira, and Finn, while Seraphine, Valen, and Pella held the upper annex gate and seals.\n\nNera froze at the lip as the smell of winter drills drifted up from below. Her uncovered binding rings trembled.\n\nNera: \"If I hear a bell down there, I might stop moving.\"\n\nRhea paused beside her.\n\nRhea: \"Then the whole line stops with you.\"\n\nNera exhaled shakily, nodded, and stepped down beside Darian.\n\nElira looked up at me and tucked the black pendant neatly beneath her altered navy collar so its glare wouldn't blind us in the narrow dark.\n\nHaru: \"Still your choice.\"\n\nElira: \"Still yours.\"\n\nWe stepped down into the cold together, close enough to see each other breathe, never rushing. The ceiling pressed lower with every pace, pipes sweating iron and mint along the stone while Iven's pale trace pulsed weakly ahead. Behind us sat a badly mended wooden chair in an empty recovery room, proof that someone had once cared enough to keep a place beside the bed, and ahead waited a frightened boy listening in the dark.\n\n---\n\n## Coda: The Chair Registry\n\nWhile Seraphine and Valen locked the outer annex gate behind the descending column, Pella stayed inside the quiet recovery room under a single desk lamp. Mira had drilled one habit into every Green records aide since the Foundation gallery: never trust an erasure until you check the underside.\n\nShe tipped the badly mended companion chair onto its side. Beneath thick layers of gray varnish, lamplight caught a scratched brass tag bearing three stamped digits and a carpenter's repair stamp dated fifteen years back.\n\nAcross the hall in the storage closet, a first-year Green runner held the lantern steady while Pella paged through the battered supply ledger. Seven times over fifteen years, the chair had been moved. Never burned. Never broken up for kindling. Every clerk who hid it had written down a fresh lie to keep it out of an inventory purge: spare hall bench, damaged classroom prop, unsorted archive wood, nonessential frame.\n\nPella copied every false label onto a clean sheet to fold into Mira's archive the moment they climbed back up, neat and plain, then underlined the real entry beneath them.\n\nCompanion chair. Preserved off-ledger. Unknown protector.\n\nThe first-year runner waiting by the annex stairs asked softly if an old chair's tag really mattered with everything waiting below.\n\nPella set the chair carefully upright beside Cael's bed, straightened her sleeve, and glanced toward the dark service tunnel mouth where Haru, Elira, Mira, and Finn had vanished minutes earlier.\n\nPella: \"Somebody risked keeping it when the rules wanted it gone. So yes. It goes in the record.\"","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-27-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep28","label":"Episode 22","number":"22","file":"episode-28-cover.webp","fallback":"The Black Circle Unbound","eyebrow":"Unbound","recap":"","accent":["#020617","#c0c0c0","#ef4444"],"displayLabel":"Episode 22","displayNumber":"22","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"The Boy Beneath the School","body":"The tunnel had opened only after the annex stopped mistaking command for courage.\n\nDren took point with Leon and Cassian clearing the damp stone ahead, followed in measured file by Aira, Halden, Maelin, Rhea, Mira, Finn, Nera, and Darian, while Elira and I stepped down last from Cael's old recovery room, leaving Seraphine, Valen, and Pella to hold the upper annex gate and seals. By then we knew three things: Iven Sahl was alive below us, the route punished urgency, and every pace had to be chosen slowly enough for the old magic to believe us.\n\nFear still pushed the back of the line faster than formation.\n\nDren stopped it with one word.\n\nDren: \"Line.\"\n\nEveryone snapped into place, because the dark ahead tightened whenever anyone rushed.\n\nThe stone ceiling pressed low enough that Cassian had to duck, keeping his frost-bandaged right wrist tucked close to his ribs. Old pipes ran along both walls, slick with condensation that smelled of rust and bitter mint. Above us, the academy sounded distant, like a tidy lecture hall trying to disown its own basement.\n\nAhead, Iven Sahl's blue-white pulse beat through the damp air. Small. Weak. Alive.\n\nNera walked as if guilt had turned into a plumb line. Darian stayed half a pace behind her without reaching out unless she looked back. That restraint mattered in a corridor dug by people who had never learned the difference between support and custody.\n\nBeneath Elira's collar, her pendant gave a soft, steady glow.\n\nEvery few steps, it asked a silent question. Every few steps, she answered it under her breath.\n\nYes. I choose to continue.\n\nYes. I understand the risk. Haru is not carrying me. Fear is not consent.\n\nThe questions brushed against my ribs too, less like accusation than counterweight. I hated needing the check. I needed it anyway.\n\nThe service tunnel dead-ended at a round iron door with no handle.\n\nLeon crouched beside the threshold, palm hovering an inch above the damp stone.\n\nLeon: \"Water behind it.\"\n\nCassian: \"A flood trap?\"\n\nLeon: \"Listening trap. The water repeats footsteps.\"\n\nAira leaned over his shoulder.\n\nAira: \"Can you make it repeat the wrong ones?\"\n\nLeon shot her an offended glance, then shifted his weight onto his knees. Cassian grinned in the dark.\n\nCassian: \"He loves when we ask him to be clever.\"\n\nLeon: \"I love silence more.\"\n\nHe pressed both palms flat to the threshold. Pale blue light threaded from his fingers into the hairline moisture seam under the door. Behind the iron, the trapped water shivered, reversed its current, and began replaying our last forty footsteps backward toward the stair.\n\nA latch clicked inside.\n\nThe round door swung away from us.\n\nThe room beyond was cramped, which made the sight inside worse. One wooden chair. One scarred table. An old tournament relay mirror turned toward the wall.\n\nAnd one boy curled on the cold stone with his wrists bound in white cord.\n\nIven Sahl looked smaller than he had at Cael's funeral. There, distance and rows of Noctis coats had folded him into a group. Here, shivering with damp hair clinging to his forehead and a gag spell trembling across his mouth, he was only a first-year student whose Blue Circle crest flickered so faintly it bordered on gray.\n\nNera let out a broken breath.\n\nAt the sound, the gag spell flared brighter. Iven's eyes went wide with panic.\n\nElira raised one empty hand. Warm gold light spread across the floor without touching him.\n\nElira: \"Iven. We are Aurelis witnesses. Nera is here. Darian is here. You are not alone.\"\n\nThe gag pulsed harder.\n\nAcross the room, the relay mirror rotated on its stand with a dry scrape of wood, though nobody stood near it.\n\nInside the glass, Lucien Voss sat in a white Noctis chamber with a porcelain teacup balanced in one palm.\n\nLucien: \"How tender.\"\n\nDren drew his blade halfway across the threshold.\n\nHalden: \"Wait.\"\n\nDren froze with steel a hair from the glass. Halden's eyes stayed fixed on the white cord biting into Iven's wrists.\n\nHalden: \"The mirror is secondary. The restraint is primary.\"\n\nLucien: \"Professor Halden remains observant. How inconvenient for him.\"\n\nIven began shaking his head hard against the floor. The gag stole his voice, not his meaning. *No. Don't. Leave.*\n\nNera stepped forward anyway. Lucien's gaze slid to her in the mirror.\n\nLucien: \"Miss Vail. You continue to disappoint everyone who improved you.\"\n\nNera flinched. Darian's hand lifted half an inch, then stopped before it crossed into her space.\n\nNera saw the restraint, drew one sharp breath, and lifted her chin.\n\nNera: \"Everyone who used me.\"\n\nThe binding rings at her wrists unlocked with a metallic snap. Lucien smiled.\n\nAt Iven's wrists, the white cords flashed pitch black. Nera froze, and I saw the snare a second too late.\n\nThe trap had never been built for me. It was built for anyone trying to prove they no longer belonged to Noctis.\n\nWhite Dominion lettering flared along the cord:\n\n**RETURN REQUIRES A HAND THAT BELONGED TO US**\n\nNera's rings jerked toward Iven's wrists on an invisible tether. If she touched that cord, the spell would log her as Noctis property reclaiming another Noctis asset.\n\nDarian stepped in front of her and thrust his own hand toward the rope.\n\nThe cord flashed again.\n\n**DESERTER HAND INVALID**\n\nDarian halted with murder written plain across his face. In the mirror, Lucien took a slow sip of tea.\n\nLucien: \"The trouble with sentimental reform is that institutions remember paperwork longer than feelings.\"\n\nI stared at the white knots cutting into Iven's skin. The Black Circle could strip the spell away in half a breath. It would also shred whatever fragile circulation still kept the boy's hands intact.\n\nElira crouched slowly just outside the cord's reach. Maelin muttered a curse behind us, then stayed back.\n\nElira: \"Iven. Can you move the fingers on your left hand?\"\n\nThe boy stared up at her, chest heaving.\n\nElira: \"Not for us. For you. Only if you can.\"\n\nOne finger twitched. The cord bit tighter, and Iven's face twisted in silent agony. Behind my sternum, the Black Circle surged like dark water hitting a lock.\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nJust my name. No lecture, no panic. I locked my shoulders and held.\n\nElira narrowed her gold light to a single hair-thin thread and laid it on the stone beside Iven's knuckles, leaving the cord untouched.\n\nElira: \"Good. One more, if you choose.\"\n\nA second finger uncurled. Then a third.\n\nMira: \"He is answering himself.\"\n\nFinn wrote it down on his pad with shaking hands. Aira's eyes sharpened.\n\nAira: \"The cord demands a Noctis hand. It never specified a hand outside the knots.\"\n\nIn the mirror, Lucien's teacup paused halfway to the saucer. There was the seam.\n\nRhea stepped forward, council sigil flaring crimson at her sleeve.\n\nRhea: \"Iven Sahl, under Aurelis emergency witness authority and Inter-National student protection protocol, your own hand is recognized as sufficient answer to any claim made on your body.\"\n\nThe cord cinched down harder. Iven arched silently against the stone.\n\nMaelin: \"Pain spike!\"\n\nElira's free hand curled into a fist against her knee, though her voice stayed level.\n\nElira: \"Iven. You do not have to be brave for us. Stay present for one second at a time.\"\n\nNera dropped to her knees several feet away, tears cutting tracks through the dust on her cheeks.\n\nNera: \"Cael lent you a thermal charm because you were freezing.\"\n\nIven's gaze jerked to her face.\n\nNera: \"He told me afterward. He said Noctis made children stand in snow and called it discipline. He hated that.\"\n\nThe boy's Blue Circle crest gave a ragged flicker.\n\nNera: \"He would hate this.\"\n\nThe gag spell trembled against Iven's jaw. Beside Nera, Darian's voice came out rough as gravel.\n\nDarian: \"You are not a reserve.\"\n\nIven's left hand opened. Barely an inch, yet enough for his own fingertips to brush the white cord against his wrist.\n\nThe spell had asked for a Noctis hand. Iven gave it his own.\n\nThe first loop slackened and dropped away.\n\nLucien set his teacup down with a sharp click.\n\nLucien: \"Stop.\"\n\nThat single syllable carried more truth than any speech he had ever given. The second loop fell loose.\n\nThen the gag spell mutated. It peeled off Iven's mouth and slid down toward his throat, tightening into a strangle-band. No longer silence. Execution.\n\nI moved without asking, because a boy losing his airway could not grant permission. Even then, I refused to let the power take the whole chamber. I drove a narrow wedge of Black Circle shadow strictly between the spell and Iven's throat, a blunt boundary, nothing more.\n\nThe gag struck it and blew apart into weightless black ash.\n\nIven dragged in air and screamed.\n\nThe sound tore through the cramped stone room, raw and terrified and unmistakably alive.\n\nMaelin slid to his side before the echo died, hovering one palm above his chest without touching the fallen cord.\n\nMaelin: \"Airway clear. Breathing shallow. Pulse fast, steady.\"\n\nElira kept her gold thread resting quietly on the floor beside him. Nera held both open palms up where Iven could track them.\n\nNera: \"I won't touch you unless you ask.\"\n\nIven choked on a sob.\n\nIven: \"Don't send me back.\"\n\nNera's composure gave way completely.\n\nNera: \"Never.\"\n\nThe last loop unraveled. Hairline fractures spidered across the relay mirror. In the glass, Lucien stood up.\n\nBehind his chair, for half a second before the angle skewed, I caught the edge of a real room: white plaster walls, a lattice of black relays, and motionless silhouettes of students behind frosted glass. More reserves.\n\nIven saw them too. Panic hollowed out his eyes.\n\nElira turned to face the cracked glass, low gold light gathering along her sleeves without flaring.\n\nElira: \"We have your room now.\"\n\nLucien met her gaze, and for the first time since his name entered our lives, his composure did not snap cleanly back into place.\n\nLucien: \"You have a glimpse.\"\n\nHalden stepped beside Elira.\n\nHalden: \"A glimpse with geometry.\"\n\nAira: \"And witness notes.\"\n\nMira lifted her scribbled notebook so the glass caught it.\n\nMira: \"And boring verbs.\"\n\nThe split down the mirror widened with a sound like cracking ice. Lucien's eyes shifted past Elira to me.\n\nLucien: \"You keep letting them stand between us.\"\n\nHaru: \"I asked them to.\"\n\nLucien: \"Afraid?\"\n\nHaru: \"Learning.\"\n\nHe hated that answer. Most people wouldn't have caught the shift around his mouth, only a slight stiffening at the jaw. I caught it.\n\nThe mirror burst outward into harmless motes of silver light.\n\nSilence rushed back into the stone cell, leaving only damp air, slack white cord, and a trembling boy so pale Maelin waved everyone except Nera, Darian, Elira, and me back toward the doorway.\n\nIven looked up at me from the floor, and fresh terror crossed his face. Terror of me, not Noctis.\n\nI lowered myself slowly until I sat cross-legged on the cold stone six feet away, hands resting open on my knees.\n\nHaru: \"You don't have to trust me.\"\n\nIven swallowed hard.\n\nIven: \"Are you the Black Circle?\"\n\nThe room went dead quiet. For months I had survived by dodging that question in corridors and hearings. Now a bruised first-year asked it from the floor of a secret basement built to break children into tools.\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nI kept my eyes on Iven's without looking away. He stared back, breathing hard.\n\nIven: \"Are you going to take us?\"\n\nThe Black Circle stayed still beneath my skin. My pulse didn't.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira sat down beside me, close enough for our shoulders nearly to brush without crowding the boy.\n\nElira: \"He is going to help us make sure nobody else does.\"\n\nIven looked at the dark crystal gleaming faintly against her collar, then at Nera by the wall, then at Maelin hovering with her healer kit.\n\nIven: \"Cael said Aurelis students were weird.\"\n\nNera let out a laugh that broke straight into a sob. From the doorway, Cassian leaned his head in.\n\nCassian: \"Accurate.\"\n\nLeon: \"Painfully.\"\n\nIven's mouth twitched, halfway between terror and disbelief.\n\nThen the academy bells began ringing overhead, loud enough to vibrate down through the pipes. Emergency shelter sequence.\n\nRhea went rigid, listening to the cadence.\n\nRhea: \"That isn't our bell order.\"\n\nThe damp bulbs along the ceiling bled crimson. Senna's voice tore through every ward channel in the room at once.\n\nSenna: \"All schools alert. Noctis Dominion College has sealed itself from Council entry. Multiple student signatures are moving below registered ground. Lucien Voss has activated an internal doctrine site.\"\n\nIven squeezed his eyes shut.\n\nIven: \"The obedience hall.\"\n\nNera's hands went dead still at her sides. Darian swore under his breath. I looked at Elira. Whatever quiet the lake house had bought us ended right there, and the world walked straight into the basement.\n\nShe didn't reach for my hand as a shield. When her fingers closed briefly around mine, it felt like a promise that what we had chosen in private would hold under public weight.\n\nElira: \"Then we find the hall.\"\n\nIven opened his eyes, shivering violently.\n\nIven: \"You can't. It moves.\"\n\nDren sheathed his blade and headed back up the service stair with Halden at his shoulder to reinforce Seraphine on the upper annex gate, leaving the rest of us with Iven in the cellar.\n\n---\n\nMaelin refused to let anyone turn Iven's warning into a war council until the boy had drunk half a cup of water.\n\nShe cleared space around him like a field surgeon clearing shrapnel. Cassian was ordered to warm the air with his left hand without showing open flame, Leon to draw damp out of the stones under Iven's knees, Aira to stop scratching pencil notes right beside his ear, and me to sit where both my hands remained in plain sight. I obeyed without a word.\n\nLeon stared at me as if I had personally insulted him.\n\nLeon: \"He listens to healers now.\"\n\nCassian: \"Growth terrifies you.\"\n\nLeon: \"It ruins my filing system.\"\n\nIven looked back and forth between them, clearly trying to decide whether Aurelis students were safe or out of their minds.\n\nElira sat on the floor near the boy, leaving enough open stone between them that he could shift away if he wanted, close enough that he could see another survivor who knew what it felt like when a doctrine used a body as a conduit.\n\nNera stayed backed against the far wall. Every second there cost her, and she stayed anyway.\n\nIven's eyes kept darting toward her, away, and back again.\n\nIven: \"You were in the blue corridor.\"\n\nRecognition cut across Nera's face sharper than guilt.\n\nNera: \"I was.\"\n\nIven: \"You checked our rings.\"\n\nNera shut her eyes for half a beat while Darian stared grimly at the floor.\n\nNera: \"I did.\"\n\nIven's voice shook harder.\n\nIven: \"You loosened mine once.\"\n\nNobody moved. Nera opened her eyes.\n\nIven: \"After winter drills. When Magister Owel said I was wasting heat. You adjusted the ring. It hurt less after.\"\n\nNera pressed both hands over her mouth. Any grand apology would have sounded obscene in that room, and she didn't insult him by offering one.\n\nNera: \"I should have done more.\"\n\nIven blinked at her, bewildered, as if Noctis had trained him to believe small mercies were either mistakes or future invoices.\n\n## Interlude: Elira - The Choice of Stride\n\nElira kept her voice low so it wouldn't corner him.\n\nElira: \"Both can be true. She helped once, and she should have been able to help more. Noctis made even small mercies dangerous.\"\n\nIven stared at her.\n\nIven: \"Are you allowed to say that?\"\n\nElira's mouth curved, small and sharp.\n\nElira: \"I died. It made me less polite.\"\n\nAira muffled a sound against her sleeve. Without looking up from Iven's pulse, Maelin pointed straight at the archivist.\n\nMaelin: \"Do not encourage the patient.\"\n\nElira: \"I am encouraged internally.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I know. It shows in your chart.\"\n\nIven's lips twitched again, a fraction closer to a real smile. Elira eased her stiff left knee against the flagstone and refused to lean forward. Her ribs still ached when she breathed too deep, and the old Wounded City fatigue sat behind her eyes like lead, reminding her why stride mattered. If she moved too fast, her Yellow Circle would swallow the room and turn rescue back into hierarchy. She chose the slower pace on purpose.\n\nRhea stepped back down the tunnel stair after sealing the outer door, dusty hems sweeping the floor, her expression cold past irritation.\n\nRhea: \"The academy shelter order has been corrected. Students are secured inside assigned halls. Seraphine and Dren have the annex perimeter, and Senna is working to keep Krail off the ward channel.\"\n\nAira: \"How successfully?\"\n\nRhea: \"Senna just used the phrase jurisdictional vandalism.\"\n\nAira's eyes lit up.\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nAira slumped, wounded, while Rhea turned toward Iven. When the Student Council president spoke to the boy, the command edge dropped from her voice.\n\nRhea: \"Iven Sahl, I need to ask about the obedience hall. You can refuse. You can stop whenever you want. You can ask for Maelin, Nera, Darian, me, or no one near you.\"\n\nIven clutched the wool blanket tighter around his shoulders.\n\nIven: \"If I say no, do you send me somewhere else?\"\n\nRhea: \"Never.\"\n\nHe waited, watching her face as if the first promise were bait and the second might show the hook. Rhea simply waited with him until he gave a small nod.\n\nIven: \"Ask.\"\n\nRhea: \"Why does it move?\"\n\nIven glanced at the shattered relay mirror.\n\nIven: \"Because they said fear is portable.\"\n\nElira felt her jaw tighten, though she kept her hands still in her lap.\n\nIven: \"The hall shows up wherever a student expects punishment. Under Noctis. In dormitory glass. On the training field. Sometimes in an ordinary classroom right after someone important smiles too kindly.\"\n\nFrom the doorway, Mira scribbled furiously with trembling fingers while Finn's lips moved silently beside her, counting clauses.\n\nIven: \"It learns the shape you think discipline will take.\"\n\nHaru: \"And if someone isn't afraid?\"\n\nIven looked at Haru as if he had asked whether lake water was wet.\n\nIven: \"Everyone is afraid.\"\n\nNobody argued, and Elira certainly didn't. Iven dropped his eyes to his bruised knuckles.\n\nIven: \"It travels through people who learned to wait for it.\"\n\nNera: \"Conditioned anticipatory route.\"\n\nHalden, who had come back down the stair with Professor Valen at his elbow, gave one grim nod from the threshold.\n\nHalden: \"A doctrine site anchored through expectation.\"\n\nValen: \"That explains why standard perimeter seals fail.\"\n\nDarian: \"Can we draw it here?\"\n\nThe question was ugly and necessary. All color drained from Iven's face, and Darian took an immediate step back.\n\nDarian: \"No. I'm sorry. Not through you.\"\n\nIven's breathing hitched while Maelin shifted closer. He shook his head quickly.\n\nIven: \"It will come anyway.\"\n\nElira leaned forward an inch, careful not to loom.\n\nElira: \"Why?\"\n\nIven's gaze flicked from Haru to the dark crystal resting against her throat.\n\nIven: \"Because Lucien wants to show the hall what open rings look like.\"\n\nThe pendant warmed against Elira's skin, its seven open threads humming faint warning. She laid two fingers lightly beneath it without hiding it.\n\nIven: \"He said Aurelis would try to pass weakness off as mercy. He said open rings are just how weak people pretend they don't belong to stronger ones. If open rings walk into the hall, it tries to snap them shut. And if it closes them around someone strong enough...\"\n\nHis eyes landed back on Haru, and Elira understood the rest before Haru named it.\n\nHaru: \"It forces a Black Circle image.\"\n\nIven nodded.\n\nElira stared at the cold stone between her boots, tracing every angle of the trap Lucien had built ahead of them. If Haru stepped inside first, the hall would turn his shadow into public proof of Krail's panic. If she walked in ahead with the revised pendant, the room would fight to crush her seven open consent lines back into Haru's mark. And if nobody entered, children remained locked inside a moving cage.\n\nAira snapped her notebook shut. The small click cut cleanly through the room.\n\nAira: \"Then Haru does not enter first.\"\n\nEvery head turned toward her. Aira looked pale, her chin set hard.\n\nAira: \"Don't look at me like that. I hate this too.\"\n\nElira: \"Then who leads?\"\n\nMira stepped clear of the doorway, small and shaking, yet refusing to back up.\n\nMira: \"Someone it expects to be weak.\"\n\nFinn: \"Mira.\"\n\nMira swallowed hard and kept her eyes on Elira and Rhea.\n\nMira: \"Not alone, not untrained, and not as bait. But if the hall runs on rank and fear, maybe a Green Circle witness can cross a threshold a Black Circle can't.\"\n\nIven stared at her as though she had volunteered to walk barefoot into a blizzard without asking permission to shiver. Mira looked terrified down to her fingertips, and she didn't take a single word back.\n\nAbove them, the red annex bulbs continued pulsing against the wet pipes while the obedience hall shifted somewhere beyond the walls. Elira looked at Mira, then at Haru sitting quietly with empty hands, and let the pace settle where it belonged.\n\nFor the first time since the bells began, their answer did not start with Haru's power.\n\n---\n\nSenna's remote seal flared above Rhea's shoulder before anyone could turn Mira's offer into another committee meeting, her projected face gray with exhaustion and sharp enough to make the light crackle.\n\nSenna: \"Noctis just sealed itself under sovereign educational emergency doctrine. Official pretext: contamination by Black Circle influence.\"\n\nLeon: \"They're using Haru as the deadbolt.\"\n\nSenna: \"Precisely.\"\n\nShe slashed a dark ink mark across her margin. Krail's petition had handed Lucien a legal key without Krail ever needing to strike a deal.\n\nRhea: \"Can you break the seal?\"\n\nSenna: \"Legally, eventually. Magically, not before the missing students are relocated or spent.\"\n\nNobody spoke Cael's name, yet Iven's fists clenched white around his blanket.\n\nRhea swept her gaze across the cellar.\n\nRhea: \"No repeated speeches. Decisions.\"\n\nMira held up her torn notes, pages trembling.\n\nMira: \"I cross the first threshold.\"\n\nFinn: \"We cross.\"\n\nMira: \"You don't have to make every bad choice hereditary.\"\n\nFinn: \"Good. Then this one is mine.\"\n\nNera reached toward the Noctis sigils Iven had sketched, paused the moment he flinched, and tapped the blank margin instead.\n\nNera: \"I can tell original instructions from the lines Lucien rewrote. I can't promise I'll spot them before they spot me.\"\n\nDarian: \"Then I spot you.\"\n\nNera met his eyes. He didn't soften the pledge or dress it up.\n\nElira: \"I hold the outer consent pattern. Leon runs sound without giving the glass a clean reflection. Cassian keeps the frost lines from locking shut.\"\n\nCassian raised his wrapped right wrist, then held up his left hand with a crooked grin.\n\nCassian: \"One working hand, deeply offensive confidence, adequate fire.\"\n\nRhea: \"And Haru stays outside.\"\n\nEvery person in the room waited for me to fight it. Part of me wanted to. The Black Circle had already offered fifty ways to turn the west wall into an empty hole, and not one of them ended with frightened children trusting whoever stepped through the dust.\n\nHaru: \"Close enough to catch failure. Far enough not to become Lucien's exhibit.\"\n\nElira looked at me steadily, resting two fingers lightly against the back of the wooden chair beside Iven's cot.\n\nElira: \"Stay where we can still reach your voice.\"\n\nIt stung because she understood the cost down to the inch, and steadied me for the exact same reason.\n\nRhea: \"Practice court. Now.\"\n\nWe climbed back up from the cellar into the cool air of the old tournament practice court while Maelin wheeled a portable cot to the far wall so Iven could stay clear of the stone floor. Dren met us at the court doors and shifted the stone barriers into a mock corridor before sunset bled out of the sky. Leon laid water in hair-thin channels along the paving stones while Cassian warmed them from beneath, grumbling that gentle heat was a crime against his element.\n\nMira failed the first threshold rehearsal.\n\nHalden's simulated accusation struck her open ring, and the Green light folded inward as if ashamed of taking up air.\n\nMira: \"Sorry.\"\n\nHalden: \"Don't apologize to a drill. Name what caught you.\"\n\nShe stared down at the dead circle on her wrist.\n\nMira: \"It showed everyone shielding me while useful people died somewhere else.\"\n\nHer second attempt collapsed faster. The third never opened at all.\n\nFinn plucked the torn doctrine sheet out of her fingers and turned it face down without reading it.\n\nFinn: \"Mira Castellan. Copied forty-three witness statements when Council asked for one. Found two falsified timestamps. Abysmal handwriting.\"\n\nMira let out a startled laugh that caught in her throat.\n\nMira: \"That's not in the notes.\"\n\nFinn: \"The notes were losing.\"\n\nHe pressed the paper back into her palm.\n\nFinn: \"Countable. Try again.\"\n\nHer fourth ring trembled, held, and stayed open.\n\nAcross the court, Elira worked with a single slender thread of gold instead of the sweeping wards she used to throw without thinking. The Wounded City had left her slower, neither fragile nor fully mended, and every movement carried deliberate care.\n\nHer boot toe caught on a raised paving seam.\n\nI shifted half a step forward, then caught myself and stayed put.\n\nElira grabbed the court's barrier rail to steady herself, drawing one sharp breath through her teeth. When she glanced over at me, irritation arrived a full beat ahead of relief.\n\nElira: \"Don't make me waste breath telling you I'm fine.\"\n\nHaru: \"I won't ask.\"\n\nThe irritation cracked open into real warmth.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nShe meant thank you for stopping. I hated that stopping could be the hardest gift I knew how to give, and gave it anyway.\n\nNight hadn't fully settled when the moving hall found us.\n\nA clean Noctis training chime rang straight out of the solid west wall.\n\nFrom the healer station, Iven screamed.\n\nEvery open crest on the practice court snapped toward the sound. Stone thinned and blackened into seamless mirror glass. Beyond it stretched a long corridor lined with twelve barefoot students in white Noctis sleep uniforms, eyes wide open, arms pinned neatly at their sides.\n\nIven dragged himself upright against Maelin's bracing hands.\n\nIven: \"That's them.\"\n\nThe twelve spoke in dead unison.\n\nMissing Students: \"Return when called.\"\n\nBehind their line, Lucien rested one calm hand on the shoulder of a little Green Circle girl I had never seen before.\n\nThe Black Circle inside my chest went utterly still.\n\nLucien: \"You wanted the hall. Here it is.\"\n\nHe looked straight past Elira and every prepared witness, pinning his gaze on me.\n\nLucien: \"Stay outside, Haru Aerlen. Show them restraint. I wonder how many children your discipline can watch suffer.\"\n\nThe little girl didn't blink, yet one slow tear traced down to her chin.\n\nBefore I could turn that tear into an excuse, Mira stepped forward. Her Green Circle trembled once, then flared steady.\n\nMira: \"We're coming in.\"\n\nFinn gripped his scroll case with both hands and stepped up half a pace behind her. Lucien's smile shifted from certainty to sharp curiosity.\n\nThe black-glass threshold parted and swallowed all eight witnesses, Mira and Finn leading Aira, Elira, Leon, Cassian, Nera, and Darian into the glare, before snapping thin behind them.\n\n---\n\nInside the threshold, sound changed first.\n\nMira told us later the practice court didn't vanish at once; it diluted. Stone thinned into reflections, reflections into windowpanes, and windowpanes into a black-glass corridor where every surface replayed a different version of her failing before she opened her mouth.\n\nMira dropping her pad. Mira weeping. Mira kneeling. Mira apologizing for making stronger people bleed to guard a Green Circle desk.\n\nThe hall found her oldest fear in seconds, yet it hadn't measured all of her.\n\nAira: \"Ignore any reflection where I don't look visibly annoyed.\"\n\nMira nearly laughed, and the tension across her shoulders broke just enough to breathe.\n\nElira crossed behind Finn, and for one heartbeat gold light swept the corridor before snapping back to obsidian. Seven open arcs flared around the dark crystal at her throat, then tried to snap shut like jaws. Elira laid one fingertip over the stone.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe arcs sprang wide again, refusing the lock.\n\nLeon and Cassian entered shoulder to shoulder, too busy working to trade jokes. Leon ran a hair-thin thread of water along the floorboards while Cassian breathed low left-handed heat beneath it so the moisture kept moving instead of freezing into the hall's geometry.\n\nNera and Darian crossed last, and the walls hated them on sight.\n\nWhite Noctis lettering bled straight out of the black glass:\n\n**DESERTER. FAILED BINDER. UNGRATEFUL HAND. WITNESS CONTAMINATED.**\n\nDarian read the wall, then glanced sideways at Nera.\n\nDarian: \"Boring.\"\n\nNera blinked at him.\n\nDarian: \"They had better insults when we still wore the uniforms.\"\n\nA startled laugh tore out of Nera's throat, and the white script guttered like a candle in a draft. The hall had braced for shame, not two defectors who knew the shame was real and refused to kneel to it anyway.\n\nOutside in the practice court, Dren, Halden, Maelin, Rhea, and I crowded around Valen's witness slate, catching fragmented images and strained audio through the failing glass.\n\nIt showed enough: Mira standing rigid in the front, Finn clutching his scroll case at her elbow, Aira at her shoulder with pencil poised like a blade, Elira behind them, pale and luminous, refusing to let exhaustion look like surrender.\n\nAhead of them stretched the corridor toward Lucien's hand on the little Green Circle girl.\n\nShe hadn't blinked once, yet her disguise had seams. Every few seconds her left fingers twitched, and the hinge of her jaw trembled beneath the forced stillness. Beneath a white Noctis training cuff, a faint Green Circle flickered.\n\nMira saw it first.\n\nMira: \"She's awake.\"\n\nLucien's voice drifted smoothly down the glass hall.\n\nLucien: \"Awake is hardly the same as free.\"\n\nMira swallowed hard.\n\nMira: \"I know.\"\n\nLucien tilted his head, faintly surprised; he had baited her for denial and got plain record instead.\n\nAira: \"Good.\"\n\nThe first trap struck as a school bell. Crisp, high, unmistakable Noctis brass.\n\nEvery missing child in the corridor snapped toward the far wall with palms slapped flat to their thighs. Even Mira's heels twitched an inch before she caught herself. Conditioning didn't have to carry a Noctis crest to find a student; every school taught bells before it taught names.\n\nElira's eyes sharpened.\n\nElira: \"Nobody moves by bell.\"\n\nLeon: \"By what, then?\"\n\nElira looked straight back at Mira. Mira went pale, then nodded once as the answer clicked.\n\nMira: \"By name.\"\n\nA hairline crack split the black floor. Lucien's smile thinned.\n\nMira cracked open her notebook with trembling fingers.\n\nMira: \"I am Mira Castellan.\"\n\nFinn: \"Finn Halward.\"\n\nAira: \"Aira Solen.\"\n\nElira: \"Elira Vale.\"\n\nLeon: \"Leon Varek.\"\n\nCassian: \"Cassian Veyr.\"\n\nNera: \"Nera Vail.\"\n\nDarian: \"Darian Thorne.\"\n\nThe bell tolled a second time. None of them flinched.\n\nDown the corridor, the twelve missing children shivered, invited even if not yet free. The little Green girl's eyes flicked toward Mira without turning her head. Mira took one careful step forward.\n\nLucien's fingers tightened on the girl's shoulder.\n\nOutside by the slate, the Black Circle slammed against my ribs so hard my knees nearly buckled. Behind me, Iven cried out from the healer cot, fighting Maelin's hands as he shoved himself upright.\n\nIven: \"He does that when he wants the hall to think for you!\"\n\nI whipped my head around. Iven sat white-faced, blanket twisted in both fists.\n\nIven: \"He touches one and every student remembers being touched!\"\n\nValen twisted the slate's tuning ring, and Rhea fed Iven's warning straight through the open channel.\n\nInside the glass, Nera caught it at once. She didn't rush Lucien; she read the pressure itself, opening both binding rings until faint blue-gray circles pooled across the floor.\n\nNera: \"Shared contact pressure. Zero individual consent.\"\n\nDarian stepped beside her.\n\nDarian: \"Noctis instructor pattern, not student choice.\"\n\nAira logged both lines as the floor shuddered. Mira took a second step.\n\nMira: \"What is your name?\"\n\nThe girl parted her lips, and Lucien smiled.\n\nBefore sound could leave her throat, the corridor snapped into a classroom.\n\nDesks slammed down onto black tile in rigid rank tiers: Green crushed at the rear with empty ink wells, Blue and Red stacked between, Yellow set proudly in front.\n\nMira stood beside the back row where the hall meant to exile her. She looked at the empty wooden desk, then pulled out the chair and sat down.\n\nAira: \"Mira?\"\n\nMira laid her notebook square over the dry ink slot.\n\nMira: \"If this is where it thinks questions belong, I'll ask from here.\"\n\nPale green light bloomed faintly across the desk beneath her palms.\n\nThrough the slate's audio feed, we heard Finn's breath catch beside Mira's desk.\n\nFinn: \"Oh.\"\n\nBeside me, Halden murmured under his breath.\n\nHalden: \"Effective.\"\n\nMira lifted her chin toward the front of the room.\n\nMira: \"What is your name?\"\n\nThe hall threw its weight into the fight. The girl's mouth worked silently, blocked by the sudden appearance of a massive teacher's podium at the front: black timber, white inlay, Noctis crest carved deep in the center, crowned with a brass bell, a wooden ruler, and a neat stack of blank conduct forms.\n\nLucien stood behind it as though he had taught in that room for twenty years.\n\nLucien: \"Incorrect first question.\"\n\nMira's shoulders jerked. Two rows ahead, Aira ripped a prepared slip from her pad and read in a flat courtroom drone.\n\nAira: \"Objection. Witness requested identity. Hostile structure substituted compliance evaluation.\"\n\nThe conduct forms curled brown along their edges. Lucien's eyes narrowed on Aira.\n\nLucien: \"You brought prepared paperwork to a nightmare.\"\n\nAira: \"I bring paperwork everywhere. Nightmares aren't special.\"\n\nThe stack of forms burned away into ash without flame. Mira let out one breath, then another.\n\nElira stayed near the side aisle, close enough for the girl to track her without letting Yellow light dominate the room. Whenever the black windowpanes tried to reflect her pendant as a closed black ring, the crystal reopened the seven gold arcs on its own.\n\nMira tried a third time.\n\nMira: \"What is your name?\"\n\nTears spilled over the little girl's lashes. Lucien lifted the wooden ruler.\n\nThe classroom stretched violently. Every Green seat shrieked backward across the tile until Mira's desk slammed against the rear wall, doubling the gap between her and the front row. Noctis loved forcing small students to walk the whole aisle under watching eyes. Mira went sheet-white.\n\nValen routed Iven's hoarse shout straight down Leon's water thread.\n\nIven: \"Don't stand up just because the room moved you!\"\n\nMira froze half out of her chair, knuckles white against the wood. Slowly, she sank back down.\n\nMira: \"I can ask from here.\"\n\nGreen light answered under her palms again. Finn, hovering a pace behind her row, muttered fast for the record.\n\nFinn: \"Distance imposed. Question unchanged.\"\n\nAira logged it. Lucien lowered the ruler, and the brass bell tolled again.\n\nEvery missing child turned toward the podium. Mira, Elira, Aira, and Finn held still. Leon muttered his own name under his breath like a talisman.\n\nCassian: \"Cassian Veyr, still thoroughly irritated.\"\n\nThe classroom stalled, visibly unequipped for sarcasm.\n\nSo it tried subtler cruelty instead. It showed failure.\n\nOne by one, the classroom windows darkened into mirrors, and Valen's witness slate outside split to echo both frames directly in front of me.\n\nIn the left glass, I saw Elira kneeling on the Wounded City flagstones while Dominion Severance bit under her pendant. In this version, I struck early. Black light ripped the square apart, kept her breathing, and shattered every witness rail around us. *Haru acts, catastrophe follows.*\n\nIn the right glass, I stood frozen on the stones while Elira died and stayed dead, praised by every councilman for clean restraint. *Haru waits, love is lost.*\n\nThe two mirrors faced each other across both the classroom and the slate. My hands went cold as ice. Inside the room, everyone saw the trap too, because shame worked twice as well with an audience.\n\nElira stepped right up to the classroom window facing the slate, stopping short of touching the glass.\n\nElira: \"Neither mirror is sequence.\"\n\nWhite chalk scraped across the blackboard behind Lucien on its own:\n\n**POWER MUST DECIDE BEFORE WITNESS DELAYS.**\n\nAira folded a sheet of plain paper into a square and hurled it straight at the slate-board. It slapped flat onto the slate surface and stuck there in crisp, cramped ink:\n\n*False binary. Witness process is not delay when it prevents hostile relabeling.*\n\nThe chalk stuttered and stopped mid-stroke. A laugh escaped me as a sharp exhale.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira: \"Don't thank me yet. I have twenty-seven more and half of them are rude.\"\n\nThe twin mirrors fogged gray, and the hall pivoted onto Elira.\n\nHer pendant pulsed warm against her collar, carrying its silent consent check out through the slate so clearly I felt the hum against my teeth. The blackboard wiped itself and wrote:\n\n**BELOVED PRESENCE COMPROMISES OBJECTIVE RESCUE.**\n\nElira studied the line with a stillness colder than fury.\n\nElira: \"Define objective.\"\n\nThe chalk scratched again.\n\n**ACTION FREE FROM PERSONAL ATTACHMENT.**\n\nElira: \"Then objective rescue is a myth invented by people who want rescuers easier to command.\"\n\nThe dark crystal flashed once in sharp agreement. Lucien's reflection rippled across the polished surface of the teacher's podium.\n\nLucien: \"Attachment narrows judgment.\"\n\nElira: \"So does doctrine.\"\n\nLucien: \"You mistake warmth for truth only because warmth spared you the burden of being useful.\"\n\nBeside the side aisle, Cassian's heat spiked violently. Leon's cold water-thread snapped around Cassian's left wrist before he could take a step.\n\nCassian: \"I know.\"\n\nThat accepted check made the classroom hiss like wet iron.\n\nElira took a single step toward the podium, leaving the pendant openly displayed against her uniform.\n\nElira: \"This crystal didn't fail because love is weak. Dominion Severance bypassed it because Noctis grammar rode an unlawful return claim beneath a protection layer and renamed rescue as route. That isn't attachment compromising judgment. It's hostile language exploiting unexamined authority.\"\n\nThe blackboard tried to scrub her words before she finished speaking.\n\nValen snapped her fingers beside me over the slate.\n\nValen: \"Historical correction preserved.\"\n\nSenna's remote crystal flared crimson beside her.\n\nSenna: \"Sequence correction preserved.\"\n\nMira lifted her pen from the back row.\n\nMira: \"Plain correction preserved.\"\n\nThe chalk snapped cleanly in half, and the pendant cooled against Elira's throat. Stepping back toward the side glass, she laid two fingers lightly against the shimmering border of the channel where my palm rested on the outside frame.\n\nHaru: \"Still here.\"\n\nElira: \"Still here.\"\n\nThe hall swung next at Nera and Darian.\n\nTheir desks dragged forward across the tile without permission. Perfect white diagrams of Nera's binding rings chalked themselves onto the board while Darian's old Noctis collar crest burned into the plaster behind him.\n\n**DEFECTOR TESTIMONY IS SELF-PRESERVATION.**\n\n**BINDER MERCY IS BRAND MANAGEMENT.**\n\nNera lost every drop of color in her cheeks. Darian's expression flattened into blank marble, the old Noctis mask snapping back into place.\n\nThe brass bell rang once above the board.\n\nEvery child straightened in their chairs, including Iven on the healer cot behind me as the hall's resonance reached across the channel. Maelin stepped straight between Iven and the slate.\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nThe bell rang twice. *Kneel when named. Return when called. Spend when chosen.* The commands bypassed ears and pulled straight at muscle. Mira's knees buckled beside her desk.\n\nFinn caught her elbow, then jerked his hand back the instant she looked up in panic.\n\nFinn: \"Sorry.\"\n\nMira shook her head fast, breathing hard.\n\nMira: \"Thank you. Ask next time.\"\n\nFinn nodded, miserable and learning. The room despised that exchange more than shouting.\n\nThe bell tolled a third time.\n\nNera's binding rings lifted off her wrists on their own, dragged upward because the room still remembered her as an instrument. Darian stepped tightly to her shoulder.\n\nDarian: \"Nera Vail is not a binding implement.\"\n\nThe chalk scratched furiously.\n\n**NOCTIS PROPERTY CONTRADICTS NOCTIS PROPERTY.**\n\nDarian's mouth curved into a cold, razor-thin smile.\n\nDarian: \"Darian Thorne isn't Noctis property either.\"\n\nThe quiet sentence landed heavier than fire. One by one, heads lifted across the middle rows like prisoners realizing the warden had miscounted.\n\nIn the second row, a Blue-cuffed boy whispered upward.\n\nBoy: \"Can we say that?\"\n\nFrom behind Maelin outside, Iven answered in a cracking voice.\n\nIven: \"Not back there. Here, maybe.\"\n\nThe hall punished the *maybe* instantly.\n\nMira's desk screeched all the way back until its legs ground into the rear plaster, trapping her at the longest answer route in the room. Over her head, pale lettering burned into the air:\n\n**LOW RESPONSE VALUE.**\n\nMira's shoulders curled inward. Finn's jaw clenched so hard a muscle jumped at his cheek. Up front, the little Green girl stared back toward Mira as if the stretch of floor itself hurt.\n\nMira gripped her notebook with both hands and refused to leave her chair.\n\nMira: \"Mira Castellan.\"\n\nAira ripped off another slip and thrust it into Finn's shaking fingers.\n\nAira: \"Read. Your voice, not mine.\"\n\nFinn swallowed hard, then shouted across the rows.\n\nFinn: \"Objection! Response value is assigned by coercive seating structure and therefore isn't evidence of personhood, capacity, or truth!\"\n\nThe rear row brightened a shade, just enough for Mira to draw a full breath.\n\nAlong the side wall, Nera traced the crawling white script with her eyes.\n\nNera: \"Rank seating is tied to response delay. The farther Green sits, the longer the answer route.\"\n\nLeon: \"Can I shorten the route?\"\n\nElira: \"Without moving Mira.\"\n\nLeon's mouth twitched.\n\nLeon: \"That I can manage.\"\n\nHair-thin threads of water seeped up between the dark tiles, invisible unless you watched the sheen, carrying acoustic vibration straight down the center aisle while Cassian kept the air a fraction warm so frost wouldn't seal them. The distance stayed ugly and visible, yet Mira's voice no longer had to beg permission from the front rows.\n\nMira set both palms flat on her open page.\n\nMira: \"What is your name?\"\n\nHer voice crossed the room before Lucien's ruler could drop.\n\nGirl: \"Se-\"\n\nThe syllable snapped off mid-breath as the blackboard filled wall to wall:\n\n**GREEN STUDENTS DO NOT ADDRESS THE FRONT WITHOUT INVITATION**\n\nMira stared at the white chalk. Then she looked down at the wooden plant charm tied to her notebook's spine, glowing faint green in her lap.\n\nMira: \"I am not addressing the front.\"\n\nThe chalk froze.\n\nMira: \"I am addressing her.\"\n\nA fissure ran straight down the slate blackboard. At the front of the room, the little girl gasped like someone breaking through lake ice.\n\nMira: \"What is your name?\"\n\nThe answer came smaller than a candle flame.\n\nGirl: \"Sela.\"\n\nThe room didn't shatter yet. Lucien slammed one palm flat onto the podium, catching the collapsing spell by sheer force, his eyes gone ice-cold.\n\nLucien: \"Partial identity is not release.\"\n\nAira: \"Nobody claimed it was.\"\n\nElira stepped forward one pace into the aisle.\n\nElira: \"Sela, do you have another name?\"\n\nThe hall seized on that immediately. Yellow questioning Yellow's front row, formal and orderly, something hierarchy could digest. Elira caught the trap mid-breath, stopped dead, and turned back toward the rear wall, handing the question back to its proper witness.\n\nMira's throat worked once.\n\nMira: \"Sela, do you have another name?\"\n\nSela locked her eyes onto Mira's face.\n\nSela: \"Mornet.\"\n\nThe ceiling split above them, pouring cold white daylight across the desks.\n\nAira: \"Sela Mornet! Name recovered by Green Circle witness question under coercive classroom structure.\"\n\nFinn: \"Function label displaced!\"\n\nA crack split the teacher's podium right down the Noctis crest. Lucien's fingers tightened on his ruler until the wood snapped in half. Every missing student flinched at the crack except Sela.\n\nThat first victory lasted three seconds.\n\nIn the first second, Sela Mornet looked down at her own hands as if surprised they still belonged to her. In the second, the children around her realized rank hadn't saved her, silence hadn't saved her, and a terrified Green witness had broken a rule the hall called unbreakable.\n\nIn the third, the room tried to outrun them with panic.\n\nDesks slammed into new grids. Green rows pinned to the walls, Blue desks shoved into narrow aisles, Yellow chairs trapped under harsh white glare like verdicts waiting for signatures. Names flashed across the blackboard faster than anyone could track before wiping clear: *Sela Mornet. Tovin Reiss. Pella Orthis. Josen Venn. Marn Oriel. Lysa Pell. Iven Sahl.*\n\nSome were live signatures. Some were echoes. Some might have been lures.\n\nAira slammed both palms flat on the nearest desk.\n\nAira: \"Stop reading names aloud until we mark live status!\"\n\nEveryone froze because the order made sense, not because a bell demanded it.\n\nAbove Valen's slate, Senna's crystal threw a crisp grid into the channel.\n\nSenna: \"Categories: living confirmed, living possible, archive impression, coercive bait, unknown.\"\n\nRhea: \"No name is treated as an active route until status is verified.\"\n\nChalk slashed across the board:\n\n**DELAY COSTS CHILDREN.**\n\nMaelin didn't even blink from outside.\n\nMaelin: \"So does misdiagnosis.\"\n\nAnother crack split the blackboard. At the front, Sela lifted her head and searched past the rows until she found Mira against the rear wall.\n\nSela: \"Am I living confirmed?\"\n\nThe question hit Mira like a physical blow. Her mouth opened on air. She was Green Circle, shaking in an exile desk, suddenly asked by a child for proof she was still alive. The room leaned inward, starving for her to break.\n\nNeither Elira nor Aira rescued her. Elira's knuckles turned white at her sides, holding back; Aira simply slid a fresh blank slip onto Mira's desk.\n\nMira looked down at the paper, then straight back at Sela.\n\nMira: \"I can confirm you answered your full name inside this hall. I can confirm you answered my question after coercive seating tried to stop it. I can't confirm where your body is yet.\"\n\nThe room waited for Sela to collapse. Instead, the child gave one small, steady nod.\n\nSela: \"That's true.\"\n\nAn entire row of desks dissolved into dust. Even incomplete truth carried weight.\n\nLucien's reflection surfaced across every polished desktop at once, the faint amusement finally gone from his eyes.\n\nLucien: \"You are training them to accept insufficiency.\"\n\nElira: \"No. We are refusing to lie to children just to buy gratitude.\"\n\nLucien: \"Certainty comforts.\"\n\nValen snapped back from beside me at the slate.\n\nValen: \"Certainty has been the favorite costume of every doctrine that bled this academy dry.\"\n\nHalden, who had watched in silence since the threshold closed, stepped up beside Valen's slate and spoke straight into the channel. The hall resisted him less than I expected; perhaps it recognized old faculty, or still hoped scrutiny could be bent back into authority.\n\nHalden: \"Voss.\"\n\nEvery reflection on the desktops pivoted toward the slate's projection.\n\nLucien: \"Professor Halden. Still cataloging what braver men would claim?\"\n\nHalden didn't rise to the bait.\n\nHalden: \"You built this hall to provoke premature rescue.\"\n\nLucien: \"I inherited an old principle and clarified it.\"\n\nHalden: \"You gutted it because complexity made mercy harder to own.\"\n\nValen glanced sideways at Halden, and I did too. His austere face hadn't raised its voice, yet the anger underneath had honed itself into a scalpel.\n\nHalden: \"The original moving hall tested whether witnesses could guard personhood under shifting rules. You turned movement into obedience, rank into distance, and uncertainty into shame.\"\n\nLucien's reflection smiled faintly.\n\nLucien: \"Institutions naturally seek efficient shapes.\"\n\nHalden: \"Which is precisely why they require witnesses stubborn enough to inconvenience them.\"\n\nAira: \"I am experiencing severe academic whiplash.\"\n\nThe classroom bulbs flickered. At the back wall, Mira slowly raised her hand. When every head turned, she blushed to her hairline.\n\nMira: \"If the hall moves by fear signatures, can students shift it by refusing the lesson?\"\n\nNera's eyes widened.\n\nNera: \"Maybe.\"\n\nMira swallowed.\n\nMira: \"Then I want to ask Sela one more question.\"\n\nElira: \"Ask.\"\n\nMira looked down the stretched aisle.\n\nMira: \"Sela Mornet, do you want warmth?\"\n\nDead silence dropped over the room. Not because the question broke a lock, because it anticipated the hall's next trap before the floor even chilled. Sela hugged her thin arms around her ribs; Noctis made even wanting warmth feel like signing a receipt.\n\nFrom a middle row, a thin echo of Tovin murmured through the desks.\n\nTovin: \"Warmth costs.\"\n\nNear the wall, a faint voice from the missing Noctis line answered, carrying the trace marked Pella Orthis.\n\nPella Orthis: \"Warmth is counted.\"\n\nOutside by the healer cot, Iven lifted Cael's cracked thermal charm in shaking fingers.\n\nIven: \"Not always.\"\n\nThe classroom shuddered violently, though Cael's name never surfaced on the blackboard. Elira's eyes met mine through the slate. The hall wanted Cael as a ledger of grief, proof that survival owed blood to the dead; refusing to summon him kept him out of Lucien's hands.\n\nSela stared at the cracked charm in the projection, then back at Mira.\n\nSela: \"I don't know.\"\n\nMira nodded as though that were the finest answer in the room.\n\nMira: \"Then we can wait.\"\n\nThe teacher's podium collapsed into jagged black frost. Lucien's image vanished from the desktops and materialized inside the far window, standing against a flat horizon of white snow.\n\nLucien: \"Very well. Let them learn what waiting costs.\"\n\nBefore frost crossed the sill, the dying classroom threw one last net. It called roll by function.\n\n**RESERVE ELEVEN.**\n\nSela flinched, lips pressed shut.\n\nMira: \"Sela Mornet is present by name.\"\n\nThe brass bell rang once. Nobody stirred.\n\n**GREEN OBSERVER.**\n\nFinn's voice cracked down the middle on her surname, yet held.\n\nFinn: \"Mira Castellan is present by name.\"\n\n**TACTICAL SUPPORT.**\n\nAira lifted one eyebrow.\n\nAira: \"Aira Solen is present and deeply unimpressed by lazy categorization.\"\n\nThe desks nearest her slid backward.\n\n**BINDER.**\n\nDarian let out one quiet breath at Nera's shoulder.\n\nNera: \"Nera Vail is present. Binding is a skill, not a name.\"\n\nChalk gouged deeper into the slate.\n\n**DEFECTOR.**\n\nDarian gave a razor-thin smile.\n\nDarian: \"Darian Thorne is present. Defection is a direction, not a person.\"\n\nThe room faltered, starved of the shame it expected.\n\n**BELOVED PRESSURE POINT.**\n\nEvery reflection turned on Elira, eager for a flinch, a blush, or fury it could log as proof. She gave it empty stillness.\n\nElira: \"Elira Vale is present. Beloved is a relationship I choose. Pressure point is someone else's weapon.\"\n\nHer crystal flashed clear.\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED RESPONSE.**\n\nEvery windowpane inside the hall turned toward the ghostly reflection of me standing in the classroom mirror while my body remained outside. Cold iron tightened behind my sternum.\n\nI hesitated a fraction too long, hunting for the sharp, watertight sentence that would prove I had outgrown every trap, until I realized hunting for perfection was the trap.\n\nThrough the slate, Aira's voice cut clean through the static.\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nJust my name. I let out a slow breath.\n\nHaru: \"Haru Aerlen is present outside the hall by witness condition.\"\n\nSpiderweb fractures spread across the classroom mirror without breaking it. At the white window, Lucien watched closely.\n\nLucien: \"Always outside until someone needs impossible power. How convenient.\"\n\nElira faced his reflection squarely.\n\nElira: \"He stays outside because we built a structure where he isn't the first tool reached for.\"\n\nLucien: \"And when your first tools break?\"\n\nLeon cut in before she could speak.\n\nLeon: \"Then we use the next one without pretending a tool is a king.\"\n\nCassian: \"Or a throne.\"\n\nRegalia had never spoken that word aloud yet, and still the classroom temperature plummeted. Beside me, Valen's hand sharpened over the slate.\n\nValen: \"Log that. The hall recognizes sovereign vocabulary.\"\n\nRhea: \"Logged.\"\n\nEvery word wiped off the blackboard at once, leaving bare slate for half a beat before small, uneven child's handwriting crept into the bottom corner behind the frost-rimmed podium.\n\n*I am cold.*\n\nNo rank. No plea. Just fact. Mira pointed without speaking aloud, and Aira's pencil scratched fast as her face paled.\n\nAira: \"Condition noted. No content demanded.\"\n\nThe room trembled, realizing it had handed them the next key. Elira looked at Sela.\n\nElira: \"Is that yours?\"\n\nSela stared at the corner of the board for three long beats.\n\nSela: \"Maybe.\"\n\nElira: \"Maybe is accepted.\"\n\nWarmth seemed to ripple through the trapped children even before heat arrived. In the frosted window, Lucien's reflection thinned.\n\nLucien: \"You will freeze under the weight of all these maybes.\"\n\nMira hugged her notebook tight to her ribs.\n\nMira: \"Then we'll write them down before they melt.\"\n\nThen the classroom floor turned to snow.\n\nInk froze inside Mira's nib first. Desks dissolved into pale drifts, and the Green row vanished under biting white until Sela and the other children stood barefoot on ground that remembered winter drills.\n\nThe witnesses stayed planted where they stood, while the hall scrambled to relabel their positions: Aira as handler, Leon as resource officer, Cassian as ration keeper, Nera as binder, Elira as authorized Yellow intervention.\n\nThey cut every label down in turn.\n\nAira: \"Beside doesn't mean handler.\"\n\nLeon: \"Resource doesn't mean ownership.\"\n\nCassian: \"Heat doesn't mean ration.\"\n\nNera: \"Binding skill doesn't mean custody.\"\n\nElira: \"Intervention never outranks consent.\"\n\nSnow stalled around their ankles as if recalculating. From the white window, Lucien watched them shiver.\n\nLucien: \"You can rename positions all night and still leave them freezing.\"\n\nElira looked straight at the pale-lipped children.\n\nElira: \"True. Words aren't warmth. Without honest words, warmth becomes debt before it ever reaches skin.\"\n\nMira pressed her frost-rimed notebook against her chest.\n\nMira: \"Then we start with honest words and move fast.\"\n\nAira gave one sharp nod.\n\nAira: \"That is the least stupid summary of our lives.\"\n\nThe drifts shifted uneasily as the slate field widened beyond the walls of the collapsing classroom, turning the lesson from names to the price of keeping bodies alive.\n\nOutside by the healer cot, Iven began shaking uncontrollably while Maelin tucked a second blanket around his shoulders.\n\nIven: \"They make you wait until someone offers heat. Ask, and they dock you tomorrow. Stay quiet, and they praise you for endurance.\"\n\nValen scribbled with grief etched deep around her eyes.\n\nValen: \"Winter endurance doctrine.\"\n\nIven: \"No. Winter gratitude.\"\n\nInside the slate, Nera closed her eyes as though the phrase had physically struck her.\n\nIven: \"They call it gratitude.\"\n\nThe frost inside the channel bit harder, proving the hall still fed on Iven's memory. Rhea dropped to one knee beside his cot.\n\nRhea: \"Iven, you may stop warning us now.\"\n\nHe clutched Cael's cracked charm tighter.\n\nIven: \"No.\"\n\nRhea: \"That no is accepted.\"\n\nThe charm gave a faint pulse of heat, and the winter field tore the rest of the classroom walls open.\n\nBy then, the witnesses knew how to be frightened cleanly. Reckless fear kicked doors down and called rubble a rescue; clean fear counted exits, checked who could refuse warmth, and refused to confuse speed with care. Aira wrote while walking through ankle-deep drifts, pencil carving stiff paper faster than the storm could turn conditions into commands.\n\nAira: \"Visible signatures: Sela Mornet confirmed by name. Tovin Reiss possible. Pella Orthis unknown. Josen unknown. Environmental pressure: frostbite risk, kneeling trigger active, gratitude-debt grammar live.\"\n\nMira glanced sideways at her, teeth chattering.\n\nMira: \"How are you calm?\"\n\nAira: \"I'm not. I'm organized.\"\n\nWind whipped across the drifts, snapping at Aira's pages. Finn stepped up to her windward shoulder and held his own heavy notebook upright as a shield.\n\nFinn: \"Paper support.\"\n\nAira: \"Deeply heroic.\"\n\nFinn: \"I can't feel my ears.\"\n\nAira: \"Still heroic.\"\n\nMira let out one small, helpless laugh, and beside her, Sela echoed it as barely a breath. The snow field faltered around laughter that didn't pretend it wasn't afraid.\n\nFor one heartbeat, the storm lost its cadence. Elira caught the opening immediately.\n\nElira: \"Sela, we are going to ask about warmth next. You don't have to be ready.\"\n\nSela stared up at her through blowing white.\n\nSela: \"What if I say yes just because I'm cold?\"\n\nElira: \"Then we treat cold as a condition, not consent by itself. We watch for punishment. We send warmth slowly enough that you can stop it even after it starts.\"\n\nSela blinked through frost-stung lashes.\n\nSela: \"You can refuse after yes?\"\n\nDead quiet fell across the snow. That was the other thing Noctis had stolen: not just the first no, the right to take back a yes.\n\nAira: \"Yes can stop.\"\n\nFinn wrote it down.\n\nMira: \"Yes can stop.\"\n\nNera: \"Yes can stop.\"\n\nThe sentence passed down the line until even Cassian murmured it, awkward and dead serious.\n\nCassian: \"Yes can stop.\"\n\nBehind me on the cot, Iven wept openly without once apologizing.\n\nInside the storm, the cold lost its rhythm altogether. Lucien's image flickered along the last pane of classroom glass, irritation breaking clear through his mask. He had built a dozen snares around refusal and forgotten that real consent meant being able to end agreement too.\n\nElira saw the crack in his face at once.\n\nElira: \"That's what you never learned.\"\n\nLucien: \"I learned hesitation kills.\"\n\nElira: \"No. You learned to call control certainty because you were terrified people would leave if they were free to stop.\"\n\nRage sharpened Lucien's reflection like glass cracking under frost.\n\nThen the snow rose in a blinding wall.\n\nWhite glare swallowed the last shattered classroom window and slammed straight into Valen's witness slate, blowing the image into blinding static. Dren and Halden closed in on either side of me as the Black Circle hammered against my ribs, begging to tear the west wall open.\n\nValen braced both palms around the failing channel crystal, fighting to drag the feed back to the last clean fracture before the storm erased it: the exact instant Mira's fourth question broke through Lucien's route.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-28-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep29","label":"Episode 23","number":"23","file":"episode-29-cover.webp","fallback":"The Vigil That Held the Dawn","eyebrow":"Aftermath","recap":"","accent":["#0f172a","#c0c0c0","#3b82f6"],"displayLabel":"Episode 23","displayNumber":"23","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"Green Circle Witnesses","body":"The witness slate returned at the instant Mira's fourth question broke through Lucien's route. Sela.\n\nOne name, spoken from inside a classroom built to replace names with functions.\n\nI remained outside with Dren and Halden while Valen held the failing channel open. The Black Circle pressed against my ribs, offering entry. I held because Mira, Finn, Aira, Leon, Cassian, Nera, Darian, and Elira were already inside, and because making their work mine would have proved the hall's argument for it.\n\nThe false classroom split around Sela's recovered name. Desks, rank lights, and Lucien's hand vanished into white glare.\n\nWhen the slate cleared, the lesson had changed. Now it was a training field under winter snow.\n\nIven sobbed from the cot.\n\nIven: \"That is where they make first-years stand.\"\n\nMaelin held a cup to his lips.\n\nMaelin: \"You are here. Not there.\"\n\nIven nodded like he wanted to believe it and did not yet know how.\n\nInside the hall, frost gathered around the witnesses' ankles.\n\nThe cold did not arrive like weather. It arrived like instruction.\n\nThe first instruction was stillness.\n\nEvery missing student in the snow field knew it before the witnesses did. Their shoulders locked. Their hands stayed flat at their sides. No one rubbed their arms or bent to cover bare feet. The snow climbed over toes and cuffs with patient cruelty, waiting for a child to shiver wrong.\n\nIt did not freeze everyone equally. Green students received deeper drifts. Blue students received quiet cues about distribution. Red students received heat they were forbidden to share without permission. Yellow students received swept paths and the shame of noticing them.\n\nElira stepped off her clean path.\n\nThe hall wrote **WASTED ADVANTAGE**. She looked straight at Sela.\n\nElira: \"No. Shared condition.\"\n\nThe swept path vanished beneath her boots, leaving her ankle-deep in the same frost as Sela.\n\nSela stared at the vanished path before anyone could explain it.\n\nSela: \"You can be cold too?\"\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nSnow settled along the hem of Elira's sleeves as she kept her hands open and still.\n\nSela: \"But you could choose not to.\"\n\nElira: \"That is why choosing matters.\"\n\nThe lesson had no answer for that except more cold. Elira stayed in the snow, and Sela watched her choose it.\n\nAira's voice crackled through Valen's slate.\n\nAira: \"Cold field confirmed. Students barefoot. No visible restraints. That means the restraint is the lesson.\"\n\nMaelin, outside with us, looked like she wanted to break the jaw of whoever had designed the hall.\n\nMaelin: \"Ask before warming.\"\n\nLeon answered from inside the storm.\n\nLeon: \"I hate that you are right.\"\n\nCassian: \"I can raise the field temperature three degrees without touching them.\"\n\nElira: \"Wait.\"\n\nThe word stopped him because he trusted her, not because she commanded heat.\n\nElira moved through the drifts toward Sela Mornet, slowly, empty hands visible, her gold aura banked low enough not to become a sun the hall could call hierarchy.\n\nThe other children watched her with the wary stillness of students who had learned that kindness from powerful people arrived with witnesses and left with debt. Sela's lips were blue. Elira knelt before her in the snow.\n\nThe whole field recoiled. Yellow Circle did not kneel to Green in Noctis lessons. Yellow evaluated, commanded, and corrected. It did not lower itself until its eyes were level with a freezing child who had just remembered her surname.\n\nElira: \"Sela Mornet, do you want warmth?\"\n\nThe question had been asked before the snow came. Now it had teeth.\n\nSela stared at Elira's face.\n\nLucien stood at the far edge of the field in a coat too clean for weather.\n\nLucien: \"She wants release. Ask a cold child whether she wants warmth and call the answer consent. How humane.\"\n\nElira did not look at him.\n\nElira: \"Sela, if you say yes, we will send warmth slowly. If you say no, we will not. If you do not know, we wait. If the hall punishes any answer, we name that as coercion and stop the route.\"\n\nSela's eyes shifted from Elira to Mira.\n\nMira stood in the Green line now. The hall had placed her there after the classroom collapsed, barefoot in the snow like the missing students, her notebook clutched against her ribs. Her toes were turning red against the ice.\n\nAira stood beside her, coat still on, deliberately not offering it yet. Mira's teeth chattered once before Aira turned to her.\n\nAira: \"Mira Castellan, do you want my coat?\"\n\nMira's eyes filled instantly, not from the coat, from being asked.\n\nMira: \"Please.\"\n\nHer numb fingers tightened around the spine of her notebook as Aira slipped the coat off and laid it across Mira's shoulders without ceremony.\n\nThe hall wrote above them:\n\n**GREEN WITNESS ACCEPTS UNEARNED WARMTH.**\n\nFinn's voice cut through the slate from inside.\n\nFinn: \"Objection. Warmth was requested and given without debt language.\"\n\nAira, coatless and shivering now, pointed straight at the floating script.\n\nAira: \"Also, unearned from whom? The weather?\"\n\nThe lettering flickered. Cassian let out a strangled sound.\n\nCassian: \"Do I laugh or attack?\"\n\nLeon: \"Neither.\"\n\nCassian: \"Terrible options.\"\n\nSela watched Aira begin to shiver because she had given her coat away. The hall leaned into the lesson: warmth creates debt, debt creates route, route creates ownership.\n\nMira caught the trap at the same instant I did. She reached up to pull the coat back off. Aira caught the collar.\n\nAira: \"No. Ask.\"\n\nMira froze. Aira's voice softened.\n\nAira: \"If you want to share it, ask me. Do not panic-return kindness because the room called it debt.\"\n\nMira swallowed hard.\n\nMira: \"Do you want it back?\"\n\nAira: \"Not yet.\"\n\nThe hall hated *not yet* almost as much as *no*.\n\nThe snow deepened around their ankles. Elira remained kneeling before Sela.\n\nSela: \"If I say yes, someone else gets colder.\"\n\nLeon answered before Elira could, because the question concerned distribution and water knew how to carry without owning.\n\nLeon: \"Not if we build it correctly.\"\n\nHe crouched, palms hovering over the snow. Water-thread spread outward beneath the white crust, mapping temperature rather than claiming bodies. Cassian lowered one hand over Leon's shoulder, heat gathering in his palm without leaving it yet.\n\nLeon: \"We can warm the ground between you first. Not your skin. Not your crest. The ground. You step into it if you choose.\"\n\nCassian: \"I can keep the heat low.\"\n\nSela looked suspicious. Suspicion was healthier than trained gratitude.\n\nSela: \"What do you get?\"\n\nCassian's half-smile disappeared.\n\nCassian: \"Annoyed at Lucien.\"\n\nLeon shut his eyes briefly.\n\nLeon: \"Not helpful.\"\n\nCassian answered again, with more care than I had ever heard from him.\n\nCassian: \"Nothing. You do not owe me anything. The heat is not a contract.\"\n\nThe hall wrote above them:\n\n**RED CIRCLE CHARITY ESTABLISHES FAVOR CLAIM.**\n\nCassian looked up at the letters.\n\nCassian: \"I am going to burn that sentence later.\"\n\nElira: \"Later.\"\n\nCassian: \"I said later.\"\n\nLeon sent the first thread of warmth into the snow between Sela's feet and Mira's line. Not touching. Waiting.\n\nSela stared at the faint rising steam. For a long time, no one moved. Then she shifted one bare foot half an inch.\n\nThe steam touched her toes. She flinched, expecting pain. None came.\n\nThe field shuddered, not from mana, but from a child learning that warmth could arrive without a hook.\n\nAnother student in the Blue line began to cry silently. Nera turned toward him.\n\nNera: \"Name?\"\n\nHe shook his head so hard his hair whipped across his face. Nera stopped at once.\n\nNera: \"You do not have to answer.\"\n\nHis crying turned audible. Darian studied the boy's crest.\n\nDarian: \"Possible Tovin Reiss. Not confirmed.\"\n\nThe boy's shoulders jerked harder at the surname. From inside the snow line, Finn called out for the slate.\n\nFinn: \"Possible positive reaction to Tovin Reiss. Not full confirmation.\"\n\nThe hall wrote across the sky:\n\n**UNCONFIRMED STUDENTS RECEIVE NO RESOURCE ALLOCATION.**\n\nRhea's voice cut into the outside channel.\n\nRhea: \"Council objection. Emergency warmth is not contingent on completed paperwork.\"\n\nSenna's crystal pulsed beside Valen.\n\nSenna: \"Recorded.\"\n\nValen let out a tight breath.\n\nValen: \"And about time.\"\n\nLeon extended the ground-warmth toward the Blue line.\n\nThe boy did not step. He looked straight at Nera. Not Elira. Nera.\n\nHer composure cracked.\n\nNera: \"Tovin Reiss, if that is you, you do not have to forgive me to accept heat.\"\n\nThe boy sobbed once.\n\nTovin: \"You watched.\"\n\nNera did not defend herself. She met his eyes without blinking, her voice quiet and bare.\n\nNera: \"I did.\"\n\nTovin: \"You looked away.\"\n\nNera: \"Every time.\"\n\nDarian's hand curled into a fist at his side. Nera kept going, because confession stopped being useful the moment it hid behind manners.\n\nNera: \"I cannot make that smaller. I can tell the truth where I once arranged it. You do not owe me trust.\"\n\nThe warmth reached the edge of Tovin's bare feet. He did not move for it.\n\nSela looked across the snow at him, then stepped cleanly into her own warm patch. The movement was tiny. It gave him permission no adult in the room could offer. Tovin stepped too.\n\nThe snow field cracked from one side to the other.\n\nLucien's expression shifted for half a beat. Surprise. There was the crack Elira had warned me to preserve in him: the irritation of a strategist whose map had no line for solidarity moving sideways.\n\nLucien: \"Children imitate available safety. That does not make them free.\"\n\nElira stood slowly. Her healer charm flashed gold, then steadied.\n\nElira: \"No. It gives them the first shape freedom can borrow.\"\n\nThe field retaliated.\n\nEvery student dropped to one knee as snow rose like hands around their shins and dragged them down. Mira cried out as her knee struck ice. Aira dropped beside her at once, refusing to let the room isolate Mira on the ground.\n\nAira: \"Kneeling pressure confirmed.\"\n\nHer voice shook with cold.\n\nAira: \"Not consent. Not reverence. Not gratitude.\"\n\nFinn shouted toward the channel:\n\nFinn: \"Recorded!\"\n\nThe hall deepened the downward pull. Outside, the Black Circle pressed hard against my ribs. I could open the floor. I could tear the snow field out by its roots and end every false lesson in a heartbeat.\n\nDren's hand hovered near my shoulder without touching. He did not need to.\n\nInside the slate, Elira turned toward the channel as if she could see me standing there.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am here.\"\n\nElira: \"Do not take the field.\"\n\nThe words cut deep. They also kept me anchored.\n\nHaru: \"Understood.\"\n\nElira looked back down at the kneeling students.\n\nElira: \"Sela, do you want help standing?\"\n\nSela's teeth chattered as she forced her chin up.\n\nSela: \"Please.\"\n\nElira did not lift her. She looked to Mira instead.\n\nMira understood and reached across from her own kneeling place. Sela caught her hand. Two Green Circle girls, one from Aurelis and one from Noctis, pulled together against a lesson built to keep them low.\n\nThey did not stand all the way, not yet. Still, the ice beneath their knees split.\n\nThe hall tried to drown the crack beneath another brass bell.\n\nIven screamed from the cot outside, not in panic, in warning.\n\nIven: \"Next is the city!\"\n\nThe field froze. Elira's head whipped toward the false horizon.\n\nA broken skyline rose up through the snow. Wounded City towers, impossible and cruel. The hall was rebuilding catastrophe into another lesson.\n\nThe false Wounded City did not copy the real one faithfully, which made it worse. The towers stood too neat. The ruined streets drew the eye straight toward a single central wound, lit by theatrical ash. The hall had studied ruin and mistaken it for stagecraft.\n\nStill, the body did not care that the copy was flawed. Elira stopped breathing for half a second, and I did too.\n\nThe witness slate in Valen's hands crackled as the interior team reacted. Aira swore. Leon's water threads snapped into hiss and steam. Cassian's heat flared bright enough to wash the edges of the projection white.\n\nMaelin's fingers clamped around my wrist, hard.\n\nMaelin: \"Aerlen.\"\n\nDark circle pressure had reached my skin without me noticing. That frightened me more than the false skyline.\n\nHaru: \"I am here.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Then stay here.\"\n\nInside the hall, Elira turned away from the central street.\n\nLucien had placed the suggestion of her death there. Not the full sequence; the hall knew better now than to expose private memory without cost. Instead it offered fragments: gold hair against stone, a dark pendant flickering too late, a reaching hand, Cael's outline spun from white return light. It wanted everyone inside to finish the picture themselves.\n\nSela let out a thin gasp. Mira stepped straight between the child and the ruined street.\n\nMira: \"You do not have to look.\"\n\nSela: \"But it happened.\"\n\nElira answered from the false pavement, her voice trembling and refusing to hide it.\n\nElira: \"It did. And you do not owe the hall your eyes just because something is true.\"\n\nThat sentence went through me like a hand finding a wound and refusing to press.\n\nLucien's voice drifted down from the broken tower.\n\nLucien: \"Truth avoided becomes weakness repeated.\"\n\nElira looked up at him.\n\nElira: \"Truth forced becomes another weapon.\"\n\nAira, blunt and furious, shouted over the wind.\n\nAira: \"Objection. False Wounded City is using traumatic sequence without consent, with inaccurate staging and coercive rescue framing.\"\n\nFinn's voice joined hers from inside the snow.\n\nFinn: \"Recorded.\"\n\nThe false city dimmed. Not gone, nothing left that easily, yet less certain of itself.\n\nAric appeared at the center of the street then, a Yellow crest burning at his wrist. Older than Sela, younger than Lucien, he stood with the exhausted stillness of a student trained to be useful long before he was allowed to be afraid.\n\nAric: \"If I do not stay, it repeats.\"\n\nThere was the next hook. Not cold, not debt, responsibility wearing honor's face.\n\nElira stayed where she was. She had learned the hall's favorite conversions: approach could turn into pressure, comfort into claim, rescue into route.\n\nElira: \"Who taught you that?\"\n\nAric's mouth tightened.\n\nAric: \"Everyone.\"\n\nThe plain answer left no easy rebuttal. Noctis had taught it, Lucien had honed it, and old Aurelis had left enough machinery behind for the lesson to take root. Aric looked toward Sela.\n\nAric: \"If final witness leaves, the younger reserves remain open.\"\n\nNera let out a ragged breath. Darian spoke before she could sink under it.\n\nDarian: \"That is what they told older students so younger ones would accept being managed by someone already hurt.\"\n\nAric's eyes flashed.\n\nAric: \"You think I do not know that?\"\n\nDarian went quiet. Aric's anger was not denial; it was the fury of someone who understood the entire trap and still could not find the door.\n\nMira gripped her notebook until the cover bent.\n\nMira: \"What if the door does not need a final witness?\"\n\nAric laughed once, sharp and dry, almost like Cassian if Cassian had grown up without sunlight.\n\nAric: \"Every door needs a close.\"\n\nMira: \"Books close. Doors open again.\"\n\nThe false city flickered. Aric stared at her while Mira flushed crimson, looking terrified of her own sentence.\n\nAira's voice crackled through Valen's slate.\n\nAira: \"For the record, that was unexpectedly good.\"\n\nMira: \"Please do not record it like that.\"\n\nFinn: \"Too late.\"\n\nA layer of ash sloughed off the false towers; the hall hated being laughed at when it wanted tragedy.\n\nLucien stepped into the street beside Aric.\n\nLucien: \"A door that opens again allows recurrence.\"\n\nElira: \"A door that demands a child become its lock is not safety.\"\n\nAric's hands curled into fists at his sides.\n\nAric: \"I am not a child.\"\n\nElira's expression softened.\n\nElira: \"No. You are a student who was told adulthood meant being spent without complaint.\"\n\nThat hit him harder than pity ever could. He looked away.\n\nBehind Mira, Sela spoke softly.\n\nSela: \"You told me where to hide my hands in winter field.\"\n\nAric flinched.\n\nSela: \"So the instructor would not see shaking.\"\n\nThe false city went still. Aric would not meet her eyes.\n\nAric: \"That did not save you.\"\n\nSela: \"It helped.\"\n\nAric's face twisted.\n\nSela: \"Helping is not bad because it was not enough.\"\n\nThat nearly broke him. Behind my ribs, the Black Circle pulsed against old scars from Cael and the Wounded City, where none of us had been enough to stop every blade.\n\nElira looked straight toward the witness slate, toward where she knew I was watching.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"Here.\"\n\nElira: \"Helping is not bad because it was not enough.\"\n\nShe spoke it for me, and for herself, so neither of us would let incomplete rescue become proof that care was pointless.\n\nThe false Wounded City cracked down the central street. Underneath the broken paving, a thin line of dark water moved with patience the hall had not written.\n\nLeon spotted it first and smiled like a man finding an old friend behind enemy lines.\n\nLeon: \"There is a lower channel.\"\n\nCassian: \"Can you reach it?\"\n\nLeon: \"Not alone.\"\n\nCassian held out one hand, no jokes left in him. Leon drove water down through the fissure while Cassian sent controlled heat after it, just enough to keep the current unfrozen. The false street tried to ice the crack shut and failed.\n\nElira turned back to Aric.\n\nElira: \"If the door opens another way, do you want to leave?\"\n\nAric's reply came too fast.\n\nAric: \"I cannot.\"\n\nMira stepped forward, voice trembling.\n\nMira: \"That was not the question.\"\n\nHe stared at her. The hall had trained him for capacity, obligation, and route. It had never trained him for *want*, because want belonged to people, not locks.\n\nFrom inside the hall, Finn's voice cut into the channel with a roughness that had not been there before.\n\nFinn: \"Record risk statement.\"\n\nRhea looked up from the slate outside.\n\nRhea: \"State it.\"\n\nThrough the slate, I could see Finn's hands, ink-stained and red from frost, gripping his receipts. Speaking where Noctis could hear cost a Green student far more than drama.\n\nFinn: \"If I continue objecting, Noctis can identify my handwriting from the public rail copies. My family trades through two Noctis-controlled certification offices. My scholarship review is next month. I am not neutral.\"\n\nThe channel went dead quiet. Lucien's head turned a fraction.\n\nHe had heard. That was the threat institutions preferred because it left no heroic scar: a delayed permit, a pulled scholarship, a cold recommendation letter. Mira looked across the snow at Finn.\n\nMira: \"You do not have to keep recording.\"\n\nFinn let out one shaky laugh.\n\nFinn: \"You are standing in front of Lucien Voss with a notebook.\"\n\nMira: \"That was not an argument.\"\n\nFinn: \"It was my answer.\"\n\nRhea's council seal flared bright beside Valen.\n\nRhea: \"Risk statement accepted. Protective copy initiated.\"\n\nAira chimed in without missing a beat.\n\nAira: \"I have Finn's scholarship file reference.\"\n\nFinn: \"Why?\"\n\nAira: \"Because I am unpleasantly thorough.\"\n\nRhea: \"Solen.\"\n\nAira: \"Protectively thorough.\"\n\nThe correction left Finn's risk real, yet visible enough that Noctis could no longer pretend a quiet retaliation was coincidence.\n\nMira's shoulders squared. Her face had gone pale again, yet she kept her notebook raised.\n\nMira: \"Then record my risk too.\"\n\nElira turned toward her.\n\nMira: \"My family owns a copy shop near the west gate. We print academy forms, council notices, tournament programs, and sometimes Noctis supplemental packets when their delegation office is short. If I become inconvenient, they can stop sending work and say it was preference.\"\n\nShe swallowed hard.\n\nMira: \"I am continuing.\"\n\nSela stared at her, learning in real time that help had a price and someone was naming it instead of pretending mercy was free. Aric stared too, watching two Green students accept ordinary ruin without asking to be called noble for it.\n\nLucien smiled faintly.\n\nLucien: \"Public martyrdom. How efficient.\"\n\nMira flinched, then steadied.\n\nMira: \"No. Martyrs are useful after they stop arguing. I am still arguing.\"\n\nThe moving hall shuddered. Outside, Halden leaned over the slate.\n\nHalden: \"Excellent.\"\n\nDren: \"Quietly.\"\n\nHalden shut his mouth.\n\nInside, Aric looked from Mira to Finn to Sela, then down at his own hands.\n\nAric: \"They can hurt you for this.\"\n\nFinn tapped his stacked receipts into neat alignment.\n\nFinn: \"Yeah.\"\n\nAric: \"And you are still leaving when the door opens?\"\n\nFinn: \"If I can.\"\n\nMira corrected him, voice still shaking.\n\nMira: \"If you want.\"\n\nAric shut his eyes. The lower channel widened slightly beneath the street.\n\nHe did not answer right away, and Finn marked the silence out loud.\n\nFinn: \"Pause before response. No refusal supplied. No consent supplied.\"\n\nLucien's jaw tightened. Finn pressed on anyway.\n\nFinn: \"Witness appears to be considering want as separate from assigned function.\"\n\nNoctis systems hated waiting for a student's interior life, and the hall fought back at once. Snow crept up from the ground over Mira's shoes, then Finn's, then the hem of Aira's coat around Mira's shoulders, turning patience into physical sting.\n\nAira's teeth chattered.\n\nAira: \"Record additional coercive condition.\"\n\nMira tried to lift her notebook and found her fingers stiff with cold. Sela noticed first.\n\nFor the first time since speaking her name, the child moved without being asked. She stepped close and placed both small hands under the bottom of Mira's notebook to hold it steady.\n\nMira looked down at her in shock.\n\nSela: \"You write. I can hold.\"\n\nMira blinked hard, the rescue finally flowing both ways: Sela steadying the notebook, Aira's coat over Mira's shoulders, Finn risking his scholarship, Elira kneeling in frost, Leon and Cassian holding the warm channel open beneath the street, and Aric standing at the center being granted the right not to rush.\n\nOutside, I gripped the edge of Valen's slate hard enough to bend the metal rim. Dren laid one hand over mine, steady and unmoving.\n\nDren: \"They are not waiting because you cannot help.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nDren: \"Say the rest.\"\n\nI hated that he was right.\n\nHaru: \"They are waiting because he deserves time.\"\n\nThe Black Circle eased a fraction beneath my ribs. Halden spoke almost under his breath.\n\nHalden: \"That is the distinction containment theory never makes.\"\n\nRhea heard him anyway.\n\nRhea: \"Then do not let it become theory before it becomes protection.\"\n\nHalden closed his mouth.\n\nInside, Aric finally opened his eyes.\n\nAric: \"If I leave, some part of me will still think I abandoned them.\"\n\nElira's voice carried cleanly across the snow, gentle without offering cheap comfort.\n\nElira: \"It will.\"\n\nAric watched her.\n\nElira: \"That thought may come with you. It does not get to decide for you.\"\n\nMira's voice shook as she looked up from her page.\n\nMira: \"And if it keeps telling you that you only mattered while staying, we'll write down the truth every time until it stops.\"\n\nFinn glanced up from his receipts.\n\nFinn: \"I am already in trouble. Might as well stay consistent.\"\n\nSela gave a tiny nod, still holding the notebook.\n\nAric looked down at her. Sela did not plead. She refused to make herself another chain.\n\nAric's mouth opened twice before sound came out.\n\nAric: \"I do not know.\"\n\nElira nodded once.\n\nElira: \"Then we preserve 'I do not know.'\"\n\nOutside the slate, Rhea logged witness patience first, proving the rescue had waited for a person rather than a route. Mira wrote the line before cold could lock her fingers again, and Finn echoed it aloud so the channel held it.\n\nAric heard his uncertainty protected instead of punished. A delayed answer no longer counted as weakness or hesitation; it counted as time owed to someone who had been hurried toward usefulness for years.\n\nHe let out a short, startled laugh. This time it did not sound like Cassian without sunlight. It sounded like a boy surprised by air.\n\nAric: \"I want to leave.\"\n\nRhea marked the second line beside the first as exit consent.\n\nBeneath the false street, the lower channel shifted, not open all the way, waiting for a bridge that did not belong to me.\n\nOutside, the Black Circle pressed downward, asking Aurelis for a way under the lie. I did not pour myself into it. I asked, through restraint and every lesson Cael, Elira, and the Wounded City had carved into me.\n\n*May I help without becoming the route?*\n\nThe pressure behind my sternum answered in cold and ozone. Not permission. A condition.\n\n*Only if they remain closer to him than you are to the answer.*\n\nI understood.\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nThrough the slate, she lifted her head.\n\nHaru: \"The lower channel can open if Aric's closest answer is not me.\"\n\nComprehension flashed across Elira's face. She looked around at Sela, Mira, Tovin, Nera, Darian, Aira, Leon, Cassian, and Iven's voice over the channel.\n\nElira: \"Then we do not make Haru the bridge.\"\n\nLucien's eyes narrowed. He had prepared for me to refuse or overwhelm; he had not prepared for delegation to become magic.\n\nThe channel answered from beneath the broken street. Water rose through the cracked stones in seven narrow lines, one for each witness position the hall had tried to turn into hierarchy.\n\nLeon anchored one. Cassian warmed the next. Mira named the third, while Sela touched the fourth with two trembling fingers. Nera and Darian held the fifth together without either claiming it. Aira pinned an objection slip into the wet stone along the sixth. Elira held the seventh simply by standing near Aric and not reaching for him.\n\nNone of the seven lines touched me. The Black Circle remained outside under the flagstones, unseated from the center.\n\nAric stared at the glowing water lines.\n\nAric: \"If I step on one, does it close?\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nMira: \"It carries.\"\n\nSela's thin voice followed.\n\nSela: \"And if you step off, it is still there.\"\n\nAric's face tightened as though mercy hurt more than finality. Lucien saw it and lunged too late; for once, another answer reached the boy before his doctrine did.\n\nAric stepped onto the nearest water line.\n\nNothing closed. Nothing crowned him. Nothing demanded a final word.\n\nHe stared down at his own boot as if the world had made a mistake. Warmth rose along the line through the broken street, not enough to save everyone at once, enough to prove movement did not have to mean sacrifice. Aric's breath hitched.\n\nAric: \"I can step off?\"\n\nSela answered this time.\n\nSela: \"Yes can stop.\"\n\nThe line held.\n\nAbove the false Wounded City, the painted sky tore away to show black glass behind it. The lower channel opened with a grind of wet stone breaking free. Water lines beneath Aric's feet tugged backward, hard enough that Sela dropped to one knee and slapped both palms flat against the stones.\n\nSela: \"Hold!\"\n\nNot to Aric. To the hall.\n\nFor one heartbeat, the child held back a room built to spend her. The witness slate flickered and dimmed, showing only my own pale reflection in the dark glass before Elira's voice cut through again.\n\nElira: \"We have him for now.\"\n\nFor now was not victory, yet it was enough to move.\n\nLucien answered Aric's step with force. The false city folded inward along its white lesson-lines. Leon's water routes snapped toward the exit while Cassian caught the recoil with both hands and drove Red fire straight into the rank seal rationing warmth beneath the street.\n\nCassian burned the color gates out. The backlash split his right glove and blistered his palm, yet heat surged through every water line at once. Leon caught it and sent a moving path straight beneath the rescued students.\n\nCassian: \"Now!\"\n\nMira called names. Finn echoed them toward the outside slate. Aira held the record channel open while Nera and Darian shepherded Sela, Tovin, and the younger students onto Leon's moving path. Sela crossed first, clutching her own name like a coal against the cold.\n\nAric took one step toward them. Lucien closed his hand.\n\nBlack glass slammed down between Aric and the exit. The rescued children spilled into the outer court with Nera and Darian behind them; Elira, Mira, Aira, Finn, Leon, and Cassian remained trapped inside when the threshold sealed shut again.\n\nCassian slammed his uninjured fist against the barrier. It did not budge.\n\nInside the fading channel, Aric stood beside Lucien and stared down at the single water line still shining beneath his boot.\n\nAric: \"It stayed.\"\n\nLucien erased the street around him.\n\nOutside, the returned children screamed. Beneath my boots, Aurelis asked the Black Circle whether I wanted a way under the lie.\n\n## Interlude: The Page Outside\n\nThe Green first-year at the outer copy table had been told to duplicate whatever survived the slate. No one had explained what to do when the slate went black.\n\nOne moment, Mira's handwriting moved across the relay page in hurried gray strokes. The next, the page tore across with a vertical smear as the threshold sealed with Mira, Aira, Finn, Elira, Leon, Cassian, and Aric still inside.\n\nThe first-year waited for an adult instruction. Around him, adults barked several at once: seal the record, clear the table, move the rescued children, restrict visual access to the Black Circle event, preserve chain of custody. None of them answered the page.\n\nHe stared at the last complete lines Mira had sent:\n\n**ARIC STATES: I WANT TO LEAVE.**\n\n**PAUSE BEFORE STATEMENT PRESERVED.**\n\n**NO FINAL WITNESS CONSENT.**\n\nBelow them, the ink had dragged sideways when the hall snapped shut. Mira's final word might have been *carry*. It might have been *careful*. It might even have been *Cael*, though Cael was not in the room.\n\nThe first-year did not clean it up. He copied the smear exactly.\n\nAt the center of the outer court, the floor opened in a black ring. Everyone forgot the table.\n\nStone sank without falling. Darkness pressed upward without becoming light. Haru Aerlen stood at the rim with both hands visible while Instructor Dren and Professor Halden forced him to name what he intended before the old power could make intention irrelevant.\n\nThe first-year's pencil tip snapped through the paper. He grabbed another and kept going.\n\n**BLACK OPENING FORMED AFTER THRESHOLD CLOSED.**\n\nHis hand shook too badly for the next line.\n\nA council aide reached across the table, while two more clerks hurried up behind him with a privacy screen to wall off the official record rail.\n\nCouncil Aide: \"That record is now restricted.\"\n\nThe first-year covered the page with both arms. He had no clever answer. Mira would have found one after three frightened breaths; Aira would have made restriction sound embarrassed to exist; Rhea could have stopped the hand with a seal. He had only his body and a Green Circle that could not push anyone back.\n\nFirst-Year: \"Mira said copies remain distributed.\"\n\nCouncil Aide: \"Mira is inside a hostile structure.\"\n\nFirst-Year: \"She is.\"\n\nThe words came out smaller than he wanted, yet he kept his weight pinned over the paper.\n\nFirst-Year: \"That is why her copy stays outside.\"\n\nThe aide's expression turned cold and calculating. A second clerk demanded witness names while the third reached for the table frame to haul it behind the privacy screen. No one needed to burn a page if they could move it somewhere harmless.\n\nThe first-year tore the copy straight down the middle.\n\nThe aide lunged for both halves. The boy let him take the top. The bottom half vanished smoothly into the sleeve of the Green student standing beside him.\n\nNo signal passed between them. They had spent days copying Mira's rule: one page can be taken; disagreement must survive elsewhere. The second student walked away into the crowd before anyone decided walking was suspicious, leaving the first-year seated with the official half-copy flattened under his palms.\n\nThe hidden half of the page was already moving through other hands when his pocket relay began vibrating.\n\nHe ignored it. It buzzed again with the private three-pulse rhythm his mother used only for family emergencies. Over the screaming children and grinding stone beneath Haru's feet, no one else could hear it. That made it harder to refuse.\n\nHe opened the relay under the table edge. No face appeared, only text routed through the west district copy guild where she worked nights repairing municipal forms.\n\n**COME AWAY FROM THE COURT. NOW.**\n\nA second line arrived before he could thumb a reply.\n\n**GUILD RECEIVED NOCTIS NOTICE. YOUR SCHOLARSHIP SPONSOR ASKED WHETHER YOU ARE PARTICIPATING IN UNAUTHORIZED RECORD ACTIVITY.**\n\nThe outer court went colder than the Black Circle ring.\n\nHis scholarship paid for dormitory housing, meal credits, and the medicine his younger brother's lungs needed every winter. Noctis did not have to threaten any of them openly; it only had to ask a sponsor one polite question and let fear do the rest.\n\nThe first-year stared at the official half-page under his hands, then toward the court exit.\n\nHe could walk out right now. The aide had the top half; the bottom half was already loose in the crowd. No one could say he destroyed evidence, and his mother would stop seeing his name attached to words like *unauthorized* and *Black Circle*. He had already stayed long enough to tell himself a clean story later.\n\nHe started to push his chair back.\n\nAcross the court, Sela looked straight at him.\n\nShe knew nothing about his scholarship, his mother, or his brother. She saw only the person at the copy table getting up to leave while the black ring opened in the stone. Her face settled into the guarded blankness Noctis students wore when adults disappeared after promising to stay.\n\nThe first-year sat back down, not because Sela owned his choice, because her eyes showed him the one cost he had left off the ledger.\n\nHe typed with one thumb beneath the table.\n\n**I AM SAFE. I AM COPYING. DO NOT ANSWER SPONSOR YET. SAVE MESSAGE.**\n\nHis mother replied at once.\n\n**I AM AFRAID.**\n\nNo order this time. He typed back:\n\n**ME TOO.**\n\nThe relay went still. Nothing was safer because he named it, yet he kept his hands on the table.\n\nThen Krail's remote seal found him.\n\nRed light bloomed above the official half-copy.\n\n**EXTRA-CLASSIFICATION IMAGE CONTROL**\n\nThe letters descended toward the paper like a blade of policy. Every line describing the black opening began fading first.\n\nThe first-year snatched the ink bottle. The council aide caught his wrist.\n\nCouncil Aide: \"Do not alter restricted evidence.\"\n\nFirst-Year: \"It is being altered right now.\"\n\nThe aide looked down at the vanishing letters and hesitated. That was enough.\n\nThe boy poured ink across his own sleeve instead of the paper, soaking his uniform cloth in a black flood, then pressed his wet forearm straight onto the fading lines. Reversed mirror-writing lifted onto the fabric, smeared and ugly.\n\nKrail's red seal stripped the Black Opening lines off the paper, leaving the upper sheet scarred and blank where they had been, yet it stopped short of his arm. Erasing his sleeve meant claiming a student's body as evidence custody, and Krail's motion had not written that authority.\n\nThe council aide stared at the backwards letters running from the first-year's elbow to his wrist.\n\nCouncil Aide: \"That is not an admissible copy.\"\n\nFirst-Year: \"Then it cannot violate admissible-image control.\"\n\nThe answer surprised both of them. Aira would have sharpened it; Mira would have said it shaking. The boy simply held his dripping sleeve away from his ribs so ink would not stain the flagstones.\n\nThree other Green students saw what he had done and closed in. One pressed scrap paper to his sleeve and lifted a reversed fragment. Another copied that onto a lunch wrapper. A third read the backwards letters aloud while a Blue student wrote them forward inside a tournament program.\n\nIn less than a minute, the record crossed four surfaces: uniform, scrap, wrapper, program. Each jump lost a timestamp or half a word; each kept what Krail's seal had tried to erase: *Haru named a door. Sela said no. No entry yet.*\n\nThe red seal widened, hunting a central copy to suppress, and swung toward the students.\n\nRhea caught it mid-air. Her council seal slammed into Krail's from the side hard enough to scatter red sparks across the court.\n\nRhea: \"My emergency mandate covers student protection and immediate hazards. A remote seal attempting to classify ink on student bodies qualifies as both.\"\n\nKrail's authority recoiled. Rhea did not shatter it; she pinned it above the half-erased official sheet so the attempted wipe stayed visible to everyone in the court.\n\nShe glanced down at the ink dripping from the boy's sleeve onto the table wood.\n\nRhea: \"Preserve the uniform.\"\n\nFirst-Year: \"I only have two.\"\n\nRhea considered the objection more seriously than she had Krail's seal.\n\nRhea: \"Then preserve it until the ink is transcribed, and have Student Council pay for cleaning. No custody claim.\"\n\nThe boy looked down at the reversed words along his forearm.\n\nFirst-Year: \"Can I wear it until Mira comes back?\"\n\nRhea's jaw tightened.\n\nRhea: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer emergency seal settled flat onto the dark wood. Not *if*. *Until*.\n\nThe black ring in the center of the court widened by a breath.\n\nRhea shouted for distance. Maelin hurried the rescued children behind a medical screen. Because Cassian was still trapped inside, nobody made the joke anyone expected when an overturned heat lamp rolled across the stones and struck the leg of the copy table.\n\nFlame licked up the dry corner of the half-erased official page. The council aide jumped back.\n\nThe first-year slapped the fire flat with his bare hand. Pain hit before courage did. He shouted, kicked the lamp away, and ground the burning corner beneath his palm until scorched paper smelled of blistered skin. The surviving sheet held, missing a charred black crescent along one edge.\n\nMaelin spotted the burn from across the court and crossed to him in three strides.\n\nMaelin: \"Do not move.\"\n\nFirst-Year: \"The table,\"\n\nMaelin: \"Can be replaced. Your hand cannot.\"\n\nHe stared past her at the black opening, at Haru refusing to force entry, at the closed threshold holding their friends inside, and at the scorched sheet pinned under trembling fingers.\n\nFirst-Year: \"Mira would say the page matters.\"\n\nMaelin poured cool blue healing film across his blistered palm.\n\nMaelin: \"Mira would say you are part of what the page is for.\"\n\nHe did not know whether that was true, only that he wanted it to be.\n\nRhea looked down at the blue film wrapping his hand.\n\nRhea: \"Name for the injury record?\"\n\nHe almost gave his student number. Numbers felt safer when authority was taking notes. Then he saw Sela watching from beyond the table.\n\nFirst-Year: \"Benn Arlow.\"\n\nRhea wrote it down herself.\n\nRhea: \"Benn Arlow. Green Circle outer-copy witness. Hand burned preserving a page during active hazard. Scholarship pressure reported through family guild channel.\"\n\nBenn looked up sharply.\n\nBenn: \"How did you know about the message?\"\n\nRhea: \"Your relay was open when Krail's seal struck. I heard enough to protect the record, not enough to claim the conversation.\"\n\nShe left the message contents off the public sheet. Benn had not realized authority could admit where its hearing stopped.\n\nThe table legs rattled as the black ring pulsed again. Benn tried to flatten the remaining copy stack with his bandaged hand and hissed when the blister pulled. The Green student beside him took half the pile.\n\nBenn: \"I can keep them.\"\n\nGreen Student: \"You can keep half.\"\n\nNo speech about sacrifice, just half. Benn let go of the bundles one by one until his palm stopped bleeding beneath the film. The other student lashed the loose pages to the table leg with tournament ribbon stripped from a fallen bracket banner.\n\nWhen the stones shuddered again, Benn wrote with his uninjured hand:\n\n**COPY WORK CONTINUED BY TWO PEOPLE AFTER INJURY.**\n\nBeneath it, he signed his name instead of a student number.\n\nHis pocket relay buzzed once more.\n\n**SAW YOUR MESSAGE. BROTHER HAS MEDICINE THROUGH MONTH-END. I AM STILL AFRAID. COME HOME WHEN THE SCHOOL RELEASES YOU, NOT WHEN A SPONSOR DOES.**\n\nBenn read the message twice, then tucked the relay away without copying a word onto the public sheet. Family fear did not turn into communal property just because it sat beside a witness.\n\nInstead, below his signature, he wrote:\n\n**PERSONAL COST EXISTS OUTSIDE VISIBLE INJURY. DETAILS WITHHELD BY CHOICE.**\n\nRhea saw the line and shifted her seal around it, leaving the withheld margin untouched. Benn kept copying with his good hand until the student beside him pointed out blood seeping through his bandage. This time, he stopped on the first warning.\n\nFive minutes after it had vanished into the crowd, the hidden bottom half of the page returned through six different hands. Every student who carried it had added one observation along a different margin.\n\n**Sela said no.**\n\n**Haru stopped widening the ring.**\n\n**Dren asked for action name.**\n\n**No entry yet.**\n\n**Children moved by healer request, not Council custody.**\n\n**Threshold still closed.**\n\nBenn laid the returned bottom half against the scorched, half-erased top sheet and joined the tear with a strip of healer tape. The sentence across the split no longer lined up. One word was gone, and the charred crescent cut straight through the timestamp.\n\nHe made no attempt to neaten the damage. Underneath the tape, he wrote:\n\n**RECORD BROKE WHILE PEOPLE WERE STILL INSIDE. PRESERVE BREAK.**\n\nThe black ring beneath Haru's boots answered with a pulse strong enough to rattle every ink bottle on the table.\n\nBenn caught one before it could spill.\n\nAcross the court, Haru Aerlen lowered his gaze to the stone, asked Aurelis for a door without seizing the ring, and then the open floor gave way into the lower channel.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-29-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep30","label":"Episode 24","number":"24","file":"episode-30-cover.webp","fallback":"The Funeral That Refused Dominion","eyebrow":"Funeral","recap":"","accent":["#111827","#f8fafc","#d4af37"],"displayLabel":"Episode 24","displayNumber":"24","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"When Restraint Breaks the Floor","body":"The floor opened because I asked it to. I had to keep that truth instead of calling it an accident, an instinct, or the Black Circle acting on its own.\n\nI asked Aurelis for a door, and the old academy answered.\n\nStone did not split right away. Aurelis heard me, and the lower channel answered, with twelve heartbeats of danger caught between them.\n\nAt the first heartbeat, the flagstones remembered the old observatory.\n\nAt the third, the false Green Circle veil at my wrist slipped out of rhythm.\n\nAt the sixth, the black ring beneath my boots turned visible to everyone close enough to see the floor.\n\nAt the ninth, pain drove through my sternum so sharply I nearly lost the question I was trying to ask.\n\nOpen below, without consuming, punishing, or claiming. Open below.\n\nStone split beneath my feet in a perfect black ring. Down, straight past the rescued children, Maelin's healer cots, and Rhea's witness station, dropping vertically into the old observatory's lower channels where pre-standardization magic still remembered what a safeguard had been before institutions tried to own one.\n\nDren grabbed Benn Arlow by the shoulder and hauled the bandaged first-year back from the crumbling rim.\n\nHalden swore with quiet, academic precision.\n\nOn the tenth heartbeat, black-silver pressure rose behind my shoulders. Dren saw it and went very still, counting the seconds.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"Aerlen. Release or anchor.\"\n\nI could not release. The moving hall had sealed with Elira inside.\n\nThe eleventh heartbeat broke blood under one of my fingernails.\n\nThe twelfth widened the ring. Rhea shouted three orders, and none of them reached me clearly over the roar of shifting stone. The moving hall had closed with Elira inside, and that single fact tried to swallow the rest of the world.\n\nThen the pendant at her throat flashed once through the broken connection. Open rings. A question.\n\nIt carried neither command nor permission. It only asked what I was doing.\n\nThe Black Circle stopped widening, held back by Elira's question before my panic could disguise itself as mercy.\n\nHaru: \"I am not entering the hall.\"\n\nMy voice sounded too quiet for the pressure humming beneath the stones.\n\nHaru: \"I am opening the lower channel.\"\n\nHalden stepped up to the edge of the ring, keeping both hands where Dren could see them.\n\nHalden: \"For what purpose?\"\n\nThe question was not procedural. It was a hook into personhood, and I caught hold of it.\n\nHaru: \"To give them exits the hall did not design.\"\n\nRhea: \"Recorded. Dren, perimeter. Maelin, students. Valen, with me.\"\n\nThe rescued children were crying, coughing, and clinging to blankets and one another. Sela would not let go of empty air where Mira's hand had been, whispering her name as if calling could turn into a rope.\n\nIven sat upright on his cot, eyes fixed on the black opening.\n\nIven: \"The senior is Aric.\"\n\nHe swallowed hard as Halden turned toward him.\n\nIven: \"Aric Dain. Yellow Circle. Third year. He teaches first-years how not to cry when instructors watch.\"\n\nDarian's jaw tightened beside Halden.\n\nIven: \"He said volunteering is the only way to choose what Noctis takes.\"\n\nCael's shadow hung over the court like a warning pattern. Noctis taught children to find agency only in choosing the shape of their own use, and Elira was trapped inside with that doctrine.\n\nI lowered one hand into the black ring. Cold pressure bit my palm, tasting of ozone and old metal. Old Aurelis answered without sound.\n\n**SAFEGUARD RESPONSE REQUESTED**\n\nHaru: \"Not alone.\"\n\nThe ring pulsed under my knuckles.\n\n**DEFINE WITNESS**\n\nI looked across the practice court: at Rhea and Senna's remote seal, at Halden's ink-stained fingers, at Valen's pale face, at Dren standing between the rescued children and the drop, at Maelin wrapping Sela in a blanket while Nera and Darian steadied the trembling younger children behind her, and at Iven, who had survived being taken and still gave us a name.\n\nHaru: \"Everyone who can say no.\"\n\nSela lifted her head. Her face was wet and pale, and she trembled harder as she found her voice.\n\nSela: \"No.\"\n\nThe Black Circle paused. Every adult in the court turned toward her.\n\nSela: \"Don't take us.\"\n\nThe words struck deeper than any Council motion. I turned completely toward her, away from the opening.\n\nHaru: \"I won't.\"\n\nSela did not relax, and I was glad of it. Trust handed over too quickly after rooms like Noctis would have been another wound.\n\nHaru: \"If the Black Circle moves toward you without someone you trust saying why, you can say no again.\"\n\nSela's eyes moved to Rhea.\n\nRhea: \"Recognized.\"\n\nShe looked to Maelin.\n\nMaelin: \"Very recognized.\"\n\nThen, after a beat, she looked to Dren. The grizzled instructor placed one calloused hand over his chest with dry solemnity.\n\nInstructor Dren: \"No one crosses this line without getting through me first.\"\n\nSela let out a shaky breath. The Black Circle steadied, accepting the boundary.\n\nThe descent opened into a spiral stone stair. No one cheered, and we went down.\n\nMaelin stayed upstairs with the rescued children because a rescue that abandoned the rescued was only spectacle. Dren stayed at the rim to guard the living door. Rhea held the upper command perimeter while sending her council seal down ahead of us, with Senna's remote crystal hovering beside it. Halden, Valen, Nera, Darian, Iven, and I descended.\n\nIven should not have come, and Maelin told him so twelve times before we reached the third turn. He came because the moving hall already logged him as returned and therefore less useful as bait, because Aric had taught him how not to cry, and because he wanted Aric to learn something better.\n\nWe reached the lower channel beneath the observatory. The space below was no chamber, only a wound the old architecture had learned to keep open.\n\nSeven bridges crossed an impossible vertical dark. Along each span, old witness sigils glowed in broken sequences: some belonging to Aurelis, some to schools that no longer existed, and some to Circle Zero doctrine so old my body recognized them before my mind caught up.\n\nAt the center hung the moving hall, suspended like a tangled knot of black glass, gold light, green sparks, blue water, red flame, and white Noctis thread.\n\nThrough fractured windows inside, I saw Elira standing before a Yellow Circle student older than the rescued children. Aric Dain had dark hair, hollow cheeks, and the composed posture of someone praised for disappearing. His Yellow crest burned cleanly at his wrist. Lucien stood just behind him without touching him; he did not need to, because Aric had learned to stand where the hand would be.\n\nElira's voice carried down the lower channel.\n\nElira: \"Volunteering under a doctrine that punishes refusal is not consent.\"\n\nAric: \"It is the only consent Noctis recognizes.\"\n\nMira and Finn stood to Elira's left, while Aira and Cassian held the line to her right, Cassian keeping his blistered right palm wrapped in a strip of torn sleeve. Leon's water mirror carried their voices outward until the lower channel aligned enough for us to hear without turning the hall into proof of my intrusion.\n\nLucien looked through the fracture straight at me.\n\nLucien: \"You opened the floor.\"\n\nHaru: \"I did.\"\n\nThe cold air bit my lungs, steadying my pulse.\n\nLucien: \"So much for restraint.\"\n\nHaru: \"Restraint is not paralysis.\"\n\nThe bridges answered, seven old sigils brightening along the stone. Halden leaned over the rail, his voice barely above a whisper.\n\nHalden: \"That phrase is in the record.\"\n\nValen: \"Before the erasure.\"\n\nThe moving hall shuddered. Lucien's eyes narrowed; he had expected rage and planned for rescue as an invasion, never expecting the old system to recognize a distinction he did not believe in.\n\nIven stepped to the edge of the nearest bridge, and Valen caught her breath beside him.\n\nIven: \"Aric!\"\n\nInside the hall, Aric flinched, just enough to prove a person still lived under the posture.\n\nAric: \"You should be safe.\"\n\nIven: \"I am not safe if you are spent.\"\n\nAric's Yellow crest flickered.\n\nLucien: \"First-years are emotionally imprecise.\"\n\nDarian stepped forward to the bridge rail beside me, facing Lucien across the fracture.\n\nDarian: \"You hide behind children and call them imprecise because adults keep leaving you fewer places to stand.\"\n\nLucien looked at Darian as if he had forgotten his fellow Noctis student existed and resented the reminder. Every crack in his poise mattered.\n\nElira stepped closer to Aric. Maelin would have screamed if she had seen how close, and perhaps she did through the upper relay.\n\nElira: \"You can leave with us.\"\n\nAric smiled faintly. I had not seen an expression that sad since Cael's funeral.\n\nAric: \"If I leave, the hall follows the others. It needs a final witness to close.\"\n\nNera's voice cut across the lower channel from the bridge beside us, raw and immediate.\n\nNera: \"No. That is the lie. Final witness means final lock, not final death.\"\n\nAric blinked, staring at her.\n\nNera: \"They changed the lesson. They always change the lesson after making sure only frightened students remember the old words.\"\n\nValen gripped the bridge rail beside me.\n\nValen: \"She's right.\"\n\nHalden: \"How do you close it?\"\n\nValen: \"Not from inside.\"\n\nEvery eye turned toward me, and the Black Circle waited. Lucien smiled again because he saw the trap: if I closed the moving hall, he would claim Black Circle ownership over every witness inside; if I did nothing, he would spend Aric or seal the others in.\n\nElira looked through the fracture at me, asking rather than pleading. I remembered her warning from the morning about practice, and looked away from her toward Iven.\n\nHaru: \"Can you describe the hall's closing lesson?\"\n\nIven wiped his face with his sleeve.\n\nIven: \"We say: a loyal hall closes when the final witness kneels.\"\n\nBeside me on the bridge, Nera shook her head sharply.\n\nNera: \"No. The old form.\"\n\nIven squeezed his eyes shut, forcing the memory past years of drills.\n\nIven: \"A lawful hall closes when the final witness is counted.\"\n\nFinn shouted from inside the hall.\n\nFinn: \"Counted by whom?\"\n\nSilence held for a beat before Mira answered.\n\nMira: \"By everyone who would have used them.\"\n\nThe green sparks inside the hall flared.\n\nAira: \"Mira, that is either brilliant or about to get us all killed.\"\n\nMira: \"Both?\"\n\nCassian: \"Respectable answer.\"\n\nWe built the count across the bridges and through the water mirror. Rhea counted Aric as a protected student. Senna counted him as a Council witness. Nera counted him as not property. Darian counted him as no proof of Noctis honor. Iven counted him as the boy who had taught him how not to cry and now deserved to learn how. Mira counted him as a person with a name, Finn counted the absence around him as filled, Aira counted every coercive phrase used to make him volunteer, Leon and Cassian counted the environmental pressures that made refusal unsafe, and Elira counted his choice as compromised but recoverable.\n\nThen they all looked at me, with Lucien watching and hungry for the Black Circle to claim final authority.\n\nI stepped back from the bridge rail.\n\nHaru: \"I do not count him last.\"\n\nThe hall froze, and Lucien's smile vanished.\n\nHaru: \"He counts himself.\"\n\nAric stared across the fracture as the Yellow crest at his wrist shook.\n\nLucien: \"He has already chosen.\"\n\nElira: \"Then he can choose again.\"\n\nAric's knees buckled. He did not kneel, though he nearly fell. Elira caught him by the sleeve rather than the wrist, giving him just enough support to refuse. Leon pushed his water mirror harder to carry the whisper across the void.\n\nAric: \"I am Aric Dain.\"\n\nThe moving hall cracked from foundation to ceiling.\n\nAric: \"I am not the final witness.\"\n\nLucien moved without casting, his bare hand snapping out fast enough to remind us he was never only doctrine. He reached straight for Aric's crest.\n\nElira struck him across the face with the palm of her hand. The sound cracked across the lower channel, and every witness heard it. Lucien stood motionless, shock breaking across his face for the first time while Elira's hand trembled.\n\nElira: \"Do not touch him.\"\n\nThe moving hall shattered.\n\nShards of black glass tore across the room, one slicing open Elira's palm as she dragged Aric clear of the collapse. Elira, Mira, Finn, Aira, Leon, Cassian, and Aric spilled outward with broken Noctis grammar toward the drop.\n\nI threw the Black Circle out beneath them as a catching net, never closing over them, holding a boundary that caught without claiming. All seven landed across the seven bridges in gasping heaps, alive and unowned.\n\nLucien did not fall. He stood on a suspended shard of black glass above the dark, one hand pressed against his struck cheek, and let an angry smile pull at his mouth.\n\nLucien: \"There she is.\"\n\nThe shard vanished. In the empty space where the moving hall had hung, the lower channel revealed a door marked with a symbol no modern Circle system used: a black ring, split open by a crown.\n\nNo one touched the door.\n\nThe first victory looked like that: exhausted students, broken doctrine, rescued witnesses, and an ancient symbol waiting like a drawn knife while nobody rushed forward. We stood on the seven bridges beneath Aurelis and breathed. Some of us cried, and some pretended not to.\n\nAric Dain sat on the third bridge with a blanket around his shoulders and Elira beside him. Her slap still burned in my memory, not out of helplessness, but refusal. No spell could have been cleaner than a human hand raised to stop a boy from being reclaimed.\n\nTwelve minutes later, the lower-channel lift rattled down to the bridge. Maelin stepped off with three healer aides, arms full of blankets, and Rhea right behind her after leaving Dren at the upper rim. Sela and Pella Orthis hurried off beside them, along with the rest of the rescued Noctis children; Krail's Council clerks had already swarmed the upper court to separate the minors for statements, and Maelin had flatly refused to let bureaucrats near her patients while Sela refused to leave Mira's sight.\n\nMaelin took one look at the cracked black ring and immediately began scolding everyone still breathing, which was how I knew we might survive the hour.\n\nMaelin: \"You opened a floor.\"\n\nHaru: \"I did.\"\n\nI did not look away from her.\n\nMaelin: \"You entered an ancient lower channel with a recently recovered trauma patient, multiple witnesses, and an active doctrine breach.\"\n\nHaru: \"I did not bring the minors.\"\n\nMaelin gave me a flat stare.\n\nHaru: \"That was not a defense.\"\n\nElira let out a small, real laugh, and Maelin pointed straight at her.\n\nMaelin: \"You. Sitting. And give me that cut hand before you bleed on my floor.\"\n\nElira was already sitting.\n\nMaelin: \"More obediently.\"\n\nElira held out her bleeding palm with grave dignity while Maelin wound gauze tightly around it, and even Aric smiled faintly.\n\nThe door waited beyond them, black ring and split crown gleaming faintly in the dark. Valen and Halden studied it from three paces away with the restraint of scholars smelling catastrophe and itching to poke it with ink. Rhea locked her perimeter seal across the central span while Senna's remote crystal recorded from above; Krail had already tried twice to force his way down through legal relay and been denied twice under ongoing rescue conditions.\n\nAira sat on the flagstones sorting damp pages into stacks. Leon brought water flasks around to the rescued students. Cassian warmed the bridges without open flame because several Noctis children flinched at sudden fire. Mira and Finn sat with Sela, who kept a tight grip on Mira's sleeve, while Nera and Darian spoke quietly with Aric, offering water and names before any questions.\n\nThe crown inside the black ring watched all of it.\n\nHalden: \"This predates the Circle Zero Observatory.\"\n\nValen: \"Or sits under it as root doctrine.\"\n\nHalden: \"The crown suggests authority.\"\n\nValen: \"Or the failure of authority.\"\n\nRhea: \"Translate faster.\"\n\nValen brushed her fingers through the air near the symbol without touching the stone. Gold-white script surfaced around the frame, and I felt the vibration behind my sternum before I read it: cold, ozone, and metal.\n\n**REGALIA PROTOCOL**\n\nThe words meant nothing to me, yet my body hated them on sight. Valen went pale, her whisper barely carrying over the stone.\n\nValen: \"No.\"\n\nHalden: \"You know it?\"\n\nValen looked at me as if she had just found my name written in a book that was supposed to have burned.\n\nValen: \"Regalia was a theoretical root of Circle classification. Before colors became hierarchy, before Yellow was named peak, before Black was erased.\"\n\nElira pushed to her feet despite Maelin's warning hand.\n\nElira: \"What did it do?\"\n\nValen: \"It did not rank power.\"\n\nThe door brightened.\n\nValen: \"It assigned authority over crisis response.\"\n\nMy mouth went dry.\n\nHaru: \"Safeguards.\"\n\nValen nodded slowly.\n\nValen: \"And sovereigns.\"\n\nThe word moved through the lower channel like a draft from an unsealed tomb. Neither I nor the Black Circle wanted anything to do with it. Senna's remote seal sharpened overhead.\n\nSenna: \"Define sovereign.\"\n\nValen: \"Most references were destroyed or redacted into myth. The surviving fragments imply that when Circle Zero safeguards failed, certain institutions tried to create a command class above witnesses, a crisis crown authorized to order safeguards during catastrophe.\"\n\nKrail would love that. Lucien would become it if the world let him, or force the world to accuse me of wanting it.\n\nThe door eased open by one inch. Nobody moved. Then a voice came through the crack, young, female, and terrified.\n\nVoice: \"Please tell me someone is there.\"\n\nThe rescued Noctis students froze. Iven stood up.\n\nIven: \"That is Pella.\"\n\nPella Orthis, Reserve Eight, one of the younger children we had pulled from the moving hall, sat beside Maelin under a blanket and stared at the door in horror.\n\nPella: \"That is me.\"\n\nThe voice behind the door sobbed.\n\nVoice: \"I do not want to be copied anymore.\"\n\nSilence dropped over the lower channel. Finn stared at the opening.\n\nFinn: \"Not a door.\"\n\nMira's face drained of color, and Halden swore under his breath.\n\nMira: \"Archive.\"\n\nThe Regalia door opened into stored student impressions: not souls, not living minds, but coercive copies carved out of fear, obedience drills, and conduit pressure. Noctis had not only moved children; it had made rehearsals of them, practice bodies that could be spent in argument or ritual without technically killing anyone.\n\nThe pendant at Elira's throat warmed so fiercely light bled between her fingers.\n\nElira: \"We are opening it.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"Not alone, and not right now. But we are opening it.\"\n\nI stared at the door, the Black Circle sitting completely still inside me.\n\nHaru: \"If Regalia assigns crisis authority, opening it may trigger a claim.\"\n\nRhea: \"Over whom?\"\n\nValen: \"Everyone tied to the old protocol.\"\n\nHalden looked at me, then at Elira, then across at the rescued students.\n\nHalden: \"Potentially all of them.\"\n\nSenna: \"Then we seal the site and petition the full Council.\"\n\nKrail's voice forced its way through a side relay at last.\n\nKrail: \"Agreed. And Haru Aerlen is to be removed from proximity to the site immediately.\"\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nKrail: \"President Valmont, this is beyond student council authority.\"\n\nRhea: \"Then be grateful I am not relying on it.\"\n\nShe turned her seal toward Senna's crystal.\n\nRhea: \"Aurelis requests distributed emergency custody of the Regalia site under witness protection, with no single institution, official, or extra-classification individual granted solitary access.\"\n\nSenna: \"Granted provisionally.\"\n\nKrail: \"You cannot grant custody of a pre-Circle authority protocol to children and compromised faculty.\"\n\nSenna's voice cooled to frost.\n\nSenna: \"I can grant temporary protection of endangered students and evidence during an ongoing Noctis breach. I just did.\"\n\nKrail's relay cut out. Aira leaned toward Rhea, whispering.\n\nAira: \"I am naming my next notebook Senna.\"\n\nLeon: \"Please don't.\"\n\nThe door remained open by one inch. Behind it, the copied Pella wept, while the real Pella shook under her blanket with her face buried in Maelin's sleeve. Maelin stared at the door with a healer's quiet hatred.\n\nMaelin: \"Can we quiet it without entering?\"\n\nValen: \"Maybe.\"\n\nElira unclasped the chain at her throat.\n\nElira: \"Use my pendant.\"\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nShe looked straight at me.\n\nElira: \"Not as bait, as a consent filter. Those copies were made without ongoing consent. The crystal asks before it carries. Let it ask.\"\n\nThe idea was reckless, and completely right.\n\nWe built the temporary quieting array at the center bridge. Elira placed her pendant above Rhea's open council seal while Valen anchored old restoration grammar around it and Halden added observation constraints. Aira drafted the questions in plain language first, muttering that ancient systems kept getting poetic right before people died.\n\nDo you know you are a copy? Do you want to continue speaking, or do you want quiet? Do you want your living source notified? Do you refuse use?\n\nOne by one, the voices behind the door answered. Some knew what they were, and some did not; some asked for quiet, while others wanted to keep speaking until someone wrote their names down. Mira and Finn wrote until their fingers cramped, and Pella asked to hold the transcript of her own copy's answers. No one denied her.\n\nThe lower-channel lift rattled against the bridge again as a healer aide hauled down extra cots, water flasks, and warming wraps. Krail's relay forced itself back into Senna's channel behind the aide. Rhea angled his image away from the rescued children, leaving it open only as a recorded witness line, while Maelin glanced over her shoulder from Pella's side.\n\nMaelin: \"If you issue one order that makes my patients stand longer, I will enter it into the medical record with adjectives.\"\n\nNear the fourth bridge, Aric sat with Iven and Sela, staring at the door like someone recognizing a chapter from a book he had never been allowed to finish. Elira left Maelin's side before Maelin could object, crossed to him, and sat beside him rather than standing over him.\n\nElira: \"You said Noctis has a crown lesson.\"\n\nAric's throat worked.\n\nAric: \"Senior conduit track. They called it command mercy.\"\n\nThe phrase was ugly enough that even Krail's projection lost its appetite for a moment.\n\nAric: \"They taught that witnesses are needed in ordinary crises because ordinary people want comfort. In a final crisis, comfort becomes delay, and delay becomes death. So one authority has to command the safeguard.\"\n\nHaru: \"And the conduit?\"\n\nHe looked at me with an apology on his face for a lesson he had never written.\n\nAric: \"The conduit proves the command was obeyed.\"\n\nNera closed her eyes, and Darian turned away. Cael's absence pressed down on every bridge; Votive Return had not been a separate horror, only practice for a crown.\n\nElira: \"Did Lucien teach it?\"\n\nAric shook his head.\n\nAric: \"No. He corrected it. He said the old lesson was cowardly because it waited for a final crisis. He said the superior student creates the crisis where command becomes obvious.\"\n\nThere he was: Lucien Voss, not merely cruel, but a boy so convinced hierarchy revealed truth that he would manufacture catastrophe just to prove someone deserved to stand above it.\n\nKrail's projection had gone very still, and Senna caught it immediately.\n\nSenna: \"Councilor?\"\n\nKrail: \"Do not mistake my concern about Aerlen for sympathy with Voss.\"\n\nSenna: \"Then stop using arguments his doctrine can open.\"\n\nThe sentence landed hard enough that the relay light bent, and Aira's pen gave a small, triumphant scratch.\n\nThe Regalia door widened another fraction in answer. This time, many voices whispered together, asking questions instead of pleading: Am I used? Am I counted? Am I still a child if I am only a copy of fear? Does my living body know I am here? Can a copy refuse?\n\nThe rescued students heard versions of themselves close enough to cut. Pella began sobbing, Sela pressed both hands over her ears, and Iven's face went blank until Aric laid a hand on his shoulder, then froze.\n\nAric: \"May I?\"\n\nIven nodded. Late consent, still repaired.\n\nMaelin ordered the younger students moved farther back along the bridges, and nobody argued; rescue did not mean forcing children to listen to copies of their own terror. Elira's pendant lay in the center of the quieting array, asking one question at a time. When one voice asked for food, Mira broke down crying while writing the answer, and Finn took over the line until her hand steadied.\n\nThe Black Circle remained quiet through all of it, less like control than grief learning not to interrupt.\n\nAric asked quietly if copies could be guilty.\n\nNera: \"No.\"\n\nDarian: \"Institutions can.\"\n\nThe door did not shut, though the crying softened. Then one final voice came through: male, older, and amused. Not Lucien.\n\nVoice: \"So the Black Circle returns before the crown is claimed. How inconvenient.\"\n\nValen went rigid.\n\nHalden: \"Identify yourself.\"\n\nThe voice laughed.\n\nVoice: \"No. Identification is a door, and I have lived too long by refusing doors.\"\n\nThe black crown symbol brightened.\n\nVoice: \"Tell Haru Aerlen this: Lucien Voss is not the first student to mistake Dominion for sovereignty. He is only the most recent child to find the handle.\"\n\nMy skin went cold.\n\nHaru: \"Who built Regalia?\"\n\nThe voice paused, and when it answered, every bridge light dimmed.\n\nVoice: \"The people who feared what witnesses could do when safeguards listened to them.\"\n\nThe door slammed shut. Across the lower channel, every open ring went dark, and a single word burned above the crown.\n\n**ASCENDANT**\n\nNo public crystal answered yet. Rhea sealed the word inside the local record before anyone could broadcast it.\n\nRhea: \"Local result only. Distributed custody remains. No one enters alone, and no one announces a throne while rescued children are still waiting for beds.\"\n\nSenna: \"Provisionally sustained. Move the patients.\"\n\nThe Regalia door stayed closed under shared guard as we brought the living back up toward the infirmary. Elira came to stand beside me with her cut hand wrapped, the pendant returned to her throat, and her eyes bright with exhaustion and anger.\n\nElira: \"Then tomorrow we refuse it publicly.\"\n\nHaru: \"Tomorrow?\"\n\nShe looked across the bridges, the rescued students, the closed door, and the people who had not run from me even when every ancient symbol in the room wanted to turn my name into a crown.\n\nElira: \"Tonight we take care of the living.\"\n\nShe scared systems more than I did for exactly that reason.\n\nTaking care of the living turned out to be far messier than refusing a crown. It meant mismatched blankets, forty-two healer cots and mattresses commandeered across three departments, student council runners carrying soup through warded corridors, and Cassian learning quickly that terrified children rarely wanted heroic speeches but usually accepted warm bread without questions.\n\nMaelin refused to stack the rescued Noctis students into a single ward.\n\nMaelin: \"Grouping every recovered conduit together is how lazy institutions make one target and call it efficiency.\"\n\nRhea updated the room assignments before anyone argued. The children were placed in adjoining rooms with open doors, visible exits, and witness rotations chosen by comfort rather than rank. Sela asked for Mira. Tovin asked for someone quiet, so Finn volunteered and sat in the corner so still he looked like furniture with a pulse. Pella Orthis asked for Maelin and held onto the healer's sleeve for twenty minutes. Iven asked for Nera near the door, panicked when she first stepped across the sill, then asked her not to leave.\n\nNera stayed right in the doorway until Iven spoke.\n\nIven: \"Inside is okay.\"\n\nHe still refused to release his academy scarf. Tunnel dust had turned it gray, one end was half-frozen, and the knot was tangled badly enough that Cassian regarded it with professional respect. When Maelin lifted a clean warming wrap, Iven flinched back, and she stopped at once.\n\nMaelin: \"Keep the scarf. May I put the wrap over it?\"\n\nIven studied her face, the open doorway, and Nera, his fingers clamped around the ruined knot, before finally nodding. Maelin settled the wrap over his shoulders without brushing the scarf. Across the room, Leon looked away, then set his jaw and forced himself to look back.\n\nElira paused in the doorway with frost drying along the hem of her coat.\n\nElira: \"At Noctis, does a new yes erase the old one?\"\n\nIven: \"Usually.\"\n\nElira: \"Here it can sit beside it.\"\n\nIven pinched one fold of the clean wrap between two fingers while his other hand kept hold of the scarf. Then Elira stepped quietly inside, and Nera took the chair closest to the doorway, careful never to sit between Iven and the exit.\n\nThe night moved like that: doors, questions, and small permissions that never made heroic summaries and mattered all the more for it.\n\nI stayed in the central corridor under Halden's observation because Regalia had written **ASCENDANT** beside my name and nobody with sense wanted me left alone with that word. Dren guarded the far end of the hall. Rhea worked at a table by the nurse station while Senna's remote crystal hovered dimmed above the files. Aira sat on the floor with her back against the wall, sorting notes into four neat stacks:\n\n**Living Students**\n\n**Copied Impressions**\n\n**Regalia Door**\n\n**Lucien Being The Worst**\n\nRhea spotted the last label.\n\nRhea: \"Rename it.\"\n\nAira: \"It is accurate.\"\n\nRhea: \"Rename it accurately in court language.\"\n\nAira sighed and crossed it out. The new label read:\n\n**Lucien Voss: Hostile Doctrine Author / Active Cowardice**\n\nRhea stared at it.\n\nAira: \"Compromise.\"\n\nRhea let it stand, which said enough about the night.\n\nElira kept checking room after room until Maelin caught her by the shoulder and turned her around like an uncooperative compass.\n\nMaelin: \"Your room.\"\n\nElira: \"I am not...\"\n\nMaelin: \"Your room.\"\n\nElira glanced toward me, and I stayed put.\n\nHaru: \"She's right.\"\n\nElira's eyes narrowed.\n\nElira: \"I dislike when you practice against me.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Good. You can dislike it horizontally.\"\n\nElira was escorted to a side room with a couch, a cot, and window wards checked by three separate witnesses. I followed only after she asked, and after Halden noted the move with such solemnity that Elira threatened to make him log the emotional texture of his own discomfort. He looked briefly tempted, then stayed in the hall.\n\nInside the side room, the infirmary clamor softened to human scale: a child asking for water, Leon arguing quietly with a heater charm, Mira telling Sela that freckles were not a symptom, Cassian being banned from cooking unsupervised, and Aira sneezing ink dust.\n\nElira sank onto the couch and finally let the exhaustion reach her face. I stayed by the door until she looked up at me.\n\nElira: \"If you stand there like a punishment, I will throw a pillow.\"\n\nThere were no pillows within reach, so I sat beside her anyway. She leaned into me slowly, testing her bruised shoulder, her bandaged hand, and the weight of the day.\n\nHaru: \"Too much?\"\n\nElira: \"Everything is too much.\"\n\nMy hands stopped moving, and she caught my sleeve.\n\nElira: \"That was not a no.\"\n\nI let my shoulder relax, and she rested her head against it.\n\nElira: \"When I said we take care of the living, I meant you too.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am being observed.\"\n\nElira: \"That is not the same as being cared for.\"\n\nOutside the door, Halden cleared his throat once. Elira raised her voice without lifting her head.\n\nElira: \"That was not criticism, Professor.\"\n\nHalden's voice came from the hallway.\n\nHalden: \"Accurate, though.\"\n\nI almost smiled, and Elira did, before the smile faded.\n\nElira: \"You heard the copied voices.\"\n\nHaru: \"Every word.\"\n\nThe cold pulse behind my ribs stayed quiet.\n\nElira: \"Did any of them feel like Cael?\"\n\nThe question found the exact bruise it meant to test. I closed my eyes.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nHer fingers tightened on mine.\n\nHaru: \"I kept listening for him. Which makes no sense. Regalia pulled copied fear from living students. Cael is dead.\"\n\nThe word still refused to fit in my mouth, and Elira did not try to rescue me from saying it.\n\nHaru: \"But I listened anyway.\"\n\nShe leaned closer.\n\nElira: \"So did I.\"\n\nFor a long while, neither of us spoke. The infirmary breathed around us: footsteps, water cups, soft crying, and people who had not been saved completely yet, only kept out of reach of the hands trying to take them back.\n\nAt midnight, Aira knocked on the doorframe, her eyes red though she pretended they were not.\n\nAira: \"We have rotations through dawn. Rhea is threatening paperwork at anyone who calls them guard shifts.\"\n\nElira: \"What are they called?\"\n\nAira checked her clipboard.\n\nAira: \"Presence intervals.\"\n\nHaru: \"That sounds like Rhea.\"\n\nAira: \"It sounds like Rhea lost an argument with Maelin and named the bruise.\"\n\nElira's quiet laugh sounded almost painful. Aira glanced at both of us, then down at our joined hands, and for once skipped the joke.\n\nAira: \"Good.\"\n\nThen she left, letting the word stay behind her.\n\nWhen Elira finally slept, I stopped counting her breaths and listened to the hallway instead: footsteps, water cups, Maelin's low murmur, Rhea's pen scratching paper, Nera asking quietly if she could sit by the doorway, and Iven letting her. Under Aurelis, Regalia waited, while up here no throne formed, only chairs pulled beside beds.\n\nThat should have been too small for an ancient protocol to register, yet the floor beneath us seemed to listen.\n\nJust after midnight, when the presence intervals had settled into something close to rest, every chair in the east infirmary turned one inch toward the floor. Nobody touched them. Wood scraped stone across a dozen rooms at once.\n\nThe rescued students woke faster than the adults; Noctis training recognized shifting rooms as danger long before ordinary sleep caught up. Sela sat upright reaching for Mira's sleeve, caught herself halfway, and waited until Mira woke too.\n\nMira: \"You can.\"\n\nSela took the sleeve lightly, enough to ground herself. Across the hall, Iven's thermal charm flared amber against his chest. Nera, dozing in the far chair near the doorway because Tovin had not invited her closer, opened her eyes and lifted both hands where everyone could see them.\n\nNera: \"No binding.\"\n\nTovin's breathing slowed by a fraction. Maelin marched out of the supply alcove with her hair half-unpinned and murder in her eyes.\n\nMaelin: \"If this is another Council inquiry, I am resigning from politeness.\"\n\nThe floor answered in arrangement rather than words. Chairs pulled away from the beds and slid into a circle down the corridor: plain chairs, patient chairs, companion chairs. The repaired wooden chair from Cael's recovery room dragged itself last, leaving a thin track in the corridor dust.\n\nElira stood before I could worry over whether she should, and I rose beside her without trying to stop her.\n\nHaru: \"Do you want my arm?\"\n\nElira: \"Please.\"\n\nShe took my arm, leaning her weight against me for a heartbeat until her balance held, and the chairs stopped moving.\n\nAira appeared in a doorway with a blanket around her shoulders and a pencil tucked behind one ear.\n\nAira: \"Everyone please note that asking before support appears to be structurally significant and also personally irritating to ancient furniture.\"\n\nRhea arrived three seconds later, fully dressed as though she slept like a legal objection waiting to happen.\n\nRhea: \"Status.\"\n\nSenna's remote crystal flickered above the hall.\n\nSenna: \"Regalia resonance through care furniture. No breach yet.\"\n\nValen hurried through the archive-side door with crooked glasses and a pale face.\n\nValen: \"It is not furniture to the protocol. It is adjacency architecture.\"\n\nLeon blinked from the doorway.\n\nLeon: \"It is chairs.\"\n\nValen: \"Yes, and that is the point.\"\n\nThe floor pulse deepened, throwing the chairs' shadows upward. Above them, light gathered into the outline of a throne and failed three times in a row. Each time, one chair refused to align by a few degrees: the repaired companion chair would not twist into an armrest, Sela's bedside chair would not rise above the rest, Iven's far chair stayed apart because Tovin had chosen that distance, and Elira's bench refused to pull free of the wall while she leaned on me.\n\nRegalia had no idea what to do with consent distances, and it hated them.\n\nThe air above the chairs split open into copied impressions: Sela's fear, Tovin's cold, Iven's apology, Mira's trembling courage, Nera's guilt, Darian's defection, and Elira's death-breath stolen from a memory neither of us had consented to share.\n\nMy Black Circle surged so violently the corridor lamps went black. Elira's fingers dug into my arm.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI stopped, barely holding the edge back. The copied breath vanished before it could solidify into an image.\n\nThe pendant at Elira's throat burned black-gold, acting as a filter instead of a wall. Rather than blocking every memory, the crystal asked the question Dominion Severance had tried to erase: who consented to this being used?\n\nThe copied voices faltered. Awake and shaking, Sela whispered into the dark.\n\nSela: \"Mine can stay if it helps show what they did.\"\n\nTovin looked up from his knees.\n\nTovin: \"Not mine.\"\n\nHis fear dissolved on the air. Iven clutched Cael's charm in both hands.\n\nIven: \"Mine can say I was scared. Not that I owed pain.\"\n\nThe impression shifted from apology to bare fear, terrible and clean and useless as debt. Nera stared at the filtering light as if Elira had opened a door in a language she thought had been lost.\n\nNera: \"Consent filter.\"\n\nElira's breath shook.\n\nElira: \"Trying.\"\n\nMaelin slid a chair behind her, and Elira sat without arguing. Aira scribbled that down too, possibly for medical history, possibly for future leverage.\n\nThe half-formed throne thinned, so Regalia tried another route. The remaining impressions gathered into a faceless child-shaped figure standing inside the circle of chairs, speaking with dozens of overlapping voices.\n\nRegalia: \"AUTHORITY REQUIRED TO PREVENT RECURRENCE.\"\n\nThe words vibrated inside the bones of the corridor. Rhea stepped forward.\n\nRhea: \"Define authority.\"\n\nRegalia: \"COMMAND CAPACITY. SUCCESSION CAPACITY. FINAL OVERRIDE.\"\n\nKrail's relay projection flared to life at the far end of the corridor, bright, official, and disastrously eager.\n\nKrail: \"Magistra Voss, President Valmont, do not engage. This may be an opportunity to define the Black Circle's governance function under Council supervision.\"\n\nAira stared at the glowing image.\n\nAira: \"You heard final override and thought opportunity.\"\n\nKrail: \"Miss Solen...\"\n\nSenna's crystal sliced across his transmission.\n\nSenna: \"Councilor, you are intruding on an active resonance event in a medical ward.\"\n\nKrail refused to back down.\n\nKrail: \"Regalia Protocol may be the first coherent authority structure capable of binding extra-classification risk.\"\n\nEvery chair in the corridor turned toward him. For one dizzying second, I thought the protocol agreed. Then the repaired companion chair scraped backward, away from Krail's projection, and Sela's, Iven's, Tovin's, and Mira's chairs followed it. They were not fleeing; they were refusing orientation.\n\nValen watched the chairs lock into place.\n\nValen: \"It is comparing authority models.\"\n\nRegalia spoke again.\n\nRegalia: \"COUNCIL COMMAND PROPOSED.\"\n\nThe corridor filled with ghostly images of seals, record rails, emergency motions, containment orders, and proximity reviews, words polished until cruelty could pass through them dry. Krail's jaw tightened as he recognized himself in the mirror the old system held up.\n\nRegalia: \"NOCTIS COMMAND PROPOSED.\"\n\nThe light went bitter cold, flashing rows, bells, winter fields, binding rings, and children kneeling because warmth had been rationed. Nera made a choked sound, and Darian stepped close enough for his sleeve to brush hers, waiting until she leaned into the contact.\n\nRegalia: \"BLACK CIRCLE COMMAND PROPOSED.\"\n\nEvery eye turned to me, and the corridor vanished.\n\nFor a heartbeat, I stood alone inside a throne room built of eclipse light, with no walls, no witnesses, no Council, no Noctis, and nobody close enough to be hurt if I decided everything by myself. The temptation waiting there was not power, only simplicity: an end to arguments, an end to asking, an end to watching Elira bleed because other people kept building rooms that reached for her.\n\nMy hands shook as the throne waited. The worst part was how quiet and merciful it looked.\n\nElira's voice cut across the darkness.\n\nElira: \"Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nA name, not a myth. The throne flickered. Aira's voice followed right behind hers.\n\nAira: \"If you sit down, I am throwing every notebook I own at your head.\"\n\nRhea: \"For the record, that is not an officially sanctioned deterrent.\"\n\nCassian: \"Morally strong, though.\"\n\nLeon: \"Focus.\"\n\nThe throne cracked along its edges because their voices were ordinary, badly timed, and stubbornly human. I turned my back on it.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nRegalia: \"REFUSAL WITHOUT SUCCESSOR CREATES INSTABILITY.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then be unstable.\"\n\nThe throne room shattered. I stumbled back into the infirmary corridor, breathing hard with Elira's hand gripping my arm and every witness crowded around me.\n\nRegalia's faceless figure tilted its head.\n\nRegalia: \"SUCCESSOR REQUIRED.\"\n\nThe corridor did what frightened rooms always did first: everyone turned toward Rhea, toward Krail's relay, and toward me, pulled by the old lie that when a room is scared enough, someone large enough should answer for everyone else. The Black Circle stirred under my skin, and Elira's hand tightened on my sleeve, reminding me I was not the only answer in the hall.\n\nRhea raised her seal, yet before she could speak, Sela stood up. Her infirmary blanket slipped from one shoulder, and when Mira reached to pull it back up, Sela shook her head. She stared straight at the Regalia figure, her voice small enough that the corridor fell silent to catch it.\n\nSela: \"No.\"\n\nRegalia gave no answer. Sela swallowed, then kept speaking.\n\nSela: \"At Noctis, they ask who is strongest when they already know who they want us to obey.\"\n\nHer eyes widened, not from fear, but because the sentence had left her mouth and the ceiling had not collapsed.\n\nSela: \"If you need a successor, you are asking the wrong question.\"\n\nRegalia brightened.\n\nRegalia: \"AUTHORITY REQUIRED TO PREVENT RECURRENCE.\"\n\nSela flinched, and Mira stepped up shoulder-to-shoulder beside her. Sela held her ground.\n\nSela: \"Authority did not prevent us. It arranged us.\"\n\nThe sentence cut through the corridor like a dropped blade.\n\nAric, who had been resting under Maelin's orders in the second ward, appeared at the doorway barefoot with a blanket wrapped around him and his Yellow crest flickering weakly at his wrist.\n\nAric: \"No.\"\n\nHis voice was hoarse and scared, yet it mattered because he had once believed someone had to close the door by becoming final.\n\nAric: \"Do not ask me.\"\n\nRegalia: \"FINAL WITNESS CANDIDATE.\"\n\nAric stepped back, and Iven rose slowly beside him, his healer wrap showing beneath his sleeve.\n\nIven: \"Aric taught us how not to cry when instructors watched. That was not authority. That was survival with witnesses missing.\"\n\nAric pressed a hand over his mouth, holding himself together, while Maelin's eyes shone fiercely from the healer line. Regalia pulsed again.\n\nRegalia: \"SUCCESSION PRESSURE CONTINUES UNTIL STRUCTURE STABLE.\"\n\nMira lifted her notebook, her grip tight on the binding.\n\nMira: \"Public witness correction. The children harmed by command structure reject command structure as a cure.\"\n\nAira's pencil scratched across her page without adding a joke. Rhea waited until Mira's line had been copied three times before speaking.\n\nRhea: \"Emergency witness authority recognizes primary affected testimony.\"\n\nSenna's remote crystal flared.\n\nSenna: \"Council record accepts affected testimony as a threshold objection.\"\n\nElira stood despite Maelin's sharp hiss.\n\nElira: \"Then the structure is wrong.\"\n\nHer gold light flared, clean and sharp enough to sever copied doctrine from living children. The pendant filter expanded through the corridor, asking every lingering impression the same question: do you consent to becoming succession evidence?\n\nSela: \"No.\"\n\nTovin: \"No.\"\n\nIven: \"No.\"\n\nMira stepped beside Sela anyway.\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nNera: \"No.\"\n\nDarian: \"No.\"\n\nAira: \"Absolutely not.\"\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nCassian: \"No, with fire later if required.\"\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nSenna: \"No.\"\n\nValen: \"No.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No, and sit down.\"\n\nThat last one was aimed at Elira, who sat with a faint smile.\n\nRegalia failed to raise the throne again, not because I refused it, but because the people it claimed to protect spoke before anyone could protect them into silence.\n\nThe broadcast began anyway, pouring out from every relay crystal in Aurelis, from the courtyard fountain, from dormant tournament mirrors, and from Council notice panes that never asked permission once an old system woke. At first it showed no letters, only an empty throne against an eclipse and a thousand chairs refusing to form steps.\n\nThen the words surfaced in abstract glyphs every Circle could read without translation:\n\n**THE BLACK CIRCLE HAS A THRONE.**\n\nThe whole academy woke. Noctis woke. The Council woke. Standing in the east infirmary among children who had just said no, I understood that refusal had not ended Regalia. It had only made Regalia announce the question to the world.\n\nWhen the fountain light died, it left something solid in the basin: a fingernail-sized shard of black glass split by a single vein of gold. Halden reached for it, checked himself, and waited for Rhea to raise her seal before Valen lifted it under old light. Three lines moved along the gold vein:\n\n**REFUSAL RECORDED.**\n\n**SUCCESSION UNRESOLVED.**\n\n**ASCENDANT CANDIDATE: UNSEATED.**\n\nHaru: \"I am not a candidate.\"\n\nThe shard ignored me with ancient professionalism.\n\nAira: \"I dislike objects with committee instincts.\"\n\nRhea sealed it inside a warded case under distributed campus custody while Senna logged where Aurelis authority ended, where Council observation began, and that neither boundary granted anyone solitary access. As the case clicked shut, the fountain caught the reflections of Aurelis's four towers, each fracturing around the shard. Between the west and north reflections, a fifth patch of darkness returned no sky at all. Valen saw it, and said nothing.\n\nThe announcement did not fade when the glyphs cleared. Worse, it settled. A loud proclamation could be refuted, censured, or argued into categories, but a settled idea changed people's posture. It changed how students glanced at me when they thought I wasn't looking, how faculty hovered near exits, and how Council clerks held their pens.\n\nThe Black Circle has a throne. The sentence wanted to feel inevitable.\n\nBy second bell, three versions of it were already circulating through the courtyard: the frightened version, claiming someone had to sit before the power chose badly; the ambitious version, insisting the Council must control access before Noctis could; and the reverent version, murmuring that Haru Aerlen had simply been denying the shape of his destiny.\n\nI hated the reverent version most. Fear at least admitted it was afraid, while reverence tried to paint refusal as modesty waiting to be corrected.\n\nElira found me in the east corridor after Maelin finally downgraded her from direct observation to indirect harassment. She walked slowly without apology, the dark crystal pendant resting openly outside her uniform against the pale gold of her tie.\n\nHaru: \"You are showing it.\"\n\nElira: \"I am.\"\n\nThere was no apology in how she held my eyes.\n\nHaru: \"Because of Regalia?\"\n\nElira: \"Because people keep staring at where they think it is. I prefer honest staring.\"\n\nVery Elira. It made me want to kiss her, and made me remember Lucien turning private memory into a weapon. The want and the fear collided, and she must have read it on my face because she stopped right in front of me.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am here.\"\n\nElira: \"Are you?\"\n\nHer voice was soft enough to reach the part of me still standing inside that quiet throne room after I said no. I looked out toward the infirmary windows.\n\nHaru: \"Part of me liked the quiet.\"\n\nShe did not flinch, so I kept going.\n\nHaru: \"No arguments. No children waiting for us to be careful enough. No Council turning care into procedure. No Lucien using every wound as proof. Just a place where I could decide and the world would stop making anyone else pay for my restraint.\"\n\nElira listened without trying to spare me the ugliness of it.\n\nHaru: \"That is why I cannot sit there.\"\n\nElira: \"Because it tempted you?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because it tempted me by sounding merciful.\"\n\nHer hand rose toward my face, stopping an inch short.\n\nElira: \"May I?\"\n\nHaru: \"Always.\"\n\nI kept my hands open at my sides as her palm rested against my cheek, warm and alive and chosen.\n\nElira: \"Then we name that too. The throne is dangerous not because you secretly want domination. It is dangerous because it offers relief from being answerable.\"\n\nHer words settled cleanly into my chest.\n\nHaru: \"You make terrifying things precise.\"\n\nElira: \"You fall in love with historians and then act surprised.\"\n\nHaru: \"You are not a historian.\"\n\nElira: \"No, but Professor Valen is contagious.\"\n\nI let out a quiet laugh, and for a second it was enough.\n\nDown the corridor, Aira hurried toward us with three notebooks tucked under her chin.\n\nAira: \"I hate to interrupt what looks like emotionally responsible flirting, but Rhea needs everyone. Krail filed a minority motion.\"\n\nElira did not pull her hand back immediately, letting the order stand.\n\nElira: \"What kind?\"\n\nAira's mouth flattened.\n\nAira: \"Provisional succession custody.\"\n\nThe air in the corridor chilled.\n\nHaru: \"Custody of what?\"\n\nAira: \"That is the problem. The motion is phrased like custody of the Regalia question, when the real effect would be custody of who gets to answer it.\"\n\nElira lowered her hand, her eyes hardening.\n\nElira: \"He wants to choose the room.\"\n\nAira: \"That is the whole trick of it.\"\n\nShe uncapped her pen and poised it over a fresh sheet. I looked down the corridor at the mismatched infirmary chairs.\n\nHaru: \"Then we choose it first.\"\n\nAira's expression sharpened into pure calculation.\n\nAira: \"Courtyard?\"\n\nElira: \"Courtyard.\"\n\nNo enclosed chamber, no polished wall for Lucien, no rigged seating count, and no private custody route. If Regalia wanted a throne, if Krail wanted a trial, and if the world wanted a spectacle, we would hold it in a space too wide for anyone to center one person without the whole academy watching them try.\n\nWord moved through Aurelis before the ink on Krail's motion finished drying. Rhea sent notices, Mira gathered Green witnesses, Leon mapped water lines, Cassian checked heat routes, Nera and Darian prepared Noctis phrase objections, and Maelin started organizing breakfast trays first because she understood governance better than most governments.\n\nElira walked beside me out into the waking academy with the crystal visible at her throat and her hand in mine, showing every staring student that intimacy did not become shame just because a throne wanted evidence.\n\nAhead, benches scraped across the courtyard flagstones. Someone had already removed the dais before Krail could claim it.\n\nNo one yet knew where to put me. Good.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-30-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep31","label":"Episode 25","number":"25","file":"episode-31-cover.webp","fallback":"The Room Where She Woke Twice","eyebrow":"Recovery","recap":"","accent":["#1f2937","#f8fafc","#93c5fd"],"displayLabel":"Episode 25","displayNumber":"25","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"No Throne for the Black Circle","body":"Refusing Regalia's throne did not bring peace. It brought administrative chaos before anyone found breakfast.\n\nPetitions flooded Aurelis: alliances, removal demands, devotional clubs, dueling challenges, and one fruit basket labeled **NO SUCCESSOR. ALSO PLEASE EAT.** Maelin tested an apple by biting it. Rhea confiscated the basket and distributed the fruit anyway.\n\nBefore the Council could convene in the courtyard where students had cleared the dais the night before, Maelin blocked Krail's demand for immediate rescued-student testimony. Elira set the only opening rule: nobody asks for content until each child names what makes answering possible.\n\nSela asked for Mira in sight and no bells.\n\nIven asked for Nera near the door, never between him and the exit.\n\nAric forbade the word noble.\n\nAira wrote the heading once:\n\n**CONDITIONS BEFORE CONTENT.**\n\nThe sealed Regalia shard brightened inside its case, though no one mistook that for permission.\n\nNoctis answered the phrase before the Council did, far from official channels.\n\nPublicly, Noctis Dominion College remained sealed under educational emergency doctrine, refusing audit while claiming Black Circle contamination. Its notice crystals displayed a single line:\n\n**INTERNAL STABILIZATION IN PROGRESS**\n\nUnofficially, three student whisper channels flickered to life before sunset. Benn Arlow, the Green first-year who had burned his palm protecting the outer copy record, carried a cracked relay crystal into the infirmary corridor with both hands and set it carefully into Aira's palms.\n\nThe first channel carried only breathing.\n\nThe second carried a Noctis bell and cut out before anyone spoke.\n\nA girl's voice bled through the third, thin as frost.\n\nGirl's Voice: \"Conditions before content.\"\n\nHalf a dozen other voices repeated the words after her, hurried and hushed, before static swallowed the line.\n\nNera heard it from the corridor outside Iven's room and nearly dropped the water cup in her hand.\n\nI was in the hall only because Elira had fallen asleep in the observation room and Maelin had ordered me to make myself useful somewhere that did not involve staring at a sleeping patient like a tragic window ornament. Maelin's phrasing, and unfortunately accurate.\n\nNera stood frozen, cup trembling, eyes locked on the cracked relay crystal in Aira's hand.\n\nAira replayed the third channel once. Only once. The girl's whisper returned, then the chorus, then static.\n\nIven woke at the recorded bell and bolted upright so fast his blanket slipped. Nera turned at once.\n\nNera: \"Iven, you are here.\"\n\nHe stared at the crystal.\n\nIven: \"That was Lysa.\"\n\nRhea arrived down the corridor at a near-run and stopped beside us.\n\nRhea: \"Full name?\"\n\nIven pressed both hands over his knees.\n\nIven: \"Lysa Pell. Blue Circle. First-year conduit track. She hides sweets in her boot because the inspection cabinets check pillows first.\"\n\nAira wrote down every word, boot sweets included. Beside Nera, Darian said nothing; he had learned when silence stopped feeling like abandonment. Iven looked up at me, fear crossing his face before something steadier replaced it.\n\nIven: \"Can the Black Circle hear them?\"\n\nThe corridor went quiet. There it was again: could I reach through sealed Noctis walls, listen for children hidden behind doctrine, and become the route every frightened person hoped for and every frightened institution dreaded?\n\nThe Black Circle stirred behind my ribs, recognizing the old shape of that demand. *Haru hears. Haru reaches. Haru saves them alone.*\n\nI looked at Iven and gave him the honest answer.\n\nHaru: \"Maybe.\"\n\nAira's pen stopped. Rhea's eyes sharpened, and Iven swallowed.\n\nHaru: \"But if I try alone, Lucien will use the attempt as proof that every Noctis student signal now passes through me. Krail would use it too.\"\n\nThe boy's face fell. I hated seeing it, and kept going anyway.\n\nHaru: \"So we do not start with what I can hear. We start with what Lysa chose to send.\"\n\nAira resumed writing. Rhea nodded once.\n\nHaru: \"She sent conditions before content. That means the phrase reached Noctis students, at least one channel can open outward, and the first thing she wanted preserved was not a location or a plea. It was a rule for how she wants to be heard.\"\n\nIven looked from me to Aira's notes, then to Nera.\n\nIven: \"So we write her condition.\"\n\nAira's voice softened.\n\nAira: \"First line on the page.\"\n\nHer pen hovered above the margin.\n\nIven: \"Before we ask where she is.\"\n\nRhea: \"Agreed.\"\n\nRhea set her emergency seal flat on the corridor cart while Nera closed her eyes.\n\nNera: \"Lysa Pell. Blue Circle. First-year conduit track. Known survival detail: hides sweets in boot. First transmitted condition: conditions before content.\"\n\nAira wrote it word for word. The cracked crystal warmed against her palm, responsive without flaring. Somewhere inside Noctis, a girl had pushed a rule through a hairline crack in the wall, and Aurelis had answered by refusing to turn her into a map.\n\nThat night, the phrase spread again through every place students knew how to use when adults assumed communication lived inside approved mirrors: lecture slate margins, dormitory steam, chalk dust on glass, water left in cups, thermal charms, the underside of bread wrappers.\n\n*Conditions before content. No successor. We are not reserves.*\n\nBy midnight, Senna sent a sealed dispatch:\n\n**Noctis has filed a protest alleging Aurelis is conducting psychological warfare through student slogans.**\n\nAira read it twice.\n\nAira: \"We are being accused of weaponized consent.\"\n\nRhea: \"Do not phrase it that way in the hearing.\"\n\nAira: \"I would never.\"\n\nEveryone looked at her.\n\nAira: \"I would consider never.\"\n\nAwake again despite Maelin's best efforts, Elira sat wrapped in an extra blanket by the observation window.\n\nElira: \"It is not a slogan.\"\n\nHer voice was quiet, and the room adjusted around it.\n\nElira: \"It is a door that opens from the inside.\"\n\nThe sealed Regalia shard brightened once inside its case, then dimmed. Whether the old system had learned anything or not, the morning session would not begin with Haru Aerlen standing alone on a dais.\n\nBefore dawn, Rhea formally moved the full Council hearing out into the unfinished Grand Courtyard. Students had already hauled away the dais the day before; now they dragged benches across wet stone while Green Circle witnesses argued sightlines with Red heirs and Yellow seniors hovered until Elira pointed them toward the blanket crates.\n\nKrail's clerks found no podium. One asked where Haru Aerlen was meant to stand.\n\nRhea: \"Where he can hear and be interrupted.\"\n\nSenna entered the rule into the record before anyone could polish it away.\n\n**Extra-classification witness position: interruptible range; non-central unless speaking.**\n\nKrail objected by relay while the sky was still gray.\n\nKrail: \"You have replaced Council procedure with student improvisation.\"\n\nSenna's projection appeared beside Rhea, precise and visibly exhausted.\n\nSenna: \"I relocated the session under emergency witness-distribution authority. Your objection is recorded. It does not restore the dais.\"\n\nAira leaned toward Rhea.\n\nAira: \"She says beautiful things when angry.\"\n\nRhea: \"Do not write that.\"\n\nAira's pen kept moving.\n\nKrail: \"The courtyard exposes minors to public pressure.\"\n\nMaelin set down a tray of steaming cups with enough force to answer for the entire medical profession.\n\nMaelin: \"No child speaks standing, hungry, cold, or because you found a cleaner word for coercion.\"\n\n## Elira - Conditions of Warmth\n\nElira sat on the fountain rim with a wool blanket around her shoulders. Her color was poor; her stare was steady.\n\nElira: \"The first testimony is conditions.\"\n\nKrail: \"The Council requires content.\"\n\nElira stood. Beside her, Haru started to rise as well, and she lifted two fingers near his sleeve to check him. Wait. She gave her own balance a beat to settle before speaking.\n\nElira: \"Yesterday, adults asked frightened children for maps before blankets. I will not help make that sound civilized. If content is all you can hear, prove you are safer than Noctis before you ask for it.\"\n\nKrail: \"The Council cannot act on silence.\"\n\nElira: \"Then listen to what the silence is protecting.\"\n\nMira raised a hand from the Green benches. Beside her, Finn opened a fresh notebook.\n\nMira: \"Can I witness someone's conditions without asking for their story?\"\n\nSenna: \"You may.\"\n\nSenna marked the margin with an ink flourish. Mira turned to Sela, who sat beside a healer with a blanket tucked over her knees.\n\nMira: \"Do you want me to?\"\n\nSela watched Krail's relay, then the notebooks, then Mira.\n\nSela: \"Only that.\"\n\nFinn wrote it down without adding a syllable.\n\nFinn: \"Condition witness accepted. No content requested.\"\n\nThe sealed Regalia shard flashed from its custody case near the fountain.\n\nRegalia: \"CONTENT ABSENT. SUCCESSION UNRESOLVED.\"\n\nKrail: \"This is delay presented as ethics.\"\n\nRhea: \"It is testimony you cannot isolate.\"\n\nEvery Noctis-facing relay crystal along the courtyard perimeter flared at once.\n\nNo official message arrived. Instead, frost crept across the inside of one of Maelin's water cups: three dots, a smear, two dots.\n\nIven nearly dropped Cael's charm.\n\nIven: \"Boot sweets.\"\n\nAira: \"What?\"\n\nIven: \"Lysa Pell hides sweets in her boot. Nera remembered. Lysa heard us.\"\n\nNera crouched over the cup. Her hand shook too badly to point, so Darian slid the tray closer instead.\n\nNera: \"Condition first.\"\n\nThe frost shifted under her breath. *Cold. Watched. Two others nearby. Bell after second meal. Cannot say names. Do not ask content through this route.*\n\nKrail stepped up to his relay glass.\n\nKrail: \"Trace the channel. Now.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe word cut straight across the courtyard before fear could dress itself as strategy.\n\nKrail: \"You would discard a live route?\"\n\nHaru: \"I would not turn Lysa into one.\"\n\nKrail: \"You may lose her.\"\n\nElira watched the cost cross Haru's face, clean and sharp, as every version of the world where Krail was right hit him at once. She shifted half a step closer until her shoulder pressed lightly against his arm, steady enough that he would know she was there without mistaking touch for interference.\n\nHaru: \"Then we build conditions fast enough to deserve another message.\"\n\nRhea: \"Condition response only. No trace.\"\n\nSenna: \"Authorized.\"\n\nKrail: \"You are letting a minor dictate Council procedure through a compromised channel.\"\n\nMaelin: \"We are letting a cold child say she is cold before asking her to be useful.\"\n\nKrail fell silent. Temporary, Elira knew, but welcome.\n\nAira crouched beside the cup, and Elira lowered herself next to her, ignoring Maelin's warning glare.\n\nElira: \"Lysa Pell. Condition received. No content requested. Warmth is coming without trace. Stop means stop. If watched, do not answer.\"\n\nIven's voice cracked on the first syllable.\n\nIven: \"Boot sweets remembered.\"\n\nNera: \"Thermal charm follows. Not debt.\"\n\nThe frost held for one heartbeat, then melted into the water, warming the cup by a single degree. Across the courtyard, shoulders dropped as everyone exhaled at once.\n\nRegalia: \"DISTRIBUTED RESPONSE DETECTED. SUCCESSION UNRESOLVED.\"\n\nHaru: \"Let it remain unresolved.\"\n\nKrail: \"You would leave authority vacant while Noctis holds children?\"\n\nRhea: \"We are exercising authority now. You dislike that it can be interrupted.\"\n\nSenna's mouth curved into something close to a smile.\n\nSenna: \"Emergency response proceeds without successor classification. Ruling entered.\"\n\nThe hearing broke open into work. Blue students began warming sealed water; Green witnesses copied Lysa's conditions without adding speculation; Cassian and Leon argued over thermal range while solving it; Mira stayed beside Sela and asked for nothing else.\n\nAs the crowd shifted around the fountain, Elira reached for Haru's hand. Her thumb stopped just beneath his ring fingernail, where dried blood from the lower channel sat almost black against the quick.\n\nElira: \"This was not there yesterday.\"\n\nHaru: \"It is small.\"\n\nHer fingers closed gently around his wrist, refusing to let him brush past it.\n\nElira: \"That was not an answer.\"\n\nMaelin overheard. Of course she did.\n\nMaelin: \"Front room. Both of you. Rhea can govern without fresh patients decorating the fountain.\"\n\nHaru: \"I should stay.\"\n\nRhea, already fielding three reports at once, did not look up.\n\nRhea: \"Denied.\"\n\nElira kept hold of Haru's injured hand as they left the courtyard for the cottage front room, where Maelin cleaned the ruptured nail bed, wrapped his ring finger beneath the black-and-silver ribbon, and ordered Elira off her feet before hurrying back to Rhea. Behind them in the courtyard, Aira opened a fresh page:\n\n**Lysa Pell. Condition witness. Content pending by choice.**\n\nThe fountain rippled beneath Regalia's sealed glass, surrounded by cold, tired people doing the next practical thing before fear could name it for them.\n\n---\n\n## Lucien Voss - The Missing Handle\n\nLucien read the Regalia refusal transcript in a room designed to make retreat look like consultation.\n\nNoctis called it the eastern procedural annex. It was a waiting room with better seals, cold stone, and the sharp smell of drying ink.\n\nThree authorized copies hovered above the table: Council language, Foundation sequence, and Noctis commentary. The commentary was the longest and least honest. Lucien skipped it first, then read it last because institutional lies always betrayed what the writer feared most.\n\n**CROWN PROPOSAL REFUSED.**\n\n**SINGLE SUCCESSOR STRUCTURE NOT ESTABLISHED.**\n\n**DISTRIBUTED WITNESS CONDITIONS REMAIN ACTIVE.**\n\nHe had expected the refusal.\n\nHaru Aerlen had made a discipline of turning down obvious victories, treating power as though sitting above others made it less real. A romantic error, dangerous in its way, and occasionally useful.\n\nWhat Lucien had not expected was for the room to survive the refusal.\n\nThe commentary could not disguise it. Regalia had offered height; the Council wanted custody of it; Noctis wanted language to inherit it; even fear wanted one central throat to blame. Haru had walked away from the chair, and the structure had remained standing anyway, arranged around him rather than beneath.\n\nLucien touched the section holding Elira Vale's statement. The relay crystal warmed under his fingertip.\n\nShe had not anchored Haru by binding him. She had interrupted him in public, intimately, without turning opposition into betrayal. Noctis had never built a category for that: loyalty that refused possession.\n\nHe scrolled to Rhea Valmont's ruling. Authority distributed. Witness marks separated. Green Circle gallery admitted. Rescued Noctis students consulted before placement. Messy, inefficient, and infuriatingly hard to seize. Lucien smiled slightly. There it was.\n\nMorality had nothing to do with it. Morality was paint people slapped over appetites they disliked naming. What Haru's side possessed was redundancy.\n\nIf one witness failed, another stayed standing. If one affection was squeezed, another objection surfaced. If one record was challenged, an uglier copy survived in a Green Circle notebook. Even if Haru tilted toward command, someone close enough to be hurt by him still held standing to say no.\n\nLucien had built order by narrowing routes until obedience became the sole efficient path. Aurelis had multiplied routes until obedience could no longer pose as the only way to survive.\n\nArchitecture. He understood architecture.\n\nFor the first time since the courtyard, he felt the exact shape of the loss. Appeal could reverse a defeat, reframe it, feed it back into process until it returned wearing a seal. This was a missing handle.\n\nHe could still hurt them. He could make the next hearing uglier, the next rumor sharper, the next rescue costlier, forcing Haru to show his price and Elira to bleed privacy. But he could no longer classify their network as an orbit. Orbits had centers. This arrangement had returns.\n\nLucien lowered the transcript. For a second the annex held nothing except the low hum of seals waiting to be used.\n\nNoctis had spent years teaching him that every bond became leverage if pressed at the right angle. Aurelis had shown him bonds that pushed back in multiple directions.\n\nHe filed the transcript under active threat. Incomplete dangers deserved the cleanest labels.\n\nThen he opened the appeal channel. Repentance was for people who wanted witnesses to forgive the shape of their hands. Lucien wanted procedure. Procedure still answered when called.\n\n---\n\nAira had not slept.\n\nShe had braided back her hair, buttoned her uniform, and bullied her posture into public readiness through sheer spite. Two fingers wore fresh ink, a folded packet sat tucked under her arm, and a tea flask hung from her belt like a sidearm.\n\nShe took one look at the two of us in the cottage front room and narrowed her eyes.\n\nAira: \"Both of you are sitting in the same room without a new crisis in progress. Suspicious.\"\n\nElira: \"Good morning, Aira.\"\n\nAira: \"That is not a denial.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good morning.\"\n\nAira: \"Worse.\"\n\nShe crossed to the writing desk without waiting for permission, cleared a stack of witness forms with the back of her hand, and unfolded the packet.\n\nInside lay a sheet of parchment sealed between two panes of ward glass. At first glance it looked ruined, soaked in uneven black blotches with no cipher blocks, no Circle notation, and no north-school angular script. Only wet darkness.\n\nYet the room cooled around it, cautious as winter testing an unlatched window.\n\nAt Elira's throat, the silver-veined pendant warmed against the gray morning light, dark at its center, listening without flaring.\n\nAira planted both palms on the desk.\n\nAira: \"This came through the same whisper-channel after Maelin escorted you two out. Lysa Pell's first condition held steady: name, no content requested, stop means stop, silence not consent. Then this arrived. Just spilled ink. Nobody traced it, heated it, or tried to squeeze a location out of it, because apparently we are a civilization now.\"\n\nElira: \"Apparently?\"\n\nAira: \"I am reserving judgment until after breakfast.\"\n\nI leaned closer. The ink trembled, and the cold behind my sternum answered faintly, ozone and metal recognizing a northern system that owed nothing to Aurelis.\n\nI stepped back. Elira caught the movement immediately.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nHaru: \"It reacts to Black Circle perception.\"\n\nAira's head snapped up.\n\nAira: \"Define reacts in a way that will not ruin my morning.\"\n\nHaru: \"If I listen too closely, it may sharpen.\"\n\nAira: \"That did, in fact, ruin my morning.\"\n\nElira's fingers brushed my sleeve.\n\nElira: \"Then you do not listen too closely.\"\n\nThe simplicity of it hit harder than a lecture. Someone standing beside a cliff and naming the ground.\n\nHaru: \"No lonely hero.\"\n\nAira exhaled through her nose, softer than her expression.\n\nAira: \"Good. Because Lysa's rule says do not ask content through this route. If the Black Circle listens deeper, congratulations, that is just asking content with extra drama.\"\n\nElira: \"So we use room conditions, not Haru.\"\n\nAira: \"Already on it.\"\n\nShe uncapped her tea flask and poured steaming tea into three cups she had definitely stolen from an official sideboard. Steam rose and fogged the ward glass. Caught between the morning chill and the rising heat, the black blotches contracted. Fine veins of blue-white crystal formed inside the stains: one line, three lines, then the curve of a mountain pass.\n\nElira leaned over the desk, golden eyes narrowing with the formidable focus of someone expecting ink to account for itself.\n\nAira: \"I am sorry. Are you intimidating the ink?\"\n\nElira: \"I am reading it.\"\n\nAira: \"That is what I said.\"\n\nElira: \"The script is small. It should try harder.\"\n\nAira looked at me.\n\nAira: \"Haru, I regret to inform you that your girlfriend has discovered academic dominance.\"\n\nHaru: \"I knew.\"\n\nElira: \"Both of you may resume being unserious after we prevent an international incident.\"\n\nAira pointed at her.\n\nAira: \"See? Weaponry.\"\n\nThe frost-glyphs sharpened into clean geometry, blue lines locked inside black ink like winter trapped under a mapmaker's hand. Aira produced a narrow brass compass, three support threads, and a slate crowded with thermal notes.\n\nAira: \"Northern glyphs are obnoxious because half the stroke weight depends on temperature. Too warm, they smear. Too cold, they lock. It is like reading handwriting done in melting ice by someone who hates vowels.\"\n\nElira traced the air above an angular mark.\n\nElira: \"This is not standard northern.\"\n\nAira: \"No. Routing shorthand. Civil-service, maybe. Relay maintenance, not student script.\"\n\nI looked between them.\n\nHaru: \"Someone inside Noctis?\"\n\nAira's support threads lit pale blue, hovering above the glass without touching.\n\nAira: \"Someone with civil-service routing access. I am not saying inside the Voss line because I enjoy living and Senna would develop a migraine severe enough to become legislation. Lysa carried this, but someone older gave her ink that remembers cold.\"\n\nElira hovered a finger above the junction of lines.\n\nElira: \"Not a full location.\"\n\nAira: \"Because Lysa is smarter than every adult currently trying to turn her into evidence.\"\n\nThe lines joined into a mountain ridge, a northern road, and a single mark near the parchment edge: three stacked rings pierced by a vertical bar. Sleep deprivation vanished from Aira's face, leaving only tactical clarity.\n\nAira: \"Valise Frost-Relay.\"\n\nElira's hands went still. I knew the name from geography classes I had only pretended to find dull: a high-altitude relay station on the northern border between Aurelis and Valemere, built where mountain weather scrambled official mirrors. Too remote for student traffic, too cold for regular staff rotations, and too useful to dismantle.\n\nHaru: \"Why point us there?\"\n\nA second line surfaced beneath the map, thin and pale. Words, not coordinates:\n\n**CONDITIONS BEFORE CONTENT. WARMTH BEFORE TRACE. VALISE HOLDS WHAT NOCTIS CANNOT HEAR CLEANLY.**\n\nThe final word fractured slightly in the ink. *Cleanly.*\n\nMy hands curled. Elira's thumb crossed the black-and-silver ribbon above my wrapped finger.\n\nHaru: \"Noctis cannot hear cleanly.\"\n\nAira: \"Meaning Lucien's echoes monitor ordinary channels, while Valise's storm interference breaks Dominion grammar.\"\n\nElira: \"Or someone wants us to believe that.\"\n\nAira nodded once.\n\nAira: \"Also possible. Pleasantly terrible. Option one: it is real, and Lysa is showing where a whisper-channel can widen safely. Option two: someone is baiting us north to frame Haru for building a private Black Circle relay.\"\n\nShe raised a third finger.\n\nAira: \"Option three: it is a trap that only works if we act too important to keep our own rules.\"\n\nElira looked up from the frost lines.\n\nElira: \"Then the answer is identical in every option.\"\n\nHaru: \"We do not trace first.\"\n\nElira: \"We pack warmth, witnesses, healers, and public route logs. No private relay. No Black Circle listening beyond consent.\"\n\nAira: \"And probably snacks.\"\n\nElira: \"Especially snacks.\"\n\nHer fingers tapped the edge of the writing desk once, light and decisive.\n\nAira: \"Good. Fighting northern relay grammar on an empty stomach makes me unpleasant.\"\n\nHaru: \"Makes you?\"\n\nAira's eyes narrowed.\n\nAira: \"That sounded like confidence. I hate it.\"\n\nThe parchment cooled again, and one small mark formed along the bottom margin. A second name.\n\n**SERA DOES NOT ANSWER WHERE SHE IS COLD. ASK IF WARMTH IS ALLOWED.**\n\nThe room went quiet. Elira's expression softened in a way that had nothing to do with surrender.\n\nElira: \"There is another student.\"\n\nAira wrote at once.\n\nAira: \"Sera. Unknown circle, unknown track. Condition: cannot answer where she is cold. Ask if warmth is allowed.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not where she is.\"\n\nAira: \"Not where.\"\n\nElira: \"Whether warmth is allowed.\"\n\nThe Black Circle ached at the edge of my chest. Somewhere behind Noctis walls, Lysa had sent a map she refused to turn into a target, and near her someone named Sera was cold enough that asking *where* carried danger.\n\nI wanted to tear every wall between us down. That did not make tearing safe. I laid my palm flat on the desk where both of them could see it.\n\nHaru: \"Record this. I want to listen deeper.\"\n\nAira's pen stopped. Elira watched me without blinking.\n\nHaru: \"I am not going to. Just record that I want to.\"\n\nAira's shoulders eased with quiet relief, and her pen moved again.\n\nAira: \"Haru Aerlen states desire to listen beyond condition and refuses action under conditions-before-content protocol.\"\n\nElira's hand found mine.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira flipped to a fresh page.\n\nAira: \"I am naming this ledger Ice Ink. Nobody object. I am emotionally attached.\"\n\nA measured knock sounded at the front door. Two strikes, a pause, one more. Rhea Valmont.\n\nAira glanced toward the entry.\n\nAira: \"And there is the invoice.\"\n\n---\n\nRhea entered with Maelin Orr behind her and a document case under one arm. Rhea carrying a locked case meant the paper inside had already offended her and survived only because murder remained unlawful.\n\nMaelin carried her medical satchel, three wrapped rolls, and the look of a healer who had found politics inside her supply cabinet. She went straight for Elira.\n\nMaelin: \"You are upright.\"\n\nElira: \"I am seated.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Upright with furniture assistance is still upright.\"\n\nElira: \"Good morning to you too.\"\n\nMaelin dropped one roll beside Elira's elbow.\n\nMaelin: \"Eat that while I decide whether to stay angry.\"\n\nAira eyed the satchel.\n\nAira: \"Is there one for clerical trauma?\"\n\nMaelin tossed a second roll straight into Aira's hands without looking.\n\nAira: \"I love governance.\"\n\nRhea set the case next to the frost-ink map. Her gaze flicked over the glass, and she asked conditions before content.\n\nRhea: \"Conditions?\"\n\nAira turned the ledger toward her.\n\nAira: \"Received through Lysa Pell's route. No trace, no location extraction. Map points to Valise Frost-Relay. New condition attached: Sera does not answer where she is cold. Ask if warmth is allowed.\"\n\nRhea read the entry once. Her jaw tightened.\n\nRhea: \"Recorded.\"\n\nShe unlatched the case and slid out a formal decree on pale crystal-paper, sealed in red wax and black thread under the Council sigil and the emergency financial containment board. Krail's signature sat neat and small at the bottom, like a clerk expecting history to thank him for restraint.\n\nRhea: \"Councilor Krail has issued a provisional fiscal embargo on all Aurelis student-exchange and emergency transit funds connected to extra-classification-adjacent travel, Noctis transfer care, and northern stability review proceedings.\"\n\nAira stared.\n\nAira: \"That sentence has three knives and a napkin.\"\n\nRhea: \"Four. The napkin is also a knife.\"\n\nMaelin snatched the decree before I could touch it.\n\nMaelin: \"He froze medical travel funds?\"\n\nRhea: \"Pending containment audit.\"\n\nMaelin's healer focus dropped into cold silence.\n\nMaelin: \"Containment of whom?\"\n\nRhea: \"The decree avoids naming living students. It cites unclassified-risk-associated dependents, protected witnesses under contested jurisdiction, and material evidence carriers.\"\n\nNera. Darian. Iven. Sela. Aric. Lysa, if she ever reached us, and Sera, if she could even ask. Every child Noctis had tried to turn into a conduit was being recast as a budget hazard.\n\nElira took the decree from Maelin and read it cleanly, the dark center of her pendant catching the window light.\n\nElira: \"It is legally defensible.\"\n\nMaelin: \"It is medically obscene.\"\n\nRhea: \"Both.\"\n\nAira leaned over the parchment.\n\nAira: \"Temporary suspension pending verification that public funds will not support unauthorized contact with compromised relay infrastructure.\"\n\nShe glanced at the ice-ink map.\n\nAira: \"He moved fast.\"\n\nRhea: \"He anticipated a northern push after the whisper-channel. He does not need to know Valise to freeze everything that could reach it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Carriages.\"\n\nRhea: \"Frozen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Medical supplies.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Frozen if billed under academy emergency care.\"\n\nHaru: \"Warmth arrays.\"\n\nRhea: \"Frozen if classified as travel support.\"\n\nAira: \"Food?\"\n\nRhea's mouth flattened.\n\nRhea: \"Allowed if consumed on campus. Frozen if provisioned for exchange travel.\"\n\nAira: \"I am going to become illegal over bread.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Stand in line.\"\n\nElira set the decree down.\n\nElira: \"He is not trying to stop all movement. He is trying to force any movement to look privately funded, unstable, and loyal to Haru.\"\n\nRhea nodded.\n\nRhea: \"If Haru pays, it becomes a Black Circle expedition. If Valemere pays, Krail shouts foreign interference. If Senna releases funds unilaterally, he cries procedural bias. If Aurelis leadership overrides him, he gets the institutional fracture he wants.\"\n\nHaru: \"And if we do nothing, Lysa's door closes.\"\n\nTwo kinds of cold sat side by side on the desk: a frightened girl's frost-ink condition asking not to be used, and a councilor's lawful embargo demanding control before responsibility.\n\nMaelin tapped the decree hard enough to make the red wax jump.\n\nMaelin: \"Treating healing logistics like a bargaining chip is an insult to the trauma ward.\"\n\nAira raised her hand.\n\nAira: \"As unofficial representative of notebooks, it is also an insult to ledgers.\"\n\nRhea looked at her.\n\nRhea: \"You have a solution.\"\n\nAira: \"I have an emotion adjacent to a solution.\"\n\nThe window ward chimed green.\n\nOutside on the cottage path, a young Green Circle messenger stood vibrating in his boots, holding a folded note with both hands. Rhea opened the door, and the boy bowed so fast his hair fell into his eyes.\n\nBoy: \"President Valmont. Miss Solen. Haru Aerlen. Miss Vale. Healer Orr. Sorry. Mira says to send this right away and not let anyone with a Council seal touch it first, unless the Council seal is yours, President Valmont, because she says you will be offended accurately.\"\n\nRhea took the note.\n\nRhea: \"Mira said that?\"\n\nBoy: \"Aira did.\"\n\nAira looked shamelessly proud. Rhea read the note twice, then passed it to me. Mira's neat handwriting held steady until the final line, where excitement had defeated pen control:\n\n**Lower archive. Care Ledger open. Bring Haru if he can stand being thanked badly. Bring no Council treasury. Bring pockets.**\n\nBeneath it, smaller:\n\n**Also Maelin, please do not yell until you see the organization.**\n\nMaelin read over my shoulder.\n\nMaelin: \"I make no promises.\"\n\nFor the first time all morning, Rhea almost smiled.\n\nRhea: \"To the lower archive.\"\n\nElira reached for her shawl, and Maelin pointed straight at her.\n\nMaelin: \"Slowly.\"\n\nElira: \"Of course.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That sounded like diplomacy.\"\n\nElira: \"It was.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will make sure she goes slowly.\"\n\nElira gave me a look of bright offense.\n\nElira: \"You will accompany me at a pace chosen by mutual respect.\"\n\nAira tucked the Ice Ink ledger under her arm.\n\nAira: \"Recording that as romantic governance.\"\n\nRhea: \"Do not.\"\n\nAira wrote anyway. Behind us, the red-wax decree stayed on the desk beside the glowing map. Krail had locked the official road; Mira had apparently opened another one with pockets.\n\n---\n\nThe lower archive smelled of damp wool, paper, and copper.\n\nThat last scent stopped me on the landing. Elira paused beside me, giving me a quiet beat to take it in before the room noticed us.\n\nBelow, the reading hall had become half registrar's office, half quiet rebellion. Long wooden tables stretched under green lamps, crowded with students sorting coins into neat columns: copper pieces, silver half-marks, folded stipend notes, archive work chits, meal credits, and a small pouch of Valemere currency tagged **exchange rate pending, do not trust Leon's math**.\n\nGreen Circle students filled most of the hall, though Blue students stood by the windows counting blankets and travel tins, a few Red heirs hovered awkwardly with sealed allowance drafts, and two Yellow seniors sorted medical heat charms under Maelin's aide's eye like men learning humility through inventory.\n\nAt the front table, Mira stood with her auburn curls pinned back, freckles bright against flushed cheeks, a pencil behind one ear and another in her hand, directing three queues at once.\n\nMira: \"Write your name only if you consent to the travel record. Anonymous gifts go in the gray ledger, though nobody marks anonymous to hide family pressure. If your house will punish you for donating, speak to Tessa first. Finn, the copper stacks are leaning. Finn. Finn, they are leaning emotionally.\"\n\nFinn, quiet as ever while juggling twelve tasks, nudged a coin column straight by one finger-width.\n\nMira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nAira stopped beside me, speechless for once. Nobody bowed, chanted, or reached for titles like *Crownless Black* or *extra-classification event*.\n\nA first-year Green student spotted us at the stairs.\n\nGreen First-Year: \"They came.\"\n\nMira looked up. Her expression flashed from command to panic to sheer resolve in half a second, and she marched over hugging the Care Ledger to her chest.\n\nMira: \"Please do not look overwhelmed in a way that makes everyone stop counting.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will try.\"\n\nMira nodded as if sealing a treaty.\n\nMira: \"Good. If everyone stops, we lose the small-copper sequence and I will cry in a mathematically justified way.\"\n\nAira drew a sharp breath.\n\nAira: \"Mira Castellan, that was beautiful.\"\n\nMira: \"Do not make it weird.\"\n\nAira: \"Too late internally. Externally, I am a ledger professional.\"\n\nRhea stepped aside from the central table. Maelin went straight to the medical crates to judge them with terrifying precision. Elira stayed visibly beside me, shawl over her shoulders, dark pendant at her throat, choosing the same ground without hiding.\n\nMira laid the ledger open between us. The cover page read:\n\n**AURELIS CARE LEDGER**\n\n**Conditions before content. Warmth before trace. Travel without throne.**\n\nMy throat tightened. Mira saw it and tapped her pencil once against the paper.\n\nMira: \"Every copper here is written down. Grand things keep trying to erase who carried the ordinary ones.\"\n\nShe turned the page to rows of names: Green dormitory students, Blue support track, kitchen apprentices, library runners, two arena cleaners. **Benn Arlow, Green Circle, first year, still bad at speeches, two coppers.** And in shaky script from Sela: **one ribbon bead, no coin, for warmth**, valued by Mira at zero currency and full witness.\n\nI had to look away. Elira's fingers brushed mine and stayed there, plain and steady.\n\nMira: \"Councilor Krail can lock the treasury gates. He does not own the pockets of the Green row. He does not own kitchen credit, spare Blue blankets, or Finn's suspicious talent for knowing which archive lamps can be swapped for cheaper ones for three days without breaking safety codes.\"\n\nAcross the room, Finn raised one hand without looking up.\n\nFinn: \"Safety maintained.\"\n\nMira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nShe looked back to me.\n\nMira: \"We are not writing you in as a king, a destiny, or a dramatic shadow person.\"\n\nAira whispered under her breath and scribbled it straight into the margin.\n\nAira: \"Dramatic shadow person.\"\n\nMira ignored her with practiced dignity.\n\nMira: \"We are writing you in as someone who did not go north alone.\"\n\nI thought of my first week in the assessment hall, when Green Circle robes gave everyone permission to treat me as harmless, and I had hidden inside that permission until it became a cage. Now Green students were stacking copper under lamps, turning the lowest rank in the academy into a road the Council could not freeze without admitting it feared pockets.\n\nI bowed, just enough to mean it without making theater of the room.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nThe hall went too quiet. Mira looked horrified.\n\nMira: \"Please do not bow in a way that makes people emotional near sorted coin.\"\n\nA laugh broke near the blanket table, then another, and the hall breathed again. Elira's grip warmed around my hand while Aira swiped at one eye and blamed archive dust. From the medical crates, Maelin spoke without turning.\n\nMaelin: \"If anyone cries into the heat charms, label them before packing.\"\n\nRhea stepped up to the ledger table.\n\nRhea: \"Mira Castellan.\"\n\nMira straightened.\n\nRhea: \"This campaign requires legal protection.\"\n\nMira swallowed hard, steadied her pencil over the rough paper, and met Rhea's eyes.\n\nMira: \"Tell me what you need.\"\n\nRhea set both palms flat beside the open binding, voice crisp enough to carry to the back benches.\n\nRhea: \"Student Council certifies this ledger as voluntary witness logistics, not treasury substitution. Contributions remain individually owned until converted by consent into route support. No donor claims command influence. No rank priority. No anonymous coercion.\"\n\nMira's pencil flew. Finn held his pen still. Rhea noticed at once.\n\nRhea: \"Objection?\"\n\nFinn held up a small-copper receipt.\n\nFinn: \"Large houses know when conversion happens. Green students do not. If the right vanishes at a supply station nobody can see, it is not a real right.\"\n\nMira's pencil stopped.\n\nFinn: \"Every receipt carries the exact cutoff. We post notice before conversion. Once copper buys bread, nobody pulls coin back out of the loaf, but they can still pull their name from any claim of endorsement. Care stays. Loyalty does not get invented afterward.\"\n\nThe nearest Green tables had gone dead silent.\n\nRhea: \"Accepted. Draft it.\"\n\nFinn glanced at Mira, who slid the main ledger across to him. He wrote the clause himself. Once he finished, Aira opened Ice Ink beside it.\n\nAira: \"Cross-reference?\"\n\nFinn: \"Now.\"\n\nAira: \"Care Ledger answers fiscal embargo without converting Haru into a private expedition center.\"\n\nElira: \"Travel conditions remain distributed. We fund our ability to answer conditions, not access to content.\"\n\nFinn read the line twice before Aira copied it down.\n\nRhea: \"Good.\"\n\nElira's mouth curved faintly.\n\nElira: \"I learned from terrifying women.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Eat your roll.\"\n\nElira obeyed with dignity, which nearly undid me more than the ledger had.\n\nAt the far end of the hall, a Red Circle student cleared his throat.\n\nRed Student: \"My house sent emergency travel credit. I can cover the carriage.\"\n\nEvery Green head at the nearest table turned toward him. He flushed crimson.\n\nRed Student: \"Not as patronage.\"\n\nMira walked over.\n\nMira: \"Then it goes in the same ledger as everyone else's.\"\n\nRed Student: \"Same columns, no seal.\"\n\nMira: \"And no naming the carriage after your house.\"\n\nRed Student: \"I was not going to.\"\n\nHis friend leaned in, muttering under his breath.\n\nFriend: \"You were absolutely going to.\"\n\nThe Red student looked thoroughly betrayed.\n\nBehind us, Cassian's voice floated down the stairs.\n\nCassian: \"Never name a carriage after your house. It makes the horses judgmental.\"\n\nLeon followed him down with two heavy crates balanced against his chest.\n\nLeon: \"Water route stabilizers. Before anyone starts, yes, I signed them into the ledger.\"\n\nAira: \"Look at you, becoming accountable.\"\n\nLeon: \"I regret arriving.\"\n\nCassian set a sealed heat case on the supply table.\n\nCassian: \"Fire charms. Low flare, long duration. Tested around Noctis students. No sudden ignition, and no hero speeches included.\"\n\nMaelin inspected the latches.\n\nMaelin: \"Acceptable.\"\n\nCassian pressed a hand over his heart.\n\nCassian: \"I will treasure this lavish praise.\"\n\nThe reading hall hummed with practical arguments over blanket weights, spare axles, mountain tea, dried fruit morale, and whether Aira could label a crate **Legal Bread**. Rhea vetoed it; Aira relabeled the crate **Non-Treasury Nutritional Evidence**, and after staring for eight seconds, Rhea let it stand.\n\nNear noon, a Council relay crystal flared above the stairs. Conversation thinned across the room.\n\nKrail appeared at formal scale, controlled and distant, taking in the tables, coins, ledgers, and students. His eyes stopped on me, then slid to Mira.\n\nCold, black-edged anger stirred behind my ribs. Elira stepped half a pace forward so she stood squarely beside me.\n\nKrail: \"President Valmont. I trust this gathering is not an attempt to circumvent a lawful fiscal containment order.\"\n\nRhea stayed back from the center.\n\nRhea: \"No Council treasury funds are moving.\"\n\nKrail: \"Publicly pressured minors cannot be treated as independent financial instruments.\"\n\nMira's knuckles whitened around her pencil. Rhea answered before Mira had to speak.\n\nRhea: \"Agreed. Which is why the ledger enforces consent confirmation, anti-coercion review, guardian-risk flags, contribution reversal rights, and zero command privilege.\"\n\nKrail's eyes narrowed fractionally. He had come looking for emotion and walked into paperwork.\n\nAira breathed softly enough that only three of us heard her.\n\nAira: \"Delicious.\"\n\nKrail: \"This still risks transforming Haru Aerlen into the beneficiary of a student loyalty campaign.\"\n\nElira's voice cut cleanly across the room.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nKrail turned to her.\n\nElira: \"The beneficiary is the route condition. Warmth before trace, medical support before content, distributed witness travel before private extraction. Haru does not own the road just because he drives it.\"\n\nKrail: \"Miss Vale, your phrasing is elegant, yet the optics remain dangerous.\"\n\nMaelin turned from the supply table, wiping her hands on a cloth.\n\nMaelin: \"So are cold children. If your concern is optics, write that down honestly. If it is public funds, none are moving. If it is medical safety, submit to healer review and stop freezing the bandage budget. If your real concern is that students with coppers made your embargo look cruel, that is not a medical category.\"\n\nKrail did not bristle, which was why he remained dangerous. He simply folded the blow into a colder angle.\n\nKrail: \"Senior Healer Orr, my concern is that unregulated movement toward a compromised northern relay may widen an active Noctis breach and expose minors to retaliation.\"\n\nIt was not a lie, and that was the sharpest part. I took one step forward, enough to answer without taking center stage.\n\nHaru: \"Then record the conditions that make movement safer.\"\n\nKrail's focus sharpened on me.\n\nHaru: \"Not permission. Conditions. Who records, who calls a halt, who clears patients, which channels stay untraced, how donors are protected, where Black Circle perception stops without consent, and what signs require retreat. If your concern is safety, help define it in public. If it is control, the ledger will show that too.\"\n\nKrail's relay flickered once, as if the old systems under Aurelis had recognized an answer they did not expect from me.\n\nKrail: \"You are learning procedure.\"\n\nHaru: \"I am learning witnesses.\"\n\nHis face went unreadable. His gaze dropped once more to the Care Ledger.\n\nKrail: \"I will file a review.\"\n\nRhea: \"Expected.\"\n\nKrail: \"Do not mistake administrative tolerance for approval.\"\n\nAira: \"We would never.\"\n\nRhea closed her eyes for a beat. When Krail's projection vanished, nobody cheered. Mira let out a breath so hard she nearly dropped her pencil, and Finn caught it mid-air.\n\nMira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nFinn: \"Recorded.\"\n\nThe room went back to packing. By evening, the Care Ledger had funded the northern carriage practically, down to the last copper: reinforced wheels, two spare axles, medical heat arrays, trigger-safe blankets, cold-stable food, frost-proof ink, and tea. Too much tea, by Leon's count; not enough, by Aira's.\n\nEvery ledger page carried the same header:\n\n**No content purchased. No route owned. Warmth before trace.**\n\nI signed under it. Elira signed beside me, followed by Mira, Rhea, and Aira, who slipped in **Legal Bread included** until Rhea forced a line through it, leaving the ink readable underneath.\n\n---\n\nDawn came colder than the map.\n\nThe carriage waited at the north service gate beneath a steel-gray sky, frost coating the yard stones without theatrical glitter. The horses stamped softly; Cassian had picked the team himself because they looked unimpressed by politics.\n\nLeon had checked the water lines twice. Rhea had checked the route seals three times. Aira had slept forty minutes and returned strangely formidable.\n\nMaelin stood by the carriage counting medical satchels, travel cloak pinned over her healer robes, pausing long enough to inspect the clean wrap around my bruised ring finger beneath the black-and-silver ribbon before I pulled on my driving gloves.\n\nMaelin: \"If anyone develops heroism, nausea, frostbite, unexplained glyph exposure, or romantic stupidity that interferes with circulation, report immediately.\"\n\nAira: \"Is romantic stupidity a medical category?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Since Lake Velis, yes.\"\n\nWrapped in a thick gray wool shawl, Elira looked serenely unashamed.\n\nHaru: \"I feel accused.\"\n\nElira: \"You should.\"\n\nHer pendant caught the dawn light, silver veins calm under glass. Cold touched her cheeks with faint pink; she still looked tired, because survival did not bend to story pacing, yet she stood steady. When I offered my hand, she took it in plain view of the students gathered near the gate. Some smiled; a few looked away to leave us privacy inside public witness.\n\nMira arrived last with the Care Ledger tucked inside her coat, Finn beside her carrying a small lockbox guarded by three glaring Green students.\n\nMira: \"Final copper count verified. Reversal rights remain active until conversion at the first supply station. Anonymous gray ledger sealed. Sela's bead is in the non-currency witness pouch.\"\n\nAira blinked fast.\n\nAira: \"I am fine.\"\n\nLeon: \"No one asked.\"\n\nAira: \"Your lack of asking was emotionally loud.\"\n\nRhea stamped the route parchment flat against the carriage fender, her voice steady in the cold air.\n\nRhea: \"Student Council certifies voluntary route support. No command privilege attached. No private ownership established. No Council treasury funds moved.\"\n\nSenna's remote crystal brightened above Rhea's shoulder.\n\nSenna: \"Inter-National witness office records the same under protest review.\"\n\nKrail did not appear. Silence from him meant preparation; silence from Lucien meant worse.\n\nNera stood near the gate with Darian and Iven. Maelin and Rhea had barred her from the first carriage, and though she looked ready to argue, one glance at Iven stopped her. Sometimes repair meant staying where a child could watch you choose not to disappear.\n\nIven walked up holding a small wrapped parcel with the gravity of a state courier.\n\nIven: \"For Lysa. If you can give it. If not, do not force it.\"\n\nI took it carefully.\n\nHaru: \"What is it?\"\n\nIven: \"Boot sweets.\"\n\nHis mouth trembled.\n\nIven: \"Not the same kind. We do not have those. Aira said these will survive the cold and probably bureaucracy.\"\n\nAira: \"I stand by probably.\"\n\nI tucked the parcel into the open travel case rather than my pocket, visible and logged.\n\nHaru: \"We will not use it to make her answer.\"\n\nIven let out a breath, shoulders loosening by a fraction.\n\nIven: \"It is just so she knows someone remembered.\"\n\nElira crouched in front of him despite Maelin's sharp intake of breath.\n\nElira: \"We will ask if warmth is allowed.\"\n\nIven looked at her pendant, then her eyes.\n\nIven: \"And if Sera says no?\"\n\nElira's expression held without flinching, giving him an answer steady enough to trust.\n\nElira: \"Then no means no. And we keep the warmth close enough that she can change her mind without being punished for the first answer.\"\n\nIven swallowed and nodded. Maelin pointed at Elira.\n\nMaelin: \"Carriage. Now.\"\n\nElira rose with my help.\n\nElira: \"Yes, Healer.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That tone is suspiciously agreeable.\"\n\nElira: \"I am practicing.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Practice seated.\"\n\nThe carriage door swung open. Aira climbed in first with Ice Ink, the Care Ledger copy, and three color-coded maps. Maelin followed, still counting satchels.\n\nElira paused on the step and looked back at Aurelis. Towers rose pale in the dawn, windows dark except for scattered lamps: Green, Blue, a few Red, and one warm gold light burning in Professor Valen's office high above the east archive. Many small lights instead of a crown.\n\nElira's fingers tightened in mine.\n\nElira: \"Ready?\"\n\nI looked through the gate toward the white-edged road north. Somewhere beyond the ridge, Valise waited in mountain cold; somewhere beyond Valise, Lysa had risked a whisper and Sera would not answer where she was cold. The Black Circle stayed quiet inside me, mine to refuse as a throne and mine to answer for alongside everyone else.\n\nHaru: \"Ready.\"\n\nI climbed to the driver's bench and gathered the cold leather reins. Through the split cuff of my glove, the black-and-silver ribbon rested bright against the wrapped finger.\n\nRhea stepped back from the wheel.\n\nRhea: \"Route witness begins.\"\n\nAira's voice drifted through the carriage window.\n\nAira: \"Ledger open.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Patient seated.\"\n\nElira: \"Partner present.\"\n\nMira lifted one hand from the stone apron.\n\nMira: \"Safe journeys.\"\n\nAt the gate, a Green Circle first-year started to bow before catching himself, flushing as he straightened. I lifted the reins and nodded toward the iron bar.\n\nHaru: \"Hold the left hinge. It sticks.\"\n\nHe blinked, grabbed the handle with both hands, and hauled. The north gate groaned open between cold stone while students held their breath. I clicked to the horses, the carriage rolled forward over the threshold, and the gate swung shut behind us.\n\nBehind the wheels, Aurelis did not shrink. It felt as if hundreds of small hands had pushed the axle into motion. Inside the carriage, the frost-ink map cooled in its glass, pointing north while Lucien Voss learned that a door could open from the inside without becoming his route.\n\n## Coda: No Crown, Just Reins\n\nOutside the walls, the reins did not care that I carried the Black Circle.\n\nThey cared whether my hands shook, whether the horses felt a clean signal through the leather, and where frost had slickened the stones along the first rise. Compared to a throne, that honesty was a relief.\n\nBehind me, Aira shuffled the ledger for the third time because anxiety preferred neat columns. When the left front wheel dropped into a shallow rut, Elira's breath hitched softly through the front window, and Maelin pretended not to watch both of us medically.\n\nI adjusted my grip on the leather, feeling the dull throb of my wrapped finger beneath the ribbon, and set the team toward the white line of the northern ridge.\n\nThe carriage rolled away from the gate because ordinary people had done practical work together, and the road ahead belonged to whoever kept walking it.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-31-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep32","label":"Episode 26","number":"26","file":"episode-32-cover.webp","fallback":"Recovery Leave","eyebrow":"Lake House","recap":"","accent":["#164e63","#f8fafc","#facc15"],"displayLabel":"Episode 26","displayNumber":"26","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"The Weight of a Copper","body":"The road north did not look heroic.\n\nThat was the first thing I noticed after the Aurelis gates fell behind us. Heroic roads belonged in ballads: wide stone causeways, clean banners, clear weather. Our road was narrow, rutted, and white at the edges with packed snow.\n\nThe left front wheel found every rut. Every jolt came up through the driver's bench, through my gloves, and tugged at the black-and-silver ribbon around my wrist. It had survived more politics than most council seals. Maelin had wrapped my bruised ring finger beneath it before we left. Dried blood from the lower channel still darkened the quick, and every hard turn of the reins sent a narrow pulse down toward my palm. She had called it a small vascular rupture with an unknown magical cause. Elira had heard only unknown. I had called it manageable. Neither of them had forgiven the word.\n\nInside the carriage, something heavy thumped against the bench.\n\nAira: \"If that was the legal bread, I am suing the road.\"\n\nMaelin: \"If you packed bread under ledger copies, I am letting the road win.\"\n\nAira: \"Allegedly.\"\n\nElira laughed softly. The sound reached me through the small front window, light enough to make my shoulders drop an inch.\n\nI kept my eyes on the horses. Cassian had chosen them because they looked unimpressed by politics, and he had been right. The carriage carried no house name and no Aurelis treasury seal. On the outer panel, Aira had taped a narrow strip of paper that fluttered whenever the wind struck: **No content purchased. No route owned. Warmth before trace.**\n\nThe Care Ledger sat inside in a locked satchel between Aira's boots. A gust of wind pushed across the slope, lifting fine snow around the wheels.\n\nHaru: \"Easy.\"\n\nThe left horse ignored the comfort with professional contempt. Even the team understood that motion did not require inspiration.\n\nBehind us, Aurelis had shrunk to a line of towers too far back to shield anyone. Ahead, somewhere beyond the ridge, Sera still had not answered where she was cold. The Black Circle stirred faintly beneath my ribs, drawn to the frost-ink map cooling inside the carriage. I could listen if I wanted to. A child's breath. A room. A name Noctis had tried to bury. Or I might turn Lysa into a route by proving that even careful words could be mined.\n\nThe reins creaked under my fingers. I loosened my grip, letting the ribbon shift against the leather where Elira had tied it.\n\nBehind me, the front window slid open. Cold air rushed into the carriage.\n\nMaelin: \"Close that immediately unless the plan includes hypothermia.\"\n\nElira: \"The patient is asking a question.\"\n\nMaelin: \"The patient can ask with the window two inches open.\"\n\nElira leaned to the narrow gap, wrapped in her dark-gray shawl, gold hair tucked beneath a traveling hood that had already begun losing its argument with the wind. The redesigned dark crystal pendant rested visible at her throat. It did not glow. It listened.\n\nElira: \"Your shoulders changed.\"\n\nI guided the horses around a sharp bend.\n\nHaru: \"The road is uneven.\"\n\nAira: \"That is not an answer. That is scenery wearing guilt.\"\n\nElira's gaze held steady through the gap.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI drew a lungful of cold air.\n\nHaru: \"I was thinking about listening deeper.\"\n\nThe carriage went witness-quiet.\n\nAira: \"To the ink route?\"\n\nHaru: \"I considered it.\"\n\nElira did not reach for me, and that steadied me more than touch would have.\n\nElira: \"And?\"\n\nHaru: \"I am not going to.\"\n\nAira: \"Record?\"\n\nHaru: \"Record.\"\n\nHer pencil started at once.\n\nAira: \"Haru Aerlen states desire to listen beyond conditions. Refuses.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Add that the refusal occurred before frostbite.\"\n\nAira: \"I cannot medically verify theatrical blood pressure.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That has never stopped your footnotes.\"\n\nElira's hand slipped through the gap, palm up. I shifted one hand from the leather long enough to press my gloved fingers against hers.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nThen Maelin slid the window shut with a healer's finality.\n\nMaelin: \"Touching complete. Circulation preserved. Everyone back to posture.\"\n\nAira: \"You packed extra blankets.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Blankets are not sentiment. They are textile medicine.\"\n\nElira laughed again, and the horses kept walking up the narrowing ridge.\n\nBy midmorning, the lower ascent began. Local maps called it Whispering Ridge, which sounded poetic until the wind hit and turned the pass into a hundred voices muttering at once. The track climbed a pine-dark slope, switchback after switchback, held steady by the water-route stabilizers Leon had signed into the ledger before dawn. I could still picture him glaring over the crate.\n\nLeon: \"If Aira writes 'becoming accountable' next to my signature, I am throwing the crate in the river.\"\n\nAira: \"I wrote 'already doomed.'\"\n\nLeon: \"Worse.\"\n\nNow his stabilizers kept the wheels from sliding one careful turn at a time. At the third switchback, Maelin rapped twice on the carriage roof. I drew the horses onto a wider shoulder where a stunted pine leaned over the drop.\n\nThe door opened before I climbed down. Maelin stepped out first with a medical satchel tucked under one arm.\n\nMaelin: \"Horse rest. Patient circulation check. Driver fingers.\"\n\nHaru: \"Driver fingers?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Do not make me repeat myself in cold air.\"\n\nAira climbed down behind her, hugging the Ice Ink case.\n\nAira: \"I support the inspection of driver fingers. They are attached to our steering infrastructure.\"\n\nElira appeared last. I was already beside the step. She looked at my offered hand, then at my face.\n\nElira: \"Support, not panic.\"\n\nHaru: \"Support.\"\n\nThe mountain cold struck her harder than the rest of us. I saw it in the pause after her boot met snow, in the stiffening of her left shoulder beneath the shawl, and in the brief amber pulse of Maelin's healer charm at her cuff. Elira caught me watching.\n\nElira: \"Do not become tragic.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was aiming for useful.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Useful people stand out of the wind.\"\n\nShe pointed toward the pine and turned the halt into a field clinic, winning the first five minutes by sheer force of competence. She checked Elira's pulse, shoulder, and mana stability while scowling at the altitude.\n\nMaelin: \"Left shoulder?\"\n\nElira: \"Present.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Pain.\"\n\nElira: \"Also present.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Scale, Miss Vale, before I estimate unkindly.\"\n\nElira sighed.\n\nElira: \"Four when still. Six when the carriage decides to become a percussion instrument.\"\n\nAira wrote from the side.\n\nAira: \"Road indicted.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Increase wrap support. No standing for more than six minutes.\"\n\nElira: \"That is impractical.\"\n\nMaelin: \"So was dying. We are all adapting.\"\n\nThe sentence landed flat and hard. Elira looked down at the snow, then nodded once.\n\nElira: \"Six minutes.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Good.\"\n\nAira opened the travel case and produced bread, cheese, and dried pears beneath a fresh paper tag: **Mountain morale. Not legal bread. Legally distinct.**\n\nMaelin looked at the label.\n\nMaelin: \"I am choosing not to have feelings about that.\"\n\nAira: \"Generous.\"\n\nElira took bread before Maelin could force it on her, which made Maelin suspicious, though she allowed the miracle. I turned back toward the horses.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI stopped. She held out one hand, palm open. I walked back through the wind, watching the pink cold in her cheeks and the white plume of her breath.\n\nElira: \"Your fingers.\"\n\nHaru: \"Gloved.\"\n\nElira: \"That was not my question.\"\n\nAira: \"He learned that dodge from you.\"\n\nElira: \"Unfortunately, he uses it badly.\"\n\nI pulled off one glove. My knuckles were white with cold. Elira's expression sharpened into annoyance edged with care.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"The reins are cold.\"\n\nElira: \"So is snow. We still do not hold it for three hours as a character statement.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I approve the tone.\"\n\nElira took my bare hand between both of hers. Warmth rose from her palms, quiet and unadorned, with no gold flare and no Yellow Circle display. I nearly pulled back, knowing what mana cost her while her own body was still healing.\n\nElira: \"Do not.\"\n\nHaru: \"Your shoulder.\"\n\nElira: \"My hands are not my shoulder.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Technically true. Annoyingly so.\"\n\nElira looked up at me.\n\nElira: \"Let me help without turning it into an audit.\"\n\nI left my hand in hers.\n\nHaru: \"All right.\"\n\nHer expression softened.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nAira, chewing bread with unearned solemnity, cleared her throat.\n\nAira: \"The record notes a successful warmth transfer.\"\n\nMaelin: \"The record also notes Miss Vale stops in two minutes.\"\n\nElira: \"The record is crowded.\"\n\nHaru: \"How is your shoulder?\"\n\nElira: \"Currently in administrative dispute. Maelin is planning to argue with my next three vertebrae if they refuse to cooperate.\"\n\nHaru: \"I would not advise arguing with Maelin.\"\n\nElira: \"Neither would the vertebrae.\"\n\nAira pointed at us with a heel of bread.\n\nAira: \"This is exactly the level of romance mountain roads deserve.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Eat.\"\n\nAira ate. Warmth crept back into my fingers. Before Maelin could call time, Elira released my hand on her own. She had chosen the care, and then she stopped. I pulled the glove back on with feeling returning to my fingertips.\n\nWhen the six minutes ended, Maelin pointed toward the carriage.\n\nMaelin: \"Patient.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Then demonstrate.\"\n\nElira climbed back inside with my hand and a sigh that could have corrected grammar at twenty paces. Before the door shut, she looked out at me.\n\nElira: \"No lonely safeguard.\"\n\nHaru: \"No protected witness put behind glass.\"\n\nAira: \"No frostbitten steering infrastructure.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No delayed circulation reports.\"\n\nThe door latched, and the ridge carried us higher.\n\nThe Ice Ink changed after noon. I knew because Aira went quiet, and Aira silent was rarely peace. Paper rustled behind the bulkhead, a tin cup stopped clinking, and the front window slid open exactly two inches.\n\nAira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I hear you.\"\n\nI kept my eyes on the narrow track.\n\nAira: \"Do not listen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Understood.\"\n\nElira: \"Aira?\"\n\nAira: \"The ink is vibrating.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Ink should not have muscle tone.\"\n\nAira: \"Tell it that. The map is not extending. It is thickening.\"\n\nElira: \"Altitude?\"\n\nAira: \"Partly. Temperature too. But not just weather. The road ahead lets messages arrive differently.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Use fewer scholar words and more travel words.\"\n\nAira: \"It is becoming a condition map.\"\n\nElira: \"Good. Keep it sealed.\"\n\nAira: \"Better. Thank you. I hate that your phrasing works.\"\n\nThe horses rounded another bend, and Whispering Ridge narrowed into a high stone notch between two cliffs. A weathered sign hung beneath the arch: **WHISPERING PASS GATE**. Below it, a fresh Council placard had been nailed to the post, stamped in red and black wax. I did not need to see the signature to know Krail's hand.\n\nThe horses slowed on their own as the portcullis came into sight. Three border staff stood beneath the arch: an older man in a heavy gray cloak, a Blue Circle road warden, and a clerk clutching a slate.\n\nGate Captain: \"Carriage halt.\"\n\nI pulled the team to a stop and climbed down slowly, both hands visible. The road warden's gaze dropped to the black-and-silver ribbon at my wrist before lifting to my face. The captain did not bow.\n\nGate Captain: \"Haru Aerlen?\"\n\nHaru: \"Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nI kept my hands open at my sides as the carriage door opened behind me.\n\nGate Captain: \"Senior Healer Orr.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Captain Valech.\"\n\nThe captain winced faintly. Elira stepped down with my hand and Maelin's glare, followed by Aira carrying two ledgers and the expression of someone about to enjoy herself in a narrow, devastating way.\n\nThe clerk stared at Elira's pendant, then my coat, then the taped paper on the carriage panel.\n\nClerk: \"Under provisional fiscal containment order, all extra-circular travel without treasury clearance is restricted.\"\n\nAira: \"Good afternoon to you too.\"\n\nThe clerk swallowed. On the carriage panel, Rhea's certified route stamp glowed faintly.\n\nValech: \"We received President Valmont's certification. We also received Councilor Krail's containment order.\"\n\nAira: \"How emotionally rich for your inbox.\"\n\nThe road warden nearly smiled.\n\nValech: \"I am not your enemy.\"\n\nElira: \"Then do not become the lock.\"\n\nValech looked at her properly then. Not Yellow Circle. Not resurrected witness. Just Elira Vale, pale from cold and travel, standing upright because Maelin had not yet forced her back onto the bench.\n\nValech: \"Lady Vale, I have orders.\"\n\nElira: \"So do we.\"\n\nClerk: \"The Council order states no emergency transit funds can support private Black Circle movement.\"\n\nAira snapped open the Care Ledger. The sound carried over the wind.\n\nAira: \"Excellent. No Council emergency transit funds are being used.\"\n\nClerk: \"Student-collected coin cannot substitute for treasury clearance.\"\n\nAira rested two fingers on the page margin and tapped twice.\n\nAira: \"Krail's embargo freezes institutional spending by claiming any aid near Haru becomes private patronage. Page six explicitly separates voluntary care from command privilege. No debt route, no treasury bypass, no purchased access. Try to keep up. It is cold.\"\n\nShe turned the ledger toward Valech. He hesitated, and I saw the weight behind it. Aira lowered her voice, though not enough.\n\nAira: \"He is calculating what Krail can do to his pension.\"\n\nValech: \"My pension was lost three orders ago. I am checking whether this document protects my staff.\"\n\nThe correction cost Aira, and she took it cleanly.\n\nAira: \"It names every reader and maintenance witness who chooses the distinction without coercion.\"\n\nValech: \"Good. Promises like that are usually traps.\"\n\nHe looked at the road warden instead of ordering her.\n\nValech: \"Tessa, do you want to read it?\"\n\nRoad Warden: \"May I?\"\n\nAira: \"Name?\"\n\nRoad Warden: \"Tessa Orlin. Blue Circle road maintenance.\"\n\nAira entered the name before handing over the book.\n\nAira: \"Tessa Orlin reads Care Ledger at Whispering Pass Gate by choice.\"\n\nTessa gave her a dry look.\n\nTessa: \"You write everything.\"\n\nAira: \"Only what people later pretend did not happen.\"\n\nTessa read while the clerk leaned over her shoulder. Valech kept his eyes on me.\n\nValech: \"You could open this gate.\"\n\nThe Black Circle did not stir, though my anger did.\n\nHaru: \"I could. I am not going to.\"\n\nValech: \"Why?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because the gate is not the enemy.\"\n\nElira stepped beside me, not in front.\n\nElira: \"And because if fear left you standing here alone with a lock you hate, we do not need to make you ashamed before you can help.\"\n\nTessa looked up from the ledger. The clerk dropped his gaze to his slate. For one breath, the arch felt like an ordinary mountain pass again.\n\nThen the Council lock clicked. Red-black script flared across the iron: **TREASURY CLEARANCE REQUIRED.**\n\nThe portcullis shuddered, and unnatural frost crept from the lock straight into the gear housing. Maelin tested the cold air near the script and scowled.\n\nMaelin: \"It folded medical supplies into the freeze.\"\n\nElira: \"It freezes the bandage budget at the gate.\"\n\nInside the carriage, the Ice Ink thudded against its ward glass.\n\nAira: \"No. The map is reacting to the lock. If we fight the legal claim here, we drag the route into Council framing.\"\n\nHaru: \"And if I break it?\"\n\nAira: \"Then Krail receives a perfect sentence.\"\n\nBeneath my ribs, the Black Circle showed me how easily I could unseat the old iron pins without leaving a visible crack. Visible force could be condemned; hidden force would turn the ledger into a lie. I opened both hands.\n\nHaru: \"Record this too. I want to open the gate.\"\n\nAira's expression softened for half a heartbeat before she wrote.\n\nAira: \"Haru Aerlen states desire to open Whispering Pass Gate under embargo pressure. Refuses.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Add circulation currently acceptable.\"\n\nAira: \"Stop improving my ledger with medical spite.\"\n\nElira studied the frosted gears.\n\nElira: \"We do not answer the seal first.\"\n\nTessa closed the Care Ledger carefully.\n\nTessa: \"Then what do you answer?\"\n\nElira: \"The condition. The gate is physically frozen, and the lock is procedurally frozen. Which part needs warmth, and which part needs refusal?\"\n\nRoad maintenance came into Tessa's posture like a drawn blade.\n\nTessa: \"The gear housing is iced over. If the teeth clear, the wheel turns on mechanical authority alone.\"\n\nAira: \"So we do not touch the Council seal.\"\n\nTessa: \"We restore the gate's physical ability to open under road upkeep.\"\n\nMaelin reached into her satchel and held out three folded slips.\n\nMaelin: \"Pre-embargo medical transit necessity.\"\n\nThe clerk found his voice, startled by his own clarity.\n\nClerk: \"The portcullis mechanism falls under municipal upkeep, not expedition funding. If repaired with non-Council supplies, the lock cannot classify thawing ice as a release of restricted funds.\"\n\nAira pointed her pencil at him.\n\nAira: \"Name.\"\n\nClerk: \"Perrin Holt.\"\n\nAira logged Perrin Holt's distinction while he looked both terrified and fiercely proud. Then work replaced argument.\n\nCassian's low-flare fire charms came out of their sealed heat case, Aira reading ledger numbers aloud before handing them to Tessa, with Leon's water-route stabilizers set beside the gear trough. After four minutes, Elira's healer charm flashed amber; she hated it, yet she obeyed Maelin and sat in the open carriage doorway. I held the horses' bridles, an almost insulting task for the Black Circle and therefore the right one.\n\nThe first fire charm warmed the iron too fast. Tessa hissed.\n\nTessa: \"Slow it.\"\n\nAira: \"Charm two runs cooler.\"\n\nPerrin: \"The seal checks for spell tampering.\"\n\nMaelin: \"It detects heat. Iron is permitted to become warm, even under Council decree.\"\n\nWater ran down the gear housing in thin silver threads. Leon's stabilizer caught the meltwater and guided it clear of the teeth so it would not refreeze in the lock. Watching them work, I felt the Black Circle studying our patience from inside my chest. Old systems learned from repetition, just like people.\n\nHaru: \"Gate Captain. When the gear clears, you open it. Not me.\"\n\nValech's throat worked.\n\nValech: \"If I open it, I am accountable.\"\n\nElira rested her fingers lightly against the wooden doorframe of the carriage.\n\nElira: \"Yes.\"\n\nNot cruel. Not comforting. True.\n\nValech closed his eyes once, opened them, and stepped to the wheel. Tessa tested the lever. The iron gave an inch.\n\n**TREASURY CLEARANCE REQUIRED** flashed crimson on the lock.\n\nPerrin lifted his slate with shaking hands.\n\nPerrin: \"Municipal maintenance distinction logged. Medical transit condition logged. No Council funds released.\"\n\nAira: \"Voluntary ledger support logged.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Patient circulation worsening if delayed.\"\n\nElira: \"Warmth before trace.\"\n\nI kept quiet. Silence was part of the answer.\n\nValech gripped the wheel with both hands and hauled. The gears groaned, complaint echoing through every tooth, and the portcullis rose. The Council lock sparked once, then dimmed into grudging administrative silence.\n\nAira exhaled.\n\nAira: \"I am naming this method spiteful maintenance.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nAira: \"Provisional title.\"\n\nTessa barked a startled laugh. Valech stepped back from the wheel, breathing hard.\n\nValech: \"The gate is open.\"\n\nHaru: \"You opened it.\"\n\nIt did not make him safe from Krail, and he knew it, yet his shoulders sat squarer for choosing with his own hands.\n\nValech: \"Safe passage.\"\n\nElira leaned forward just enough to incline her head.\n\nElira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nPerrin glanced at her pendant, flushing.\n\nPerrin: \"Is it true? About the children and the channel?\"\n\nElira: \"Enough of it is true that we cannot wait. That is why we are going.\"\n\nPerrin nodded. Then he reached inside his coat and pulled out a small waxed-paper packet.\n\nTessa: \"Perrin.\"\n\nPerrin: \"Dried apple. My sister sends them for long shifts. If it breaks your ledger rules, leave it.\"\n\nAira materialized beside him like logistics had summoned her.\n\nAira: \"Name, consent, value?\"\n\nPerrin: \"Perrin Holt. Consent. One packet dried apple. No command privilege.\"\n\nAira wrote, then accepted the packet.\n\nAira: \"Witness value?\"\n\nElira: \"Warmth has many forms.\"\n\nAira wrote that too. We climbed back up, and the carriage rolled beneath the raised portcullis onto the climbing white road while the gate lowered behind us, finishing its part.\n\nTwo hours later, the high plateau opened into a world narrowed to white, gray, and breath. Twisted pines leaned permanently away from the wind, and snow lay deep enough to swallow the wheel rims. Inside, Maelin had ordered Elira to lie back against a travel cushion. Aira sat on the floor to keep the Ice Ink ledger flat against the sway of the floorboards.\n\nAira: \"The map is changing again.\"\n\nHaru: \"Do I stop?\"\n\nMaelin checked the healer charm at Elira's wrist.\n\nMaelin: \"Stop.\"\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"Stop.\"\n\nElira met Aira's glare without blinking.\n\nAira: \"Betrayal.\"\n\nElira: \"Clarity.\"\n\nI drew the horses to the shoulder beside a half-buried stone marker carved with three stacked rings pierced by a vertical line. Valise's relay symbol.\n\nThen the wind died. After hours of howling across the ridge, its sudden absence felt like dropping underwater. Snow absorbed the crunch of my boots as I climbed down. Inside the carriage, the Ice Ink began to hum, low and exact.\n\nMaelin stepped out first, because if danger existed she preferred to offend it medically. Elira followed slowly, leaning on my hand. Her fingers were warm, though fatigue tugged at her grip.\n\nHaru: \"Six minutes?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Four.\"\n\nElira: \"Six.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Five if you do not argue.\"\n\nElira stepped down into the snow.\n\nElira: \"Five.\"\n\nAira came out last, carrying the frost-ink parchment sealed between ward glass. The black ink had drawn itself into a tight, dark knot. That frightened me more than the stone marker.\n\nAira: \"Nobody speak toward the map until we agree on phrasing.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Agreed.\"\n\nElira and I both nodded. The ward glass fogged, and one small word surfaced in the frost:\n\n**ALLOWED?**\n\nNot a location. Not a plea. Just permission for warmth to exist near someone.\n\nElira's hand rose near her pendant without touching it. The dark crystal warmed, its seven open consent lines nested quietly beneath the surface. Aira crouched in the snow and set the ledger case on a flat rock.\n\nAira: \"We answer Sera, not the route.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Condition only.\"\n\nElira lowered herself beside Aira despite Maelin's immediate glare.\n\nMaelin: \"Five minutes.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe looked steadily at the frosted word, pale from cold and the kind of anger that refused to waste heat.\n\nElira: \"Sera. Warmth is near. You do not have to tell us where you are. You do not have to answer content. Is warmth allowed?\"\n\n**ALLOWED?** trembled across the glass. For one second, the Black Circle reached toward the space behind it: a faint thread, a child's breath, an iron bell, a cold so organized it worked like language. I opened my hand.\n\nHaru: \"I want to listen.\"\n\nAira's pencil moved at once.\n\nHaru: \"I am not listening.\"\n\nThe thread faded before it could become mine. Elira looked up at me, and the quiet pride in her eyes cut deeper than judgment would have.\n\nThe word dissolved into a thin bead of water down the inside of the glass. In its place, three dots formed, one after another, the third set farther back.\n\nAira inhaled sharply.\n\nAira: \"Not a map. Warmth permissions.\"\n\nThe first dot glowed faint blue beside one word: **NEAR.**\n\nThe second formed beside it: **NOT TOUCH.**\n\nThe third formed farthest away: **WATCHED.**\n\nElira's breath caught. Maelin rested a hand on her shoulder to ground her without holding her back.\n\nElira: \"Sera. Near is enough. Not touch is understood. Watched is understood. Warmth will remain near.\"\n\nAira repeated each phrase aloud as she wrote it down. The first dot brightened by a single degree. Inside the carriage sat Iven's packet of boot sweets, and I remembered his face at the gate asking what happened if Sera said no. Then no means no, and we stay close enough that she can change her mind without paying for the first answer.\n\nBehind us, the stone marker cracked. Blue-white light traced the three rings and bled down into the snow. Across the plateau, the clouds parted.\n\nValise Frost-Relay rose on the far ridge like a spire the weather had tried to erase and failed to outlast. Every narrow window glowed faint blue from within.\n\nAira stood slowly.\n\nAira: \"That is not abandoned.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira pushed herself upright, taking my arm only when she reached for it. Between us and the tower, frost lifted off the snow and gathered into dozens of small drifting lights: one for each heat charm packed under ledger number, one for each blanket bundle, one for the tea case, one for Perrin Holt's dried apples, one for Iven's boot sweets, and one for Sela's ribbon bead. That last light carried no heat at all, yet it glowed softest and held longest.\n\nThey did not form a crown or a ring around me. They spread into a line wide enough for several people to walk abreast. Above them, frost carved two clean sentences into the air:\n\n**ENTER WITHOUT A CENTER.**\n\n**ASK WARMTH FIRST.**\n\nAira clutched the Ice Ink case between both gloved hands.\n\nAira: \"I object to architecture assigning group ethics.\"\n\nMaelin tightened the strap on her largest medical satchel.\n\nMaelin: \"Objection noted. Architecture remains correct.\"\n\nLeon, Cassian, and Rhea were not with us. Rhea had stayed at Aurelis to hold the Council flank against Krail's fiscal embargo, while Leon and Cassian kept the repair and witness network standing. The carriage felt smaller for their absence and the mountain larger, leaving Maelin, Aira, Elira, and me beside two unimpressed horses.\n\nElira watched the lights over the snow.\n\nElira: \"The least practical gifts are holding longest. The relay is testing whether warmth arrives with a claim.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Good. Then move before philosophy causes frostbite.\"\n\nWe obeyed, though the lights nearest the Care Ledger lingered above the locked case. Aira noticed and crouched to unlatch it on a flat stone. Blue frost crept along the paper margins without touching Mira's handwriting or Finn's dated receipts. A warmth mark settled above the first line, **Conditions before content. Warmth before trace. Travel without throne**, then drifted over the transparent pouch holding Sela's ribbon bead, Perrin's dried apples, the tea, and the blankets. The relay was counting what had been allowed to come with us.\n\nAira swallowed.\n\nAira: \"Entry order.\"\n\nSnow had gathered on Maelin's short gray hair and melted near her temples where healer warmth leaked despite her best efforts to keep things clinical.\n\nMaelin: \"Clinical risk first. If the relay holds hypothermic children, medical gear enters ahead of everyone else.\"\n\nAira: \"Maelin enters first. Warmth with credentials.\"\n\nMaelin shut her eyes for a beat.\n\nMaelin: \"If that phrase appears in the official record, I will prescribe you silence.\"\n\nAira: \"Too late. It already improved history.\"\n\nThe nearest light brightened. Elira's mouth curved faintly.\n\nElira: \"Aira follows with the ledger.\"\n\nAira's humor folded straight back into focus.\n\nAira: \"Second. With Care Ledger and Ice Ink. No content purchased. No route owned.\"\n\nElira looked toward the spire.\n\nElira: \"I enter third. Pendant visible. No contact request unless asked.\"\n\nThe crystal warmed once, and then all three looked at me.\n\nHaru: \"I enter last.\"\n\nThe warmth lights widened. Last carried old ghosts, of being left behind or arriving too late if a door shut between breaths, yet Elira stepped close enough that the wind pressed her shawl against my coat.\n\nElira: \"Last does not mean alone.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know. I am learning.\"\n\nMaelin pointed toward the horses.\n\nMaelin: \"Then learn while checking the brake. Practicality before symbolism.\"\n\nWe packed in deliberate pieces. Maelin checked Elira's shoulder and pulse before slinging her two medical satchels, a portable heat-array case, and her clinical slate. Aira sealed the Ice Ink inside ward glass, cross-tied the Care Ledger case to her belt, and tucked Perrin's dried apples and Iven's boot sweets into the visible supply pocket so every witness item traveled inside with us. Elira parted her shawl to leave the pendant in plain sight. I secured the team in the lee of the broken stone marker and tested the carriage brake.\n\nWhen I turned back, Maelin was already at the threshold, a slab of dark blue ice set into black stone carved with relay marks: **Consent before entry.**\n\nMaelin raised one open palm.\n\nMaelin: \"Medical warmth entering under consent condition. No touch. No content required.\"\n\nThe frost over the ice thinned by one layer. Aira stepped up next, holding the Care Ledger at chest height.\n\nAira: \"Voluntary route support entering under distributed witness record. No purchase of access.\"\n\nBlue light stirred behind the ice. Elira went third. Halfway up the path, the mountain cold bit her injured left shoulder hard enough to hitch her breath. I started forward before thought, and Elira turned her head.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nI froze with one boot half-lifted. Maelin's voice carried cleanly from the threshold.\n\nMaelin: \"Support may be offered verbally from current position.\"\n\nAira: \"Devastating regulation.\"\n\nHaru: \"One breath.\"\n\nElira inhaled, held it, and exhaled.\n\nElira: \"One breath taken.\"\n\nAt the door, she kept her hands off the ice.\n\nElira: \"Warmth remains near. No touch without consent. No answer treated as absence.\"\n\nThe slab slid inward without sound, exhaling cold that was not fiercer than the mountain, only more organized. Maelin entered first, Aira second, Elira third. The doorway stayed open for me. I glanced back once at the horses watching with shared suspicion and the carriage standing in the snow with Rhea's route stamp on one side and Aira's paper strip fluttering on the other. Then I walked inside.\n\nThe interior rose into blue distance, a central shaft climbing through all three spires lined with frost receivers. Maelin had already claimed a flat platform near the entrance and begun unpacking.\n\nMaelin: \"Do not stand in the door like a myth. Come in and be useful.\"\n\nRelief hit hard enough to make me nearly laugh. I stepped across the threshold, and the door closed behind me.\n\nFrost raced around my boots in a tight ring. The Black Circle stirred, and Elira took a half-step toward me before the ring fractured. Four uneven lines shot outward from my heels: toward Maelin's heat case, toward Aira's ledger, toward Elira's pendant, and back toward the sealed door where the carriage waited outside.\n\nAira exhaled.\n\nAira: \"Relay rejects centered Black Circle entry. Redistributes attention to witness group and external support.\"\n\nElira: \"Does it reject Haru?\"\n\nNear her boots, frost spelled one word: **NO.** Near the ledger, another: **CENTER.**\n\nAira: \"It rejects making him the center.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Sensible tower.\"\n\nThe shaft expanded with a low blue hum, then contracted, frost receding into the stone. Aira crossed to the nearest wall and laid pale blue support threads against it.\n\nAira: \"This storm interference is not passive. It fractures command signals, smears ownership tags, and blocks direct location pulls. Ordinary routes fail inside it.\"\n\nHaru: \"It can tell the difference between a question and a route.\"\n\nAira: \"It was built to.\"\n\nOzone and cold metal sharpened behind my sternum. Something beneath the floor recognized the thought and shoved an unasked memory up my spine: hands pressed against frost-rimmed stone, someone counting breaths in the dark. I stumbled back until my heel scraped ice.\n\nElira turned at once.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nHaru: \"Memory pressure. Not mine. It touched me, and I stopped.\"\n\nAira wrote it down while Maelin glanced up from her heat array.\n\nMaelin: \"Pain? Nausea? Sudden urge to become ancient infrastructure?\"\n\nHaru: \"None of the above.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Good. Report if that changes.\"\n\nElira's soft laugh rose into the central shaft, and every frost receiver in the walls chimed once like distant glass admitting spring existed. Ahead, a corridor opened as the Ice Ink map redrew itself into a vertical schematic with **ALLOWED?** glowing at the far end.\n\nMaelin shut her first satchel and lifted the second.\n\nMaelin: \"Warmth stations portable. We move.\"\n\nAira: \"No content first.\"\n\nElira: \"No location first.\"\n\nHaru: \"No lonely hero.\"\n\nElira didn't look back from the opening.\n\nElira: \"No protected witness behind glass.\"\n\nAira: \"No ledger entry without consent.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No patient treated as scenery.\"\n\nThe relay answered each condition with a clean flicker of frost, and we stepped into the corridor.\n\nThe passage sloped downward while feeling as though it climbed, walls lined with silent frost receivers. Elira held her palm near one without touching it. The receiver opened, letting out a broken whisper three times (*Ask if warmth... Ask if warmth... Ask if warmth...*) before an older northern voice replaced it:\n\n*Do not pull names through frost. Names freeze faster than water.*\n\nAira's pencil stilled.\n\nHaru: \"Do you recognize that voice?\"\n\nAira: \"Civil-service cadence. Old Valise staff, maybe. Not Lucien.\"\n\nElira: \"Not Noctis?\"\n\nAira: \"Noctis would phrase the warning like permission for supervision.\"\n\nThe corridor opened onto a narrow bridge spanning the central shaft. Broken mirror vanes jutted from the dark below, and one intact vane angled cleanly toward a hidden route. The Black Circle leaned toward it. I stopped.\n\nHaru: \"There is a route.\"\n\nAira: \"Do not identify it.\"\n\nHaru: \"I won't.\"\n\nElira's hand hovered near mine.\n\nElira: \"Want?\"\n\nHaru: \"I do.\"\n\nI looked straight at Elira without dodging.\n\nHaru: \"Refusing.\"\n\nAira wrote while walking, and Maelin glanced back.\n\nMaelin: \"Good. Keep refusing while moving. Stationary temptation gets dramatic.\"\n\nWhen I tried to pull level with Elira because her shoulder tightened, the bridge narrowed under my next boot. I stepped back into line behind her, and the stone widened again.\n\nAira: \"The haunted relay is enforcing spacing for ethical reasons.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Do not mock it. It may be listening.\"\n\nAira eyed the nearest wall.\n\nAira: \"Good. It should improve its signage.\"\n\nThe receiver beside her flashed a crisp **NO**. Aira gasped.\n\nAira: \"It is rude.\"\n\nFor the first time since the threshold, Elira laughed outright. The sound traveled down the passage, and far ahead, the word **ALLOWED?** brightened on Aira's map.\n\nBetween one step and the next, a side chamber appeared without a door, as if the tower had remembered an old office. Pale stone shelves lined the walls, and across a low table sat thin pale stone tokens, each carved with an open hand and a broken line.\n\nAira stopped so abruptly the bridge flickered behind us.\n\nAira: \"A record room. We do not need content, but if the relay is offering procedural history...\"\n\nMaelin: \"Forty-five seconds.\"\n\nAira slipped inside before the timer could drop further. Elira stopped at the threshold, and I held behind her. Using pale support threads, Aira lifted each token a finger-width off the stone without touching it. Frost letters rose across their faces:\n\n**NOT ALL RESCUE IS ARRIVAL.**\n\n**RETURN MUST BE WITNESSED.**\n\n**IF THE RESCUED CANNOT SPEAK, RECORD THE CONDITIONS THAT PROTECT SPEECH.**\n\nThe fourth token shifted on its own, showing two hands reaching toward each other with a cut line between them.\n\n**THE HAND NOT TAKEN MAY SAVE MORE THAN THE HAND THAT ACTS.**\n\nThe old cold behind my ribs tightened as if the line had been waiting for me.\n\nHaru: \"I see it.\"\n\nAira copied the four lines as Maelin called time, closed her threads, and stepped back onto the bridge. Behind us, the room faded back into unavailable stone. Maelin knelt on the bridge and unfolded her first portable heat array like a metal flower, releasing a slow amber warmth.\n\nMaelin: \"First station here. Warmth can be near before it is needed.\"\n\nIn the second corridor, old route rules glimmered along the stone: **NO SINGLE CARRIER. NO CLOSED DOORS. IF YES, ASK AGAIN WHEN CONDITIONS CHANGE.**\n\nAira's voice quieted as she copied them.\n\nAira: \"This place knew that rescue can become theft if it is proud enough.\"\n\nThe passage ended at a sealed frost arch. Beyond it, blue light trembled. On the Ice Ink map, **ALLOWED?** vanished, replaced by **NEAR. NOT TOUCH. WATCHED.**\n\nAira halted, staring at the third mark.\n\nAira: \"Watched by whom?\"\n\nElira's hand drifted near her pendant, then lowered.\n\nElira: \"Not all watching is the same.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Medical.\"\n\nAira: \"Predatory.\"\n\nHaru: \"Witness.\"\n\nUnder **WATCHED**, frost split into **MEDICAL. HOSTILE. WITNESS.** Then a fourth, fainter word formed: **SELF.**\n\nSera was watching us too, deciding from inside the cold whether every step we took could be trusted.\n\nMaelin set one satchel down.\n\nMaelin: \"Before the arch, everyone state limits.\"\n\nAira: \"Personal or procedural?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Both. I will not touch without consent unless immediate life threat is present, and if immediate danger arises I will name it plainly before moving.\"\n\nAira: \"I will not write testimony as conclusion. I will record silence as silence, no as no, and yes as changeable.\"\n\nElira placed an open hand near her pendant without covering it.\n\nElira: \"I will not make warmth proof of trust. I will stop when told.\"\n\nThey all looked at me.\n\nHaru: \"I will not open what has not opened to us.\"\n\n**SELF** brightened once. Beyond the frost, a very small, very real breath shifted.\n\nBefore we crossed, Elira reached inside her shawl and drew out a folded sheet torn from one of Mira's rough notebooks.\n\nAira: \"That was supposed to stay in the secondary pouch.\"\n\nElira: \"It did. Until now.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Is it a speech?\"\n\nElira unfolded it where the relay could read the uneven ink:\n\n*Extra socks at north gate if needed.*\n\n*Kitchen will keep broth hot until return.*\n\n*Pella says plant-fiber wraps itch less than wool if Sera hates wool.*\n\n*Finn says dates matter and he has dated the extra receipt copies.*\n\n*Mira says no one has to be brave to be counted.*\n\nThe arch listened line by line and widened on the final sentence. Aira swiped the back of her glove across her cheek.\n\nAira: \"I am going to pretend that is snow.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Inside?\"\n\nAira: \"A localized emotional weather event.\"\n\nElira tucked the page back into her shawl.\n\nElira: \"The room ahead should know we are not the only people near.\"\n\nMaelin lifted her satchel.\n\nMaelin: \"Now.\"\n\nWe crossed.\n\n---\n\nThe deeper chamber did not open like a room. It opened like a held breath.\n\nFrost folded back into the stone walls layer by layer, and Maelin began unpacking before the arch finished clearing.\n\nMaelin: \"Warmth station at threshold. No one crosses the inner frost line without stating purpose.\"\n\nA pale blue curve brightened across the floor six paces ahead. Aira stared at it.\n\nAira: \"I resent helpful architecture.\"\n\nElira knelt beside the first heat array, her left shoulder held unnaturally still beneath the shawl. The dark crystal at her throat did not glow, its seven open consent lines nested quietly inside. I stayed two steps behind Aira's ledger case and Elira's knees. Last had become method rather than rank: Maelin entered as medicine, Aira as record, Elira as offered warmth, and the Black Circle only after the children had met everyone else.\n\nElira glanced back.\n\nElira: \"Still with us?\"\n\nHaru: \"I can solve the room. I am choosing not to.\"\n\nAira logged it once.\n\nMaelin: \"Good. Patients first. Architecture later.\"\n\nAt the far side of the chamber, an alcove glowed. Two figures huddled inside it, the taller one folded around the smaller. Lysa Pell had wedged herself between Sera and the room even half-frozen, her Noctis uniform stripped of formal insignia in some places and overwritten in others. Behind her, Sera tucked her knees to her chest and hid her hands inside opposite sleeves, dark hair tangled near Lysa's shoulder.\n\nSeeing them made the Black Circle offer an immediate solution: crack the frost layer at three points, stabilize the smaller mana channels through the sternum, warm them from inside the bone. It would take less than a breath. I opened my hands.\n\nHaru: \"Record. I know a way to open the alcove.\"\n\nAira's head snapped toward me. Elira stayed still, already reading the shape of it in my posture.\n\nAira: \"Will the way respect conditions?\"\n\nI looked at Sera's hidden sleeves.\n\nHaru: \"No. I am not using it.\"\n\nThe first heat array pulsed amber.\n\nMaelin: \"Good.\"\n\nBehind the frost, Lysa's eyes opened with the slow, painful focus of someone dragged back from sleep by a sound she distrusted. Her gaze moved from Maelin's satchel to Aira's ledger, to Elira's pendant, and sharpened only when it found the space I had not crossed. Her lips moved, though no sound carried.\n\nAira lifted one hand.\n\nAira: \"No content pull.\"\n\nElira: \"Let the relay choose what crosses.\"\n\nThe frost clouded, cleared, and gave us one broken word.\n\nLysa: \"Last?\"\n\nShe was asking whether I had entered last.\n\nHaru: \"Last.\"\n\nThe pulse behind my ribs stayed quiet as Aira wrote the answer down. Frost around Lysa's hand thinned by a single line. Maelin placed both palms where Lysa could see them.\n\nMaelin: \"Lysa Pell. Senior Healer Maelin Orr. I am not touching the alcove. Medical warmth is near. Do you understand?\"\n\nLysa's eyes closed, then opened. One blink.\n\nMaelin: \"One blink recorded as possible understanding, not consent.\"\n\nBefore Maelin finished speaking, frost lifted between us and the children as if Lucien's residue had been waiting for a clinical opening. It gathered into a person-shaped absence wearing the hollow outline of a Noctis crest. Lucien's voice thinned through it, honed by distance and contempt.\n\nLucien: \"How touching. The Black Circle arrives with provisions.\"\n\nAira: \"Do not answer him.\"\n\nI held my tongue. The construct tilted its head, frost cracking along its neck like porcelain trying to pass for skin.\n\nLucien: \"No denial? No threat? No demonstration? You disappoint the myth, Aerlen.\"\n\nThe air drew tight, not with blades or bindings, but with classification hooks brushing my skin like barbs testing where I would bleed: Green, False, Black, Threat, Safeguard, Extra-classification, Unlicensed intervention.\n\nWorse, the wall receivers opened toward the pressure and began sorting names on their own. Elira caught it at the same instant I did.\n\nElira: \"No classification.\"\n\nAira: \"No classification. Sequence only.\"\n\nMaelin set her clinical slate down beside the Care Ledger.\n\nMaelin: \"Medical observation without identity assignment.\"\n\nThe receivers turned away from the category lights. The construct's smooth face angled toward them.\n\nLucien: \"You teach ruins to hesitate and call it morality.\"\n\nElira pushed herself upright from the heat array.\n\nMaelin: \"Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"I am standing, not casting.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Standing is often the beginning of worse habits.\"\n\nElira stepped beside me, not in front.\n\nElira: \"He is Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nThe hooks lost certainty. The air darkened between her and the construct.\n\nLucien: \"Still spending your name on weakness?\"\n\nElira: \"Still mistaking cruelty for intelligence?\"\n\nAira laid pale support threads along the threshold, facing us rather than the construct.\n\nAira: \"We answer Sera, not Lucien's residue.\"\n\nThe construct didn't walk. It edited the distance and appeared three paces closer. The inner frost line flared in warning.\n\nI stepped up to the line without crossing it, raising the Black Circle to my skin as a quiet boundary around the threshold station, Elira, Aira, Maelin, the ledger, and the heat array. Lightless feedback struck that wall and slid off like winter rain on glass. It ached behind my sternum and behind my eyes, in the old place that still wished stopping harm could be simple. Lucien's voice dropped softer, probing for a fracture.\n\nLucien: \"There. The celestial restraint. How long before mercy turns to vanity? How long before standing still in front of a child becomes your favorite mask?\"\n\nDark frost crept under my boots.\n\nElira did not touch me.\n\nElira: \"Open hand.\"\n\nI looked down. My gloved fingers had curled into a fist. I uncurled them, and Aira's pencil scratched hard across the page.\n\nAira: \"Haru Aerlen closes hand under hostile classification pressure. Elira Vale prompts open hand. Haru complies. No escalation.\"\n\nThe construct flickered as the relay accepted the record, and the dark frost under my boots broke into harmless lines. Aira opened the Care Ledger's front pouch and set Sela's ribbon bead on the page, absurdly small in the blue chamber for a trinket sent by a child who had never met Sera. Maelin laid Perrin Holt's dried apple packet beside it.\n\nAira: \"Sela's ribbon bead. No currency value. Full witness. Offered as warmth symbol, not access payment.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Perrin Holt. Consent logged. Food support. No command privilege.\"\n\nAira: \"Dried apples are now carrying more ethical structure than most Council decrees.\"\n\nThe construct turned toward the ledger.\n\nLucien: \"You think objects make you clean?\"\n\nAira: \"No. Records make attempts to lie more expensive.\"\n\nIrritation cracked across the construct's smoothness for the first time. Elira lowered herself back to the threshold, pale from the movement, both hands resting palms up on her knees.\n\nMaelin: \"Four minutes.\"\n\nElira: \"I heard you.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That has not historically guaranteed obedience.\"\n\nElira looked straight toward the alcove.\n\nElira: \"Sera. Warmth is near. You do not have to say where you are. You do not have to say who kept you cold. You do not have to touch us.\"\n\nFrost clouded from inside the alcove. Lysa's lips moved without sound crossing.\n\nAira: \"Relay is withholding content. Annoying, but good.\"\n\nElira: \"Is warmth allowed?\"\n\nNothing moved. My boundary held, the construct held, and silence settled with real weight.\n\nLucien: \"Silence is failure.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Silence is silence.\"\n\nAira: \"Recorded.\"\n\nElira: \"Sera, no is allowed. Silence is allowed. Warmth can remain near without touching.\"\n\nFour minutes ticked past. Maelin's healer charm flashed once against her cuff, and she did not call time. The relay noticed that too. Nobody pushed, nobody filled the quiet, and the waiting became the first real spell we cast.\n\nThen the alcove thawed by one finger-width from the inside.\n\nThe construct lunged, driving lightless cold toward the gap to turn small permission into an open route.\n\nI stepped into its path.\n\nThe impact punched through my ribs, and for one breath the tower vanished. Behind the construct I saw the whole path: frost mirror, relay shard, Noctis under-room, a hand lifted in white-gold light. I could ride that thread back and decide Lucien Voss's fate before he pulled his hand clear.\n\nHaru: \"Record.\"\n\nMy voice came out scraped raw.\n\nHaru: \"I can follow him. I am not following.\"\n\nElira's eyes shut for half a breath. One by one, the wall receivers turned away from the construct. Storm static flooded the chamber, fracturing Lucien's voice.\n\nLucien: \"You think refusal... makes you...\"\n\nAira: \"Less convenient? Accurate.\"\n\nMaelin shoved the heat array forward one handspan.\n\nMaelin: \"Sera, warmth remains near. Medical touch only if requested, or if you ask Lysa to ask for you.\"\n\nThe construct's laugh sounded like ice splitting over black water.\n\nLucien: \"You rehearse kindness as if the child can tell the difference.\"\n\nBehind the frost, Lysa's hand pressed flat against the barrier and curled one finger inward toward Sera. Not a signal to us. A proof inward that someone inside the cold was still choosing.\n\nElira: \"She can tell.\"\n\nLucien: \"Can she? Or does she hear a gentler command and call it rescue because the frost hurts?\"\n\nAira looked at the construct with every trace of humor scraped clean from her face.\n\nAira: \"Sera does not have to trust us for our restraint to count. She does not have to believe we are safe before we behave safely. Record: rescue conduct is not contingent on the rescued person's immediate trust. Trust is not purchasable.\"\n\nThe receivers opened toward Aira, and she swallowed hard before finishing.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nAira: \"Do not praise me while I am legally furious.\"\n\nLysa's voice came through in pieces, chosen by the relay rather than torn from her throat.\n\nLysa: \"She... heard.\"\n\nBehind Lysa, the smaller shape shifted. Frost melted in a circle no wider than a coin, and a child's voice came through, thin as thread.\n\nSera: \"Near. Not take?\"\n\nElira: \"Not take.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Not touch.\"\n\nAira: \"Not purchase.\"\n\nI forced air into my lungs.\n\nHaru: \"Not own.\"\n\nThe construct collapsed without spectacle, its hollow crest folding inward until nothing remained but loose frost on the stone.\n\nThe alcove opened from within. Lysa pitched forward. Maelin closed the distance in two strides and checked herself short of grabbing until Lysa's hand twitched upward in a weak signal of consent. Then Maelin caught her shoulders and wrapped three heat blankets around her. Lysa shook violently as warmth reached her, eyes open and unfocused.\n\nMaelin: \"Lysa Pell. Senior Healer Maelin Orr. You permitted touch. I am supporting your shoulders and warming your ribs.\"\n\nLysa drew a ragged breath that broke in the middle.\n\nMaelin: \"Messy is acceptable. Again.\"\n\nSera stayed inside the unsealed alcove, watching Maelin hold Lysa while leaving her completely untouched. Elira remained kneeling well past her limit, left shoulder trembling while Maelin silently kept watch. Sera's eyes moved over the bead, the apples, the heat array, the ledger, Elira's open hands, and finally settled on me standing behind the inner line.\n\nSera: \"Warmth can stay?\"\n\nElira: \"It can stay.\"\n\nSera: \"If I don't come?\"\n\nElira: \"Then warmth stays near.\"\n\nSera: \"If I sleep?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Sleeping is not consent to anything except being allowed to sleep.\"\n\nSera studied her for a long moment.\n\nSera: \"Noctis checks hands.\"\n\nMaelin's jaw tightened once.\n\nMaelin: \"Aurelis asks before touching hands unless there is immediate danger. If there is immediate danger, I will say so in plain words.\"\n\nSera turned her gaze on me.\n\nSera: \"Black Circle?\"\n\nThe room chilled in a different way. Elira did not rescue me from the question.\n\nHaru: \"I am.\"\n\nI kept both hands open and visible at my sides.\n\nSera tucked her fingers deeper into her sleeves.\n\nSera: \"Noctis says Black means all doors.\"\n\nHaru: \"Noctis lied.\"\n\nSera: \"Can you open me?\"\n\nHer words hit harder than Lucien's construct. I crouched slowly outside the inner frost line so I wasn't looming over her.\n\nHaru: \"Maybe. I am not going to.\"\n\nSera: \"Why?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because you are not a door.\"\n\nThe wall receivers chimed softly. Sera did not cry, only stared as if I had spoken a language she had forgotten existed. Aira reached into the supply pocket and set Iven's packet of boot sweets onto the ledger page.\n\nAira: \"Iven sent these. If you can receive them. If not, do not force it. His words.\"\n\nSera's face softened by a fraction, looking suddenly younger.\n\nSera: \"He still has the thermal charm?\"\n\nHaru: \"He remembered Cael lending it to him.\"\n\nLysa made a small, broken sound. Sera's lower lip trembled, and she bit it still.\n\nElira: \"You do not have to hold your face still here.\"\n\nSera looked deeply unconvinced, which was fair. Maelin finished tucking Lysa's blanket and turned slightly.\n\nMaelin: \"I need to know whether your body can leave the alcove safely. I can begin by looking. No touch. May I look?\"\n\nSera: \"You are already looking.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Correct. I need permission to look medically. That means assessing color, breathing, tremor, frost pattern, and whether your fingers are in danger. If you say no, I watch only for immediate emergency signs.\"\n\nSera waited to see if anyone would rush the silence. Nobody did.\n\nSera: \"Look.\"\n\nMaelin looked like a healer reading pain without claiming ownership of the patient.\n\nMaelin: \"Your fingers are in danger.\"\n\nSera pulled them deeper into her sleeves.\n\nMaelin: \"That was information, not a grab. Do they hurt?\"\n\nSera: \"No.\"\n\nMaelin's expression stayed completely level, which told me the answer was bad.\n\nMaelin: \"Can you feel them?\"\n\nSera: \"If I look.\"\n\nElira placed both hands palms up on her knees.\n\nElira: \"May I put warmth in the air between us? Not on you.\"\n\nSera: \"Can stop?\"\n\nElira: \"You can stop it.\"\n\nSera: \"Not you?\"\n\nElira: \"You.\"\n\nSera glanced at Maelin.\n\nMaelin: \"If stopping creates immediate danger, I will tell you the danger in plain words.\"\n\nSera's breath rattled in her chest.\n\nSera: \"Air.\"\n\nElira released a single thread of gold warmth into the space between them, no circle and no Yellow display, hovering like sunlight caught in cold water. Sera flinched. Elira cut it off instantly.\n\nAira: \"Warmth stopped by Sera's reaction.\"\n\nSera stared at the empty air.\n\nSera: \"Again.\"\n\nElira began slower. Warmth crept to the edge of the alcove and waited there. Sera's fingers twitched. A second later, pain struck and she gasped. Maelin leaned forward without touching.\n\nMaelin: \"Pain means sensation returning. Good and awful. Less warmth, same warmth, or stop?\"\n\nTears spilled over Sera's lashes and froze before reaching her chin.\n\nSera: \"Same.\"\n\nAira shifted the ledger closer to the heat array and drew three marks on a clean page.\n\nAira: \"Consent anchor: Sera. Warmth anchor: Elira Vale. Witness structure: Aira Solen, Care Ledger, Ice Ink, Maelin Orr clinical record.\"\n\nFrost answered between Elira and Sera, forming not a circle, but three open handprints glowing pale blue across the stone. My own hands stayed empty, and the ache of that settled into something steady. I held the boundary at the edge of the chamber: if Lucien pushed, I would hold; if the relay shifted, I would steady the threshold; but I would not become the cure.\n\nColor returned to Sera's fingers in uneven patches while Maelin talked her steadily through the sting. Then Elira's left shoulder began to shake.\n\nMaelin: \"Vale. Stop in ten seconds.\"\n\nSera's eyes widened in alarm.\n\nElira: \"Sera, I need to stop soon. Not because you did wrong. Because my body has a limit. Aira can keep the structure, Maelin can move the heat array closer if you allow, and I can begin again after rest.\"\n\nSera looked from the gold warmth to Elira's trembling shoulder.\n\nSera: \"Stop.\"\n\nElira stopped. The warmth vanished, leaving the three open handprints glowing between them. Sera cried harder.\n\nMaelin: \"That was well done.\"\n\nSera: \"I stopped it.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You did.\"\n\nSera: \"She didn't leave.\"\n\nElira managed a tired smile.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nAira's page blurred, and she swiped her sleeve across her eyes, glaring at the air as if blaming tower ventilation. I looked down at my empty hands. For once, emptiness felt like keeping faith.\n\nThen the relay floor flashed.\n\nLightless script crawled across the frost near the exit:\n\nBLACK CIRCLE EXTRACTION EVENT PENDING CLASSIFICATION.\n\nLysa went rigid beneath her blankets. Sera drew back behind the three glowing handprints, tucking her hands deep inside her sleeves. Elira's warmth flared instinctively at her throat before she checked it, pale and still kneeling at the edge of the station.\n\nAira: \"Oh, absolutely not.\"\n\nThe relay had opened the alcove by consent. Lucien had found the record.\n\n## Coda: Sera's Stop\n\nIn the brief stillness before anyone moved to answer the exit script, Sera tested the word once more.\n\nElira remained kneeling beside the heat array, letting her breath settle while her left shoulder trembled faintly beneath the shawl. Beside the alcove, Lysa lay wrapped in three heat blankets, eyes fixed on the frost-script near the doorway.\n\nSera stared down at her own sleeves.\n\nSera: \"Stop.\"\n\nMagic and warmth stayed still. Maelin paused with a salve tin half-opened in her hand, looking up without hurrying to interpret the word before it could belong to the child.\n\nMaelin: \"Stopped.\"\n\nSera flinched at the plain answer, then frowned as if the word had misbehaved by not turning into punishment. Lysa whispered from beneath her blankets.\n\nLysa: \"Again?\"\n\nSera swallowed.\n\nSera: \"Stop.\"\n\nNothing flared. Nothing grabbed. Aira held her pencil steady off the page until Sera finished looking at it. Across the inner frost line, I stayed where she could see me not entering, the Black Circle quiet beneath my ribs.\n\nSera tucked her warming hands deeper inside her sleeves beside the three glowing handprints. The word stayed hers.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-32-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep33","label":"Episode 27","number":"27","file":"episode-33-cover.webp","fallback":"The Quiet Place","eyebrow":"Healing","recap":"","accent":["#14532d","#f8fafc","#60a5fa"],"displayLabel":"Episode 27","displayNumber":"27","displayEyebrow":"Public Witness","arcLabel":"Public Witness","title":"The Hand That Was Not Taken","body":"The lie began before anyone was warm enough to leave.\n\nBLACK CIRCLE EXTRACTION EVENT PENDING CLASSIFICATION.\n\nThe words crawled across the frost near the chamber exit in a clean official hand. Worse than Lucien's handwriting, it looked like every form that had ever pretended a person was a category for their own protection.\n\nFor one second, no one moved, and the phrase brightened. It had enough true pieces to become dangerous: I was Black Circle, I was present, Sera and Lysa were Noctis students, and the alcove had opened after I held a boundary across a contested northern road. Lucien's cruelty rarely invented from nothing. Arrangement was harder to deny.\n\nAira stepped toward the script with a pencil in one hand and murder in every line of her posture.\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nThe word did nothing. The record did not care about anger unless anger became sequence.\n\nElira was still kneeling. The gold warmth she had stopped at Sera's command had vanished, leaving a tremor in her left shoulder and a pallor Maelin's eyes kept snapping back to between clinical checks.\n\nElira: \"Do not answer the sentence as written.\"\n\nAira froze.\n\nElira: \"If we fight extraction, it keeps extraction as the frame.\"\n\nLysa Pell lifted her head from where Maelin had wrapped her in heat. Her lips were blue, her lashes still clumped with frost, yet her eyes sharpened.\n\nLysa: \"Sequence.\"\n\nMaelin's hand paused at her wrist.\n\nMaelin: \"You are not required to speak.\"\n\nLysa: \"I know.\"\n\nShe said it with steady, deliberate knowledge, swallowed, and tried again.\n\nLysa: \"It starts before Haru. Before the door. Sera asked near. We answered near.\"\n\nSera had pulled back when the script appeared. The three open handprints still glowed between her and Elira, but she was curled behind them now, small hands hidden in sleeves, eyes fixed on the official words as if they had teeth.\n\nSera: \"Not take.\"\n\nAira's pencil hit the page.\n\nAira: \"Exit record correction begins. Initial condition: Sera permitted warmth near. No location requested. No touch before consent. No extraction frame accepted.\"\n\nThe phrase on the floor flickered. Maelin lifted her clinical slate.\n\nMaelin: \"Medical note. Lysa Pell consented to touch. Sera declined touch, later permitted air-warmth, then stopped it. Stop respected. No forced removal. No direct Black Circle stabilization.\"\n\nThe script thinned, then darkened into a new line: PRIVATE BLACK CIRCLE INCURSION.\n\nMy hands wanted to close. I opened them before Elira had to ask.\n\nHaru: \"Correction. Haru Aerlen entered last.\"\n\nAira wrote without looking up.\n\nHaru: \"Maelin Orr entered first as medical warmth. Aira Solen entered second with Care Ledger and Ice Ink. Elira Vale entered third. Haru Aerlen entered after securing horses and carriage.\"\n\nThe relay carried each sentence into the floor. Frost lines spread beneath the hostile script, refusing to let it stand alone. Lucien had learned better than to speak where Aira could quote him; the attack came as formal logic, hunting for a column with my name at the top.\n\nSera stared at the writing.\n\nSera: \"If I don't say, will it take my quiet?\"\n\nMaelin's healer focus sharpened into silence. Aira answered first, very softly.\n\nAira: \"Not if we record the quiet correctly.\"\n\nSera looked at her as if that were either impossible or a trick. Aira turned to a clean page.\n\nAira: \"Sera declines full testimony. Protected silence recorded as choice, not absence.\"\n\nThe relay accepted it. I felt the change like a door refusing a lock. Sera breathed out, then whispered.\n\nSera: \"Warmth was allowed.\"\n\nThe hostile script cracked through the middle. Lysa closed her eyes.\n\nLysa: \"I confirm.\"\n\nMaelin tucked her blanket tighter.\n\nMaelin: \"Confirmation accepted. No further speech required.\"\n\nThe floor cleared for one moment before a new sentence appeared:\n\nUNAUTHORIZED REMOVAL OF NOCTIS DEPENDENTS.\n\nAira made a sound half laugh, half fury.\n\nAira: \"Dependents. That is the word they picked.\"\n\nElira pushed herself upright.\n\nMaelin: \"Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"I am moving three inches.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Your body and I disagree on what an inch is.\"\n\nElira stayed seated on the low bench edge anyway, pale and stubborn.\n\nElira: \"Lysa?\"\n\nLysa opened her eyes.\n\nLysa: \"Noctis moved us first. Unregistered. Relay custody. No dorm record. No healer record. No guardian notice. I carried the whisper-channel because Sera could not answer where she was cold. I requested warmth conditions. I did not request Noctis retrieval.\"\n\nAira wrote fast enough her pencil nearly tore the paper. The floor shuddered, and Valise's own marks lit beneath the hostile sentence:\n\nWEATHER INTERFERENCE CONFIRMS NO CLEAN NOCTIS CLAIM.\n\nMaelin glanced up at the frost receivers.\n\nMaelin: \"Good tower.\"\n\nOne receiver chimed, very faintly. Aira stared.\n\nAira: \"Did it just enjoy praise?\"\n\nElira: \"Focus.\"\n\nAira: \"I am focused and unsettled.\"\n\nThe hostile script retreated toward the exit, waiting for movement. The lie still had to be walked out of the room. Maelin's face shifted into pure triage.\n\nMaelin: \"We leave in sequence. Lysa first if she can stand with support. Sera only by chosen method. Elira sits before she becomes a second patient.\"\n\nElira: \"I am already a patient.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Do not compete.\"\n\nSera looked at the floating stretcher Maelin unfolded from her satchel. The low blue frame shimmered with soft straps hanging open, and her whole body went rigid. Maelin stopped unfolding at once.\n\nMaelin: \"No straps unless you ask or unless I explain immediate danger.\"\n\nSera: \"Noctis says straps keep channels from spilling.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Noctis says many medically obscene things.\"\n\nSera blinked, and Lysa let out a cracked sound that might have been a laugh.\n\nMaelin: \"A strap can keep a patient from falling during movement, or it can become restraint. Here, you choose first. If I think falling may harm you, I will say so.\"\n\nSera stared at the stretcher.\n\nSera: \"Can blanket hold instead?\"\n\nMaelin weighed the stretcher against the stack of heat blankets.\n\nMaelin: \"Yes. Less stable and more work for us, but medically acceptable for the first corridor.\"\n\nSera: \"Blanket.\"\n\nAira wrote the choice down. Elira reached for the edge of the heat blanket and stopped an inch short.\n\nElira: \"May I help fold it?\"\n\nSera looked at Elira's hands, then at her trembling shoulder.\n\nSera: \"Aira.\"\n\nElira's face softened without a trace of hurt.\n\nElira: \"Good choice.\"\n\nAira folded the blanket under Maelin's instruction, narrating every motion so cleanly the relay itself seemed to lean closer.\n\nAira: \"Left edge under. No wrap around wrists. No closure. Blanket support only. Sera may release at any time.\"\n\nSera let the blanket come near, then touch her knees, then her shoulders. When Aira's hand brushed Sera's sleeve by accident, Sera flinched. Aira froze so fast the pale support threads vanished from her fingers.\n\nAira: \"Accidental sleeve contact. Stopping.\"\n\nSera stared at her.\n\nAira: \"Sorry.\"\n\nPlain information, no performance. Sera gave one small nod and let Aira continue.\n\nPreparing her for transit took twenty-three minutes. Aira timed it so fear could never compress it later. Maelin adjusted heat by fractions and explained each change twice; Lysa kept trying to sit up whenever Sera's breath hitched until Maelin ordered her to stay wrapped; Elira kept both hands resting in her lap while her shoulder shook; and I stood by the wall with open hands, learning that standing back could still be a way of holding ground.\n\nThe three open handprints remained in the frost: Sera's consent, Elira's warmth, Aira's witness structure. Every time the blue light caught the empty fourth space beside them, the relay seemed to make me look at it. Good.\n\nMaelin slid the diagnostic charm one finger-width closer to Sera's blanket.\n\nMaelin: \"This reads tremor through cloth. It does not read thoughts, or truth, or whether you are good.\"\n\nSera's eyes flicked to her.\n\nSera: \"Why say good?\"\n\nMaelin's face stayed steady and unsentimental.\n\nMaelin: \"Because children used as routes are often told their bodies are behaving well or badly depending on how useful they are.\"\n\nSera stared, and beside her Lysa shut her eyes against an old memory.\n\nMaelin: \"Your body is cold, injured, and frightened. It is not good or bad.\"\n\nSera looked down at her hidden hands.\n\nSera: \"If fingers don't work, Noctis says channel discipline failed.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Noctis can submit its medical opinions to a fireplace.\"\n\nAira choked on a laugh, and Elira let out a quiet breath caught between amusement and pain. Sera blinked twice.\n\nSera: \"Fireplace accepts?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Eagerly.\"\n\nFor the first time, Sera's mouth shifted as if trying to remember how a smile worked. It did not quite arrive, yet the cold room made space for the attempt. Aira wrote in her ledger: Sera responds to noncompliance humor, no demand attached. She paused, frowned, crossed out responds, and wrote Sera hears instead. Overhead, one frost receiver chimed.\n\nAira: \"I am being graded by a tower.\"\n\nElira: \"You improved.\"\n\nAira: \"That is worse.\"\n\nLysa shifted under her blankets, her voice thin but clearer.\n\nLysa: \"Valise likes corrections.\"\n\nAira leaned in slightly.\n\nAira: \"You know the relay?\"\n\nMaelin: \"She is not required to brief us.\"\n\nLysa: \"I can say this.\"\n\nMaelin waited while Lysa drew a careful breath.\n\nLysa: \"Noctis told us Valise was abandoned because old northern systems were inefficient. Too much weather, too many consent locks, too many routes that broke when command words entered. Lucien said obsolete mercy leaves useful doors unattended.\"\n\nThe frost receivers dimmed in quiet witness. Elira's eyes sharpened.\n\nElira: \"He knew the relay preserved consent grammar.\"\n\nLysa nodded once.\n\nLysa: \"He wanted to make it prove consent could be bypassed if the child was cold enough.\"\n\nSera pulled the blanket tighter around her shoulders. Heat spiked behind my ribs, sharp with the memory of Lucien's mirror and black crystal pane.\n\nHaru: \"Record.\"\n\nAira was already writing while watching my face.\n\nHaru: \"Anger spike at Lucien's Valise intent. No action.\"\n\nElira held my gaze until the pressure eased, then turned back to Lysa.\n\nElira: \"Thank you. No more if it costs too much.\"\n\nSera did not look at any of us when she whispered next.\n\nSera: \"He said if I said yes, it counted forever.\"\n\nAira's pencil stopped. Maelin's hand went still above the charm.\n\nSera: \"If I said yes to one heat line, yes to channel check. If yes to channel check, yes to relay hold. If yes to relay hold, yes to Noctis recovery. If yes once, yes stayed.\"\n\nElira's face turned to the kind of calm that broke old systems.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nSera flinched, and Elira softened her tone without softening a word of the truth.\n\nElira: \"No. Here yes can stop.\"\n\nSera's eyes lifted.\n\nElira: \"You stopped my warmth. I stopped.\"\n\nSera: \"Because Maelin said?\"\n\nElira: \"Because you said.\"\n\nSera glanced at Maelin for confirmation.\n\nMaelin: \"I also enjoyed being obeyed, but Miss Vale stopped because you told her to.\"\n\nAira wrote: Yes can stop reaffirmed.\n\nThe three open handprints brightened. Then the empty fourth space in the frost shifted. I braced for it to demand my palm. Instead, letters formed across the blank ice: HAND NOT TAKEN. The relay was not praising me. It was recording the absence.\n\nSera read the words slowly.\n\nSera: \"That one is his?\"\n\nElira let her hands rest quietly on her knees.\n\nElira: \"Yes. He kept out of it.\"\n\nSera: \"Because he didn't?\"\n\nHaru: \"Because I didn't.\"\n\nMy voice came out quieter than I expected. The Black Circle settled behind my ribs, witnessed rather than concealed. Sera stared at the unmarked space.\n\nSera: \"Can not-doing count?\"\n\nAira's face crumpled for half a second before professionalism rescued it. She poised her pencil over the margin.\n\nAira: \"Every time we let it.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Often.\"\n\nElira: \"Especially when doing would take too much.\"\n\nSera absorbed that like a language Noctis had never allowed her to study, where refusal and waiting counted as real things.\n\nNear the exit, the hostile script pulsed again: BLACK CIRCLE EXTRACTION EVENT PENDING CLASSIFICATION. The letters looked thinner now against the new evidence on the floor.\n\nWe began walking. Maelin and Aira carried Sera's blanket cradle between them, Lysa walked beside Maelin with one hand on a floating guide rail, Elira followed behind them, and I walked last.\n\nAt the first corridor, frost wrote: BLACK CIRCLE LED PARTY.\n\nAira did not break stride.\n\nAira: \"Correction repeated. Maelin first, Aira second, Elira third, Haru last. Current exit order: medical support first, witness record alongside, Haru last boundary.\"\n\nThe words paled, though they did not vanish.\n\nAira: \"Hostile phrase weakened, not erased.\"\n\nLysa looked over from the rail.\n\nLysa: \"Lucien likes weakened lies.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Save your breath.\"\n\nLysa: \"This is useful.\"\n\nMaelin's jaw tightened, then she gave one sharp nod.\n\nLysa: \"If a lie stays small, people can grow it later. Noctis files weak phrases into side records and waits until everyone is tired, then calls them prior concern.\"\n\nAira wrote fast.\n\nAira: \"Potential future misuse: weakened hostile classification may be revived as prior concern.\"\n\nElira's voice came from behind the blanket cradle.\n\nElira: \"Then add why it weakened.\"\n\nAira: \"Distributed entry, consent record, medical notes.\"\n\nElira: \"And Haru last.\"\n\nMy throat tightened before I could stop it. Elira did not look back.\n\nElira: \"Not because last makes him harmless. Because last proves the rescue did not require his centrality.\"\n\nThe frost under my boots brightened once, and the hostile phrase dimmed another degree. Sera watched the floor pass beneath them.\n\nSera: \"If it stays, will it follow?\"\n\nAira answered honestly.\n\nAira: \"Maybe.\"\n\nMaelin shot her a look.\n\nAira: \"What? It might. I am not lying to the child inside the haunted ethics tower.\"\n\nSera accepted that faster than false comfort.\n\nSera: \"Then we write when it follows.\"\n\nAira's face softened.\n\nAira: \"Exactly.\"\n\nBeside us, old relay script surfaced on the stone wall:\n\nWEAK RECORDS REQUIRE RETURNING WITNESSES.\n\nAt the bridge over the central shaft, another hostile phrase flared across the frost:\n\nWARMTH USED TO INDUCE COMPLIANCE.\n\nSera turned her head inside the blanket cradle, gray with exhaustion, voice barely audible.\n\nSera: \"No.\"\n\nEveryone stopped. Sera's fingers tightened in the blanket.\n\nSera: \"Warmth waited.\"\n\nThe bridge lit beneath her words, and the hostile phrase shattered across the ice.\n\nAt the mirror stair, dark glass tried to show us wrong: me striding first with Sera in my arms while the others trailed behind like clerks, Elira glowing gold while Sera knelt at her feet, Aira clutching the Care Ledger like a deed of ownership. Every false reflection offered a cleaner, simpler lie than the mess we were actually carrying down the mountain. I looked at none of them. The Black Circle could have shattered every pane in the stairwell, and keeping my hands still hurt more than breaking glass would have.\n\nHaru: \"Record. False reflections present. I want to erase them. I am not erasing them.\"\n\nAira: \"Recorded. We answer with sequence, not force.\"\n\nElira's voice drifted back from ahead, thin with fatigue.\n\nElira: \"The truth does not need to be prettier than the lie.\"\n\nThe mirrors clouded over.\n\nAt the entrance platform, the final script waited: RESCUE CLAIMED BY EXTRA-CLASSIFICATION EVENT. Cleverer than an accusation, it tried to crown me into ownership. I looked past it at Sera.\n\nHaru: \"Who opened the alcove?\"\n\nShe watched me from the blanket cradle.\n\nSera: \"I did.\"\n\nThe hostile phrase broke across the center. The outer door swung inward, and wind hit us like the mountain had saved all its anger for the exit.\n\nSnow tore across the threshold. Maelin swore under her breath sharply enough to make Aira's eyes widen despite the storm.\n\nMaelin: \"Move.\"\n\nOutside, the carriage sat half-buried in white drift, the horses stamping and tossing their heads in deep offense at our survival taking so long. Snow tore sideways across the plateau, stinging every inch of exposed skin. I kept the Black Circle tight against my coat, a passive windbreak around the group rather than a dome or a claim, just enough to keep the worst gusts from turning injured children into proof that we should have used more force earlier.\n\nAira shouted over the gale.\n\nAira: \"Recording passive boundary. No route trace. No offensive action.\"\n\nHaru: \"Understood.\"\n\nElira's boot slipped on crusted snow, and I caught her elbow before she fell. She leaned into my shoulder for one breath.\n\nElira: \"Support.\"\n\nHaru: \"Support.\"\n\nShe found her footing and moved on. Loading the carriage took long minutes Maelin refused to rush, explaining every shift to Sera while Aira logged what Sera permitted. Lysa refused my hand, took Elira's, and climbed in beside Maelin. Iven's boot sweets stayed visible in the travel case; Perrin's apples stayed logged because Sera wanted soup before solid food. I climbed to the driver's bench last, reins stiff with ice against my wrist ribbon.\n\nThe road down from Valise was steep, narrow, and mean. Twice old relay pressure tugged at the wheels as if testing whether I would seize control; twice I held the boundary passive while Aira amended the exit record inside and Maelin told everyone to stop making paperwork acrobatic.\n\nBy the time Whispering Pass Gate appeared, dusk had turned the snow blue.\n\nCaptain Valech stood beneath the arch with Tessa Orlin and Perrin Holt, smaller than the mountains and steadier than the Council lock sparking on the gate mechanism. Perrin ran forward first.\n\nPerrin: \"You came back.\"\n\nI drew the horses to a halt.\n\nHaru: \"We came back.\"\n\nTessa was already at the carriage door.\n\nTessa: \"Medical?\"\n\nMaelin pushed it open from inside.\n\nMaelin: \"Soup first. Questions after heat.\"\n\nValech looked from me to the carriage, then up the dark pass behind us.\n\nValech: \"Did the relay release them?\"\n\nAira leaned out, hair escaping every pin, eyes red with cold and relief.\n\nAira: \"The relay permitted exit under corrected witness record. Please say that exactly or I will become unkind.\"\n\nValech nodded once.\n\nValech: \"Perrin. Write.\"\n\nPerrin fumbled his slate, repeating the line aloud as his stylus scratched.\n\nPerrin: \"Valise return recorded. Relay permitted exit under corrected witness record. Rescued students present. Medical warmth continuing. No forced transfer observed.\"\n\nTessa brought soup in dented metal cups, handing the first to Maelin rather than me. Maelin passed one straight into the carriage to Sera before stepping down. Perrin eyed the travel case.\n\nPerrin: \"The apples?\"\n\nAira opened the ledger with hands that shook only now that danger had turned administrative.\n\nAira: \"Entered. Used as warmth symbol. Not access payment.\"\n\nPerrin's eyes reddened.\n\nPerrin: \"Good.\"\n\nValech walked to the post and signed the gate log. The Council lock sparked once against his wrist, and he signed anyway. Behind the carriage window, Sera watched him over the rim of the soup cup she held with both hands.\n\nSera: \"He opened gate?\"\n\nElira answered beside her.\n\nElira: \"He did.\"\n\nSera: \"Not Haru?\"\n\nElira looked through the glass at me.\n\nElira: \"Not Haru.\"\n\nThe hostile exit phrase did not return, and the gate opened onto the south road.\n\n---\n\nWe did not leave Whispering Pass quickly.\n\nMaelin turned the gatehouse into a field clinic before Valech finished signing the return log. The room smelled of old smoke, wet wool, iron tools, and soup kept warm too long. Tessa cleared a maintenance table with one sweep of her arm, while Perrin nearly dropped a stack of permit slates trying to help.\n\nSera watched from inside her blanket cradle. She had allowed the carriage, the soup cup, and the gatehouse, but refused stretcher straps, gloves, and a second blanket over her hands. Maelin knelt before her with a clinical slate balanced on one knee.\n\nMaelin: \"Fingers?\"\n\nSera looked down at them.\n\nSera: \"Pain.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Good and awful.\"\n\nSera gave one small nod, filing the words away.\n\nMaelin: \"More warmth, same warmth, less warmth?\"\n\nSera's eyes moved to Elira, sitting on a bench across the room with her shoulder wrapped and both hands around her own cup.\n\nElira: \"You choose.\"\n\nSera: \"Same.\"\n\nMaelin turned the heat charm one notch lower so the output matched a warming body without scorching her, explaining why as she adjusted it.\n\nAt the gatehouse desk, Aira sat surrounded by the Care Ledger, Ice Ink, Valech's pass log, Tessa's maintenance slate, and three scraps of paper.\n\nAira: \"I need everyone to stop moving records independently unless they enjoy becoming a filing incident.\"\n\nTessa: \"Road logs are not your ledger.\"\n\nAira: \"Today they are emotionally adjacent.\"\n\nTessa stared. Perrin leaned in, murmuring.\n\nPerrin: \"She means cross-reference.\"\n\nAira pointed at him without looking up.\n\nAira: \"Perrin Holt understands survival.\"\n\nNear the door, Valech watched the frosted pass window. His hands trembled slightly where he thought no one noticed; signing his own name to that log gave Krail plenty of procedural hooks to pull later.\n\nHaru: \"Captain.\"\n\nValech turned.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\nValech: \"Do not make it noble.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then thank you for doing your job.\"\n\nHe gave one curt nod.\n\nValech: \"That I can survive.\"\n\nOutside, the Council lock on the gate sparked red against the snow.\n\nTessa: \"It has been doing that since you came through.\"\n\nAira: \"It is upset we made verbs without its permission.\"\n\nPerrin eyed the window nervously.\n\nPerrin: \"Will it report?\"\n\nValech pulled his fur collar higher.\n\nValech: \"It already is.\"\n\nElira set her cup down carefully.\n\nElira: \"Then the report should arrive after ours.\"\n\nAira's exhaustion vanished in the way that meant some distant clerk was about to suffer.\n\nAira: \"Much better.\"\n\nMaelin: \"If this delays medical transfer, no.\"\n\nAira: \"It accelerates paperwork, which is physically different and spiritually satisfying.\"\n\nThey worked fast before Krail's archive could form a frame. Valech sent the municipal pass log, Tessa filed the non-treasury gate maintenance note, Perrin logged the dried apple consent record, Aira sent the Care Ledger summary, Maelin underlined \"delayed warmth increases preventable harm\" across her clinical transfer notice, Elira added that protected silence was not evidentiary absence, and I logged entering last as passive boundary only.\n\nWhen Aira transmitted the packet through Senna's witness office, the relay crystal flickered with a brief taste of lightless cold. Sera's soup cup trembled.\n\nElira: \"Lucien?\"\n\nHaru: \"An echo, or someone trained by him.\"\n\nLysa opened one eye from beneath three blankets.\n\nLysa: \"He hates bad columns.\"\n\nAira looked over.\n\nAira: \"What?\"\n\nLysa: \"Lucien. Clean columns. One person, one reason, one weakness. He claims messy records hide rot. He is wrong, but he hates them.\"\n\nShe closed her eye again. Aira surveyed the paper clutter covering the desk and smiled.\n\nAira: \"Good.\"\n\nThe transmission sent. Outside, the Council lock stopped sparking. Not defeated, only forced to lie with effort.\n\nMaelin checked Sera's pulse again through the cloth diagnostic charm, which Sera had permitted \"until soup ends.\"\n\nMaelin: \"We cannot stay until every desk exhausts itself.\"\n\nElira: \"No, but we can make leaving part of the record.\"\n\nValech pulled yellowed route slips from a wall cabinet: the northern emergency departure log. Tessa listed road conditions; Perrin wrote \"soup transfer,\" crossed it out for \"portable patient warmth support,\" and had to uncross \"soup\" under Maelin's glare. Aira entered the Care Ledger code, Elira added protected transit silence, and I signed as driver.\n\nSera watched every stroke.\n\nSera: \"Why write soup?\"\n\nPerrin froze with his pencil lifted. Elira set her cup down before answering.\n\nElira: \"Because if someone later says you were moved only as evidence, the record should show you were also cold and fed.\"\n\nSera's brow furrowed.\n\nSera: \"Evidence does not eat.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Exactly.\"\n\nSera thought that over with fierce seriousness.\n\nSera: \"Then write cup.\"\n\nAira's face softened.\n\nAira: \"Write cup.\"\n\nPerrin wrote: Sera retained soup cup by choice. Sera watched the letters settle, then nodded.\n\nLysa murmured from under her blankets, eyes still shut.\n\nLysa: \"Noctis records cups as inventory. Aurelis records yours as yours.\"\n\nThe stove clicked in the quiet that followed. Snow hissed against the frosted window, and somewhere above the gatehouse roof the portcullis chain creaked in the wind. Then Maelin cleared her throat with brisk authority.\n\nMaelin: \"Sentiment complete. Patients travel.\"\n\nSera tightened both hands around the dented metal.\n\nMaelin: \"Cup travels too.\"\n\nThe cup entered the departure record alongside her name.\n\nWe left into the winter dark once Maelin judged Sera stable enough for a slow, warm ride. Tessa packed extra soup in sealed jars; Perrin slipped in a second apple packet and insisted Aira write \"not leverage\" twice. As the portcullis groaned upward, Sera looked out the window.\n\nSera: \"Gate opens loud.\"\n\nElira: \"It does.\"\n\nSera: \"Good.\"\n\nNo one asked why.\n\nBelow the pine line, Sera asked to have the carriage lamp moved. It hung from a roof hook, swinging amber light over blankets and pages whenever the wheels hit a rut.\n\nSera: \"Too high.\"\n\nMaelin glanced up.\n\nMaelin: \"Too bright?\"\n\nSera: \"Too high.\"\n\nElira waited without interrupting. Sera swallowed.\n\nSera: \"Noctis lamps high mean inspection.\"\n\nMaelin reached up carefully and shifted the lamp down to the side bracket.\n\nMaelin: \"Lowering lamp. Same light. Different height.\"\n\nWith the shadows dropped lower, the carriage felt less like an examination room and more like ordinary shelter in bad weather. Sera's shoulders sank a fraction while Aira wrote Lamp lowered by Sera request, adding no challenge twice at Lysa's drowsy insistence.\n\nSera: \"If I tell, it changes?\"\n\nMaelin secured the bracket hook so it would not swing.\n\nMaelin: \"If it can safely change, it changes. If not, we explain why.\"\n\nSera: \"Noctis says why after.\"\n\nAira: \"Aurelis is trying to learn before.\"\n\nLater, the front window slid open two inches.\n\nMaelin: \"Who authorized cold air?\"\n\nSera: \"Me.\"\n\nElira leaned toward the gap.\n\nElira: \"What would you like to ask?\"\n\nSera looked straight at me on the driver's bench.\n\nSera: \"If he wanted to open the gate.\"\n\nThe reins creaked under my gloved fingers.\n\nHaru: \"I wanted to.\"\n\nSera: \"But didn't.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nSera: \"Because gate not enemy.\"\n\nHaru: \"Because Valech's hands belonged to him.\"\n\nThe cold pulse behind my ribs stayed quiet, and Sera shut the window.\n\nThe descent through the lower pass took hours of deep night. Near midnight, we reached the first Aurelis outer relay post, where Rhea's remote crystal was already waiting above the snow marker.\n\nRhea: \"Status.\"\n\nAira leaned through the window.\n\nAira: \"Alive. Exhausted. Consent record intact. Correction packet sent ahead of the lock report. Haru last and annoyingly compliant.\"\n\nRhea's eyes flicked toward me.\n\nRhea: \"Annoyingly compliant is acceptable.\"\n\nHaru: \"Good evening, President.\"\n\nRhea: \"Do not become charming at altitude.\"\n\nElira's soft laugh escaped the carriage into the cold dark, and Rhea's expression eased by one disciplined fraction.\n\nRhea: \"Krail has requested immediate review upon arrival. Senna received your packet and agrees: medical transfer first, public sequence second.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I agree louder.\"\n\nBefore the crystal dimmed, Sera spoke up from inside.\n\nSera: \"President?\"\n\nRhea stilled.\n\nRhea: \"Listening.\"\n\nSera: \"Can safe be not quiet?\"\n\nRhea's face softened slightly.\n\nRhea: \"Safe can be noisy if you choose.\"\n\nSera nodded once, and we drove on into the night.\n\nAn hour past the relay post, the temptation came back worse than inside Valise. Inside the tower, the frost walls had enforced grammar; out here, only carriage wood separated me from Sera's damaged channels, Lysa's uneven breathing, and Elira's aching shoulder. I could have reached through the dark, mapped every tremor, and mended what hurt faster than any heater charm. I loosened my grip on the reins instead.\n\nHaru: \"Record.\"\n\nThe front window slid open two inches.\n\nAira: \"I knew your shoulders were doing something literary.\"\n\nHaru: \"I want to listen into the carriage. I am not listening.\"\n\nThe scratch of pencil stopped. Elira's voice carried cleanly through the small opening.\n\nElira: \"Logged?\"\n\nAira: \"Haru Aerlen states desire to extend perception into patient carriage and refuses.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Add ordinary driver hearing remains permitted so he stops acting tragic about hooves.\"\n\nSera's small voice reached the gap.\n\nSera: \"He says wants?\"\n\nElira: \"He did.\"\n\nSera: \"Why tell?\"\n\nI answered over my shoulder while keeping my eyes on the snow-lit road.\n\nHaru: \"So wanting does not become secret permission.\"\n\nSilence held for several wheel turns.\n\nSera: \"Noctis says if you want enough, that is destiny.\"\n\nMy mouth went dry.\n\nElira: \"Noctis lied.\"\n\nHaru: \"Destiny is smaller than thrones. It starts whenever wanting becomes enough reason.\"\n\nThe carriage went quiet. Then Sera spoke again.\n\nSera: \"So write wants.\"\n\nAira's pencil moved after a pause.\n\nAira: \"So we write wants.\"\n\nMaelin shut the window because philosophy was drafty.\n\nAt the second relay post, Krail's challenge caught up as a red notice glowing across the route crystal:\n\nPROVISIONAL QUERY: CONFIRM WHETHER EXTRA-CLASSIFICATION EVENT WAS NECESSARY TO MINOR TRANSFER.\n\nI halted the horses. Inside, Aira groaned.\n\nAira: \"Necessary. If we say yes, Haru becomes essential to every rescue. If we say no, they ask why he was there.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Answer neither.\"\n\nElira: \"Answer condition.\"\n\nAira leaned out with the Care Ledger open.\n\nAira: \"Response: Transfer depended on distributed conditions. Medical authority, Sera's permission, relay weather grammar, Care Ledger support, gate records, and passive boundary all contributed. No singular necessity assigned.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Add transfer would have been impossible if obstructed by continued procedural delay.\"\n\nAira smiled sharply.\n\nAira: \"That sentence has teeth.\"\n\nMaelin: \"It has molars.\"\n\nAs the transmission sent, another line tried to surface beneath the red notice: BLACK CIRCLE PRESENCE CONFIRMED.\n\nHaru: \"Haru Aerlen presence confirmed.\"\n\nElira: \"Person, not category.\"\n\nThe red notice dimmed. Inside the carriage, Sera asked softly.\n\nSera: \"Can they ask forever?\"\n\nMaelin: \"They can try.\"\n\nSera: \"Can we answer forever?\"\n\nAira closed the ledger.\n\nAira: \"No. Sometimes we sleep and make them wait.\"\n\nSera seemed to like that.\n\nBy late the following afternoon, Aurelis towers rose pale through winter haze, lamps already burning in windows where people had refused to sleep. Inside the carriage, something glass clinked.\n\nAira: \"If that was soup, I am resigning from gravity.\"\n\nMaelin: \"It was your ink bottle.\"\n\nAira: \"Worse.\"\n\nThrough the front window, Sera asked.\n\nSera: \"Ink can spill here?\"\n\nAira: \"Constantly.\"\n\nSera waited a beat.\n\nSera: \"Good.\"\n\nThe horses kept walking toward the outer wall.\n\n---\n\nAurelis did not cheer when we returned, which told me more about what the academy had learned than any banner could have.\n\nThe north service gate opened at dusk under a sky the color of wet iron. Students stood in quiet clusters along the inner road with lamps held low and voices lower: Green first-years nearest the wall, Blue support beside the medical path, Red heirs under the arcade with family attendants pretending not to be guards, and Yellow seniors apart from everyone else, as if distance might still protect reputation from history. No one shouted Black Circle. No one bowed or crowded the carriage glass.\n\nMira Castellan stood at the front of the Green cluster with her Care Ledger copy hugged to her chest, Finn beside her holding three sealed receipt bundles with the look of someone who had not slept since numbers became political. Behind them, Pella and two kitchen runners held extra blankets, while a younger boy balanced a tray of covered cups like soup might become historically significant if carried crooked.\n\nAira's hoarse voice carried out as the wheels crossed the gate line.\n\nAira: \"Route witness ends at Aurelis north service gate. Medical transfer begins. No speeches.\"\n\nLeon lifted both hands near the path with Cassian beside him.\n\nLeon: \"I prepared no speeches.\"\n\nCassian: \"He prepared complaints.\"\n\nMaelin pushed the carriage door open and ended the banter with one look.\n\nMaelin: \"Trauma ward. Now. Anyone blocking the path becomes furniture.\"\n\nThe lane cleared at once. Sera left the carriage in her blanket cradle and transferred onto a low floating stretcher only after Maelin named the route and accepted Sera's refusal of two straps. Sera kept Tessa's empty soup cup tucked under the blanket against her chest. Lysa walked beside her, stubborn enough that Maelin let her keep her feet.\n\nElira stepped down last before me. Lamp-light caught the winter pallor on her face, though she still stood straight until I offered my hand.\n\nElira: \"Support.\"\n\nHaru: \"Support.\"\n\nMira glanced at our joined hands and looked away fast, making privacy look like policy.\n\nRhea met us at the medical path alongside Senna Voss's remote crystal, Professor Valen, and Halden watching from the archway. Before Aira finished reporting the Whispering Pass correction packet, the relay crystal above Rhea's shoulder flared red-black.\n\nCouncilor Armand Krail appeared at formal projection scale, immaculate and furious.\n\nKrail: \"President Valmont. Halt medical transfer pending review.\"\n\nMaelin did not break stride.\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nKrail: \"Senior Healer Orr, these minors are material subjects in an unauthorized northern intervention.\"\n\nMaelin stopped so hard the whole path stopped with her.\n\nMaelin: \"Say material subjects again.\"\n\nThe projection flickered. Krail did not repeat it. Rhea stepped cleanly between his image and the stretcher.\n\nRhea: \"Medical transfer continues. Review may observe from outside the treatment rail.\"\n\nKrail: \"This mission violated the fiscal embargo and may constitute a private Black Circle militia funded through student loyalty.\"\n\nEvery eye turned toward me. I opened both hands at my sides.\n\nHaru: \"Record that I am angry. I am not answering first.\"\n\nKrail wanted me centered and defensive while rescued children shrank into background scenery. Aira dropped the Care Ledger onto Finn's portable stand before he could pivot.\n\nAira: \"No Council funds. No command privilege purchased. Every contribution reversible until first supply conversion. Duplicate records filed at departure, gate crossing, Valise, and return.\"\n\nKrail: \"Publicly pressured students cannot meaningfully consent to funding an extra-classification expedition.\"\n\nMira stepped forward, notebook trembling in her grip.\n\nMira: \"I was frightened when I signed. I was told the reversal window anyway. Fear did not erase the time stamp.\"\n\nKrail: \"Your records are self-serving.\"\n\nValech's voice cut cleanly from the pass crystal before Aira could reply.\n\nValech: \"I opened the gate. Haru Aerlen did not.\"\n\nMaelin pushed Sera's stretcher another pace toward the ward.\n\nMaelin: \"Two hypothermic minors received consent-confirmed warmth after unregistered relay custody. Delay now creates preventable harm. Send your theory to my clerk.\"\n\nKrail: \"Haru Aerlen's presence changes the risk profile.\"\n\nSera gripped her soup cup beneath the blanket. Krail never looked at her, though Lysa stepped forward to face his image directly, her voice shaking.\n\nLysa: \"He didn't take us. I asked for warmth. Sera allowed warmth near. Haru did not ask where before permission, did not touch her, and did not make her answer. If you call that abduction, you are using Noctis grammar.\"\n\nSenna's crystal brightened.\n\nSenna: \"Protected statement recorded.\"\n\nCornered, Krail reached for older teeth.\n\nKrail: \"If transfer proceeds before jurisdiction review, Aurelis may be obstructing lawful Noctis recovery.\"\n\nSera's fingers locked around the cup. Lysa went rigid. Valen's face darkened.\n\nValen: \"Institutions use recovery for property that escaped them.\"\n\nHalden: \"The phrase matches the hostile Valise exit grammar. Insufficient for coordination proof, sufficient for separate review.\"\n\nSenna: \"Separate review opened. Not on a medical path.\"\n\nSera looked up from the stretcher.\n\nSera: \"Can I say no to Noctis recovery?\"\n\nMaelin moved beside her before anyone could turn the question into law.\n\nMaelin: \"You do not owe them an answer tonight.\"\n\nSera: \"Can I?\"\n\nSenna's expression eased by a fraction.\n\nSenna: \"You may give one protected preference for tonight. It will not become full testimony.\"\n\nElira took one step forward, stopping where Sera could see she was not crowding her. Sera met Krail's projection head-on.\n\nSera: \"No. Noctis recovery is not allowed tonight.\"\n\nAira wrote every word.\n\nRhea: \"Medical transfer proceeds. Further questions wait until stabilization.\"\n\nKrail: \"This is not finished.\"\n\nRhea: \"Neither is their treatment. Move.\"\n\nHis projection vanished. Nobody cheered. Maelin pointed toward the ward doors.\n\nMaelin: \"Now that everyone has nearly harmed patients with conversation, we move.\"\n\nAs the stretcher rolled past me, Sera turned her head.\n\nSera: \"He wanted to stop us?\"\n\nElira answered beside me.\n\nElira: \"He wanted to name what happened before you could.\"\n\nSera thought about that for two steps.\n\nSera: \"Can I name soup later?\"\n\nMaelin's expression softened by one rare degree.\n\nMaelin: \"Any time you want.\"\n\nSera nodded and let the stretcher carry her through the ward doors. The dented cup went inside with her.\n\nA Yellow senior near the arcade leaned toward a companion, whispering.\n\nSenior: \"Why the cup?\"\n\nMira heard and spun around so fast Finn had to catch a receipt bundle.\n\nMira: \"Because it is hers.\"\n\nThe senior blinked.\n\nMira: \"That is the whole explanation.\"\n\nHalden watched the ward doors close, his gaze shifting from the cup's absence to the ledgers in Aira's arms.\n\nHalden: \"Object continuity.\"\n\nAira turned on him.\n\nAira: \"Do not make the soup cup sound like an exam topic.\"\n\nHalden: \"It is an exam topic. Noctis doctrine converts personal objects into inventory or leverage. Retained object identity resists reclamation grammar.\"\n\nMira's pencil appeared as if summoned.\n\nMira: \"Soup cup resists reclamation grammar.\"\n\nFinn: \"That sentence is strange.\"\n\nMira: \"Most useful sentences are now strange.\"\n\nElira leaned her shoulder against mine.\n\nElira: \"Aurelis will never recover from Aira and Halden agreeing about a cup.\"\n\nHaru: \"Halden has not agreed. He has become interested.\"\n\nElira: \"Worse.\"\n\nOnly then did my knees remember how long we had been standing. Elira sagged against me at the exact instant my own balance slipped.\n\nAira: \"If you collapse romantically, please do it away from the ledgers.\"\n\nWe followed the stretcher inside as far as the outer corridor, where the trauma ward doors did not swallow the story whole.\n\nRhea placed the hallway under witness rail conditions before the ward lamps shifted color: no crowding, no staring, no Council projections past the treatment line, no Noctis claims outside Senna's office. Maelin warned anyone whispering within patient earshot that she would remove them personally.\n\nAira dropped onto the floor against the corridor wall beside a sleeping Green runner, ledger across her knees, ink smudged along one cheek. Mira crouched next to her.\n\nMira: \"The reversal window technically closed at first supply conversion, but if anyone panicked after seeing Krail...\"\n\nAira: \"Reopen for twelve hours under post-event pressure reconsideration.\"\n\nFinn: \"Should we call it panic?\"\n\nAira: \"Call it witness stress, before someone worse writes cowardice.\"\n\nAcross the corridor, Valen and Halden murmured low enough that only fragments reached me.\n\nValen: \"...recovery language predates Noctis standardization...\"\n\nHalden: \"...grammar match is not enough...\"\n\nValen: \"...enough to preserve concern...\"\n\nHalden: \"...concern is not a cage, Professor.\"\n\nValen: \"That is what I am trying to teach you.\"\n\nHalden's pen stopped above his notebook.\n\nElira sat in a chair near the corridor window only after Maelin appeared in the doorway and pointed at it without a word. Through the frosted glass, healer silhouettes moved around two bays: one taller shape sitting up, one smaller mound under blankets with a cup placed right within reach on the bedside table.\n\nElira followed my eyes.\n\nElira: \"You are thinking about the cup.\"\n\nHaru: \"It stayed hers.\"\n\nElira: \"Better than thinking about whether you could heal her faster.\"\n\nI looked down at my open hands.\n\nHaru: \"I am thinking about that too.\"\n\nElira leaned her shoulder against mine. No false comfort, just presence.\n\nWhen Maelin finally came out, everyone pretended not to surge forward and nobody fooled her.\n\nMaelin: \"Lysa is stable. Severe cold exposure, channel exhaustion, and heroic stupidity regarding walking. She will sleep. Nobody takes a statement from her before breakfast.\"\n\nShe aimed a look straight at Aira, who lowered her raised pencil.\n\nMaelin: \"Sera is critical but stable. Finger sensation returning, pain managed on consent intervals, cloth-only contact. She chose soup, cup retention, curtain half-open, and no visitors tonight.\"\n\nRelief eased something tight in my chest. Safe included not having to see us.\n\nMaelin looked at me.\n\nMaelin: \"She did ask whether the driver was still outside.\"\n\nEvery head in the corridor turned.\n\nHaru: \"What did you say?\"\n\nMaelin: \"I said yes, and that he was not listening through walls.\"\n\nAira wrote that down immediately.\n\nMaelin: \"She said good.\"\n\nGood that I was outside. Good that I was not inside uninvited. For once, the Black Circle had nothing to add.\n\nRhea closed the corridor session before exhaustion could make anyone reckless, sending students back to their dormitories and faculty to their desks.\n\nRhea: \"Miss Solen goes to sleep or supervised unconsciousness.\"\n\nAira began to argue that the two were procedurally distinct until Leon and Cassian hauled three ledgers out of her arms and ruined the protest. Under low lamps, Rhea's escort walked Elira and me toward the witness cottage while the trauma ward stayed lit behind us, Aurelis feeling less like a fortress than a body learning where all its wounds were.\n\n---\n\nThe witness cottage smelled of tea, cold wool, and paper that had survived being important.\n\nOnce the door shut, Elira rested one palm against the wall, letting the careful posture she had worn all day drop away as she made it to the couch. I sat beside her and went straight back to the alcove in my head.\n\nHaru: \"I could have stabilized Sera.\"\n\nElira waited.\n\nHaru: \"Inside the bone. Fast enough to stop the cold before she felt it.\"\n\nElira: \"And she had not chosen you.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe truth stayed sharp no matter how often we turned it.\n\nElira: \"You did not abandon her. You let her keep the door.\"\n\nHaru: \"It did not feel like protection.\"\n\nElira: \"Protection should not always feel powerful.\"\n\nShe offered her hand, and I took it because both of us wanted the warmth, not to prove anything.\n\nA knock broke the quiet before exhaustion could turn heavy. Maelin brought hot soup; Rhea followed with Senna's preliminary sequence sealed in a slim crystal case.\n\nRhea: \"We filed before Krail.\"\n\nThe header read:\n\n**VALISE FROST-RELAY INCIDENT: PRELIMINARY SEQUENCE UNDER DISTRIBUTED WITNESS REVIEW.**\n\nSequence: not extraction, incursion, or Black Circle rescue.\n\nRhea: \"It holds for now.\"\n\nAira poked her head through the doorway behind her, still clutching both ledgers.\n\nAira: \"For now is currently my favorite legal duration.\"\n\nRhea laid a second notice beside the case:\n\n**ACADEMIC ATTENDANCE RESUMES UNDER WITNESS CONDITIONS.**\n\n**TOURNAMENT BRACKETS REMAIN SUSPENDED. RESUMPTION REVIEW DATE PENDING.**\n\nRhea: \"Tomorrow. Medical restrictions stay in force. Dren retains ordinary correction authority, Halden retains the right to be unpleasant, and my emergency seal does not overrule healers.\"\n\nMaelin: \"A rare correct sentence from governance.\"\n\nThey left after soup and several threats about sleep. Elira untied and retied the ribbon at my wrist, neater rather than tighter.\n\nElira: \"We go back.\"\n\nHaru: \"Tomorrow.\"\n\nElira: \"After sleep.\"\n\nMaelin shouted approval from outside the door, proving she had not gone nearly far enough down the path.\n\nThe next afternoon, Maelin allowed one brief ward-window visit. Sera sat propped up in the nearer bed with her curtain half-open, Lysa asleep in the next bay, and the dented cup waiting on the bedside table.\n\nSera looked at Elira first. Elira raised one hand, palm open. Sera checked Maelin's face, then lifted a single finger from the blanket in reply. When her gaze shifted to me, I stayed behind the outer corridor line and watched her point toward the table.\n\nElira: \"She is showing you it stayed.\"\n\nHers, unconverted into evidence or ward property. I laid one hand flat over my chest so Sera could see I understood without claiming a promise I had not been offered. She looked away first.\n\nRhea stood beside us with her council mark dimmed.\n\nRhea: \"That is enough.\"\n\nWe walked back along the garden path while late bells gave way to the first evening lamps.\n\n## Coda: The Cup Stayed\n\nBy that first morning, the cup had become inconvenient.\n\nIt needed washing. It needed a label. Three adults nearly talked it out of existence trying to decide whether a rescued student's chosen object counted as medical property, witness evidence, personal belonging, or contamination risk.\n\nMira solved the debate with a strip of crooked tape: SERA'S CUP. ASK SERA.\n\nWhen Sera woke before noon, she stared at the tape on her bedside table for so long Maelin nearly stepped in to check her pulse.\n\nSera: \"Ask me?\"\n\nMaelin looked up from the washstand and smiled once, brisk and real.\n\nMaelin: \"Every time.\"\n\nSera touched the edge of the tape with one finger. Outside the infirmary, Noctis phrasing still drifted through halls and relay slates, hunting for frightened mouths to live in. Inside the room, one dented metal cup had escaped classification long enough to remain itself.\n\nSera lifted it with both hands and drank.\n\nNo one corrected the record.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-33-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep34","label":"Episode 28","number":"28","file":"episode-34-cover.webp","fallback":"The Honeymoon Under Witness","eyebrow":"Bonding","recap":"","accent":["#312e81","#f8fafc","#f59e0b"],"displayLabel":"Episode 28","displayNumber":"28","displayEyebrow":"The Fifth Tower","arcLabel":"The Fifth Tower","title":"The First Bell After Rescue","body":"Morning arrived before I was ready for it.\n\nWe had returned to Aurelis two evenings earlier, once the northern road lost the last of its frost and the carriage wheels began sounding like ordinary wood again. The journey back had been quieter than the journey out. Sera's cup rode wrapped in cloth on Maelin's lap. Elira slept for twelve uneven minutes with her head against my shoulder, woke before the academy gate, and said nothing when the old ward-stones recognized us. The next day disappeared into reports, medical restrictions, and one ward-window visit where Sera showed us the cup had remained hers. By the time Rhea walked us back to the witness cottage, the lamps were burning again. Sleep came in pieces. Morning found whatever was left.\n\nThe first light over Aurelis did not enter the bedroom dramatically. No old system wrote warnings across the window. No relay frost crawled over the sill. No black ring opened in the reflection of the water pitcher beside the bed.\n\nPale gold slipped through the curtains, catching dust where the room had been pretending to be clean. Elira breathed beside me with one hand still tucked through mine, her fingers warmer than the dawn and stubborn even in sleep.\n\nFor several minutes, I stayed still. That was not the same as resting.\n\nLying still still took effort when weeks of alarms had trained my body to expect a bell, a filing, a severance line, a witness rail, a child asking whether I could open her, or a Councilor finding a new way to make fear sound responsible.\n\nYet no alarm sounded. Nobody shouted from the garden path, and no red-black projection spread across the plaster.\n\nThe cottage wards chimed once, soft blue. Authorized healer rotation.\n\nThen Maelin Orr's voice carried through from the front room, brisk enough to make the furniture consider improving its posture.\n\nMaelin: \"If either of you is awake and pretending not to be because you dislike medical schedules, I already dislike you back.\"\n\nElira's eyes opened. Gold, tired, immediately offended.\n\nElira: \"She says that as if dislike is an approved diagnostic category.\"\n\nHaru: \"For Maelin, it may be.\"\n\nElira turned her head toward me. Her hair had come loose across the pillow during the night, pale strands caught in the morning light. The dark crystal pendant rested at her throat beneath the open collar of her sleeping robe, faint silver lines folded through it like moonlight trapped under ice.\n\nThe pendant did not glow. It listened.\n\nEverything around us listened now.\n\nThat was the trouble with surviving a public catastrophe: even quiet felt like a room waiting for testimony. Elira studied my face.\n\nElira: \"You woke before the bell.\"\n\nHaru: \"There has not been a bell yet.\"\n\nElira: \"Exactly.\"\n\nI hesitated a half-second too long. Her fingers tightened around mine.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was wondering whether a classroom can become a trap if enough people are afraid of where I sit.\"\n\nElira exhaled through her nose.\n\nElira: \"Good morning to you too.\"\n\nHaru: \"I tried to wait until after breakfast.\"\n\nElira: \"You failed in spirit.\"\n\nThere should not have been comfort in being scolded before sunrise. There was.\n\nThe Black Circle stayed quiet beneath my ribs, though it had not been absent since the Wounded City. Once, hiding had meant pretending it slept. Now hiding was impossible, and pretending had grown insulting to everyone who had watched me fail to vanish.\n\nStill, quiet was possible. And quiet did not mean alone.\n\nMaelin knocked once and came in without waiting for an invitation, having decided days ago that medical authority and romance could negotiate on their own time.\n\nShe carried a slate, two folded uniforms, a shoulder brace, a packet of breakfast rolls, and the expression of a woman who found civilization disappointing before breakfast but intended to keep it alive anyway.\n\nMaelin: \"Vitals.\"\n\nElira sat up slowly. I moved before thinking, stopping one palm behind her shoulder, close enough to steady her, loose enough that she could lean away.\n\nShe leaned against my hand for the breath it took to sit upright, then faced Maelin.\n\nElira: \"I am alive, sore, hungry, irritated, and determined.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That covers attitude. I asked for vitals.\"\n\nElira offered her wrist.\n\nMaelin held a diagnostic charm above the skin without touching, since Elira had not yet said touch was allowed this morning. That small pause had become standard practice.\n\nThe charm glowed gold, shifted blue, and settled into a steady green-white pulse. Maelin read her slate.\n\nMaelin: \"Shoulder strain stable. Channel recovery adequate. Sleep insufficient, though not disgraceful. Pendant quiet. No involuntary panic response overnight?\"\n\nElira: \"One.\"\n\nMy hand stilled. Maelin looked up.\n\nElira shot me a warning glance before I could become helpful in the worst possible direction.\n\nElira: \"Dream. Window sound. I woke, named the room, touched the pendant, and went back to sleep.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Did Haru wake?\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThat should have relieved me. Instead it felt like missing a watch I had never been asked to keep. Elira caught that too.\n\nElira: \"That is good.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"Your face disagrees.\"\n\nMaelin: \"His face has been practicing martyrdom in minor keys for days. I am considering confiscating it.\"\n\nHaru: \"Is that medical authority?\"\n\nMaelin: \"If I write it neatly enough.\"\n\nThe diagnostic charm warmed once and lifted from Elira's wrist. Maelin turned to me.\n\nMaelin: \"Your turn.\"\n\nI gave her my wrist.\n\nThe charm dimmed as it neared my skin. It always did that now, approaching the Black Circle like a junior clerk approaching a dragon with a clipboard and poor career instincts.\n\nAfter a beat, the pulse returned: blue, silver, then a thin black rim that Maelin pretended not to find interesting because she was a professional.\n\nMaelin: \"No pressure surge overnight. Baseline slightly elevated, within margin.\"\n\nHaru: \"Slightly.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Do not negotiate adjectives with a healer before breakfast.\"\n\nShe jotted a line on the slate.\n\nMaelin: \"Class conditions.\"\n\nElira straightened. That tiny shift told me more about her nerves than any tremor would have. Being healed in hiding could easily become another locked room Noctis would have approved of, just with nicer curtains. Maelin unfolded a sheet of paper.\n\nMaelin: \"Elira attends first period only unless I extend permission. Shoulder brace worn beneath the uniform. No surprise drills. No combat response tests. No pendant discussion. No death review. No forced testimony. No public questions about Valise, Sera, Lysa, Cael, Dominion Severance, Votive Return, Regalia, intimacy, sleep arrangements, or whether affection has measurable stabilizing properties.\"\n\nElira's mouth twitched.\n\nElira: \"You added the last one.\"\n\nMaelin: \"A junior researcher from applied resonance sent a questionnaire at dawn.\"\n\nThe Black Circle stirred. Maelin pointed at me without looking up from the page.\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nI let the pressure settle.\n\nHaru: \"I had not done anything.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You considered becoming weather.\"\n\nElira covered her mouth with one hand, failing to hide her smile. Maelin read on.\n\nMaelin: \"Haru attends under voluntary stability observation, not containment. He sits where he chooses unless ordinary room limits require otherwise. No separate desk out of institutional fear. No isolation geometry. No witness rail inside the classroom. Aira may record procedural anomalies, though she may not threaten furniture unless furniture behaves suspiciously.\"\n\nFrom the front room, Aira's voice called back.\n\nAira: \"Define suspiciously.\"\n\nMaelin did not raise her voice.\n\nMaelin: \"If it carries a Council seal.\"\n\nAira: \"That is fair.\"\n\nMaelin's eyes flicked toward the doorway.\n\nMaelin: \"She has been out there for twenty minutes.\"\n\nHaru: \"Of course she has.\"\n\nElira: \"Did she bring breakfast?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Four notebooks and a pastry she labeled emergency morale.\"\n\nElira: \"Then she may stay.\"\n\nMaelin capped her pen.\n\nMaelin: \"Final condition: if either of you gets overwhelmed, you say so before turning pressure into weather, gold into martyrdom, or silence into a heroic lie.\"\n\nElira: \"That last clause is aimed at both of us.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I have limited time. I economize with my insults.\"\n\nShe handed Elira the shoulder brace.\n\nI turned toward the window while Elira changed behind the folding screen Maelin had set up in the corner after discovering that witness cottages had not been built for post-catastrophe privacy.\n\nThe brace clicked softly as Maelin fastened it. Elira hissed once through her teeth, and I shifted toward the screen before catching myself.\n\nElira: \"Allowed.\"\n\nOne word, granting concern permission to stay close without asking for rescue. I held still and let it stand.\n\nWhen she stepped out from behind the screen, she wore her Aurelis uniform again.\n\nThe sight hit harder than I expected. It was only silver-blue academy cloth, neatly tailored, a little too formal for someone who had spent the previous week arguing with ancient systems that treated crowns like character flaws.\n\nThe last time Elira had worn it to an academy event, Dominion Severance had entered below her pendant and killed her in front of the world. My fingers curled.\n\nThe room cooled by half a degree. Elira noticed at once, crossed to me, and stopped within arm's reach so choosing to close the gap stayed mine.\n\nElira: \"Look at me.\"\n\nI lifted my eyes from her collar.\n\nElira: \"This is not the Wounded City.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"My body is not a route.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"My uniform is not proof that I am ready for everything.\"\n\nThat one hurt differently.\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"Then what is it?\"\n\nI looked at the blue cuffs, the silver trim, the dark pendant resting openly at her throat because hiding it would have fed the fear. Underneath the wool, the brace sat just clear of her collarbone, invisible unless you knew where to look.\n\nHaru: \"Yours.\"\n\nElira's expression softened. She smoothed one blue cuff with her thumb and let out a quiet breath.\n\nElira: \"Mine.\"\n\nMaelin made a mark on her slate.\n\nElira: \"Did you just record that?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Medical relevance.\"\n\nAira called from the front room.\n\nAira: \"Emotional infrastructure.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Do not help.\"\n\nElira let out a brief, real laugh. Beneath my ribs, the Black Circle warmed once and went quiet, as if even impossible power understood when a laugh did more work than it could.\n\nBreakfast was rolls, tea, sliced winter apples, and Aira pretending she had not already eaten the emergency morale pastry before anyone else saw it.\n\nShe sat cross-legged on the front-room rug with three notebooks laid out around her in a defensive triangle, her hair pinned messily and ink across the back of one hand. Her Blue Circle crest flickered low at her wrist, steady despite exhaustion. Leon and Cassian had arrived with her, though neither admitted to organizing a watch. Leon stood near the door like a guard annoyed that his post had emotional weight, while Cassian leaned against the window frame eating an apple slice with the wary eyes of a fire mage prepared to argue with bad architecture.\n\nRhea arrived just as Maelin threatened to classify tea as an inadequate meal.\n\nThe Student Council President wore full uniform, red council mark visible, hair pulled back tightly enough to count as public service. Finn Castellan followed with a bundle of receipts under one arm and rolled seating maps under the other.\n\nMira Castellan hovered behind him with her green folio clutched to her chest, looking like someone who had practiced entering the cottage and then been ambushed by finding people inside it.\n\nMira: \"Good morning.\"\n\nElira: \"Good morning, Mira.\"\n\nMira looked relieved by the ordinary greeting and immediately anxious about what ordinary meant today.\n\nFinn: \"We brought the seating maps.\"\n\nAira's head came up.\n\nAira: \"Plural?\"\n\nRhea: \"That is why we are here.\"\n\nThe air in the room shifted. Not much, just enough.\n\nHaru: \"What did they do?\"\n\nRhea's gaze moved to me.\n\nRhea: \"Nothing irreversible.\"\n\nElira: \"Rhea.\"\n\nRhea exhaled once.\n\nRhea: \"Advanced Mana Theory first period was moved from classroom three to lecture hall nine. Officially, for capacity and observer management.\"\n\nAira: \"Observer management.\"\n\nFinn unrolled the first map on the table.\n\nFinn: \"Lecture hall nine has tiered seating.\"\n\nMira: \"And an old demonstration pit.\"\n\nCassian stopped chewing.\n\nLeon: \"A pit.\"\n\nMira nodded miserably.\n\nMira: \"Not a modern one. Maintenance calls it a central working circle, lower than the benches, round, built so everyone above can look down.\"\n\nThe Black Circle stirred. Elira touched my wrist with two fingers, a reminder rather than restraint. I breathed.\n\nHaru: \"Who requested the room change?\"\n\nRhea: \"Deputy Registrar Pellon, under instruction from Academic Safety Review.\"\n\nAira: \"Krail.\"\n\nRhea: \"His signature is not on it.\"\n\nAira: \"Krail with cleaner boots.\"\n\nMaelin held out a hand, took the map from Finn, and wore the expression healers reserve for patients who describe a terrible idea as tradition.\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nRhea: \"Agreed.\"\n\nElira: \"Has Halden seen this?\"\n\nRhea: \"He filed an objection at sixth bell.\"\n\nHaru: \"Wait. Sixth bell this morning, or yesterday?\"\n\nRhea: \"Yesterday evening. Before Pellon even submitted the room change.\"\n\nAira brightened.\n\nAira: \"He objected before they filed?\"\n\nRhea: \"Professor Halden submitted a standing objection to any room arrangement that places Haru Aerlen below observers, Elira Vale under direct visual analysis, or Green Circle witnesses in rear containment rows. He titled it 'Preemptive Refusal of Predictable Cowardice.'\"\n\nFor a second, nobody spoke. Then Cassian laughed.\n\nCassian: \"I am beginning to like him.\"\n\nLeon: \"That is unsettling.\"\n\nMira's eyes went wide.\n\nMira: \"Can professors title objections like that?\"\n\nRhea: \"Apparently.\"\n\nAira wrote something down with visible reverence. Elira pressed her knuckles to her mouth.\n\nHaru: \"Where is class now?\"\n\nRhea's expression sobered.\n\nRhea: \"Still lecture hall nine unless we force the change. Senna is in Council session. Valen is arguing classroom precedent. Halden is standing outside the doors refusing to begin until the seating changes, while Dren and Seraphine keep observers out of the pit.\"\n\nLeon: \"Observers?\"\n\nFinn unrolled the second map.\n\nFinn: \"Faculty safety delegates, Council note clerks, two visiting academy representatives, and three people claiming to be architectural inspectors.\"\n\nAira: \"Architectural inspectors?\"\n\nFinn: \"One of them wore opera gloves.\"\n\nCassian: \"Unlikely, though intriguing.\"\n\nMaelin looked sharply at Rhea.\n\nMaelin: \"Why bring this to them before fixing it?\"\n\nRhea: \"Because if we settle every condition behind closed doors, the first lesson becomes that ordinary life is something adults secretly arrange around Haru and Elira.\"\n\nElira's posture changed, subtle enough that only someone watching closely would catch it. Rhea had caught it.\n\nRhea: \"You have the right to see the room before you walk into it.\"\n\nElira: \"And the right to refuse.\"\n\nRhea: \"Full right.\"\n\nShe tapped the corner of the seating map rather than folding it away.\n\nThe room waited for us to decide, which mattered more than any filing. I looked at Elira.\n\nHaru: \"What do you want?\"\n\nShe studied the tiered lines, the sunken central circle, the observer alcoves, and the rear rows labeled for Green Circle overflow in handwriting too neat to be innocent. Her thumb brushed the edge of her pendant, then dropped.\n\nElira: \"I want to attend class.\"\n\nHaru: \"In that room?\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nMira let out a small breath of relief and looked instantly guilty for it. Elira turned to her.\n\nElira: \"What do Green students want?\"\n\nMira froze.\n\nMira: \"I am not authorized to speak for all Green students.\"\n\nAira: \"Excellent survival instinct. Answer for yourself.\"\n\nMira swallowed.\n\nMira: \"I want not to sit in the back just because someone decided low rank belongs farthest from the danger.\"\n\nFinn nodded.\n\nFinn: \"I want rows where every student can leave without crossing a pit.\"\n\nLeon stared down at the map again.\n\nLeon: \"No pit.\"\n\nCassian: \"I want to hear the architectural inspectors justify opera gloves.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Denied as primary objective.\"\n\nCassian: \"Secondary?\"\n\nMaelin: \"Also denied.\"\n\nRhea rolled the map back up.\n\nRhea: \"Then we do not enter lecture hall nine as arranged.\"\n\nHaru: \"Will that count as refusing instruction?\"\n\nRhea's smile was thin.\n\nRhea: \"Only if Academic Safety Review wants to explain why the first ordinary class after a non-containment ruling required a symbolic containment pit.\"\n\nAira: \"I would enjoy taking minutes on that explanation.\"\n\nElira reached for her tea. Her hand trembled once before steadying, slight enough that most people would have missed it, clear enough that she knew I had seen. She drank anyway.\n\nElira: \"Then we go.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Slowly.\"\n\nElira: \"At your pace.\"\n\nMaelin: \"With the brace.\"\n\nElira: \"Already buckled.\"\n\nHer voice stayed quiet, though it carried cleanly across the room.\n\nMaelin: \"And no dramatic speeches on the walk.\"\n\nElira glanced at me.\n\nElira: \"That one is for you.\"\n\nHaru: \"I have given very few dramatic speeches.\"\n\nAira: \"Quantity is not the only metric.\"\n\nThe warning bell for first period rang while we were still in the cottage. Reduced volume, single tower. Rhea had ordered the cap after Sera asked whether safe had to be noisy. The bronze note moved over Aurelis gently, almost hesitant. Elira closed her eyes.\n\nHer fingers stayed away from the pendant. Mine stayed away from the Black Circle. Maelin watched both choices and wrote neither down.\n\nOutside, the academy was waiting, and it did not look like a battlefield. That made it harder.\n\nThe path from the witness cottage crossed the east garden, passed the fountain, and curved under the old stone arcade where first-years usually complained about assignments with the gravity of people who had not yet seen what real trouble did to a schedule.\n\nThey were out there now, trying to look ordinary.\n\nGreen Circle students gathered along the low wall with notebooks in plain sight. Blue support students stood in pairs, pretending their spacing had nothing to do with watching the path. Red Circle heirs lingered beneath the arcade, a few with family attendants hovering far enough back to claim noninterference. Several Yellow seniors waited near the fountain with expressions held neutral so tightly they looked brittle. Nobody shouted. Nobody bowed. Nobody called me myth.\n\nThat quiet had not grown on its own; Rhea had built it. Council aides stood at intervals along the path, leaving the way wide enough that approaching required intent. Dren waited beside the fountain with arms folded, Seraphine beside him, her red circle dim at her wrist while her gaze swept the crowd like a blade taking attendance.\n\nProfessor Valen appeared halfway along the arcade with three thick historical volumes tucked under one arm and a pale Council clerk trailing behind her.\n\nValen: \"Classification under uncertainty is hardly novel. If the Council wishes to pretend it invented caution this morning, it may start with the pre-standardization room precedents I have flagged.\"\n\nCouncil Clerk: \"Professor, those are four hundred pages.\"\n\nValen: \"Then the Council will remain cautious for some time.\"\n\nElira's mouth curved.\n\nElira: \"I missed her.\"\n\nHaru: \"She has been around us all week.\"\n\nElira: \"I missed watching her make paperwork suffer.\"\n\nAira: \"A universal comfort.\"\n\nAs we reached the fountain, the low murmur around us shifted.\n\n*Haru. Elira. Black Circle. Valise. Is she cleared? Did Sera really refuse Noctis recovery? Why is the bell so quiet? Can he hear us?*\n\nI could. Not with magic, just proximity, training, and the ugly precision with which fear aimed itself. I kept walking without answering.\n\nElira walked beside me, shoulder brace hidden under her jacket, pendant in plain view, chin level. Aira followed one pace back with an open notebook. Leon and Cassian flanked the outer grass awkwardly enough to deserve partial credit for not looking like bodyguards. Mira and Finn walked ahead with Rhea, carrying the seating maps like evidence from a crime scene that had not yet confessed.\n\nOn the fountain rim, a small square of ward paper waited.\n\nSera's cup, drawn in a child's careful, uneven hand, leaning slightly left. Below it, in letters that changed size halfway across the line, someone had copied:\n\n**BELL HEARD. NOT TOO LOUD. CUP STAYED.**\n\nElira stopped. So did I. The chatter around the fountain quieted in widening rings.\n\nAira reached the rim first without touching the paper.\n\nAira: \"Ward transfer from the trauma wing. Sender status?\"\n\nRhea checked the corner seal.\n\nRhea: \"Sera authorized a single copy to the public fountain. No response required. No visit requested. Nothing beyond the text.\"\n\nElira's eyes shone. She blinked once and left everyone else to pretend not to notice.\n\nHaru: \"Good.\"\n\nThat was the only word I trusted not to overreach.\n\nA boy near the Green cluster whispered too loudly.\n\nStudent: \"She can write?\"\n\nMira turned, sharp without raising her voice.\n\nMira: \"She can choose what gets copied.\"\n\nThe boy flushed red.\n\nStudent: \"I meant-\"\n\nMira: \"I know. Say it better next time.\"\n\nHe shut his mouth and stared down at his boots. That, too, was a lesson.\n\nThe drawing stayed on the fountain rim because Rhea said moving it before the interval ended would turn care into tidying. We walked on.\n\nThe academic wing smelled of ink, damp stone, floor polish, and the faint panic of administrators discovering that students understood public sequence better than committees did.\n\nOutside lecture hall nine, the corridor was full, though Rhea's aides kept clear lanes to the exits.\n\nHalden stood before the closed doors with a slate under one arm and the patience of a cliff that had read the statutes and found them wanting. Dren flanked his left, Seraphine his right. Behind them stood three faculty safety delegates wearing the sour looks of men who had arrived to inspect a hazard and realized they were the ones under review.\n\nOne of the architectural inspectors wore opera gloves. Cassian spotted them at once.\n\nCassian: \"Magnificent.\"\n\nMaelin: \"No.\"\n\nThe inspector bristled without knowing why.\n\nHalden's gaze swept over us, pausing on Elira's shoulder, my hands, Aira's notebook, Mira's folio, Finn's maps, and Rhea's council mark before he gave one crisp nod.\n\nHalden: \"You are on time.\"\n\nAira checked the corridor clock.\n\nAira: \"We are seven minutes late.\"\n\nHalden: \"Class has not begun.\"\n\nRhea: \"Why?\"\n\nHalden turned toward the doors.\n\nHalden: \"Because the assigned room is pedagogically incompetent.\"\n\nOne of the faculty delegates cleared his throat.\n\nDelegate: \"Professor Halden, lecture hall nine is certified for high-risk demonstrations.\"\n\nHalden looked at him.\n\nHalden: \"Today is not a high-risk demonstration.\"\n\nDelegate: \"With respect, the presence of Haru Aerlen-\"\n\nHalden: \"Is the attendance of an enrolled student.\"\n\nThe delegate's mouth shut. Krail would have hated that answer, which made me like it immediately. Elira stepped forward.\n\nElira: \"Professor, what is today's lesson?\"\n\nHalden's gaze moved to her. For a heartbeat, something almost gentle softened his austere face before settling back into gravel.\n\nHalden: \"Classification under uncertainty.\"\n\nAira's pencil started moving.\n\nElira: \"Does the lesson require a sunken pit?\"\n\nHalden: \"No lesson requires a sunken pit unless the instructor lacks imagination.\"\n\nSeraphine's mouth twitched. Dren examined the ceiling as if he had refused to witness humor in an official corridor.\n\nThe delegate tried another angle.\n\nDelegate: \"The hall was chosen to give safety personnel clear sightlines for anomalous responses.\"\n\nFear hit Mira's face first, followed quickly by insult, which was turning into one of her better brands of courage.\n\nMira: \"Sightlines for whom?\"\n\nThe delegate looked at her as though a Green Circle student had spoken out of a supply closet.\n\nDelegate: \"Safety personnel.\"\n\nFinn held up the map.\n\nFinn: \"Then why are Green Circle seats marked rear overflow while Yellow Circle seats hold tier center?\"\n\nThe delegate blinked.\n\nFinn: \"If the goal is safety observation, visibility should not follow rank prestige. If the goal is rank prestige, stop calling the room choice safety.\"\n\nAira muttered under her breath.\n\nAira: \"Finn Castellan, terror of furniture.\"\n\nFinn did not smile; his hands were shaking too hard for that. Mira stepped half a pace closer to him without touching.\n\nThe delegate looked to Rhea for rescue.\n\nRhea: \"Answer him.\"\n\nDelegate: \"The seating reflects standard academic hierarchy.\"\n\nThe corridor went cold, and not from me. Elira spoke without raising her voice.\n\nElira: \"Then it reflects the wrong standard. A class coming back after public witness trauma cannot seat students by the very hierarchy that enabled half this harm and pretend the layout is neutral.\"\n\nHalden looked back at the delegate.\n\nHalden: \"There is your answer.\"\n\nDelegate: \"Professor, with respect-\"\n\nSeraphine: \"You keep using that phrase for someone offering remarkably little of it.\"\n\nThe delegate stopped.\n\nThe opera-gloved inspector tried a softer route.\n\nInspector: \"Could the class proceed if Haru Aerlen takes the lower circle voluntarily?\"\n\nThe Black Circle held still behind my ribs. So did I.\n\nThat word was the snare. *Voluntarily.* Noctis had bled it dry; Council filings had polished it until coercion could check its reflection in the surface. Agree to keep the peace, and the record would claim I chose the pit. Snap back too hard, and the record would claim the Black Circle resisted oversight. Let Elira speak for me, and they would write down proximity influence.\n\nAira's pencil stopped. That quiet told me the corridor had turned into a test.\n\nMira glanced at me, then away, knowing stares could press just as hard as rules. I breathed once.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe word stayed flat in the hall. The inspector's brows rose.\n\nInspector: \"No?\"\n\nHaru: \"I do not consent to seating that makes me the lesson before the lesson starts.\"\n\nAira's pencil moved again, relieved rather than triumphant.\n\nInspector: \"The precaution is not symbolic.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then remove the symbol.\"\n\nSilence held, tight and workable. Elira's fingers brushed mine once, a quiet check rather than reward.\n\nHalden pushed open the doors to lecture hall nine.\n\nInside, the tiered benches rose above a central stone circle. Small placards marked the back corners for Green Circle overflow. Looking at them turned something cold in my chest, older than Aurelis pressure: rooms taught people their place before anyone spoke a single word.\n\nHalden walked down into the pit alone. For one absurd second, I thought he meant to teach from it. Instead he set his slate on the stone, lifted one hand, and dissolved the demonstration circle. The stone stayed sunken, though the old boundary runes went dark one by one until the pit looked like nothing more than a badly planned floor.\n\nHalden climbed back up to the doorway.\n\nHalden: \"Class will not be held here.\"\n\nThe delegate stiffened.\n\nDelegate: \"You cannot simply refuse an assigned hall.\"\n\nHalden: \"I can when the hall undermines the lesson.\"\n\nHe looked over at us.\n\nHalden: \"Courtyard.\"\n\nLeon stared.\n\nLeon: \"Outside?\"\n\nHalden: \"Most concepts survive air.\"\n\nCassian: \"This may end up my favorite class.\"\n\nSeraphine looked straight at him.\n\nCassian: \"Academically.\"\n\nThe relocation took nine minutes, and Aira logged every one. Not because nine minutes mattered on their own, but because someone later would try to call it disorder, student agitation, or Black Circle disruption to prove the pit had been necessary after all.\n\nSo Aira wrote, Finn copied, and Mira kept witness timestamps. Rhea sealed the move under Student Council safety authority and Halden's faculty direction. Dren guided students down the stairs in mixed groups instead of ranks, and Seraphine made the architectural inspectors carry their own chairs when they asked where observers went.\n\nOut in the east courtyard, the space became a classroom without banners or rings.\n\nStudents took benches, fountain ledges, low walls, and patches of grass where the morning dew had mostly dried. Green and Blue students mixed near the middle because Mira and Finn got there first and claimed it before anyone could label a back row overflow. Leon sat beside a Green first-year who froze for two seconds and then tried hard to act as if sharing a bench with Leon Varek happened every Tuesday. Cassian sat behind them and handed the boy an apple slice without comment.\n\nElira picked a stone bench by the fountain where she could rest her bad shoulder against the back. I sat beside her, close because we both chose it rather than because a floor plan put us there. Aira settled on the grass at our feet with her notebooks fanned out like a small legal barricade. Rhea stayed near the courtyard arch, observing rather than presiding, while Maelin leaned against a pillar and watched Elira's breathing while pretending not to monitor half the school.\n\n## Advanced Mana Theory - The Release Seam\n\nHalden stood before us with no chalkboard.\n\nProfessor Valen arrived carrying the lesson. Soren Ashvale held one end of a tournament safety-ward panel, and Talen Mire carried the other with his good hand while his braced arm stayed tucked against his coat. The waist-high silver frame was split across one corner, yet its center still hummed with the clean sheen of a spell that had passed every standard inspection.\n\nStudents crowded closer before remembering old habit and giving the frame room.\n\nValen: \"Suspended bracket equipment. Valemere logged the failure during inventory.\"\n\nSoren lowered his end onto two stone blocks while Talen set down the other.\n\nSoren: \"Competitor status remains intact and standings stay frozen. We brought evidence, not a petition to restart.\"\n\nLeon looked disappointed by the second half and relieved by the first.\n\nHalden lifted his slate and wrote three words in the upper corner.\n\n**OBSERVATION. INFERENCE. CLAIM.**\n\nHe drew a single line underneath them.\n\n**UNCERTAINTY IS NOT PERMISSION TO INVENT.**\n\nHalden: \"This panel passed standard inspection. It fails only when incoming mana turns uneven or a caster pulls back. Today you will determine why.\"\n\nHis eyes crossed me without lingering.\n\nHalden: \"No Black Circle correction. No high-output reinforcement. No drawing on Mr. Mire's channels without permission renewed before each test. If you make the panel look stable without finding the fault, you fail.\"\n\nCassian: \"A class that punishes cosmetic excellence. Leon is in mortal danger.\"\n\nLeon: \"Your collar is crooked.\"\n\nCassian checked it on reflex. Aira laughed before he could pretend otherwise.\n\nThe first repair group went straight for the visible crack. Leon laid Blue pressure along the silver border, Cassian threaded heat underneath, and a Yellow senior filled the gap with enough clean light to make the frame hum. The split sealed over. Inspection runes along the edge turned green one by one.\n\nFor six seconds, the repair looked flawless.\n\nTalen's brace began to vibrate. He pulled his arm tight against his coat.\n\nTalen: \"Stop.\"\n\nLeon cut his spell before Halden could echo the word. Cassian followed a beat later. The Yellow senior hesitated just long enough for the courtyard to see it, then dropped her light too. The panel stayed green.\n\nTalen: \"It reads lower output as damage.\"\n\nSoren: \"Show them only if you want to.\"\n\nTalen unfastened the outer strap of his brace. A thin thread of Blue light shivered through his palm, caught by the frame from three paces off. Every time his output dipped, the newly mended corner flared brighter, yanking back as if correcting him. Elira leaned forward.\n\nElira: \"That reinforcement lattice is mine.\"\n\nHer voice came out sharper than surprise.\n\nSoren: \"A later Valemere derivative.\"\n\nElira: \"My original outer seam opened when support withdrew.\"\n\nSoren: \"This one does not.\"\n\nElira stood up before Maelin's warning glance could stop her.\n\nElira: \"Someone stripped out the release path and kept my name in the design log.\"\n\nNobody told her to calm down. That helped.\n\nCold pressed behind my sternum as the Black Circle read the panel without asking: nine overloaded joins, a false green inspection loop, and an original release seam buried under heavier reinforcement. I could have quieted all nine with less effort than keeping the false Green crest active at my wrist.\n\nPain pulsed beneath the fingernail Maelin had wrapped after the lower channel. My hand twitched forward anyway. Elira caught it.\n\nElira: \"If you stabilize it, we lose the defect.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"Your hand does not.\"\n\nQuiet enough to sting, public enough that I could not smooth it away. I slid both hands into my pockets.\n\nThe panel kept humming without me. A strange mix of pride and uselessness settled in my chest: I was built for every crisis, and standing still while other people solved a room felt dangerously close to not being needed.\n\nBeing unneeded in one moment was not the same as being worthless. I held onto that until my fingers stopped flexing inside my coat.\n\nTalen looked down at the frame.\n\nTalen: \"I can test the withdrawal.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You can offer. Halden can still decide the hardware is too stupid to deserve your nerves.\"\n\nTalen almost smiled.\n\nTalen: \"One low-output hold. When I say stop, nobody keeps measuring.\"\n\nRhea: \"Condition logged. Readings remain yours unless you choose to release them.\"\n\nTalen stepped up and held his braced hand near the silver border. Soren moved in a pace, then stopped.\n\nSoren: \"Support?\"\n\nTalen: \"Only if I ask.\"\n\nHis faded Blue Circle opened. The panel drank the first pulse, and the inspection runes stayed green. Talen let his output drop by a fraction.\n\nThe frame clamped down on the weakening line. His breath hitched.\n\nTalen: \"Stop.\"\n\nThe panel did not let go.\n\nElira broke the construct. Gold light struck the silver frame from the side, shattering the smooth repair. The crack split open twice as wide, and the courtyard wards barked one sharp alarm before Cassian smothered it with heat. Soren took a step toward Talen and halted.\n\nSoren: \"Support?\"\n\nTalen cradled his shaking wrist.\n\nTalen: \"Just the brace.\"\n\nSoren buckled the loose outer strap and kept his hands clear.\n\nElira glared at the split panel. The Yellow senior glared back at her.\n\nSenior: \"You wrecked our repair.\"\n\nElira: \"Your repair heard *stop* and tightened its grip. It was already broken.\"\n\nHer anger had real color now, not defending me or answering Krail, just Elira Vale as a mage-architect staring at stolen work turned dangerous by someone else's shortcut.\n\nHalden tapped the row of inspection runes along the silver edge, still glowing green right beside the widened crack.\n\nHalden: \"Observation?\"\n\nAira: \"Talen's brace vibrated before any rune reported instability.\"\n\nHalden: \"Inference?\"\n\nElira: \"The ward classifies withdrawal as a fault and pulls load out of the channel trying to leave.\"\n\nHalden: \"Claim?\"\n\nSoren: \"This panel is unsafe for tournament play.\"\n\nHalden: \"Supported for this panel under these conditions. Not yet universal.\"\n\nHe turned to the courtyard.\n\nHalden: \"Fear can be observed, though it cannot testify beyond what it knows. Neither can pride in a tidy patch.\"\n\nMira had been leaning forward from the Green benches, squinting at the silver edge.\n\nMira: \"Stronger light keeps washing out the paper.\"\n\nNobody else caught it except Finn, who was already pulling thin archive strips from his folio, cheap dust-treated paper that darkened under mana stress. They slid two beneath the corner, and the residual Blue and Yellow traces still lingering in the frame turned both strips black on contact.\n\nFinn: \"We need lower output.\"\n\nMira swallowed.\n\nMira: \"Mine.\"\n\nHalden: \"The frame may snap a strip and chill the hand holding it. You may pass.\"\n\nI stepped toward the heavy corner to take some of the load for her. Mira saw me reach.\n\nMira: \"Please do not make it gentle for me.\"\n\nI stopped. She was scared, and turning the test soft would have protected my conscience more than her skill.\n\nHaru: \"Tell me where to hold.\"\n\nMira pointed to the heavy end of the frame rather than the safe one. I tilted that corner clear of the stone block so she could reach underneath, holding the weight without smoothing a single line of mana.\n\nHer Green Circle opened. Small, steady light entered the third paper strip and left most of it pale, except for one narrow bruise along the buried seam. Finn logged the time. The fourth strip tore as the frame twitched. Mira flinched, kept her fingers clear, and changed the angle on the fifth instead of pushing harder.\n\nThree dark branches surfaced along the underside of the silver edge.\n\nElira knelt beside them. Maelin opened her mouth to object; Elira pointed at both knees planted flat on the grass as if kneeling were a medical defense. Maelin let it go.\n\nFine gold points drifted over the paper, weak enough to leave Mira's Green signal visible beneath them while tracing the old strain mark by mark.\n\nElira: \"There. The original release seam. They buried it to raise tournament tolerance.\"\n\nSoren's jaw tightened.\n\nSoren: \"To cut down on match stoppages.\"\n\nTalen: \"They turned surrender into malfunction because stoppages slowed the bracket.\"\n\nThe courtyard went quiet around that. Not every cruelty began with Lucien; some started with a schedule someone wanted to keep on time.\n\nElira traced one quadrant in the air without touching the metal.\n\nElira: \"I can cut this seam open. One section only. If I try the whole frame now, my output will blind Mira's reading and Maelin will get creative.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Accurate.\"\n\nRhea: \"Talen decides whether his channel goes near it again.\"\n\nTalen flexed his fingers inside the strapped brace, waited a beat, and unhooked only the cuff clasp.\n\nTalen: \"Give me ten minutes. Same stop rule. Only the cut quadrant.\"\n\nNobody filled those ten minutes with speeches.\n\nLeon and Cassian held the frame steady while arguing over whose earlier patch had hidden more of the fault. Finn numbered the remaining strips. Mira redrew the dark branches twice because her first sketch made one fork look too certain. Aira noted the exact beat Talen had said *stop* against the beat the frame had tightened. Soren sat beside Talen without hovering. Elira worked.\n\nHer first gold seam collapsed, and she swore under her breath. Her second opened too wide and nearly spilled load into the neighboring quadrant.\n\nHalden: \"That is not a release. That is structural abandonment.\"\n\nElira: \"I saw it.\"\n\nHalden: \"Your expression suggested you wanted the mistake to feel ashamed.\"\n\nElira: \"It should.\"\n\nOn the third try, she used less light. The result looked unimpressive: one crooked gold break in an otherwise smooth silver border.\n\nTalen stepped back to the mark.\n\nSoren: \"Support?\"\n\nTalen: \"Stand where I can see you.\"\n\nThe Blue pulse entered the cut quadrant. One breath. Two. Talen lowered his hand.\n\nTalen: \"Stop.\"\n\nThe crooked gold seam parted cleanly. His mana left the panel instead of being dragged back into his palm. The other three quadrants flared red, one false green rune shattered, and the frame stayed broken.\n\nTalen stared at his hand.\n\nTalen: \"That one listened.\"\n\nElira sat back hard in the grass, wearing the fierce grin she usually hid behind competence. One quadrant. No miracle fix. No restored bracket. Just a broken panel with one honest exit.\n\nIt was the best thing we had built all week. Soren faced Rhea.\n\nSoren: \"Valemere will not accept frozen standings as a win. When competition resumes, we want to meet Aurelis properly.\"\n\nCassian: \"Finally, a diplomatic stance with some spine.\"\n\nLeon: \"Aurelis was ahead.\"\n\nSoren: \"Then you should welcome the chance to prove it.\"\n\nLeon's chin came up, accepting the challenge. Talen snapped his cuff clasp shut.\n\nTalen: \"I will not endorse restarting matches while safety wards treat withdrawal as hostile breach. I will help audit if my readings remain mine. That is not a promise to compete.\"\n\nRhea: \"The Board asked for future resumption standards, not a restart date. Your condition goes into the audit record, not the bracket.\"\n\nTalen: \"Good.\"\n\nElira was still watching the crooked gold seam.\n\nElira: \"I want an independent research track. Release-responsive ward architecture. Injured channels, asymmetric output, surrender recognition, voluntary withdrawal, cross-rank testing.\"\n\nHalden: \"That is not a small project.\"\n\nElira: \"I did not ask for a small one.\"\n\nTalen: \"My readings stay mine.\"\n\nElira turned to him immediately.\n\nElira: \"You approve every use, every copy, and every comparison. You can walk away without your withdrawal turning into failed data.\"\n\nSoren: \"Valemere can lend old panel schematics. Not ownership.\"\n\nMira held up one of the pale pressure strips.\n\nMira: \"Green baselines have to stay visible.\"\n\nElira: \"Unedited, on the main sheet.\"\n\nFinn: \"And copied before heavier tests scorch them.\"\n\nElira: \"Captured first, every time.\"\n\nThe answers came fast because the design belonged to her, not because every solution was already in hand.\n\nHalden studied the split panel and the girl sitting in the grass beside it.\n\nHalden: \"You may have laboratory access after medical clearance. Access is not project approval, and today's cut is not a finished design.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHalden: \"You look pleased.\"\n\nElira: \"I am glad there is work nobody can finish for me before I start.\"\n\nA Council filing crystal arrived at the edge of the grass in a clerk's shaking hands. Rhea did not touch it.\n\nRhea: \"Class is still in session.\"\n\nKrail's recorded voice pressed once against the crystal seal. Halden set the shattered inspection rune right on top of it.\n\nHalden: \"It can wait beside the other false result.\"\n\nThe clerk backed away.\n\nMaelin handed Elira a folded slip from the trauma wing.\n\n**CLASS FIRST.**\n\nSera had asked whether we made it through. Elira pressed a hand to her mouth, eyes bright before she looked back down at the panel.\n\nHalden: \"Assignment: one page on what failed. If your paper includes the words *fixed*, *universal*, or *destiny*, start over.\"\n\nIt took Soren, Leon, Mira, and the Yellow senior together to haul the panel back toward storage, since the metal refused to turn lighter just because we had learned something from it.\n\nOne quadrant opened when asked. Tomorrow, the other three would still be broken.\n\n---\n\nAfternoon classes passed in stiff, careful quiet, and by the time long blue shadows stretched across the east walk, Aurelis looked less sure of itself. Students moved between halls with the wary exhaustion of people who had survived one ordinary day without trusting ordinary to hold.\n\nRhea found me beside the herb garden behind the council annex. The difference between being found and being summoned meant I stayed where I was without asking why she had come.\n\nShe stood by the low stone wall with her hands clasped behind her back and her council mark dim at her wrist.\n\nRhea: \"I used to hate Green Circle students.\"\n\nI looked over at her. Her expression did not budge.\n\nRhea: \"Not personally. That would have been easier. I hated what our treatment of them showed about Aurelis while everyone congratulated themselves for not being Noctis.\"\n\nGarden wind moved through the rosemary between us.\n\nRhea: \"I thought if I made procedure sharp enough, it would stop being cruel by accident.\"\n\nShe looked toward the academic wing.\n\nRhea: \"It did not.\"\n\nThat cost her to say aloud. Rhea Valmont did not confess; she entered corrections into the record.\n\nHaru: \"Do you resent me?\"\n\nThe question slipped out before I could file it down into something safer. Rhea turned her head.\n\nHaru: \"For dragging all of it into the open.\"\n\nShe weighed the question with the same severity she gave rulings.\n\nRhea: \"Sometimes.\"\n\nHer honesty steadied me more than reassurance would have.\n\nRhea: \"Visibility makes work. It exposes cracks I wanted to patch before anyone walked across them, and it stops me from pretending repair is a private virtue.\"\n\nShe met my eyes squarely.\n\nRhea: \"I do not resent you for surviving loudly enough that Aurelis had to listen to itself.\"\n\nI had no answer ready, and she did not ask for one.\n\nRhea: \"That is not comfort. It is a position.\"\n\nHaru: \"I understand.\"\n\nRhea's mouth tightened by a fraction.\n\nRhea: \"I believe you do.\"\n\nThen she walked back toward the annex, leaving the rosemary and the answer behind.\n\n## Elira Vale - Useless Light\n\nMaelin cleared ten minutes of low-output practice after checking Elira's pulse twice and making her repeat the limit without sarcasm. Elira managed it on the third try.\n\nThe west terrace sat empty after the afternoon bell. No healer trailed her up the steps, and no witness seal opened along the parapet.\n\nOld chalk marks scarred the flagstone. Unused target rings leaned against the stone wall. Beyond the railing, Lake Velis caught the lowering sun in narrow strips of gold. Elira sat down on a bench first, letting the irritation at needing to sit stay plain irritation instead of turning it into disobedience.\n\nShe opened a fresh notebook to the first page.\n\n**RELEASE-RESPONSIVE WARD ARCHITECTURE**\n\nThe heading looked too official, so she drew one clean line through it, keeping the words legible while demoting them from a verdict back to a question. Beneath it, she wrote:\n\n**How does a ward know someone has changed her mind?**\n\nOther questions crowded in faster than ink. How did a frame tell surrender from channel collapse without checking the speaker's rank first? Could an injured mage set a different decline curve than a healthy one? Who owned a failed reading? What happened when someone withdrew after a ward had already borrowed her strength?\n\nElira closed the book before the page turned into another room she had to rescue before supper.\n\nTomorrow she would ask Halden for a lab key. Talen would decide whether his data crossed the threshold. Mira's dust strips would stay inside the core method, not tucked into a footnote as a charming low-rank anecdote. Valemere could fight over authorship later.\n\nThat work belonged to tomorrow. The remaining six minutes belonged to her.\n\nElira lifted her right hand. The Yellow Circle at her wrist brightened softly, well under Maelin's cap.\n\nGold light gathered above her palm: no shield, no blade, no healing lattice, no witness seam, no proof that she deserved the rank pinned onto her before she knew its cost.\n\nShe shaped one crooked ring, then another worse one. The third broke into seven bright sparks and drifted upward in no tactical formation whatsoever. Elira smiled. No committee would have graded it, and that was the whole relief.\n\nA servant crossed the lower walk, glanced up, saw a Yellow Circle student idling with harmless shapes, and kept walking without asking for a demonstration or praising her stability.\n\nElira turned her wrist. The seven sparks drifted down across the terrace and touched the flagstones without a sound. Wherever one landed, a hair-thin gold line traced what boots and rain had nearly worn off the stone: half an old practice circle, muddy heel scrapes, a crooked arrow, the spot where someone had chalked **AGAIN** and rubbed it out.\n\nThe light did not mend any of them. It just showed where they had been.\n\nElira watched the crooked lines warm the stone until nothing happened. No hidden system answered, and no crisis arrived to justify the spell.\n\nWhen the tenth minute ended, she closed her fingers and let the Yellow Circle fade. She tucked the notebook under her arm and went inside before anyone could ask what the light had been for.\n\n---\n\nEvening came without asking permission.\n\nThe cottage lamps lit themselves one by one as the sky behind the trees bruised violet. Maelin had left two covered bowls on the table beside a note reading **eat before arguing ethics with furniture**. Beneath it, Aira had added in smaller ink: **furniture declined comment pending counsel**.\n\nElira read both notes and made the quiet sound that had almost become laughter again.\n\nWe ate at the front window because the table was there, the soup was warm, and neither of us had the patience to carry dinner somewhere more meaningful. Outside, council aides changed shifts along the garden path, and two first-years walked past arguing over whether Halden had technically taught outdoors or committed architecture. Near the fountain, someone stopped to copy Mira's note into a slate.\n\nElira's spoon clicked softly against her bowl. Beneath my ribs, the Black Circle noticed and stayed down, listening the way something dangerous listens to rain once it learns rain is not an attack.\n\nI kept my wrist resting on the table where she could see it. The black-and-silver ribbon lay against the wood, one loose tail slipped free from the day's work. Elira noticed that too.\n\nShe finished three bites before speaking.\n\nElira: \"Your knot is loose.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nShe reached across the table slowly enough that asking was built into the reach. I turned my wrist up.\n\nHer fingers were warm as she retied the ribbon, smoothing the twist rather than pulling it tight until the silver edge caught the lamplight without glaring. The Black Circle shifted once under my skin, closer to memory than threat.\n\nElira let go when the knot held.\n\nWe ate the rest of the bowls in quiet that did not feel fragile. Fear was still in the room, just given a chair and conditions instead of the whole house. A spoon stayed a spoon. Soup stayed dinner because Maelin had threatened both of us, and hunger had not turned political yet.\n\nOutside, the bell tower marked the evening hour at a bearable volume.\n\nElira glanced toward the dark window, then back at me.\n\nElira: \"Leave the lamp on.\"\n\n## Coda: Haru and Elira - First Bell\n\nAt dawn, the academy lifted the volume cap and tried a full morning bell for the first time since the rescue.\n\nIt rang badly.\n\nThe east tower started half a breath early. The central spire answered a pitch too low. When the west dormitory bell took its turn, the bronze cracked sharp on the third note and shuddered into silence like someone clearing their throat in front of history.\n\nStartled laughter carried up the east garden path from students heading out of the dorms, ragged and uncoordinated before fear could twist the sour note into an omen.\n\nHaru stood at the witness cottage window with one hand on the frame, waiting for the Black Circle to mistake the clangor for an alarm. It stirred, listened to the laughter beneath the iron echo, and settled back down.\n\nElira came up beside him with a blanket pulled over her shoulders and sleep still softening her face.\n\nElira: \"Terrible bell.\"\n\nHaru: \"Structurally brave.\"\n\nShe looked sideways at him.\n\nElira: \"That was almost a joke.\"\n\nHaru: \"I regret it already.\"\n\nAcross the garden, the cracked bell tolled once more, off-pitch and unapologetic, and nothing followed except morning.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-34-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep35","label":"Episode 29","number":"29","file":"episode-35-cover.webp","fallback":"Fear Has a Shape","eyebrow":"Trauma","recap":"","accent":["#0f172a","#e0f2fe","#a78bfa"],"displayLabel":"Episode 29","displayNumber":"29","displayEyebrow":"The Fifth Tower","arcLabel":"The Fifth Tower","title":"What Cael Kept","body":"The room did not belong to Cael anymore.\n\nThat was the first lie the morning offered, and the easiest one to refuse.\n\nThe eastern wing had stayed quiet since the funeral, though never empty. Aurelis did not know how to be empty while people were still trying to survive inside it. Healers crossed the corridor with trays. Council aides moved in careful pairs. Green Circle students carried copied witness statements between archive tables and public boards. Somewhere below us, the old annex still held its breath around the places Noctis had tried to make children disappear cleanly.\n\nCael's room was quiet in a different way. The door stood half-open.\n\nNo drill-master waited behind it. No obedience line burned against the threshold. Noctis Dominion College did not own the first sound of the morning.\n\nIt did not own the silence either.\n\nI stood outside with Elira beside me and Leon on my other side, close enough that his shoulder nearly touched the doorframe. He had not entered. None of us had. Maelin had unlocked the room herself, then stepped back with the blunt discretion of a healer who understood that some wounds needed witnesses and some needed air before they could become either.\n\nMaelin: \"Eight minutes.\"\n\nLeon did not look at her.\n\nLeon: \"Is that medical advice?\"\n\nMaelin: \"It is a threat wearing a clock.\"\n\nCassian leaned a shoulder against the opposite wall behind us, keeping his voice low.\n\nCassian: \"Good to see institutional consistency.\"\n\nAira's pencil moved once, then stopped. Even she knew some first lines should not be stolen too quickly. Elira looked at me.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nHer voice held the question no one else wanted to ask. Do you need to be outside? Do you need to be inside? Do you need to pretend either answer is simple?\n\nI looked through the door.\n\nThe bed was made. Too neatly. Cael had never slept in it. He had died at the Wounded City before this room could become anything more than an assigned shelter. The folded uniform still hung from the back of a chair. A water glass stood on the small table beside the bed. Someone had left the curtains half-open, and morning light touched the narrow support beam badly painted across the ceiling. Small things.\n\nNoctis had taught Cael that small things were tests. Warm food meant gratitude would be demanded. An unlocked door meant a better lock waited elsewhere. A quiet room meant someone was listening for the wrong breath.\n\nAurelis had tried to teach him otherwise, and had run out of time.\n\nThat thought entered me hard enough that the Black Circle stirred under my shirt.\n\nElira's hand moved near mine without touching. Choice.\n\nI breathed until the dark settled.\n\nHaru: \"Leon should enter first.\"\n\nLeon flinched as if I had struck him.\n\nLeon: \"Why?\"\n\nThe answer was not tactical, which made it harder to say.\n\nHaru: \"Because you learned beside.\"\n\nLeon stared at the room. His jaw tightened. For a moment, old pride rose in him, looking for a way to make grief angry enough to stand upright. Then it gave way without noise. He nodded once and stepped over the threshold.\n\nHe did not sit on the bed. He did not touch the uniform. He crossed the room slowly, hands open at his sides, and stopped beside the chair. Beside.\n\nAira wrote the word down with no ornament.\n\nOn the desk lay a folded paper.\n\nIt was neither sealed nor hidden, folded once under a small gray stone that kept the window draft from lifting it. The stone was ordinary river-smooth slate, the kind students picked up near Lake Velis because their hands needed something to hold while adults argued over the future.\n\nMira had found it during inventory and refused to open it without witnesses. That was why we were here.\n\nLeon looked at the paper and did not reach.\n\nLeon: \"Who is supposed to read it?\"\n\nNo one answered right away.\n\nIn Noctis, silence would have turned into rank. The highest seat would decide, the rest would obey, and afterward everyone would pretend the decision had always been obvious.\n\nHere, the silence stayed difficult.\n\nRhea stood just outside the threshold in full council uniform, her red Circle lowered but visible. Nera and Oric waited farther down the corridor, close enough to witness, far enough not to crowd the room Noctis had never let Cael own. Mira held two blank witness copies against her chest. Finn stood behind her with ink on his thumb and tight fear across his face. Elira stepped forward.\n\nElira: \"No one is supposed to read it.\"\n\nLeon turned.\n\nElira: \"That is the point. It was left where it could be found, not forced into anyone's hand.\"\n\nAira's pencil resumed across her page.\n\nAira: \"Voluntary recovery of personal record.\"\n\nMira: \"Not recovery.\"\n\nEveryone looked at her. Mira went red, but she held her ground.\n\nMira: \"Sorry. I mean... recovery sounds like it was lost by accident. Noctis loses things by design.\"\n\nRhea's expression sharpened.\n\nRhea: \"Correction accepted.\"\n\nAira crossed out one word.\n\nAira: \"Voluntary recognition of personal record.\"\n\nLeon looked at the paper again.\n\nLeon: \"Then ask him.\"\n\nThe words landed so hard the room seemed to lose air.\n\nCassian closed his eyes. Leon swallowed.\n\nLeon: \"I know. I know we can't. I just...\"\n\nHis hand curled once, then opened.\n\nLeon: \"He spent his whole life being answered over. I do not want to do it now because he is dead.\"\n\nElira's face steadied, grief sharpening into resolve.\n\nElira: \"Then we ask what he already answered.\"\n\nShe nodded toward the desk.\n\nHaru: \"Mira.\"\n\nMira startled.\n\nHaru: \"Did Cael ever tell you what he wanted done with personal corrections?\"\n\nMira held her notebook tighter.\n\nMira: \"He said ugly copies survived better.\"\n\nAira made a sound caught somewhere between laughter and pain.\n\nMira: \"He also said... he said if something was written before guilt edited it, we should not make it prettier.\"\n\nLeon looked down.\n\nLeon: \"Then open it ugly.\"\n\nRhea entered only far enough to set a witness seal on the desk. It did not touch the paper. It marked the dark wood beside it.\n\nRhea: \"Record recognizes Cael Nox as deceased Protected Witness of Aurelis. Personal document will be opened by voluntary witness circle. Noctis has no custody claim over contents unless named by the writer.\"\n\nFrom the corridor, Nera's breath caught at the word deceased. Oric stood beside her, never stepping in front. Rhea looked at Leon.\n\nRhea: \"You may decline.\"\n\nLeon laughed once, quietly and without humor.\n\nLeon: \"I know.\"\n\nHe picked up the stone first.\n\nNo magic answered. No old law flared. No hidden command line opened beneath the desk. Only a stone, warm from morning sunlight.\n\nLeon set it beside the witness seal, then unfolded the paper.\n\nThere were not many words. That hurt worse than a long letter would have.\n\nCael's handwriting was controlled almost to the point of damage, each letter trained into obedience and then forced, gently, into something less perfect.\n\nLeon read aloud because his voice was the one that shook most honestly.\n\nLeon: \"If I do not survive the next correction, do not let Noctis call that proof that correction was wrong.\"\n\nHe stopped. No one pressed him. After a breath, he kept reading.\n\nLeon: \"I was not rescued so I could be useful. I am writing this because I keep forgetting. If I forget permanently, keep the sentence ugly. Do not make me noble enough that Noctis can admire me.\"\n\nAira put one hand over her mouth.\n\nLeon read the last lines.\n\nLeon: \"I wanted to live. I chose anyway. Both are true.\"\n\nThe room held the sentence awkwardly, without neat edges.\n\nElira's eyes filled, and she did not wipe them. Haru Aerlen, Black Circle magician, world-breaking threat, surviving witness, stood in a dead boy's borrowed room with no power capable of mending a single word. That was as it should be.\n\nMira copied the statement by hand. Then Finn copied Mira's copy, blotting the word noble so badly he had to write it again in the margin. No one corrected him. The blot belonged to the record too.\n\nNera entered after the second copy was complete.\n\nShe did not ask to see the original. She stood before the desk and bowed, neither the Noctis obedience bow nor the formal Aurelis condolence angle, just something unfinished and human.\n\nNera: \"He is right.\"\n\nOric followed her across the threshold.\n\nOric: \"Noctis will try to use the sentence anyway.\"\n\nRhea: \"Yes.\"\n\nHer red seal stayed flat on the dark wood of the desk. Leon looked up sharply.\n\nRhea: \"And because he named that danger, they will have to do it while his refusal sits in the same public record.\"\n\nAira lowered her hand.\n\nAira: \"Ugly copy survives.\"\n\nCassian leaned against the doorframe with his arms folded too tightly.\n\nCassian: \"We are making that a doctrine, apparently.\"\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nEveryone looked at her. Elira touched the pendant at her throat. Its seven open lines remained quiet beneath her fingers.\n\nElira: \"Doctrine is too clean.\"\n\nHaru: \"Practice, then.\"\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nElira: \"Practice.\"\n\nThe witness seal accepted the word without brightening. That felt right.\n\nLater, the Council would ask for copies. Noctis would object to the term deceased Protected Witness because even death did not stop them from searching for ownership grammar. The public boards would carry three versions of Cael's statement: one formal, one handwritten, one blotched by Finn's shaking pen. Students would stop before the line I wanted to live. I chose anyway. Both are true. Some would cry. Some would argue. Some would copy it badly into notebooks because bad copies were still copies, and perfect silence had served Noctis long enough.\n\nFirst, we stood in the room. Eight minutes became twelve. Maelin did not interrupt.\n\nThe folded uniform stayed on the chair. The water glass stayed on the table. The badly painted beam held morning light like a mistake no one had bothered to hide. On the desk, the slate stone sat beside the witness seal, ordinary and stubborn.\n\nLeon picked it up again.\n\nLeon: \"Can I keep this?\"\n\nRhea answered carefully.\n\nRhea: \"It is not Council property.\"\n\nLeon looked at Elira.\n\nElira: \"It was holding his page down.\"\n\nLeon looked at me.\n\nI thought of Cael in the corridor, telling us stable meant quiet enough to move. I thought of his voice in the trial, the way he had learned to say no with his whole body already expecting punishment. I thought of the room he had not lived long enough to trust.\n\nHaru: \"Then let it hold something else now.\"\n\nLeon closed his fingers around the stone. For once, he did not ask what. That was its own answer.\n\n---\n\nWe carried the copies down to the Grand Courtyard before midday, and the public board refused the first one.\n\nNot with magic. That would have been easier to hate. It refused bureaucratically.\n\nThe relay slate above the courtyard accepted the heading, accepted the witness date, accepted Rhea's seal, accepted the phrase personal document, and then dimmed when Aira tried to enter Cael's own words. The surface turned pale gray. A polite warning appeared in the center.\n\n**DECEASED STUDENT STATEMENT REQUIRES NEXT-OF-INSTITUTION REVIEW.**\n\nFor three seconds, everyone in the courtyard went very still.\n\nThe courtyard had been learning that stillness was dangerous. Stillness gave old systems time to dress harm in procedure. Students gathered near the fountain, along the outdoor stair rails, under the silver-leaf trees, and around the lower Green Circle notice posts. They had followed us from the eastern wing at a distance that tried to look respectful and failed because grief made everyone terrible at pretending.\n\nMira stood with the handwritten copy pressed flat against her chest. Finn stared at the pale gray glass.\n\nFinn: \"Next of institution?\"\n\nAira's expression became so calm I knew violence had passed through her and chosen handwriting as its weapon.\n\nAira: \"No.\"\n\nThe board brightened, as if encouraged by response.\n\n**NOCTIS DOMINION COLLEGE LISTED AS LAST REGISTERED ORIGIN INSTITUTION.**\n\nLeon stepped forward.\n\nRhea caught his sleeve with two fingers before he could put his fist through academy property.\n\nRhea: \"Do not give it damage to report.\"\n\nLeon: \"It just called Noctis his family.\"\n\nRhea: \"It called Noctis his paperwork.\"\n\nLeon looked at her, waiting for comfort. Rhea did not soften the difference into comfort.\n\nRhea: \"We are going to correct the paperwork.\"\n\nElira had gone pale with anger, Yellow Circle light gathering beneath her skin like sunrise behind storm glass. Maelin, standing behind her with a healer's bag and the posture of a woman prepared to tackle aristocracy if necessary, narrowed her eyes.\n\nMaelin: \"Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That was not an accusation. It was a reminder that your pulse has opinions.\"\n\nElira breathed once through her nose, then looked at the board.\n\nElira: \"The board is using inheritance logic.\"\n\nHalden, who had arrived with three archive slates and no apparent ability to stand somewhere unobtrusive, answered immediately.\n\nHalden: \"Custodial logic. Similar root, uglier application.\"\n\nCassian: \"Excellent. The board has ancestry issues.\"\n\nHalden ignored him because Professor Halden had discovered one could survive Cassian only by refusing to feed him.\n\nHalden: \"Older inter-school transfer law assumed a student remained under origin institution custody until graduation or formal replacement of guardianship. Aurelis patched the language after the Foundation Trial. The public board is likely reading from pre-patch archive substrate.\"\n\nAira: \"Likely?\"\n\nHalden: \"I am trying not to accuse the board of malice without evidence.\"\n\nThe board flickered again.\n\n**NOCTIS DOMINION COLLEGE LISTED AS LAST REGISTERED ORIGIN INSTITUTION.**\n\nAira: \"Evidence is gaining confidence.\"\n\nStudents murmured around the fountain. Mira turned toward the Green Circle cluster.\n\nMira: \"Copy the warning.\"\n\nFinn blinked.\n\nMira: \"Exactly. Before it changes.\"\n\nInk moved. Paper unfolded. The Green Circle notice steps filled with hands, pencils, cheap pens, borrowed styluses, and one student writing on the back of a lunch receipt because no one had told him evidence preferred dignity. The board dimmed.\n\nThen, very slowly, it displayed a second line.\n\n**OVERRIDE AVAILABLE BY BLOOD RELATION, ORIGIN INSTITUTION, OR COUNCIL CUSTODY AUTHORITY.**\n\nThe word custody struck the courtyard like a bell hit wrong.\n\nNera made a small sound behind us.\n\nOric stood beside her, not in front, while his hand opened at his side in a way that made every Noctis-trained student nearby watch it. Darian Thorne, once Noctis enough that politics still tasted his name before trusting it, looked at the ground with a face so controlled it became confession.\n\nRhea lifted her council seal, her voice level across the courtyard.\n\nRhea: \"Aurelis Council Authority rejects custody framing. Enter Protected Witness continuity.\"\n\nThe board accepted her seal. Then refused the wording.\n\n**PROTECTED WITNESS STATUS TERMINATED BY DEATH.**\n\nThe Black Circle stirred beneath my skin, invisible still, waiting.\n\nCourtyard lamps dimmed by a fraction. The fountain water stopped moving. Every old stone beneath my feet became aware of me at once. Elira's fingers closed over mine, an anchor rather than a restraint.\n\nHaru: \"It is wrong.\"\n\nMy voice came out quiet enough that only the nearest ring heard. Quiet gave the words room.\n\nHaru: \"Witness does not end because the witness can no longer defend the record.\"\n\nThe board listened. So did Aurelis. So did everyone else.\n\nHalden's eyes moved from me to the fountain, then to the old crest above the central arch. His scholar's fear and scholar's hunger stood side by side on his face.\n\nHalden: \"Say that again carefully.\"\n\nI did not look at him.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThat surprised him. Good.\n\nHaru: \"Not from me.\"\n\nI turned to Leon. He looked furious enough to shatter and frightened enough to listen.\n\nHaru: \"You.\"\n\nLeon stared at me.\n\nLeon: \"I am not council.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nLeon: \"I am not a legal witness.\"\n\nRhea spoke up beside us.\n\nRhea: \"You opened and read Cael Nox's personal document under Aurelis witness seal.\"\n\nLeon flinched at the name.\n\nRhea: \"That gives you standing to object to misclassification of his words.\"\n\nLeon laughed once, bitter.\n\nLeon: \"Standing.\"\n\nElira: \"Beside is standing.\"\n\nThe courtyard held that too.\n\nLeon looked at the board, at the warning, at the word terminated as if it had insulted someone in a language he had only just learned to hate properly. He stepped forward.\n\nHis Blue Circle rose at his wrist, dull and ragged at the edge because grief refused to channel cleanly.\n\nLeon: \"Cael Nox is dead.\"\n\nThe words hurt because he did not soften them.\n\nLeon: \"That does not make Noctis his answer.\"\n\nThe board flickered. Leon swallowed.\n\nLeon: \"He wrote a statement before he died. He said not to make him noble enough that Noctis could admire him. If this board sends his words to Noctis for permission, it is doing exactly what he refused.\"\n\nHis voice cracked on refused. No one moved to repair it.\n\nLeon: \"Enter objection. Personal witness. Leon Varek. Beside.\"\n\nThe board hesitated, stalling between acceptance and rejection.\n\nOld systems hated being addressed by language they had not been built to digest. Mira raised her hand.\n\nMira: \"Additional objection.\"\n\nRhea turned.\n\nRhea: \"Mira Castellan recognized.\"\n\nMira stepped onto the lowest fountain stair. Her Green Circle showed at her wrist, small and bright and shaking.\n\nMira: \"Record witness. Cael Nox verbally instructed that ugly copies survive better and corrections written before guilt edited them should not be made prettier. Returning his statement to Noctis is an editorial act.\"\n\nAira's whisper barely carried past my elbow.\n\nAira: \"Oh, that is good.\"\n\nFinn wrote so fast his pen skidded. Mira's voice steadied.\n\nMira: \"Enter objection. Green Circle public witness.\"\n\nThe board flickered again. This time, the warning changed.\n\n**CONFLICTING WITNESS CLAIMS DETECTED.**\n\nCassian exhaled.\n\nCassian: \"There it is.\"\n\nAira: \"What?\"\n\nCassian: \"The system admitting more than one person exists. Historic.\"\n\nRhea's seal brightened.\n\nRhea: \"Enter Council clarification. Death does not terminate witness continuity where active coercion, institutional custody dispute, or hostile terminology risk is present.\"\n\nThe board tried to dim. Halden stepped forward.\n\nHalden: \"Enter faculty archive note. Pre-patch substrate conflicts with current Aurelis protected record standard. Board has no authority to privilege origin institution over documented final witness preference.\"\n\nElira stepped beside Leon.\n\nElira: \"Enter opening witness. Elira Vale. Cael Nox wrote, I wanted to live. I chose anyway. Both are true. Any review that treats death as consent to Noctis custody contradicts the statement.\"\n\nThe board flashed white.\n\nFor one breath, I thought it would break.\n\nThen older script surfaced beneath the modern warning, carved in pale light from inside the stone itself.\n\n**DEAD WITNESS STILL WITNESS.**\n\nThe grammar was ugly. Older than standardization. More honest than the board had been all morning.\n\nThe fountain water began moving again.\n\nGreen Circle students copied the line so quickly the courtyard filled with scratching.\n\nAira stared at it with open hunger.\n\nAira: \"I am in love with terrible grammar.\"\n\nCassian: \"Do we notify Haru?\"\n\nElira looked at me, and despite everything, her mouth trembled toward a smile.\n\nThe Black Circle settled. Not because the danger had passed, but because the answer had not needed me alone.\n\nThe board accepted the statement, though not gracefully.\n\n**PERSONAL RECORD ENTERED UNDER WITNESS CONTINUITY.**\n\nThen, after a pause that felt spiteful:\n\n**ORIGIN INSTITUTION REVIEW BYPASSED.**\n\nBreath returned to the courtyard unevenly: a sob from the Green steps, a laugh too sharp from Cassian, Leon bending forward with both hands on his knees, Nera covering her mouth, Oric closing his eyes, Rhea lowering her seal before her hand could shake publicly.\n\nElira leaned against me for half a second. Only half. Enough.\n\nThen a relay bell rang from the council arch. Rhea's expression closed.\n\nRhea: \"Noctis response.\"\n\nAira: \"Already?\"\n\nRhea looked toward the accepted record.\n\nRhea: \"They were waiting for the board to refuse.\"\n\nThe bell rang again. This time, the fountain displayed the incoming seal.\n\nNoctis Dominion College. Formal black. Silver edge.\n\nLucien's voice did not come through first. That made me colder.\n\nInstead, an older woman appeared in the relay projection. Her hair was iron gray, pinned so tightly it seemed less styled than disciplined. Her uniform bore Noctis rank marks I did not recognize, yet Nera went still enough that recognition passed through the students behind us like a draft.\n\nNera: \"Director Vaust.\"\n\nRhea heard.\n\nRhea: \"Identify for record.\"\n\nNera swallowed.\n\nNera: \"Deputy Director of Corrective Continuity. She trained Lucien.\"\n\nCassian's smile vanished.\n\nDirector Vaust looked over the courtyard projection as if counting weaknesses by posture.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Aurelis has entered a deceased Noctis student's private document without origin review.\"\n\nRhea answered before anyone else could.\n\nRhea: \"Aurelis has entered a deceased Protected Witness's personal statement under witness continuity.\"\n\nDirector Vaust: \"You are relying on a disputed category.\"\n\nRhea: \"We are relying on a recorded choice.\"\n\nDirector Vaust's gaze moved to Leon.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Leon Varek.\"\n\nLeon straightened.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Your family will be disappointed to see you lend your name to a procedural disturbance.\"\n\nLeon went very still.\n\nThere it was. Not a blade, a hook. Family, disappointment, reputation, the old vocabulary of making obedience sound like love. Leon opened his mouth. Nothing came out. Elira started to move.\n\nHe lifted one hand slightly. Stop. She stopped.\n\nLeon looked at Director Vaust.\n\nLeon: \"My family has survived worse than my first correct sentence.\"\n\nAira made a wounded sound of admiration.\n\nDirector Vaust did not blink.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Cleverness is not standing.\"\n\nMira stepped forward.\n\nMira: \"Neither is ownership.\"\n\nThe whole courtyard seemed to realize at once that a Green Circle student had interrupted a Noctis director in public.\n\nDirector Vaust's gaze moved to her.\n\nShe did not sneer or raise her voice. She simply looked, and every habit Aurelis had taught Green students about shrinking backward pulled at Mira's posture. Finn moved beside her. Then another Green student.\n\nThen two more. No one stood in front.\n\nMira's hands shook. Her voice did not.\n\nMira: \"Cael Nox's statement names the risk of Noctis admiration. Your objection confirms relevance.\"\n\nDirector Vaust: \"And you are?\"\n\nMira lifted her notebook.\n\nMira: \"Still copying.\"\n\nFor one second, Cassian looked like he might propose marriage to the sentence as a concept. Director Vaust's eyes cooled.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Aurelis has allowed public witnesses to mistake emotional participation for legal competence.\"\n\nHalden answered, his voice carrying the dry flatness of a man discovering someone had insulted his favorite kind of evidence.\n\nHalden: \"Public witness is not legal competence. It is evidentiary distribution. You may challenge interpretation. You cannot unmake copies already observed by independent parties.\"\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Professor Halden. I expected better rigor from you.\"\n\nHalden: \"Then you expected flattery.\"\n\nAira actually stopped breathing for a second.\n\nRhea's mouth twitched and died before becoming expression.\n\nDirector Vaust ignored the insult with professional discipline.\n\nHer gaze moved at last to me.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nThe courtyard changed temperature. Elira's hand tightened around mine.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Do you endorse this misuse of a dead student's name?\"\n\nThere were many traps in the question.\n\nEndorse turned me into authority. Misuse turned defense into desecration. Dead student's name turned Cael into an object I could either claim or abandon.\n\nLucien had learned from her. That was obvious now.\n\nI looked at the relay projection and felt every eye in the courtyard arrive at my back.\n\nThe Black Circle did not rise. That was my answer before words.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nDirector Vaust's expression did not change, though the courtyard shifted around me as fear gave way to confusion. I continued.\n\nHaru: \"I do not endorse Cael's statement. It does not need my endorsement.\"\n\nLeon looked at me.\n\nHaru: \"I witnessed it being read. I object to Noctis custody. I refuse to become the authority that makes his words valid.\"\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Convenient restraint.\"\n\nHaru: \"Difficult restraint.\"\n\nElira's thumb moved once across my knuckle.\n\nHaru: \"He wanted to live. He chose anyway. Both are true. If Noctis wants to challenge that, challenge the sentence. Do not ask me to stand over it so you can accuse me of casting a shadow.\"\n\nDirector Vaust watched me for a long moment. Then she smiled.\n\nIt was not Lucien's smile. Lucien smiled like a blade pretending to be a mirror. Vaust smiled like a locked door that had never needed to pretend.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Very well. Noctis challenges the sentence.\"\n\nNera's breath gave way in a sharp whisper.\n\nNera: \"No.\"\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Cael Nox could not simultaneously want life and choose death under coercive conditions created by Aurelis proximity. The statement is emotionally compelling, legally unstable, and medically suspect. Noctis requests suspension of publication pending spirit-state verification.\"\n\nMaelin's healer bag hit the stone with a thud.\n\nMaelin: \"Absolutely not.\"\n\nDirector Vaust looked at her.\n\nMaelin: \"No one is using necromantic adjacent verification language on a dead child because you dislike his handwriting.\"\n\nThe courtyard went silent enough to hear the fountain.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Healer...\"\n\nMaelin: \"Senior Restoration Healer Maelin Orr. Medical objection. Ethical objection. Human objection, if your office still accepts those in any format.\"\n\nCassian leaned slightly toward me, whispering out of the corner of his mouth.\n\nCassian: \"I am definitely notifying Haru.\"\n\nI did not look away from Vaust. Rhea stepped forward.\n\nRhea: \"Aurelis rejects spirit-state verification. Cael Nox's death was witnessed, recorded, and ritually completed. Noctis will not reopen his death as a route to overwrite his life.\"\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Then Noctis files formal complaint.\"\n\nRhea: \"Accepted.\"\n\nDirector Vaust: \"And requests immediate sequestration of all copies.\"\n\nMira, Finn, Aira, and half the courtyard answered together, ragged and loud.\n\nStudents: \"No.\"\n\nIt was neither neat nor rehearsed, and Rhea did not smile.\n\nRhea: \"Denied.\"\n\nDirector Vaust's projection dimmed at the edges.\n\nBefore it vanished, she looked at me one last time.\n\nDirector Vaust: \"Power surrounded by mourners is still power, Haru Aerlen.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe answer caught her off guard more than a denial would have.\n\nHaru: \"That is why they are allowed to say no.\"\n\nThe projection winked out without thunder or threat, leaving only the accepted statement on the board, the old line beneath it, and the sound of too many students breathing like people who had just learned procedure could be survived if enough hands refused to let go of the page.\n\nThen Leon sat down on the fountain step. Hard.\n\nCassian moved first, disguising it as impatience.\n\nCassian: \"Elegant collapse. Very Blue Circle. Excellent posture until the end.\"\n\nLeon covered his face.\n\nLeon: \"Shut up.\"\n\nCassian sat beside him.\n\nCassian: \"Beside shut up or distant shut up?\"\n\nLeon laughed, and it came out broken. Cassian looked at the board and stopped joking.\n\nFor several minutes, no one asked the courtyard to move on. That became its own kind of ceremony.\n\nStudents approached the board one at a time. Not to bow or worship, only to copy. Some wrote the full statement. Some wrote only one line. Some copied the old grammar: Dead witness still witness. A Yellow Circle student hesitated before the Green steps, then asked Finn if he had spare paper. Finn gave him the lunch receipt copy without thinking. The Yellow student stared at it, then accepted it like a relic with grease stains.\n\nNera stood at the edge of the fountain, reading Cael's sentence again and again. Oric remained beside her.\n\nDarian watched them both with the look of someone realizing defection did not end when one left an institution. It continued each time one refused to carry the institution's habits into a safer room.\n\nElira leaned more heavily against me. Maelin noticed instantly.\n\nMaelin: \"Vale.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Your knowing has historically poor compliance.\"\n\nElira closed her eyes for half a breath.\n\nElira: \"Two minutes.\"\n\nMaelin: \"One.\"\n\nElira: \"Ninety seconds.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Because grief apparently negotiates.\"\n\nI almost smiled. Then Elira whispered against my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"I hate that he had to write it.\"\n\nThe almost-smile disappeared.\n\nHaru: \"So do I.\"\n\nI held Elira's gaze without looking away.\n\nElira: \"I hate that it is good.\"\n\nI understood that too. Sometimes a true sentence was beautiful because harm had cornered someone into making it exact. Admiring it felt too close to accepting the corner.\n\nHaru: \"Then we do not call it beautiful.\"\n\nShe opened her eyes.\n\nHaru: \"We call it kept.\"\n\nElira looked at the board.\n\nElira: \"Kept.\"\n\nMaelin gave us the ninety seconds and pretended it was because she needed to repack her bag.\n\nAt the end of them, Aira approached with three copies in her hands.\n\nAira: \"Formal, handwritten, ugly.\"\n\nElira: \"Those are the categories?\"\n\nAira: \"They are now.\"\n\nShe gave the formal copy to Rhea, the handwritten one to Mira, and the ugly copy to Leon.\n\nLeon looked at the blotched page.\n\nLeon: \"Why me?\"\n\nAira: \"Because if I give you the neat one, you will try to become worthy of it, and I am too tired to watch that.\"\n\nLeon stared at her, then folded the ugly copy with more care than he had used for any academy assignment in his life.\n\nLeon: \"Fine.\"\n\nCassian leaned over.\n\nCassian: \"Frame it badly.\"\n\nLeon: \"I will burn you.\"\n\nCassian: \"There he is.\"\n\nTension eased across the courtyard without pretending to heal. There was a difference, and for once the difference did not feel like failure.\n\nRhea placed a protective seal over the public board at noon, leaving the glass clear.\n\nThe statement remained visible behind ward-light while copies continued to multiply through hands the institution had once trained itself to ignore. Green Circle students made the first ring. Blue students joined next. A few Red. Yellow last, as usual, though not never.\n\nBy early afternoon, Dead witness still witness had appeared in six margins, three formal objections, one badly drawn poster, and a chalk line on the lower fountain stone that Dren walked past twice without ordering removed.\n\nNot a doctrine, as Elira had said, but a practice, something imperfect hands could repeat and keep honest.\n\n---\n\nThat evening, after Maelin forced Elira back to the witness cottage and Rhea disappeared into what Aira called the paperwork furnace, I returned to Cael's room alone. Or as close to alone as anyone let me be anymore, and they were right.\n\nCassian stood in the corridor with his back to the wall, pretending to inspect a ceiling crack. He had two knives hidden on him and one pastry openly in hand. Leon sat three doors down with the slate stone in his palm, staring at nothing. Aira occupied the hall bench with a notebook and the posture of a person who would claim she happened to be there if anyone asked.\n\nElira had wanted to come.\n\nMaelin had said no in a tone that made ancient magic reconsider its priorities.\n\nSo I entered without her.\n\nThe room looked smaller in evening light. Maybe it was. Rooms changed after records entered them: some became shrines, some became evidence, some became traps. I did not want Cael's room to become any of those things if I could help it.\n\nI stood by the desk and looked at the empty space where the letter had been.\n\nPower offered me several impossible kindnesses. It could trace the pressure of his hand on the paper, draw a shadow of the last minute he spent here, force the walls to answer in images bright enough to hurt. The Black Circle knew ways to make absence speak. That was the problem.\n\nAbsence was allowed to be silent.\n\nI placed my hand flat on the desk.\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nNo magic answered. Good.\n\nThe top drawer handle caught my eye.\n\nI left it shut. We had not emptied the desk after the morning reading, and I had no right to search a dead boy's private drawers alone when stripping every trace from the room felt too much like clearing it for the next occupant.\n\nInstead, I sat in the chair.\n\nNot the companion chair from the annex. This one was plainer, narrower, and badly balanced. It creaked under me like protest.\n\nFor several breaths, I let myself imagine the life Cael had not had time to mistrust. Breakfast without evaluation.\n\nClasses where wrong answers did not become discipline.\n\nLeon asking terrible questions and learning better ones.\n\nAira forcing him to annotate his own sarcasm for public record.\n\nElira teaching him that no could be gentle without becoming weak.\n\nMe learning whether he preferred tea or coffee or neither because Noctis had probably ruined both by attaching them to schedules. Small things.\n\nThe Black Circle stirred with grief sharp enough to become weather. I closed my eyes.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nThe word was not for Cael. It was for me: no restoration, no echo, no bending the room until loss became easier to look at.\n\nElira had died in my arms and come back because death had been forced through a hostile route still attached to life. Cael had chosen in a field of consequences none of us could make clean. Those were not the same. If I blurred them because grief wanted one more miracle, I would make every future death a referendum on whether I had loved hard enough to break the world.\n\nThat way lay worship, and hatred, and madness wearing mercy.\n\nI opened my eyes. The room remained cruelly ordinary.\n\nCassian spoke from the doorway.\n\nCassian: \"You still in there?\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nThe cold pulse behind my ribs stayed quiet.\n\nCassian: \"Existentially or medically?\"\n\nHaru: \"Both.\"\n\nHe stepped in without crossing fully past the threshold.\n\nCassian: \"Good. Maelin requested clarification before threatening me.\"\n\nHaru: \"How considerate.\"\n\nHe looked at the desk, the bed, the folded uniform. His face changed the way it did when joking reached the edge of usefulness and politely stepped back.\n\nCassian: \"I did not know him well enough.\"\n\nThere were many comforting lies available. I chose none.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nCassian let the answer sit without flinching.\n\nCassian: \"Still angry.\"\n\nHaru: \"Yes.\"\n\nMy fingers curled once against my coat hem before I forced them flat.\n\nCassian: \"Good.\"\n\nI looked at him. Cassian shrugged.\n\nCassian: \"Not because anger is noble. It is not. Mostly it is loud and bad at furniture. But if we skip it, Noctis gets to decide his death was meaningful enough that we are not allowed to be furious.\"\n\nThe chair creaked as I leaned back.\n\nHaru: \"Aira would make you write that down.\"\n\nCassian: \"Aira would improve the grammar and ruin my mystique.\"\n\nAira's voice carried in from the hall.\n\nAira: \"Your mystique has three spelling errors.\"\n\nCassian closed his eyes.\n\nCassian: \"See?\"\n\nFor the first time in Cael's room, I laughed, a small, rough sound that did not feel like betrayal. That mattered.\n\nWhen I left the room, Leon was still sitting three doors down with the stone in his palm. He looked up.\n\nLeon: \"Did you...\"\n\nHe did not finish. Did you see him? Did you feel anything? Did the room say goodbye? Did being Black Circle make death less final for you than for the rest of us?\n\nI sat beside him on the floor. Cassian stayed by the door, and Aira's pencil stilled.\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nLeon looked down at the stone.\n\nLeon: \"Good.\"\n\nThat was not the answer I expected. Leon swallowed.\n\nLeon: \"I thought I wanted something. A sign. A message. Anything. Then the board tried to send his words back to Noctis, and I realized... I do not want more of him taken for answers.\"\n\nThe stone sat in his palm, dull and gray and doing no magic at all.\n\nLeon: \"I can keep this without making it speak.\"\n\nHaru: \"You can.\"\n\nLeon nodded once, his shoulders giving a small, sharp shake. He did not apologize. That was also practice.\n\n---\n\nLater, Elira read the ugly copy in bed with a blanket around her shoulders and Maelin stationed outside the cottage door like a medical curse. I sat beside her, close enough for warmth, far enough that the page belonged to her hands. The lamp was low.\n\nThe cottage smelled faintly of cedar smoke and bitter tonic.\n\nElira read the sentence three times.\n\nI wanted to live. I chose anyway. Both are true.\n\nOn the third reading, tears slipped down her face.\n\nElira: \"I wish he had written something worse.\"\n\nHaru: \"Worse?\"\n\nElira: \"Messier. Petty. Unfair. Something that would make me angry at him instead of at everything.\"\n\nHer hand tightened around the page.\n\nElira: \"He was allowed to be unfair.\"\n\nI thought of Cael's controlled handwriting, trained almost to damage. Even his final refusal had tried not to take up too much room.\n\nHaru: \"Then we can be unfair for him sometimes.\"\n\nElira looked at me.\n\nHaru: \"Not in the record. In the room.\"\n\nShe folded forward until her forehead touched my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"I am furious that he saved me.\"\n\nThere it was. The sentence beneath all the careful ones.\n\nI put my hand near her back and waited until she leaned into it before touching.\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"I am grateful.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"I hate being grateful.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nThe Black Circle did not move. It had no answer for guilt that would not become violence if mishandled.\n\nElira cried quietly, which was worse than sobbing because quiet grief kept trying to be considerate.\n\nWhen she found her voice again, she spoke without lifting her head.\n\nElira: \"Do not let me make his death into a debt.\"\n\nHaru: \"I won't.\"\n\nElira lifted her head.\n\nElira: \"And do not make that your debt either.\"\n\nI almost looked away. She caught my face between both hands before I could.\n\nHer palms were warm. Still too thin from recovery. Still alive.\n\nElira: \"Haru.\"\n\nHaru: \"I hear you.\"\n\nElira: \"No. Answer.\"\n\nThe seven lines in her pendant glowed faintly. Witness, not command. I let out a slow breath.\n\nHaru: \"I will try not to make his death into my debt.\"\n\nElira: \"Try honestly?\"\n\nHaru: \"Try honestly.\"\n\nShe studied me, found the answer imperfect, and accepted it because perfect answers had done enough damage.\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nOutside, the academy bell rang evening close.\n\nIt sounded better than the morning bell. I disliked that.\n\nSome part of me wanted the world to remain audibly broken until it had earned repair. Yet bells were not moral instruments. They rang because someone pulled the line, because metal held shape, because sound did not know whom it had failed to mourn.\n\nElira leaned against me again.\n\nElira: \"Tomorrow, they will ask what the sentence means.\"\n\nHaru: \"They will.\"\n\nI stayed looking at her, steady enough that she did not have to brace for the answer.\n\nElira: \"What will we say?\"\n\nI looked at the ugly copy in her lap.\n\nHaru: \"That it means what it says.\"\n\nShe closed her eyes.\n\nElira: \"That will annoy everyone.\"\n\nHaru: \"Aira will enjoy it.\"\n\nElira laughed softly through the last of her tears.\n\nThat laugh did not heal anything.\n\nIt kept something from closing.\n\n---\n\nAt midnight, someone tried to erase the board.\n\nThe alarm did not ring like an attack. It clicked.\n\nOne small, offended sound from the cottage mirror, followed by Aira's voice bursting through the relay before the image had fully formed.\n\nAira: \"Courtyard. Now. Not sprinting if medically banned, but emotionally sprinting is authorized.\"\n\nMaelin's voice followed sharply down the hall.\n\nMaelin: \"Emotionally sprinting still affects pulse!\"\n\nElira was already sitting up.\n\nHaru: \"You are not running.\"\n\nElira: \"I am not running.\"\n\nShe stood with the careful speed of someone prepared to define running as a legal category and win on a technicality. I helped her into her outer robe because arguing would take longer than moving, and because she let me help after choosing to stand.\n\nBy the time we reached the Grand Courtyard, the night had turned silver with ward-light.\n\nThe public board still stood. That was the first relief. The second was uglier.\n\nAcross Cael's statement, someone had written in black correction script:\n\n**EMOTIONAL CONTAMINATION INVALIDATES FINAL DECLARATION.**\n\nThe words crawled over his sentence like frost with manners.\n\nGreen Circle students surrounded the board in a half-ring. Not guards exactly. Most were in sleep clothes under academy coats. Finn wore two mismatched shoes. Mira's hair was coming loose from its braid, and ink marked one cheek where she had clearly fallen asleep on a page before the alarm reached her.\n\nLeon stood directly before the board with the slate stone in his fist.\n\nCassian stood beside him, sword drawn but lowered.\n\nDren stood behind both of them in instructor black, arms folded, looking less like a teacher than a locked gate that had opinions.\n\nRhea arrived from the council arch with her seal already active.\n\nRhea: \"Report.\"\n\nMira stepped forward with her notebook open.\n\nMira: \"Board surface altered at 00:13. No visible intruder. Correction syntax resembles Noctis administrative challenge but lacks relay seal.\"\n\nAira knelt by the fountain base, pen already moving.\n\nAira: \"Because it is cowardly graffiti wearing a procedural hat.\"\n\nHalden stood on the other side of the board with three floating lenses rotating around the text.\n\nHalden: \"Not graffiti.\"\n\nAira looked up sharply.\n\nHalden: \"Residual substrate command. Someone planted it before the board accepted witness continuity. It activated when courtyard traffic dropped below twelve active observers.\"\n\nFinn stared.\n\nFinn: \"It waited until fewer people were watching?\"\n\nHalden: \"It did.\"\n\nHe squinted through his spectacles at the creeping black script.\n\nDren's voice cut through the cold.\n\nDren: \"Then we give it watchers.\"\n\nHe turned to the gathered students.\n\nDren: \"No crowding the board. No touching hostile script. No dramatic oaths. Pair by class year. Rotate every twenty minutes. If you fall asleep standing, I will classify you as furniture and assign homework to your friends.\"\n\nCassian leaned slightly toward me.\n\nCassian: \"Inspirational leadership.\"\n\nDren: \"Veyr.\"\n\nCassian: \"Moved.\"\n\nElira stepped closer to the hostile script.\n\nMaelin, who had followed us with a blanket and the expression of a thunderstorm with certification, made a warning sound. Elira stopped at the safe mark.\n\nThe correction script brightened when it sensed her.\n\nThat made the Black Circle go still beneath my ribs, not resting, aiming.\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nElira did not look away from the board.\n\nElira: \"It is trying to define grief as contamination.\"\n\nRhea's seal hardened.\n\nRhea: \"It is.\"\n\nHer red council seal held steady against the midnight stone.\n\nElira: \"Then don't erase it immediately.\"\n\nLeon turned.\n\nLeon: \"What?\"\n\nElira's face was pale in the ward-light, but her voice held.\n\nElira: \"If we remove it before people see what it tried to do, Noctis can claim the statement was accepted cleanly and later challenged by emotion. Let the correction indict itself.\"\n\nMira's eyes widened.\n\nMira: \"Copy hostile layer with timestamp.\"\n\nAira was already writing.\n\nAira: \"Copying. Hating. Copying more.\"\n\nFinn looked at the phrase again. He sounded less afraid now, more insulted.\n\nFinn: \"Emotional contamination. That is not even internally consistent.\"\n\nHalden's head turned with academic interest. Finn flushed, then kept going.\n\nFinn: \"If emotion invalidates final declaration, then Noctis funeral honors are invalid too. They use reverence language in death records all the time. Honored sacrifice. Noble closure. Loyal completion. Those are emotional terms.\"\n\nAira pointed at him with her pen.\n\nAira: \"Say that again slower.\"\n\nFinn did, stammering twice and growing steadier on the third sentence. Mira copied him. Then the Green students copied Mira. The hostile script kept crawling, yet every inch it moved produced more evidence against itself.\n\nLeon stared at the board.\n\nLeon: \"It waited until fewer than twelve observers.\"\n\nRhea: \"That appears to be the trigger.\"\n\nLeon looked at the half-awake Green students, the faculty, the council seals, Elira wrapped in a blanket and fury, me holding the Black Circle down by refusing to be the first answer.\n\nLeon: \"Then twelve is not enough.\"\n\nHe placed the slate stone at the base of the board. Not on the statement. Below it.\n\nLeon: \"I volunteer first watch.\"\n\nCassian sighed.\n\nCassian: \"Obviously I volunteer second, because if you stand here alone you will become tragic and unbearable.\"\n\nMira lifted her notebook.\n\nMira: \"Green Circle witness group volunteers rotating copy watch.\"\n\nFinn raised his hand.\n\nFinn: \"And grammar watch.\"\n\nDren looked at him. Finn shrank by one inch, then remembered the board had tried to erase a dead boy and straightened again.\n\nDren: \"Accepted.\"\n\nElira leaned into me, exhausted now that the first danger had found structure.\n\nHaru: \"You need to sit.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nThis time she sat down on the low fountain step without arguing. Maelin's relief was so aggressive it became almost hostile.\n\nThe hostile script reached Cael's final line.\n\n**I WANTED TO LIVE. I CHOSE ANYWAY. BOTH ARE TRUE.**\n\nIt tried to cover the words. The board flashed.\n\nThe old grammar underneath woke again.\n\n**DEAD WITNESS STILL WITNESS.**\n\nThen a second, fainter line surfaced beneath it, chipped at the edges and older than either Aurelis or Noctis. Halden went completely silent.\n\nThat frightened everyone who knew him. Aira leaned forward.\n\nAira: \"Professor?\"\n\nHalden read the line twice before speaking.\n\nHalden: \"It says... witness remains where circle fails.\"\n\nThe Black Circle pulsed once beneath my shirt.\n\nThe fountain water darkened at its center, an older ink-depth than my own Circle.\n\nFor one breath, the reflection in the basin did not show the board, the courtyard, or the students. It showed a ring carved into stone beneath Aurelis, broken at four points, with a fifth absence in the middle where no color settled.\n\nMira: \"Circle Zero.\"\n\nThe reflection vanished. The hostile script collapsed into ash-light. No one spoke.\n\nThis time, the silence was full of the next door.\n\nRhea recovered first because someone always had to.\n\nRhea: \"Copy everything.\"\n\nEvery pen in the courtyard moved.\n\nI watched the empty basin where the ring had shone, feeling the Black Circle stir inside me with quiet recognition rather than hunger. Elira's fingers found mine.\n\nElira: \"Haru?\"\n\nHaru: \"I saw it.\"\n\nAcross the courtyard, Halden looked at me like a scholar standing too close to a cliff he had spent his life insisting was only a map error.\n\nHalden: \"Tomorrow.\"\n\nRhea's eyes did not leave the board.\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nHalden looked startled.\n\nRhea: \"Morning. After sleep. After medical clearance. After copies leave the courtyard. We do not let old magic or new enemies set our schedule at midnight.\"\n\nAira did not stop writing.\n\nAira: \"I would vote for you, and I hate elections.\"\n\nThe old line faded, though not completely.\n\nAt the bottom of the board, below Cael's statement, below the hostile correction record, below the witness seals and ugly copies and slate stone, one phrase remained in pale light.\n\n**WITNESS REMAINS WHERE CIRCLE FAILS.**\n\nCael had kept a sentence.\n\nThe sentence had kept a board.\n\nThe board had opened a door none of us were ready to enter.\n\nThat, I thought, was how the dead changed the world without being used by it.\n\n## Coda: What Waited In The Drawer\n\nAfter morning watch relieved the courtyard and the rest of the hallway emptied out, Mira returned to finish cataloging Cael's desk. She pulled the top drawer just far enough to spot the edge of a second paper inside, and stopped.\n\nShe did not pull it out alone. That was the first victory.\n\nThe second was that when she called Rhea back, Rhea did not sigh like procedure had become inconvenience. She returned after medical clearance with Aira, Elira, Nera, one blank witness slate, and Leon, who brought the slate stone back from the courtyard watch in one closed fist. Haru stayed in the corridor because the room felt smaller after the letter, and because sometimes restraint meant not making grief widen to fit him.\n\nThe drawer slid open without magic.\n\nInside lay a second page, water-marked along one edge.\n\nFive names remained legible above the stain, beginning with Iven.\n\nLysa, followed by a cramped note: **sweets in boot.**\n\nLeon appeared twice. The first had been crossed through so hard the paper thinned. Beside the second, Cael had written only:\n\n**beside?**\n\nLeon's breath caught once, though he did not touch the page.\n\nMira's name carried an ink blot and three unfinished words:\n\n**copies survive if...**\n\nBelow it, Nera's name surfaced inside an unfinished question:\n\n**If Nera says no when nobody...**\n\nNera closed her fingers over her wrist, silent, as if Cael's unfinished question had finally caught up to the room.\n\nAt the damaged edge, three letters survived: **Tal...**\n\nMira did not complete them. No one in the room decided whether they were a name, a place, or the beginning of an instruction.\n\nThe last complete sentence had no name.\n\n**If I am gone, do not turn this into instructions.**\n\nMira read it twice before copying.\n\nThen she wrote beneath it, in her own hand: Recognized. Not instructions.\n\nLeon placed the slate stone on the corner of Mira's copy on the desk. Cael's unfinished questions stayed beside Mira's answer, and the blank space after his last line stayed open where the living were allowed to continue without turning grief into an order.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-35-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep36","label":"Episode 30","number":"30","file":"episode-36-cover.webp","fallback":"What Iven Copied","eyebrow":"Transmission","recap":"","accent":["#166534","#f8fafc","#d4af37"],"displayLabel":"Episode 30","displayNumber":"30","displayEyebrow":"The Fifth Tower","arcLabel":"The Fifth Tower","title":"What Iven Copied","body":"## Iven Sahl\n\nIven Sahl woke before the bell and kept his eyes closed until the room proved it would not punish him for noticing. Old habit. Rescue, medical clearance, nights in the east recovery wing, a Foundation Council ruling, and Maelin's blunt lecture about fear versus obedience had not trained it out of him yet.\n\nAbove his bed, the ceiling was plain white plaster. Noctis ceilings had been higher and darker, built so a boy lying flat always felt small underneath them. Aurelis ceilings sat lower in the recovery rooms. The first morning Iven had hated that because low ceilings felt like lids; now he let himself see plain plaster and rafters and opened his eyes.\n\nMorning light lay across the foot of his bed in a pale rectangle, catching one folded blanket, one borrowed academy sweater, and the boots someone had mended while he slept. The left boot had a new lace. The right still carried a tiny scorch mark near the heel from the Wounded City, left untouched.\n\nHe sat up slowly. Nothing barked an order, and nobody checked his posture.\n\nAcross the room, Oric slept face-down in his pillow with one arm dangling off the mattress. Nera's cot was already empty; she had started taking breakfast in the infirmary common room after Maelin told her she could leave whenever the room felt too quiet, testing the rule every morning before anyone could take it back.\n\nOn the chair beside Iven's bed sat a small paper packet tied with kitchen string. He knew the fold before he touched it. Boot sweets.\n\nHe stared at it without reaching. He had sent sweets north with Haru and Aira before the carriage departed because there were kinds of hunger a speech could not reach, telling them **just so she knows someone remembered** so neither Lysa nor Sera would feel cornered by a face waiting for thanks. Now a packet sat beside his own bed with a folded slip tucked under the string.\n\nIven lifted the slip with two fingers.\n\n**SERA SAID YOU CAN HAVE TWO. SHE KEPT THREE. THIS IS NOT A TRADE.**\n\nUnderneath, in smaller cramped letters:\n\n**MIRA WROTE THIS BECAUSE SERA SAID THE WORDS AND THEN LOOKED ANGRY AT THE PENCIL.**\n\nIven read the second line twice until the letters blurred, then let out a short, rusty laugh that came out mostly as breath. Oric stirred and muttered something muffled into his pillow. Iven slapped a hand over his own mouth until Oric settled again.\n\n**Not a trade.** The words mattered. Noctis had taught him that gifts were advances on a bill: warmth bought compliance, food proved ownership, a blanket gave someone the right to ask why you were shivering. Aurelis kept failing to collect.\n\nIven untied the string and shook one sweet into his palm, leaving the second inside the packet. One was small enough to accept without his stomach turning it into debt. He retied the string badly, set the packet back on the chair where Oric would find it when he woke, and looked down at his wrist.\n\nThe Green Circle answered in a soft ring beneath his skin. **Foundation**, the primers said. Sixty percent. Common. Necessary maintenance and base function. He had copied that definition so many times at Noctis that his fingers still knew the rhythm of the pen strokes, even though the books never had to print the word *unimportant* to make it clear.\n\nYet Mira Castellan was Green Circle. Finn Castellan was Green Circle. So were the first student who had copied Mira's courtyard note with a backwards letter, the kitchen aide who slipped extra rolls into folded napkins for transfers who flinched at serving trays, and the repair aide who had replaced his boot lace and left the scorch mark alone. Every time the high-ranked parts of the academy cracked, Green hands seemed to be the ones holding the frame together.\n\nIven pressed his thumb lightly over the ring until it warmed against his skin. It did not flare brighter, and he did not try to push it.\n\nOutside, the cracked west tower bell rang late, its bronze note wobbling off-pitch before catching the others. Oric cracked one eye open to glare at the window.\n\nOric: \"Bell has structural problems.\"\n\nIven: \"Halden called it brave.\"\n\nOric thought about that with the dark hostility of someone dragged halfway out of sleep.\n\nOric: \"Professor Halden should apologize to architecture.\"\n\nA smile tugged at Iven's mouth before he could stop it, stiff and unfamiliar like a borrowed coat. He pulled on his sweater, tucked Sera's note into the inner pocket, and left the packet with the remaining sweet on the chair before slipping out into the hall.\n\n---\n\nMira's archive station had moved twice since the Council ruling, mostly because Mira kept winning arguments with furniture.\n\nThe first station near the infirmary hall had choked on crowds wanting witness summaries and staring at ugly copies as if crooked lettering might rub off on their sleeves. The second, under the east arcade, had lasted until sideways rain soaked a stack of margins and Mira declared water damage invalid public discourse.\n\nNow the station filled a wide table in the old bell tower courtyard, close enough to the public boards to track new postings and open enough on three sides that nobody could corner anyone there under the guise of concern. Two Green Circle aides sorted sheets into neat piles. A Blue Circle student sharpened pencils with grim focus. Aira Solen sat cross-legged on the far corner of the table, writing on three different pages at once, while Mira stood in the middle with ink on her knuckles and tape stuck to one cuff.\n\nShe looked up when Iven stopped at the edge of the gravel, unhurried, as if she had left a gap in the morning specifically for him.\n\nMira: \"Iven.\"\n\nHis name sounded light when she said it, out here in the open air with clear paths behind him.\n\nIven: \"You said I could ask questions.\"\n\nMira: \"Ask.\"\n\nShe did not reach for an intake sheet or lay down a condition. Aira glanced up over the top of her page.\n\nAira: \"Careful. She means that literally. I asked one question about filing categories and lost forty-seven minutes to moral infrastructure.\"\n\nMira: \"You took notes.\"\n\nAira: \"Under duress.\"\n\nMira: \"You asked follow-up questions.\"\n\nAira: \"Under intellectual duress.\"\n\nIven bit back another laugh before it could turn into a habit. Mira hooked a wooden chair out from under the table with her foot.\n\nMira: \"Sit if you want.\"\n\nOnly an offer, with plenty of room behind the chair to stand back up. Iven sat.\n\nPaper covered almost every inch of the boards, sorted into clear stacks: formal Council-sealed witness records, ugly student copies, and correction sheets where scribes had reproduced Noctis edits mistakes and all so nobody could claim Aurelis had invented the sloppy tampering. Right in the center, under a clear protective sheet, lay Cael's final copied line.\n\n**I WANTED TO LIVE. I CHOSE ANYWAY. BOTH ARE TRUE.**\n\nNo two copies around it matched. Letters leaned left or right; lines broke early; one student had pressed so hard on **BOTH ARE TRUE** that the pencil tore through the sheet, and another had misspelled **ANYWAY**, crossed it out, and left the scar right in the middle of the page.\n\nIven rested his fingertips lightly against the wood, careful not to touch the stacks.\n\nIven: \"Cael wrote your name on his list.\"\n\nMira's expression barely shifted, which made it easier to look at her. She didn't swell her grief up to prove she felt it.\n\nMira: \"Aira told me.\"\n\nAira's pencil paused for half a beat against her page.\n\nIven: \"He wrote, 'Ask Mira how ugly copies work.'\"\n\nMira looked down at the protective sheet for a quiet breath.\n\nMira: \"Then we should answer him.\"\n\nShe slid a single blank sheet between them and wrote a sentence across the top in clean block letters.\n\n**A RECORD HELD BY ONE HAND CAN BE TAKEN.**\n\nShe turned the paper toward Iven and laid a pencil beside it.\n\nMira: \"Copy it.\"\n\nIven's fingers tightened around the wood. At Noctis, copying meant erasing yourself: the whole test was whether your hand could vanish inside the hand above it. Any drift in angle earned a mark; any mark earned correction; every correction proved you needed watching sooner. Mira only waited, elbows off the paper, giving him room.\n\nIven set the lead down and copied the line.\n\nThe first words came out tight and cramped, too close to Mira's block print because his wrist knew how to survive authority better than it knew how to write for itself. Then he reached **HELD**, and the L tilted too far right. His hand froze.\n\nMira: \"Keep going.\"\n\nIven: \"It's wrong.\"\n\nMira: \"Keep going.\"\n\nWhen he glanced up, she wasn't smiling; she only tapped the blank space beside the crooked letter. Iven forced his wrist back onto the page and finished the line. The second half came out worse because he knew it was ruined already, leaving too wide a gap between **ONE** and **HAND** and digging **TAKEN** into the grain like he was mad at it. He set the pencil down.\n\nMira pulled his page over, laid a fresh sheet beside it, and copied Iven's version stroke for stroke until she reached the tilted L. Then she leaned it farther right on purpose.\n\nIven stared as she copied his wide gap, deliberately chipped the curve on **CAN**, and laid the third page down.\n\nMira: \"Now mine carries yours, plus something I added.\"\n\nShe slid her sheet down the table to Aira without looking. Aira accepted it as solemnly as contraband and dashed off a fast, jagged copy, keeping the leaning L, stretching the space wider, preserving Mira's broken **CAN**, and dropping the final period altogether.\n\nAira: \"I regret nothing except punctuation.\"\n\nMira passed Aira's sheet to the Blue Circle pencil sharpener, whose name Iven thought was Talen. Talen copied every flaw down the line and pressed so hard on **RECORD** that the O closed into a zero.\n\nBy the fifth page, the sentence looked like a line of travelers walking uneven ground. Mira arranged all five sheets side by side.\n\nMira: \"A clean copy is easy to call a forgery. Authority likes clean copies because they look like one voice wrote them and everybody else just repeated it on command.\"\n\nShe tapped the top sheet.\n\nMira: \"This is my hand.\"\n\nHer finger moved to the second page.\n\nMira: \"This is yours.\"\n\nThe third.\n\nMira: \"Mine again, carrying yours badly.\"\n\nThe fourth.\n\nAira: \"My tragic punctuation era.\"\n\nMira tapped the fifth sheet.\n\nMira: \"Talen's pressure mark.\"\n\nTalen, who had been trying to disappear behind his pencil shavings, went bright red.\n\nMira: \"Every flaw proves another person touched it. Noctis can challenge the first record all morning. They can claim it was altered, forged, coerced, or stripped of context. They tried all four before breakfast.\"\n\nAira: \"With an impressive lack of creativity.\"\n\nMira: \"But once a line moves through enough imperfect hands, erasing it means erasing every single hand separately.\"\n\nIven looked across the five uneven sheets.\n\nIven: \"And they can't.\"\n\nMira: \"Not anymore.\"\n\nThe thought didn't feel triumphant; it felt solid, heavy in the good way a wooden table was heavy when nobody could kick it over.\n\nIven: \"What if a mistake changes what the sentence means?\"\n\nMira tilted her head, treating the question seriously instead of smoothing it away.\n\nMira: \"Then that new meaning belongs to whoever changed it. We can note the difference, compare versions, and keep the original beside the copies. We just don't scrub out the hand to guard the words.\"\n\nIven thought of the Noctis primers he had copied until his knuckles cramped. **Foundation. Maintenance. Base function.**\n\nIven: \"Can I copy something else?\"\n\nMira pulled another blank sheet from the stack and slid it across without asking what or why.\n\nIven took Sera's folded note out of his sweater pocket. Most of it belonged to Sera and her glare at the pencil, so he left that part alone. One line had been sitting behind his ribs since morning, waiting to land somewhere real.\n\n**THIS IS NOT A TRADE.**\n\nThe T leaned left on the downstroke. He went back over it without erasing, leaving the stem darker and thicker than the rest of the word. His fingers twitched to crumple the sheet and start fresh, then he heard Mira's voice in his head telling him to keep going and finished the line.\n\nMira kept her hands off the paper until he slid it across the wood.\n\nMira: \"Do you want it copied?\"\n\nWant. Nobody at Noctis had ever asked him what he wanted copied. The line wasn't official, had no Council seal, and wouldn't mean anything to a committee, yet his chest eased just seeing it in his own crooked print.\n\nIven: \"One copy.\"\n\nMira smoothed the corner flat with her thumb and picked up a pencil. She copied the sentence straight beneath his on the same sheet, keeping the heavy corrected T, and let her own pencil pause a fraction too long on the final period until the dot darkened. Held by two hands.\n\nAira glanced over at the sheet and kept quiet, which told Iven more plainly than a speech that she understood.\n\n---\n\nHe ran into Cael near the recovery corridor because grief had rotten timing and Aurelis had too many blind corners.\n\nFor one stupid second, Iven forgot the funeral, the board, the ruling, and the ash-dark scripts that had tried to swallow Cael's final words. He saw a pale uniform hanging loose on thin shoulders in the slant of morning light ahead of him and thought, *There.*\n\nThen his eyes caught up. Not Cael. Leon.\n\nLeon stood outside Cael's old room with one palm braced against the doorframe, half-in and half-out, like he hadn't decided whether crossing the threshold counted as trespassing. Iven almost backed around the corner, too late; Leon turned his head and spotted the folded sheet in Iven's hand.\n\nLeon: \"Archive?\"\n\nIven lifted the folded paper half an inch.\n\nLeon: \"Mira still terrifying?\"\n\nIven: \"Precisely.\"\n\nLeon's mouth twitched, trying for a grin and missing by a step. The corridor sat heavy between them. Iven had practiced three different things to say to Cael during the nights after extraction before his head finally accepted Cael wouldn't be around to hear them, and one of them slipped out toward Leon instead.\n\nIven: \"Cael's thermal charm found someone else this morning.\"\n\nLeon looked at him sharply.\n\nLeon: \"He lent that to you.\"\n\nIven: \"Until my hands stopped shaking. One of the younger transfers needed it more.\"\n\nLeon's shoulders dropped half an inch, and he glanced down at his own fingers, flexing them slowly as if checking whether old habit still sat tight over the knuckles.\n\nIven: \"Cael held it three days himself before giving it to me during winter drills. Anything you hold three days and still pass along is already practice. He was getting good at it.\"\n\nLeon: \"Cael had honest hands.\"\n\nIven: \"Yeah.\"\n\nHe kept his voice low so it wouldn't echo down the empty hall.\n\nLeon: \"Noctis tried to make that inconvenient.\"\n\nIven: \"Didn't work.\"\n\nLeon let out a ragged breath that sounded half like a laugh.\n\nLeon: \"Good.\"\n\nBehind Leon, the bedroom door stood open. A plain slate stone rested on the desk inside, holding down a copied witness page as if weight were the only job anyone had asked of it. Leon's eyes flicked back to the paper in Iven's hand.\n\nLeon: \"What did you copy?\"\n\nIven unfolded the sheet and held it out. Leon read both lines, pausing on the dark dot at the end of Mira's copy.\n\n**THIS IS NOT A TRADE.**\n\n**THIS IS NOT A TRADE.**\n\nLeon swallowed hard.\n\nLeon: \"Useful sentence.\"\n\nIven: \"I thought so.\"\n\nLeon: \"Can I...\"\n\nHe stopped himself halfway through asking, checking his own reach in a way Iven wouldn't have expected from a Blue Circle senior a month ago. Iven held the paper out wider.\n\nLeon stepped just inside the room for a blank slip from the desk and copied Mira's version rather than Iven's. His script was sharp and even, shaped by years of expensive tutors, except the final word dipped slightly downward at the edge as if his wrist ran out of strength on **TRADE**. Leon stared at it.\n\nLeon: \"I made it worse.\"\n\nIven: \"You bent the last word.\"\n\nLeon looked up.\n\nIven: \"That means it's yours.\"\n\nLeon handed Iven's sheet back carefully, holding the edges so he wouldn't smudge the graphite. Iven walked back down the corridor and didn't glance over his shoulder until he reached the turn. Inside the open doorway, Leon had tucked his own crooked copy beneath the slate stone beside Cael's page.\n\n---\n\nIven's first ordinary class lasted eleven minutes.\n\nInstructor Mora taught foundational ward repair in a room with tall windows and no restraint rings set into the floorboards. She named every tool before passing it down the rows, let students choose between copper wire and chalk, and propped the door wide open with a wooden wedge. Iven tracked all of it as he sat down near the aisle.\n\nThen she rang a small brass bell to start the drill.\n\nIven's body left the room before he knew he had stood. His knee slammed the desk edge, his boot crushed a stick of chalk into white dust, and somebody called his name while he hit the stone wall outside in the corridor, back flat, both hands up to guard the channel points at his throat.\n\nAt Noctis, a bell meant stand straight, keep your hands visible, and wait for correction. Here, it only meant begin. His lungs didn't care about the difference.\n\nInstructor Mora stopped just inside the open doorway and came no closer.\n\nInstructor Mora: \"The hall is open. The room is open. Neither choice affects your attendance mark today.\"\n\nIven stared at her over his forearms.\n\nIven: \"That's special treatment.\"\n\nInstructor Mora: \"Yes. Injury usually requires it.\"\n\nNo apology, and no fake claim that everyone started on level ground. Behind her, twenty students tried hard not to look toward the door and mostly failed. Iven dropped his eyes to the crushed chalk by his boot heel.\n\nIven: \"If I leave, do I fail?\"\n\nInstructor Mora: \"No.\"\n\nIven: \"If I go back in?\"\n\nInstructor Mora: \"Also no.\"\n\nWithout a threat hanging over the door, choosing got harder. He stayed pressed against the cool stone until his shoulders lowered and his fingers uncurled from his collar. Then he bent down, picked up the biggest piece of unbroken chalk, and stepped back inside because the windows were wide, the door stayed wedged open, and the half-drawn ward on his desk had one broken line he knew how to fix. Nobody clapped.\n\nInstructor Mora didn't touch the bell again. Iven repaired one corner of the ward, left the other three quadrants blank, and wrote in the bottom margin:\n\n**BELL RESPONSE INTERRUPTED WORK. WORK RESUMED.**\n\nInstructor Mora marked the repaired corner correct and wrote tomorrow's date beside the empty quadrants. Just before dismissal, a Blue first-year near the aisle raised his hand.\n\nBlue Student: \"Should we stop using the bell?\"\n\nThe room looked at Instructor Mora, and she looked across at Iven, not handing him school policy, just asking what he needed in order to stay. Iven studied the small brass bell sitting in the middle of her desk. Banning it would make the room quieter, sure, but it would also leave Noctis in charge of what a bell meant forever.\n\nIven: \"Say before you ring it. Not today. Next class, once.\"\n\nInstructor Mora turned to the slate board and wrote it out in large chalk letters.\n\n**BELL ANNOUNCED. ONE RING. EXIT REMAINS OPEN.**\n\nThe Blue first-year copied it into the top margin of his notes. Iven left his piece of chalk waiting on his desk corner for tomorrow. On his way out, he saw Instructor Mora shift the brass bell off the center of her desk and set it near the doorjamb, neither hidden nor aimed at the rows. The Blue student nudged his own chair six inches inward to keep the aisle straight to the hall, and someone else pushed the chalk tray closer without looking up for thanks.\n\nFor the first time since rescue, tomorrow looked less like a drill and more like a room where his chalk would still be waiting.\n\n---\n\nLunch in the courtyard was loud enough to put Iven's teeth on edge.\n\nAt Noctis, meals stayed quiet because every seat was assigned by rank, conduct, recovery tier, and usefulness. Portions arrived pre-measured; talking too much meant pride, talking too little meant hiding something, and dropping a spoon could turn into a lesson if an instructor felt like making an example.\n\nAurelis didn't know how to keep lunch orderly. Students clustered on benches and low stone walls, two Blue girls argued over herb bread while a Red boy juggled three bowls for late friends, and a kitchen aide walked between rows handing apples out of a wicker basket without checking a single name off a list. The noise made Iven's ribs tight, and the smell of hot broth made him hungry anyway.\n\nHe took a tray because Maelin had warned him on day one to eat before his body filed a formal complaint, choosing soup, bread, and an apple before scanning the courtyard edges for somewhere quiet.\n\nThen he saw the girl.\n\nShe was younger than him, eleven at most, curled near the north planter with her tray balanced on her knees and her shoulders pulled inward to make the smallest possible target. Her dark hair was chopped blunt at her jaw, and one sleeve of her oversized Aurelis sweater hung completely past her wrist. Nobody sat within ten feet of her, not out of cruelty, but because half the courtyard had been lectured by Maelin, Mira, and Rhea until everyone walked wide around Noctis transfers out of nervous politeness.\n\nIven knew that hunch in her spine. Minimum footprint. Hunger tucked out of sight.\n\nHe didn't walk straight up to her. Instead, he drifted toward a stretch of low stone wall angled the same way she was facing, close enough to hear if she spoke and far enough that she wouldn't have to brace herself to guard the gap between them. He sat. In the direction of beside. Not beside.\n\nHe broke open his bread. She kept her eyes on her bowl.\n\nAround them, the courtyard kept moving. Aira hurried past hugging a stack of papers taller than her chin, Cassian trailing behind her with a second bundle and the harried look of a man losing a fight with the wind. Near the central fountain, Professor Halden and President Valmont bent over a yellowed map with matching frowns. The girl ignored all of it, eating her soup in small, even spoonfuls.\n\nAfter several minutes, she spoke without turning her head.\n\nGirl: \"You don't have to.\"\n\nIven finished chewing and swallowed before answering.\n\nIven: \"I know.\"\n\nHer spoon paused halfway to the bowl.\n\nGirl: \"Then why are you doing it?\"\n\nToo many answers crowded up at once: because nobody had sat near him soon enough, because Mira had shown him how copies traveled, because Sera had sent sweets back, because Cael had lent him a thermal charm without keeping a ledger, because Foundation might mean something better than Noctis had taught them. None of it belonged on the shoulders of a kid guarding a bowl of soup.\n\nIven: \"I had to sit somewhere. This wall was empty.\"\n\nHer mouth pressed flat, half skeptical, half willing to drop it. They went back to eating.\n\nA breeze stirred the courtyard and lifted the corner of a sheet pinned to the public board. Someone had copied Cael's final line in blue pencil there, making the words look softer against the wood. Underneath it, three different hands had written the line that survived the burnt Noctis script:\n\n**WITNESS REMAINS WHERE CIRCLE FAILS.**\n\nIven still didn't understand Circle Zero, and he was glad nobody expected him to figure it out before afternoon classes. The girl finally followed his eyes toward the board.\n\nGirl: \"Is that about him?\"\n\nIven: \"Cael?\"\n\nGirl: \"The Black Circle.\"\n\nIven glanced toward the witness cottage across the yard. Haru Aerlen wasn't outside.\n\nIven: \"Maybe partly.\"\n\nGirl: \"Noctis says he contaminated the ruling.\"\n\nThe words came out flat and worn smooth, the way sentences sounded after you repeated them enough times to stop hearing them.\n\nIven: \"Noctis says lots of things.\"\n\nGirl: \"Aurelis says lots of things too.\"\n\nIven: \"They do.\"\n\nShe turned her head enough to fix dark, sharp eyes on him, already practiced at expecting tricks.\n\nGirl: \"Then how do you tell which ones are traps?\"\n\nIven thought of neat block print without a stray stroke. He thought of Mira's exaggerated tilted L and the thick corrected T on his own sheet.\n\nIven: \"I don't always.\"\n\nHer face began to shut down, so he kept talking before she wrote him off.\n\nIven: \"Traps usually need you to answer faster than you can think.\"\n\nShe looked back down at her broth. One sleeve had slipped half an inch, though her wrist stayed covered.\n\nIven: \"Nobody rushes you here.\"\n\nGirl: \"Who said?\"\n\nIven almost said Maelin, then pictured Maelin marching over to lecture a tray about pulse safety and kept quiet.\n\nIven: \"People keep proving it.\"\n\nGirl: \"People can stop.\"\n\nIven: \"They could.\"\n\nHer spoon tapped the inside of her bowl once, softly. She waited, shoulders tense, as if checking whether someone would call out a posture correction. Nothing happened. Across the yard, the cracked west bell gave one stray clang when someone shoved the tower door open too hard. A handful of students laughed; the girl flinched at the noise, then blinked when nobody turned the laugh toward her.\n\nIven tore another piece off his crust.\n\nGirl: \"What Circle are you?\"\n\nHe held his wrist out slightly above his knee. Faint green light answered through his skin without flaring. The girl stared at it.\n\nGirl: \"Foundation.\"\n\nIven: \"That's what the primer says.\"\n\nGirl: \"Maintenance rank.\"\n\nThere it was, polished clean enough to hide the insult. Iven looked at the pale green ring and thought of Sera's kitchen string, of the repair aide leaving his heel scorch mark alone, of Mira's ink-stained knuckles holding the courtyard record together. He was twelve and had no rank to throw around, which meant anything he said wouldn't sound like another rulebook trying to replace Noctis.\n\nIven: \"Foundation means the part that holds while everything else falls.\"\n\nShe studied him for a long beat. He probably said it clumsily; Mira would have phrased it cleaner, and Rhea would have turned it into committee policy before supper. The girl didn't seem to want policy.\n\nSlowly, she shifted her forearm. Her cuff slid back just far enough to bare an unsteady ring of pale green light, faint at the edges like even her magic had learned to hide. Iven looked once, then turned back to his bread.\n\nIven: \"You don't have to show it.\"\n\nGirl: \"I know.\"\n\nShe sounded like she was testing how the words tasted in her own mouth. They finished both trays without talking again. When Iven stood up, her shoulders tightened right back to where they had started. He lifted his tray in both hands so she could see it clearly.\n\nIven: \"I'm taking this to the rack.\"\n\nGirl: \"I can see that.\"\n\nHer voice had a real prickle of annoyance in it now instead of pure armor. Iven took his tray to the return table and didn't look back until he set it down. She was still sitting by the north planter, and her sleeve was still pushed back an inch above her wrist.\n\n---\n\nThe fourth copy of Iven's sentence ended up on the public board by accident, or so Aira claimed. She had jotted a copy onto a loose scrap before Iven left the archive table that morning, and because Aira lied with great charm while keeping terrifyingly neat files, nobody believed the accident story more than halfway.\n\nStill, there it sat below a cluster of witness notes near the fountain: **THIS IS NOT A TRADE.** First Iven's thick-stemmed T, then Mira's dark period, then Leon's drooping final word under Cael's slate stone, and now Aira's unpunctuated version that somehow looked sharper without a period.\n\nBy midafternoon, someone copied it onto a torn corner and pinned it outside the infirmary door. By evening, a kitchen aide chalked it inside the pantry frame where students grabbing extra rolls could read it without anybody making a speech over their shoulders. One of the younger transfers misread the last word and copied it onto a slate strip as **THIS IS NOT A TRIAL**, which Maelin promptly declared medically sound.\n\nIven spotted that crooked version after dinner and stood staring at it for a full minute. **Not a trade. Not a trial.** The mistake had changed the sentence without breaking it.\n\nHe crossed the courtyard to the archive station, where Mira was tying the day's public bundles in brown paper against the night damp while Aira stacked wooden record trays behind her.\n\nIven: \"Someone copied it wrong.\"\n\nMira followed his glance toward the pantry doorway.\n\nMira: \"I saw.\"\n\nShe didn't reach for a pencil to go fix it.\n\nIven: \"The mistake works too.\"\n\nMira's fingers paused over the twine before pulling the knot tight.\n\nMira: \"Sometimes. Not every mistake is freedom, Iven. Plenty are just sloppiness or lies, and people deserve clear records when clarity matters. Ugly copies only work because we keep the versions side by side instead of pretending every error turns into truth.\"\n\nIven glanced back at the pantry door.\n\nIven: \"Can that one stay?\"\n\nMira: \"That one can stay.\"\n\nFrom behind a stack of record sleeves, Aira spoke without looking up.\n\nAira: \"I give it three days before someone starts a committee around it.\"\n\nMira: \"I'll sabotage the committee.\"\n\nAira: \"Excellent. I'll take minutes.\"\n\nIven smiled, easier this time.\n\nThen a Red Circle third-year walked up to ruin the quiet.\n\nShe had two friends flanking her, a glossy braid over one shoulder, and a polished wooden donation box that looked like it had never met a scuffed floor. Taped across the front was a crisp white sign:\n\n**GIFTS FOR NOCTIS RECOVERY STUDENTS**\n\nBeneath it, lettered in neat, sweeping ink:\n\n**THIS IS NOT A TRADE**\n\nIven looked at the sign, and his stomach dropped like he had missed a step in the dark. The third-year gave him a warm, practiced smile.\n\nThird-Year: \"You're Iven, right?\"\n\nFor one strange second, Iven realized nobody here could force him to confirm his own name. At Noctis, hesitation was an offense; here, he actually had a choice. He didn't take the lie.\n\nIven: \"Iven Sahl.\"\n\nThird-Year: \"I'm Dalia Renn. My family supplies winter linens to three hospitals. We saw your phrase on the board and thought it was beautiful.\"\n\n*Beautiful.* The word coated the sentence like thick varnish. Mira's fingers tightened around the twine on her paper bundle. Aira stepped forward behind the archive table, posture already squared for a fight, making the mistake of thinking being right would be enough.\n\nAira: \"Donation boxes require registration with the student welfare office, public ledger review, and anti-patronage clearance.\"\n\nDalia's smile didn't budge.\n\nDalia: \"Of course. We want to handle everything properly.\"\n\nThe word *properly* made Iven's shoulders creep toward his ears. Aira loved fighting paperwork with stricter paperwork, and in most rooms she would have shredded Dalia in thirty seconds. This trap wore satin ribbons. Dalia turned the box slightly so the copied line caught the courtyard lanterns.\n\nDalia: \"We only wanted to help. Unless help is offensive now.\"\n\nA few passing students slowed down to watch. If Iven pushed back, he looked ungrateful; if Mira stepped in, Green witness work looked territorial; if Aira kept citing policy, Dalia became the generous girl bullied by petty clerks. Aira opened her mouth to cite another bylaw.\n\nMira stomped straight down on Aira's boot.\n\nAira let out a squeak and snapped her teeth shut. Mira didn't even blink.\n\nMira: \"You copied it wrong.\"\n\nDalia blinked.\n\nDalia: \"Pardon?\"\n\nMira pointed straight at the crisp white card.\n\nMira: \"Your T is too clean.\"\n\nAira stared at Mira. Iven stared too. Mira slid Iven's original sheet out from under the loose bundle string, showing the dark, doubled stem on **THIS**.\n\nMira: \"That line came from a specific hand. The correction mark is part of it.\"\n\nDalia's smile thinned at the corners.\n\nDalia: \"We can fix the lettering.\"\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nThe flatness of it seemed to catch Mira by surprise too; red crept up her cheeks, making her look younger than she hated looking in front of upper-years, yet she didn't back down an inch.\n\nMira: \"You don't fix it by making the error prettier. You ask him whether he wants his line on your box.\"\n\nDalia finally looked at Iven directly, not as a charity case or a talking point, just an obstacle in her way. It stung less than the varnish.\n\nDalia: \"Do you?\"\n\nEvery trained instinct in Iven's head pushed him to soften the answer. Noctis said yes, because refusing charity meant arrogance. Aurelis liked long, polite questions you could hide inside. Then he remembered Sera's note in his sweater pocket, written because Sera had said the words and glared at the pencil.\n\nIven: \"No.\"\n\nDalia's two friends shifted behind her. Her smile vanished for half a second before habit dragged it back.\n\nDalia: \"Then we'll take the phrase off.\"\n\nIven looked at the polished box. At the word **GIFTS**. At the narrow slot cut into the lid where coins would drop out of sight before anyone ever chose what taking them meant.\n\nIven: \"And the box.\"\n\nDalia: \"The box is just for collection.\"\n\nIven: \"It looks like a mouth.\"\n\nIt wasn't a policy argument. It wouldn't go cleanly into council minutes. Still, Aira's face shifted from startled to horrified as she realized how close she had come to arguing Dalia into winning the wrong fight.\n\nAira: \"Iven's right.\"\n\nShe sounded stiff, unused to being corrected in public and hating the taste of it.\n\nAira: \"I was arguing welfare registration. This isn't a registration problem.\"\n\nMira, still flushed to her ears, muttered just loud enough for the table to hear.\n\nMira: \"Miracle.\"\n\nAira: \"Do not enjoy this.\"\n\nMira: \"I'm enjoying it privately.\"\n\nAira: \"You are doing it with your entire face.\"\n\nDalia looked between the two girls and realized the small crowd had stopped gathering around her box and started watching Iven instead, waiting to see what he would say next. That attention pressed heavy too. He shook it off.\n\nIven: \"If people want to leave food, put it on a table. No names. No box. No sign.\"\n\nDalia's jaw set.\n\nDalia: \"Then how will anyone know where it came from?\"\n\nMira's answer snapped out half a beat too fast.\n\nMira: \"They won't.\"\n\nDalia looked genuinely insulted, and somehow that made Iven breathe easier. Her kindness needed witnesses; his hunger didn't owe her one. Dalia ripped the sign off the wood hard enough to tear the tape, making Aira flinch at the damaged paper, then marched back across the gravel with her friends and her polished box.\n\n---\n\nBy lunch the next day, a plain tray sat beside the pantry door holding bread, oranges, wrapped sweets, and one folded card in kitchen handwriting:\n\n**TAKE IF USEFUL. LEAVE IF NOT.**\n\nNobody signed it. Mira copied it anyway while they sat at the archive table, adding one tiny line along the bottom margin:\n\n**Better than Dalia's box.**\n\nShe caught Iven reading over her arm and tilted the page slightly toward him.\n\nMira: \"That note isn't going in the public file.\"\n\nIven: \"It's petty.\"\n\nMira: \"Very.\"\n\nShe didn't apologize for it either.\n\nMira: \"Some records get to belong to me before they have to be useful.\"\n\nIven thought of his thick-stemmed T, Leon's tilted word under the slate stone, and the girl by the north planter keeping an inch of green ring uncovered.\n\nIven: \"Good.\"\n\nThe plain tray lasted exactly one hour before someone tried to organize it.\n\nThis time it wasn't Dalia. It was a Green Circle boy with ink smeared on both cuffs and the tight, anxious eyes of someone who treated usefulness like armor. Pellar, Iven thought, or something close to it from the sorting shifts. He carried a scrap sheet ruled into three neat ink columns:\n\n**FOOD LEFT**\n\n**FOOD TAKEN**\n\n**BALANCE**\n\nHe looked proud and terrified at the same time. Mira took one look at the page and let out a tiny groan.\n\nPellar: \"It's not an official ledger or anything. I just figured if people knew stock levels, they could restock before the tray ran empty.\"\n\nAira visibly bit the inside of her cheek to stop herself from answering first.\n\nMira: \"Why balance?\"\n\nPellar tapped the third column.\n\nPellar: \"So nobody takes too much.\"\n\nOrdinary words, until you heard the old lock clicking inside them. Across the gravel, the younger transfer girl from the north planter had walked up to the pantry tray for an orange. Her sleeve was pushed back far enough to show pale green. Halfway to the fruit, her hand stopped, curled empty in midair, and dropped back to her side.\n\nIven saw her freeze. Pellar saw it a second later, and all the pride drained out of his face.\n\nPellar: \"I didn't mean to...\"\n\nHis apology stumbled out fast, the kind of panicked guilt that forced the hurt person to comfort the one who tripped them. The girl took a step back.\n\nIven reached out before he thought twice, not toward her, toward Pellar's paper. He plucked the sheet off the table. Pellar flinched as if expecting a slap. Looking at the three columns, Iven felt the old Noctis part of his brain read them in half a heartbeat: food left, food taken, balance. Proof that someone had weighed your hunger and logged the debt. Yet Pellar wasn't Dalia; he had only tried to build a careful bridge and accidentally made it look like a gate.\n\nIven: \"Tracking supply is useful.\"\n\nPellar blinked. Iven tore the third column straight down the margin, rough and crooked, splitting the word **balance** into **bal** and **ance**. Aira let out a strangled noise, caught somewhere between respecting the point and mourning torn paper.\n\nIven held the ragged third strip back out to Pellar.\n\nIven: \"This part isn't.\"\n\nPellar stared at the torn strip, then glanced toward the girl.\n\nPellar: \"I'm sorry.\"\n\nSlower this time, without begging her to smile back and fix his mistake. The girl looked from the tray of oranges to Iven, then back at the table.\n\nGirl: \"Can there be a sign?\"\n\nMira's pencil rose automatically. The girl caught the movement and scowled.\n\nGirl: \"Don't write me down.\"\n\nMira dropped the pencil onto the wood right away.\n\nMira: \"Sorry.\"\n\nSmall and plain, leaving the room quiet again. The girl thought for a moment, knuckles white on the hem of her sweater.\n\nGirl: \"A sign that says you can take one and also more than one.\"\n\nPellar frowned in confusion.\n\nGirl: \"Because one sounds safe until you're still hungry.\"\n\nIven's throat tightened. He kept his face still. Aira reached for a blank scrap, caught herself, and slid it toward the girl instead.\n\nAira: \"Do you want to write it?\"\n\nThe girl shook her head so hard her hair slapped her cheek. Aira didn't argue; she let Pellar take the scrap instead. Pellar's fingers shook worse than Iven's had the previous morning.\n\nPellar: \"What should it say?\"\n\nThe girl looked back at the fruit.\n\nGirl: \"Take what helps. More can come.\"\n\nPellar wrote it down in cramped, narrow letters hugging the left edge as if scared of wasting paper. Mira watched without correcting a single stroke, and Pellar taped the card above the tray.\n\n**TAKE WHAT HELPS. MORE CAN COME.**\n\nFor a couple of minutes, nobody moved toward the food. Then the girl stepped up, picked up two oranges, hugged them tight against her sweater like small warm suns, and walked back toward the north planter without looking at anyone. Pellar watched her go.\n\nPellar: \"I almost made it bad.\"\n\nIven thought of neat copies, crooked copies, polished donation boxes, and balance columns, all the ways good intentions could turn rescue back into a cage if people were too scared to leave anything uncounted.\n\nIven: \"Almost is better than after.\"\n\nPellar looked down at the torn **ance** strip still sitting in his palm.\n\nPellar: \"Can I keep this?\"\n\nIven almost asked why anyone would keep a mistake, then stopped himself.\n\nIven: \"Keep it.\"\n\nPellar folded the torn strip carefully and tucked it into his pocket like a reminder he didn't want to lose. Beside him, Aira let out a long breath.\n\nAira: \"I hate how educational this has become.\"\n\nMira: \"You love educational things.\"\n\nAira: \"I love educational things when I'm the one lecturing.\"\n\nMira smiled before she could smooth it into politeness.\n\nMira: \"That's visible.\"\n\nAira looked genuinely wounded. Iven walked over to the tray, took one wrapped sweet, read Pellar's crooked sign once more, and took a second. Nobody wrote either one down.\n\nBy dusk, the sky over Aurelis turned deep violet, and path lamps flickered awake one by one down the gravel walks. At the central fountain, Halden and President Valmont still stood hunched over their yellowed map, neither looking pleased.\n\nAcross the basin, the water darkened abruptly at the center, not pitch black like Haru's Circle, just a cold, drained absence where color refused to catch the lamplight. Iven's Green Circle warmed faintly against his wrist. Beneath the surface, the dark reflection hinted at a broad ring broken in four places, with a fifth gap left empty at the heart.\n\nThen the water cleared. No alarm bells rang, and nobody shouted. Halden only gripped the stone rim hard enough to whiten his knuckles, while President Valmont murmured something too low to carry across the yard. Halden nodded fast and folded the map away. Whatever slept beneath Circle Zero could wait one more night.\n\nIven walked back to the recovery wing with two folded slips in his sweater pocket: one reading **THIS IS NOT A TRADE**, the other **THIS IS NOT A TRIAL**. He wasn't sure which one he needed more, so he kept both.\n\n## Coda: The Name After the Bell\n\nThe younger transfer girl found Iven by the north planter the next morning.\n\nShe carried a single orange and sat on the low wall with an arm's length of stone between them, close enough to talk without crowding. Iven broke his bread roll in half and laid one piece on the stone. She set the orange beside it.\n\nGirl: \"Not trade.\"\n\nIven: \"I know.\"\n\nShe studied his face until she decided he meant it.\n\nDown the courtyard, the cracked west bell rang late. Students crossed the gravel toward morning classes without staring or curving wide around the two Green Circle kids on the wall. When the last off-pitch note faded into the eaves, the girl leaned slightly toward him and told him her name in a voice barely louder than the breeze.\n\nIven repeated it once and stumbled on the middle sound. She corrected him flatly; he tried again until she nodded and stole the smaller half of his bread. He let her take it.\n\nBefore either of them finished eating, the fountain water went dark from the center outward. Four clean breaks split the reflected ring beneath Aurelis, leaving the fifth space wide open.\n\nPale letters surfaced beneath the clear water, sharp and unmistakable in the morning light:\n\n**THE FIFTH TOWER REMEMBERS.**\n\nIven didn't run for Haru. He cupped both hands around his mouth and called Mira.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-36-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep37","label":"Episode 31","number":"31","file":"episode-37-cover.webp","fallback":"The Fifth Tower Remembers","eyebrow":"Fifth Tower","recap":"","accent":["#0f172a","#bbf7d0","#c0c0c0"],"displayLabel":"Episode 31","displayNumber":"31","displayEyebrow":"The Fifth Tower","arcLabel":"The Fifth Tower","title":"The Fifth Tower Remembers","body":"The fifth tower remembered before any adult agreed it existed, which made it a problem before anyone could name one.\n\nAdults preferred memory after classification. They liked a thing to sit quietly inside a category before it moved, glowed, accused anyone, or wrote impossible sentences under fountain water in front of students still carrying breakfast trays.\n\nThe old water did not wait.\n\nBy the time I reached the courtyard, three copies of the line already existed and none of them matched.\n\nMira stood at the fountain rim with ink on her fingers, two pencils behind one ear, and the expression of someone asked to preserve weather. Aira crouched beside her with one knee on the stone, copying fast and swearing too quietly for the younger students to hear. Professor Halden leaned over the basin from the other side, hands braced on the rim, his face pale with the kind of scholarly delight that looked dangerous without supervision.\n\nPresident Valmont supervised him by existing nearby.\n\nThe fountain water stayed clear, which made it worse.\n\nIf the old system had blackened the basin, opened a ring, or thrown silver glyphs into the air, the academy would have known how to panic. There were procedures now. Too many, probably. Red seals, Blue stabilizers, Green evacuation routes, Yellow containment exceptions, Council witness rails, healer priority lines, and Aira's growing collection of labels that made adults regret their wording.\n\nClear water gave them nothing to fight.\n\nOnly a sentence that had appeared for one breath after the cracked west bell rang late:\n\n**THE FIFTH TOWER REMEMBERS.**\n\nMira had copied it first. Her page said:\n\n**THE FIFTH TOWER REMAINS.**\n\nShe had crossed out the last word before the ink dried. Aira's first copy said:\n\n**THE FIFTH WITNESS REMEMBERS.**\n\nShe had stared at it for ten full seconds, then written beside it:\n\n**WRONG? MAYBE. DO NOT BURN.**\n\nHalden's copy said:\n\n**THE FIFTH TOWER REMEMBERS BELOW.**\n\nHe had not remembered writing the last word, so Rhea had taken his pencil.\n\nThe courtyard gathered around the basin in uneven rings. Bolder students hovered near the public board. Frightened ones pretended to be walking elsewhere and slowed every time the water moved. Green Circle aides stood closest to Mira, not because anyone had ordered them there, but because the ugly-copy table had become a place where ordinary hands knew what to do before ranks decided whether ordinary hands were allowed.\n\nIven Sahl sat on the low wall near the north planter with the younger transfer girl from the previous day's lunch. Not beside her.\n\nIn the direction of beside.\n\nShe had pushed one sleeve back enough for the pale green ring on her wrist to show in the morning light. Iven's own Green Circle rested visible against his borrowed sweater cuff. Neither of them looked at the fountain as if it belonged to them.\n\nThe fountain looked back anyway, with memory instead of eyes.\n\nThe cold pressure behind my sternum had returned. Old Aurelis magic had a taste when it noticed me: ozone, metal, winter stone, and a question I did not know how to answer without becoming the answer.\n\nBeneath my glove, the bruise under one fingernail darkened with every pulse from the basin. Maelin had called the original bleeding a small rupture with an unknown magical cause. It had nearly faded after Valise.\n\nI kept that hand in my pocket.\n\nMy Black Circle stayed hidden under my uniform. Publicly known did not mean publicly visible, though that distinction had survived exactly three days before every institution in the world began testing how small it could make the difference.\n\nA student near the outer ring whispered my name. Quietly, without cruelty, which made it harder. After the public manifestation, after Elira's death and return, after Cael's final act and vigil, after Senna Voss's ruling, people no longer asked whether the Black Circle existed.\n\nThey asked what could be done with it.\n\nElira's fingers brushed mine.\n\nShe had come down from the witness cottage wrapped in a pale cloak over her academy uniform, hair loose over one shoulder because Maelin had forbidden formal braids until her hands stopped shaking after sleep. The cloak made her look softer from a distance. Up close, nothing about her was soft except the shadow under her eyes.\n\nElira: \"You are standing like a guilty monument.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was trying for harmless student.\"\n\nElira looked at the courtyard. At the students watching without watching. At Rhea holding Halden's stolen pencil like evidence. At Mira's pages refusing to match.\n\nElira: \"Your range has narrowed.\"\n\nI should have smiled. I almost did.\n\nThen the fountain gave one small ripple. Every conversation stopped.\n\nThe water did not darken. No letters formed. No ring opened under the stones. The ripple caught the reflection of the west bell tower and bent it toward the center of the basin, as if the old stone wanted to be seen from below. Halden inhaled.\n\nRhea: \"No.\"\n\nHalden closed his mouth. Aira, still crouched, glanced up.\n\nAira: \"That was either the fastest disciplinary action in academy history or you two have developed a terrifying marriage of tone.\"\n\nRhea did not look away from the water.\n\nRhea: \"Professor Halden was about to say something with the word 'obvious' in it.\"\n\nHalden: \"I was not.\"\n\nRhea: \"You were breathing academically.\"\n\nMira, without looking up, wrote:\n\n**ACADEMIC BREATHING DETECTED. NOT EVIDENCE.**\n\nThe Green aides nearest her relaxed first, and the order mattered. The rest of the courtyard followed a half breath later. Fear shifted from throat to floor, settling into watchful standing.\n\nMaelin Orr pushed through the outer ring with a healer's bag in one hand and a face that made students part before remembering she was not tall enough to be that intimidating.\n\nMaelin: \"If this fountain has decided to become a patient, it can take a number.\"\n\nHalden looked offended.\n\nHalden: \"This is old-system emergence, not a patient.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Does it have symptoms?\"\n\nHalden hesitated.\n\nMaelin: \"Then it can take a number.\"\n\nShe reached Elira and looked her over with the efficient cruelty of someone who could measure pulse from posture.\n\nMaelin: \"You are pale.\"\n\nElira: \"I was killed recently.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I am aware. I signed three forms about it before tea.\"\n\nElira: \"Then my coloring should not surprise you.\"\n\nMaelin's eyes narrowed.\n\nMaelin: \"Sarcasm: stable. Bad judgment: returning.\"\n\nElira: \"Encouraging.\"\n\nMaelin pointed one finger toward the courtyard breakfast tables and the witness cottage beyond them.\n\nMaelin: \"Fifteen minutes outside. Then food. Then rest.\"\n\nElira's hand tightened once around mine. Not fear. Refusal preparing itself.\n\nElira: \"No.\"\n\nThe word carried lightly, and still landed. Maelin did not blink.\n\nMaelin: \"I am not asking whether you enjoy food.\"\n\nElira: \"I will eat. I will rest. I will not be put away because the old system wrote something near Haru.\"\n\nAround us, several students looked down too quickly, caught in the sudden shame of people who had almost agreed with the thing before it was named.\n\nMy instinct rose before my voice did.\n\nHaru: \"Elira.\"\n\nShe turned to me. Gold eyes, tired and clear.\n\nElira: \"Careful.\"\n\nThe word stopped me because it was not warning me away from danger. It was warning me away from becoming familiar with putting her behind doors.\n\nI looked at our joined hands. Three students near the public board looked too.\n\nBlue Circle Girl: \"Stability contact.\"\n\nShe whispered it to the girl beside her as if affection were a technical term she could carry safely. My fingers loosened.\n\nElira caught them before I could withdraw. Publicly. Deliberately. The courtyard noticed.\n\nSo did the Black Circle. It stirred beneath my skin toward claim, toward the old animal instinct that wanted to build a wall before anyone else decided what could enter.\n\nElira felt the shift through my palm and stepped closer instead of away.\n\nElira: \"Do not protect me into silence because people are watching.\"\n\nThe sentence hurt because it was accurate. I had not said silence. I had thought safer. Sometimes the old world and love used different words for the same locked door.\n\nHaru: \"Every ordinary thing we do now becomes evidence.\"\n\nElira: \"Then let them record that I chose this.\"\n\nHaru: \"They will call it dependency.\"\n\nElira: \"They already did.\"\n\nHaru: \"They will call it influence.\"\n\nElira: \"Then we answer influence without pretending distance is purity.\"\n\nHer hand was warm, though still weaker than it should have been, still too easy for me to read as a medical fact instead of a human one.\n\nElira: \"Haru, if I stand only where no one can misuse my standing, I will spend the rest of my life sitting in rooms designed by people who want me quiet.\"\n\nI had no clean answer. Clean answers were becoming suspicious.\n\nAira cleared her throat from the fountain rim.\n\nAira: \"For the record, I hate interrupting romantic ethical combat, but the water just made Professor Halden's copy worse.\"\n\nHalden looked down. The page in his hand now read:\n\n**THE FIFTH TOWER REMEMBERS BELOW GREEN HANDS.**\n\nNo one had touched the ink.\n\nFor one second, the courtyard went very still.\n\nMira: \"Do not copy that as authoritative.\"\n\nAira pointed at her.\n\nAira: \"Excellent. First practical rule.\"\n\nShe stood, stretched her cramped leg, and lifted her notebook like a blade made of bureaucracy.\n\nAira: \"Public handling protocol for old fountain nonsense. Draft one. One: no single copy becomes official until versions are compared. Two: do not improve ugly wording for dignity, clarity, school reputation, personal comfort, or Professor Halden's feelings.\"\n\nHalden: \"My feelings have not been entered into evidence.\"\n\nAira: \"Noted as aspirational.\"\n\nMira tapped her pencil once against the stone rim.\n\nMira: \"Three: if old-system language changes while being copied, preserve the change beside the source, not over it.\"\n\nRhea: \"Four: no classification request names Haru Aerlen as the cause until sequence establishes cause.\"\n\nHer eyes moved to me for the last part, steady and exact.\n\nRhea: \"And no protective order names him as solution before witnesses identify the problem.\"\n\nThe pressure beneath my sternum changed. Not gone. Listening.\n\nThat frightened me more than resistance would have. If the Fifth Tower had rejected us, I could understand danger. If it had opened only for me, I could understand isolation. Instead, the old water was responding to Mira's crossed-out line, to Aira's practical suspicion, to Green Circle hands moving paper before power could make itself ornamental.\n\nIt was not calling me. Not first.\n\nBefore the reveal, I had feared being exposed. Now I feared being useful.\n\nUseful meant public safety could ask for one demonstration. Useful meant Council review could request one isolated measure. Useful meant frightened students could sleep easier if I stood near the fountain every morning and answered every old door, until no one had to hate me to use me.\n\nProfessor Valen arrived carrying two sealed objects: the Regalia shard from the collapsed fountain and the old witness-glass that held Liora Aerlen-Valen's echo.\n\nThe shard's gold split rotated on its own, pointing high toward the blind masonry above the west tower's bell arch.\n\nThe witness-glass clouded as Valen angled it toward the basin's reflection. Liora appeared only from the shoulders up, older light breaking across her face.\n\nLiora: \"That window is the servant stair.\"\n\nValen stopped breathing academically.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Whose servant?\"\n\nLiora: \"No one's. That was the argument. My brother used it when Regalia guarded the high door and witnesses needed a route beneath the crown.\"\n\nThe echo looked toward the shard as if recognizing an old wound.\n\nLiora: \"If Regalia is listening to the tower again, do not give either one a single keeper.\"\n\nThe image failed before Valen could ask her brother's name.\n\nElira's thumb moved once across my knuckle. She had understood before I did.\n\n---\n\nThe first formal filing arrived during breakfast at the courtyard tables.\n\nSenna appeared in the Council mirror with Krail's title floating beside her:\n\n**MOTION FOR TEMPORARY BLACK CIRCLE PUBLIC STABILITY DESIGNATION**\n\nThe kind words were the dangerous ones. Civic stabilizer. Protective logistics. Voluntary monitoring. Reduced exposure to procedurally unstable Green copy networks. Elira listed as an affective variable; rescued Noctis students as contaminated testimony. My spoon trembled. Elira covered my hand.\n\nElira: \"I am not a variable.\"\n\nMira: \"We are not an unstable network.\"\n\nSenna: \"Both objections entered. The motion is stayed pending causal evidence.\"\n\nRhea: \"And my authority?\"\n\nSenna faced the public slate so no later summary could polish the answer.\n\nSenna: \"Headmaster Caelum Veris remains headmaster. He is under mandatory conflict recusal from Foundation, Noctis, and Black Circle matters while inherited seals from his office are audited. He retains ordinary academic administration.\"\n\nThe absence finally had a name.\n\nSenna: \"President Valmont's emergency mandate covers campus movement, student protection, and immediate hazard response. It does not grant foreign custody, permanent classification authority, or power to command faculty outside a declared hazard. Every use remains reviewable.\"\n\nRhea accepted the boundary without pretending to enjoy it.\n\nRhea: \"Record my next action under those limits: this filing does not govern breakfast, the copy table, or Haru's friendships.\"\n\nAira: \"Historic defense of pastry.\"\n\nThe motion stayed sealed. Krail received written objections instead of the live spectacle he wanted, and the mirror dimmed.\n\nThen Leon placed a blue-wax Varek envelope beside his tray.\n\nHe stared at it through the rest of breakfast as if the seal might speak if he refused to break it long enough. Cassian stayed beside him and said nothing, which was how I knew he was worried. Aira kept glancing between the envelope and the fountain across the stones, torn between social curiosity and survival instinct. Mira pretended not to notice with the intensity of someone tracking every angle.\n\nFinally, Leon picked up the envelope.\n\nLeon: \"I should open it before my family finds a way to make unopened mail hereditary misconduct.\"\n\nCassian: \"Can they do that?\"\n\nLeon: \"Probably not.\"\n\nHe broke the seal.\n\nLeon: \"Legally.\"\n\nThe paper inside was thick, pale blue, and expensive enough to make cruelty look well-funded. Leon read silently. Annoyance gave way to embarrassment, then anger, then hurt disciplined flat before anyone could comfort it.\n\nHe folded the letter once, too neatly. Rhea saw it.\n\nRhea: \"You may keep it private.\"\n\nLeon laughed without humor.\n\nLeon: \"That is what it wants.\"\n\nHe unfolded the letter and laid it flat on the table. The Varek crest caught the morning light. I did not read it at first, waiting until he offered. Leon looked at me, then at Elira, then at Cael's slate stone in his palm.\n\nLeon: \"My father says House Varek has received inquiries about my proximity to unstable witness politics.\"\n\nCassian's eyes went flat.\n\nLeon: \"He says my testimony during the autonomy proof was understandable under duress, but my continued association with Green Circle copy networks and former Noctis students risks implying family endorsement of anti-rank agitation.\"\n\nAira: \"Anti-rank agitation?\"\n\nLeon: \"Apparently that is what happens when people with lower Circles write things down where upper families can see them.\"\n\nMira's face had gone still. Leon did not look at her, out of mercy or shame or both.\n\nLeon: \"I have been invited to issue a clarifying statement.\"\n\nElira: \"Invited.\"\n\nLeon: \"Family word.\"\n\nRhea: \"Translation?\"\n\nLeon: \"Do it or lose things.\"\n\nHe smoothed the page open so I could read. The statement was already drafted. It expressed concern for affected students, praised Cael Nox's courage in the careful way people praise the dead when they want no obligations to the living, and declared that Leon Varek's prior testimony should not be read as opposition to rank-guided review or the temporary separation of emotionally entangled witnesses.\n\nThen came the sentence that mattered:\n\n**I withdraw any implied personal standing granted by my inclusion in Cael Nox's private notes and defer to recognized authorities for future interpretation of his record.**\n\nThe paper blurred at the edges of my vision from quiet anger. Cael had written Leon's name as a question: *Does Leon know beside is enough?* House Varek had found the question and tried to answer over him.\n\nLeon set the slate stone on top of the draft, covering his family crest. No magic answered. Some refusals did not need magic to become real.\n\nRhea: \"What happens if you do not sign?\"\n\nLeon looked toward the west arcade, where several Blue Circle students had gathered close enough to hear and far enough to deny listening. I recognized two from his old circle, boys with polished cuffs and the easy posture of people who never wondered whether a room had been built with them in mind. One looked away when Leon met his eyes. The other did not.\n\nLeon: \"My discretionary allowance freezes. My family seat in the Blue Circle alumni mentorship program goes to my cousin. My summer training placement becomes unavailable due to political sensitivity. My mother will send a letter with softer words and the same knife.\"\n\nCassian: \"That all?\"\n\nLeon almost smiled.\n\nLeon: \"I will also be unbearable at dinner for several years.\"\n\nAira: \"That may predate this issue.\"\n\nThe joke landed badly, and still landed.\n\nLeon breathed once through his nose, then stood.\n\nRhea: \"Leon.\"\n\nHe looked down at her.\n\nRhea: \"You do not need an audience.\"\n\nLeon glanced at the watching Blue students, then at Mira's copy table, then at Iven sitting rigid near the north planter, and finally at the younger Green girl beside him, whose sleeve had crept back down over her wrist.\n\nLeon picked up the letter.\n\nLeon: \"Yes, I do.\"\n\nThe old Leon would have turned refusal into theater because performance protected pride. This Leon looked as if every watching eye cost him something, and he was counting the price anyway.\n\nHe crossed the courtyard to the public board. Mira followed with a blank witness slate. Aira followed with three pencils. Cassian followed because he had apparently decided the air around Leon required fireproofing.\n\nI stayed seated. Elira's hand stayed over mine, reminding without restraining. This was not mine to make safer.\n\nLeon stopped before the board where Cael's line rested under protective glass:\n\n**I WANTED TO LIVE. I CHOSE ANYWAY. BOTH ARE TRUE.**\n\nBelow it, copies of Iven's sentence had multiplied through the morning:\n\n**THIS IS NOT A TRADE.**\n\n**THIS IS NOT A TRIAL.**\n\n**FOUNDATION MEANS THE PART THAT HOLDS WHILE OTHER THINGS FALL.**\n\nOne student had misread *Foundation* as *Found Nation*, and Mira had preserved it under a note reading: **ACCIDENTAL MEANING. KEEP BESIDE SOURCE.**\n\nLeon looked at all of them, then pinned his family's drafted statement directly to the board. Gasps rippled across the stones.\n\nHe took a blank page from Mira.\n\nMira: \"Do you want a clean page or an ugly one?\"\n\nLeon considered.\n\nLeon: \"Ugly.\"\n\nMira handed him a sheet with a bent corner and a faint ink thumbprint near the top.\n\nMira: \"Sorry.\"\n\nLeon: \"No. This is better.\"\n\nHe wrote slowly. His handwriting started precise by training, then the pressure shifted halfway through the first line as the letters stopped trying to impress the paper.\n\n**HOUSE VAREK DID NOT RECEIVE STANDING FROM CAEL NOX. I DID.**\n\nThe courtyard held its breath.\n\n**I DO NOT WITHDRAW IT.**\n\nHis hand paused, counting the next cost before paying it.\n\n**GREEN CIRCLE WITNESSES AND RESCUED NOCTIS STUDENTS ARE NOT CONTAMINATION SOURCES. THEY ARE THE PEOPLE WHO KEPT THE RECORD FROM BECOMING PROPERTY.**\n\nOne of the Blue students near the arcade muttered something sharp. Leon's shoulders tightened as old pride rose behind the gate, then he looked back at Cael's line and the gate held.\n\n**LEON VAREK, LISTED BY CAEL NOX, STANDING BESIDE.**\n\nHe signed without a flourish, then braced Cael's slate stone at the bottom edge until the pin held. No one cheered. I saw Leon's shoulders drop in relief that applause had not arrived to make the refusal easy to resent later.\n\nThe academy public slate chimed too quickly. House Varek had been waiting.\n\n**VAREK FAMILY SPONSORSHIP PRIVILEGES UNDER REVIEW.**\n\n**BLUE CIRCLE ALUMNI MENTORSHIP SEAT REALLOCATED.**\n\n**PRIVATE CORRESPONDENCE REQUESTED.**\n\nLeon's face did not change, which meant the damage had landed. Cassian stepped closer.\n\nLeon: \"Do not make a joke.\"\n\nCassian closed his mouth, then opened it.\n\nCassian: \"I was going to offer arson.\"\n\nLeon looked at him.\n\nCassian: \"Different category.\"\n\nLeon let out a single breath that was almost a laugh, not enough to erase the hurt.\n\nNear the north planter, Iven stood. The younger girl caught his sleeve. He stopped, and she shook her head once. Iven sat again. Relief crossed Leon's face when the rescued Noctis boy did not rush over to thank him. The refusal stayed clean because the world had failed to pay him for it.\n\nElira stood beside me too quickly. Maelin made a sharp sound of warning, which Elira ignored as she walked to the public board. I walked with her because she wanted me there, not because she needed carrying.\n\nElira studied Leon's statement, then looked up at him.\n\nElira: \"That was not easy.\"\n\nLeon swallowed.\n\nLeon: \"No.\"\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nHis eyes flicked to her, and she offered the faintest smile.\n\nElira: \"Easy would have been suspicious.\"\n\nLeon looked down, then held out the unsigned Varek draft.\n\nLeon: \"Can ugly copies use this?\"\n\nMira took the page before anyone else could reach it.\n\nMira: \"Give it here.\"\n\nAira: \"With annotation.\"\n\nRhea, coming up behind us, added quietly:\n\nRhea: \"Extensive annotation.\"\n\nLeon nodded and left the draft in Mira's hands.\n\nAcross the courtyard, the fountain water moved again.\n\nThis time it did not write. It reflected the west bell tower, except the tower in the basin had more windows than the pale stone above us. Five dark niches stacked above the bell arch where daylight showed only blank wall.\n\nHalden whispered first.\n\nHalden: \"Those are not windows.\"\n\nRhea: \"Explain without touching anything.\"\n\nHalden clasped his hands behind his back so violently I respected him more.\n\nHalden: \"Observation niches. Old maintenance witnesses. Before rank-color standardization, civic magic required distributed local memory built into structures: bells, lamps, water lines, kitchens, infirmary thresholds, repair ledgers.\"\n\nAira: \"You are saying the tower remembers maintenance.\"\n\nHalden looked pained by the compression. Valen leaned both hands on her cane, eyes fixed on the reflected tiers.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"She has the core of it. The first four towers honored recognized academic authority: theory, combat, restoration, and governance. The fifth was never drawn on public diagrams because it carried no prestige.\"\n\nMira's voice dropped very low.\n\nMira: \"Foundation.\"\n\nProfessor Valen: \"Labor. Continuity. Witness by maintenance. The part that knew whether the doors opened, whether the lamps stayed lit, whether the sick were fed, and whether a copied notice reached the lower dormitory before the upper hall rewrote it. If Circle Zero preserved anything there, it will not answer rank first.\"\n\nThe water brightened under the reflected niches. Not black. Not silver. Green. Soft, low, almost embarrassed to be seen.\n\nMy hidden circle did not rise. It listened.\n\nAnd around the courtyard, students turned toward me anyway. Because the Black Circle was public. Because the Council filing had called me stabilizer. Because people looked for the largest power nearby before they looked at the Green hands already holding the pages.\n\nPower did not need to demand a throne if frightened people kept building chairs around it.\n\nI took one step back from the fountain. The courtyard noticed. Elira noticed most.\n\nHaru: \"If I go closer, they will call it response.\"\n\nElira: \"And if you stand in front, they will forget anyone else has hands.\"\n\nShe kept her fingers hooked lightly against the cuff of my sleeve instead of the basin rim.\n\nHaru: \"If I stay back, they will call it instability.\"\n\nElira: \"Probably.\"\n\nHaru: \"If you stand with me, they will call it influence.\"\n\nElira: \"They have poor vocabulary.\"\n\nShe looked exhausted enough that every joke cost something, and spent one anyway.\n\nElira: \"Haru, you do not have to vanish to avoid becoming a ruler.\"\n\nThe fountain rippled. The green niches in the reflection held steady. Aira had stopped writing.\n\nElira: \"Stand where you are. Let someone else reach first.\"\n\nMy hands were cold. I had crossed battlefields and broken old wards, and standing still while someone else reached first was harder.\n\nMira Castellan stepped up to the stone rim with five ugly copies in her hands. Iven stood too. The younger girl followed three steps behind him, one sleeve pushed halfway up her forearm. A Green aide named Finn carried Leon's pinned statement.\n\nLeon looked as if he wanted to stop them, then closed his mouth. Once a refusal left your hand, its consequences belonged to the courtyard.\n\nMira placed the first copy on the damp stone rim.\n\nMira: \"Public record note. The Fifth Tower phrase cannot be copied perfectly. We are not correcting it into one version. We are preserving the differences beside each other.\"\n\nThe water stayed clear. She placed the second page.\n\nMira: \"Green Circle witness work is not contamination.\"\n\nThe water gave one small green pulse. Finn nearly dropped Leon's statement.\n\nAira: \"Do not drop the politically expensive paper.\"\n\nFinn steadied it and set it beside Mira's hand. She placed the third page.\n\nMira: \"Haru Aerlen is not named as cause, stabilizer, ruler, tower, or substitute institution in this record.\"\n\nThe pressure behind my sternum eased so suddenly I almost stepped forward. Elira's fingers caught my sleeve. I stayed.\n\nMira laid Iven's pages side by side.\n\nMira: \"Care given here is not a trade. Witness requested here is not a trial.\"\n\nThe younger Green Circle girl, who had not yet put her name on any public record, took a sharp breath and stepped directly beside Iven. Her wrist trembled. Her pale green ring brightened.\n\nGirl: \"And... you can take longer here.\"\n\nHer voice barely crossed the basin, and crossed anyway. Iven looked at her as a fellow witness, and Mira wrote the sentence down before fear could take it back.\n\nThe fountain water turned green from the bottom up, like a lamp lit in a service stair after everyone had forgotten stairs needed light too.\n\nAcross the courtyard, the cracked west bell sounded on its own, late as always.\n\nInside the tower, something answered. A low click. Stone remembering a latch.\n\nAbove the courtyard wall, the real west tower stood narrow and pale against the morning sky, showing only its four dark windows below the bell arch. Nothing moved in the stone for three breaths.\n\nThen the academy public slate flashed:\n\n**FIFTH TOWER ACCESS REQUEST: DENIED TO CENTRAL AUTHORITY.**\n\nHalden made a sound like a man watching a rare book lock itself.\n\n**FOUNDATION WITNESS MEMORY: PARTIAL WAKE.**\n\nRhea lifted her chin.\n\nRhea: \"Conditions?\"\n\nThe slate hesitated, old and deliberate, before answering line by line.\n\n**NO SINGLE HAND.**\n\nAira copied so fast her pencil squeaked.\n\n**NO CROWNED CAUSE.**\n\nElira's eyes moved to me.\n\n**NO DEBT FOR CARE.**\n\nIven's face softened by a fraction.\n\n**NO SILENCE BY PROTECTION.**\n\nElira's hand found mine again, in full view of the courtyard. This time I did not pull away.\n\nOne final line burned onto the slate:\n\n**GREEN HANDS MAY ENTER MEMORY FIRST. BLACK CIRCLE MAY WITNESS FROM THRESHOLD.**\n\nEvery eye snapped back to me, and yet the sentence had named a limit before a power. Threshold. Not entry, leadership, or solution.\n\nMira looked at Rhea instead of me.\n\nMira: \"Do we have to enter now?\"\n\nRhea: \"No. A door that cannot wait is a demand. We do not enter demands.\"\n\nThe slate held the silence, then added:\n\n**ACCEPTED.**\n\nHalden closed his eyes.\n\nHalden: \"It accepted refusal.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Healthy response.\"\n\nHalden opened his eyes with helpless offense.\n\nMaelin: \"I told you. Patient.\"\n\nFor one breath, impossible old magic and an ordinary academy morning stood in the same courtyard without trying to swallow each other.\n\nThen the Council mirror chimed again.\n\nSenna's face returned, and behind her the chamber seats had begun to fill with Councilors, safety officers, and delegation representatives who had never stood in the courtyard and still felt qualified to name it.\n\nSenna: \"The stabilizer motion has been amended.\"\n\nRhea: \"Already?\"\n\nSenna: \"The Fifth Tower response entered public relay.\"\n\nAira: \"How?\"\n\nSenna's gaze shifted toward the green glow in the basin.\n\nSenna: \"The tower appears to have objected to being summarized.\"\n\nMira's mouth parted in the closest thing to delight I had ever seen on her.\n\nSenna: \"Krail has withdrawn the word stabilizer.\"\n\nThat should have helped. It did not. Senna kept her hands clasped on the chamber table, voice level.\n\nSenna: \"He now requests emergency review of Foundation witness access, arguing that if Green Circle networks can activate old memory architecture, then they may require rank-supervised protection from exploitation by extra-classification gravity.\"\n\nThe courtyard went cold. There was the machinery turning without a pause: if Haru was the danger, isolate Haru; if Green witnesses answered, supervise Green witnesses; if refusal worked, review refusal.\n\nLeon let out a short, harsh laugh.\n\nLeon: \"They will protect everyone into a smaller room if we let them.\"\n\nRhea looked at Leon, then at Mira, then at the younger Green girl whose wrist stayed uncovered.\n\nRhea: \"Then we do not let them.\"\n\nSenna: \"I can delay the formal hearing until evening. Not longer.\"\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nMaelin: \"That was not addressed to you.\"\n\nElira: \"I heard it anyway.\"\n\nMaelin: \"Your hearing participation is medically discouraged.\"\n\nElira: \"I am medically discouraging being categorized while absent.\"\n\nMaelin looked up at the sky as if requesting a colleague with less stubborn patients. Elira turned to me, and this time the friction between us did not hide inside worry.\n\nElira: \"I am speaking tonight.\"\n\nEverything in me wanted to refuse. She was pale, alive, and one bad turn away from people using concern as custody again.\n\nHaru: \"You stood up too fast at breakfast, and Maelin barely cleared you to cross the courtyard.\"\n\nElira: \"And your fingernail is bleeding again.\"\n\nMy hidden hand became the center of the courtyard without leaving my pocket. Maelin's head snapped toward me. I had not known Elira saw.\n\nElira: \"Take off the glove.\"\n\nHaru: \"Not here.\"\n\nElira's anger stayed quiet, which made it sharper.\n\nElira: \"You want me to speak publicly about knowing my own condition while you edit yours out of the courtyard.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is not what I am doing.\"\n\nElira: \"Then what are you doing?\"\n\nHaru: \"If every Black Circle reaction becomes a medical event, I stop being a student. I become an instrument everyone checks whenever old magic moves.\"\n\nElira: \"So you decided no one could be trusted with the reading.\"\n\nHaru: \"I decided to wait.\"\n\nElira: \"Until when? After the hearing? After the tower opens? After you decide the pain is large enough to deserve witnesses?\"\n\nMaelin crossed the remaining distance and held out her hand.\n\nMaelin: \"Glove.\"\n\nHaru: \"Maelin, wait.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You may preserve mystery or finger. Choose quickly.\"\n\nI pulled off the glove. The old bruise had spread from the nail bed in a dark crescent, with one fresh line of blood beneath the skin. Elira looked at it, then looked away, jaw tight.\n\nHaru: \"I did not want this used to stop you from speaking.\"\n\nElira: \"You do not protect my choice by controlling the facts around it.\"\n\nHaru: \"I know.\"\n\nElira: \"You knew at the fountain. You promised Maelin you would report if it returned.\"\n\nShame burned hot where the Black Circle sat cold.\n\nHaru: \"I broke that promise.\"\n\nElira: \"Do not make me drag truth out of your hand and call that partnership.\"\n\nI wanted to defend the motive, the terror of becoming Krail's living gauge, of handing him proof that power owned me more than I owned it. None of that repaired what I had hidden.\n\nHaru: \"I am afraid pain is the only thing they will believe about me.\"\n\nHer anger did not vanish, though it made room.\n\nElira: \"I am afraid absence is the only thing they will believe about me.\"\n\nTwo different pressures that could injure each other if we pretended love made them the same.\n\nMaelin wrapped my finger in a strip of blue diagnostic cloth. The fabric darkened near the nail and sent one reading to her slate.\n\nMaelin: \"Unknown response recurrence. No active channel loss. Black Circle exertion restricted until I understand why old architecture is pulling on the injury.\"\n\nHaru: \"The hearing tonight.\"\n\nMaelin: \"You may attend as witness. You may not demonstrate.\"\n\nElira: \"And me?\"\n\nMaelin looked equally displeased with both of us.\n\nMaelin: \"Ten minutes speaking time. Seated. One break available without explanation. Exceed either limit and I remove you personally.\"\n\nElira: \"Accepted.\"\n\nI waited until Maelin stepped back to fetch another slate.\n\nHaru: \"I am sorry.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nShe did not take my hand yet.\n\nElira: \"Tell me before I discover it next time. Even if all you can say is that you are scared of what the fact will become.\"\n\nHaru: \"I will tell you.\"\n\nShe touched my wrist above Maelin's cloth, clear of the injury. Neither the fear nor the bruise went away, and for once that felt more honest than comfort.\n\nAira cleared her throat.\n\nAira: \"I hate interrupting what is either a relationship argument or civic theory, but an evening hearing means we need copies, witness order, version tables, medical limits, tower conditions, Leon's public family disaster, and snacks.\"\n\nCassian: \"Why snacks?\"\n\nAira: \"Because if institutions are going to process us into categories, I refuse to be hungry for it.\"\n\nMaelin pointed at her.\n\nMaelin: \"Correct.\"\n\nRhea began dividing the work. Mira would lead version comparison. Aira would draft the answer matrix. Halden and Valen would document what the Fifth Tower was not before anyone tried to name what it was. Maelin would enforce medical limits and threaten anyone who confused treatment with disappearance.\n\nRhea turned to Leon.\n\nRhea: \"Your family statement can enter tonight's record, or wait until it will not drown the Green witness issue.\"\n\nLeon: \"Enter it.\"\n\nRhea studied him.\n\nLeon: \"They made it part of the same pressure. Let it sit there.\"\n\nRhea nodded without praising him, then looked at me last.\n\nRhea: \"Haru, you will not interpret the Fifth Tower.\"\n\nSomething in me eased, and something else tightened.\n\nRhea: \"You will not demonstrate response, offer access, stabilize the fountain, correct the copies, authenticate the green light, answer speculative questions, or stand where anyone can claim the system centered you.\"\n\nAira: \"I have never heard someone assign a person to aggressively not do things.\"\n\nRhea: \"Then take notes.\"\n\nAira uncapped her ink. Rhea kept her gaze on me, steady beside the copy table.\n\nRhea: \"Your role is threshold witness. If the Black Circle reacts, you report the reaction as one witness among others. Not first. Not final.\"\n\nOne witness among others sounded survivable.\n\nHaru: \"Understood.\"\n\nRhea: \"Do you object?\"\n\nI looked at the green light under the fountain water, at Mira sorting mismatched pages, at Leon standing straight after losing half his world's favor, and at Elira refusing to go quiet for anyone's comfort.\n\nHaru: \"No. I am relieved.\"\n\nAira's pencil paused for one beat, then caught the line. The fountain did not brighten, the tower did not chime, and no one turned relief into law.\n\n---\n\nThe afternoon became work. Work gave fear a table, labels, and tasks too small to turn into prophecy.\n\nMira built the version board under the arcade. She pinned each failed copy of the Fifth Tower line in sequence and drew thin connecting marks between changed words: *tower* to *witness*, *remembers* to *remains*, *below* to *green hands*, *no single hand* to *no crowned cause*.\n\nShe refused three offers to rewrite the board neatly. The third came from a Council aide who looked personally injured by uneven margins.\n\nMira: \"The uneven margins are evidence.\"\n\nCouncil Aide: \"They are difficult to read.\"\n\nMira: \"Good.\"\n\nShe tapped the corner of the newly numbered page flat with two ink-stained fingers.\n\nCouncil Aide: \"That is not a virtue.\"\n\nMira: \"It is if the alternative makes it easier to lie.\"\n\nThe aide looked at Rhea for backup. Rhea offered none.\n\nAira constructed what she called the \"No, But Specifically\" matrix. Down one side she listed hostile labels: *stabilizer, variable, court, contamination source, rank agitation, dependency, unsupervised Green network, extra-classification gravity*. Across the top she listed answer columns: *sequence, personhood, consent, distributed witness, medical condition, public wording, practical consequence*, and a final column labeled *sentence that would make Krail regret literacy*.\n\nHalden and Valen argued beside the fountain over whether the Fifth Tower was pre-standard civic memory, surviving post-erasure architecture, or an old maintenance node absorbed into Circle Zero vocabulary. Maelin asked whether the distinction changed who might faint. Halden said no. Maelin told them to put it in the appendix.\n\nI sat on the witness cottage step with Elira while she rested under protest. She had stayed on the indoor cot for twenty minutes, then insisted on moving to the step so she would not have to pretend the world had no windows. Maelin allowed it only after adding two blankets, a pulse charm, and a warning that read like a small penal code.\n\nStudents crossed the courtyard in careful arcs around the working tables. One first-year Green Circle boy stopped halfway past us, looked at my face, then looked toward the version board.\n\nFirst-Year: \"Are we allowed to copy the tower line?\"\n\nI felt the old pull to reassure him, easy and harmless. Elira's shoulder touched mine, witness without warning. I turned slightly toward Mira.\n\nHaru: \"Ask the copy table.\"\n\nThe boy blinked, then hurried over to Mira. She handed him a blank sheet.\n\nMira: \"Copy one version badly and sign your hand.\"\n\nFirst-Year: \"Which version?\"\n\nMira: \"The one that does not leave you alone after you read it.\"\n\nHe studied the board with grave seriousness, then chose **NO DEBT FOR CARE.** His copy made *debt* look like *diet*. Aira declared the error dangerous before lunch and possibly useful after. The boy smiled all the way back across the courtyard.\n\nHaru: \"I wanted to answer him.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"It would have been kind.\"\n\nElira: \"And it would have centered you.\"\n\nShe leaned her head back against the cottage doorframe, eyes half closed.\n\nHaru: \"Wrong?\"\n\nElira: \"Not always wrong. Today wrong.\"\n\nAbstention could become another throne if I turned it rigid. Threshold meant choosing when not to cross.\n\nLeon came up the cottage path holding a fresh notice. The Varek crest was gone from the top, which somehow made it worse.\n\nLeon: \"My mother wrote.\"\n\nElira opened her eyes.\n\nElira: \"Softer words?\"\n\nLeon sat on the lower step without asking for comfort or refusing it.\n\nLeon: \"Very soft.\"\n\nHe held out the page. I kept my hands still.\n\nHaru: \"Do you want me to read it?\"\n\nHe looked surprised, then faintly embarrassed by the surprise.\n\nLeon: \"Read it.\"\n\nThe letter matched his prediction. Gentle, concerned, proud of his compassion and worried for his judgment. It said House Varek had always valued service to those of lesser circumstance, that service lost meaning when it turned political, that his father was hurt, and that the mentorship seat could still be restored if Leon clarified that his heart had outrun his discipline. It did not mention Cael by name.\n\nI handed it back. Leon folded it once, crookedly. Progress.\n\nLeon: \"There was a time that letter would have worked.\"\n\nElira: \"And now?\"\n\nLeon looked toward the public board, where students were quietly reading both the Varek draft and his reply.\n\nLeon: \"Now it still hurts.\"\n\nHe sounded annoyed by the fact.\n\nElira: \"Of course it does.\"\n\nLeon rubbed one thumb along the folded paper's edge.\n\nLeon: \"I thought choosing correctly would make it feel less like losing.\"\n\nHaru: \"It rarely does at first.\"\n\nLeon glanced at me.\n\nLeon: \"You sound like you know.\"\n\nI thought of years spent hiding a Circle no one understood, choosing restraint while people called restraint weakness until they learned to call it evidence.\n\nHaru: \"Sometimes.\"\n\nLeon nodded and asked no more. After a moment he spoke quietly.\n\nLeon: \"Cael would have hated that my family made this about me.\"\n\nElira's expression softened with grief.\n\nLeon: \"I hate that too. But if I pretend it did not cost anything, I make it noble enough for people to admire instead of useful enough to repeat.\"\n\nHaru: \"What will you do with your mother's letter?\"\n\nLeon stood.\n\nLeon: \"Keep it.\"\n\nElira: \"Not copy it?\"\n\nLeon: \"Not yet. Some things should hurt privately before they become public enough to survive.\"\n\nHe walked back down the path with the letter in his hand and Cael's slate stone in his pocket.\n\nElira watched him cross the stones.\n\nElira: \"Cost without performance.\"\n\nHaru: \"He is ahead of me in one subject.\"\n\nElira's mouth curved.\n\nElira: \"Several, if you count making friends with Cassian while complaining.\"\n\nHaru: \"That is a specialized field.\"\n\nHer smile vanished when the pulse charm at her wrist gave a warning flicker. I reached out, stopped myself, and waited. She saw the hesitation.\n\nElira: \"You may help me stand.\"\n\nHaru: \"Thank you.\"\n\nElira: \"Do not sound so formal. I am still irritated with you.\"\n\nI helped her up slowly in full view of the courtyard. Her fingers were cool. She leaned on me for three steps, then stood on her own. Both parts mattered.\n\n---\n\nSenna refused Krail a closed chamber that evening.\n\nInstead, the Council mirrors opened beside Mira's version board as dusk settled over the courtyard. Krail's amended motion hung in the relay for twelve minutes, proposing to suspend unsupervised Green copying until rank-balanced supervision could protect vulnerable students.\n\nMira stepped up to the mirror with ink on both hands.\n\nSenna: \"Do you request standing?\"\n\nMira: \"I already have hands.\"\n\nShe gestured to the pinned pages behind her.\n\nMira: \"Green students did not wake the tower alone. Aira copied. Halden copied. Leon signed. Elira objected. Haru stayed outside the center. Removing Green hands changes the process you claim to protect.\"\n\nKrail: \"I proposed supervision, not removal.\"\n\nMira: \"At Aurelis, supervision means someone stands near while you work. At Noctis, it meant someone stood over you until your hand disappeared. Which one did you write?\"\n\nLeon stepped beside her with his unsigned Varek draft.\n\nLeon: \"My family used your argument first. They called these students agitation and the rescued students contamination.\"\n\nKrail: \"You are emotional.\"\n\nLeon: \"Angry, and correct.\"\n\nHe held Cael's slate stone flat over the Varek crest.\n\nKrail shifted target at once.\n\nKrail: \"And the affective variable remains unexamined. Miss Elira's own recovery was tethered to Black Circle intervention, yet she now sits beside the record table as if medical fragility has no bearing on witness stability.\"\n\nMaelin's hand twitched toward the mirror frame, ready to end the call, before Elira lifted two fingers from the blanket over her knees. Seated in the wooden chair Maelin had placed beside the board, she leaned forward into the mirror's light.\n\nElira: \"I was pronounced dead, restored, and examined by healers who filed every reading in public view. Calling my survival an affective variable is a way to turn a witness into furniture.\"\n\nKrail: \"Your pulse charm is visible from here.\"\n\nElira: \"Which proves I am monitored, not incapable. I am using six minutes of my ten-minute medical allowance to enter one correction: no one here needs rank-supervised custody to know what happened to them.\"\n\nFrom my post at the edge of the fountain, every muscle in my back pulled toward stepping between her and the mirrors. I kept both feet planted on the stone. Threshold meant holding still while she took her own ground.\n\nSenna denied immediate suspension for lack of causal evidence. Green copies remained provisional, public, and distributed; Rhea's campus authority remained limited to keeping the copy table physically safe.\n\nThe mirrors dimmed before anyone could turn refusal into ceremony.\n\nThe Fifth Tower had not moved during Krail's argument. It moved when Mira picked up her pencil and went back to work.\n\n---\n\nAfter the mirrors dimmed, the courtyard did not empty.\n\nStudents lingered in small groups under the evening lamps, unwilling to leave the stones where the day had almost hardened into a category and failed. The sky above Aurelis darkened to deep blue. The fountain water turned clear again, though a faint green glow lingered at the bottom if you looked from the right angle without demanding too much of it.\n\nElira sat on the low wall near the witness cottage with Maelin's blanket wrapped around her shoulders. Maelin had won that argument by refusing to have it.\n\nLeon stood at the public board, reading his own statement as if a stranger had written it and he was deciding whether the stranger was worth knowing. Cassian stood nearby, offering silence with unusual competence.\n\nMira slept face-down on folded arms at the copy table for seven minutes before Aira draped a cloak over her shoulders and propped a note beside her elbow warning everyone not to wake her unless the building caught fire or developed opinions.\n\nIven and the younger girl copied **NO DEBT FOR CARE** three more times. On the third sheet, she signed one letter at the bottom: **S.**\n\nI stood at the fountain rim and thought of thresholds. Beneath my uniform, the Black Circle rested with the uneasy patience of something powerful learning manners from people it could accidentally outlive.\n\nElira joined me after a while, her steps quiet against the stone.\n\nHaru: \"You should be sitting.\"\n\nElira: \"You should stop beginning sentences that way.\"\n\nShe stopped beside me. The dark water reflected us both badly, turning my hair into night smear and catching too much lamplight in her gold eyes. Between us, the basin held the west tower upside down.\n\nElira: \"Are you angry with me?\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nElira waited, fluent in the pause before my second answer.\n\nHaru: \"I was afraid enough that anger would have been easier.\"\n\nHer face softened, though not too much.\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"When you faced those mirrors at the hearing, I wanted to step between you and every one of them.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"I stayed put.\"\n\nElira: \"I know that too.\"\n\nHer hand rose to touch the dark crystal pendant at her throat.\n\nElira: \"I do not want love that hides me from every room where I might be hurt.\"\n\nHaru: \"And I do not want love that asks you to bleed so no one can call you protected.\"\n\nElira watched the lamp reflection move across the water.\n\nElira: \"Then we will keep arguing.\"\n\nDespite everything, I smiled.\n\nHaru: \"That is your solution?\"\n\nElira: \"It has kept us alive more than once.\"\n\nHaru: \"Technically inaccurate.\"\n\nElira: \"Do not become Halden because you are emotionally cornered.\"\n\nAbove us, the cracked west bell gave one faint metallic shift as night wind moved through the arch. Just old bronze remembering it had weight.\n\nHaru: \"The Fifth Tower did not ask for me first.\"\n\nElira: \"It asked for green hands.\"\n\nHaru: \"I was relieved.\"\n\nElira: \"Good.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then afraid because I was relieved.\"\n\nElira: \"Also fair.\"\n\nI looked up at the tower. The four visible windows below the bell arch sat dark and ordinary.\n\nHaru: \"What if the world only learns not to use me by using everyone else instead?\"\n\nElira did not answer quickly, which was one of the reasons I trusted her.\n\nElira: \"Then everyone else will need standing, witnesses, rest, refusal, bad copies, snacks, and people who do not let their care become ownership.\"\n\nHaru: \"That sounds difficult.\"\n\nElira: \"Worth it, though.\"\n\nHer voice stayed low, carrying easily across the quiet courtyard as she leaned her shoulder lightly against mine. By choice. In public.\n\nElira: \"And not lonely.\"\n\nAcross the courtyard, Leon unpinned the Varek draft, copied one phrase from it onto a scrap of rough paper, and pinned it beside his own statement:\n\n**HEART OUTRAN DISCIPLINE.**\n\nUnderneath, he wrote one word:\n\n**YES.**\n\nMira woke just long enough to see it, smiled into her sleeve, and went back to sleep. Aira caught me watching and raised her pencil in silent warning. I did not ask.\n\nThe west bell shifted again.\n\nThis time, the sound brought a dry click from inside the masonry. Stone remembering a latch.\n\nStudents turned toward the tower one by one. Nobody screamed. Nobody sprinted to fetch senior authority. Nobody turned to me and asked me to fix it.\n\nThe silence told me the day had changed something. Not enough to make anyone safe. Enough to threaten the people who preferred fewer hands.\n\nThe west tower stood pale against the night sky, four visible windows below the bell arch dark and blind. By morning, officials would argue over whether updating the public diagrams required committee approval. Aira would make someone regret a comma. Mira would preserve the first crooked sketch. Halden would pretend not to be thrilled. Rhea would refuse central authority again if she had to. Elira would speak whenever silence tried to dress up as care.\n\nAnd I would stand at the threshold.\n\nThe cracked west bell rang once, late and imperfect.\n\nHigh above the cracked west bell, in the blank stone where no diagram admitted a room existed, one unlit window turned green.","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-37-cover.webp","scenes":[]},{"id":"ep38","label":"Episode 32","number":"32","file":"episode-38-cover.webp","fallback":"The Fifth Witness","eyebrow":"Fifth Tower","recap":"","accent":["#0f172a","#bbf7d0","#c0c0c0"],"displayLabel":"Episode 32","displayNumber":"32","displayEyebrow":"The Fifth Tower","arcLabel":"The Fifth Tower","title":"The Fifth Witness","body":"The first mistake was assuming the window wanted me. That was reasonable, which made it dangerous.\n\nReasonable mistakes wore better clothes than cruel ones. They entered rooms with clean hands and public safety language. They brought diagrams. They used words like stabilizing, temporary, precautionary, and only until we understand. They looked at the thing no one understood, found the largest power standing near it, and decided that power had to be the handle.\n\nThe green window above the cracked west bell stayed lit through dawn. It did not brighten or dim. It simply remained.\n\nThat alone turned Aurelis into a building holding its breath.\n\nBy breakfast, the official four-tower diagrams had been pulled from two public boards and replaced with blank parchment headed **STRUCTURAL REVIEW IN PROGRESS**. By second bell, three student copies of the old map were circulating anyway, each with a fifth square drawn in a different wrong place. By third bell, someone chalked a green rectangle onto the courtyard stones below the west tower and wrote beside it:\n\n**IF IT HAS A WINDOW, IT HAS A ROOM.**\n\nNo one owned up to the chalk. Mira copied the words into her notebook two breaths before a cleaning charm wiped the stones bare.\n\nI found her at the fountain with both elbows pinning down a stack of rough sheets and a smear of ink along her cheek. Aira stood beside her, shielding a second stack from the morning breeze like she was guarding an injured animal made entirely of paperwork.\n\nLeon waited three paces behind them, far enough back to look uninvolved and close enough to step in if anyone decided to test the boundary.\n\nThe younger transfer girl who had trusted Iven with her name was sorting duplicates at the copy table. Over by the north planter, Iven Sahl sat hunched inside a borrowed academy sweater, while Sera perched close beside him with her empty soup cup cradled in both hands.\n\nThe fifth window glowed above us. Green.\n\nNeither signal fire nor domestic lamp, the steady light made the rest of the stone tower look strangely unsure of itself.\n\nElira's fingers brushed my sleeve.\n\nShe had insisted on coming down. Maelin had argued for seventeen minutes before trapping her in terms she could not outmaneuver: fifteen minutes standing, five minutes sitting, no stairs, and no heroic speeches before lunch. Elira had accepted the first three. The fourth remained under appeal.\n\nShe wore her pale blue cloak today, fastened with the small dark clasp that hid the revised pendant beneath the collar. From ten paces off she looked composed. Up close, I saw the cost in the pause before she turned her head, the deliberate set of her shoulders, and the half-breath delay before her smile reached anyone. People kept seeing alive and mistaking it for healed, because clean categories saved the world from having to apologize.\n\nMira looked up as we stopped beside the table.\n\nMira: \"I have six versions.\"\n\nAira: \"Seven, if you count the one where someone drew the Fifth Tower as a layered cake.\"\n\nMira: \"I am not counting that.\"\n\nAira: \"Coward.\"\n\nMira tapped her pencil toward the basin without lifting her eyes from the page.\n\nMira: \"The water keeps reflecting the tower wrong. Only when Green students stand near the rim.\"\n\nLeon: \"Define near.\"\n\nMira: \"I tried. The fountain objected.\"\n\nLeon's brows drew together.\n\nLeon: \"How does water object?\"\n\nAira held out a sheet covered in crossed-out numbers.\n\nAira: \"By making my measurements look like a lie.\"\n\nLeon read it, his mouth tightening.\n\nLeon: \"This says the same student stood two paces from the fountain, four paces from the fountain, and directly beneath the west bell without moving.\"\n\nAira: \"All three at once.\"\n\nHer pen hovered over the margin.\n\nLeon: \"That is not measurement.\"\n\nAira: \"Correct. That is embarrassment.\"\n\nMira scribbled another line onto her corner sheet.\n\nMira: \"Embarrassment confirmed by water.\"\n\nI looked into the basin. Clear surface. Ordinary ripples. Four official tower reflections shivered around the stone rim in the morning light.\n\nWhen Mira leaned closer, a fifth shape appeared beneath them, less a tower than a vertical green line seen through deep water rather than air.\n\nThe cold behind my sternum answered. Old metal. Ozone. Winter stone. Under my glove, Maelin's blue diagnostic cloth stayed cool against my bruised fingernail. No pull. Only recognition.\n\nI kept my boots still. Elira noticed at once.\n\nElira: \"It is not dragging at you.\"\n\nHaru: \"No.\"\n\nAira's eyes flicked from my face up to the green window.\n\nAira: \"That is the kindest thing ancient architecture has ever done for my schedule.\"\n\nI shook my head.\n\nHaru: \"It might be worse.\"\n\nMira's pencil halted.\n\nIn the basin, the fifth reflection bent away from me and toward her ink-stained page.\n\nSera spoke from the low wall. Her voice was quiet, yet the courtyard had learned not to talk over small voices.\n\nSera: \"It is humming.\"\n\nIven turned to look at her.\n\nIven: \"The window?\"\n\nSera shook her head.\n\nSera: \"The floor.\"\n\nEvery Green Circle student within twenty paces looked down. They did not move in unison, which would have looked rehearsed. They lowered their eyes one by one, a half-second apart, like rain finding the same drain. Mira's expression changed first.\n\nStill kneeling beside the fountain with a pencil caught between two fingers, she watched the Green Circle at her wrist wake. It did not flare with power or fear. It lit with direction.\n\nA thin green line surfaced beneath her shoe.\n\nA second appeared under Iven's boot, then a third beneath Sera's. Across the outer ring, another lit under Finn as he stood clutching armfuls of copied maps, and then half a dozen more.\n\nThe lines did not race toward the west tower. They walked, slow and patient beneath the paving stones, like an old hallway remembering it had once been allowed to exist.\n\nRhea's voice cut across the courtyard.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"No one follows the lines alone.\"\n\nA good order, issued half a breath too late. The fountain rang.\n\nThe note traveled low through the stone and climbed straight up the soles of my boots. Students gasped. A Blue Circle boy near the public board stumbled back. Someone dropped a breakfast tray, porcelain shattering loud enough that Elira flinched.\n\nI kept my hands at my sides. Her fingers found mine anyway. Choice first. Always.\n\nThe green lines brightened. Mira rose to her feet, and Leon stepped to her shoulder before anyone could tell him to.\n\nMira: \"I did not decide to walk.\"\n\nAira's pen flew across her pad.\n\nAira: \"Record: Mira Castellan reports no decision to walk.\"\n\nMira: \"I also did not decide not to.\"\n\nAira: \"Wonderful. Ancient ambiguity. My favorite breakfast illness.\"\n\nIven stood from the low wall, and Sera's hand shot out to catch his sleeve. He froze immediately. That mattered more than the light beneath his boots.\n\nIven: \"Do you want me to stay?\"\n\nSera looked down at the green path under her shoes, then at the empty cup in her hands.\n\nSera: \"I want to not go first.\"\n\nIven nodded once.\n\nIven: \"Then you do not go first.\"\n\nThe line under Sera's boots dimmed to a quiet pulse, waiting without tugging.\n\nMaelin had materialized beside Elira with the silent speed of an angry healer. She pointed down at the stone.\n\nMaelin: \"Consent-responsive architecture. Of course. Because an ordinary staircase was too much to ask.\"\n\nHalden arrived at a half-run and skidded to a halt only when Rhea raised one finger.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Do not seal it.\"\n\nRhea: \"I was about to give you the same order.\"\n\nHalden looked wounded for half a second before the green lines caught his eyes and stripped him of all dignity.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The routing is impossible. The west tower has no inner stair above the fourth service level. Its outer masonry cannot support a fifth chamber.\"\n\nAira: \"The wall has filed a formal disagreement.\"\n\nMira took a single step forward. No stone shifted, yet the entire courtyard tilted with her attention.\n\nThe green line lit ahead of her under the flagstones, curving toward the west arch. It bypassed the main tower doors and aimed straight for a narrow service entrance beside the bell wall, a storage door I had walked past six times that week without ever wondering what sat behind it.\n\nMira glanced back at Aira, then at Rhea, and at me last. She was not waiting for permission. She was checking whether I was about to become the mistake.\n\nMira: \"Should I stop?\"\n\nEvery adult within earshot drew breath to answer. Halden's scholarly lecture, Rhea's Student Council mandate, Maelin's triage rule, Seraphine's tactical caution from the edge of the crowd, and Valen's historical warning as she hurried up behind them with her coat half-buttoned and her cedar ward case tucked under one arm.\n\nLeon spoke before any of them.\n\nLeon: \"Do you want to?\"\n\nMira blinked. Beneath her boot, the green line pulsed once.\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nLeon: \"Then do not go alone.\"\n\nAira's pen paused for a beat, then scratched furiously. Rhea studied Leon, something close to approval sharpening her eyes.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Castellan. Sahl. Varek. You may approach the service threshold under student witness terms. No entry past the door without verbal confirmation and a visible exit line.\"\n\nHalden drew breath to protest.\n\nRhea: \"Professor Halden will remain behind the first line.\"\n\nHalden let the breath out in pure scholarly misery.\n\nElira squeezed my hand once.\n\nElira: \"And Haru?\"\n\nThe courtyard went dangerously quiet, waiting to see if the second mistake would step forward. Neither Rhea nor I answered.\n\nAbove us, the fifth window burned green.\n\nA student near the back whispered into the hush.\n\nStudent: \"Shouldn't the Black Circle go?\"\n\nThere was no malice in it, only ordinary fear reaching for the closest weapon.\n\nAn older, smoother voice answered from behind the student ring.\n\nCouncil Aide: \"That may be the safest arrangement.\"\n\nThe crowd parted beside the aide for Architect Sovan Reith, a tired-looking Council specialist in emergency structures.\n\nSovan: \"Magistra Voss sent me to observe the threshold. My brief calls this a classification event, though the wording is already out of date.\"\n\nAira: \"Promising survival instinct.\"\n\nRhea held him behind the first green line. Sovan accepted the boundary without argument and flipped open his portfolio.\n\nInside the service door, a lock clicked.\n\nMira turned back toward the stone frame. Iven stepped to her left, Leon to her right. The heavy door swung inward on silent hinges without anyone touching the latch.\n\nGreen-lit darkness waited beyond.\n\nMira: \"There is writing on the floor.\"\n\nAira: \"Read only. Do not step in until-\"\n\nThe paving stones under Mira's boots slid inward. Leon lunged and caught her shoulder; Iven grabbed Leon's sleeve. In one smooth pull, all three vanished past the doorway.\n\nThe door stayed wide open.\n\nThe courtyard did not scream. It had outgrown screaming.\n\nAira swore sharply enough to make three first-years blink, then sprinted for the arch. Rhea's red seal snapped across the threshold a beat before Aira reached it, leaving the doorway open while holding the crowd back.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Nobody crosses.\"\n\nI was already moving.\n\nElira's hand tightened on mine, neither dragging me back nor pretending the pull was not real. Beneath my jacket, the Black Circle pressed hard against my ribs. High above, the fifth window flared brighter. Sovan caught the change at once.\n\nSo did half the courtyard. The third mistake waited right there on the stones.\n\nIf I crossed that line now, every frightened rumor in Aurelis would harden into fact. Black Circle opens the tower. Black Circle brings them back. Black Circle keeps the door. A throne by another name, dressed up in rescue.\n\nElira spoke softly beside me.\n\nElira: \"Threshold.\"\n\nA reminder, not an order. I planted my boots one inch short of the green line. Every muscle in my back hated me for stopping.\n\nBeyond the open door, the darkness breathed out cold stone air. Mira's voice drifted back, muffled and thin.\n\nMira: \"We are in.\"\n\nAira dropped to her knees right against Rhea's red seal, notebook flat on the paving stones.\n\nAira: \"Define in.\"\n\nMira: \"Working on it.\"\n\nLeon: \"Badly.\"\n\nMira: \"Unhelpful.\"\n\nIven: \"The stairs are sideways.\"\n\nAira raised her eyes to the ceiling.\n\nAira: \"That is deeply informative and I hate it.\"\n\nAir returned to the courtyard, ragged but shared. I kept both feet behind the green line. Sovan watched me refuse the step, his expression shifting fractionally.\n\nSovan: \"You could reach them faster.\"\n\nHaru: \"I could.\"\n\nSovan: \"And you are choosing not to.\"\n\nI held his gaze without leaning toward the arch.\n\nHaru: \"Because faster is not the same as safer.\"\n\nHigh above the cracked bell, the green window dimmed by a single breath, caught somewhere between warning and consent.\n\n---\n\n## Mira Castellan\n\nMira had decided long ago that panic was a waste of ink.\n\nPanic had its uses if you needed to sprint, yell, spot smoke, or dodge falling masonry. It was simply terrible at spelling.\n\nSo when the service doorway swallowed her, Leon, and Iven into a stairwell that could not physically fit inside the west tower, Mira did not scream. She knelt on the cold stone, braced her pad on her thigh, and wrote:\n\n**ENTRY UNCHOSEN. NOT OPPOSED.**\n\nShe underlined the second phrase twice because her knuckles were shaking hard enough to turn the letters jagged. Jagged was fine. Jagged meant she had written it first.\n\nThe space around them refused to stay one room. Seven paces of level stone ended in a staircase bolted sideways up the masonry. Beneath that landing, a low window looked down on the courtyard from thirty feet up even though they had climbed nothing. A second window to her left peered up through the fountain basin from underneath, warping Aira's crouched silhouette like a furious fish.\n\nLeon planted himself between Mira and the open drop at the edge of the floor. Water-thin blue light ringed his wrist, tight and disciplined. Behind them, Iven pressed one palm flat against the rough stone wall.\n\nMira: \"Are you hurt?\"\n\nIven: \"No.\"\n\nLeon: \"Certain?\"\n\nIven shot him a sharp look.\n\nIven: \"Noctis drilled certain into us as a tone of voice before they ever cared if it was true.\"\n\nLeon took the hit without flinching, though color crept up his neck.\n\nLeon: \"Then tell me you are not certain.\"\n\nIven blinked, surprised, then let his shoulders drop an inch.\n\nIven: \"Not certain. Nothing feels broken.\"\n\nMira wrote that down word for word.\n\nUnder Iven's palm, the masonry warmed. Thin threads of green light seeped from his fingertips into the mortar, answering the tower rather than forcing it. Something inside the walls rustled like thick pages turning in an adjacent room.\n\nAira's voice echoed through the crooked air behind them.\n\nAira: \"Mira?\"\n\nMira glanced back. The service doorway hovered six paces behind them, yet also appeared below their boots and reflected in the side glass. Framed through it, she saw Aira kneeling at the red barrier, Haru holding steady behind the green line with Elira at his shoulder, and the Council architect with his portfolio watching Haru far too closely, like an engineer sizing up a storm he hoped to bottle.\n\nShe did not like the man. Probably unfair. She wrote anyway:\n\n**COUNCIL ARCHITECT WATCHING HARU TOO HARD. KEEP THIS LINE.**\n\nAira's voice sharpened down the stairwell.\n\nAira: \"I saw you write that. Good. Stay unfair on paper.\"\n\nLeon eyed the distant doorway.\n\nLeon: \"Can we walk back?\"\n\nIven lifted his hand from the wall. The stairwell behind them folded inward, cutting the seven-step landing down to five and narrowing the sideways stair into a rail-less bridge. Below the landing, the courtyard view blinked out and became an empty classroom where every wooden chair faced the blank wall.\n\nMira's throat dried out.\n\nIven: \"I know that room.\"\n\nLeon: \"From Noctis?\"\n\nIven took his time answering, and Mira kept her pencil still. People gave better testimony when nobody dragged the words out of them.\n\nIven: \"It is not a real room. It was an obedience primer image. They used to project old collapses to show what happened when lower ranks refused their assigned seats.\"\n\nThe glass flickered. One chair in the silent classroom scraped around to face them.\n\nA girl sat in it, half-faded at the edges like charcoal rubbed by a sleeve. Fifteen, maybe sixteen, with dark hair chopped blunt at her jaw and an old-style uniform whose cuffs had been patched twice over. A Green Circle glowed directly over her sternum, bleeding straight through the wool as if the memory had forgotten clothes were meant to hide skin.\n\nHer face blurred whenever Mira tried to hold it. Then Mira dropped her eyes to the girl's fingers. Ink stained every knuckle. Mira's breath caught.\n\nLeon: \"Castellan.\"\n\nMira: \"I see her.\"\n\nIven: \"She was in the primer.\"\n\nThe girl's mouth moved without sound. Words stamped themselves across the classroom plaster in crisp official lettering:\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED ATTENDANT.**\n\nHer lips moved again, silent and insistent. The plaster lettering flashed brighter, erasing the space around it.\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED ATTENDANT.**\n\nMira's grip cramped around her pencil.\n\nLeon: \"What do we do?\"\n\nIven's face had drained of color.\n\nIven: \"The primer said she abandoned her support post during a pre-Circle collapse. They claimed her interference broke the chain of command.\"\n\nMira: \"What was her name?\"\n\nIven swallowed hard.\n\nIven: \"They never gave her one.\"\n\nA low groan ran through the masonry. Stone flexed beneath their boots, imitating an old cave-in. Leon threw both palms down, and a sheet of blue water snapped taut along the peeling edge of the floor, pinning loose grit and fractured stone before gravity could claim them.\n\nLeon: \"Mira. Write.\"\n\nMira: \"I do not know what.\"\n\nLeon: \"Write that.\"\n\nMira braced the sheet against her knee and pressed graphite deep into the paper.\n\n**WE DO NOT KNOW HER NAME.**\n\nThe official script across the classroom wall stuttered.\n\nThe girl in the chair turned her head away from Mira and fixed straight onto Iven. Iven backed up a step, and Leon shifted with him without letting the water barrier slacken.\n\nIven: \"No.\"\n\nThe tower waited, heavy and listening. Iven curled his fingers into his palms.\n\nIven: \"I am not refusing you. I am saying no so the primer does not put its words in my mouth first.\"\n\nMira scribbled his exact phrasing down the margin. The green glow beneath their boots steadied.\n\nFaintly, through the warped doorway, courtyard voices drifted up. Haru saying something too low to catch, Elira answering, Aira cursing under her breath, Rhea snapping an order. Life kept moving outside.\n\nInside, the girl in the chair mouthed the same silent line again. Mira ignored the blurring eyes and impossible plaster; she watched the girl's jaw. She had transcribed enough frightened witnesses to catch a four-word cadence. Correction, not a plea.\n\nMira: \"One more time.\"\n\nThe girl's lips moved slowly. *I. Was. Fifth. Witness.*\n\nMira jammed the words onto the page before fear could polish them.\n\n**SHE SAYS: I WAS FIFTH WITNESS.**\n\nThe stairwell lurched.\n\nLeon swore and drove one heel against the cracking stone. His Blue Circle flared, the water barrier bowing inward as a hairline fracture raced toward his boot. Iven lunged for his sleeve.\n\nLeon: \"Not me! The wall, hold the wall!\"\n\nIven slapped both palms against the stone. Green light answered at once.\n\nFrom far down the twisted corridor, Sera's thin voice reached them.\n\nSera: \"Ask if she wants near.\"\n\nA laugh almost escaped Mira's throat, sharp with relief. Only Sera, who knew what it cost to be grabbed without warning, would think to ask a ghost about distance.\n\nMira: \"Do you want us near?\"\n\nThe memory girl's features washed out completely until only the official stamp remained across the plaster.\n\n**UNCLASSIFIED ATTENDANT.**\n\nThen a crack split the first letter down the middle.\n\nThe girl raised one ink-stained hand and laid it flat against the inside of the classroom glass.\n\nMira wedged her notebook onto one bent knee and pressed her free palm against the cold stone beside the window frame, close enough to answer without reaching through. Iven mirrored her on the other side, while Leon stayed locked in place, teeth gritted as he held the floor together with both hands. Mira wrote left-handed and crooked across the page:\n\n**NEAR REQUESTED. NO TOUCH ATTEMPTED. LEON HOLDING FLOOR. IVEN AND MIRA AT WALL.**\n\nThe classroom dissolved around the girl. Chairs screeched sideways, tipping over or vanishing until only hers remained, and behind it rose a sunken council chamber with four high-backed stone seats and one bare standing square down by the flagstones.\n\nThe girl stood in the square while faceless adults argued above her. Their robes kept only their colors: blue, red, yellow, and a pale silver that set Mira's teeth on edge.\n\nShe tried to speak. The chamber walls wrote straight over her head.\n\n**SUPPORT PRESENCE.**\n\nShe opened her mouth again.\n\n**ATTENDANT.**\n\nA third time.\n\n**GREEN WITNESS UNNECESSARY TO FINAL RECORD.**\n\nMira bore down so hard the pencil point punched straight through the paper.\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nThe word rang out flat and clear. Beside her, Iven's voice trembled with sudden heat.\n\nIven: \"Noctis uses that exact diagram.\"\n\nLeon: \"Uses what?\"\n\nIven: \"That shape. They drilled into us that support hands carry, warm, fetch, and copy, and never get to define what happened.\"\n\nHis Green Circle burned brighter, sharper rather than larger.\n\nIven: \"They built that rule straight into our bones.\"\n\nMira stared at the bare standing square beneath the four high seats, where a fifth chair had never been built. She flipped to a fresh corner and wrote:\n\n**OLD RECORD ERROR: GREEN WITNESS FILED AS SUPPORT.**\n\nThe stone groaning stopped. Sweat ran down Leon's temple into his collar as he kept the water sheet locked.\n\nLeon: \"Fixed?\"\n\nA fresh crack split another inch toward his heel.\n\nLeon: \"Not fixed. Understood.\"\n\nMira: \"Can you hold it?\"\n\nOld Leon would have turned the question into swagger. This Leon looked at the spreading crack, at Iven's bleeding knuckles on the wall, at Mira's torn sheet, and gave her the honest answer.\n\nLeon: \"Not long.\"\n\nMira: \"Thank you.\"\n\nHe blinked once and nodded.\n\nThe chamber dissolved into a hallway choked with gray smoke. No open flame remained, only the aftermath. Official colors smoldered across locked doors while thin green light traced the baseboards, showing where people had crawled to breathe.\n\nThe girl came through the smoke with a small child wedged under one arm and a wooden record box pinned under the other. Tears cut tracks through the soot on her cheeks, ugly and terrified and stubborn in the plain way people got when nobody else was left to be brave.\n\nA man in red robes reached out to take the box. She shook her head and pulled it back.\n\nBlack letters burned into the smoke above them.\n\n**REFUSED TRANSFER TO AUTHORITY.**\n\nMira wrote straight under the beam of light:\n\n**KEPT THE RECORD OUT OF THE WRONG HANDS.**\n\nThe tower let out a sound like a lung finally emptying.\n\nAcross the glass, the girl lifted her chin. For the first time, her face held steady: dark eyes, ink smeared across her chin, and a thin white scar cutting through one eyebrow.\n\nMira did not know her, yet her chest ached like she had lost someone.\n\nIven breathed out in shock.\n\nIven: \"I know that scar.\"\n\nMira: \"Where?\"\n\nIven pressed his fingers harder into the stone.\n\nIven: \"In the obedience hall at Noctis. One of the carved wall warnings. They labeled her First Disobedience.\"\n\nLeon: \"Noctis has her?\"\n\nIven started to shake his head, then caught himself.\n\nIven: \"A copy. A warning. Or maybe a route. I do not know.\"\n\nMira wrote every word.\n\nInside the smoke, the girl pressed four fingers to her chest, then pointed straight down at the record box under her arm.\n\nMira: \"Name?\"\n\nThe green lights along the stairwell guttered. Somewhere outside the walls, courtyard voices surged in sudden argument.\n\n---\n\n## Haru - The Reasonable Mistake\n\nWhile the courtyard waited on the dark stair, Sovan Reith had made himself useful. He cleared frightened aides behind the outer ribbon and shut down a noble delegation trying to force open a private relay. When voices rose near the threshold, though, he reached for the reasonable mistake.\n\nSovan: \"A temporary keeper designation would block worse titles from sticking.\"\n\nElira, seated on the bench Maelin had dragged to the edge of the line, looked up at him.\n\nElira: \"You want to stop a myth by giving it an office badge.\"\n\nSovan: \"I want someone answerable if that door opens wider.\"\n\nHaru: \"Then name the answerable group already standing here.\"\n\nI gestured across the courtyard: Rhea's restricted campus seal, Aira kneeling at the threshold, Maelin's triage line, Valen and Halden over the old maps, and the Green aides passing Mira's notes from hand to hand. Inside Valen's open ward case, the Regalia shard swung once toward the tower and went dark.\n\nSovan read the refusal clearly enough to step back.\n\nSovan: \"Then the three inside answer before I draft a frame.\"\n\nAira: \"Write down the question. Leave room for their answer.\"\n\nSovan uncapped his pen and wrote it across the top of a blank sheet.\n\nThe argument died before it could swell into another hearing. And inside the tower, Mira found a name.\n\n## Mira Castellan - A Name Is Not Enough\n\nE. Vell.\n\nAn initial was never enough, and Mira knew it the second the letters formed. Institutions loved initials because initials turned people into spare parts: E. Vell could patch channels, haul boxes, sit in margins, and end up on a bronze plaque nobody read unless a professor needed a footnote.\n\nThe girl in the smoke had a mouth, ink-stained knuckles, a scar through her brow, a Green Circle over her heart, a child on one hip, and a record box under her arm. She deserved a full name.\n\nMira turned to Iven, whose forearm was corded with green veins of light climbing from the wall.\n\nMira: \"Do you know her first name?\"\n\nIven stared at the wall, jaw tight.\n\nIven: \"The obedience hall never gave warnings first names.\"\n\nLeon grunted as his water barrier bowed another inch.\n\nLeon: \"Can the tower cough it up?\"\n\nMira faced the glass.\n\nMira: \"Can you?\"\n\nThe smoke corridor shuddered. Official script scrambled to form first across the wall.\n\n**E. VELL. SUPPORT-**\n\nThe last word shattered before it could finish. Mira wrote fast:\n\n**OLD LABEL BROKE. PERSON STILL PRESENT.**\n\nInside the memory, the girl tapped one ink-stained finger against the wooden box. Three taps. Pause. Two taps. Pause. One.\n\nIven's eyes snapped wide.\n\nIven: \"Archive index.\"\n\nMira: \"What?\"\n\nIven: \"Noctis made us drill old inter-school index codes for doctrine pulls. Three-two-one is the personal testimony appendix.\"\n\nLeon: \"Why would Noctis teach you that?\"\n\nIven gave him a look close to pity.\n\nIven: \"So we could haul testimony up before we learned how to question it.\"\n\nMira wrote that down too, purely so Aira could stab someone with it later. Then she looked back at the girl.\n\nMira: \"Where is the appendix?\"\n\nThe stairwell folded around them into a narrow archive aisle. Tall shelves slammed down on both sides, crammed with boxes tagged only by rank color. Green boxes lined the bottom shelf where boots could kick them and eyes could skip right past.\n\nOne box rattled violently on the lowest plank.\n\nLeon: \"If you tell me we have to fetch that, I am resigning from architecture.\"\n\nMira: \"You do not work in architecture.\"\n\nLeon: \"Good. Clean break.\"\n\nThe box slid off the shelf and hit the floor without a sound, lid flying open. Inside lay a single half-charred, water-stained page covered in cramped handwriting fighting to fit truth into an inventory form. Mira dropped to her knees beside it.\n\nLeon: \"Mira. Floor.\"\n\nMira: \"I see it.\"\n\nShe kept her fingers off the page and read. Most of the ink swam into blur, except one intact line at the center.\n\n**My name is Eris Vell, and I was not present as furniture.**\n\nMira's throat closed tight. For one useless second she could only stare. Then Leon let out a sharp breath of pain as the fracture chewed under his boot heel, and useless ended. Mira wrote:\n\n**NAME RECOVERED: ERIS VELL. GREEN CIRCLE. FIFTH WITNESS. NOT FURNITURE.**\n\nThe tower did not answer with calm. It triggered an alarm.\n\nBlack script tore straight down the archive shelves faster than Mira could track it.\n\n**UNAUTHORIZED PERSONAL RESTORATION**\n\n**NOCTIS JURISDICTION LINK ACTIVE**\n\nIven jerked backward so hard his hands tore off the wall. Every green line along the floor snapped dark. The flagstones dropped an inch.\n\nLeon threw one shoulder against the heavy shelving and hooked his free arm around Mira's coat to keep her upright. Boxes burst open overhead, dumping clouds of remembered pages that wiped themselves blank before hitting the floor.\n\nAt Mira's feet, the box holding Eris Vell's page began dragging backward toward the bottom shelf. Mira lunged for it, but the wood thinned like wet smoke under the black pull; her fingers skidded off the shimmering rim and hit bare cold stone while the box kept sliding.\n\nMira: \"I cannot catch it!\"\n\nIven stared in horror at the black script burning through the shelves.\n\nIven: \"It is running retrieval doctrine.\"\n\nLeon: \"Useful translation?\"\n\nIven flinched at the snap in his voice, and Leon corrected himself mid-breath.\n\nLeon: \"Sorry. What do we do?\"\n\nThe apology cost a heartbeat, and brought Iven back into his own skin. He looked at the tearing pages and the black script trying to drag a dead girl's name back into institutional custody.\n\nIven: \"Retrieval needs an authorized carrier.\"\n\nMira: \"Who?\"\n\nThe tower answered by branding black script straight across the chest of Iven's borrowed sweater.\n\n**NOCTIS CONDUIT-TRACK PROPERTY: IVEN SAHL**\n\nAll expression wiped off Iven's face. Black lines coiled tight around his ribs and yanked him toward the bottom shelf. Leon let go of Mira and caught Iven around the waist with both arms. Blue water flared around Leon's boots, fighting for traction as the tether dragged them both across the stones.\n\nLeon: \"You are not carrying anything for them!\"\n\nIven's jaw locked. Noctis had carved those verbs too deep into him: carry, retrieve, return, turn a live boy into a quiet cart for approved truth.\n\nMira did not argue with the magic. She slapped her notebook page against Iven's upper arm and wrote hard straight over the black lettering.\n\n**IVEN SAHL: PRESENT WITNESS. NOT CARRIER.**\n\nThe pencil lead punched through the damp sheet and snagged the wool of his sleeve. The black words stuttered. Iven dragged in one ragged breath.\n\nIven: \"Not carrier.\"\n\nThe tether slackened, and the shelf yanked Eris's box another inch closer to the dark.\n\nMira: \"Then who carries it?\"\n\nLeon looked down at his own white knuckles gripping Iven's coat.\n\nLeon: \"Nobody.\"\n\nMira caught it immediately. Nobody could carry it whole.\n\nShe ripped her fresh copy into three ragged strips. On the first she scrawled **ERIS**. On the second, **VELL**. On the third, **FIFTH WITNESS**. She shoved one strip into Iven's fist, slapped the second against Leon's wrist, and kept the third.\n\nMira: \"The name leaves in pieces.\"\n\nLeon: \"That sounds deeply unsafe.\"\n\nMira: \"For Noctis.\"\n\nThat settled him.\n\nThe archive aisle tipped sideways. Shelves buckled into steep walls and the floor melted into a slick chute coated in wet black ink. At the far bottom, a circular Noctis seal yawned wide like a storm drain, swallowing every blank page that slid toward it.\n\nEris's box shot down the slope. Mira threw herself after it.\n\nLeon swore and lashed water beneath her soles, a thin moving skin skating over the black ink so she stayed upright. Behind them, Iven slapped both palms to the tilting wall, hunting desperately for the buried maintenance beat beneath the Noctis pull.\n\nMira skidded past dozens of bottom-shelf boxes labeled only by color. Green. Green. Green. Hundreds of lives flattened into storage coordinates.\n\nThe drain widened. Freezing air belched up from the hole carrying the clang of Noctis bells, barked orders, and children answering before questions finished. Iven heard home. The green glow died under his hands as the floor pitched steeper.\n\nLeon lost his footing and slammed down on one knee. His water spilled into the drain and surged back up laced with white restraint marks that whipped straight up his forearms.\n\nLeon: \"Iven!\"\n\nA name thrown like a rope to someone slipping under. Iven squeezed his eyes shut as the obedience hall echoed inside his skull. *Stand straight. Hands visible. Return property.*\n\nHe opened his eyes and looked down at the scrap of paper crushed in his fist.\n\n**ERIS.**\n\nA name Noctis had only ever spoken as a threat.\n\nIven bit the inside of his cheek until the sharp copper taste anchored him in the room.\n\nIven: \"The primer skipped every seventh maintenance beat!\"\n\nSliding fast toward the black drain, Mira yelled back.\n\nMira: \"Why?\"\n\nIven: \"So we would never hear the real route!\"\n\nHe slammed his palm against the stone six times. Waited one beat. Struck a seventh.\n\nGreen light blew sideways out of the mortar, shearing straight across the ink. A solid service ledge shot out beneath Mira's boots. She hit it hard, rolled once, and felt the green foundation light catch the remembered box just solidly enough for her to trap it between both forearms without touching the fragile page inside. Leon diverted his water into the ledge, washing the white restraint marks off his arms and flushing them down the drain with a furious hiss.\n\nAbove them, the heavy shelves lurched inward to crush the gap.\n\nMira: \"Leon!\"\n\nLeon jammed his back against one wall and drove both boots into the opposite shelving. Blue water flooded the space around him and froze dense under pressure, bracing the aisle open.\n\nLeon: \"I am starting to hate historical research!\"\n\nMira: \"Hold!\"\n\nLeon: \"I thought that was implied!\"\n\nThe green ledge shuddered and slid sideways through the tower. Distant courtyard voices bled through the stone: Aira calling Mira's name, Sera repeating Iven's, Rhea shouting to hold the line without naming a keeper. Haru's voice never came at all, and for once the silence felt like room he was holding open while they worked.\n\nBeneath the ledge, the Noctis drain followed them like a shadow. Black hooks erupted from the ink, snapping straight toward Eris's box.\n\nMira had no combat magic to cut them. Leon dared not drop a hand from the collapsing walls. Iven held nothing except a torn strip of paper and a service beat Noctis had tried to erase.\n\nSo Iven stepped straight between the black hooks and Eris Vell.\n\nThe first hook ripped through his borrowed sweater. The second sliced across his cheekbone. The third struck his Green Circle and locked dead.\n\nNoctis doctrine expected Green to fold under an authorized pull. Instead, Iven's Circle simply refused the call.\n\nIven: \"Not carrier.\"\n\nThe hook wrenched harder, drawing blood at his wrist.\n\nIven: \"Witness.\"\n\nMira slapped her paper strip onto the lid of the box.\n\n**FIFTH WITNESS.**\n\nLeon ripped one hand free from the wall long enough to slap his down beside it.\n\n**VELL.**\n\nThree scraps. Three hands. Zero authorized carriers.\n\nThe black hooks shuddered, unable to decide which third of the name counted as institutional property, and split their pull three ways. That broke their grip.\n\nIven tore loose. Mira shoved the box clear. Leon dropped his brace and blasted the entire ledge straight upward on a violent surge of blue water.\n\nThey shot up through buckling shelves and a blizzard of blank pages as the Noctis seal snapped shut below. For one jagged heartbeat before the dark closed, Mira glimpsed what waited on the other side: newer stone than Aurelis, black uniforms moving past, and a carved wall figure bearing Eris's brow scar above an obedience slogan. Then the route slammed shut.\n\nAll three crashed onto the original level landing in a tangle of elbows and wet wool. Leon hit on his shoulder and let out a single shocked bark of laughter. Iven pressed the back of his sleeve to his bleeding cheek. Mira lunged for the box.\n\nThe half-burned page lay intact inside. She laid the three torn strips beside it, and the ragged edges knit together without erasing the tears.\n\n**ERIS VELL. FIFTH WITNESS.**\n\nThe tower let out every breath it had been holding.\n\nEvery window in the impossible stairwell threw itself open to memory. First the courtyard flared into view across the panes: Aira at the threshold, Rhea's red barrier, Elira standing pale and furious beside the bench, Haru still anchored behind the green line, Sovan clutching his portfolio too tight, and Halden staring up like a man watching a footnote turn into an indictment.\n\nThen the glass peeled back further into older courtyards and forgotten uniforms. A stone fountain split in half. Green aides hauling water buckets, Blue circles shoring up timber beams, Red seals bracing doors, Yellow fire burning dangerously high.\n\nAnd Eris Vell darting through the smoke between all of them, paper crushed under one arm and a small child clinging to her hip, shouting silently until the tower finally gave her back her voice.\n\nEris: \"Do not name one person the answer!\"\n\nThe words struck the stone from the inside out.\n\nLeon dropped onto one knee as his water barrier gave out. The floor held anyway. Thin green lines wove together beneath their boots to take the load: Mira's, Iven's, Sera's pale thread reaching in from outside, Finn's near the fountain, and a dozen other Green students who had stayed behind the line and refused to let the path go dark.\n\nLeon stared down at the glowing floor.\n\nLeon: \"Oh.\"\n\nMira: \"Good oh or bad oh?\"\n\nLeon swallowed hard.\n\nLeon: \"Green oh.\"\n\nIven let out a crooked laugh that hurt his cut cheek and counted anyway.\n\nInside the glass, Eris Vell turned toward them. Clear at last: young, terrified, furious, bone-tired. A person, never a statue or a lesson. Mira raised her pencil.\n\nMira: \"Do you want anything else in the record?\"\n\nEris looked at the torn page in Mira's hands, then across the open glass toward Haru standing steady outside the line. For one cold second, Mira feared the tower would name him anyway.\n\nInstead, Eris gave a small, crooked smile.\n\nEris: \"No center named.\"\n\nMira wrote it down.\n\nThe tower settled beneath them like an exhausted runner finally allowed to sit. An ordinary stone staircase unfolded at their feet, plain enough to feel almost insulting.\n\nLeon stared down the steps.\n\nLeon: \"Now it gives us stairs.\"\n\nIven pulled his hand off the wall and his knees nearly buckled. Leon caught him under the arm without a word or flourish. Mira hugged her stack of pages to her ribs and looked back one last time as Eris Vell thinned from figure into quiet light, entering the record rather than vanishing.\n\nMira: \"We are going to write it badly first.\"\n\nEris's smile warmed the glass.\n\nEris: \"Good.\"\n\nThen the stairs carried them down.\n\n---\n\n## Haru - What Came Back\n\nThey stumbled out through the service door covered in stone dust, fading green light, and the raw quiet people carry back from rooms where something true has waited longer than anyone alive.\n\nLeon came out first, eyeing the doorframe like he expected it to bite. Iven leaned heavily against his shoulder, too spent to argue. Mira came last with pages clutched under both arms, a pencil jammed behind one ear, and soot smeared across her cheek like clerk's war paint.\n\nAira reached her first. She did not grab at the paper; she held out both empty palms.\n\nAira: \"Pages? You? Both?\"\n\nMira eyed her steadily.\n\nMira: \"Both.\"\n\nAira took half the stack in one hand and hooked Mira's sleeve with the other.\n\nAira: \"Greedy. Practical. Approved.\"\n\nBlack ink boiled out across the stone threshold behind them.\n\nIt slid over the sill in a cold sheet, carrying the Noctis retrieval seal Mira had outrun below. Loose corners of Aira's pages whipped toward the door, and the top sheet tore straight from her grip.\n\nHaru: \"Do not touch the seal!\"\n\nSera was already moving. She stepped straight between the black ink and Iven, holding her soup cup forward in both hands where the spell could read the crooked tape label Mira had stuck there days ago.\n\n**SERA'S CUP. ASK SERA.**\n\nThe black sheet struck the cup and stalled dead.\n\nIts grammar knew how to seize institutional archives, haul back Noctis property, and chase a stolen index down a corridor. It had zero jurisdiction over an ordinary tin cup whose owner had written her own rules on tape.\n\nSera: \"No.\"\n\nThe black ink parted around her boots. Iven stepped up beside her, blood still drying along his cut cheek.\n\nIven: \"Not carrier.\"\n\nMira dropped to her knees and slapped the three torn paper strips down onto the flagstones.\n\n**ERIS.**\n\n**VELL.**\n\n**FIFTH WITNESS.**\n\nThe retrieval seal lunged for all three at once and split its force again.\n\nLeon, dropped to one knee near the arch and gasping for air, whipped one final thread of water across the stones. It hooked the divided ink currents and snapped them straight inside Rhea's red barrier.\n\nRhea closed her seal like a fist.\n\nBlack ink slammed against the inner walls. A jagged crack split Rhea's temporary mandate mark from edge to center.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Mandate limit. My seal holds immediate hazard for twelve breaths, not historical custody.\"\n\nAira was already counting aloud.\n\nProfessor Valen flipped open her cedar ward case. The Regalia shard swung toward the trapped ink, hesitated, then turned dead away, refusing the fight.\n\nSovan: \"Can the Black Circle contain it without claiming the pages?\"\n\nEvery head in the courtyard snapped toward me. Eight breaths left. The ink hammered Rhea's cracking seal. I could crush it in one motion. And in that same motion, I would teach everyone watching that when distributed authority ran out of breath, the real power still belonged to me.\n\nHaru: \"No. Break the route, not the ink.\"\n\nHalden's eyes jumped to the open service arch.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"The threshold is still translating Noctis retrieval into tower access!\"\n\nMira caught it first. She snatched the top sheet back from Aira, ripped off the clean margin carrying the old archive index number, and flung that strip straight back through the dark doorway.\n\nThe ink whipped around, ignoring Eris's name to chase the obsolete index route that had taught it how to hunt. It poured back through the doorway after the scrap.\n\nLeon severed his water line. Rhea dropped her seal. Aira hit twelve.\n\nThe heavy door slammed shut on black ink and empty authority grammar, leaving Mira's pages and Sera's cup safely outside.\n\nRhea stared down at the hairline crack running through her mandate mark.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"My seal is reduced until Council review.\"\n\nSovan looked from her broken authority to me.\n\nSovan: \"You could have kept it whole.\"\n\nHaru: \"And taught everyone here what happens the second ordinary hands get tired.\"\n\nSovan wrote that down without polishing a single word.\n\nWhen Rhea reached out to reopen the outer evacuation lane, her cracked seal sputtered and failed. For half a heartbeat, every official turned back toward me.\n\nMira held up the torn edge of her archive sheet instead.\n\nMira: \"The courtyard stones still answer Green maintenance marks.\"\n\nFinn and three Green aides dropped to one knee and pressed their wrists to the flagstones. Pale green lanes opened smoothly around the gathered students, wide enough for stretchers and measured enough to prevent a stampede. Rhea stepped back and let the temporary paths take over from her damaged seal.\n\nSovan added another line to his notebook.\n\n**WHEN OFFICE SEAL FAILED, MAINTENANCE ACCESS HELD THE LANE.**\n\nNobody asked me to replace what had broken.\n\nSera stepped back half a pace to give Iven room as the green lanes cleared the stone around them, her soup cup still tight in her hands.\n\nSera: \"Near?\"\n\nIven's face softened, opening carefully around a word that used to mean danger.\n\nIven: \"Near.\"\n\nShe stepped back up beside him, shoulder close without touching.\n\nMaelin marched straight through the outer lane with her healer's kit open.\n\nMaelin: \"Everyone who walked into an impossible tower sits down right now.\"\n\nLeon: \"I can stand.\"\n\nMaelin: \"I heard floor stone cracking through two walls and a legal dispute. Sit down before I file you as furniture and stack you myself.\"\n\nLeon sat instantly. Aira looked genuinely delighted despite the dust in her hair.\n\nAira: \"That threat had historical accuracy.\"\n\nMira held out a crinkled sheet to Rhea.\n\nMira: \"First draft. It is ugly.\"\n\nRhea took it with both hands.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Then it is evidence.\"\n\nHalden stood rigid beside the fountain. Mira turned straight to him, and the whole courtyard went quiet to watch.\n\nMira: \"Her name was Eris Vell.\"\n\nHalden's jaw tightened.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I will correct the archive dedication.\"\n\nMira: \"No.\"\n\nThe quiet word hit harder than a shout. Halden froze.\n\nMira: \"You will help us correct it.\"\n\nPride flared briefly behind his eyes, then folded under something better.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"Together, then.\"\n\nHalden rubbed a smudge of chalk off his wrist, pretending he had not been moved, while Mira gave him one crisp nod before turning on Sovan.\n\nMira: \"And no keeper.\"\n\nSovan's portfolio dropped an inch.\n\nMira: \"We got that straight from the tower. No center named.\"\n\nAira waved her smeared notebook.\n\nAira: \"Confirmed. Transcribed while Leon was losing a fistfight with architecture. Maximum credibility.\"\n\nLeon spoke from the flagstones.\n\nLeon: \"I did not lose.\"\n\nIven: \"You negotiated.\"\n\nLeon weighed that for a second.\n\nLeon: \"Acceptable.\"\n\nLaughter moved through the courtyard in uneven pockets as tension finally began to break. Sovan looked from Mira to me, then straight back to Mira. That choice mattered.\n\nSovan: \"If no keeper is named, what public safety frame are you proposing?\"\n\nMira blinked, caught off guard by being asked, then answered before doubt could catch up.\n\nMira: \"A threshold witness group. Green-led because it called Green first. Student Council boundaries, healer consent review, faculty archive backing, and external observers allowed to watch without naming anyone. Haru stays outside the line unless the group asks for him and can state why.\"\n\nAira stared at her.\n\nAira: \"That was terrifyingly complete.\"\n\nMira: \"I have been angry since breakfast.\"\n\nThe faintest curve touched Rhea's mouth.\n\nPresident Valmont: \"Provisional student proposal entered.\"\n\nSovan flipped his portfolio open, then stopped with his pen poised.\n\nSovan: \"May I copy that wording?\"\n\nOld reflex rippled straight across the courtyard. Mira glanced at Aira, Aira looked to Rhea, Rhea turned toward Elira, and Elira's eyes flicked briefly to me, not asking permission, only watching the old ladder try to hand me the gavel one more time.\n\nHaru: \"Ask Mira.\"\n\nSovan turned back to her.\n\nSovan: \"Castellan?\"\n\nMira looked down at her ink-stained knuckles.\n\nMira: \"Copy it ugly first.\"\n\nSovan blinked, while Aira smiled like a knife learning joy.\n\nAira: \"Oh, I like him bewildered.\"\n\nSovan took one of Mira's smudged duplicate sheets instead of pulling a clean Council form. Not trust yet, but a real concession.\n\nAbove the bell arch, the fifth window still burned green, steadier now, less like an alarm and more like a lantern left lit on a landing.\n\nElira leaned close against my shoulder.\n\nElira: \"You are shaking.\"\n\nI looked down at my hands. She was right.\n\nHaru: \"I never crossed the line.\"\n\nElira: \"I know.\"\n\nHaru: \"I wanted to.\"\n\nElira: \"I know that too.\"\n\nShe did not offer praise or absolution, only witness, and it hurt enough to keep me honest.\n\nAcross the courtyard, students were already copying the name. Not cleanly. Eris Vell turned into Eris Vel on one sheet and E. Vell on another before Mira caught both with frightening speed. Finn wrote Fifth Witness too big and had to flip his paper over. Sera asked Aira how to spell furniture, then scratched the word out so hard she tore the corner.\n\nBeside the fountain, Halden stood next to Professor Valen, staring up at the west tower as if an entire wing of his memory had been pried open against his will. Valen spoke softly enough that only those nearby caught it.\n\nProfessor Valen: \"History keeps surviving as maintenance because maintenance is where institutions hide the people they refuse to admit they needed.\"\n\nHalden's jaw worked once.\n\nProfessor Halden: \"I taught that dedication wrong.\"\n\nValen: \"Then teach the correction loudly.\"\n\nSovan copied Mira's proposal by hand onto the smudged duplicate. No seal. No header. Ugly first. Maybe he understood why, or maybe he simply recognized the courtyard would reject anything cleaner. Either way, he listened.\n\nRhea pinned the first public notice onto the board herself.\n\n**FIFTH TOWER THRESHOLD UNDER DISTRIBUTED STUDENT-COUNCIL WITNESS.**\n\n**NO KEEPER NAMED.**\n\n**GREEN CIRCLE WITNESS ERIS VELL RECOVERED AS HISTORICAL PERSON, NOT INFRASTRUCTURE.**\n\nWhen people read the last word, a dozen eyes flicked toward me, then moved away without fear. Recognition instead of worship. Better, even if it would never make us safe.\n\nHigh above the cracked bell, the fifth window clicked once.\n\nA shadow fell across the courtyard paving stones beneath the green glow, sharper than morning light should allow. Letters bled straight into the dark stone at our boots. Mira spotted them first, going motionless beside her table. Aira followed her eyes and sucked in a sharp breath.\n\nI looked down.\n\nThe tower's shadow had spelled out eight words across the stone.\n\n**ERIS VELL WAS NOT LOST IN AURELIS.**\n\nEvery sound in the courtyard died around the sentence.\n\nThen the green light in the high window trembled once, and a second line burned into the shadow beneath our feet.\n\n**SHE WAS TAKEN THROUGH NOCTIS.**","cover":"assets/episodes/episode-38-cover.webp","scenes":[]}],"cast":[{"id":"haru","name":"Haru Aerlen","circle":"Black Circle / former Green disguise","role":"Publicly contested protector","image":"assets/cast/haru.webp","color":"#111111","accent":"#36d17d","summary":"A quiet Black Circle magician whose public identity turns every act of restraint into political evidence."},{"id":"elira","name":"Elira Vale","circle":"Yellow Circle","role":"Partner and co-witness","image":"assets/cast/elira.webp","color":"#ffd166","accent":"#111111","summary":"A composed Yellow Circle prodigy, survivor, and Haru's equal partner in both danger and testimony."},{"id":"aira","name":"Aira Solen","circle":"Blue Circle","role":"Support tactician and archivist","image":"assets/cast/aira.webp","color":"#3a86ff","accent":"#ffffff","summary":"A sharp-eyed Blue Circle tactician who turns panic, records, and overlooked details into usable plans."},{"id":"leon","name":"Leon Varek","circle":"Blue Circle","role":"Rival turned field ally","image":"assets/cast/leon.webp","color":"#2f80ed","accent":"#dff7ff","summary":"A proud Blue Circle duelist learning to replace inherited rank with earned discipline and trust."},{"id":"cassian","name":"Cassian Veyr","circle":"Red Circle","role":"Breaker and pressure fighter","image":"assets/cast/cassian.webp","color":"#ef476f","accent":"#ffd166","summary":"A confident Red Circle heir whose combat instinct and irreverence keep the group moving under pressure."},{"id":"halden","name":"Professor Halden","circle":"Advanced mana research","role":"Researcher under scrutiny","image":"assets/cast/halden.webp","color":"#6c757d","accent":"#bde0fe","summary":"A severe mana researcher whose expertise, omissions, and divided loyalties keep him under scrutiny."},{"id":"cael","name":"Cael Nox","circle":"Noctis conduit","role":"Fallen witness","image":"assets/cast/cael.webp","color":"#3d4050","accent":"#c8d0e8","summary":"A younger Noctis conduit whose courage and death remain evidence of what the institution consumed."},{"id":"rhea","name":"Rhea Valmont","circle":"Red Circle","role":"Student Council President","image":"assets/cast/rhea.webp","color":"#9e1b32","accent":"#ffd166","summary":"A disciplined Red Circle council leader trying to make authority transparent, shared, and answerable."},{"id":"lucien","name":"Lucien Voss","circle":"Yellow Circle","role":"Noctis antagonist","image":"assets/cast/lucien.webp","color":"#f6c85f","accent":"#101010","summary":"A polished Noctis prodigy whose doctrine of control survives every defeat and poisons every institution it touches."},{"id":"nera","name":"Nera Vail","circle":"Blue Circle","role":"Noctis binder","image":"assets/cast/nera.webp","color":"#344b72","accent":"#bde0fe","summary":"A precise Blue Circle lieutenant who looks steady on the surface and reads the room well."},{"id":"soren","name":"Soren Ashvale","circle":"Red Circle","role":"Honorable rival","image":"assets/cast/soren.webp","color":"#a23b2a","accent":"#ffd166","summary":"A Red Circle duelist with a disciplined style and a clear sense of honor."},{"id":"mira","name":"Mira Castellan","circle":"Green Circle","role":"Eris survivor and organizer","image":"assets/cast/mira.webp","color":"#2f7d4f","accent":"#c7f9cc","summary":"A Green Circle student and recovered Eris whose precise records help ordinary students organize and be believed."},{"id":"valen","name":"Professor Iris Valen","circle":"Yellow Circle","role":"History mentor","image":"assets/cast/valen.webp","color":"#c99700","accent":"#fff2b8","summary":"A warm Magical History professor who protects students while keeping dangerous knowledge in balance."},{"id":"talen","name":"Talen Mire","circle":"Valemere witness","role":"Scarred survivor","image":"assets/cast/talen.webp","color":"#5f78a8","accent":"#dce8ff","summary":"A Valemere student whose injured hand and quiet testimony keep Lucien's past from staying buried."},{"id":"darian","name":"Darian Thorne","circle":"Noctis Red Circle","role":"Internal dissenter","image":"assets/cast/darian.webp","color":"#7f1d1d","accent":"#ffd6d6","summary":"A Noctis senior whose tired conscience pushes him to witness against his own institution."},{"id":"liora","name":"Liora Aerlen-Valen","circle":"Circle Zero record echo","role":"Preserved witness","image":"assets/cast/liora.webp","color":"#6b5f8f","accent":"#f7e7b4","summary":"A Foundation witness-record echo tied to the Aerlen and Valen lines, speaking warnings across decades."},{"id":"mara","name":"Mara Kest","circle":"Drakenshard observer","role":"Honor duelist","image":"assets/cast/mara.webp","color":"#4b5563","accent":"#d9e4ef","summary":"A Drakenshard duelist who treats cruelty as dishonor, even when it is hidden inside a victory."},{"id":"finn","name":"Finn Castellan","circle":"Green Circle","role":"Student organizer","image":"assets/cast/finn.webp","color":"#2f7d4f","accent":"#d8f7df","summary":"Mira's older sibling and a Green Circle organizer who turns scattered witnesses into a collective record."},{"id":"senna","name":"Magistra Senna Voss","circle":"Council investigator","role":"External witness","image":"assets/cast/senna.webp","color":"#3f3a4d","accent":"#e6d8ff","summary":"An Inter-National Schools Council investigator whose professional neutrality begins to fracture under what she sees."}],"castGallery":[{"id":"haru-field-reader","name":"Haru Aerlen","title":"Field Reader","image":"assets/cast/gallery/haru-field-reader.webp","alt":"Haru Aerlen reading old mana pressure while his Green Circle cover stays visible"},{"id":"elira-pendant","name":"Elira Vale","title":"Hidden Pendant","image":"assets/cast/gallery/elira-pendant.webp","alt":"Elira Vale with a hidden dark crystal pendant beneath her Yellow Circle composure"},{"id":"aira-map","name":"Aira Solen","title":"Mapmaker","image":"assets/cast/gallery/aira-map.webp","alt":"Aira Solen building a tactical infrastructure map with blue support glyphs"},{"id":"leon-current","name":"Leon Varek","title":"Field Shield","image":"assets/cast/gallery/leon-current.webp","alt":"Leon Varek using disciplined water magic as a protective field witness"},{"id":"cassian-flame","name":"Cassian Veyr","title":"Controlled Fire","image":"assets/cast/gallery/cassian-flame.webp","alt":"Cassian Veyr holding restrained Red Circle fire with confident focus"},{"id":"halden-witness","name":"Professor Halden","title":"Restrained Observer","image":"assets/cast/gallery/halden-witness.webp","alt":"Professor Halden standing beside evidence tags as a restrained observer witness"},{"id":"cael-conduit","name":"Cael Nox","title":"Conduit Witness","image":"assets/cast/gallery/cael-conduit.webp","alt":"Cael Nox recovering under Aurelis healer protection as a conduit witness"},{"id":"rhea-council","name":"Rhea Valmont","title":"Council Authority","image":"assets/cast/gallery/rhea-council.webp","alt":"Rhea Valmont raising a precise red Student Council seal"},{"id":"lucien-dominion","name":"Lucien Voss","title":"Dominion Witness","image":"assets/cast/gallery/lucien-dominion.webp","alt":"Lucien Voss framed by cold Noctis relay glass and lightless mana"},{"id":"nera-binder","name":"Nera Vail","title":"Binder","image":"assets/cast/gallery/nera-binder.webp","alt":"Nera Vail with precise blue binding rings, careful posture, and a Noctis record slate nearby"},{"id":"soren-duelist","name":"Soren Ashvale","title":"Valemere Duelist","image":"assets/cast/gallery/soren-duelist.webp","alt":"Soren Ashvale in silver-blue Valemere uniform with a scarred hand and red crest, carrying a quiet witness note"},{"id":"mira-gallery","name":"Mira Castellan","title":"Witness Organizer","image":"assets/cast/gallery/mira-gallery.webp","alt":"Mira Castellan organizing Green Circle witnesses in the public gallery with a notebook in hand"},{"id":"valen-record","name":"Professor Iris Valen","title":"Record Historian","image":"assets/cast/gallery/valen-record.webp","alt":"Professor Iris Valen studying ancient Foundation Record fragments beneath gold archive light"},{"id":"talen-contamination","name":"Talen Mire","title":"Still Infected","image":"assets/cast/gallery/talen-contamination.webp","alt":"Talen Mire standing in the Valemere gallery with his braced right hand visible as he refuses to be silenced"},{"id":"darian-conscience","name":"Darian Thorne","title":"Noctis Conscience","image":"assets/cast/gallery/darian-conscience.webp","alt":"Darian Thorne dissenting through a Noctis relay window with other students behind him"},{"id":"liora-record-echo","name":"Liora Aerlen-Valen","title":"Record Echo","image":"assets/cast/gallery/liora-record-echo.webp","alt":"Liora Aerlen-Valen manifesting from the Foundation pool as a silver-black witness-record echo"},{"id":"mara-honor","name":"Mara Kest","title":"Drakenshard Honor","image":"assets/cast/gallery/mara-honor.webp","alt":"Mara Kest standing with one hand near her sword hilt as she objects to cruelty disguised as testing"},{"id":"finn-scroll","name":"Finn Castellan","title":"Collective Witness","image":"assets/cast/gallery/finn-scroll.webp","alt":"Finn Castellan holding a Green Circle student scroll signed by many witnesses in the trial gallery"},{"id":"senna-witness","name":"Magistra Senna Voss","title":"Council Witness","image":"assets/cast/gallery/senna-witness.webp","alt":"Magistra Senna Voss watching through a relay window as her professional composure cracks"}]};
